(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("nonnus-dionysiaca",[{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book I"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"First it has the son of Cronus, the light-bearing ravisher of a maiden, and the starry vault battered by the hands of Typhon."},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell, goddess, of the son of Cronus' messenger of blazing radiance,","st":"1.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"the birth-pang breath of the thunderbolt, with its bridal spark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lightning that served Semele's bridal chamber; tell also the birth"},{"sp":"N","t":"of twice-born Bacchus, whom, still moist, he lifted from the fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus lifting the half-finished infant from a mother delivered without a midwife,","st":"1.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with careful hands cutting open the flesh of his thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brought him to birth from a male womb, both father and lady mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"well acquainted with another birth, since from his generative head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing an unbelievable seedless swelling in his pregnant brow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shot forth Athena flashing in her armor.","st":"1.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bring me the fennel-stalk; clash the cymbals, Muses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and place in my hand the thyrsus of Dionysus whom I sing."},{"sp":"N","t":"But from beside neighboring Pharos, touching the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set many-turning Proteus before me, so that he may appear"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a manifold form, since I am sounding a manifold hymn.","st":"1.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"For if he should crawl forward as a serpent, curling in his coils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall sing the divine contest, how with an ivy-wreathed thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slaughtered the dreadful tribes of serpent-haired Giants."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if as a lion he should bristle, shaking the hair upon his neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall cry Euoi for Bacchus upon the grim arm of Rhea,","st":"1.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealthily sucking the breast of the lion-nursing goddess."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if, lifted high in a tempestuous leap of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he springs as a leopard, changing into that richly patterned form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall hymn the son of Zeus, how he destroyed the race of Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding in leopard-drawn cars against their elephants.","st":"1.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"If he should make his body like the form of a boar, I shall sing Thyone's son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for Aura, the boar-slaying bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Cybele-born mother of the late-born third Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"If he should become imitative water, I shall sing Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"plunging into the bosom of the sea before armed Lycurgus.","st":"1.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"If he should wave as a plant and spread a counterfeit whispering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall remember Icarius, and how beside the inspired wine-vat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grape-cluster was crushed beneath the competing tread of feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bring me the fennel-stalk, Mimallons, and the raw"},{"sp":"N","t":"many-spotted fawnskin, instead of my accustomed tunic,","st":"1.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"bind it tightly over my breast, filled with the fragrance of Maronian"},{"sp":"N","t":"nectar; but with sea-dwelling Eidothea and Homer"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the heavy sealskin be kept for Menelaus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Give me Bacchic drums and aegises, but the sweet-toned"},{"sp":"N","t":"double-voiced pipe give to another, lest I provoke","st":"1.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"my Phoebus; for he rejects the breathing echo of reeds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ever since, after defeating Marsyas' god-challenging pipe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hung his skin from a tree, swelling out in the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having stripped bare every limb of the skinless shepherd."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, goddess, begin with Cadmus, the wandering seeker.","st":"1.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"Once on the Sidonian shore high-horned Zeus became a bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a counterfeit throat imitated a desirous bellow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had the sweet gadfly of love; and as he shifted the woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"little Eros, circling his hands around her belly in a double bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightened her weight; and close by, the bull that would travel the water,","st":"1.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading his curved back beneath the mounting maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crouched sideways and stretched out his lowered back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted Europa; as the bull rushed onward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his swimming hoof scored the silent water of the passable sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"with gentle-stepping tracks; and above the sea the maiden,","st":"1.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling with fear, voyaged upon the bull's back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken and unwetted; seeing her, you might quickly have said"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was Thetis, or Galatea, or the wife of the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or Aphrodite seated upon Triton's ridged back."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the dark-haired god marveled at the voyage of the shambling-footed bull,","st":"1.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Triton, hearing the deceptive bellow of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowed back to the son of Cronus a responsive melody upon his conch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing a wedding hymn; and the woman borne aloft"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus, mingling wonder with fear, pointed out to Doris,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he saw the strange horned voyager; and with only her extremities dipped,","st":"1.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden, her bull serving as a ship, sailed as the driver of the ox,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, trembling at the lofty leap of her watery journey,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she held his horn as a steering-oar, while Desire became the sailor."},{"sp":"N","t":"And deceitful Boreas, stirred by the breeze of marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swelled out the whole of her robe, himself ill-starred in love, and toward both","st":"1.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"he whistled, secretly envying them, across the maiden's unripe breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"And just as one of the Nereids, rising above the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated upon a dolphin, cuts through the outspread calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while, as she is carried, her dripping hand moves to and fro"},{"sp":"N","t":"in imitation of one swimming, though the creature bears her unwetted by the brine,","st":"1.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the watery traveler, half-visible, carries her through the water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arching his back, while, creeping through the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-forked tip of the fish's tail traces its watery path;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so Zeus raised his back; and as the bull stretched forward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros the herdsman lashed his enslaved neck with the embroidered girdle,","st":"1.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, raising his bow upon his shoulder like a shepherd's staff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Aphrodite's crook he shepherded Hera's bridegroom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leading him toward Poseidon's watery pasture; and in maidenly shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"motherless Pallas flushed crimson upon her cheek"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she watched a woman serving as charioteer of the son of Cronus.","st":"1.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as Zeus rushed along his watery course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea did not extinguish his desire, because the pregnant waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the furrow of Heaven, had once labored to give birth to sea-dwelling Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maiden, directing the foaming bull's journey,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was at once his cargo and his sailor. But when he saw","st":"1.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"this counterfeit, swift-kneed, living ship of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wandering Achaean sailor cried aloud these words:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My eyes, what marvel is this? How is it that, cutting the waves with his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a pasture-roaming bull swims through the water of the barren sea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Is the son of Cronus making the earth navigable? Is through the sea","st":"1.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"a watery track being carved for a wave-drenched wagon?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I gaze across the waves at this counterfeit voyage. Is it perhaps Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who, having an unyoked bull, travels the sea instead of the upper air?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Or is it deep-dwelling Thetis who drives a watery course?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Yet a sea-bull does not possess a form resembling a land-dwelling bull...","st":"1.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"for it has a fishlike body - but instead of a naked maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some strange-looking Nereid drives through the waters an unbridled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"foot-going traveler, an unfamiliar bull, while her robe trails behind her."},{"sp":"N","t":"If this is grain-crowned Demeter, and with the water-traveling"},{"sp":"N","t":"foot of a bull she cleaves the blue back of the sea,","st":"1.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"then you too, Poseidon, leave the wave within the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pass over the thirsty back of the earth as a land-going plowman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting Demeter's furrow with your sea-going ship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making upon the earth a navigable course for terrestrial winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bull, you have wandered away as an exile! Nereus is no","st":"1.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"cattle-herd, Proteus no plowman, Glaucus no gardener;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there is no marsh, no meadow among the waves, but upon the"},{"sp":"N","t":"barren sea sailors traverse the unplowed, ship-receiving water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting it with a steering-oar and not cleaving it with iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"The attendants of the Earth-shaker do not sow furrows;","st":"1.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"rather, seaweed is the plant of the sea and water is its seed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sailor is its farmer, the voyage its furrows, and the ship its plow."},{"sp":"N","t":"But why are you carrying some maiden away? Can it really be that"},{"sp":"N","t":"bulls themselves, maddened with love, carry women off?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Or has deceitful Poseidon abducted a maiden,","st":"1.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"assuming the horned, bull-shaped form of a river?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has he woven another deception after the bed of Tyro,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he did before, when as a watery lover"},{"sp":"N","t":"he falsely murmured like Enipeus with counterfeit streams?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such were the words the astonished Greek sailor spoke as he passed.","st":"1.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"But the maiden divined the bull-marriage awaiting her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, tearing at her locks, broke into a mournful cry:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Deaf water, speechless shores, tell the bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if bulls can hear: 'Pitiless creature, spare the maiden!'"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, shores - tell my child-loving father","st":"1.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Europa has abandoned her homeland, seated upon a certain bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who is her abductor and her sailor and, as I think, her future husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"Circling winds, carry these locks of hair to my mother."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, Boreas, I beg you: as you carried off the Attic maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive me upon your wings and bear me through the air - but restrain yourself, my voice,","st":"1.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after the bull I find Boreas too maddened with love.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the maiden was ferried upon the bull's back;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cadmus meanwhile, wandering from land to land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"followed the uncertain tracks of the bride-bearing bull."},{"sp":"N","t":"He came even to the murderous cavern of the Arimi, when wandering mountains","st":"1.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"were battering the unbreakable gates of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the winged gods, high above the winterless Nile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated the untraceable journey of birds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing their strange course through the air upon the wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the seven-zoned heaven was being scourged. For the son of Cronus, Zeus,","st":"1.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"had gone to the bed of Plouto, so that he might beget"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tantalus, the reckless thief of the heavenly cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and had placed the weapons of the upper air concealed within the recess of a rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hidden the lightning; but beneath the roof the thunderbolts"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched smoke, and the white cliff was blackened.","st":"1.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"And by the hidden spark of the fire-pointed missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the springs grew hot, while through the ravine-streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Mygdonian gorge, foaming, roared with steam."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then, stretching out his hands at the bidding of his mother Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cilician Typhon stole the snowy weapons of Zeus -","st":"1.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"the weapons of fire; and spreading wide his row of deep-roaring throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raised every kind of battle-cry in the mingled voices of beasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"Serpents grown together with him rushed about the faces"},{"sp":"N","t":"of leopards and licked the shaggy manes of lions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the coils of their tails they girdled the horns of bulls,","st":"1.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the jaws of long-tongued creatures"},{"sp":"N","t":"they mingled darting venom with the foam of boars."},{"sp":"N","t":"Having placed the weapons of the son of Cronus beneath a cavernous rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched the crop of his towering hands toward the upper air.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[18,20],[23,21]]},{"sp":"N","t":"With his many-handed host around the topmost foot of Olympus,","st":"1.165","hvShift5":[[0,0],[42,42],[50,48]]},{"sp":"N","t":"with one hand he squeezed Cynosura, while with another he pressed"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pulled backward the ridge of the Parrhasian Bear leaning toward the axis;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with another he seized Boötes and drove him back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while with another he dragged Phosphorus; in vain beneath the circling turning-post"},{"sp":"N","t":"the morning crack of the heavenly whip whistled.","st":"1.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"He dragged away the Dawn; and with Taurus held back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horse-driving Season, untimely and unfinished, ceased her course."},{"sp":"N","t":"And among the shadowy locks of his serpent-haired heads"},{"sp":"N","t":"light was mingled with darkness, while by day"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene shone beside the Sun as they rose together.","st":"1.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the Giant cease. Turning back upon his course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from Boreas toward Notus, leaving one pole, he stood at the other;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, grasping the Charioteer with his long hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lashed the back of hail-bearing Capricorn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, dragging the twin Fishes down from the upper air toward the sea,","st":"1.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"he battered the Ram, the central star of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"high beside the neighboring, fiery circle of spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the balanced day hangs equally poised against the night."},{"sp":"N","t":"Typhon was suspended aloft upon his trailing feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"close to the clouds; and spreading the multitudinous host of his arms,","st":"1.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"he covered the silvery brilliance of the cloudless upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing his crooked army of serpents. One of them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing upright, ran across the rim of the axial circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leapt upon the spine of the heavenly Dragon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hissing war; another, beside the daughter of Cepheus,","st":"1.190","hvShift5":[[0,0],[11,21],[51,52]]},{"sp":"N","t":"winding his equal-poised circle with starry hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bound captive Andromeda in a second bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying obliquely beneath his coils; another horned serpent"},{"sp":"N","t":"curled around the pointed horn of the bull of like form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raging in coils above the bull's forehead","st":"1.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"against the opposing Hyades, image of the horned Moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with jaws spread open; and the venom-shooting bands"},{"sp":"N","t":"of other intertwined dragons girdled Boötes."},{"sp":"N","t":"Another bold serpent sprang forward when he saw another Serpent of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dancing around the serpent-bearing arm of Ophiuchus.","st":"1.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"And, intertwining another crown about Ariadne's Crown,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he arched his neck and writhed with the trailing coil of his belly."},{"sp":"N","t":"Shaking the girdle of Zephyrus and the wing of opposing Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"many-armed Typhon wheeled about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward either turning-post, dragging Hesperus after Phosphorus,","st":"1.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and toward Atlas' ridge. And in the swelling bosom of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"often seizing Poseidon's chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he dragged it from the depths onto the land; and by its sea-drenched mane,"},{"sp":"N","t":"again he hauled a stalled horse from beside its underwater manger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled the wandering colt toward the heavenly rim,","st":"1.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting it like a spear against Olympus; and beneath the yokes of the Sun's"},{"sp":"N","t":"speeding chariot, his circling horses neighed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Often, too, seizing a bull released from the plow-beam of the field,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[37,30],[43,41],[50,46],[56,54],[70,64],[71,67]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he brandished the lowing beast in his threatening arm"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled it as a likeness of equal form against Selene,","st":"1.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing her course to a halt; and, jerking backward upon the reins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made the goddess's white bull-harnesses whistle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring forth the deadly hiss of his venom-shooting viper."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yet Titan-born Mene did not yield to the armed attacker;"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting against the Giant's similarly horned heads,","st":"1.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"she gashed them with the light-bearing circles of her bull-like horn."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Selene's gleaming cattle bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"astonished at the gaping jaws of Typhon's chin."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then the fearless Seasons armed the starry battle-lines."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ranks of heaven, in the guiding circle of Helice,","st":"1.225","hvShift5":[[0,0],[27,27],[29,30],[37,31],[56,51],[58,55],[60,56]]},{"sp":"N","t":"flashed toward battle; with fiery brilliance there rushed"},{"sp":"N","t":"through the raging upper air the shifting host - those allotted to Boreas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those to the western back of Lips, those who possessed the bounds of Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those of the southern reaches; and amid their rival tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unshaken chorus of the fixed stars wandered from its place,","st":"1.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and met the wandering stars coming from the opposite direction; with a roar"},{"sp":"N","t":"the upright axis, fixed through the middle, thundered in heaven's empty space."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when Orion, the dog-slayer, saw the tribes of beasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew his sword; as the bearer armed himself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bright back of his Tanagraean blade glittered.","st":"1.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"And, flashing radiance from his fire-heated throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thirsty Dog foamed at his starry jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending forth a scorching bark, and instead of his accustomed hare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bared the steaming breath of his teeth against Typhon's beasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vault of heaven crashed; answering in turn,","st":"1.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"from as many throats as there are zones encircling heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seven-mouthed echo of the Pleiades raised a sevenfold battle-cry,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wandering stars thundered back with an equal measure of noise."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when shining Ophiuchus saw the Giant's terrible serpent-shaped form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his evil-averting hands","st":"1.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shook loose the grey-green backs of his fire-nourished serpents,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[21,20],[23,29]]},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling the spotted, crooked missile; around its fiery path"},{"sp":"N","t":"tempests whistled, while obliquely there were shot"},{"sp":"N","t":"serpent-arrows raging through the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Sagittarius, running beside fish-tailed Capricorn -","st":"1.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sagittarius shot his arrow; and within the circle of the Wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dragon, visible midway and divided between the twin Bears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"writhed the light-bearing coil of his heavenly spine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Erigone's neighbor, the driver who travels beside the Wagon -","hvShift6":[[0,0],[3,13],[23,22]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Boötes brandished his shepherd's staff with gleaming arm;","st":"1.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the knee of the Phantom and the neighboring Swan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the starry lyre prophesied the victory of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then Typhon seized and shook the peaks of Corycus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, constricting the inhabited stream of the Cilician river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crushed Tarsus and Cydnus together in a single embrace.","st":"1.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"And shooting the ranks of the brine with rocky missiles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he displaced the sea onto the cliffs, lashing the ocean after the upper air."},{"sp":"N","t":"As the Giant advanced, along the track of his sea-drenched foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his waist appeared laid bare through the water, untouched by the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while the heavy-roaring water boomed around the middle of his thigh.","st":"1.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"His swimming serpents, from their sea-thundering throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"hissing the war-cry of Ares, marched against the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting venom; and in the fish-filled ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Typhon stood within the swelling channel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his feet were fixed in the depths, while his belly mingled with the air,","st":"1.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressed among the clouds; and from the Giant's head"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the dreadful roaring of high-maned lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea-lion hid itself within a muddy hollow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the whole host of sea-monsters was cramped within the ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the Earth-born Giant filled the entire sea, greater than the earth,","st":"1.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"with flanks untouched by the waves; and the seals bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while dolphins hid themselves in the deep sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And weaving an intertwined trail with crooked coils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the many-witted octopus fastened itself to its accustomed rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and its form became a ravine-like image of the rock's limbs.","st":"1.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"No creature remained without fear; even the"},{"sp":"N","t":"passion-maddened moray, pursuing its desire for a serpent's bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shuddered at the god-defying breath of the sea-going dragons."},{"sp":"N","t":"The sea towered upward and came into contact with Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"through its sky-high waters; and in the airborne stream","st":"1.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dry-winged bird bathed in the neighboring sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Typhon, displaying an imitation of the deep-sea trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with an earth-shaking blow of his immeasurable hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut an island, a detached portion of sea-bordered land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled the whole thing, spinning it backward like a ball.","st":"1.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"As the Giant fought, mountain summits neighboring the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting their shadows over the Sun, were armed against Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the projectile-bearing arms of a sky-high mountain."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the boundary of the deep, after Earth's fertile seat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the counterfeit Zeus armed his hand with the fire-pointed thunderbolt.","st":"1.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Raising the weapons of the son of Cronus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his two hundred invincible hands, monstrous Typhon struggled"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath their weight - though the son of Cronus lifted them with a single hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"Upon the dry arms of the cloudless Giant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunder sent forth a muted strain of soundless echo -","st":"1.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"rumbling only faintly; and scarcely for him, amid the drought of the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did a thirsty dew fall from the snow that shed no drops."},{"sp":"N","t":"The lightning grew dim, and, resembling blazing smoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"its gleam gave only a slender fire with downcast radiance."},{"sp":"N","t":"And recognizing the hands of their unaccustomed bearer,","st":"1.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunderbolts, though possessing a masculine blaze, grew womanish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"repeatedly slipping from those immeasurable hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"in self-propelled leaps; the fiery bolts went wandering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for the familiar hand of their heavenly bearer."},{"sp":"N","t":"Just as when some horseman encounters a steed that spits out the bit,","st":"1.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, an inexperienced stranger, repeatedly lashes the disobedient colt"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struggles in vain, while the bold horse, with understanding spirit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"recognizes the strange hand of an unfamiliar charioteer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, goaded, rears upward, rising erect,"},{"sp":"N","t":"planting firmly the unshaken hoof of its hind foot,","st":"1.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"while lifting its projecting fore-knees and pawing the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tossing its crest, while over both shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"its abundant mane swings loosely beside its neck -"},{"sp":"N","t":"so Typhon exhausted himself, lifting from hand to hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fugitive brilliance of the wandering thunderbolt.","st":"1.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"While wandering Cadmus was roaming among the Arimi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile, upon the Dictaean shore, the water-traveling bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"set down from his back his maiden, still untouched by the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera, seeing the son of Cronus shaken by desire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mad with jealousy, joined her voice to laughing anger:","st":"1.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Phoebus, stand beside your father, lest some plowman"},{"sp":"N","t":"seize Zeus and drag him beneath an earth-shaking plow-beam!"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indeed, I wish someone would seize and drag him, so that I might cry this to Zeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Endure the double goads - those of farmers and those of Loves!'"},{"sp":"N","t":"As Nomios, lord of the pasture, glorious archer, shepherd your own father,","st":"1.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest Selene, driver of cattle, yoke the son of Cronus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, hastening to see the bed of her shepherd Endymion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stripe Zeus' back beneath her unsparing whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"Lord Zeus, horn-bearing Io, when she was a heifer, missed her chance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because she never saw you as such a husband as this, so that she might bear","st":"1.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"some bull of matching form to a similarly horned mate."},{"sp":"N","t":"Beware Hermes, the cattle-thief, practicing his accustomed craft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he seize you as a bull and steal his own father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and once again give a lyre to your son Phoebus"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the price paid by one thief for what another thief has stolen! But what can I do?","st":"1.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"If only Argus, his whole body gleaming with sleepless eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were still alive, so that, dragging you into some inaccessible pasture,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera's herdsman might strike Zeus' flanks with his cattle-goad!\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So she spoke. But the son of Cronus abandoned his bull-shaped form"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, resembling a young man, encircled the unwed maiden.","st":"1.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"He touched her limbs, and from the maiden's breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"first loosened the encircling girdle; then, as though unwilling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pressed the swelling rim of her unyielding breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kissed the edge of her lips; and silently unfastening"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sacred knot that guarded her unwedded maidenhood -","st":"1.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"he plucked the unripe fruit of the Cyprian Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And her belly swelled, burgeoning with twin offspring;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zeus, her husband, left his pregnant bride, destined for a most holy labor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Asterion, her deeply wealthy consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"And rising beside the ankle of the Charioteer,","st":"1.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"the starry Bull, bridegroom of Olympus, glittered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping his dew-loving back for the springtime Phaethon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rising crosswise in a crouching posture; and, half-immersed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching his projecting right foot toward Orion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he appears; but, moving more swiftly toward the western rim,","st":"1.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"he passes the Charioteer who rises alongside him on the same course."},{"sp":"N","t":"Thus the Bull was established in heaven. But Typhon"},{"sp":"N","t":"was not destined much longer to possess the weapons of Zeus; for with bow-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros, Zeus the son of Cronus left the revolving vault of heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the mountains encountered Cadmus, the wandering seeker,","st":"1.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he strayed; and together they devised a many-sided plan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weaving for Typhon the threads of Fate that cannot be escaped."},{"sp":"N","t":"And goat-herding Pan, traveling with all-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave him cattle and sheep and ranks of well-horned goats."},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus wove a hut from reeds and, binding it with twisting fastenings,","st":"1.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"fixed it upon the ground; then, disguising him in an unrecognizable form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fastened shepherd's clothing upon Cadmus' body,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with counterfeit garments cloaked him as a false herdsman."},{"sp":"N","t":"And carrying a deceitful shepherd's pipe, he gave it to clever Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to serve as the pilot steering Typhon toward destruction.","st":"1.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"Then Zeus summoned the counterfeit herdsman and the winged charioteer"},{"sp":"N","t":"of generation, and joined them together in a single plan:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear Cadmus, play your pipe, and heaven shall become calm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"delay, and Olympus will be scourged - for Typhon"},{"sp":"N","t":"is armed with my own celestial weapons.","st":"1.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"Only my aegis remains to me. But what can"},{"sp":"N","t":"my aegis accomplish when contending against Typhon's thunderbolt?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear that old Cronus may laugh; and I dread"},{"sp":"N","t":"the proud neck of my adversary, arrogant Iapetus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Still more I fear tale-producing Hellas, lest some Achaean","st":"1.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"call Typhon 'Rain-giver' and 'High-ruler,'"},{"sp":"N","t":"or 'Most High,' defiling my own name. Become a herdsman"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn, and with your mind-bewitching,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clear-toned shepherd's instrument save the shepherd of the cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me not hear the sound of Typhon the Cloud-gatherer,","st":"1.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the thunder of another counterfeit Zeus; instead I shall stop him"},{"sp":"N","t":"from fighting with lightning flashes and wielding the thunderbolt as a spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"If indeed you possess the blood of Zeus and descend from Inachian Io,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the evil-averting melody of your crafty shepherd's pipe"},{"sp":"N","t":"enchant Typhon's mind. And I shall give you rewards worthy of your labor -","st":"1.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"two gifts: for I shall make you the savior"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the harmony of the cosmos and the husband of Harmonia."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, first-sown source of marriage that brings generation to fulfillment,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros, draw your bow, and the cosmos shall wander no longer."},{"sp":"N","t":"If all things came from you, loving shepherd of life -","st":"1.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretch one more arrow, so that you may save the whole universe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaze forth against Typhon in your fiery form, and may the fire-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"thunderbolts, through you, return again into my hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"All-subduer, strike that one man with your fire, and let him, enchanted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be captured by your arrow - the man whom even the son of Cronus has not defeated;","st":"1.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let him feel for Cadmus' mind-bewitching song an irresistible frenzy"},{"sp":"N","t":"as great as the desire I felt for the marriage-bed of Europa.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, Zeus sped away, wholly resembling a horned bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from which the mountain received the name Taurus. Then Cadmus, sharply sounding"},{"sp":"N","t":"the deceptive echo of his concordant reeds,","st":"1.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaned his back beneath a neighboring oak of the woodland pasture;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wearing the rustic clothing of a genuine herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sent his guile-weaving song into Typhon's ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"releasing a delicate breath from his swollen cheek."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then the music-loving Giant, advancing on the trailing coils of his serpentine feet,","st":"1.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprang up when he heard the deceitful melody; and within the cavern,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the blazing weapons of Zeus beside his mother Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he went searching for the nearby song of the delightful pipe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"following the melody; but when Cadmus saw him near the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling, he hid beneath a cleft in the rock.","st":"1.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"But high-headed Typhon noticed him trying to escape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the monster summoned him with a wordless gesture;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did he recognize the clear-voiced deception. To the counterfeit"},{"sp":"N","t":"shepherd he stretched out one right hand, unaware"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the net of destruction; and from the middle of his blood-red faces,","st":"1.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughing with a human face, he broke into this boastful speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Goatherd, why do you tremble before me? Why cover your eyes with your hand?"},{"sp":"N","t":"It would be glorious for me to pursue a mortal man after pursuing the son of Cronus!"},{"sp":"N","t":"It would be glorious for me to raise a shepherd's pipe together with the lightning!"},{"sp":"N","t":"What fellowship is there between reeds and a smoking thunderbolt?","st":"1.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"Keep your own instrument for yourself, since Typhon has received another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-sounding instrument of Olympus! But sitting there,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus, with hands that make no thunder and are deprived of their accustomed sound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloudless Zeus has need of your shepherd's instrument."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let him possess the clatter of your few reeds; for I do not","st":"1.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"weave together worthless reeds, joining reed to reed in ordered rows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, joining rolling clouds to clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I send forth a yoked crash with the thunderous noises of heaven."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yet, if you wish, I shall establish a friendly contest. You,"},{"sp":"N","t":"play your reedy melody, while I shall sound my thunderous one.","st":"1.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"You swell your projecting cheek, bursting with breath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blow through your mouths; but for me, when they are lashed"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the blowing breath of Boreas, my thunderbolts roar."},{"sp":"N","t":"Herdsman, you shall receive payment for your music; for when,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as sceptre-bearer in place of Zeus, I guide the throne of heaven,","st":"1.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall carry you too from earth into the upper air to follow me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"together with your very pipe and, if you wish, your flock as well."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor shall you be separated from your herd; for your goats"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall establish above the back of Capricorn, their counterpart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or near the Charioteer, who in Olympus","st":"1.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretches the light-bearing Olenian Goat upon his gleaming arm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beside the broad neck of the rain-producing Bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall establish your cattle as stars rising over Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or beside the dewy turning-point where, from life-warming throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene's cattle send their windy bellowing.","st":"1.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor will there be any need for your little hut; instead of the woodland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let your flock flash among the heavenly Kids."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I shall fashion the image of another manger, so that it too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shining in corresponding form, may stand beside the neighboring Manger of the Asses."},{"sp":"N","t":"You too shall become a star beside the herdsman, where Boötes","st":"1.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"appears; and you too, stretching out a starry shepherd's staff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall become driver of the Lycaonian Bear-Wagon."},{"sp":"N","t":"Sharing the dwelling of heavenly Typhon, fortunate shepherd,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sing today upon the earth, and tomorrow within Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as rewards worthy of your song, beside the star-bright circle","st":"1.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall establish your Olympian form, and your sweet-toned"},{"sp":"N","t":"shepherd's pipe I shall join beside the heavenly Lyre."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you wish, I shall give you the virgin Athena in marriage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if the grey-eyed goddess does not please you, receive Leto,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a Grace, or Cytherea, or Artemis, or marriage with Hebe.","st":"1.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"Only do not seek the bed of my Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you have a horse-driving brother skilled in the chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him receive the Sun's fiery four-horse car."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if, as a goatherd, you desire to brandish the aegis of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give you this gift as well. But I shall journey to Olympus,","st":"1.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"caring nothing for the unarmed son of Cronus; for what could"},{"sp":"N","t":"worthless Athena, being a woman, accomplish against me with her weapons?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Come, herdsman, strike up the song of Typhon's victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hymning me as the legitimate new sceptre-bearer of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying the sceptre of Zeus and wearing his lightning-flashing robe.\"","st":"1.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"So he spoke, and Adrasteia recorded that mighty boast."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when Cadmus recognized that the Earth-born son had willingly entered"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hunting-net, carried along by the thread of Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the sweet goad of the delightful reeds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crafty Cadmus, without smiling, uttered his cunning reply:","st":"1.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You were astonished when you heard only the feeble sound of my pipe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me, what would you do if I should sing of your throne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sounding a victory hymn upon a seven-stringed lyre?"},{"sp":"N","t":"For once, competing even with heavenly plectrums,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I surpassed Phoebus with my lyre; but the son of Cronus","st":"1.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"reduced my sweet-sounding strings to ashes with his thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"granting favor to his defeated son. But if ever I find"},{"sp":"N","t":"strong sinews again and stretch a melody beneath my plectrum,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall enchant every tree, every mountain, and the minds of beasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Ocean, the self-circling crown yoked to the earth of equal age,","st":"1.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"though rushing swiftly in his backward-turning course, I shall restrain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stopping his circling water from traveling around the same turning-point;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall halt the host of fixed stars and the wandering stars racing opposite them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phaethon too, and the ox-team of Selene.","hvShift5":[[0,0],[22,37],[49,43]]},{"sp":"N","t":"But when you have struck the gods and Zeus with your fiery missile,","st":"1.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"spare only the glorious archer, so that around the banquet table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Typhon feasts, Phoebus and I may compete"},{"sp":"N","t":"to see which of us can best celebrate mighty Typhon in song."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not kill the Pierian maidens of the dance either, so that they too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Phoebus - or your shepherd here - leads the revel,","st":"1.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"may weave their feminine song in harmony with our masculine music.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and Typhon nodded beneath his flashing brows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shook his locks; and from his belching hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hills were sprinkled with serpentine venom."},{"sp":"N","t":"Swiftly he hurried into his cavern; and from there, lifting","st":"1.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sinews of Zeus, he gave them as a guest-gift to deceitful Cadmus -"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sinews that once had fallen to earth in Typhon's battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the deceptive shepherd praised the immortal gift;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he handled them, and, as though they were suitable strings"},{"sp":"N","t":"destined for his lyre, hid them within a hollow rock,","st":"1.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping them safe for Zeus the Giant-slayer. Then, sparingly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a delicate breath through his closed lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and secretly diminishing the volume of sound from the compressed reeds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he played an even sweeter melody; and stretching out his many ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Typhon listened to the harmony and understood nothing.","st":"1.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"And for the enchanted Giant the counterfeit shepherd played alongside him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"piping upon his shepherd's pipe as though singing the flight of the immortals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while in truth his melody celebrated the approaching victory of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing Typhon's doom to Typhon himself as he sat nearby."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he aroused an even greater frenzy. And just as a delicate young man,","st":"1.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"maddened with love beneath the sweet goad, is enchanted by a maiden of his own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now gazes upon the silvery circles of her lovely face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now upon the wandering cluster of her deeply rooted hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now upon her rose-colored hand; and at another moment watches"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rosy curve of her breast constrained beneath its girdle,","st":"1.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at her neck as it is uncovered; and around her beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"his unsatisfied gaze wanders this way and that in enchantment,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor does he ever wish to leave the maiden - so Typhon"},{"sp":"N","t":"surrendered his whole mind to Cadmus, utterly enchanted by his melodies."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book II"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The second holds the star-roaming war of Typhon, and the lightning, and the contest of Zeus, and the revel of Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"So he stayed there at the foot of the pasture-woodland,","st":"2.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing his shifting lips across the tip of the pipe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus, son of Agenor, the counterfeit goatherd; but unforeseen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus the son of Cronus, untraceable, creeping noiselessly beneath the cave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed his hands a second time with the fire that knew them,","st":"2.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a cloud sheltered Cadmus beside the unseen rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, learning the deceitful trick and the stealthy thief of his thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Typhon, wise too late, should slay"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fickle herdsman; but he, beneath the sweet goad, wished all the more"},{"sp":"N","t":"to hear the mind-bewitching rhythm of the song.","st":"2.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"And just as some sailor, hearing the thievish hymn of a Siren,"},{"sp":"N","t":"is drawn untimely to a self-chosen doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"enchanted by the melody, and no longer scoring the wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whitens the grey-green water no more with his waveless oars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, fallen into the nets of clear-voiced Fate,","st":"2.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"delights, forgetting his rudder, abandoning the star"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the seven-pathed Pleiad and the rim of the circling Bear —"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he, shaken by the breath of the cunning song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the pipe's sweet arrow as escort to his destruction."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he was veiled in the shadowy girdle of clouds,","st":"2.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"the breathing reed of the sweet-sounding herdsman fell silent"},{"sp":"N","t":"and broke off the harmony. Then high-soaring Typhon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seized with the frenzy of war, ran down into the recess of the cave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with wandering madness sought the wind-roaming thunder"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the untraceable lightning, and with searching foot","st":"2.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"hunted the blazing flash of the stolen thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and found the cave empty. Learning too late"},{"sp":"N","t":"the guile-devising purpose of the son of Cronus and the shifting wiles of Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he leapt against Olympus, hurling crags for spears."},{"sp":"N","t":"And, dragging the crooked track of his foot on serpentine soles,","st":"2.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spat venom from his missile-hurling throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the high-crested Giant rained springs"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his viperish hair, the ravines swelled in flood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he rushed, the Cilician foundation of the unbending ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"quivered, its seat sunk deep into the earth,","st":"2.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath his serpent feet; and in the loud-crashing tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the crested hollow of Taurus was battered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighboring Pamphylian banks danced in terror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the caverns of the earth boomed, the headlands trembled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the deep places shook, and the shores slid away","st":"2.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the sand was loosened by the earth-shaking tread of his feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"No pasture, no beast was then unharmed; for raw-devouring"},{"sp":"N","t":"bears were butchered by the bear-jaws"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Typhon's face, and the tawny limbs of shaggy-breasted lions"},{"sp":"N","t":"were gulped down by the lion heads","st":"2.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"with gaping mouths of like form, and in his viperish throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cold backs of earth-bred serpents were torn apart;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the birds of the air he devoured with a neighboring throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they flew through the pathless upper air, and one that came near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an eagle, he ate the more gladly, since it is called the bird of Zeus;","st":"2.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ate the plowing ox, and had no pity as he looked upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood-stained neck scored by the yoke-strap."},{"sp":"N","t":"He turned rivers to dust, drinking their water as an after-dinner draught,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drove off the hosts of Naiads from their haunts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Nymph of the deep, walking the passable stream with dry-shod leap,","st":"2.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood unsandaled, her limbs unwetted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Naiad of the watery ways; and as the maiden struggled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling, along the thirsty floor of her haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her knees, fixed fast, were bound in a muddy fetter."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the many-shaped face of the raging Giant,","st":"2.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fearful old shepherd flung away his pipe"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fled far off; and beholding the wide-scattered nation of arms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the goatherd cast his unsteady flute to the winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"The toiling plowman did not cover the seed in the soil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the fresh-furrowed earth with the grain he threw behind him,","st":"2.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, while Typhon's hand swung its guiding stroke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did he cleave with earth-shaking iron the furrow being cut,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he let his oxen go; and beneath the Giant's missile"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flanks of the riven cornfield were laid bare."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he loosed the liquid vein; as the chasm opened,","st":"2.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the channel below bubbled up with spring-fed streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring forth the water hidden in the bosom of the roofless ground;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crags were hurled; falling from the sky"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the torrent-streams, they buried themselves in the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning the waters to dry land; and from the earth-born missiles","st":"2.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-fixed feet of new-born islands took root."},{"sp":"N","t":"Trees were heaved up, roots and all, from the soil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unripe fruit fell to the ground; the fresh-blooming"},{"sp":"N","t":"garden was wiped away, the rosy meadow reduced to dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zephyrus was shaken by the cypresses rolling","st":"2.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"with withered leaves; and in grief-loving strains"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phoebus sang a dirge for the hyacinths torn apart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weaving a mournful hymn, and far more than for the Amyclaean clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"he groaned over the neighboring laurel as it was cut down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Pan in sorrow raised up his fallen pine;","st":"2.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, remembering Moria, the Attic bride who bears the city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Grey-eyed Athena moaned over the olive being felled;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Paphian wept for the anemone laid in the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, ceaselessly lamenting the fragrant hair of the flower-cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she tore a delicate curl from the rose-bed lying in the dust;","st":"2.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Deo mourned the half-grown corn destroyed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer leading the harvest-revel; and the Hadryad"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nymphs, of one age with them, wailed for the trees bereft of shade."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as a well-branched cluster was split apart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Hamadryad of the same age sprang, unveiled, from the laurel,","st":"2.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another maiden, fleeing from the pine with feet that could tread the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appeared near and spoke to her neighbor, the Nymph newly come:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hamadryad of the laurel who fled the marriage-bed, let there be one path"},{"sp":"N","t":"for us both, lest you see Phoebus, lest I perceive Pan."},{"sp":"N","t":"Woodcutters, pass these trees by; do not cut the plants","st":"2.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the wretched Daphne, sorrow-stricken; spare them, carpenter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not finish a merchant-ship by cutting pinewood timbers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest it touch the surges of Aphrodite of the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, woodman, grant me a final favor: instead of the branches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut me with your axes, and through my breast","st":"2.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"drive the chaste bronze of unwedded Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may die before marriage and go a virgin to Hades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still ignorant of Eros, just like Pitys, and just like Daphne.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, she made herself a counterfeit girdle of leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a green belt covered the curve of her breast","st":"2.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"in shame, and clamped thigh fast to thigh;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the other, gazing at her, broke into downcast speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I have the fear that belongs to my kind, the fear of maidenhood, since I too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"born of Daphne, am pursued, just like Daphne."},{"sp":"N","t":"Where shall I flee? Shall I creep under the crags? But the hills","st":"2.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled at Olympus have been turned to ash by the thunderbolts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I tremble at your Pan, ill in love, who will torment me"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he did Pitys, as he did Syrinx; and I too, pursued,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall become another mountain-running Echo, a second voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do I go among these branches; I shall dwell","st":"2.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-seen among the mountains, after my tree, where even"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, lover of maidens, hunts; but the son of Cronus"},{"sp":"N","t":"won the bed of Callisto by changing his form into Artemis."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to the swell of the sea; what is marriage to me? But in the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Earth-shaker, mad for women, pursued Asterie.","st":"2.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"If only I had light wings; along the high-soaring path"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would travel a course in company with the winds of the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but perhaps the running of wings is vain, since Typhon"},{"sp":"N","t":"touches the clouds with his sky-high hands."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if he forces me into a lawless marriage, I shall change my shape,","st":"2.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall mingle with the birds, and, flying, a nightingale,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heralding the rose and the flowery dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be the swallow, beloved of Zephyrus in spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chattering bird singing her tune beneath the eaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about her nest in winged dance.","st":"2.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"Procne, who suffered bitterly, do you in your mourning song"},{"sp":"N","t":"weep for your son, and I shall lament my marriage-bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Lord Zeus, do not make me a swallow, lest Tereus too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winged and enraged, pursue me, just like Typhon."},{"sp":"N","t":"Air, mountains, sea — no entry; within the earth","st":"2.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"I hide myself; but from the Giant's viperish feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"venom-shooting hydras of the lair burrow beneath the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me be a native stream of running water, as Comaetho"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingled her fresh-flowing streams with her father Cydnus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I do not wish that, for it is against the tale, that I should join","st":"2.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"my maiden-loving water to the outpourings of a maiden ill in love."},{"sp":"N","t":"Where shall I flee? Shall I couple with Typhon? But then I shall bring forth"},{"sp":"N","t":"a strange-natured, many-shaped son resembling his father."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me be another tree, and pass from oak to oak,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the name of a chaste maiden; but instead of Daphne,","st":"2.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not hear myself called a shoot named for lawless Myrrha."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, I beg you, beside the stream of mourning Eridanus"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me too be one of the Heliades; and thickly shall I send"},{"sp":"N","t":"amber from my eyelids, and with grief-loving branches"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall spread my leaves, entwined with the neighboring poplar,","st":"2.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"moaning for my maidenhood with tears of precious wealth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for it is not Phaethon I lament. Be gracious, laurel:"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to be another plant after the earlier plant of the wood."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be, like Niobe, a stone myself, so that even me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a stone moaning, the wayfarers may pity;","st":"2.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"but why should I take the shape of the slanderous-tongued? Be gracious, Leto;"},{"sp":"N","t":"away with the god-fighting name of the Nymph who bore such dreadful children.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So she spoke. And Phaethon, leaving the whirling vault,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turned his chariot toward the west; and leaping up from the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the air, stretched high like a cone, silent Night","st":"2.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"wrapped heaven about in a starry cloak,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorning the upper air; and beside the cloudless Nile"},{"sp":"N","t":"the immortals wandered, while beside beetling Taurus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus the son of Cronus awaited the light of battle-rousing Dawn."},{"sp":"N","t":"It was night; and the ranks of guards stood about Olympus,","st":"2.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"about its seven zones, and as though high on towers"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a shouting through the night, and the varied cry of the stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"was carried abroad on every side, and the crashing echo of the axle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the turning-post of Selene received from the starting-line of Cronus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with cloud upon cloud, in successive veils set crown-wise,","st":"2.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Seasons, guardians of the upper air, fenced heaven about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the attendants of Phaethon; and the stars barred the gates"},{"sp":"N","t":"that no thief may plunder, with the bolt of Atlas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest an ambush enter the vault while the blessed ones were away;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of the shrill of pipes and the accustomed flute,","st":"2.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winds whistled a melody with their nocturnal wings."},{"sp":"N","t":"And, traveling in company with the heavenly Dragon and the Arcadian Bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching from on high for Typhon's night assault,"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Boötes kept guard with sleepless eyes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dawnstar watched the east, the star Hesperus","st":"2.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the west, and Cepheus, leaving the southern gates to the guider of arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran the round of the rainy gates of Boreas."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there were fires on every side, since the blazing flames of the stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the nightly lamps of unsleeping Selene"},{"sp":"N","t":"glittered like torches, and with stormy whirl","st":"2.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting thickly from the upper air across the summit of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shooting stars inscribed the air with fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"favorable omens for the son of Cronus; and with tumbling leap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing thickly through the clouds as they were scored,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lightning skipped, and with alternating rushes","st":"2.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unstable gleam hid itself and shone again, running back and forth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, twisting the locks of its plaited fire in clusters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a comet roughened its radiance with a shaggy glow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stranger beams shone bright, and like long"},{"sp":"N","t":"rafters they were stretched from the air with far-reaching fire,","st":"2.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting beside Zeus with their spears; and shining beneath the rays"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Phaethon on the opposite side, the curved circles of Iris"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent, running together with the rain, weaving a many-colored trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing green with darkening, and white with rose."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to Zeus in his solitude Victory came as a comforter,","st":"2.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"tracing the highest paths of the air with her sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the form of Leto, and, arming her father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried out in resounding tones a many-turning speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Lord Zeus, stand as champion of your own children, and let me not see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athena, untaught in marriage, coupled with Typhon;","st":"2.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not make the motherless one a mother; but fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift the lightning, the light-bearing spear of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gather the clouds a second time, rainy Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already the foundations of the steadfast cosmos are shaken"},{"sp":"N","t":"by Typhon's hands, and with the four yoke-fellow elements","st":"2.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbound, Deo has refused her harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hebe has left her cup, Ares has flung away his spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes has laid down his rod, Apollo has cast off his lyre,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winged he has flown, abandoning his winged arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the form of a swan, and the cosmos has become unsown","st":"2.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Aphrodite, the goddess who brings marriage to fulfillment, wanders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the cables of indissoluble harmony are loosed; for the bride-adorning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-subduing, unsubdued, bold Eros has flown in fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving his generative bow; and abandoning his accustomed Lemnos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your fiery Hephaestus, dragging his disobedient knees —","st":"2.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"ah, how slow — keeps an unsteady course. Ah, great wonder:"},{"sp":"N","t":"I pity even your Hera, angry as she is with me."},{"sp":"N","t":"Shall your father come back again to the chorus of the stars?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Never let that be; even if I am called a Titaness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not wish to see the Titans ruling Olympus,","st":"2.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"but you and your children. Do you, with the conquering thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fight in the front for chaste Artemis; am I really guarding"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden without bride-price for a husband forced upon her?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Shall the steward of childbirth see a childbirth? Shall she stretch out"},{"sp":"N","t":"her hands to me? And what gracious Eileithyia shall I call","st":"2.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the shooter of arrows, when Eileithyia herself is in labor?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke, Sleep, folding his shadowy wing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lulled all breathing nature to rest; but the son of Cronus"},{"sp":"N","t":"alone was sleepless then; and Typhon, stretching out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"propped his sluggish back upon the burdened couch of the ground,","st":"2.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling his mother Earth; and as her bosom opened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the beds of his lair were hollowed out with a gaping chasm"},{"sp":"N","t":"for his viperish heads as they sank into the earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when the sun appeared, from his throats of common speech"},{"sp":"N","t":"many-armed Typhon roared out for battle,","st":"2.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling great Zeus; and his grim voice reached"},{"sp":"N","t":"to where the root-fixed floor of the back-flowing Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraces the rim of the cosmos, cut fourfold,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling the whole earth crown-wise with its circling band;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the Giant spoke, answering the ranks of his voice,","st":"2.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"the echo crashed in every kind, and not one voice in concert;"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he armed himself with the many-shaped form of his nature,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there sounded the howl of wolves, the roar of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the panting of boars, the bellowing of bulls, the hissing of serpents,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bold gaping of leopards, the jaw of bears arming for fight,","st":"2.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"the madness of dogs; and from the middle, in his mortal-seeming shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Giant belched out a threatening cry against Zeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hands of mine, batter the house of Zeus; shake the foundation"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the cosmos together with the blessed ones, and smash the divine bolt"},{"sp":"N","t":"of self-turning Olympus, and when the pillar of the upper air","st":"2.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"is dragged down upon the earth, let shaken Atlas flee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let him cast off the star-roaming rim of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing the spiral course no longer — for I shall not allow"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Earth, bowed with crushed shoulders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to heave up the back-whirling necessity of the upper air —","st":"2.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"but let him leave the endless burden to other gods"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fight against the blessed ones, and let him break open the rocks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting with rough missiles at the vault of stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"which he once carried; and let the weak Seasons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scourged with hills, flee terrified from heaven,","st":"2.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaids of Helios. And you, seizing them in your embrace,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingle earth with air, water with fire, sea with Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I shall bring the four winds to the necessity of slavery:"},{"sp":"N","t":"I lash Boreas, I harry Notus, I flog Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I would strike Zephyrus, and mingle night with day","st":"2.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a single hand; and let my brother Ocean, with many-fountained throat,","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing his water uplifted against Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried on high above the five parallel circles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"deluge the stars, and let the thirsty Bear wander in water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the plow-beam of the Wagon plunged beneath.","st":"2.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"My bulls, shaking the equinoctial rim of the circles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellow at the upper air, and with your scored horns"},{"sp":"N","t":"break the horns of the blazing Bull of like form;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the cattle of Selene change their watery paths,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing the heavy-thundering bellow of my heads;","st":"2.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, opening wide the vast gape of her grim jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the bear of Typhon goad to frenzy the Bear of Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let my lion, matching himself against the heavenly Lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drive him unwilling from the path of the Zodiac;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the Serpent of the Wagon shudder at my dragons, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"2.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed as he is with a few lightning-flashes. But the raging"},{"sp":"N","t":"waves of the sea, the ridges of the earth, the glens of islands"},{"sp":"N","t":"are swords for me, and the hills are shields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crags are unbreakable breastplates, rocks are spears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rivers are the quenchers of the feeblest thunderbolt.","st":"2.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"The bonds of Iapetus I keep for Poseidon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and around the peak of Caucasus let another, better, well-winged"},{"sp":"N","t":"eagle draw blood, tearing the ever-regrowing liver"},{"sp":"N","t":"of fiery Hephaestus, since for the sake of fire he suffers"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Prometheus, his self-growing liver forever scored;","st":"2.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, taking a course opposite to the sons of Iphimedeia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall hide the son of Maia, wound about with fetters no one can escape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"locked in a bronze jar, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Hermes, who loosed the bonds of Ares, was hidden away a prisoner.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And let Artemis, unloosing the untouched seal of her maidenhood,","st":"2.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"be the forced wife of Orion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Leto spread her ancient robes for Tityus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged into a violated marriage; and binding man-slaying Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stripped of his shattered shields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall carry off the lord of battle as spoil, and, in place of the killer,","st":"2.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"make him mild, and Pallas, coupled with late-wedded Ephialtes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give as a captive bride, so that I may see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares serving as a hireling and Athena in labor."},{"sp":"N","t":"And let the son of Cronus, lifting upon his weary shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"the back-whirling heaven of Atlas, lighten it","st":"2.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing upright, and let him hear the wedding-hymn of my marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding his jealousy, when I become bridegroom of Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of torches; the lightning's flash"},{"sp":"N","t":"is of itself the torch of my bridal chamber, and instead of pine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Phaethon himself, kindling fire from his own flame,","st":"2.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretch out for bridegroom Typhon his slavish gleam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the stars, flashing wedding-sparks over Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaze as the lamps of my Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stars my evening lanterns; and together with Aphrodite of the bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Selene, bedfellow of Endymion, my handmaid,","st":"2.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"spread my couch; and if there is need of bathing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bathe in the waters of starry Eridanus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, circling Seasons, after the bed of Zeus, build for Typhon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridal chamber of Eros; and you yourselves from Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leto, Athena, the Paphian, the Grace, Artemis, Hebe,","st":"2.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"carry kindred water for Typhon the bride-adorner;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with wedding plectrums beside the feast of my table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Apollo the lackey sing of Typhon instead of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I carry no desire for a foreign floor; for it is my own"},{"sp":"N","t":"brother, the star-backed heaven, that I shall drive as charioteer,","st":"2.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"having heaven, my mother's house, I the son of Earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And dragging raw-eating Cronus a second time into the light,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my kinsman and fellow-combatant, from the pit beneath the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall loose his violent bonds, and I shall bring the Titans"},{"sp":"N","t":"back home to the upper air, and I shall bring the earth-born Cyclopes","st":"2.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"to heaven to share my roof, and I shall forge other"},{"sp":"N","t":"weapons of fire; for I have need of many thunderbolts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since I fight with two hundred hands, and not with two,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly unlike the son of Cronus; and I shall hammer out"},{"sp":"N","t":"other lightnings, counterparts, stronger, later-born,","st":"2.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a greater, many-gleaming blaze, and higher up"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall build another, wider, loftier, eighth heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned with brighter stars; for this vault, seen so close,"},{"sp":"N","t":"is not able to cover the whole of Typhon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the female children and the male-born brood","st":"2.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the son of Cronus, father of many, I shall plant another far-scattered"},{"sp":"N","t":"stock of new blessed ones, many-necked; I shall not leave"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chorus of the stars useless, without marriage, but I shall join"},{"sp":"N","t":"female to male, so that the winged maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bedded with Boötes, may bring forth slave-children.\"","st":"2.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke in menace; and the son of Cronus laughed as he listened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the battle roared upon them both; and Strife was"},{"sp":"N","t":"the escort of tumult for Typhon, while Victory led Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the fray. It was no contest for a herd of cattle, nor about a flock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was the quarrel over the beauty of a bride,","st":"2.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the turmoil about some lesser city; but for the upper air itself"},{"sp":"N","t":"the strife stood, and upon the knees of Victory lay"},{"sp":"N","t":"the scepter of Zeus and his throne as the prizes of the fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus, striking a thunderous crash from the scourged clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trumpeted the bellowing of the upper air as the melody of Enyo,","st":"2.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fastening the clouds in spirals about his breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"held them as shelter from the Giant's missiles; nor was Typhon"},{"sp":"N","t":"silent; his heads of bulls, sending forth their bellowing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed as self-sounding trumpets against Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his mingled serpents hissed, the flutes of Ares.","st":"2.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Typhon armored the ranks of his sky-high limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fencing great crag with crag, until thickly"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbroken cliffs were towered up in rows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he set rock torn from its roots upon rock, one after another;"},{"sp":"N","t":"it was the image of an army arming; for, standing close,","st":"2.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"rift propped rift, ridge propped ridge, neck propped neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, high in the clouds, bend thrust against many-folded bend;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rocky hills were helmets for Typhon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his heads hidden beneath a steep-crested headland."},{"sp":"N","t":"As the Giant fought, in his many-necked shape","st":"2.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was one body, but battle-lines without end: some of arms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some the sharp-whetted spear-points of lion jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others of viperish locks that mounted the stars."},{"sp":"N","t":"And trees were brandished from Typhon's hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken against the son of Cronus, and other shoots of the earth,","st":"2.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-leaved, which with a heavy stroke"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus unwillingly turned to dust with one spark of the thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"many an elm was hurled together with its age-mate the pine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the huge plane-tree, and poplars were shot like javelins"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Zeus; and many a flank of the earth was broken open.","st":"2.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the whole four-sided rim of the cosmos was battered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the four winds, fighting beside the son of Cronus with their spears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"towered up a dark dust of the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arching the waves; and as the sea was scourged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sicily was shaken, the banks of Pelorus roared","st":"2.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sinews of Aetna, and the rocks of Lilybaeum bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophets of things to come, and the headland"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Pachynus thundered beside the western stream; and near the Bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the Thracian glen the Nymph of Athos shrieked,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and on the Pierian ridge the Macedonian forest cried out;","st":"2.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the foundations of the east were shaken, and the fragrant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tree-haired courts of Assyrian Lebanon rang."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many missiles were hurled from Typhon's hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"to match the spear-cast of the unwearied thunderbolt of Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and some, darting beside the chariot of Selene,","st":"2.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"traced unmarked tracks of the unsteady bulls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while others, whirled through the air with sharp whistling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winds fanned aside with opposing breaths;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many, straying from the untouchable thunderbolt of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the delighted hand of Poseidon received,","st":"2.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparing the earth-cleaving point of his trident;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and old Nereus, carrying the sea-drenched missiles beside the strait of Cronus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set them up as an offering to Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And arming the two grim sons of Enyalius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grandfather Zeus had Terror and Panic as his attendants,","st":"2.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"shield-bearers of the upper air who came together, and beside the lightning"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set Terror, and he fixed Panic beside the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing panic to Typhon; and Victory lifted the shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding it out before Zeus, and Enyo shouted back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ares crashed. And rushing with tempests,","st":"2.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"aegis-bearing Zeus was carried on high from the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated in the winged four-horse chariot of Time;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the horses of the son of Cronus, yoked together, were the winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he armed himself with lightnings, now with the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and at one time he assailed with thunders, at another, pouring forth","st":"2.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stone-hard backs of hail from congealing showers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with rainy missiles; and thick columns of water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharp-pointed, broke upon the Giant's heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Typhon's hands, as if by a knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were cut by the hail-laden missile from the air;","st":"2.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a hand was flung in the dust, and did not let go of its hill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, struck by the cut of the snow-thrown hail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought on even as it fell, and darting across the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"in self-rolling leaps the maddened hand throbbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though wishing still to strike the circle of Olympus.","st":"2.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the champion of the heavenly ones, shaking the fiery missile on high,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shepherding the right wing of the war from the left,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought, appearing aloft; and to the water-bearing ravines"},{"sp":"N","t":"the many-armed Giant rushed, and joining finger to finger"},{"sp":"N","t":"one after another, clamped them, interlaced, in a self-made bond,","st":"2.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"hollowing his capacious palms, in which lifting"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the midst of the wintry rivers their mountain-running water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending streams divided among his deep-hollowed hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he flung it against the lightning; and in the torrent-stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flame of the upper air, when struck, flashed through the water","st":"2.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a fiercer spark, and the thirsty water boiled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"smoking, and the wet substance was dried by the glowing mass;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the bold Giant longed to quench the fire of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fool that he was; nor did he understand that the fire-bright thunderbolts"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lightnings are born from the rain-bearing clouds.","st":"2.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"And again, seizing the straight-cut caverns of the glens,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he longed to strike the breast of Zeus, unwoundable by iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a peak was stretched out against Zeus; but with the edge of his lip"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus blew a little, and though it was a lofty precipice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his slight breath turned the circling rock aside.","st":"2.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"And, battering an island ridge with his whirling hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Typhon, spinning many times, was lifted high for battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled it down at the unbreakable face of Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he, bending his head aside, avoided the marble spear-point"},{"sp":"N","t":"coming straight at him, and Typhon met the lightning","st":"2.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it changed to a hot spiral course, and straightway the rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"white-capped at its tip, was blackened by the witness of smoke."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he sent forth a third; and as it rushed on, the son of Cronus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the middle, steering wrist of his spread palm,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[15,16]]},{"sp":"N","t":"struck it with his boundless hand as though it were a bounding ball,","st":"2.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sent it back to Typhon; and, turned about in a great"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirl of the air on its homeward course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hill of its own accord shot its own shooter."},{"sp":"N","t":"A fourth he hurled, higher still; but touching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the topmost tassels of the aegis, the rock was split in two.","st":"2.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"Another he sent forth; but the tempestuous rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-burnt, flashed when it was shot by the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"The peaks did not tear open the wet cloud, but the hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the watery clouds, were broken in pieces."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the war was shared, evenly balanced, by both,","st":"2.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"by Zeus and by Typhon; and with the loud-roaring missile"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunderbolts, the dancers of the upper air, raged in Bacchic frenzy."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the son of Cronus fought, fully armed; in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had thunder for shield, cloud served him as breastplate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he brandished the lightning for spear, and thunderbolts sped from Zeus","st":"2.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"were sent from the air, fire-pointed arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already the dry vapor of the earth, wandering"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the hollow of the ground, had run up on high, borne on the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, shut within a cloud, with its blazing throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"it choked, heating the pregnant cloud; and amid the smoke","st":"2.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fire-nourished clouds rubbed together with a crash,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flame, pressed within the inmost recess, was carried about, hard to escape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking a middle path, since to walk on high"},{"sp":"N","t":"is not lawful for the radiance; for the moist air, washed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the rainy drop, holds back the lightning as it leaps up,","st":"2.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"having thickened the wet cloud above; but as the dry part"},{"sp":"N","t":"opened from below, the fire ran through with a leap."},{"sp":"N","t":"As a stone, bringing to birth a fiery travail upon a stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoots forth a much-pressed, self-begotten stony fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the female, fire-born, is beaten against the male rock,","st":"2.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"so the heavenly flame is kindled in the pressed"},{"sp":"N","t":"soot and clouds; and from the earthly smoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since it is born thin, the winds are brought to birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another vapor, migrating from the waters of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun, milking it face to face with his fiery rays,","st":"2.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew up, dripping, in the scorching trail of the upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it, thickening, was in travail with a veil of clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, shaking its thickest body with a thinner vapor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dissolving the soft cloud back again into a pouring of rain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it went back to its earlier watery, native substance.","st":"2.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such is the fiery nature of the clouds, with which the thunderbolts too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of like form, are in travail together with the lightnings."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father fought; sending against his adversary,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hurled his accustomed fire, the spear-caster against lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the varied-voiced rank of measureless throats","st":"2.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the heavenly hurricane; and from the missile hurled by Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"one flash burned the boundless hands, one flash reduced"},{"sp":"N","t":"the countless shoulders to dust and the shifting tribes of serpents,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the spear-points of the upper air tore apart his endless heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a spiral comet reduced Typhon's locks to dust,","st":"2.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling its shaggy-haired fire with an opposing spark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the heads blazed, and as the hair caught fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"silence set its seal upon the hissing curls"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the heavenly spark, and as the serpents withered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the venom-shooting drops of their jaws were dried.","st":"2.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the Giant fought, his eyes were turned to ash"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the sooty smoke, and on his snow-beaten faces"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cheeks were whitened with drops of snow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he had the four-yoked necessity of the four winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for if he turned his unsteady eyes toward the east,","st":"2.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"he received the fiery battle of neighboring Eurus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he looked toward the ill-winded slope of the Arcadian Bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was struck by the unwarming frost of the wintry hurricane;"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the cold blast of snow-beating Boreas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was shaken by a missile both wet and smoking;","st":"2.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"and looking toward the west, face to face with grim Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shuddered at the tempestuous war of the evening,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the sound of the whip of Zephyrus in spring;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Notus, about the southern ridge of Capricorn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the rims of the air, a hot wind,","st":"2.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing scorching heat to Typhon with the fire-bright vapor of burning."},{"sp":"N","t":"If again rainy Zeus poured down a streaming shower,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Typhon bathed his whole skin in the toil-releasing drops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling his hot limbs, exhausted by the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as her son was scourged with the rocky missiles of hail-snow,","st":"2.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"his nourishing mother was lashed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing upon the Giant's skin the witnesses of Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stone-fixed missiles and the watery point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she besought the Titan Sun with downcast voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"begging for one light, hottest of all, so that with a warmer","st":"2.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"fire he might loose the stone-hardened water of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring upon snow-beaten Typhon a kindred gleam;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she melted together with him as he was scourged; and seeing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fire-crowned nation of his sky-high arms burning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she prayed for a wintry, stormy breeze to come","st":"2.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn, so that with cold winds"},{"sp":"N","t":"it might quench the thirsty necessity of Typhon."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the son of Cronus tipped the scales of the evenly matched battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And casting off with her hand her veil of trees,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mother Earth grieved, watching the smoke","st":"2.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Typhon's heads; and as the faces withered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the knees of the earth-born were loosed; and, foretelling victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trumpet of Zeus bellowed with thunderous crashes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fell, drunk with the flaming missile of heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a wound of war made without iron, Typhon","st":"2.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched on high, and casting his back upon mother Earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lay, spreading his serpent limbs in the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spewing fire. And the son of Cronus laughed and taunted him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring forth such words from a jesting throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"A fine helper old Cronus found, Typhon;","st":"2.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"Earth was hard put to bring forth a son, the great offspring of Iapetus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet is the avenger of the Titans; and as I see,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunderbolts of the son of Cronus have quickly become powerless."},{"sp":"N","t":"How long do you delay to dwell in the untrodden upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you lying scepter-bearer? The throne of Olympus awaits you;","st":"2.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive the scepter of Zeus and his robes, god-fighting Typhon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring Astraeus up to heaven; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Eurynome return to the upper air, and Ophion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cronus, traveling in company with them both; and as you go"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the many-patterned rim of the high-pathed stars,","st":"2.575","hvShift6":[[0,0],[9,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"let crafty Prometheus, escaped from his bonds, accompany you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the bold bird of heaven, the unsparing feaster"},{"sp":"N","t":"on his ever-youthful liver, as guide for the journey."},{"sp":"N","t":"What more did you want, beyond the tumult, than to see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus and the Earth-shaker as attendants of your throne?","st":"2.580"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus powerless, no longer scepter-bearer of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stripped of thunder and clouds, and instead of the lightning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of the most holy fire and the accustomed thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a torch beside Typhon's bridal chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chamber-servant of your captive wife Hera,","st":"2.585"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous of your bed, with a resentful eye;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Earth-shaker, his yoke-fellow, unyoked from the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the deep serving at your table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing your cup in his thirsty hand instead of the trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have Ares as lackey, Apollo is your servant;","st":"2.590"},{"sp":"N","t":"send the son of Maia as messenger to the Titans"},{"sp":"N","t":"to announce your power and your heavenly radiance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leave workman Hephaestus in his accustomed Lemnos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he may fashion for your newly yoked bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"a many-colored, fair-hued necklace for her neck, in gleaming array,","st":"2.595"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the bright glitter of ground-treading sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in which your consort delights, or make"},{"sp":"N","t":"another golden-shining Olympian throne, so that your golden-throned Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"may laugh, possessing a mightier seat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, keeping the earth-born Cyclopes as dwellers in Olympus,","st":"2.600"},{"sp":"N","t":"fashion a new spark of a more warlike thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Eros, who charmed your mind by guile with the hope of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bind him in gold together with golden Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamp in bronze Ares, the pilot of iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"The lightnings flee and do not abide the battle;","st":"2.605"},{"sp":"N","t":"how did you fail to escape the unwarlike fire of a little lightning-flash?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Or why, hearing with your measureless ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did you fear the small rainy echo of thunder?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who made you so feeble? Where are your spears?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where are the heads of your dogs? Where those gaping jaws of lions","st":"2.610"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the earthy bellowing of your deep-voiced throats?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where is the long-shadowed venom of your serpent hair?"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do you hiss with your circling snaky mane?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where are the bellowings of your ox-mouths? Where the missile-casting arms"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your hands, a sky-high headland?","st":"2.615"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do you lash the spiral rims of the stars?"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do the jutting tusks of your boars whiten"},{"sp":"N","t":"a chin drenched with the foam-haired spray?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where, I ask, are the dreadful grinning jaws of the raging bear?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Yield to the heavenly ones, earth-bred creature; for with one hand","st":"2.620"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have conquered the rank of your two hundred hands."},{"sp":"N","t":"But let three-headed Sicily, clasping him about with her deep-cliffed hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive the whole of Typhon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his hundred heads pitiably crowned with dust."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nevertheless, though you had an overweening mind, though you leapt","st":"2.625"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Olympus itself with fruitless hopes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall build you, all-ill-fated one, an empty tomb, and last of all"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside your empty grave, reckless one, I shall carve this:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'This is the tomb of earth-born Typhon, whom once, as he scourged"},{"sp":"N","t":"the upper air with rocks, the fire of the upper air burned up.'\"","st":"2.630"},{"sp":"N","t":"So he spoke, mocking the breathing corpse, the son of Earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And pouring forth a victory-echo for all-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cilician Taurus bellowed with a stony trumpet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spiraling Cydnus danced with watery feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi for the victory of Zeus with a liquid roar,","st":"2.635"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring forth from midway his inhabited stream to Tarsus of like age."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Earth, tearing open her rocky tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grieved, laid low, and in place of the knife of mourning"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shore off her tree-hair, cut down by the winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lopping the curls from her wooded head","st":"2.640"},{"sp":"N","t":"as in the leaf-shedding month, and she tore her cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ravines, and through the well-watered hollows"},{"sp":"N","t":"the river-tears of weeping Earth flowed loud-sounding."},{"sp":"N","t":"And from Typhon's limbs the eddies of the tempest"},{"sp":"N","t":"lash the waves, and rushing on to overwhelm","st":"2.645"},{"sp":"N","t":"the merchant ships, they ride down the calm that no wave touched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"strangers not to the surges alone; but on the land"},{"sp":"N","t":"often the stormy dust, darting about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"deluged the upright, ripening fruit of the orchard."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the steward of the cosmos, Nature of matter reborn,","st":"2.650"},{"sp":"N","t":"fixed fast the gaping hollow of the shattered earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sealed again with the bond of indissoluble harmony"},{"sp":"N","t":"the island ridges severed from their haunts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was no longer turmoil among the stars; for the sun"},{"sp":"N","t":"set the maned Lion, who had swerved from the path","st":"2.655"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Zodiac, beside the Maiden of the corn-ears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Selene, reining back once more, fixed in place"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Crab, skipping upon the face of the heavenly Lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on his course opposite to unwarming Capricorn."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Zeus the son of Cronus was not forgetful of Cadmus","st":"2.660"},{"sp":"N","t":"the minstrel; calling him, he uttered such a speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scattering the shadowy cloud of the airy darkness:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cadmus, with your pipe you have crowned the gates of Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too honor your marriage with the heavenly Lyre;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall make you son-in-law both to Ares and to Cytherea,","st":"2.665"},{"sp":"N","t":"and have the gods as feasters at your earthly banquet."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall come to your house; what dearer thing will you know"},{"sp":"N","t":"than the king of the blessed ones touching your table?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you wish to escape the changeful waves of fortune,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ferrying the course of a life in calm,","st":"2.670"},{"sp":"N","t":"always beware of provoking Dircaean Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares angered apart from his ambush; and by night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching your eye toward the heavenly Dragon opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"take the fragrant serpent-stone and sacrifice upon the altar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling upon Olympian Ophiuchus, burning in the fire","st":"2.675"},{"sp":"N","t":"the many-pointed horn of an Illyrian stag,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that you may escape whatever bitter things are ordained for your lot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which the whirling spindle of Fate's necessity has spun,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the threads of the Fates can be persuaded. But of your father"},{"sp":"N","t":"Agenor, angry as he is, let the memory go, and about your unsettled","st":"2.680"},{"sp":"N","t":"brothers do not be afraid; for, scattered apart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they all still live, since Cepheus has settled the southern land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ruler of the Cephenes of Ethiopia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thasos has come to Thasos, and about the ill-snowed ridge"},{"sp":"N","t":"of high-crested Taurus Cilix is king over the Cilicians,","st":"2.685"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phineus has come, driven forth, to the Thracian floor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"him, glorying in deep-wealthy mines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I bring as son-in-law to Oreithyia and Thracian Boreas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the oracular bridegroom of garland-loving Cleopatra."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, by the thread of Fate equal in weight to your brothers',","st":"2.690"},{"sp":"N","t":"be king of the Cadmeians and leave your name to the citizens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"renounce the back-turning circles of your wandering path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and refuse the unsettled track of the bull; for your sister,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the Cyprian's yoking ordinance, Asterion has wedded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dictaean lord of Corybantic Ida.","st":"2.695"},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things I myself shall prophesy, but the rest I shall leave"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Phoebus; and you, Cadmus, treading the axle of the earth's navel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go to the speaking vales of Delphic Pytho.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, Zeus the son of Cronus sent away the wandering"},{"sp":"N","t":"son of Agenor; and swiftly to the rim of the stars of the upper air","st":"2.700"},{"sp":"N","t":"he turned his golden chariot, and Victory, mounting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove her father's horses with a heavenly whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god came to the vault a second time; and as he came"},{"sp":"N","t":"the high-necked Seasons opened the heavenly gates"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crowned the upper air; and in their returning shape,","st":"2.705"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Zeus victorious the gods returned to Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and exchanged the winged counterfeit of their faces."},{"sp":"N","t":"And in soft tunic, without iron, Athena came to heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Ares as her Revel, and Victory was her Song;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Themis displayed the weapons of the dead Giant to witless Earth,","st":"2.710"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother of the Giants, as a terror for things to come,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hanging them high-fixed beside the gateway of Olympus."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book III"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the third, seek the much-wandering ship of Cadmus, and the halls of Electra, and the hospitality of her table."},{"sp":"N","t":"The contest was ended when winter had passed; and showing the tip","st":"3.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the light-bearing back of his blade on a cloudless baldric,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orion rose, and no longer in the circling lake"},{"sp":"N","t":"were the frosty tracks of the sunken Bull bathed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer beside the slope of the rain-bearing, thirsty Bear","st":"3.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"was the marble water walked with unwetted footsteps;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer did the Massagete, lashing his migrant house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crossing the stream in the wheeled track of his wooden wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"score the watery furrows of the frozen Ister;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already the pregnant Season, forerunner of Zephyrus,","st":"3.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the dewy breezes drunk with the splitting of flower-cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the shrill swallow, sharer of men's hearths, herald of spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chattering bird, twittering, stole away the sleep of dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"newly appeared, and the flower, naked from its fragrant covering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughed, bathed in the life-giving dews","st":"3.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"of spring. And beside the saffron-colored haunts of the Cilicians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the horned headland of high-crested Taurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus went early, when Dawn split the darkness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sailing was seasonable; and as Cadmus hastened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the moorings of the ships were heaved up from the land;","st":"3.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the high-headed mast, striking the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was fixed upright, and, gently lashing the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a light wind hummed with the breaths of morning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an escorting murmur, and with squalls arching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swells this way and that, it broke off the dolphin's watery dance,","st":"3.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tumbler of the silent calm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the plaited ropes whistled with a sharp hiss,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the wind hurried on the forestays groaned, and the straight-running"},{"sp":"N","t":"sail was bellied, forced by the pregnant breeze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unstable swell was split, falling back, and the water foamed,","st":"3.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"swollen, and as the ship hurried through the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the echo of the booming wave cried out about the keel;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the tips of the rudder, as the brine was parted in its wake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inscribed the arched, white-crested backs of the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the stormless turning-post of the tenth circling Dawn,","st":"3.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus, carried by the waveless breezes of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the Trojan passage of Helle who dwells in the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was dragged by the bellowing strait from the snatching wind"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Samos, lying opposite the battle-rousing Scamander,"},{"sp":"N","t":"neighbor of Sithonia, where still a maiden for Cadmus","st":"3.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia was being kept; and the prophetic breezes escorted the ship"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the Thracian shore of divine Rhea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the flame of the unsleeping pine-torch of Samos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sailors, near the land, furled their sails rejoicing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and ferrying the ship close to the waveless anchorage,","st":"3.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"they scored the windless water with the tips of their oars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and put in beneath the shelter of the harbor; and the pierced,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rocky claw received the cables of the unswaying ships,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[33,10],[61,48]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and through the deepening bosom of the wet sand"},{"sp":"N","t":"the crook-toothed bridles of the merchant ship were clamped fast","st":"3.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Phaethon sank; and upon the beach the sailors"},{"sp":"N","t":"spread their unspread couches on the sand"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the evening meal; and to the heavy-laden eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the men wandering Sleep brought his noiseless footstep."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when, beside the wing of purple, blazing Eurus,","st":"3.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the rift of Teucrian Ida scored at its summit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dawn, watcher of harbors, appeared, spitting out the morning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shining upon the black swell of the sea opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then Cypris, in order to yoke Harmonia to a husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched out the unnavigable back of the silent calm.","st":"3.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"Already the bird of morning had cried, cutting the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the well-helmeted ranks of the Corybants who dwell in the wilderness"},{"sp":"N","t":"beat out the Cnossian shield-brandishing leap of their dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"with measured steps; and as the loud-crashing ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"was struck in spirals by the rival iron,","st":"3.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"the double-noted flute made melody, and, urging on the dancers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed out a concordant tune with a bounding stroke."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the oaks whispered, and the rocks bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the raving woods were shaken with an intelligent shudder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Dryads cried out; and the circling bears rushed thick","st":"3.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the dance with opposing leaps,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lions with a roar from their rival throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated the ritual cry of the mystic Cabeiri,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a cry with a sane madness; and for the goddess who loves her hounds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hecate, as she was celebrated, the revel-flutes roared,","st":"3.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unyoked flutes which the horn-carving art of Cronus invented."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at the clashing din of the crash-loving Corybants"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus woke early, and, entwined together, they too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the unsilent ox-hide of dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sidonian sailors, from their beds of fine shingle,","st":"3.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"left the sea-beaten backs of their couch upon the shore."},{"sp":"N","t":"And tracking the city, Cadmus the wayfarer wandered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the ship to his companions, hurrying away; and as he went"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the house of Harmonia, Persuasion, the chamber-servant, met him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the form of a mortal, and in the burden-bearing fold of her bosom,","st":"3.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a toiling woman, having drawn a draught from the spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lifted a silver, well-rounded pitcher in her arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"messenger of things to come, since by accustomed rite"},{"sp":"N","t":"they sprinkle the bridegroom before marriage with life-giving baths."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she was near the city, where in hollow troughs","st":"3.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"women trample with much-leaping feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dirty row of tangled robes heaped one upon another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with rival feet. And from the tips of his soles"},{"sp":"N","t":"up to his head veiled in a dark cloud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Persuasion led Cadmus through the unmarked city,","st":"3.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeker of the hall of the guest-receiving king,"},{"sp":"N","t":"escort of his path at the nod of the Paphian; and there a bird,"},{"sp":"N","t":"perched beneath the soft, grey-eyed shelter of an olive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a crow, opening wide her oracular, greedy mouth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rebuked the young man, because toward Harmonia his bride","st":"3.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was going, a sluggish wayfarer, with sparing bridal foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking her wings she cried out a mocking speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cadmus is a fool, or he is a stranger to the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift Eros knows no slow bridegroom; be gracious, Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Cadmus delays while Aphrodite hurries.","st":"3.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hot Eros calls you; why, bridegroom, do you travel sluggishly?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sweet is he who was neighbor to lovely Adonis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet is he who dwells in a homeland with fields adjoining the daughters of Byblos."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have erred: you never saw the stream of Adonis, you never beheld"},{"sp":"N","t":"the land of Byblos, where the house of the Graces is, where dances","st":"3.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"Assyrian Cytherea, and not Athena who flees the marriage-bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have as escort Persuasion, the nurse of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who delights in weddings, not Artemis; cease from your labors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"enjoy Harmonia and leave Europa to the bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten, and Electra will receive you, from whose hands","st":"3.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"take aboard, yes indeed, the bridal freight of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting the merchandise of love to Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and receive the daughter of Cypris, kept for your bridal chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another Cypris; and you will praise the crow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and call me the bridal bird, the prophet of the Loves.","st":"3.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have erred; but Cypris breathed upon me; for from the Paphian"},{"sp":"N","t":"I prophesy your marriage, even if I am the bird of Athena.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, she sealed her chattering mouth with witnessing silence."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when, as he walked the public circles of the road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the all-receiving court of the king appeared to him from afar,","st":"3.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"raised high upon columns, then, stretching out to Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"her finger, the intelligent witness of an answering voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Persuasion showed him the house with her silent herald,"},{"sp":"N","t":"many-colored and gleaming; and the goddess plunged into the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changed in appearance, darting through it on winged sandal.","st":"3.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cadmus surveyed the house with wandering eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the skilled work of Hephaestus, which once for the bride Electra"},{"sp":"N","t":"the laboring craftsman of Lemnos made with Myrinaean art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing many cunning devices. Of the newly founded hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the threshold was bronze, well-wrought; and on either side of the door","st":"3.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"the posts of the richly carved gateways stretched long,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the crest was rounded with a bossed head"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the middle of the roof; and the backs of the stone-paved wall"},{"sp":"N","t":"were fixed firm, set in white gypsum,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the threshold to the inmost recess. And near the court an orchard,","st":"3.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"abundant, was weighed down with the dewy fruit of its plants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"four acres before the house; and spreading its male leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the palm pledged its desire to the female palm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pear of splendid fruit, grown together with a pear of like age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whispered at dawn, and, twining with its clusters,","st":"3.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the neighboring bush of the rich olive;"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the laurel that refuses the winds of spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"the myrtle leaves were shaken, and the fragrant breeze"},{"sp":"N","t":"fanned at dawn the hair of the fair-leaved cypress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the sweet-bearing fig and the juicy pomegranate","st":"3.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reddening fruit blossomed beside the wine-dark fruit"},{"sp":"N","t":"that grew near it, and apple flowered beside neighboring apple;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many tree-borne letters were embroidered"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the wise Phoebean leaves of the tear-loving hyacinths;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as Zephyrus breathed through the plant-nourishing garden,","st":"3.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo, unsated in his longings, stretched a restless eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, seeing the young man's plant shaken by the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembled, remembering the discus, lest even among the leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the jealous wind should once again grudge the boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if truly Apollo once, seeing him gasping in the dust,","st":"3.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"wept with eyes that never weep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the flowery pattern gave shape to the tears of Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inscribing on the hyacinth a self-bidden cry of woe."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was the well-shaded orchard; and near it a spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"with two mouths, whence the dwellers had their drink, whence the gardener","st":"3.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"channeled from a trench the crooked water, split many ways,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to one plant after another; and a stream, as though from Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hummed softly at the foot of the laurel as it made its music."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many a well-made golden youth stood, propping"},{"sp":"N","t":"his foot on a stone, stretching out before the feasters","st":"3.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"the after-dinner radiance of the evening torch;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many ranks of counterfeit hounds, in the artful silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"of their matching limbs, with the wrought gapings"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a grinning throat, stood intelligent near the doors"},{"sp":"N","t":"on this side and that, and, neighbor to a silver dog,","st":"3.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"a golden dog barked along with swelling throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fawning on a familiar man; and as Cadmus passed by"},{"sp":"N","t":"it sent forth the stranger-greeting Echo of a mimic bark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wagged the image of a wrought, affectionate tail."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Cadmus still, rolling the eyes of his well-turned face,","st":"3.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"was measuring the garden of the lords,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the carvings and the whole beauty of the painted hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the bright glitter of the stone quarried from the mines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile, leaving the assembly and the quarrels of the people,"},{"sp":"N","t":"radiant, seated upon the back of a high-crested horse,","st":"3.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"Emathion, who held Thracian Samos, the seat of Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came to the royal house of his mother Electra,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he who then ruled alone, holding his brother's"},{"sp":"N","t":"reins of sovereignty, since, leaving his ancestral soil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dardanus had settled the floor of the land opposite,","st":"3.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"marking out well-towered Dardania, the city named for him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tracing the dust of Ida with the furrow of the plow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drinking the stream of Heptaporus and the streams of Rhesus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he left his brother the inheritance to hold, and the scepter of the Cabeiri."},{"sp":"N","t":"Dardanus, brother of Emathion, whom the bed of Zeus","st":"3.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"had sown, whom Justice as nurse tended, when, taking"},{"sp":"N","t":"the scepter of Zeus and the robes of Time and the rod of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Seasons ran to the house of queen Electra,"},{"sp":"N","t":"foretelling the indissoluble sovereignty of the Ausonians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they nursed the infant, and by the unswerving oracle of Zeus","st":"3.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"the boy, springing up in the flower of ever-renewed youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"left the house of Electra, when the pouring of the third rain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"towered up in waves, deluged the foundations of the cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"For at the first roaring snowstorm Ogygus made trial of it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the upper air through the sky-high water,","st":"3.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the whole earth lay hidden, flooded over, and the peaks of the rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Thessaly were covered, and on high the Pythian summit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rock near the clouds, was awash with the snowy stream."},{"sp":"N","t":"The second rain was when the ill-snowed water hid the rim"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the circling earth beneath a raging flood,","st":"3.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Deucalion alone, traveling with Pyrrha his age-mate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while mortals perished, cutting in a hollow chest"},{"sp":"N","t":"the back-whirling flood of the unfathomable snowstorm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was ferried, a spiraling sailor, through the watery air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the third rain of Zeus was when it deluged the seat of the earth","st":"3.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hid the crags, and the unwetted backs"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Sithonian hill, even of Athos itself, were covered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"then, cleaving the flood of the high-pathed snowstorm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dardanus set foot upon neighboring ancient Ida."},{"sp":"N","t":"His brother Emathion, then ruler of the snowy land","st":"3.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Sithonia, leaving the heavy-roaring assembly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"marveled at the man's form, since the youth born in him"},{"sp":"N","t":"blended manliness and beauty in a yoked shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he marveled at such a form; for of manifest lords"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eyes are self-appointed, voiceless heralds.","st":"3.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"And taking him, he entertained him, and together with laboring Electra"},{"sp":"N","t":"he adorned the varied dishes of a rich table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fawning on the stranger with friendly, blameless speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting much before him. But he, bending his neck bowed to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew his unenchanted eyes away from the handmaids,","st":"3.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely feasted; and as the guest-loving lady"},{"sp":"N","t":"sat opposite, with a face that kept its secret,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched a modest, chaste hand to the table."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they feasted, one after another in close succession"},{"sp":"N","t":"the breathing reed of Corybantic Ida crashed;","st":"3.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the many-holed passage, with a leaping of hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dancing fingers beat out a concordant tune"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the bellowing flute, pressing out the song;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, clashing a concordant echo with rolling shakes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the booming cymbals crashed together with double bronze","st":"3.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the united reeds; and beneath the plectrum even"},{"sp":"N","t":"the upright string of the seven-toned lyre quivered."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when after the meal he had his fill of the Bistonian flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"approaching the throne of the queen who asked him, eager to learn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus, letting go the sting of his sea-wandering care,","st":"3.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"told his radiant lineage, and belched forth a row of ever-flowing words"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a fountain from his opened throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear lady, why do you ask me so much about the blood of my lineage?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I liken the generation of short-lived mortals to leaves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"some leaves the raving winds have poured down upon the earth","st":"3.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the season of autumn stands in, others at the time"},{"sp":"N","t":"of spring the tree-clad woods tend in bloom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so the brief-lived generation of mortals: one is subdued"},{"sp":"N","t":"by destruction, having ridden the course of life, another still flourishes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"only to yield to another; since, creeping back to be recaptured,","st":"3.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"time flows from grey to new, taking shape again."},{"sp":"N","t":"But I shall tell my far-famed lineage, blessed with children:"},{"sp":"N","t":"there is a city, glorious Argos, the horse-loving foundation of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the navel of the island of Tantalus' son; there a man sowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"a daughter of fair maidenhood with female-bearing seed,","st":"3.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"Inachus, a citizen of famed name in the Inachian land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a temple-warden, and the dreadful rites of the city-holding goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pondered deep, god-speaking, with mystic art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the elder-born; and Zeus, champion of gods, leader of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he refused to have as son-in-law, keeping his reverence for Hera, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"3.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the heifer of bull's nature, her face transformed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Io was driven into the country herd as a pasture-mate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hera set over the heifer a sleepless herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"many-spotted Argus, adorned with unerring eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spy upon the ox-horned wedlock of Zeus,","st":"3.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Zeus unseen, and the maiden went to pasture"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling at the eyes of the many-orbed herdsman;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Io, her body scored by the limb-devouring gadfly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inscribed the swell of the Ionian sea with wandering hoof;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came even to Egypt, my stream — which the citizens","st":"3.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"have named the Nile, a name that bears its meaning, because upon the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"from year to year, borne as a watery husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it wraps her in new silt with its muddy flood —"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came to Egypt, where, after her bovine shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changing the likeness of her divine horn,","st":"3.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"she became the fruit-bearing goddess; and when the fruit is kindled"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Egyptian Demeter, my horn-drawing Io,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vapor spirals along in company with the fragrant winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"There she bore Epaphus to Zeus, because the divine husband"},{"sp":"N","t":"had touched the unblemished bosom of the Inachian heifer","st":"3.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"with love-maddened hands; and from the god-begotten father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from Epaphus, came Libya; and traveling to the bridal chamber of Libya"},{"sp":"N","t":"Poseidon came as a migrant as far as Memphis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tracking the maiden daughter of Epaphus, and then the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the dweller of the deep as a land-going traveler,","st":"3.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore Belus, the Libyan Zeus, the plowman of my lineage."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the new oracle of Zeus Asbystes, answering"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Chaonian dove, the thirsty sands cry aloud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophetic; and in fifth place, equal in number,"},{"sp":"N","t":"father Belus sowed a further generation of children,","st":"3.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phineus and Phoenix who left his city, together with whom, flourishing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a citizen of alternating cities, wandering Agenor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unsettled life, my father, held his course"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Thebes after Memphis, to Assyria after Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wise Aegyptus, dweller in the Egyptian land,","st":"3.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"father of dreadful offspring, father of many, who with male-born seed"},{"sp":"N","t":"sowed the short-lived flocks of so many sons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Danaus who left his homeland, who armed the female race"},{"sp":"N","t":"against the male stock, stretching out the bridal sword, when the bridal chambers"},{"sp":"N","t":"were reddened with blood as the weddings were butchered,","st":"3.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with hidden swords upon the iron-bearing beds"},{"sp":"N","t":"a female Enyo put the naked male Ares to sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the deed of the evil bridal did not please Hypermnestra;"},{"sp":"N","t":"rather, thrusting aside the ordinances of her father, cruel to a son-in-law,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she entrusted her father's word to the tempest of the air,","st":"3.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kept her hand pure and bloodless of iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the marriage of the two was holy. And our sister,"},{"sp":"N","t":"just in her bloom, a bold wandering bull carried off,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if truly it was a bull; but I do not know how to believe"},{"sp":"N","t":"that oxen desire the wedlock of women.","st":"3.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Agenor sent me in company with my brothers"},{"sp":"N","t":"tracking our sister and the savage ravisher of the bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bull, counterfeit sailor of the waveless sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"for whose sake I come here, wandering without rest.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Cadmus told within the hall of fine pipes,","st":"3.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring speech from his eloquent throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"telling of the child-driving sting of his father's threat"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lying bull-traveler of the Tyrian surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the untraceable ravisher of the Sidonian bride whose fate was unknown."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Electra, hearing, cried out a comforting speech:","st":"3.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Stranger, your sister and your homeland and your father"},{"sp":"N","t":"give over to the whirl of Lethe and to forgetful silence;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for so the life of mortals is carried on, having one toil"},{"sp":"N","t":"after another, because all whom a mortal belly has borne"},{"sp":"N","t":"have been enslaved to the necessity of Fate's spindle;","st":"3.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am witness, even if I am a queen, if ever I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"was one of those Pleiades, whose mother once"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought forth female birth-pangs within her womb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seven times calling Eileithyia to her childbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lighten the sting of alternating birth —","st":"3.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am witness; for I dwell in a house far from my fathers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having neither Sterope, nor Maia my companion, nor Celaeno"},{"sp":"N","t":"my sister near me as a sharer of my hearth; nor in the bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"of my sister Taygete did I take up Lacedaemon, the rejoicing boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the twofold embrace of a child-tending arm;","st":"3.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor do I see the house of Alcyone near, nor hear"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heart-delighting tale of Merope herself as she speaks."},{"sp":"N","t":"And besides this I lament still more: for, just in his bloom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my son who left his homeland, when he had the down of his first beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dardanus, migrated to the bosom of the Idaean land,","st":"3.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave to Phrygian Simois the first-fruits of his hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the alien water of the Thymbraean river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the boundary of Libya my father still labors"},{"sp":"N","t":"with crushed shoulders, old Atlas bowed down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the seven-zoned hollow of the upper air.","st":"3.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nevertheless, having suffered so much, I feed a comforting hope"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the promises of Zeus, that together with my other sisters"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go from the Atlantean earth to the vault of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having heaven for my home, and I shall be the seventh star."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you too soothe your cares; unforeseen,","st":"3.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling upon you the whirl of life-wandering fortune,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dreadful thread of immovable Fate has set its seal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure to bear, exile, the unbending bond of necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"feeding a higher hope, forerunner of things to come,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if first-sown Io rooted your stock,","st":"3.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"if from Libya you received the Poseidonian blood of your lineage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"stay among strangers, like Dardanus, dwelling in a house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inhabiting a foreign city, as your father Agenor did,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as did Danaus, your father's brother; since another man too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"house-carrying, having the inspired stock of Io,","st":"3.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"a heavenly shoot fallen from Zeus, Byzas by name,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the seven-mouthed water of self-born Nile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"settled the neighboring land, where beside the Bosporus shore"},{"sp":"N","t":"the water is drawn that the Inachian heifer crossed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting a light for all the neighbors, when he bent aslant","st":"3.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neck of that unbending, maddened bull.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, lulling the cares of Agenor's son."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father sent forth the long-winged son of Maia"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the house of Electra as a swift messenger, so that to Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she might give Harmonia for the harmony of wedlock,","st":"3.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden migrant from heaven, whom in secret love"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Aphrodite Ares the marriage-thief had sown;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the mother, ashamed of the infant, self-announcer of a hidden bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not rear her, but from her heavenly bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought the girl, nestled in her arm, at the breast,","st":"3.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the nursing house of Electra, whose fresh childbirth the Seasons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"midwives of the childbed, had brought forth, whose thick breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still swelling, gushed forth their white moisture;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and receiving the bastard daughter by an equal ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she joined her at a single breast with Emathion, her age-mate,","st":"3.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"the newly born girl, and with a love-sharing purpose"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lifted the twofold brood in her nursing arm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as some wild lioness within the thicket, mother of twins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaggy, with the dews of her milk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fits her twin breasts to both her cubs","st":"3.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gives a teat shared between her twin children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, licking their skin and their still hairless necks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with equal care rears the brood of one age —"},{"sp":"N","t":"so then with child-tending, loving breast she nursed them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending the double pair of newborn children;","st":"3.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"often the infant son, companion to the girl of his own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning this way and that at the moisture of her rich breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she embraced in the loving hollow of her outspread hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and on her knees she seated the male child together with the female girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading her thigh wide open beside the neighboring thigh,","st":"3.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"widening the fold of her deepening tunic;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, crooning to her children a melody that charms to sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lulled them both to slumber with a nurse's art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading her arm beneath them, yoked to the children's necks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she made her own knee their bed, and on their twofold","st":"3.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"faces she waved the edge of her cloak, darting it to and fro,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling the children, and a manufactured breeze quenched"},{"sp":"N","t":"the onset of the heat, pouring an answering breath."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Cadmus was seated near the prudent queen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile, escaping the gatekeeper on his thievish foot,","st":"3.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen, untraceable, Hermes went into the house"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the likeness of a youth; and about his rosy face"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trailing sweep of his uncovered hair hung loose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"abundant, and, crowning the tips of his freshly haired cheek,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a thin circle of growing down was reddening,","st":"3.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"newly sprung, running round on either side; and like a herald"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted his accustomed rod; and with unseen face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned with clouds from head to the tips of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came upon the finished dinner of the rich table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did Emathion see him close by, nor even","st":"3.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia herself and Cadmus, sharer of the hearth, nor the chorus"},{"sp":"N","t":"of servant-men; but to god-fearing Electra alone appeared"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes of the varied speech; and taking her into the recess of the house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen, he conversed with her and spoke with a human voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My mother's sister, bedfellow of Zeus, hail, most blessed","st":"3.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"of all women hereafter, since the son of Cronus"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeps the sovereignty of the cosmos for your children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your stock shall govern all the cities of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-gifts of your love, and together with my mother Maia"},{"sp":"N","t":"may you flash among the seven-pathed stars in Olympus,","st":"3.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"running with Helios, rising together with Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am Hermes, child-loving lady, kinsman of your race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long-winged messenger of the immortals, and from heaven"},{"sp":"N","t":"your consort, the high-ruling god of strangers, has sent me"},{"sp":"N","t":"concerning your god-fearing guest; but do you too","st":"3.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"obey your son of Cronus, and send your daughter Harmonia"},{"sp":"N","t":"to go without bride-price as companion to Cadmus, her age-mate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do a favor to Zeus and the blessed ones; for the stranger"},{"sp":"N","t":"saved the immortals, all of them, in their affliction, by his singing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this man came to the aid of your husband in his toil,","st":"3.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"this man spread a day of freedom over Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let not your daughter charm you with her mother-loving lament;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but give her in wedlock to Cadmus, averter of evil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"obeying the son of Cronus and Ares and Cytherea.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book IV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Tracking the fourth, you will see, over the sea, Harmonia sailing in company with Cadmus, her age-mate."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, Hermes of the fair rod went to Olympus,","st":"4.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting on light wings, and as his sandals stretched out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his foot rowed the air, running with the winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the Thracian woman, the pilot of the Cabeiri, refuse, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","hvShift5":[[2,6],[6,8],[9,11]]},{"sp":"N","t":"but held Zeus in reverence, and to the unwed daughter of Ares","st":"4.5","hvShift5":[[0,0],[6,5],[10,6],[15,8],[34,31]]},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling her upright fingers in an intelligent beat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"called Harmonia with the artful pattern of a voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she, stretching the answering gleam of her eyelids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"saw the unsmiling circle of Electra's eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the deep, newly rushing mass of unspoken grief","st":"4.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"her silent cheeks, as heralds, foretold."},{"sp":"N","t":"The maiden leapt up and followed her mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the high-built house; and the mother, unfolding"},{"sp":"N","t":"the many-sealed bolt of the seven-chambered room,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crossed the stone threshold; and with a purpose of tender love","st":"4.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"the knees of the frightened bride trembled unsteadily;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she lifted the rosy-armed hand of her daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grasping it with her snowy hand; and you would quickly have said"},{"sp":"N","t":"you saw white-armed Hera holding Hebe by the hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when, treading the floor in purple sandals,","st":"4.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"she had entered the innermost circles of the gleaming hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of Atlas seated the grieving maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"on a well-made throne; and in turn she herself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sitting in a row upon a silver-shining seat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"declared the message of the son of Cronus to the disobedient girl,","st":"4.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taught her everything that in mortal-seeming shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changed in appearance like a youth, the Olympian herald had said."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the maiden, hearing of a much-wandering wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an unsettled husband, a migrant beneath her roof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"refused to have a stranger, and all that from Zeus the father","st":"4.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"cattle-driving Hermes, host of Cadmus, had said;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she wished rather for a husband of her own city, so that she might escape"},{"sp":"N","t":"the house-carrying yoke of a wedlock without gifts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clasping in her palm the downcast hand of her nurse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping with tears, she broke into a speech of many reproaches:","st":"4.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My mother, what has befallen you, that you have disowned your daughter?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you thus join your daughter to a newly arrived man?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What gift will the sailor ever put into my hands?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Will he give me the stern-cables of his ship as bride-gifts?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I did not know, child-loving lady, that you were keeping your daughter,","st":"4.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden who leaves her homeland, for a wandering wedlock."},{"sp":"N","t":"Other suitors, better men, are mine, citizens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what need was there to have without bride-price some naked husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a foreigner wandering about, fleeing his father?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, you will say, he came to the aid of your son of Cronus, your husband;","st":"4.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"how did he not receive an Olympian prize from Zeus, if indeed"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fought in the front for Olympus, as you say, and why did Hera, bedfellow of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not join the maiden Hebe to the helper of Zeus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Your husband, high-ruling Zeus, has no need of Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"may the son of Cronus be gracious; divine Hermes lied","st":"4.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"about Zeus the father; and I do not know how to believe"},{"sp":"N","t":"that he left furious Ares, the pilot of tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and called a mortal man as fellow-combatant in his contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he who rules the cosmos and the upper air. Ah, great wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so many Titans he locked in the pit,","st":"4.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had need of Cadmus, in order to destroy one alone."},{"sp":"N","t":"You know the twin, kindred bed of my fathers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus my forefather had the bed of his sister Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the ordinance of the bridal chamber within the family; and both"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares and Cytherea, mounters of a single bed,","st":"4.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"the parents of Harmonia, were born of one father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending a couch of common blood — ah, the necessity of it:"},{"sp":"N","t":"sisters have a brother, and I a husband who left his homeland.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke, her mother, distressed, washed away"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rain of her mourning eye; and in division of purpose","st":"4.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"she pitied Harmonia, and yet shrank from the threat of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, binding her body tight with the mind-bewitching cestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cunning girdle, guile-devising Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and putting on her skin the love-robes of Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entered the fragrant maiden-chamber of Harmonia;","st":"4.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, changing the pattern of her heavenly face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she made her body like that of Peisinoe, a neighboring girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though she longed for Cadmus, and as if with some hidden sickness"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a faint radiance from her pale face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she drove away the handmaids; and sitting beside her alone,","st":"4.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though ashamed, she uttered a guileful speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Happy one, what a fine wanderer you have in your house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what a suitor you have, most blessed one; what a husband"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall see, full of desire, whom no other maiden has won;"},{"sp":"N","t":"truly he carries blood from an Assyrian homeland,","st":"4.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the stream of graceful Adonis is; for full of desire"},{"sp":"N","t":"is this youth, from Lebanon, where Cytherea dances."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have erred: perhaps no earthly belly bore Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he has the race of Zeus, and has lied about his stock."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence this Olympian youth comes; if ever Titanian Atlas","st":"4.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"sowed Electra, sister to Maia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then Harmonia's husband has come as her cousin, wingless Hermes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor is he sung in vain as Cadmilus; for his heavenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"form alone he has changed, and still he is called Cadmus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if he is some other god wearing a mortal-seeming shape,","st":"4.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"perhaps Emathion has entertained Phoebus in his hall."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden of whom all speak, you are more blessed than your mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"in desire, in an Olympian wedlock; ah, great wonder:"},{"sp":"N","t":"in secret wise Zeus wedded Electra,"},{"sp":"N","t":"openly Apollo himself woos Harmonia;","st":"4.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"happy she whom the far-shooter has desired; if only Apollo"},{"sp":"N","t":"would hasten to have the wedlock of Peisinoe herself;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall never refuse Phoebus, as Daphne did,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not imitate Harmonia's mind; rather, leaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"my inheritance and my house and the parents I love,","st":"4.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go with Apollo as his companion to wedlock."},{"sp":"N","t":"I remember once such a form; for following my father"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the oracular house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I saw the Pythian image, and when I saw your wanderer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I thought I was gazing here again upon the statue of Phoebus.","st":"4.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say that Phoebus has a gold-gleaming headband;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus is golden through his whole body; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"take all my slaves, without number, and in exchange for him"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall put into your hands all my gold and silver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and give you royal robes of the Tyrian sea","st":"4.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, if you wish, my father's house; if it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"take my father and my mother, take all"},{"sp":"N","t":"my handmaids, and give me only this one man as husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, why do you tremble? You shall cross the sea in spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"sailing the narrow water, and with lovely Cadmus","st":"4.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would cross the boundless Ocean in winter."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not tremble at the heavy-roaring swell of the sea, because"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aphrodite, daughter of the sea, will keep safe at sea the freight of Eros."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, you have Cadmus; do not seek the throne of Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I desire neither the glittering Erythraean stone of the Indians,","st":"4.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the all-golden plant of the Hesperides, nor does the amber"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Heliades delight me as much as one mist of night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in which this wanderer would embrace Peisinoe."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you trace your descent from Ares, from Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your mother, steward of weddings, has found you a worthy wedding.","st":"4.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"Never have I gazed upon so great a flower; for of its own accord"},{"sp":"N","t":"nature has given Cadmus the gift of springtime;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I saw a rose-fingered palm, I saw an eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping sweet honey; and on his love-breeding face"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cheeks redden like roses, and, gleaming at the tips,","st":"4.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"two-colored, the traces of his snowy feet glitter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpling in the middle, and his arms are like a lily."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall pass over his locks, so as not to provoke Phoebus"},{"sp":"N","t":"by reproaching the color of the hyacinth of Therapne."},{"sp":"N","t":"If ever, rolling the heart-delighting circle of his glance,","st":"4.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made his eyes quiver, the whole Moon shone"},{"sp":"N","t":"with glittering light, and if ever, shaking his curls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bared his neck, the star Phosphorus appeared."},{"sp":"N","t":"Of his lips I shall be silent; but his mouth, the ferry of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Persuasion, dwelling there, pours forth a honey-sweet voice,","st":"4.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Graces pervade his whole body; and the tips of his hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to judge, lest I put white milk to shame."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me, wretched, as a sharer of your hearth; and touching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the young man's right hand and handling his tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I might find comforting remedies for my hidden sickness;","st":"4.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me see his bare neck, and as he sits let me press"},{"sp":"N","t":"his finger as though against my will, and through my bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me die when only, loosening his unsparing hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he squeezes the free curve of both my breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fixing his closed lips upon my lips,","st":"4.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting me with the lightest kisses; and still embracing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the young man I shall cross even to Acheron"},{"sp":"N","t":"of my own accord, and beside much-wept Lethe I shall tell"},{"sp":"N","t":"my sweet doom even to the dead, so that I may rouse"},{"sp":"N","t":"pity together with envy in unenchanted Persephone;","st":"4.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall teach those kisses breathing of the Graces"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the women unhappy in love, as many as the lovely fire has killed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall make the corpses jealous, if beside Lethe"},{"sp":"N","t":"women still envy the Paphian after death."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall follow, if you wish, as a companion too, and I do not fear","st":"4.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering I have never learned. Pitiless one, become Cadmus'"},{"sp":"N","t":"wedded wife; let me be the chamber-servant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a handmaid to both, to Harmonia and to her husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"But again I fear you, even if you long to hide it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ever, because of the bed, I rouse in you anger and jealousy,","st":"4.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, though she is a goddess and leader of the upper air, Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"grudges the mortal bastard wives of Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was angry with Europa and she afflicted unsettled Io;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did she let goddesses go; and when the mother was angry,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares drove off pregnant Leto in her birth-pangs.","st":"4.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"If jealousy does not hold you, so that I may find a remedy for my longings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn grant me this husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yes, I beg you, and for one course of night; but if you grudge it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tear me apart with your own hand, so that I may find some rest"},{"sp":"N","t":"from so great a fire, never soothed, that night and day","st":"4.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"I tend in my inmost heart, feeding upon my mind.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and drove Harmonia, who fled the bed, with her cestus"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the voyage, having goaded the girl to obedience with desire."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, turning her mind about with a twofold purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed to have the stranger and yet wished to dwell in her homeland,","st":"4.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spoke some such words, her mind lashed by the sting:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Alas, who has changed my heart? Farewell, homeland;"},{"sp":"N","t":"rejoice, Emathion and all my house; caves of the Cabeiri,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rejoice, and Corybantic peaks; no longer do I see"},{"sp":"N","t":"the nocturnal revel-torch of my mother's Hecate.","st":"4.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"Farewell, maidenhood; I am wedded to sweet Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, do not be angry; I shall cross the swell of the grey-green sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say the sea is pitiless; I care nothing"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the raging surge; let my mother's water receive"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia and Cadmus, perishing together.","st":"4.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"I follow the young man, proclaiming the marriages of goddesses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if my husband carries me sailing toward the east,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall tell Dawn of the desire of Orion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall recall the bridal chamber of Cephalus; and if ever I come"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the misty west, Selene of Latmus, having suffered the same","st":"4.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Endymion, will herself comfort me.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the girl cried with mind-wandering cares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"uncontrollable, her mind torn by the lovely sting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the plaintive drops of her drenched face"},{"sp":"N","t":"she kissed Electra's hand and eyes and the tips of her feet","st":"4.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her head and breast, and the face of Emathion"},{"sp":"N","t":"with shame-faced lips, brother though he was,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she embraced all the handmaids; and lamenting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she kissed the wrought circles of the richly carved doors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"breathless things, and her couch and the walls of her maiden-chamber;","st":"4.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking up the dust of her father's house the girl pressed it to her."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then, holding her by the hand, beneath the witnessing escort of the gods,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Electra brought Harmonia, without bride-price, as she was owed, to Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having wiped the streaming rain from her face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And taking the daughter of Cypris, the traveler of the morning","st":"4.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"left the house with an old handmaid, the queen's gift,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a servant as escort through the city as far as the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the maiden by the shore, high above the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"following the stranger, seething with fiery necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Mene, reproaching Cypris, cried out in mockery:","st":"4.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cypris, do you arm yourself even against your own children, and has the sting"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Loves not spared even the fruit of your own birth-pangs?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Her whom you bore you did not pity, pitiless one? And what other"},{"sp":"N","t":"girl will you pity, when you drag your own race into desire?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Wander you too, dear one; child of the Paphian, tell your mother:","st":"4.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon mocks you, and Selene shames me."},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia, ill in love, leaving your homeland, leave to Mene"},{"sp":"N","t":"her bridegroom Endymion, and tend the wanderer Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure to bear an equal toil, and in your love-breeding care"},{"sp":"N","t":"remember, you too in your suffering, desire-stricken Selene.\"","st":"4.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke, Cadmus, urging his companions over the shore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosed the homeward cables of the straight-running ship"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bellied his sail with the stormless breeze of spring;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fastening the two ropes to a peg,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he escorted through the swell the timber-built chariot of the sea,","st":"4.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"balancing on either side the sheets of the ship, and like a Phoenician,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing the ancestral, accustomed art of seamanship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stayed beside the rudder; and on the stern he seated"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia herself, the untouched girl, his fellow-voyager,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing strangers aboard the ship, whom the sailors then","st":"4.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"received as paying passengers. And quietly a man sailing with them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"looking at the two, mingled his voice with wonder:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This sailor is Eros himself; for it is no cause for blame"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Aphrodite of the sea should bear a sailor as her son;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but little Eros has arrow and bow and lifts a torch,","st":"4.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned with wings; and I see"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Sidonian ship. Perhaps guileful, thievish Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"sits in the stern, carrying into Lebanon"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aphrodite sailing westward from Thrace."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious, mother of Eros, and with waveless calm","st":"4.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"send me a favoring wind upon the stormless sea, your mother.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such secret words the man sailing with them spoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a sidelong, answering glance toward Harmonia."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cadmus completed his voyage to Hellas, having in his heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sting of the Phoebean oracle, and the inspired word of Zeus,","st":"4.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"ever urging him, ran through his unerring ears."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, stretching out newer gifts to the Panhellenes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he eclipsed the life-bringing art of Danaus, beginner of evils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"water-bearing Danaus; for what more did he find for the Achaeans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if once, with bronze earth-digging mattocks,","st":"4.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"hollowing the flowing cavity of the chasm in the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ended the thirst of Argos, and to the dust-covered citizens"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave a wet foothold for their feet, water as a guest-gift,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a small stream from the flanks of the deep? But he, bringing"},{"sp":"N","t":"to all Hellas gifts endowed with voice and mind,","st":"4.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"fashioned instruments of the tongue that speak in concert, and, mixing"},{"sp":"N","t":"in rows the vowels with the consonants of a harmony grown together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he rounded out the written pattern of a silence that is never silent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having learned the ancestral rites of the divine art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a migrant of Egyptian wisdom, when Agenor,","st":"4.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"dweller in Memphis, founded hundred-gated Thebes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, milking the unspeakable milk of the most holy books,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the slanting scratches of a hand that travels backward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wrote crooked circles; and, revealing the Euian rites"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Egyptian Dionysus, the wanderer Osiris,","st":"4.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was taught the initiations of the nocturnal mystic art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cried out the magic hymn in a secret, raving voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a thin ululation; and the carved devices"},{"sp":"N","t":"graven on the deepening wall of the stone-hewn temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"he learned as a boy; and with much-considering purpose","st":"4.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"measuring the fiery rim of the numberless stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"and learning the course of Helios and the measure of the land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bending the interlaced fingers of his well-whirling hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he understood the restless circles of returning Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how with three spirals she changes her turning shape,","st":"4.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"new-shining, at the half-month, glittering with her whole face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and how, joining with and flowing away from the male fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her father the sun, she is born with a motherless gleam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing from her father a self-begotten, ever-regrowing fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was he; and swiftly, treading the cities of Achaea,","st":"4.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"he left seafaring behind; and bringing together with Harmonia"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swarm of his sea-wandering companions, now travelers on land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with horse-chariots and burden-bearing wagons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he marched to the prophetic shrine; there, reaching"},{"sp":"N","t":"Delphi, the navel-axle of never-silent Pytho,","st":"4.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"he asked for prophecy, and the intelligent Pythian axle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-sounding all around, gave oracle in a hollow voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cadmus, in vain, wanderer, you wind your much-wandering track;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you seek a bull that no bovine belly bore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you seek a bull that no mortal knows how to overtake;","st":"4.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"renounce Assyria, and as leader of your journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"tend an earthly cow; do not seek the bull of Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom of Europa no herdsman knows how to drive;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pursues no pasture, no meadow, obeys no goad,"},{"sp":"N","t":"is commanded by no whip; he knows how to lift","st":"4.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"the soft yoke-straps of Cypris and not the yoke-band of plows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretches his neck to Eros alone and not to Demeter."},{"sp":"N","t":"But letting go the longing for your Tyrian father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stay among foreigners, and of your Egyptian Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your homeland, found a city named alike, where, falling,","st":"4.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"the divine cow will lay to rest her heavy-kneed foot.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, he lulled the raving voice of the tripods,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the crags of Parnassus were shaken as they heard"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighboring Phoebean echo, and in its oracular stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"the inspired water of intelligent Castalia bubbled.","st":"4.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"The god spoke; and Cadmus withdrew and beside the temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"saw a cow, and went along with her as she walked; and following"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slow-obedient hoof of the unwandering cow, the men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his eager servants, made their journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparing and equal in measure; whence then Cadmus, traveling,","st":"4.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"saw the conspicuous holy place where the Pythian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"perceiving the nine-circled coil of the mountain serpent's spine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had put to sleep the death-bearing venom of the Cirrhaean viper."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the peaks of Parnassus, the migrant man"},{"sp":"N","t":"marched to the bordering soil of Daulis, whence I hear","st":"4.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chattering garment of silent Philomela, ill-spindled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Tereus defiled, when Hera of the yoke fled"},{"sp":"N","t":"the danceless yoke of the mountain-haunting wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the girl moaned upon the unspread couch"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a bridal chamber by the roadside, and as the tongueless girl","st":"4.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"lamented the Thracian, forced Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with mimic tears voiceless Echo groaned,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mourning the maiden Philomela who fled the bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, mingled with the crimson trail of blood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of her maidenhood gushed together with that of her fresh-cut tongue;","st":"4.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he saw the city of Tityus, where the bold son of Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striding through the fair-leaved groves of Panopeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pulled back the chaste robes of Leto as he forced her;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he set the track of his foot on the hollow of Tanagra,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from Coronea, passing over the soil of Haliartus","st":"4.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the city of the Thespians and deep-glened Plataea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came near the Aonian plain, traveling through Boeotia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where once Orion, ill-loving son of Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Scorpion, helper of the loveless archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid low, great as he was, as he was still lifting","st":"4.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lowest hem of the tunic of the unwedded goddess —"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slow creeper, an earthly monster, which, reaching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the soles of its adversary, scored them with its hail-cold sting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he set foot on the land of Chaeronea, where, cutting"},{"sp":"N","t":"the silvery dust, the hoof of the cow was whitened,","st":"4.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, following the many-bending circles of her rocky course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shook off the white defilement of her dusty feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the oracular hoof of the cow sank down to lie on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"forerunner of the city to come. But when for Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pythian oracle of the underground echo was fulfilled,","st":"4.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting the sacred cow beside a fragrant altar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sought a flow of spring-water, in order to cleanse"},{"sp":"N","t":"his prophecy-serving hands, and pour over the sacrifice"},{"sp":"N","t":"pure water; for not yet in the vine-planted orchards"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the soft fruit of the growing harvest appear.","st":"4.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he set his feet beside serpent-feeding Dirce;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stood astonished, where, appearing aslant in snaky bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shifting-backed serpent of Ares girdled the spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified the host, all the many that followed Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"one it bit beneath the breast with its grey-gleaming jaws,","st":"4.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"another it reached and scored with its blood-red tooth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tearing the life-saving liver of another as he fought"},{"sp":"N","t":"it made him a corpse; and his crumbling hair flowed down his neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"of its own accord, slipping from his sodden head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swift, wrapping about, creeping up his shins,","st":"4.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"the serpent girdled Cadmus in a threatening bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, raising its body with the mounting stroke of its limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it leapt at the round boss of his bull's-hide shield;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the man, his feet girdled in crooked coils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"labored, heavy-bound in the trailing viperish chain,","st":"4.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a frightful burden, and the bearer, weighed down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing upright, it dragged down, pulling him to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosed its bitter mouth, and as the cruel creature gaped,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the murderous gateway of its raw-devouring throat widened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it bent its head aslant, and as the head was shaken","st":"4.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neck, stretched high, arched in the middle and writhed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another it terrified, leaping over the rim of a human"},{"sp":"N","t":"temple, and across the chin of another it ran"},{"sp":"N","t":"uncontrollable, and striking the eye with its venom-shooting dew"},{"sp":"N","t":"it dimmed the marble gleam of the closed eye;","st":"4.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foot of another it seized, and held it, scored by its jaw,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its bite, and belched the green foam of its teeth"},{"sp":"N","t":"onto the body of the youth, and with livid, iron-like"},{"sp":"N","t":"pallor the body was chilled by the green venom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"as another panted beneath the blows of its jaws,","st":"4.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unsteady membranes of his head swelled"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the poisonous bite, and as the brain poured out"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ichor ran rushing down from his dripping nostril."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when Cadmus grew weary, then Athena came near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking the aegis, forerunner of the victory to come,","st":"4.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"tressed with the viperish Gorgon head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to him in his dismay the host-rousing goddess cried:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cadmus, fellow-combatant in the tumult of Giant-slaying Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you fear at the sight of one serpent alone? In the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trusting in you, the son of Cronus laid Typhon low,","st":"4.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"tressed with so many serpent heads."},{"sp":"N","t":"Cease trembling at the hiss of the beast's teeth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pallas urges you on, and beside murderous Dirce"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze Ares will not rescue his creeping guardian."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, taking the frightful teeth of the slain","st":"4.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"beast, sowing the earth with the viperish fruit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reap the snaky crop of the Giants' battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bring the battle-lines of the earth-born together in a single destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving five alive; and for Thebes to come"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the fair-fruited generation of the Sown Men spring up.\"","st":"4.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, Athena heartened astonished Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and inscribed the deep air with windy sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entering the house of Zeus. And he, beside a firm clod,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood lifting a rocky missile, the marble, well-rounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"boundary-stone of a broad-floored field, and with the straight-flying","st":"4.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"stone he crushed the tip of the serpent's head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drawing his sharpened knife from beside his thigh"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut the beast's neck; and the severed head"},{"sp":"N","t":"remained apart from the body, and, rolling in the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the restless tail wound its accustomed circle, unwinding again,","st":"4.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the serpent lay stretched a corpse on the ground. And over the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Ares, heavy in wrath, cried out; and as he raged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus, with his limbs changing into a spiraling shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was destined, transformed, beside the feet of the Illyrian land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to have the strange likeness of a serpent's face.","st":"4.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"But those things were fated for a later time. Meanwhile, in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his bronze helmet he gathered the fruit of destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grim harvest of the beast's jaws; and dragging"},{"sp":"N","t":"the humped plow of native Pallas from the meadow to the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and plowing the war-breeding furrow of the grey-green earth,","st":"4.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sowed a many-rowed swath of venom-shooting teeth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the self-delivered crop of Giants grew up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one ran up high-headed, stretching the tips"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his well-corseleted breast, another with head leaping forth"},{"sp":"N","t":"held his dreadful shoulder above the opening earth;","st":"4.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"another stooped upward as far as the navel, and one upon the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose half-finished, lifting his earth-bred weapon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, stretching his jutting crest that peered above,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed no breast as yet, and still creeping up from his mother's"},{"sp":"N","t":"flanks little by little he fought against fearless Cadmus,","st":"4.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed with self-grown weapons; ah, great wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eileithyia armed one whom no mother had delivered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one hurled a kindred spear, handling it"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-seen, while another, light, dragging his whole body into the light,"},{"sp":"N","t":"left the unfinished tips of his feet fixed in the earth.","st":"4.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus did not forget the command of Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but reaped the straw of the regrowing Giants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"one he struck above the breast with his windy lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another he smote down the collarbone beside his broad neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"and split the bones of his shaggy throat;","st":"4.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"another he struck with a hurled stone and scored him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"visible as far as the belly; and as the blood of the dread Giants"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured out like a river, Ares slipped in the gore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening his limbs, and as she stood by in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tunic of Victory was reddened with purple drops.","st":"4.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"Another as he fought he struck with his sword beside the hip"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cut through the back of the ox-hide grown to him, along with the loins."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was slaughter unspeakable; as the Giants were butchered"},{"sp":"N","t":"a deadly pipe of bloody dew gushed up"},{"sp":"N","t":"from those smitten with the sword. And he, by the intelligent counsel of Pallas,","st":"4.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"poised a stone above the heads of the earth-born;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they, drunk with a blood-red longing for Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raged in Bacchic frenzy for Ares, and with kindred iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"were buried in the dust, destroyers of one another."},{"sp":"N","t":"Each strove against another; and with reddening gore","st":"4.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spotted back of the ox-hide was blackened as it was drenched"},{"sp":"N","t":"when an earth-born was slain; and by the brother-slaying point"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the underground knife the fruit of the field was butchered."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book V"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Look still at the fifth and you will see Actaeon, whom no doe bore, the wandering fawn torn by hounds."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when, reaping the snaky crop of war,","st":"5.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus had mowed the straw of the tooth-grown Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring gore to Ares as the first-fruits of battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having cleansed his limbs beside serpent-feeding Dirce,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sacrificed the Delphic cow upon god-built altars,","st":"5.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fair offering to Pallas. And as he began the sacrifice"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twin horns on this side and that were sprinkled about"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the barley-groats; and drawing the naked sword beside his neighboring thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blade hanging from an Assyrian baldric,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut the topmost hair of the long-muzzled head","st":"5.10","hvShift6":[[0,0],[39,37],[41,41]]},{"sp":"N","t":"with the hilted sword; and Theoclymenus, grasping"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horn of the calf, pulled back its neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thyestes crushed the sinews of the neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a double-edged ox-axe, and with the bloody trail of the cow"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stone altar of Oncaean Athena was reddened,","st":"5.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the horned bovine forehead was battered"},{"sp":"N","t":"the calf fell headlong; and as it was butchered with iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"they cut through the ribs and carved them with the knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stripped off the rough covering of ox-hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spread out full length; and rolling his bright cloak on the ground,","st":"5.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"the king himself labored, and cutting the raw flesh"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the well-grown thighs he wrapped it in a double layer of fat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carving it little by little, and stretching upon the embers"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long entrails, pierced with iron spits,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew them off, roasting them in a gentle fire; and, fixed at the middle,","st":"5.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing them in rows pierced through with sharp-pointed bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set them upon the flower-decked, low-set table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the carver, lifting the seething spits one after another."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fragrant vapor of Assyrian frankincense"},{"sp":"N","t":"spiraled wandering through the air. And when the sacrifice was completed","st":"5.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a feast, and Cadmus, taking it, distributed to each,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding out an equal-weighted portion of the divided food."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ranks of the feasters at the well-rounded table"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid aside their desire for the banquet with satisfied heart."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was there an end of toils for serpent-slaying Cadmus,","st":"5.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"but after the creeping thing, after the wild tribes of Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fought with the champions of the Ectenes and the Aonian people,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowing a barbarian crop of Ares, and he assailed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighboring Temmices; and when he called for warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a varied swarm of allies from the neighborhood came.","st":"5.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"And upon the twin armies Strife joined with Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in travail with battle; and as they came together in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bow was bent in a circle, the spear was brandished, the helmet shaken,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the missile whistled, and upon the bossed circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ox-hide shield crashed when struck by a millstone.","st":"5.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the blood of the slain flowed; many a man upon the pasturing earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-dead, head foremost, was rolled in the dust."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the host of adversaries bowed down as suppliants to Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the contest was ended. And after the murderous whirl of tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus fixed the foundations of untowered Thebes.","st":"5.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"Many furrows on this side and that were cut"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the hollows were divided, and the seats of the many-branching"},{"sp":"N","t":"roads were scored by the jagged-toothed iron of the oxen;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many streets, apportioned in the fourfold arrangement"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the opposing winds, were measured out among the grass.","st":"5.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Aonian city was embroidered with the stony beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Tyrian art; and one after another labored,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the workman, cutting with earth-cleaving point the stone of varied color"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the Boeotian slope, the stone which beside the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"of oak-clad Teumessus the hills brought forth,","st":"5.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"which Helicon sprouted and which Cithaeron bore in travail."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he completed temples of the gods and houses of men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rounding them with rules; and upon unbreakable foundations,"},{"sp":"N","t":"marking out the city with a circuit of seven gates,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he imitated the seven-zoned heaven by his art,","st":"5.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the wall to come for the inhabitants to Amphion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept in reserve for his tower-building lyre. And to the heavenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"wanderers, equal in number, he dedicated seven gates"},{"sp":"N","t":"of matching form; first, fixing it toward the western slope,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he assigned the Oncaean gate to grey-eyed Mene,","st":"5.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"named from the bellowing of the cow, because she too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene of bull's nature, horned, driver of cattle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a triple form, is Tritonian Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the second he gave to gleaming Hermes, neighbor of Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a gateway as his prize; and marking out the fourth","st":"5.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Electra, named for Phaethon, because when he appears"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gleam of dawn glitters with the same color as electrum,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he dedicated to the fiery sun the middle gate"},{"sp":"N","t":"facing Dawn, since he is the middle of the planets;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fifth he gave to Ares, he granted the third to Aphrodite,","st":"5.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"on either side of both, so that Phaethon might be in the middle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"parting furious Ares from his neighbor Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sixth he made an image of Zeus in a more shining arrangement,"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-visible; and the last, seventh, star of Cronus took the last."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a seat he made; and having founded the holy city","st":"5.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"he called it by the same name as the city of Egyptian Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having fashioned a varied earthly pattern, equal to Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the daughters of the Aonians struck up in dances"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wedding-hymn of Harmonia; and in the fair-chambered hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dancers voiced the name of the Thracian bride.","st":"5.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Paphian adorned the new-built bridal chamber for Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing the god-summoned wedding of her own daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lovely mother; and the father, in joy for his daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"danced naked without shields, gentle Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning his ironless right hand upon Aphrodite,","st":"5.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the wedding trumpet he made melody of the Loves' spirit","hvShift6":[[0,0],[37,48],[39,55],[40,57]]},{"sp":"N","t":"in answer to the pipe, and from his iron-bearing head"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shook off the accustomed crests of his war-loving mane,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling his locks with bloodless clusters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weaving a revel for Eros; and dancing with the immortals","st":"5.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ismenian Apollo came to the wedding of Harmonia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking a love-song on his seven-toned lyre;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the nine Muses struck up a life-saving melody,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Polymnia, midwife of the dance, made her palms quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and traced the mimic image of a voiceless speech,","st":"5.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"uttering with her palms a wise pattern in intelligent silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling her eyes; and on much-turning sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to Zeus, Victory stood as chamber-servant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi for Cadmus, champion of Zeus, and about the bridal bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with maiden lips she wove a wedding-song,","st":"5.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wound the track of her foot, and in the well-circling dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shamefaced, she shook her wings beside the wings of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And from the wide-scattered torches of a light of one radiance"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose the glow of a lying evening Dawn."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with shrill mouths beside the leap-loving bridal bed","st":"5.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"an all-night revel of unsleeping dance went on"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the singers; for Hermes, hastening to the sleepless wedding, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","hvShift5":[[0,0],[20,28]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he left his accustomed rod, since it was the steward of sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Thebes was an Olympian chorus; and one could see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus and Zeus together touching a single table.","st":"5.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"And bringing to the bridal chamber the bride-adorning hour,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dragon rose, companion of the Arctic Wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"messenger of things to come, that, pasture-mate with his age-mate bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from a mortal shape he was to have a serpent's form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom of Harmonia. And of the blessed ones one after another","st":"5.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave a prize to Cadmus as he hastened to the bridal chamber:"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus gave all things in perfection; and honoring his sister"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera, of whom all speak, since she was mother to Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-haired horse-god gave gifts of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes gave a scepter, Ares a spear, Apollo a bow,","st":"5.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a crown luxuriant with stones in an array of varied colors"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hephaestus set upon Harmonia's head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hanging a golden headband upon her temples;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and golden-throned Hera gave a throne of fair stone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorifying Ares, much-devising Aphrodite","st":"5.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"fitted to the reddening neck of the girl a white golden necklace"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the many-wrought gleam of stones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the skilled work of Hephaestus, which he had made for the Cyprus-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that it might be a gift at the first sight of archer Eros;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the heavy-kneed husband ever hoped that Cytherea","st":"5.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"would bear a limping son, an image of his father's feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he thought in vain, and perceiving a son sound of foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shining with wings, like the son of Maia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made a many-colored necklace, which on its star-gleaming back"},{"sp":"N","t":"was like a serpent with a spiraling body; for just as","st":"5.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-mouthed amphisbaena herself winds in a middle coil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting venom from either head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"writhing about with the unequal-yoked stroke of her limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and creeping head to head joins them together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about her curved back in a slanting course —","st":"5.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that many-colored necklace, stretching its bent back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was curved, having a twin neck arched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"abundant in scales, reaching as far as the navel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a plaited two-headed serpent; and beneath the whirl of art"},{"sp":"N","t":"the golden circle of its trailing spine writhed,","st":"5.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and its head, spiraling with a many-whirling stroke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched a counterfeit hiss from its throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And on either side of the mouths, where end and beginning are,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a golden eagle, as though cutting the broad air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright between the twin viperish heads,","st":"5.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-visible in the four-yoked mask of four wings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"on one wing yellow jasper ran, on another it held"},{"sp":"N","t":"the all-white stone of Selene, which, as the fair-horned goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"wanes, diminishes, and grows when Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"newly shining, gleaming forth the moist light of her horn,","st":"5.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"milks the self-begotten fire of her father the sun;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another wing held the light-bearing pearl, by grace of whose radiance","hvShift6":[[0,0],[13,30],[14,35]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the grey-green swell of the Erythraean sea sparkles,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,2],[7,3],[23,14]]},{"sp":"N","t":"shining; and in the middle of another, in blazing array,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a thin-shining Indian agate spat forth a moist radiance.","st":"5.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the heads were joined to one another on either side"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two gapings of the serpent's heads widened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"enclosing the eagle with both jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"interlocked on this side and that; and through the well-lit face"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lychnis-stones shot in the eyes a native gleam","st":"5.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a sharp radiance, like a blazing lamp"},{"sp":"N","t":"when it is lit; and, luxuriant with the many-formed shape of stones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a sea, and a green stone of grey-green emerald,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving a yoke-fellow crystal like foam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held the white-crested image of the darkening brine;","st":"5.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"in it all cunning devices were wrought, in it all"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea-nourished flocks of the deep glittered gold-shining,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though skipping about; and many a wet traveler,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dolphin, danced half-seen, grazing the sea —"},{"sp":"N","t":"and writhed its counterfeit, self-moving tail —","st":"5.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a chorus of birds of varied colors, of whose flying wings"},{"sp":"N","t":"you might quickly have said you heard the windy beat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Cytherea gave the necklace as a prize to the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"golden, of fair stones, hanging at the bride's neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"And yoked by the cestus, the steersman of bridal longings,","st":"5.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia brought forth in her womb a brood of many children"},{"sp":"N","t":"born little by little; and in alternating childbirth"},{"sp":"N","t":"she released the pregnant burden of daughter-bearing labor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having completed four times nine circles of Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"First Autonoe leapt up from the fertile womb","st":"5.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her mother, unfolding the nine-month childbirth"},{"sp":"N","t":"with first-born pangs; and in a birth of the same blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-natured Ino grew, the wife of Athamas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mother twice delivered; and third arose Agave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, wedded in a Giant's marriage,","st":"5.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore a son like her tooth-grown husband;","hvShift5":[[0,0],[19,19]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and, an image of the Graces in her desire-stricken face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Semele, kept for Zeus, sprouted fourth"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the daughters, and to her alone, though she was the youngest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nature gave the elder's rights of unconquered beauty.","st":"5.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a late-fulfilled male, yoke-fellow to the female stock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia bore, a new son, to rejoicing Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Polydorus, the morning star of the Aonian homeland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"younger than rosy Semele, whom beside Thebes"},{"sp":"N","t":"lawless king Pentheus, having taken the scepter, deprived him of it.","st":"5.210","hvShift5":[[0,0],[58,62]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things old Time was to fulfill thus, late."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cadmus locked his chosen daughters to husbands"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the four-yoked bridal chamber of alternating marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fitted one bed after another; for, bearing gifts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first Aristaeus, Nomios, and by name Agreus,","st":"5.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of wise Phoebus and of skilled-handed Cyrene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wedded Autonoe by the ordinance of the yoked plowing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did Agenor's son refuse to have as son-in-law one skilled"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the much-nourishing art, the life-saving son of Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but to him who had lulled with the life-sustaining breezes","st":"5.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the winds fallen from Zeus the deadly star of fire-eyed Maera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he locked his child to the husband of surpassing mind."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the marriage was rich in blessings, since as a prize to the unwed girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave cattle, he granted goats, he bestowed a mountain-reared flock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a furrow, weighed down by the necessity of the burden-bearer,","st":"5.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightened the load of the amphora of olive-oil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-gifts of the wedding, and he brought much of the cunning"},{"sp":"N","t":"travail of the wise bee, of its many-celled childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"That man first, running the mountains with the leap of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"found the toil of the deer-driving hunt that loves the crags,","st":"5.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"how with intelligent nostril beside the feet of the pasture-woodland"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hound divines the scent of the unmarked beast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching upright its ears toward the slanting-pathed course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he learned the many-plaited nets of the guileful art"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the upright pattern of the stakes, and the early track of beasts","st":"5.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored over the sand on the untrodden ground, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and teaching the hunter-man to wear boots on his feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he darted uncontrollable into the hound-driving course of the hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to carry his robes only as far as the shining knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ever the onrush of the hurrying hunter's foot","st":"5.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"be reined back by a tunic let down."},{"sp":"N","t":"That man devised the row of many-celled hives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set in place the works of the wandering bee of the wilderness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which within the meadow passes from flower to flower"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the fair-fruited plant, and, flying upon the clusters,","st":"5.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"milks the tip of the dew with the tips of her lips;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and binding his limbs tight in the linen loops"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a many-plaited tunic from his nails to his hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the guileful, choking, artful smoke of fire","hvShift5":[[0,10],[5,32],[17,34],[28,35],[38,44],[44,51]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he soothed her as she did harm, and, shaking beneath the wind","st":"5.250","hvShift5":[[0,32],[3,36],[5,41],[6,50],[27,58]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the torch that threatens the swarm-loving bee,","hvShift5":[[0,26],[27,31],[41,38],[48,44]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted the double bronze, and in the childbed beneath the roof,","hvShift5":[[1,0],[20,6],[24,9],[31,29],[38,33],[41,48],[44,52],[45,65]]},{"sp":"N","t":"as the unsilent tumult harried them with humming,","hvShift5":[[0,4],[3,11],[4,27],[7,28],[13,35],[33,37],[54,38],[58,41],[65,48]]},{"sp":"N","t":"striking a twin din with much-rattling hand,","hvShift5":[[0,9],[2,10],[6,15],[8,18],[35,19],[41,28],[45,35],[48,43]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he guarded against the dreadful stings of the bee as she armed herself,","st":"5.255","hvShift5":[[0,16],[2,24],[5,34],[8,49],[10,52],[15,56],[16,61],[38,62],[43,70]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting off first the many-angled covering of wax,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drained the varied gifts of the honey-dripping birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"He first found the dew of fine-dripping oil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, cutting the fruit with the heavy millstone-like rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pressed the rich offspring of the moisture-bearing olive.","st":"5.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"And beneath the many-treed slope of the shady, well-pastured wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leading them to marsh, to meadow, he taught the herdsmen"},{"sp":"N","t":"to pasture from the shining of the sun until evening."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the untraceable, disobedient row of sheep wandering"},{"sp":"N","t":"on straying hoof, following a slow path in the rear,","st":"5.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brought together into a single road toward the flowery pasture,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking a goat as leader of the emulous journey."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he devised the pastoral mountain song of Pan."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he lulled the star of Maera, blazing with fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, kindling the fragrant altar of Zeus Icmaeus,","st":"5.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"he poured over the bull's blood a sweet libation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the varied gifts on the altar of the roaming bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling delicate cups with the mixed drink of honey-mead;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zeus the father heard, and honoring the son of his son"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent the counter-blowing breeze of the evil-averting winds,","st":"5.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding back the smoky fever of Sirius."},{"sp":"N","t":"Even now, heralds of the sacrifice of Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Etesian breezes from Zeus cool the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the wine-harvest with its varied clusters is growing."},{"sp":"N","t":"Him Eros escorted to the Aonian wedding,","st":"5.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Cean son of Phoebus; and with the sacrifice of slaughtered oxen"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole city was garlanded, and the straight-cut streets"},{"sp":"N","t":"were busy with dancing, and beside the gateway of the bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"the citizens moved their rolling-footed wedding-dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the women struck up a song of lovely voice,","st":"5.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Aonian flutes clashed in company with the bridal pipe."},{"sp":"N","t":"Thence from the bed of Aristaeus and Autonoe"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon arose; and with crag-loving purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the blood of Agreus, he took as his pattern the ancestral hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mountain-running servant of Artemis — and it was no cause for blame","st":"5.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"that ill-fated Actaeon should learn the cares of the chase,"},{"sp":"N","t":"born the grandson of lion-slaying Cyrene —;"},{"sp":"N","t":"never did a mountain bear escape him, nor did the deadly"},{"sp":"N","t":"eye of the lioness herself in childbed terrify him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often, watching the leopard leaping high in flight,","st":"5.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"he laid it low; and always from high above the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pan the sheep-herder watched him with astonished eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he outran the course of the swift deer."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the running of his feet did not help him, nor did the quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"suffice, nor the straight aim of his arrows, nor the guile of the hunt;","st":"5.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Fate destroyed him, a wandering fawn torn by hounds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still breathing of the tumult after the Indian strife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, seated high upon a long-trunked oak,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he saw the whole body of the archer-goddess bathing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, an unsated gazer upon the goddess not to be gazed upon,","st":"5.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"he measured the chaste body of the unwedded maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"from near at hand; and him, watching with secret eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"the form of the naked queen, with sidelong eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Naiad Nymph, unveiled, saw from afar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, terrified, she shrieked, and announced to her queen","st":"5.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"the savage boldness of the love-maddened man; and half-visible"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, snatching up with her garment her circling headband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered her chaste breasts with her maiden girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, plunging with her wet limbs into the streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden in shame little by little hid her whole body.","st":"5.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon, heavy-fated, at once your human form"},{"sp":"N","t":"left you, and the hoof of four feet was cloven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and long cheeks were stretched out at the jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shins were thinned, and two curved horns on the forehead"},{"sp":"N","t":"grew, the long clusters of far-branching antler,","st":"5.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with spotted limbs the bastard shape was embroidered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had a shaggy body; but to the storm-swift fawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mind alone remained still steadfast; and with the swift-passing"},{"sp":"N","t":"leap of his hoof he ran through the inhospitable mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hunter trembling at hunters. And, changed in nature,","st":"5.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hounds no longer knew their former master; for at the unswerving"},{"sp":"N","t":"nods of the grieving, heavy-hearted archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"maddened with the stinging breath of roaming madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they scored the yoked swath of their fawn-slaying teeth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, misled by the lying appearance of a deer,","st":"5.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"they feasted on the spotted counterfeit body in witless madness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess devised something else: that the hounds with slow jaws"},{"sp":"N","t":"should tear little by little the still-breathing Actaeon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"endowed with an intelligent spirit, so that she might rend his heart the more"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sharper pains; and with a mortal's purpose,","st":"5.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"moaning his own doom, he roared with a plaintive voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Blessed Teiresias, for you saw without destruction"},{"sp":"N","t":"the naked form of Athena, who refused but was merciful;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you did not die, you did not get a deer's body, nor upon your"},{"sp":"N","t":"forehead were jutting antlers hung;","st":"5.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"you live, having lost the light of your eyelids; but the sparkle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your eyes Athena transferred to your mind;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the archer-goddess is angrier by far than the Tritogeneia."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that she had given me a like pain, would that she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"had assailed my eyes as Athena did,","st":"5.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"would that she had changed my mind, as she changed my body; for a strange-natured"},{"sp":"N","t":"beast's shape holds me, and I keep growing a man's character."},{"sp":"N","t":"When do beasts ever groan over their own destruction?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Witless, they live and do not know of their end."},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone keep a prudent mind; and as I perish","st":"5.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"I pour intelligent tears from a beast's brows."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be more savage now, hounds; never so much"},{"sp":"N","t":"did you rush upon lions with raging leap."},{"sp":"N","t":"Cry woe for Actaeon, dear hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yes, I beg you, and beasts likewise; tell Autonoe,","st":"5.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cithaeron, what you saw, and to my father Aristaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaim my end with rocky tears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maddened, unsparing hounds. Alas for necessity:"},{"sp":"N","t":"with my own hands I myself reared my murderers."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that a mountain-running lion had subdued me, would that a shifting-backed","st":"5.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"leopard, dragging me, had torn me open, would that raging bears"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bitter, unsparing claws fixed on either side"},{"sp":"N","t":"had butchered my fawn-seeming self with their grey-gleaming jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and that hounds of my own household had not subdued me; no longer my shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do they know my voice, its echo changed.\"","st":"5.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"Half-dead he said these things, and with a beast's mourning voice"},{"sp":"N","t":"he besought the ravening hound that did not hear his prayers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sent forth intelligent words, but instead of a human"},{"sp":"N","t":"voice the murmur of an unmarked echo sounded."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already self-fulfilling mountain Rumor was flying,","st":"5.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying to Autonoe the hound-torn necessity of her son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not how he had put on the shaggy shape of a deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but only that he was dead. And with the purpose of tender love"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother, unshod, unveiled, was lashed by grief;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she tore her locks, and rent her whole tunic,","st":"5.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with mourning nails she scored her cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening them with blood, and over her bare breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"she reddened the life-bringing curve of her child-nourishing breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembering the birth; and with the ever-flowing tears"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her grief-loving face the lady bathed her robes.","st":"5.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the hounds of Actaeon, coming from the crag,"},{"sp":"N","t":"confirmed the tale of ill tidings; for with silent tears"},{"sp":"N","t":"they prophesied the young man's end."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing them mourning the mother groaned far more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and old Cadmus shore off his grey locks,","st":"5.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Harmonia shrieked; and of the grief-loving women"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mingled, heavy-thundering echo roared through the whole house."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Autonoe, going in company with her husband Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went seeking the much-scattered remains of the corpse;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw and did not know her own son, she beheld the shape","st":"5.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a dappled deer and did not see a man's face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often she passed by the bones of the unrecognized fawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying on the ground and did not know them; for of her perished"},{"sp":"N","t":"son she expected to see a mortal-seeming shape."},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not blame ill-fated Autonoe; for she looked upon","st":"5.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"the strange-natured remains of her son, and of the unrecognizable face"},{"sp":"N","t":"she perceived the jaws and did not see the circle of the eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she touched the antlers and did not know her son's temple;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she found thin feet and did not recognize the soles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw thin feet and did not see the circles of sandals.","st":"5.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not blame ill-fated Autonoe; for of the departed"},{"sp":"N","t":"she did not see mortal eyes, she did not see the likeness"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a human shape, and she did not perceive a chin"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored with the purple flower of first down."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with roaming feet passing over the ridge of the wood","st":"5.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"she trod the hard-to-tread backs of the rough hill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tunic loosened, unshod; and from her mountain-wandering toils"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came home returning; and with unaccomplished purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grieving, she scarcely slept beside her husband, father of a dreadful child."},{"sp":"N","t":"And both of them held converse with shadowy dreams,","st":"5.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"having snatched with their eyes the nightingale-wing of Sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the soul of the young man stood beside his downcast father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the spotted, shadowy body of a deer, and from his eyelids"},{"sp":"N","t":"he poured intelligent tears, and spoke with a human voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Father, you sleep, and you do not know my necessity;","st":"5.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"wake and recognize my counterfeit, unknown face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wake and embrace the antler of your dear deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kiss the intelligent beast which the belly of Autonoe bore."},{"sp":"N","t":"You look upon me myself, whom you reared; for you both"},{"sp":"N","t":"see Actaeon and hear Actaeon.","st":"5.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"If you long to touch your son's palm and fingers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"look at his forefeet, and you will perceive his hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you long for his head, gaze upon the head of a deer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if for mortal temples, look at the long antlers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if for the feet of Actaeon, see the hind hoof;","st":"5.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you saw hair on his limbs, those have become my tunic."},{"sp":"N","t":"Recognize your son, father, whom Apollo did not save;"},{"sp":"N","t":"groan for your son, father, whom Cithaeron did not protect."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hide your strange-natured child in the downcast dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not leave your perished fawn without funeral rites;","st":"5.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"let not the counterfeit and unbelievable face mislead you."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would, father, that you had kept me unaccustomed to hunting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would not have had desire for the archer-goddess of the wilderness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would not have seen an Olympian body. Would that I had had"},{"sp":"N","t":"love for a mortal girl; but leaving earth-born women","st":"5.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"to others, and short-lived weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I desired an immortal; and when the goddess was angered"},{"sp":"N","t":"I became the dinner of my own hounds, father; the hills are"},{"sp":"N","t":"witnesses; if you do not believe the crags, ask the Naiad"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nymphs; my oaks know; and ask my beasts","st":"5.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"of like form, and the herdsmen whom I called."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, father, grant me a final favor, and in your child-loving"},{"sp":"N","t":"grief do not kill my witless murderers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pity the blameless child-slayers; for by my"},{"sp":"N","t":"beast's appearance they were led astray against their will.","st":"5.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"What hound ever spares a deer? Or what man"},{"sp":"N","t":"is angry with fawn-killing hounds? Ah, how much the wretches"},{"sp":"N","t":"run round and round the circling hills on this side and that,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and track the corpse which they killed; and from their eyelids"},{"sp":"N","t":"they pour intelligent tears, and with the tips of their feet","st":"5.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"they embrace the nets in a kind of affectionate bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like men in grief, and over my couch"},{"sp":"N","t":"they weep with mourning howls."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, I beg you, do not kill the mourners; for they gazed"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the shaggy hide of my face,","st":"5.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they were not persuaded by my prayers, and did not check their teeth"},{"sp":"N","t":"when they heard the bellowings of my altered voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with plaintive mouths they questioned my crag:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Today someone has snatched Actaeon; tell, rocks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where he pursues the deer-driving course; tell, Nymphs.'","st":"5.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the hounds uttered; and the hill answered:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'What mountain-roaming doe holds the deer-driving hunt?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not heard of a deer that shoots deer; but strange-natured"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon has been transformed and become an intelligent fawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once slew beasts; and by the man-slaying archer-goddess","st":"5.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"he himself, bearing the blood of Agreus, is hunted.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the crags cried to the grieving hounds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And often Artemis said to my seeking murderer:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Cease, heavy-toiling hound, winding your much-wandering track;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you seek Actaeon, whom you carry within your belly,","st":"5.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"you seek Actaeon, whom you killed; if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you will see only bones, the remains still of your own fodder.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, father, I shall cry my doom to you in order."},{"sp":"N","t":"There was a bush of long leaves, one of wild olive, one of olive;"},{"sp":"N","t":"wretched me; for leaving the shoot named for Phylia,","st":"5.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"I ran up the chaste trunk of the olive on the neighboring path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the naked skin of Artemis, who may not be gazed upon."},{"sp":"N","t":"I was infatuated; for, growing a twofold reckless insolence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I came to the plant of Pallas, to see the body of the archer-goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"with daring eyelids, whence the heavy-wrathful threat","st":"5.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"of both Artemis and Athena assailed Actaeon."},{"sp":"N","t":"For just then, sweating in the fire-bright vapor of the heat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, after the course of the well-toiled accustomed hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was bathing in pure waters, and as she bathed"},{"sp":"N","t":"her answering radiance dazzled my eyes,","st":"5.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting snowy rays into the streams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you would have said that beside the back-flowing stream of Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"bountiful Mene shone through the water in the evening."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Naiads shrieked together; Loxo cried,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Upis in concert with her, and in the calm stream","st":"5.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"she checked her sister Hecaerge as she swam."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a darkness, roaming the air, covered my eyes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the plant I slipped head foremost into the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and suddenly got a dappled body, and instead of a human"},{"sp":"N","t":"shape shaggy hair covered my unrecognizable body,","st":"5.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hunting hounds all alike scored their teeth."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be silent about all this; why should I tell a second grief?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me not approach you again with groans even as you sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"Often you passed by that tree, where lies"},{"sp":"N","t":"the remains of Actaeon, and over the ground, scattered,","st":"5.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"often you passed by the bones of the dappled fawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiable, of the much-devoured limbs, divided upon the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"far apart from one another. And I shall cry to you another"},{"sp":"N","t":"faithful token of my death; for you will see the quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"and my arrow near the tree that began the evil,","st":"5.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"unless even the winged arrows have been transformed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unless Artemis, angry again, has changed my bow"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a plant of the wood, and altered my quiver."},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed was Otus, because he was no wandering fawn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no hounds killed hound-driving Orion; would that the scorpion &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"5.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"had killed Actaeon himself too with its sharp sting."},{"sp":"N","t":"Wretched me; for an empty rumor deceived my mind;"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing that Phoebus, brother of the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay beside Cyrene, and sowed my father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I thought to draw Artemis into a marriage within the family.","st":"5.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"And again hearing that bright Dawn snatched"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orion as her bridegroom, and Selene Endymion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and that Deo embraced mortal Iasion as her husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I thought that such was the mind of the archer-goddess."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, father, give funeral rites to my counterfeit horn-drawing shape,","st":"5.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not leave it to become the plaything of other hounds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you hide my remains in the hollow earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant me this gift as well: fix my bow and arrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside my tomb, which is the prize of the dead."},{"sp":"N","t":"But leave the arrow and the bow, father, since in missiles","st":"5.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"the archer-goddess delights, and stretches the curved bow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beseech some much-turning painter of living things, that he may draw"},{"sp":"N","t":"my spotted counterfeit form from neck to the tips of the feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only of my face let him make the pattern of a mortal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that all may know my lying shape.","st":"5.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"But do not, father, write my doom; for a wayfarer cannot"},{"sp":"N","t":"weep at once for both my form and my fate.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So spoke the intelligent doe of the dream, and unforeseen"},{"sp":"N","t":"it departed on wings; and the husband of Autonoe"},{"sp":"N","t":"leapt up, casting off the wing of oracular Sleep.","st":"5.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"And rousing from the bed his wife, much dismayed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he declared the beastly, horn-drawing appearance of their son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and told the words, all that the intelligent fawn had said."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lament grew greater; and the bride of Aristaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"went seeking a second time, and, grieving,","st":"5.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"she traversed the thick haunts of the long-trunked thicket;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and treading the hard-to-tread circles of the rocky paths"},{"sp":"N","t":"she scarcely found that murderous plant, she found also"},{"sp":"N","t":"the quiver and the bow beside the lonely tree."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bones, scattered here and there over the heaped earth,","st":"5.545","hvShift6":[[0,0],[21,14],[24,23],[25,28],[35,39],[38,44],[47,50],[48,55],[53,61]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the fallen remains, the mother scarcely gathered together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with loving palm she embraced the sweet antler,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kissed the shaggy lips of the fawn of dreadful fate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And shrilly wailing the mother buried the corpse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and inscribed for him beside the tomb everything that to his father","st":"5.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"the nightly dream-voice of Actaeon had said."},{"sp":"N","t":"While grief roared in the hall of Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile fair-breasted Agave bore to Echion"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bold, god-fighting son of the earth-born; and as the fresh-spoken"},{"sp":"N","t":"grief stood in, Pentheus came to be, bearing a name from it.","st":"5.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the bed of Nephele, after the earlier wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Ino reveled into the chambers of Athamas;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she bore Learchus, who suffered dreadfully, and Melicertes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she who was to be a migrant of the sea, as the child-tending nurse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"life-bringing, of Bromius; for to both","st":"5.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"she gave a single breast in common, to Palaemon and to Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Semele was kept for a more shining wedding;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already high-ruling Zeus longed to rear a new Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bull-natured image of the ancient-born Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having desire for Zagreus of dreadful fate,","st":"5.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Persephoneia bore to the serpent bed of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she the consort of the black-cloaked king of the underworld,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Zeus, many-coiled, wearing a lying shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a gentle serpent circling in a lovely coil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plundered the maidenhood of unwedded Persephone","st":"5.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she lay hidden, when all who dwell in Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"were bewitched by a single girl, and about the maiden ripe for marriage"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a Cyprian strife for a wedlock none could plunder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing gifts; not yet going to the bed of Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes stretched out his rod as a prize for the bridal chamber,","st":"5.575"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Apollo offered as bride-gifts his fair-hymned lyre,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ares gave a spear and a breastplate as wedding-gifts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a shield as bridal present, and the Lemnian"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched out a many-colored, many-hued necklace, newly finished"},{"sp":"N","t":"and still breathing of the sweet-sounding furnace, the god lame in both legs;","st":"5.580"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already, unwilling, he had disowned his former bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching Aphrodite in Bacchic frenzy for Ares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he showed even to the blessed ones the marriage-thieving ravisher of his bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the message of Phaethon, in a kind of spider's-web bond"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding naked Ares tight to naked Aphrodite.","st":"5.585"},{"sp":"N","t":"But Zeus the father was bewitched far more by Persephoneia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as Zeus gazed at the fair-maidenly bloom of her nature"},{"sp":"N","t":"his eye became the leading escort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsated with Persephone; and beneath his heart forever"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tempests of unsleeping care whistled;","st":"5.590"},{"sp":"N","t":"and little by little he was kindled by the Paphian with a greater blaze"},{"sp":"N","t":"from a small spark; and by the fair-bosomed goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eyes of love-maddened Zeus were enslaved."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once the girl, holding a translucent bronze, delighted"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the judge of her reflected beauty, and with the self-moving","st":"5.595"},{"sp":"N","t":"silent herald she confirmed the pattern of her shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"judging her lying body in the shadowy mirror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laughed at the mimic image; and Persephone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the self-drawn statue of her face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beheld the counterfeit form of a lying Persephoneia;","st":"5.600"},{"sp":"N","t":"and once, in the fire-bright vapor of the thirsty heat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the midday track of the parched Season,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl rested from the loom-toiling labors of the shuttle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, wiping away the wet sweat from her face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she loosed the chaste band bound tight about her breast,","st":"5.605"},{"sp":"N","t":"and moistening her skin in toil-releasing baths"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was carried in the cooling stream of the spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the threads pierced on the loom of Pallas."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did she escape the all-seeing eye of Zeus; and uncovered"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she bathed, he beheld the whole form of Persephoneia;","st":"5.610"},{"sp":"N","t":"not so much in desire did he rage for the Cyprus-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for whom, unattainable, he sowed his seed in the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting the hot, self-moving foam of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whence within horned Cyprus, that fosters birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-colored race of the well-horned Centaurs bloomed.","st":"5.615"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ruler of the cosmos and charioteer of heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"great as he was, bent his neck to desire; neither thunderbolts"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor lightning helped him when Aphrodite armed herself;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he left the house of Hera, he renounced the bed of Dione,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cast away love for Deo, he fled Themis, he abandoned Leto,","st":"5.620"},{"sp":"N","t":"and was bewitched into wedlock with Persephoneia alone."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book VI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Seek the divine sixth, where, honoring Zagreus, rainy Zeus deluged all the seats of the earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the father alone then have desire; but they too,","st":"6.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"having one equal arrow, all who dwell in Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"offered bride-gifts for the wedlock of Deo's divine daughter."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then, changing the radiance of her rosy face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deo was lashed in her mind, surging with pains;","st":"6.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she unclasped the fertile veil from her head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosing the curls of her hair let down upon her neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shuddering for her child; and as the goddess was weighed down"},{"sp":"N","t":"her cheeks were moistened with self-flowing tears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because so many suitors, with one flaming missile,","st":"6.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"goaded into strife over an emulous wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"common Eros drove into frenzy, rivals of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she trembled at them all, but most of all the bountiful mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"feared to have lame Hephaestus as her child's husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she went with fair-footed sole to the house of Astraeus,","st":"6.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"the oracular god; and the unplaited curl of her hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"streaming behind her, was shaken by the unsteady breezes."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seeing her, the Dawnstar announced her; and hearing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Astraeus rose; he had been scoring with lines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling dark dust across the back of a table,","st":"6.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, tracing in spirals beneath the crook-toothed iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he inscribed a four-sided base in the blazing ash,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made another figure with an equal-angled triangle."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he let these things be and came near the doors"},{"sp":"N","t":"to meet Demeter; and as they passed through the hall","st":"6.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hesperus led the way, and seated Deo on a throne"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside his father's seat; and with a purpose of like affection,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing from the nectarous bowl, the Winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sons of Astraeus, pledged the goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"with toil-releasing cups; but Deo refused to drink,","st":"6.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"drunk with care for Persephone; for parents of a single child"},{"sp":"N","t":"tremble forever for their late-born children;"},{"sp":"N","t":"yet at length sweet-spoken Astraeus, having mind-charming Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prevailed upon Deo while she still refused."},{"sp":"N","t":"There the old man prepared a great dinner, so that at table","st":"6.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"he might scatter the heart-gnawing cares of Demeter."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the four Winds, fastening baldrics about their flanks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were girdled as servants of their father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching out cups beside the nectarous bowl"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eurus poured the wine, and in a jug Notus held","st":"6.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"the after-dinner water, and Boreas, bringing it, set upon the table"},{"sp":"N","t":"ambrosia, and Zephyrus, pressing out the murmur of a flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the female Wind, made melody on the reeds of spring;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Dawnstar plaited garlands, binding flowers"},{"sp":"N","t":"luxuriant with dew-sprinkled morning clusters;","st":"6.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifting the accustomed torch of the nightly lamp"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hesperus whirled on the dancing sole of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tossing his curving step, since he was the escort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and delighted in the leap of the dance-woven wedding."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when after the meal the goddess had her fill of the dance,","st":"6.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking off the heavy sting of mind-robbing care,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she asked for prophecy, and with her left hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"she touched the knees of the affectionate old man, and, entreating,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with her right she touched his thick-haired beard;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she told him of the many suitors of her daughter,","st":"6.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking comforting oracles; for prophecies"},{"sp":"N","t":"steal away pains with hopes of things to come."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did old Astraeus refuse; and reckoning the newly born"},{"sp":"N","t":"natal measures of the only-begotten girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the time that does not stumble, and the course of the unerring","st":"6.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"first-born Hour, bending the turning fingers of his hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the returning circle of the migrant number,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he measured from palm to palm with a twofold beat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and at his call a servant, lifting the well-rounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning sphere, the pattern of the upper air, the image of the cosmos,","st":"6.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterion set it beside him, taking it from the lid of a chest."},{"sp":"N","t":"There the old man labored, and, turning the tip of the axle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched his gaze about the circle of the Zodiac,"},{"sp":"N","t":"looking this way and that at the fixed stars and the wanderers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he whirled the vault about; and with much-whirling rush","st":"6.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the counterfeit upper air, circling with wrought stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent to an unsteady course at an endless turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pierced by the axle through its middle; and the god found,"},{"sp":"N","t":"looking at the sphere crown-wise, that with full face"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene was running across the curved backs of the conjunction,","st":"6.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phaethon held an equal share, opposite Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried in the underground center, and a misty"},{"sp":"N","t":"cone, borne aloft, creeping up sharp from the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered the whole of Selene opposite Phaethon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hearing of the rivals in bridal love","st":"6.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sought Ares the more, and above the western house"},{"sp":"N","t":"he perceived the thief of weddings with the evening star"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Cyprus-born; and he found the lot named for parents"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the starry ear of corn of the Maiden; and around it"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran the light-bearing star of rain-bearing Cronides.","st":"6.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he had understood everything, having counted the rim of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hid away the ever-whirling sphere in a hollow chest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sphere with the many-patterned back; and to the goddess who asked"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched forth a threefold oracle in prophetic voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Child-loving Demeter, beneath the shadowy cone,","st":"6.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the ray of unlit Selene is stolen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"guard against a ravishing bridegroom for Persephoneia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hidden plunderer of your daughter whom none may plunder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the threads of the Fates can be persuaded; unforeseen"},{"sp":"N","t":"you will see before marriage a bastard, secret husband,","st":"6.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"beast-mingled, guileful, since beside the western center"},{"sp":"N","t":"I see marriage-thieving Ares walking with the Paphian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I watch the Dragon rising beside them both."},{"sp":"N","t":"I call you most blessed; for in the four-yoked cosmos"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall be splendid in fruit, since you shall give fruit","st":"6.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the barren earth; for above the lot of the parents of the girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Astraea stretches her hand full of corn-ears.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, he lulled the oracle beneath his prophetic mouth."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when sickle-bearing Demeter heard of the hope of fruits"},{"sp":"N","t":"to come and of the self-bidden husband,","st":"6.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwarranted ravisher of her late-born, untamed daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she groaned, smiling; and along the high-pathed road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hastening to her house, she went with downcast foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, balancing the well-turning yoke beside the viperish manger"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the twin crest of the beasts,","st":"6.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"she clamped the unyoked creeping things in the yoke-strap;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and binding their jaws tight with the crook-toothed bit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yellow-haired Deo led through the grim chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"her child veiled in the dark-fillet band of a cloud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the beast-driving whip she made the counter-rushing crash","st":"6.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"whistle down of Boreas roaring against the wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they rushed horse-like on the course of the airy breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"folding the light wings of the unsteady serpents"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the horn of Libyan Ocean flowing back again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And hearing the helmet-glancing melody of the Dictaean echo,","st":"6.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"she passed by the Cretan chorus skipping about the back"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the heavy-crashing ox-hide with tumbling iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"And surveying a certain stone house, the goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"entered the Pelorian rock of three-crested Sicily"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the Adriatic shores, where the unsteady pouring of the brine,","st":"6.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawn toward the west, is bent round like a sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"escorting the crooked water toward Lips from the north;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and where the stream often bathed the girl Cyane,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the water of first sight with a spring-fed whirl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw a neighboring hollow standing level with a hall,","st":"6.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"crowned with a covering of stone roof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which nature had coped with a torrent-gate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a stone loom that was the care of the neighboring Nymphs."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess, creeping through the dark hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid her many-sealed child in the rock of the lair;","st":"6.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosing the serpents from the well-winged chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one beside the crag of the right-hand door,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the other beside the left-hand, stone-pointed bolt of the gate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she set as guardian of Persephoneia, who may not be gazed upon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there Calligeneia, her nurse blessed in children,","st":"6.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"she left with the baskets, and all that for the female stock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wool-working sweat of skilled-handed Pallas apportions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she cut the air with her feet, and entrusted the curved chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the rock Nymphs who dwell in the wilderness to keep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And about the jagged-toothed jaw of iron the girl labored,","st":"6.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"carding the fleeces about the wool-dressing comb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wound them on the distaff; and with much-whirling rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spiral spindle, twirling with bounding stroke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"danced, circling in the trail of the threads being spun;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with roaming feet, back and forth from end to end,","st":"6.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"she made the first-fixed warp, the beginning of the robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wound it about the loom; and the girl wove with the shuttle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing out the bobbin past the thread, and over the robe"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sang shrill of Athena, her kinswoman who perfects the loom."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden Persephoneia, you did not find how to escape marriage,","st":"6.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"but you were wedded in a serpent's wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Zeus, many-coiled, his face transformed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bridegroom, a serpent circling in a lovely coil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passed through into the recess of the dark maiden-chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his shaggy jaws; and, carried past, he lulled the eye","st":"6.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the serpents of like form standing at the door, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with bridal jaws he licked the body of the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gentle. And from the heavenly serpent wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the belly of Persephone swelled with a fertile birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing Zagreus, the horned infant, who alone","st":"6.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"mounted the heavenly seat of Zeus, and with his little hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the lightning quiver, and in the infant palms"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the newborn bearer the thunderbolts were lightened."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did he hold the throne of Zeus for a time; but, their thievish"},{"sp":"N","t":"circles of face smeared with cunning gypsum,","st":"6.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the wrath of the loveless goddess, heavy-wrathful Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Titans did him harm with a Tartarean knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he gazed at his counterfeit form in a reflecting mirror."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, as his limbs were divided by the Titan iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, having the end of life as a beginning to be won again,","st":"6.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"was shaped anew, changing his far-scattered form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now as guileful Cronides, young, shaking the aegis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now as an old man heavy-kneed, like Cronus sending rain;"},{"sp":"N","t":"at one time he was an infant of varied shape, at another like a youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"goaded to frenzy, and the new flower of down","st":"6.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"traced the circles of his face, darkening at the edges;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now, a mimic lion sending forth a frightful wrath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raged with a dreadful roar from a grinning throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising his neck shadowed with thick mane,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, as the shaggy-haired tail whirled about above his back,","st":"6.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"pricking his body with a self-moving whip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"then, leaving the likeness of a lion's face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he neighed with a whinny like a high-crested horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unyoked, shifting the proud tooth of the bit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, rubbing his jaw, whitened it with grey foam;","st":"6.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time, pouring a whistling hiss from his beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a horned serpent abundant in scales writhed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his tongue thrust out from a gaping throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he leapt upon the grim head of a Titan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding a viperish necklace entwined about his neck;","st":"6.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, leaving the ever-whirling body of a creeping thing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was a tiger, spotting his shifting body; at another time like"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bull in nature, and sending a counterfeit bellowing from his mouths"},{"sp":"N","t":"he battered the Titans with his whetted horn."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he fought in the front for his life, until with jealous throat","st":"6.200","hvShift5":[[1,1],[3,6]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera roared a harsh bellowing through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavy-wrathful stepmother, and in answer to the goddess's voice"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gates of Olympus rang with a heavenly clangor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bold bull sank to his knees; and with alternating knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"the murderers carved up bull-natured Dionysus.","st":"6.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"But Zeus the father, when the earlier Dionysus was butchered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing the shadowy pattern of the guileful mirror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving the mother of the Titans with avenging fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"locked the murderers of well-horned Zagreus"},{"sp":"N","t":"within the Tartarean gate; and from the burning trees","st":"6.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hot curls of the burdened earth withered."},{"sp":"N","t":"He set the east ablaze, and with smoking missile"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eastern soil of Bactria burned, and beside the neighboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"Assyrian waves the Caspian water was kindled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Indian ridges; and as the Erythraean gulf","st":"6.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"swelled with fire the Arabian Nereus grew hot."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the west on the opposite path fiery Zeus laid low"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his thunderbolt, loving his child; and beneath the sole of Zephyrus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the half-burnt western brine spat forth a moist radiance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Arctic ridges; and the northern back of the sea,","st":"6.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"itself blazing alike as it froze, bubbled;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beneath the snowy slope of southern Capricorn"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bend of the south seethed with a hotter spark."},{"sp":"N","t":"And pouring river-tears from his wet eyelids"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ocean entreated, shedding suppliant water;","st":"6.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zeus soothed his wrath, and seeing the earth withering"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the thunderbolt he pitied her, and wished to wash away with water"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ashen defilement and the fiery wound of the land."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then rainy Zeus flooded the whole earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"thickening the whole vault with clouds, and the heavenly","st":"6.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"trumpet of Zeus bellowed with thunderous crashes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when all the stars, each in its own house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept their appointed course, since in his four-yoked chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun blazed upon the back of the Lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding his own house; and running with her chariot","st":"6.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"over the eight-footed Crab circled threefold Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beneath the dewy floor beside the equinoctial circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris, having moved from the horn of the Ram,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held her house of spring, the stormless Bull of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ares, neighbor of the Sun, held the Scorpion,","st":"6.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"forerunner of the Plow-beam, girdled by the blazing Bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching aslant Aphrodite on the opposite path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the twelve-month traveler, completing the year,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus ran over the starry Fishes, rising at nightfall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the spiraling Moon on his right in trine,","st":"6.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cronus passed through the rainy back of Capricorn"},{"sp":"N","t":"drenched in a frosty light, and beside the bright"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden Hermes was raised aloft on wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the judge held Justice as his house. And the watery"},{"sp":"N","t":"bolts of the seven-pathed upper air were opened","st":"6.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Zeus rained down; and the ravines of the loud-roaring gulf"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowed with fuller springs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the watery daughters severed from Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lakes, were lifted up, and into the air the nether water"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spear-hurling springs of Ocean gushed forth,","st":"6.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the peaks dripped, and with a mountain-poured stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thirsty hills murmured like rivers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sea was raised on high, and upon the mountain above the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Nereids became Oreads. Ah, greatly wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with inexperienced hands swam the maiden Echo,","st":"6.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging for her ancient fear another fear about her girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ever, having escaped Pan, she be coupled with Poseidon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And sea-faring lions within an unaccustomed rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"danced in the cave of land lions"},{"sp":"N","t":"with dripping limbs; and in a torrent-bosom","st":"6.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wandering boar met a dolphin of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the common surges of the mountain-poured snowstorm"},{"sp":"N","t":"beasts sailed with fish; and the coiling"},{"sp":"N","t":"octopus, roaming the mountains, leapt upon a hare."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the wet Tritons beneath the feet of the wood of lairs,","st":"6.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting their green double tail upon the loins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid themselves in the mountain-haunting halls of Pan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting to the airy winds as fellow-sailor"},{"sp":"N","t":"their accustomed spotted conch; and on a well-watered hill"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus, a migrant, met crag-loving Pan,","st":"6.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"who had left behind his pipe, dripping, to sail away,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rock-dweller, exchanging mountain for sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held the dripping cave, the house of Echo beneath the roof."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then men, swelling in the watery destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were entombed in the waters, and many a corpse, one after another,","st":"6.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"floated carried in the wavy trail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the roaring snowstorm, with gaping throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking greedily from the crag the mountain-running water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lion fell, the boar fell. And in a yoked stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"lakes together with rivers, the flood of Zeus, the waters of the sea","st":"6.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"were mingled with one another, and in one the four breezes"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the mingled winds lashed the undivided water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the whole earth wet beneath a greater stroke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by the threatening rain of Zeus alone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea-dwelling Earth-shaker cast away his point","st":"6.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"in distress, having no land to heave up with his trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ranks of the Nereids sailed over the unwetted water;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[54,57],[56,58]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and a wet traveler held Thetis upon his green loins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"broad-bearded Triton, and on a fishy back"},{"sp":"N","t":"roaming Agave drove a pilot-fish through the air,","st":"6.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, bearing his crest circling with a watery trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting Doris, a migrant dolphin ran."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a whale of the deep, skipping about the hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandered, seeking the caves of the lioness who lies on the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then, seeing Galatea swimming in the waves","st":"6.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath a neighboring rock, dripping Pan cried out:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Where are you carried, Galatea, through the mountain instead of the sea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Are you perhaps seeking the lovely song of the Cyclops?"},{"sp":"N","t":"By the Paphian I beg you, and by your Polyphemus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not hide, knowing my heavy longing, if beside the rocks","st":"6.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have seen my mountain-running Echo swimming."},{"sp":"N","t":"Does she hold a wet course equal to yours? Does she too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated upon a dolphin of Aphrodite of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like unveiled Thetis, my Echo, sail?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest the hard-facing waves of the sea have troubled her;","st":"6.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest the great flood has hidden her; thus the wretched one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady, walks the waves in the seas after the mountains;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she who once was of the rocks will appear as Echo of the waters."},{"sp":"N","t":"But leave your slow Polyphemus; if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself, lifting you on my shoulders, will save you;","st":"6.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"the roaring flood does not deluge me; if I wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with goat's footsteps I shall go to the vault of the stars.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"To him as he spoke Galatea cried back in such a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear Pan, lift up your Echo, who cannot swim, through the swell;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not ask me in vain why I walk here today;","st":"6.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus has found me another, higher voyage."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leave the song of the Cyclops, sweet though it is."},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do I seek the Sicilian sea; for I have terror"},{"sp":"N","t":"of so great a snowstorm, and I care nothing for Polyphemus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and passed by the haunts of Pan who travels the waters.","st":"6.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the irresistible snowstorm surged thick,"},{"sp":"N","t":"every city, every people was a flood; no bend"},{"sp":"N","t":"was unwetted, no ridge was bare, not the peak of Ossa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not then the summit of Pelion; and beneath the three-crested hill"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Tyrrhenian sea roared; and as the sea was lashed","st":"6.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Adriatic rocks dashed against the Sicilian waters"},{"sp":"N","t":"with rainy surges. And in the airy path"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sparklings of Phaethon were made womanish by the streams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the seventh zone, above the rim of the low-lying floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene, having cooled her radiance with the sky-high waves,","st":"6.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"checked the bathed neck of her dripping bulls;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rainwater mingled with the starry battle-line"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the rim of the Milky Way whiter with foam."},{"sp":"N","t":"And pouring his seven-mouthed water in a fertile surge,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering Nile met Alpheius, unhappy in love,","st":"6.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom the one wished to creep through the fruitful furrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting the thirsty bride with moist kisses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while the other, having swerved from the former road of his accustomed hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was carried along in grief; and seeing lovely Pyramus"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping along with him he uttered such a speech:","st":"6.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Nile, what shall I do, when Arethusa is hidden?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyramus, why do you hasten? To whom have you left your accustomed Thisbe?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed is Euphrates, because he has never got the sting of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have jealousy and fear mingled; for perhaps watery"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cronides lies beside lovely Arethusa;","st":"6.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest with his outpourings he has wedded your Thisbe."},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyramus, comfort of Alpheius, us both"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rain of Zeus has not troubled so much as the arrow of the foam-born."},{"sp":"N","t":"Follow me, the lover; and of Syracusan Arethusa","hvShift6":[[0,0],[9,10],[14,14],[19,20]]},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall seek the tracks, and you, Pyramus, search for Thisbe.","st":"6.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say that the earth is shaken, that heaven"},{"sp":"N","t":"is in trouble, that the sea is forced, that even the upper air"},{"sp":"N","t":"itself, unnavigable, surges with a foaming flood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I care nothing for the maddened snowstorm. Ah, great wonder:"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rain of Zeus has cleansed the whole burning earth and the flame of the sea","st":"6.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rivers, but from Alpheius alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"it has not quenched the paltry fire of the Paphian that touches him."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nevertheless, if so great a flood harries me, if I labor with fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a small remedy for my pain is this, that even"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate Adonis himself wanders, grieving Aphrodite.\"","st":"6.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had his speech ended, when fear overpowered his voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then Deucalion, crossing the water raised on high,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was an untraceable sailor, having a voyage roaming the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the self-guided vessel without foot, without share in a harbor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inscribed the ill-snowed water of the self-traveling chest.","st":"6.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"And now the cosmos would have become no cosmos, and now"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-nourishing Aeon would have dissolved the unsown harmony of men;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but at the most holy nods of Zeus the dark-haired god,"},{"sp":"N","t":"digging the navel-summit of the Thessalian crag,"},{"sp":"N","t":"split it with his earth-cleaving trident, and through the middle","st":"6.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the broken headland the marble water withdrew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and thrusting aside the high-pathed pouring of the snowstorm"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth appeared back again; and as the streams were driven"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the caverns of the deep, the cliffs were laid bare."},{"sp":"N","t":"And pouring his much-thirsting radiance on the wet brow of the earth","st":"6.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun dried it; and as the floods thickened"},{"sp":"N","t":"with hotter rays the silt was made dry land again"},{"sp":"N","t":"as before. And built with greater mortal art"},{"sp":"N","t":"cities were fixed upon stone foundations,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and halls were built, and the streets of newly founded cities","st":"6.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"were fortified for newly born mortals."},{"sp":"N","t":"And nature laughed again; and with the wings of birds"},{"sp":"N","t":"flying together in the storms the air was rowed once more."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book VII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The seventh sings the grey-haired supplication of Aeon, and Semele, and the love of Zeus, and the stolen bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Already, with the ever-regrowing fruit of ever-flowing life,","st":"7.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing male fertile seed into the female furrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros, the plowman of love, had plowed the unsown cosmos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and nature was rooted, the nurse of generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mingling fire with earth and water entwined with air","st":"7.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shaped the human race in a four-yoked bond."},{"sp":"N","t":"But a changeful distress held the life of mortals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beginning with toil and never ceasing from care."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to all-ruling Zeus the race of much-suffering men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"without share of gladness, Aeon, their foster-brother, displayed;","st":"7.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"for not yet, having loosed the childbed threads of birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had the father shot forth Bacchus from his pregnant thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the respite from human care; not then did libation"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine-dipped make the paths of the air drunk with fragrant smoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the daughters of the year, the joyless Seasons,","st":"7.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"plaited garlands for the gods with meadow grass;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there was need of wine; and of a dance without Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grace was half-finished and profitless; for it charmed"},{"sp":"N","t":"only the eyes of those gathered, when with whirling rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the many-circling dancer spun in a tempest of feet,","st":"7.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"having nods for speech, a palm for a mouth, fingers for a voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, spreading his white hair upon the knees of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeon of the many-changing shape, holding the key of generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching out the loosened trail of his beard in supplication,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made his prayers; and as his head was dragged down to the floor","st":"7.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"his arching neck pressed the spine stretched full length;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, his foot sinking down, stretching out his endless hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the old shepherd of ever-flowing life spoke:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Lord Zeus, do you too look upon the pains of the downcast cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you not see that Enyo has goaded the whole earth to frenzy,","st":"7.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowing in season the ear of swift-perishing youth?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet have those remains passed away, since you deluged"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the nations of men, and the flood of the airy rain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"surging in the air, bubbled up to neighboring Mene."},{"sp":"N","t":"Farewell to the life of swift-fated mortals, at whose doom","st":"7.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"I refuse the heavenly helm; no longer shall I steer"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cable of the cosmos; let some other, better one of the blessed ones"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive the rudder of returning life;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let another hold the course of my years; for pitying the dread-suffering"},{"sp":"N","t":"race of much-enduring men I have grown weary.","st":"7.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"Old age is not enough, which withers youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"and makes a man slow, with head nodding downward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, bowed, with trembling, halting steps,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-kneed, he leans upon the accustomed staff that tends old age;"},{"sp":"N","t":"doom is not enough, which has often hidden in Lethe","st":"7.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bridegroom fresh from the dance in company with his bride of equal age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosing the life-bringing cables of the indissoluble yoke."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know that marriage is lovely, where sings shrill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in concert with the pipe of Pan, the flute of Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless, what profit is it, when beside the yoked bridal chamber","st":"7.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"the upright echo of the seven-toned lyre is struck?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harps do not loose cares; but even Eros himself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"danceless, has shaken off the bridal pine-torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching weddings that lack delight."},{"sp":"N","t":"But let there be rooted a life-saving remedy that makes forgetful","st":"7.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the distress of much-toiling mortals; for that heavenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"veil of the jar Pandora ought never to have opened"},{"sp":"N","t":"for men, a sweet evil. But Prometheus himself too"},{"sp":"N","t":"is guilty of human misery,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he who cares for toiling mortals; for instead of the fire","st":"7.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"that began the evil, sweet nectar, which delights the heart of the blessed ones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ought rather to have stolen and given as a gift to men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that with your drink he might scatter the cares of the cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, leaving the cares of loud-roaring life,"},{"sp":"N","t":"look upon your downcast rites; does the wind-borne wandering vapor","st":"7.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"of libation-less sacrifices really charm you?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As the old man so spoke, for a time in intelligent silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"wise Zeus turned about his endless counsel;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he loosed the reins of his mind; and with successive counsels"},{"sp":"N","t":"the purposes of his generative brain were whirled.","st":"7.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cronides cried to Aeon in god-speaking voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"revealing oracles higher than those of the oracular axle:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"O father, self-sown shepherd of the ever-flowing years,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be not angry; for mortal nature, untimely, never ceases"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling and waning, an image of the moon.","st":"7.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave nectar to the blessed ones, and to men as a bulwark against distress"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give sweet wine, self-poured, like nectar;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another drink fitted for mortals; and the first-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"cosmos grieves still, until I bring forth one child."},{"sp":"N","t":"I the begetter give birth, and I shall endure in my male thigh","st":"7.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"the birth-pangs of women, so that I may save the birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yesterday, at the nod of my Deo of the broad threshing-floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth, scored by the iron that woos the corn-ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought forth the dry fruit of the sheaf-bearing field."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already my son of splendid gifts shall fix in the earth","st":"7.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"the moist, fragrant fruit of the pain-healing harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grief-free Dionysus, rearing the grape-cluster free of grief,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a rival to Demeter; and you will praise me, watching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine reddening with wine-bearing dews,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the herald of gladness, and the field-dwellers beside the wine-vat","st":"7.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the harvest with heavy-laden feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Euoi-loving battle-line of Bassarids from high on their shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting their unplaited, raging hair into the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having made their hearts Bacchic with alternating cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all shall cry Euoi at the sweet-sounding table","st":"7.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Dionysus, defender of the human race;"},{"sp":"N","t":"him, after his contest upon the earth, running with the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the strife of the earth-born, together with the battle of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shifting upper air shall receive, flashing alongside Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god of the vines, having wound about his locks","st":"7.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a garland a creeping thing lying upon wine-dark ivy, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall have a serpent headband as the sign of his new divinity;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, equal in honor with the blessed ones, he shall be named for men"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus of the vine, as Hermes is of the golden rod,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Ares is bronze, as Apollo is the far-shooter.\"","st":"7.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"So the father spoke; and the Fates assented; and about his word"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-footed Seasons, heralds of things to come, sneezed the omen."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having so spoken they parted, the one going"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the house of Harmonia, the other to the many-colored house of Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And wise self-taught Eros, shepherding the age,","st":"7.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"battering the gloomy gates of first-born Chaos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought the god-driven quiver, in which alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the fickle desire of earthly weddings"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fire-nourished arrows were kept for Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"twelve, and a golden line in measure for each","st":"7.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wrote in the middle of the back of the desire-striking quiver:"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first leads Cronion to the bed of ox-eyed Io;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the second woos Europa for the ravishing bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the third leads the ruler of Olympus to the wedlock of Plouto;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fourth calls the golden husband to Danae;","st":"7.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fifth prepares for Semele a flaming wedlock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sixth escorts the eagle, champion of the upper air, to Aegina;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seventh joins Antiope to a guileful Satyr;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eighth leads an intelligent swan to naked-skinned Leda;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ninth brings a horse's bed to Perrhaebian Dia;","st":"7.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the tenth the three-mooned husband of Alcmene is bewitched;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eleventh pursues the nuptials of Laodameia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twelfth draws the thrice-coiled husband to Olympias."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when Eros had seen them all, handling them in order,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the other fire-pointed arrows he let be,","st":"7.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the fifth he lifted in his hand and fitted to the blazing string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting ivy upon the point of the winged arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that it might be a fitting garland for the god of the vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dipping the whole missile in the moisture of the nectarous bowl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that Bacchus might rear a nectarous harvest.","st":"7.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"While Eros was lifted with a bound to the house of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile Semele too, running with the rosy dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched the sound of her silver whip through the city"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving her mules, and the thin, upright trail of the well-greaved"},{"sp":"N","t":"wagon inscribed the surface of the dust;","st":"7.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"for having plucked from her eyes the Lethaean wing of Sleep"},{"sp":"N","t":"she escorted her wandering spirit with the answering dream"},{"sp":"N","t":"that embroidered oracles, and among the newly born clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"she hoped to see a fair-leaved plant within the garden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"green, weighed down with swelling, unripe fruit,","st":"7.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"snowed upon by the plant-nourishing dews of Cronion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and suddenly a heavenly flame, falling through the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid the whole tree low, but did not touch its fruit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but a long-winged wandering bird, snatching it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-finished, lacking the childbed that perfects birth,","st":"7.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"held it out to Cronion; and the father, taking it, received it"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his sweet bosom, and sewed it into his thigh; and instead of fruit"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bull-natured man, shaped in horned pattern,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-perfected, sprouted above the groin of his father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Semele was the plant, and the girl, shuddering,","st":"7.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"leapt from her bed and dismayed her father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"telling of the light-bearing vapor of the fair-leaved dreams."},{"sp":"N","t":"And king Cadmus was shaken, hearing of Semele's fire-burnt plant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and calling the god-speaking son of Chariclo"},{"sp":"N","t":"early he declared the smoky dreams of his child.","st":"7.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"And then, having received the inspired oracle of Teiresias,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the father sent his child to the accustomed temple of Athena"},{"sp":"N","t":"to sacrifice to Zeus, the hurler of the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bull, an image of the same-horned nature of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a goat, the grape-cutting enemy of the harvest to come.","st":"7.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thence she went before the city, so that she might kindle an altar to Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ruler of the lightning; and standing by the sacrifices"},{"sp":"N","t":"she wetted her bosom with blood, and the girl was sprinkled with gore;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unsparing trails of blood drenched her locks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her tunics were purpled with the drops of the ox;","st":"7.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and steering her course beside the deep-rushed grass"},{"sp":"N","t":"she passed to the ancestral water of neighboring Asopus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden of shifting robe, in order to cleanse in the streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"her robes spotted, drenched with much-dripping gore."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she took another garment, and high on the neighboring bank,","st":"7.175","hvShift5":[[0,0],[3,15]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[9,19],[13,27],[15,28],[17,35],[18,40],[24,47],[35,52],[51,54],[58,60],[63,62]]},{"sp":"N","t":"by the eastern strand of Dionysus, averter of evil,","hvShift5":[],"hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"she shook off into the flood, into the winds, the terror of the dreams."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not without a god did she go to the streams, but the prophetic"},{"sp":"N","t":"Seasons led her to the outpourings of that river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing Semele bathing beside the streams of Asopus","st":"7.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"the roaming Erinys laughed in the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembering Cronion, that with a common doom"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was to strike them both with the flaming thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"There she brightened her body, and naked with her handmaids"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl ran through the water, rowing with her hands;","st":"7.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with skilled art she lifted her unwetted head"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched high above the swell, water-dipped as far"},{"sp":"N","t":"as her hair, and, spreading her breast upon the stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with alternating feet she thrust the water behind her."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did she escape the all-seeing eye of Zeus; and about the girl","st":"7.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-visible he whirled the endless circle of his gaze."},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing in the air his bow, the helper of life,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros stood face to face with his watching father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an archer untraceable; and upon the flower-tressed missile"},{"sp":"N","t":"the string flashed, and as the back-bent bow","st":"7.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"was drawn, the wise Euian arrow whistled an echo."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father, great as he was, was the target; and to paltry"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros he bent his neck; and like the trail of a star"},{"sp":"N","t":"the arrow of the Loves, shaken with a bridal hiss,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came into the heart of Zeus, swerving with an intelligent stroke,","st":"7.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"inscribing with its topmost notches the fold of his thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"forerunner of the birth to come. Then Cronion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing an unsteady eye, the channel of bridal necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was lashed by the cestus of longing into love for the maiden;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing Semele he leapt up, lest near the bank","st":"7.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had seen Europa a second time; and in his heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"he labored again, bearing a Phoenician longing; for the pattern"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the same splendor she had, and forever about her face"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparkled the kindred gleam of her father's sister."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father changed his guileful shape,","st":"7.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for love of Semele he flew before his time as an eagle"},{"sp":"N","t":"high above Asopus, the river father of daughters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a prophet of the well-winged wedlock of Aegina,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the sharp-eyed mimicry of a bird's gaze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he left the upper air and near the neighboring bank","st":"7.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the naked body of the fair-tressed girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he did not long to see from afar, but he wished to watch"},{"sp":"N","t":"from close at hand the whole all-white body of the bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, sending everywhere so great and such an endless,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-encompassing eye, gazer upon the whole cosmos,","st":"7.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not think it enough to see one unwed girl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And by her rosy limbs the black water was reddened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lovely river-stream became a meadow"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing with the Graces; and watching the maiden","hvShift6":[[0,0],[49,41],[54,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled Naiad belched forth a voice in wonder:","st":"7.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Did Cronus, after the earlier Cypris, with marriage-robbing sickle"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut the members of his father, until once more the intelligent foam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing shaped water to a self-perfected birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought forth a younger Aphrodite of the sea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did a river, after the sea, in emulous childbirths,","st":"7.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding the childbed trail of a self-begotten wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bear another Cypris, and not yield to the sea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has one of the Muses entered my ancestral water"},{"sp":"N","t":"from neighboring Helicon, and left to some spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"the honey-dripping horse-water of the Pegasid fount","st":"7.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the stream of Olmeius? I watch within the river"},{"sp":"N","t":"a silver-footed maiden stretched in the streams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that, wishing to go to the Latmian bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the couch of Endymion, the unsleeping herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene is bathing in the Aonian outpourings;","st":"7.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if she brightened her body for the sake of her sweet herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what need of Asopus after the stream of Ocean?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if she wears a heavenly, snowy shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what token of the moon does she have? For the yoke-bands"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unbridled mules and a silver-circled wagon","st":"7.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"stand by the shore, and Selene, driver of cattle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"does not know how to yoke mules beneath the strap."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if some heavenly goddess has come — for I see"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grey-green sparklings of the maiden's calm eyelids —,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then perhaps, after the ancient quarrel with Teiresias,","st":"7.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"grey-eyed Athena has bathed again, having cast off her skin."},{"sp":"N","t":"The rosy-armed girl has a godlike shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if an earthly belly of splendid burden bore her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was worthy of the heavenly bed of Cronion.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the submerged voice spoke in the surges;","st":"7.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zeus, shaken by the fire-pointed sting of longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed at the rosy-skinned fingers of the swimming girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he whirled the wandering circle of his unsteady gaze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now watching the spark of her rosy face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now the radiance of her ox-orbed eyelids, at another time her hair","st":"7.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"straying in the winds, and as her locks were drawn aside"},{"sp":"N","t":"he surveyed the free, uncovered neck of the girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he looked more at her breasts, and against Cronides the naked"},{"sp":"N","t":"breasts were armed, spear-hurlers of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he watched all her skin; but only the rites of her lap","st":"7.265","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"that may not be gazed upon he passed by with shame-faced eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mind of heavenly Zeus, creeping as a migrant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swam together with swimming Semele; and, bewitched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the sweet-maddening spark in his accustomed heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the father yielded to his child; and with the feeblest missile","st":"7.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"little Eros set on fire the hurler of the thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"neither the pouring of snow, nor the lightning helped"},{"sp":"N","t":"its flaming bearer, and so great a heavenly flame"},{"sp":"N","t":"was itself conquered by the small fire of the unwarlike"},{"sp":"N","t":"Paphian; and little Eros' cestus strove against the shaggy-haired hide,","st":"7.275","hvShift5":[[0,0],[24,25],[26,43],[27,48],[33,51]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the aegis, and to the love-breeding quiver","hvShift5":[[27,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavy-thundering crash of the thunderous echo was enslaved."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he was shaken by the mind-charming sting of longing for Semele,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding wonder; for love is neighbor to friendly wonder."},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely did guile-weaving high-ruling Zeus come to the vault,","st":"7.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending the inspired shape of his face recovered again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And wishing to mount the nightly bed of Semele"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched his eye toward the west, for when sweet Hesperus should come;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he blamed the long afternoon hour of Phaethon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with loving lips cried out a love-sick voice:","st":"7.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Tell me, lingering Night, when does envious Dawn set?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you lift the torch, forerunner of the Loves of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"foretelling the lamp of night-roaming Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous Phaethon forces me; does he too himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"desire Semele and grudge me in my longing?","st":"7.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"Helios, you harry me even if you have learned the sting of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"why with sparing whip do you lash a slow horse?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know another, swifter setting; if I wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall veil both you and Dawn with my clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and while you are hidden a Night by day shall appear","st":"7.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"as bridal-adorner of Zeus in his haste, so that it may shine"},{"sp":"N","t":"with stars at midday, and the accustomed escort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hesperus, I shall make rise and not set."},{"sp":"N","t":"But dragging your forerunner the Dawnstar toward the west"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to yourself and to me in my longing, and all night","st":"7.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"enjoy your Clymene, and let me come swiftly to Semele."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yoke your chariot for me, light-bearing Selene, you too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a plant-nourishing glitter, since this marriage"},{"sp":"N","t":"foretells the birth of plant-increasing Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rising over the lovely halls of Semele","st":"7.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"shine for me in my longing together with the star of the Cyprus-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lengthen the sweet darkness, chamber-servant of Zeus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the father spoke, which longing knows how to command."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when for him in his haste, stretching an earth-born leap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tapering, all-encompassing cone of mist ran up,","st":"7.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing the wet darkness, counter-shadow of setting Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then airy Zeus passed from the starry house"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the wedlock of Semele, and on untraceable sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping his first bound he measured with his sole the whole"},{"sp":"N","t":"path of the air; and with the second he reached Thebes","st":"7.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a wing or a thought; and as he rushed through the hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bolts of the gateway opened of their own accord."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he embraced Semele in the loving bond of his palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now over the bed bellowing an ox-like echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a horned face upon human limbs,","st":"7.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"an image of like nature to ox-horned Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now he put on the thick-haired shape of a lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time he was a leopard, as one begetting a bold son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the driver of leopards and charioteer of lions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time, his hair girdled beneath the coils of serpents,","st":"7.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom was bound tight with a bond of vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the wine-dark spiraling ivy of his mane,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the plaited ornament of Bacchus; and a certain crooked serpent, creeping,"},{"sp":"N","t":"licked the rosy-skinned neck of the frightened bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"with gentle lips, and going down her breast","st":"7.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled the rounded rim of her firm breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hissing a wedding-hymn, pouring forth the sweet honey"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the well-hived bee, not the deadly venom of a viper."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus lingered over the marriage, and as beside a neighboring wine-vat"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crashed the Euian cry, begetting a Euoi-loving son;","st":"7.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fixed love-maddened mouth to mouth, and, bubbling up"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely nectar, the husband made Semele drunk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might bear a child, scepter-bearer of the nectarous harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the messenger of things to come, lifting the care-forgetting cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning his heavy arm upon a torch-bearing fennel-stalk;","st":"7.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time he lifted a thyrsus much-entwined with wine-dark ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the skin of a deer; and in the left arm of the woman-mad bearer"},{"sp":"N","t":"the many-patterned fawnskin was shaken."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the whole earth laughed, and with self-grown leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"a vine-orchard ran round the bed of Semele,","st":"7.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the walls sprouted the flowers of a dewy meadow"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the begetting of Bromius, and upon the cloudless bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus within the chamber crashed with thunderous din,"},{"sp":"N","t":"foretelling the drums of night-celebrated Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the bed he embraced Semele with loving speech,","st":"7.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"comforting his bride with hopes of things to come:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I am, woman, Cronides, your bridegroom; lift up your proud neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"joined to a heavenly husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but seek no measures greater than a mortal birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"The marriage of Danae does not rival yours; but even","st":"7.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"your father's sister's wedlock of the bovine Loves"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have eclipsed; for Europa, glorying in the bed of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came to Crete, but Semele comes to Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"What more would you wish, beyond the upper air and the vault of the stars?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And some day someone will say that Cronides gave honor","st":"7.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Minos below and to Dionysus in heaven."},{"sp":"N","t":"But after the mortal son of Autonoe and the offspring of Ino,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one fallen to his own hounds, the other destined to die"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the winged missile of a child-slaying father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the short-lived son of raging Agave,","st":"7.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring forth an imperishable son, and I shall call you immortal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"blessed, because for gods and men you shall bring forth a joy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having conceived a son who makes forgetful of mortal distress.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book VIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The eighth, of shifting tale, holds the savage envy of Hera, and the fiery marriage of Semele, and Zeus the slayer."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the god went to Olympus; but in his high-roofed","st":"8.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"hall he kept his mind wandering near the bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the sting of Thebes more than of the upper air; for the house"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Semele was a lovely heaven for Cronides, and about the bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-footed Seasons of Zeus were handmaids of Cadmus.","st":"8.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"And by the bridal drop of the wedlock fallen from Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the belly of Semele swelled, pressed by its burden;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in witness of the birth of garland-loving Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she delighted her heart with a wreath, and on her flower-tressed head"},{"sp":"N","t":"she plaited the self-coiling ivy of the raving hair,","st":"8.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a prophetess of the Bassarids, and to brides to come"},{"sp":"N","t":"near their childbed she gave the late name of ivy."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bearing the heavy burden of the god-begotten birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if ever some old shepherd made melody on his pipe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the song of neighboring, field-loving Echo,","st":"8.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a single tunic she passed out of the chamber with a raving rush;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the mountain-roaming din of the double flute was heard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping unshod from the high-roofed hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"she marched self-bidden to the ridge of the lonely wood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if a cymbal clashed, she whirled with the beat of her feet,","st":"8.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping a curving step with slanting sandal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if she heard a long-horned bull bellowing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bellowed from her throat an answering mimicry of the ox;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often beneath a shepherd's ridge with raving voice"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sang a song in company with Pan and Echo was in concert,","st":"8.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"and answering the pastoral din of the horned flute"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bent her step into the dance; and the unborn, intelligent child"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped along with his mother in leaps within her womb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a flute-mad mimicry, and a self-taught song"},{"sp":"N","t":"the half-finished infant sounded, pouring an echo beneath her bosom.","st":"8.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"So he grew in the burden of a male-bearing belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the messenger of gladness, an intelligent infant; and about the boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Seasons, handmaids of Cronion, garlanded heaven."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Envy, watching the bed of high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the birth-pang of Semele's god-begotten labor,","st":"8.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"received jealousy of Bacchus even while he was within the belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-suffering, loveless, struck by his own venom."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with cunning mind he devised a crooked plan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the lying shape of Ares his counterpart"},{"sp":"N","t":"in mimic armor, and as though with a trail of blood","st":"8.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"he inscribed the back of his ox-hide shield with a drug-colored flower"},{"sp":"N","t":"in manufactured drops, and as if from slain men"},{"sp":"N","t":"dipping his cheating fingers in matching red ochre"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reddened his hands with counterfeit, reddening gore;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he belched from his throat the din of nine thousand,","st":"8.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring from dreadful mouths a man-breaking voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with mind-stealing whispers he dismayed Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and goaded envious Hera still more into wrath;"},{"sp":"N","t":"both he provoked; and with such words he chided them:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Seek yourself another new bridegroom in the upper air, Hera,","st":"8.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, since Semele has snatched yours, for the sake of whose bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging the bridal soil of seven-gated Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he refuses the seven-zoned heaven; and instead of you"},{"sp":"N","t":"he delights to hold in his arms an earthly pregnant bride."},{"sp":"N","t":"Where has my mother's jealousy gone? Has even the wrath","st":"8.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Hera herself turned womanish before Semele's wedlock?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where are the stings of your unsparing gadfly? No longer in the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"is the sea-terrified heifer driven? No longer does the herdsman"},{"sp":"N","t":"Argus with sleepless, wide-scattered eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"guard the newer beds of marriage-thieving Cronides?","st":"8.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"But what is this Olympian house to me? Going to the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall leave my father's upper air, and dwelling in my own"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thrace I shall not look upon the pains of my grieving mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor Zeus the marriage-thief; and if ever he comes"},{"sp":"N","t":"to my land, desiring a Bistonian girl,","st":"8.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shall know what Ares is when he is angry; for having"},{"sp":"N","t":"my death-bringing spear, destroyer of the Titans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall drive woman-mad Cronion out of Thrace;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking as my excuse that he drags a maiden to his bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be self-bidden avenger of my own bed,","st":"8.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, consorting with earth-born weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has filled the many-patterned upper air with his loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"May heaven be gracious, a house of mortals; do I walk the axle?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Callisto whirls through Olympus, where shines"},{"sp":"N","t":"the circle that bears the name of the high-crested Arcadian Bear.","st":"8.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"I hate the course of the seven-pathed Pleiad; for in Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Electra harries me, flashing together with Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"Now why are you at rest? The son of Leto still in the womb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo, you afflicted, and do you not trouble Dionysus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hephaestus, midwife of Tritogeneia at her birth,","st":"8.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-bearing Zeus shall deliver the son of a bastard wife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laboring with another, higher birth in his male thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there is no more need of your ox-axe. Yield, Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cease to proclaim the childbed rim of the head of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because Dionysus puts to shame the wise birth-pang","st":"8.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the head that perfects generation, because from an earthly stock"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shall be self-delivered, Olympian, just as Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding the boast of motherless Pallas. But I myself too"},{"sp":"N","t":"am ashamed far more, whenever some mortal says:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Zeus gave strife to Ares and gladness to Dionysus.'","st":"8.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, leaving the vault of Cronides to his bastard children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go as a migrant from heaven; and let water-fixed"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ister receive its scepter-bearer in his wandering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"before I see here Ganymede, cup-bearer of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-haired herdsman, after Pergamum a citizen of Olympus,","st":"8.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"handing the untouched cup of heavenly Hebe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"before I see Semele and Bacchus as dwellers in Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the star-roaming crown of earthly Ariadne"},{"sp":"N","t":"running with Helios, companion of the dawn."},{"sp":"N","t":"There I shall stay, lest I see the Sea-Monster, or the sickle of Perseus,","st":"8.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the pattern of Andromeda, or the eye of the Gorgon Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Cronides may hereafter set in Olympus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and confounded the mind of self-born Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and increased still more the wrath of heavy-jealous Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Envy rushed sharply away, and tossing his crooked knees","st":"8.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"went by slanting paths through the air; and to human"},{"sp":"N","t":"eyes and hearts he sped like smoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arming his Telchinian mind for guile, for evil."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did heavy-wrathful Hera, bedfellow of Zeus, cease;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but darting with tempestuous sandal","st":"8.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"across the many-patterned heaven adorned with well-lit stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"she ran through countless cities on roaming sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking cunning Deceit, if somewhere she might find her."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when high above the Dictaean Corybantic rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"she beheld the childbed water of neighboring Amnisus,","st":"8.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"there the fickle mountain goddess met her;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she always stayed beside the lying tomb of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting in the Cretans, since they were deceivers."},{"sp":"N","t":"And about her flanks flowed a Cydonian girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in which lie all the cunning charms of mortals:","st":"8.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"in it much-devising thievery, in it whispered"},{"sp":"N","t":"cajolery, in it deceits of many kinds, and in it also"},{"sp":"N","t":"the deceitful oath itself, running with the winds of the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And guileful Deceit with guileful speech Hera soothed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the goddess of shifting counsel, defending herself against her husband:","st":"8.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hail, goddess guile-devising, guile-wandering; not even"},{"sp":"N","t":"wily Hermes himself surpasses you in mind-stealing whispers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me too the all-shifting girdle, which once Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"bound about her flanks, until she beguiled her husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not bring Cronion a rocky shape,","st":"8.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor do I cheat my husband with a guileful stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but an earthly woman forces me, for the sake of whose bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Ares, heavy in wrath, refuses to dwell in the upper air."},{"sp":"N","t":"What profit, if I was born an immortal goddess? For a paltry"},{"sp":"N","t":"mortal woman has my husband, whom the goddess Leto did not snatch;","st":"8.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus did not lie a second time beside Danae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but after the seal of the iron-bearing hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride sailed the sea, blaming her golden marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and got as bride-gift of Eros the water of the sea; and in the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chest swam, fellow-sailor with the unsteady winds.","st":"8.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor after Crete did the bull of Olympus sail again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not see Europa after the bed; and, water-dipped,"},{"sp":"N","t":"goaded by the gadfly, horn-bearing Io swam."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did a goddess have a free marriage, but she too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a burden in her belly, ran much-turning, Leto,","st":"8.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"looking restlessly at the feet of the much-wandering islands"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the never-resting flood of the inhospitable sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely saw the free shoot of the olive of her childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Leto toiled so much, and her husband did not help her;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for the bed of a single swift-fated mortal bride","st":"8.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has renounced the couch of his heavenly sister Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest Cronides, my husband and brother, hearing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drive me out of the upper air for the sake of a woman's bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he make Semele queen of his Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you do favor to Zeus Cronion rather than to Hera,","st":"8.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not give me your all-charming girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that my wandering son may come again to Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to the farthest floor of Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the upper air for the sake of mortal weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to share the hearth of first-born Tethys; thence I shall come","st":"8.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the house of Harmonia, and stay near Ophion."},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you, glorifying the all-mother bride of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant me to have the helping girdle, so that I may charm"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Ares, who has fled, to dwell in the upper air a second time.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke, the goddess answered with obedient speech:","st":"8.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Mother of Enyalius, first-throned bride of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give my girdle, and if you command anything more"},{"sp":"N","t":"I obey, because you rule among the gods with Cronion."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive this strap; and binding it tight about your bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"you may bring Ares to heaven; and if you wish,","st":"8.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"charm the mind of Cronides and, if need be, of Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his anger; and leaving the wedlock of earthly Loves"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-ruling Zeus shall come self-bidden to heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his heart bent round by my guileful cestus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this puts to shame the mind-charming cestus of my Paphian.\"","st":"8.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the guile-devising goddess fled with the wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striding through the air on flying sandal."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the shield-brandishing cave of the Dictaean cliff"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the childbed cavern of the goddess who perfects birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"deceitful Hera came to the chamber of Semele,","st":"8.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"puffing with jealousy; and she was shaped in body, like"},{"sp":"N","t":"a honey-tongued old woman, to a child-loving, child-tending"},{"sp":"N","t":"nurse, whom Agenor himself had brought up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and given her an inheritance, and bestowed her as wife upon a man"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a father would; and she in turn, paying the favor of her upbringing,","st":"8.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"had nursed Cadmus, still an infant, at her breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"and embraced the babe Europa in her loving arm."},{"sp":"N","t":"Having a body like hers, Hera passed into the house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"angry with Semele and Cypris and Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"who had not yet seen the light, and beside the newly wed bridal chamber","st":"8.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"she turned her gaze toward the wall opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"averting her eye, lest she see the bed of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Peisianassa seated her high upon a chair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaid of Semele, a shoot of Tyrian stock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thelxinoe fitted coverlets to the gleaming chair.","st":"8.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"There the guile-weaving goddess sat close by; and she found the girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"weighed down with the birth-pang of a ripening labor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the birth, not yet touching the Moon that perfects generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her pale cheek proclaimed, from her unmarked belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pallor of her limbs, wine-dark before; and as she sat,","st":"8.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"the guileful body of lying Hera trembled with an answering"},{"sp":"N","t":"shudder, and she bent downward toward the ground, nodding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an old woman with arched neck and crushed shoulders."},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely did she find a pretext; and she groaned over her words,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having wiped a well-made tear from her face,","st":"8.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and told a guileful tale in a mind-charming voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Tell me, queen, whence are your cheeks pale?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where is that beauty of yours? Who, grudging your looks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has dulled the purple sparks of your face?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And who has changed your roses into swift-fading anemones?","st":"8.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"And why do you waste away, downcast? Have you too"},{"sp":"N","t":"heard those shameful things which the citizens cry aloud?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Away with the ruinous mouth of women who begin evils."},{"sp":"N","t":"But tell me, do not hide the plunderer of your girdle:"},{"sp":"N","t":"which of the gods defiled you? Who snatched your maidenhood?","st":"8.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"If Ares in secret has wedded my girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lain beside Semele, sparing not Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him come to your bed handling his bridal spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your mother knows her own steadfast father."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if swift-sandaled Hermes reveled to you as bridegroom","st":"8.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for Semele's beauty disowned his own Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him give his rod, self-announcer of your bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or let him adorn you with his golden sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a gift worthy of your couch, so that you too"},{"sp":"N","t":"may be gold-sandaled, just as Hera, bedfellow of Zeus.","st":"8.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"But if from heaven fair Apollo came to you as husband"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for love of Semele has forgotten Daphne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"apart from hidden guile let him dance to you through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate, riding a chariot of never-silent swans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing his lyre as bride-gift of your love,","st":"8.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"a faithful token of his bridal chamber; for looking on it"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus will recognize the heavenly lyre of Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which he saw, varied in voice, beside the dinner of his own table"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing the earthly wedding of Harmonia."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if the dark-haired god, mad for women, forced you,","st":"8.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"and preferred you to wise Melanippe of whom song tells,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him revel openly, and beside the gateway of Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him fix the point of his bridal trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharing an equal prize beside viper-tressed Dirce,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as among the Argives beside lion-feeding Lerna","st":"8.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set a sign of his marriage with Amymone, where still"},{"sp":"N","t":"a place of the Lernaean bride bears the name of the trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"But why do I call you bedfellow of the Earth-shaker?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What tokens of Poseidon's bed do you have?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Poured out with watery hands he embraced Tyro,","st":"8.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mimic Enipeus bubbling in a guileful stream."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if also, as you say, your bridegroom is Cronion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him come to your bed with his lovely thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed with bridal lightning, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The thunderbolts are bride-adorners of Hera and of Semele.'","st":"8.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"Jealous though she is, the wife of Zeus will not trouble you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for your mother's Ares would not allow it."},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed is Europa more than Semele, whom on his shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"horned Zeus lifted; and the hoof of the desire-stricken bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran unwetted through the surface of the water,","st":"8.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and so great a god was the boat of Eros. Ah, great wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden drove the charioteer of the upper air."},{"sp":"N","t":"I count Danae blessed more than Semele, through whose bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"golden rainy Zeus flowed down from the roof"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a wealthy drop of woman-mad snow;","st":"8.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"the most blessed bride did not ask for golden gifts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she had as bride-gift of Eros her whole husband. But let there be"},{"sp":"N","t":"silence between us, lest your father Cadmus hear.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, she left the house and the bride still grieving,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having jealousy of Hera's inimitable wedlock,","st":"8.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming Cronion; and on her returning path"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came within the upper air, and beside the heavenly seat"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the weapons of Zeus lying apart from their bearer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though they could hear, she soothed them with friendly speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Thunder, has my cloud-gathering Zeus abandoned you too?","st":"8.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who has snatched you again and stripped you of your bearer?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thunder, you have been plundered — Typhon is not to blame —"},{"sp":"N","t":"comforter of Hera, having suffered in common with her; for the bride-adorning"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus disowns us both."},{"sp":"N","t":"The earth is no longer sprinkled with snows, and as the moist-poured","st":"8.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"rain fails, the drought of the field grazes upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the furrow, having a useless crop; and by the field-dwellers"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of the black-clouded, Zeus is called the cloudless."},{"sp":"N","t":"Lightnings, break forth a fiery voice against Cronion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"against woman-mad Zeus, my friends, speak out, thunderbolts.","st":"8.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, avengers of the heavy-jealous griefs of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go to Semele as bride-adorners, and as bride-gifts of her girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"let her, entreating, receive her flaming murderers.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Hera cried to the voiceless weapons of Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in grief, and the goddess surged with envious wrath.","st":"8.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Semele, heavy-bound in her newly grieving heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for the lightning, the fire-bright escort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with reproaching lips entreated her husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wishing for the pattern of Hera's fire-crowned bed:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"By the streaming-wealthy wedlock of Danae I beg you,","st":"8.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant me a favor, horned husband of Europa; for I am ashamed"},{"sp":"N","t":"to call you Semele's husband, whom I saw as in a dream."},{"sp":"N","t":"Acrisius is more blessed than Cadmus; but I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"would have wished, had I seen a golden marriage, rainy Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the mother of Perseus had not snatched this prize of yours;","st":"8.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would have wished that you had carried me in the waters, a traveling bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on your shoulders, so that he too might wander,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my brother Polydorus, seeking the ravisher of a wandering bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Cadmus did, my Cronion, my bearer."},{"sp":"N","t":"But what is the pattern of a bovine marriage or of a snowfall to me?","st":"8.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not wish an equal prize, which an earthly bride got."},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave the bull to Europa, allow Danae the pouring of rain;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only the envy of Hera's marriage holds me. If you honor me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorn my bridal chamber with your heavenly fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting the lovely radiance of the clouds, and show the lightning","st":"8.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"as bride-gift of my love to disobedient Agave;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Autonoe shudder beside my neighboring chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the thunderous song of the bride-adorning Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-proclaimed symbol of your unheralded bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And grant that I may embrace the dear flame and delight my heart","st":"8.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching the lightning and handling the thunderbolts."},{"sp":"N","t":"Give me the yoking flame of your bridal chamber; every bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"has a torch as escort of the wedding that perfects marriage."},{"sp":"N","t":"Am I not worthy of your bridal thunderbolts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the blood of Ares and of your own Aphrodite?","st":"8.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"Wretched me; the marriage of Semele has a swift-perishing fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"and earthly lamps, but grasping the thunderbolt"},{"sp":"N","t":"and touching the lightning your Hera is wedded."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bridegroom who delights in the thunderbolt, you in a many-lit chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having your inspired form, come to the couch of Hera,","st":"8.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"shining upon your bride with bridal lightnings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery Zeus, but to Semele you come as a serpent or a bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she hears the heavy-thundering Olympian sound of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Semele, in the shadowy pattern of a shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hears the bastard bellowing of a lying bull;","st":"8.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"noiseless, cloudless Zeus comes down to my bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the cloud-gatherer couples with high-necked Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And my father, fleeing the shameful things of his ill-wedded daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Cadmus, within the house shuns the path of men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ashamed to appear to the dwellers, because the citizens","st":"8.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"all mock at your secret wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming Semele, because she has a stolen husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"A fine bride-gift you have given me, the reproaches of women;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the chorus of handmaids blames me, and beyond the others"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear the greedy mouth of my never-silent nurse.","st":"8.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"Remember who, weaving a guile-minded doom for Typhon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave you back the spark of the stolen thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"show my father what he gave; for aged"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus demands of me a token of your bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet have I seen the true face of Cronion,","st":"8.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the light-bearing ray of your eyelids, nor perceived"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sparklings of your face and your flashing beard;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet have I seen your Olympian form, but I watch"},{"sp":"N","t":"a leopard or a lion, and I have not seen a god as husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"As a mortal I look upon you, though I am about to bear a god.","st":"8.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have heard of another flaming marriage; for Helios"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bridal fire embraced the bride Clymene.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, asking for her own doom; for equal with Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"the short-lived bride hoped to behold at her marriage"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gentle spark of a calm thunderbolt.","st":"8.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father, hearing, reproached the envious Fates,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pitied Semele, untimely; and concerning Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he knew the cunning wrath of implacable Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he commanded Hermes to snatch from the flaming thunderbolt"},{"sp":"N","t":"the new son of fire-struck Thyone.","st":"8.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the father spoke some words to the high-necked girl:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"O woman, truly the envious mind of Hera has beguiled you with guiles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you really think, woman, that thunderbolts are gentle?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Endure to wait another time, while you still lift your burden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure to wait another time, until you bring forth my son;","st":"8.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not before the birth demand of me the fiery murderers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not wielding the lightning did I plunder the maidenhood of Danae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the clangor of thunder, and it was not thunderbolts that wedded"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wedlock of your Tyrian bride Europa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Inachian heifer did not see the radiance; but you alone,","st":"8.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mortal, demand of me what the goddess Leto did not ask.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such words he told, and did not long to strive"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the threads of the Fates; and through the bosom of the upper air"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing he was carried, and the supplication of his bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"unwilling her husband fulfilled, Zeus who rouses the lightning,","st":"8.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"and danced toward Semele, stretching with downcast hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridal sparks of the marriage-robbing thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the chamber shone with lightnings, and with the vapor of fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ismenus blazed, and all Thebes sparkled."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Semele, seeing her flaming murderers,","st":"8.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted her proud neck and spoke with high-minded voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I need no shrill-sounding harp, there is no need of a flute;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunders are for me the pipes of the Loves of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this Olympian din is my flute, and the radiance"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the heavenly lightning is the torch of my bridal chamber; for paltry","st":"8.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"torches I care nothing; the thunderbolts are my torches."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am the wife of Cronion, Agave is Echion's,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let them call Autonoe the wife of Aristaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ino has Nephele, Semele got Hera as fellow-wife."},{"sp":"N","t":"I was not the wife of Athamas, nor did I bear","st":"8.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-fated Actaeon, hound-torn, pasture-mate of the wood."},{"sp":"N","t":"I need no lesser lyre; for the heavenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"starry Lyre sings the wedding of Semele.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke exulting, and wished to handle with her hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroying lightning, and, sparing not for Fate,","st":"8.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"with daring palm she touched the murderous thunderbolts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the marriage of Semele was death-bearing, and by a single ordinance"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Erinys made the bridal chamber both pyre and tomb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with childbed rays the bridal breath of the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Zeus spared not, turned the whole bride to ash;","st":"8.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lightning was midwife, and the thunderbolts Eileithyia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he leapt through the burning bosom of his mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavenly flame, sparing him, delivered Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the wedlock withered in the mother-slaying spark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the infant, born out of due month in an unharmed birth,","st":"8.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunderbolts washed with sparing breaths;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Semele, having beheld her fiery end,"},{"sp":"N","t":"perished delighting in her childbed doom; and one could see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Desire, Eileithyia, the Erinyes in a single bridal chamber."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the half-finished infant, its limbs bathed in heavenly fire,","st":"8.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes brought for his father to bring to birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus, turning aside the mind of heavy-jealous Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothed the savage mass of her threat, unloosed again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought up flaming Semele, a migrant to the vault of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to have heaven as her home, the mother of Bacchus,","st":"8.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharing the hearth of the dwellers in the upper air, as the kin of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the offspring of Harmonia from Ares, from Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having bathed her new body in the pure blazing fire &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"she got an imperishable Olympian life; and instead of Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the earthly floor and Autonoe and Agave","st":"8.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"she found Artemis sharing her throne and consorted with Athena"},{"sp":"N","t":"and received the vault as bride-gift, touching a single table"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Zeus and Hermes and Ares and Cytherea."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book IX"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Look at the ninth and you will see the son of Maia and the daughters of Lamus and Mystis and the running of Ino."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father, receiving Dionysus, the steersman of Semele's","st":"9.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"flaming womb, half-finished, leaping through the childbed thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sewed him into his male thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and awaited the gleam of the Moon that perfects birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the palm of Cronides, pilot of the childbed,","st":"9.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"was itself the midwife of the much-stitched labor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosing the birth-pain threads of the child-bearing thigh."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as Zeus labored the rim of his thigh turned womanish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a child born out of due month was brought forth by a motherless ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passing from a female belly into a male belly.","st":"9.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"Him, as he peeped up from the god-begotten birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the childbed Seasons crowned with a wreath of ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heralds of things to come, and on his flower-tressed head"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the crooked coils of well-horned serpents"},{"sp":"N","t":"they girdled bull-natured Dionysus the horned.","st":"9.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"And him, about the childbed hill within Dracanus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes son of Maia took in the hollow of his arm"},{"sp":"N","t":"and flew from the air; and to Lyaeus at his birth"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave his father's name for the birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling him Dionysus, because, lifting the burden on his foot,","st":"9.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cronides went limping with his heavy-laden thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because in the Syracusan tongue a lame man is called nysos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they named the newly born god Eiraphiotes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because his father sewed him into his fair-laboring thigh."},{"sp":"N","t":"And him, darting from the unwashed childbed,","st":"9.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"his kinsman Hermes lifted in his arm, a tearless boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the infant, an image of the well-horned nature of Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave to the daughters of Lamus, the river Nymphs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to tend the grape-tressed child of Zeus; and they, taking"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, embraced him, and into the child's mouth each","st":"9.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushed the milky moisture of her unpressed breasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the child, stretching his eye toward heaven on the opposite path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay on his back sleepless, and with alternating rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"kicking the air delighted in the twofold beat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and surveyed the unaccustomed vault, and in wonder","st":"9.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughed, gazing at his father's rim of stars."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beholding the infant, the bride of Zeus was lashed;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[32,25],[42,43]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and the daughters of Lamus, by the wrath of heavy-wrathful Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were driven to frenzy by the whip of divine malice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the house they assailed the servant-women, and at the crossroads","st":"9.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"they carved the wayfaring man with a stranger-slaying knife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"dreadful they shrieked, and with whirling rush"},{"sp":"N","t":"they rolled the eyes of their disordered faces;"},{"sp":"N","t":"everywhere, this way and that, with mind-wandering purposes"},{"sp":"N","t":"they ran with the rolling leap of unsteady feet;","st":"9.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the raving breezes drove their locks in Bacchic frenzy into the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"straying; and about the breast of each girl the saffron"},{"sp":"N","t":"tunic was whitened with a foam-tressed drop."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now, in the mind-changing wave of roaming madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they would have carved up Bacchus, still an infant, with the knife,","st":"9.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Hermes, stealing Bacchus away on the thievish sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"of an unmarked foot, had not snatched him again, winged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, holding the infant just rescued in his life-sustaining bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought him to the childbed house of newly delivered Ino."},{"sp":"N","t":"She was lifting the boy, still an infant, who had leapt forth","st":"9.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"from her childbed, Melicertes at her arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with child-tending palms; and her breasts were swelling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing the dew of pressed milk."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with loving lips the god soothed the lady,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a divine word from his oracular throat:","st":"9.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Receive, woman, a new son, and lay in your bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"the child of your sister Semele, whom beside the bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"the radiance of the lightning did not wholly reduce to dust, nor did"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother-slaying sparks of the thunderbolt themselves harm him."},{"sp":"N","t":"And let the infant be kept in a shadowy house,","st":"9.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let neither the daytime eye of Phaethon nor the nightly"},{"sp":"N","t":"eye of Selene behold him within the hollow hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, as he plays, even though she is called ox-eyed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let jealous heavy-wrathful Hera see him, veiled."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive the son of your sister; and to you Cronion","st":"9.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall give rewards for his nursing worthy of your toils."},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed among all the daughters of Cadmus have you become;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already Semele has been subdued by the flaming missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Autonoe the earth will hide together with her dead son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cithaeron shall raise a single tomb for them both,","st":"9.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mountain-roaming Agave shall behold the doom"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Pentheus perishing, touching the counterfeit dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"become a child-slayer who leaves her city; but you alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall be exultant, dweller of so great a sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging your house for that of Poseidon, and in the sea","st":"9.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Thetis, like Galatea, you shall be called Ino of the waters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cithaeron shall not hide you in an earthly hollow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but you become one of the Nereids; and instead of Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with better hope you shall call Nereus your father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"living with your child, with immortal Melicertes,","st":"9.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leucothea, holding the key of the poured-out calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ruling over fair sailing after Aeolus; and in fair weather"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trade-loving sailor at sea shall sail trusting in you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having set up a single altar to the Earth-shaker and to Melicertes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sacrificing to both; and of the chariot of the sea","st":"9.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-haired god shall receive Palaemon as charioteer.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, Hermes ran untraceable to heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling in the air the windy soles of his sandals."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Ino did not disobey, but with a purpose of tender love"},{"sp":"N","t":"she embraced motherless Bacchus in her child-tending arm,","st":"9.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spreading in her arm the double pair of children"},{"sp":"N","t":"she held out a double breast to Palaemon and to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she gave the infant into the keeping of her handmaid, the lady Mystis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-haired Sidonian Mystis, whom while still a girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"father Cadmus had brought up as chamber-servant of Ino;","st":"9.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"she then, taking Bacchus from the god-nourishing breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"locked him unforeseen in a gloomy pit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a self-proclaiming gleam, announcing the childbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Zeus, flashed, shining from his face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the gloomy walls of the hall were whitened,","st":"9.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the light of Dionysus, who may not be gazed upon, hid the darkness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Ino sat all night beside Bromius as he played;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often Melicertes, leaping up unsteadily,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew with answering lips the neighboring teat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping alongside Dionysus as he babbled Euoi.","st":"9.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Mystis nursed the god after the breast of her queen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sitting beside Lyaeus with sleepless eyes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the prudent handmaid, with an art that bears the name of Mystis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being taught the rites of night-celebrated Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and preparing the sleepless initiation for Lyaeus,","st":"9.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"first shook the rattle, and clashed for Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cymbals, whirling them, resounding with double bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first, having kindled the night-danced flame of the pine-torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed the Euian cry for unsleeping Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first, having plucked the curving flower with its cluster,","st":"9.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled her unplaited hair with a bond of vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she herself plaited the thyrsus yoked with wine-dark ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and on the topmost cluster she clamped the iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"hidden by leaves, so that it might not tear Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she devised the fastening of bronze bowls upon","st":"9.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"naked breasts, and skins of fawns upon the loins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, teaching the mystic chest pregnant with the most holy rite"},{"sp":"N","t":"as playthings to Dionysus as he played,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she first fastened about her skin the viperish strap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entwined, and a coiling serpent about her double girdle,","st":"9.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"circling its knots, was bent in a snaky bond."},{"sp":"N","t":"And him, beneath the seal of the many-locked hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-seeing Hera saw with her unerring eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept in the recess of the unspoken mystic house;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she swore by the nether water of Styx, avenging late,","st":"9.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"to deluge the house of Ino with every kind of malice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now she would have destroyed the offspring of Zeus; but Hermes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"snatching him, carried him to the ridge of the Cybelian wood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swift-sandaled Hera ran after on fair-footed sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady from on high; but he outstripped the running of Hera,","st":"9.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and put on the endless shape of first-born Phanes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hera, revering the first-sown god, gave way,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the lying rays of his face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did she perceive the guile-weaving image of the counterfeit shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and passing over the mountain floor with lighter feet,","st":"9.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing in his entwining hands the horn-bearing son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave him to lion-feeding Rhea, the mother of Zeus his father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spoke some words to the goddess of noblest travail:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Receive, goddess, the new son of your Zeus, who after his contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the war of the Indians will come after the earth to the vault of the stars,","st":"9.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"a great favor to angry Hera; for it was not fitting"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Ino should have as nursling him whom Cronides bore;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the mother of Zeus be the nurse of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being mother of Zeus and nurse of his grandson.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, swift-kneed Hermes went to heaven,","st":"9.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"circling his wind-swift wing with the nimble breezes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and exchanging the surpassing form of self-born Phanes"},{"sp":"N","t":"he put on again his own ancient shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving ever-growing Bacchus to the child-tending mother."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess tended him, and while he was still a boy","st":"9.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"she set him as mounter of a chariot of raw-devouring lions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the nimble Corybants within the god-receiving court"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled Dionysus with a child-tending dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clashed their swords, and with alternating rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck their shields with tumbling iron,","st":"9.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away the boyhood of growing Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the child, hearing the nursing echo of the shields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grew with the fatherly care of the Corybants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the youth of nine years, holding the beast-slaying hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with swifter feet outstripped the running of the hare,","st":"9.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with infant hand pursuing the deer-driving valor"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted a dappled fawn, hanging it on his neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and holding a bold shifting-backed tiger stretched upon his shoulder"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted it up on high outside any bond; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding cubs in his hands he showed them to his mother Rhea,","st":"9.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"having snatched the young children from the many-milked breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dragging dreadful lions still living"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched them out as gifts to his mother, that she might yoke them to her wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing both the feet of the yoked pair in his two palms."},{"sp":"N","t":"And in wonder, with rejoicing laughter, Rhea looked upon","st":"9.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"the manliness and the contests of newborn Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing Iobacchus the driver of grim lions"},{"sp":"N","t":"father Cronion laughed with delighted eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Euius cloaked his skin with shaggy tunics,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his boyhood bloom just finished,","st":"9.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing on his shoulders a dappled covering of deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a mimic shifting pattern of the stars of the upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and driving lynxes beneath the floor of Phrygia into the folds"},{"sp":"N","t":"he yoked his wagon with spotted leopards,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though honoring the likeness of his father's floors;","st":"9.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, riding the chariot of immortal Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding in his little soft-skinned hand the circles of the bit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reined back the swift chariot of the hurrying lions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, growing in his heart the boldness of high-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched his right hand to the mouth of a raging bear,","st":"9.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting his fearless fingers into the dreadful jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his boyish fingers; and the gentle beast stood still,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting her mouth as a slave to infant Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kissed the fingers of Bacchus with sharp-toothed kisses."},{"sp":"N","t":"So he grew beside crag-loving Rhea,","st":"9.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"still a boy fresh in bloom, mountain-reared. And about the rocks"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pans circled the dance-beating son of Thyone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about the cliffs on shaggy-shinned feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi for Bacchus; and as they whirled in the dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"the goat's hoof rattled with the leap of their feet.","st":"9.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Semele in Olympus, still breathing of the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted her proud neck and spoke with high-minded voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hera, you have been plundered; the offspring of Semele is better;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus brought forth my son and became mother in my place,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the father sowed and bore what he sowed, and with a self-bearing","st":"9.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"counterfeit belly bore the child, and changed his nature by necessity."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus is mightier than Enyalius; for your Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"he only sowed and did not beget in his thigh."},{"sp":"N","t":"Thebes has hidden the fame of Ortygia; for heavenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leto, pursued, bore Apollo in secret;","st":"9.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leto bore Phoebus, and Cronion did not labor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maia bore Hermes, and the husband did not give birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but openly the father bore my son. Ah, great wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold Dionysus in the arms of her who bore you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying in a child-tending arm; and the steward","st":"9.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the ever-flowing cosmos, the first-sown beginning of the gods,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the all-mother, has become the nurse of Bromius; for to infant"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus she held out the breast which high-ruling Zeus once drew."},{"sp":"N","t":"Which Cronides labored, which Rhea nursed Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your child? But Cybele, who is called your mother,","st":"9.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore Zeus and reared Bacchus in a single bosom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lifted both, the son and the father."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor would fatherless Hephaestus rival the offspring of Semele,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsown by a father, whom self-begetting Hera bore"},{"sp":"N","t":"limping on the unequal sole of his thin feet,","st":"9.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away the unfinished childbed of his mother."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not wholly like Semele was Maia, whose son Hermes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"guileful in appearance, armed like Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"deceived Hera, until he drew the milk of her breasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yield to me; for Semele alone got a husband","st":"9.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"who both sowed and labored with the same offspring."},{"sp":"N","t":"Most blessed is Semele for the sake of her son; for our"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus shall come without guile to the chorus of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling in his father's upper air, because of so great"},{"sp":"N","t":"a goddess he received the milk of the god-nourishing breast;","st":"9.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shall come self-bidden to heaven, and has no need"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Hera's milk, having milked a better breast.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke exulting even in the upper air; and the angry"},{"sp":"N","t":"wife of Zeus dismayed migrant Ino,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen, falling heavily upon the hall of Athamas,","st":"9.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"enraged at Dionysus while he was still a boy."},{"sp":"N","t":"Fleeing from her chamber ill-wedded Ino ran through,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshod, skipping over the rough hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushing after the track of unheralded Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and roaming, the lady went through mountain after mountain,","st":"9.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"until she entered torrent-cleft Delphic Pytho;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely did she bend her step beside the serpent-feeding thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"quivering uncontrollably; and down her bare breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"tearing her tunic, the herald of mourning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was carried along in dreadful madness; and hearing the outlandish","st":"9.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"wail of the mind-wandering lady the shepherd trembled."},{"sp":"N","t":"Often upon the divine seat entwined with the tripod"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fitted a thrice-coiled serpent in spirals with her squalid hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and binding it tight about her slender head"},{"sp":"N","t":"she girdled her long hair in a serpent bond;","st":"9.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she pursued the maiden envoys. Not then was there libation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor public sacrifice, nor beside the temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"did a Delphian man dance; and with the limb-devouring tendrils"},{"sp":"N","t":"of long-plaited ivy the women were lashed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the hunter, seeing mountain-running Ino, shrank away,","st":"9.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the linen guile of the stakes; and the goatherd"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove his goats beneath the fold of the high-crested rock of lairs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and driving his sweating oxen beneath the yoke-band"},{"sp":"N","t":"the old plowman shuddered at the leaps of Ino."},{"sp":"N","t":"And binding tight the strange-voiced echo of her earthly cry","st":"9.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"the oracular Pythian girl ran through the mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having shaken on her head the accustomed laurel of Panopeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entering the peaks of the deep-glened cliff"},{"sp":"N","t":"she dwelt in the Delphic cave in fear of raging Ino."},{"sp":"N","t":"But rushing through the haunts of the many-winding wood","st":"9.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"she did not escape all-seeing Apollo; near the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"in pity he came swiftly, and changing his shape into a mortal"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came near the lady, and the topmost body of Ino"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sparing palms he entwined with wise laurel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought sweet sleep upon her; and with ambrosia","st":"9.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"he anointed all the skin of sleepy mourning Ino,"},{"sp":"N","t":"moistening her maddened limbs with a toil-releasing drop."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she stayed there for a time within the Parnassian wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the fourth year, and beside the oracular rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"she set up dances of Bacchus while he was still an infant","st":"9.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the prophecies of Phoebus; and with sleepless pine-torches"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Corycian Bacchants passed to the fragrant rites,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with most holy palms gathered drugs"},{"sp":"N","t":"that ward off madness and healed the woman."},{"sp":"N","t":"At the call of Athamas his attendants were wanderers","st":"9.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking everywhere; and the servant-women too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mountain-wandering, marched in much-turning journeys"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the roaming track of the queen whose fate was unknown,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering beyond reach; and of the grief-loving women"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hateful nail scored the reddening cheek,","st":"9.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they armed willing fingers against their rosy breasts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sending much noise with wailings through the city"},{"sp":"N","t":"the house shrieked, crammed with mourning distress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and more still did Mystis of shifting counsel receive the necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and had a double pain, for wretched Ino still wandering,","st":"9.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"the much-enduring, and for Dionysus snatched away."},{"sp":"N","t":"But king Athamas did not lament his plaintive bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaving forgotten the longing for unheralded Ino,"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the bed of his earlier bride Nephele, mother of twins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he passed to the soft couch of deep-girdled Themisto,","st":"9.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leading the daughter of Hypseus into a third wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cast off love for Ino; and like a nurse playing daintily,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending him aloft in the air with a high-pathed whirl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted Melicertes babbling, comforting him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to him weeping he held out a male breast in place","st":"9.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the milk-bearing teat, and checked his longing for his mother."},{"sp":"N","t":"And from the bed of Athamas Themisto reared"},{"sp":"N","t":"sons well-corseleted, defenders against Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Schoeneus and Leucon, a new stock of brave men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with first-born pangs; and after both the mother","st":"9.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore twin births of a common, single childbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Porphyreon, and reared at her rich breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ptous, the boyish shoot of evil-averting youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"both late-born and of one age, whom once their mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Themisto crushed as if they were the children of a stepmother,","st":"9.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"thinking them the twofold race of Ino blessed in fair children."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book X"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the tenth, the madness of Athamas and the running of Ino, how she fled into the swell of the sea with newborn Melicertes."},{"sp":"N","t":"So she became a murderous, child-slaying mother","st":"10.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"in her madness; but the father of the children, beneath a witnessing vengeance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he kept Themisto, the destroyer of his offspring, as sharer of his hearth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athamas, goaded by the maddening whip of Pan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came into the middle of the flock, and lashing them as though servants","st":"10.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursued the innocent flocks of woolly sheep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one he lifted, as though she were his yoke-mate bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"together with the twin infants at her fresh-milked breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking a goat; and he clamped her shaggy feet in a twofold"},{"sp":"N","t":"bond; and loosing the circling girdle beside her loins","st":"10.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lashed the body of the lying Ino as she was bound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not perceiving the counterfeit shape. And always within his hearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the din of the whip of Pan, son of Cronus, boomed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often, unsteady, he leapt up from his seat"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving in his fearful ears the breath of serpents.","st":"10.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thickly he stretched his bow, and drew an arrow on the circling string"},{"sp":"N","t":"against an empty target, striking the unwounded air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the snaky likeness of the Tartarean goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"he quivered, fearing the many-colored phantoms of her shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting snowy foam, the witness of madness,","st":"10.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling his drunken, threatening eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as he watched with much-wandering rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"his eyes reddened; and through his temple the thin"},{"sp":"N","t":"membranes of his unsteady head were whirled."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the third portion of his soul was lost; for the unerring","st":"10.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"purposes of his witless brain were turned aside,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with tottering spirals the eyes of the goaded man"},{"sp":"N","t":"were driven to frenzy; and his hair strayed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken about the back of his unshorn head."},{"sp":"N","t":"And his mouth stammered, and loosing his lips to the air","st":"10.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sent forth the strange-voiced echo of meaningless words."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the breezes of the Eumenides snatched away the mortal"},{"sp":"N","t":"cares of life, and his tongue was weighed down with raving voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gazing in spirals beneath the whirl of the face"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the strange-natured counterfeit form of Megaera, who may not be gazed upon,","st":"10.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad Athamas was shaken with a mind-changing throb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the grim hand of the mind-robbing goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"he longed to snatch the viperish whip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and baring his knife against the temple of the Erinys"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wished to cut the snaky curls of Tisiphone.","st":"10.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"And with empty whispers he conversed with the neighboring wall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the shadowy, thievish image of the shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Artemis, and seeing the light phantom with his eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came to a desire for the hunt through the answering phantoms."},{"sp":"N","t":"And late, after the fourth year, Ino of the varied tears","st":"10.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"came home returning; and the lady, watching"},{"sp":"N","t":"both her goaded husband and Themisto with her male children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received a double pain. And her husband did not know her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beholding his bedfellow Ino recovered after so long;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, swift-kneed, having a longing for the deer-driving hunt,","st":"10.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"he darted to the peaks on tempestuous sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing his son as a horn-bearing beast; and holding"},{"sp":"N","t":"his straight-stretched bow he leapt untraceable upon Learchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"thinking him a high-antlered stag in lying shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a beast in his limbs; but he, fleeing,","st":"10.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"flew in terror, tossing his swifter knees."},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing with raging hands the wind-swift arrow"},{"sp":"N","t":"the father fettered his young son with the child-slaying missile;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he crushed with his knife the unrecognized head"},{"sp":"N","t":"turned to a fawn's by the phantom; and touching the tip","st":"10.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the bloody chin of the unmarked face he laughed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"handling it as though a beast, and ran with a leap of madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while his son, unburied Learchus, was still gasping,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the mother, and rolling his whirling eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did any of the attendants come near; and roaming","st":"10.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"he passed through the seven-chambered room on swift sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling his son, whom he had killed. And in the hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"beholding infant Melicertes newly brought in,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting a fire-breathing cauldron on the hearth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he put his son in the middle; and as the fire was kindled","st":"10.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bloody cauldron bubbled with watery vapor."},{"sp":"N","t":"Crying \"Papa\" his child shrieked, nor did any"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the attendants help him; but the storm-swift mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"snatching him half-burnt, scorched with fire, from the cauldron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wind-footed Ino ran with roaming leaps;","st":"10.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting the dust of the White plain"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was called Leucothea, bearing the name; and from the hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athamas in dreadful madness, tossing his windy knees,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursued in vain swifter mountain-running Ino."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when her much-dismayed husband came near her,","st":"10.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unsteady track on tottering foot, then indeed the wretched woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting her drenched foot beside the neighboring sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"roared with plaintive voice to her mourning-loving child,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reproaching Cronion and the messenger son of Maia:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Fine rewards, lord of the bright thunderbolt, you have given me for nursing Bacchus;","st":"10.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"look at the half-burnt child, of one age with Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you wish, lay low with your unsparing thunderbolt"},{"sp":"N","t":"both mother and young son, whom I nourished in one bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"as foster-brother of your god-begotten Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Child, Necessity is a great god; to whom do you flee?","st":"10.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"What mountain receives you in flight near the sea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What Cithaeron will hide you in a dark hollow?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What mortal pities you, whom your father does not pity?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Either the sword or the sea shall receive you; if it must be,"},{"sp":"N","t":"better to be subdued in the seas than by the knife.","st":"10.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence this woe has rolled upon your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence; for Nephele sends the Erinyes against me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may die in the sea, where the maiden Helle fell."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have heard that, carried from the air to the land of the Colchians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the aloft charioteer of the ravishing ram,","st":"10.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phrixus, still lives as a migrant; would that he too"},{"sp":"N","t":"might travel the aloft path of the golden-fleeced ram,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my son Melicertes, escaping, leaving his homeland;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and would that, after Phoebus, pitying your Ino,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Poseidon, host of Glaucus, might save you.","st":"10.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest after the doom of unburied Learchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I see you too perished, a corpse unburied, unwept,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gasping beneath your father's bloody knife."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hasten to flee maddened Athamas, and do not perceive"},{"sp":"N","t":"a child-slaying father, destroyer of your mother.","st":"10.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me too, sea, after the earth; receive, Nereus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with hospitable hand after Perseus, Melicertes also;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive after the chest of Danae Ino as fellow-sailor."},{"sp":"N","t":"I too have suffered things worthy of impiety, because Cronion"},{"sp":"N","t":"has made even my own race unsown,","st":"10.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsown as I made the life-bringing furrow of the earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"being a stepmother I chose to mow down"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bastard stock of Athamas, and Hera is angry with me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being stepmother of newly nursed Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, on trembling feet she leapt into the sea,","st":"10.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"swiftly tumbling with her son; and Leucothea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-haired god received with outspread palms"},{"sp":"N","t":"as sharer of the hearth with the gods who travel the waters; thence she helps"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering sailors, and Ino became a sea-Nereid,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pilot of noiseless calm.","st":"10.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"Her king Cronides showed to the mother of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that for Bromius' sake she had become a goddess; and she, rejoicing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried to her sea-faring sister in mocking voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Ino, you have the sea, Semele got the circle of Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"yield to me; for Cronides, the plowman of my stock,","st":"10.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"I got as immortal husband, bearing the birth-pang of my childbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"in my place, but you were wedded to an earthly husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athamas, the destroyer of your stock."},{"sp":"N","t":"Your child got the sea, my offspring shall dwell in the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall come to the higher house of Zeus; for I do not liken","st":"10.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavenly Dionysus to submerged Melicertes.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the heavenly bride Semele bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mocking the life of her sister Ino who dwells in the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"Meanwhile Dionysus too, beneath the Lydian slope of the land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the Euian instruments of Cybelian Rhea,","st":"10.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"bloomed, having such stature as he wished; and fleeing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the midday whip of high-pathed Helios"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brightened his body in the quietly bubbling bath"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Maeonian river, and doing favor to Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pactolus murmured, pouring gold-sown water","st":"10.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"with purple sands, and in the wealthy depth of deep-rich"},{"sp":"N","t":"mines the fish rolled, gilded;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs at play, tossing their feet in the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured themselves into the river head foremost, tumbling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one, self-borne, swam rowing with his hands,","st":"10.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"and prone in the surges and swells, planting his track,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with back-stretched feet cleft the flowing water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one plunged into the depth of the submerged haunts"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking below the varied-colored prey of fish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a blind hand upon the swimming fish,","st":"10.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and left the depth again, and held out to Bacchus fish"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening with the silt of the treasure-holding river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and joining his feet fitted together, entwined,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stooping, wandering Silenus, striving against a Satyr,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-rolling, head first, leapt into the stream","st":"10.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"from on high into the deep gulf, and his hair touched the silt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he planted his two feet in the glistening mud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mining the fine-pebbled wealth of the river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one in the outpourings, showing his back to the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"left his shoulder unwetted through the water, dipping his hips,","st":"10.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"near the deep, unshaken; and another, stretching his bare ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wetted his shaggy thighs in the marble stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his self-coiling native tail lashed the flood."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, raising his head and spreading his breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing with his hands, scored the golden calm;","st":"10.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the waveless banks spat forth a self-perfected rose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lily sprouted, and the Seasons garlanded the shores"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Bacchus bathed, and in the flashing stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unplaited curls of his dark hair reddened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, hunting beneath the shadowy wood of lairs,","st":"10.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was bewitched by the rosy-faced shape of a youth of his own age."},{"sp":"N","t":"For already beneath the Phrygian ridge the boy at play,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, had grown, a newly nursed shoot of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor yet had the delicate down of a reddening chin"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the downless circles of his snowy cheek,","st":"10.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"the golden flower of youth; and the coiling clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his back-streaming hair ran upon his silvery shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"unplaited, and fluttering together with the shrill breeze"},{"sp":"N","t":"were lifted by its breath; and as his hair was drawn aside"},{"sp":"N","t":"his neck rose bared in the middle, showing at the tip,","st":"10.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shot forth radiance, free of shadow, just as shines"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene, seen in the middle, cleaving a wet cloud;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from his rosy mouth flowed a honey-breathing voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his limbs the whole spring appeared; and as he went"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his silvery foot a meadow of roses reddened;","st":"10.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if in the well-lit circle of his ox-orbed eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made his eyeballs quiver, the whole Moon shone."},{"sp":"N","t":"Him Dionysus had as playmate, daintily sporting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and asked, pouring forth a wondering voice at his beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a mortal, and guile-weaving hid his immortal shape:","st":"10.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"What father begot you? What heavenly belly bore you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Which of the Graces brought you forth? Did fair Apollo sow you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, friend, do not hide your race; if you come"},{"sp":"N","t":"another wingless Eros without arrows, apart from a quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which of the blessed ones begot you, lying beside Aphrodite?","st":"10.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"For I too tremble to name Cypris your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I call Hephaestus or Ares your father."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if you are he whom they call Hermes, come from the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"show me your light wings and the breathing soles of your sandals."},{"sp":"N","t":"How do you keep hair unshorn hanging at your neck?","st":"10.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"Have you yourself come to me without lyre, without bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phoebus of unshorn hair, shaking your loosened curls?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If Cronides begot me, and you from an earthly stock"},{"sp":"N","t":"carry the short-lived blood of the ox-horned Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reign equal with me, a mortal with a god; for your heavenly","st":"10.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"form will not disgrace the Olympian blood of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But why do I call you of a short-lived stock?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know your blood, even if you long to hide it;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene bore you to Helios, lying beside him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like graceful Narcissus; for you have","st":"10.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"a form like the upper air, an image of horned Selene.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such words he told; and the youth exulted at the speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorying that he surpassed the beauty of youth of his own age"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a more shining form. And within the mountain thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the boy wove a song, Bacchus delighted to hear;","st":"10.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the youth stayed away, he had unsmiling cheeks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if a Satyr beside the meal of the leap-loving table"},{"sp":"N","t":"beat the drums with his hands, striking a resounding echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the youth was away, pursuing the deer-shooting hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the boy not present he refused the double-beat echo;","st":"10.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"if ever beside the flowery stream of Pactolus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lingered and waited, so that at dinner there might be"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweeter water, himself bringing it for his king,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while the boy stayed apart Bacchus was lashed with distress."},{"sp":"N","t":"If he lifted the bold flute, the instrument of Libyan Echo,","st":"10.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a thin breath from his swollen cheek,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus thought he was hearing the Mygdonian piper,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom divine Hyagnis begot, who strove to his hurt against Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the pierced, double-voiced flute of Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if with the youth he touched a single table,","st":"10.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"while the boy spoke he had ears full of delight,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when the youth ceased he had downcast cheeks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if, carried by the sting of a deep-leaping longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus whirled with the dancing beat of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entwined his hand with a playing Satyr as he danced,","st":"10.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting, exchanging sole for migrant sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, watching, was shaken with envious care."},{"sp":"N","t":"If ever he consorted with the Sileni, if with some boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his own age, a hunter, he ran together in the course of the hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous Dionysus held him back, lest some servant of the Loves,","st":"10.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by an equally mind-charming arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should lead astray the purpose of the light-minded boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and steal away the lovely youth from Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a boy fresh in bloom delighting an age-mate of the same years."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he lifted the thyrsus against a raging bear","st":"10.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"or hurled with the sturdy fennel-stalk at a lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched his eye toward the west, looking aslant into the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the death-bearing breeze of Zephyrus blow again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as before the bitter wind killed a youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"overturning the spear-hurling discus against Hyacinthus;","st":"10.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"he feared lest Cronides, the wing-loving bird of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen, untraceable, should appear above Tmolus"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying the boy into the air with sparing talons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like the Trojan boy, the server of his cups;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he trembled too at the ill-loving pilot of the sea,","st":"10.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after Tantalus' son, mounter of golden chariots,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leading his winged wagon into an air-roaming course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Earth-shaker, mad with love, should snatch Ampelus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he had sweet dreams on dream-breeding beds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whispered loving tales to the lying youth,","st":"10.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the shadowy phantoms of a mimic shape."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if the lovely face had anything ill-formed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this was lovely to desire-stricken Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dearer than the youth's whole skin; and if at the tip"},{"sp":"N","t":"the upright tail hung loose, joined through his loins,","st":"10.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"even that was to Bacchus more honeyed than sweet honey;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the filthy locks of his uncared-for head"},{"sp":"N","t":"themselves delighted still more the eye of the love-maddened one."},{"sp":"N","t":"By day he rejoiced in his companion; but he grieved always"},{"sp":"N","t":"as night came on, when no longer, hearing the boy,","st":"10.275","hvShift6":[[0,0],[23,22],[33,33]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he received the accustomed voice with bewitched ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sleeping in the caves of Rhea of the mighty child."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one of the Satyrs, seeing him, was bewitched by his divine shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, stealing away a hidden loving voice, said:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Steward of the human heart, loving Persuasion,","st":"10.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"may this lovely youth be gracious to me alone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having him as playmate, like Bacchus, I do not long"},{"sp":"N","t":"to dwell as a migrant in the upper air, I would not wish to be a god,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor Phaethon who lights mortals, I feel no desire"},{"sp":"N","t":"for nectar, and I lack no ambrosia; I care nothing","st":"10.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Ampelus loves me and Cronion hates me.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So he, tending the venom of the Loves beneath his heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried out a hidden word in a jealous voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a love-charm mingled with wonder. But he too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Euius, struck by the sweet sting of the youth,","st":"10.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried smiling to Cronides, his father ill in love:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Nod me one favor as I love, O Phrygian Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"when I was an infant my nurse Rhea told me"},{"sp":"N","t":"that you gave the lightning to Zagreus, the earlier Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"while he was still babbling, your fiery point,","st":"10.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the clangor of thunder and the pouring of airy rain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he became a second rainy Zeus while still an infant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I refuse the heavenly fire of your hurricane,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I want no cloud, no crash of thunder; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"give the spark of the thunderbolt to fiery Hephaestus,","st":"10.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Ares have the covering of your clouds as breastplate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant Hermes the pouring of rain fallen from Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Apollo too lift the lightning of his father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a lesser will is mine, friend, for leap-loving Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"it is fine for me to lift the small lightning of Semele,","st":"10.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother-slaying sparks of the thunderbolt are joyless."},{"sp":"N","t":"I dwell in Maeonia; for what has the upper air to do with Dionysus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"The beauty of my Satyr is dearer than Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, father, do not hide it; let your youth be your oath:"},{"sp":"N","t":"when as an eagle you lifted the boy beneath the feet of Teucrian Ida","st":"10.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"to heaven with sparing, ravishing talon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the herdsman get so great a beauty, whom you joined"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the heavenly table while he still breathed of the ox-stalls?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Father Zeus, be gracious, long-winged one; do not tell me"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Trojan wine-pourer, the server of your cups,","st":"10.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, bearing the sparkle of a more shining face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely Ampelus puts to shame the form of Ganymede;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Tmolian is better than the Idaean. And there are many"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely ranks of other youths, all of whom together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you wish, love, leaving one boy to Lyaeus.\"","st":"10.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such words he told, shaken by the sting of longing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not so within the shaggy Magnesian wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Apollo the herdsman shepherd the cattle of Admetus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the sweet sting of a love-breeding boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Bacchus at play delighted his heart in the youth.","st":"10.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"And both took their pleasure together within the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now hurling into the air the wandering thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now beside a shadowless flat rock, at another time on the crags"},{"sp":"N","t":"they went hunting the mountain-reared children of lions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and once, alone high on a lonely bank,","st":"10.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"playing in the sands of the river of fine shingle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they held a contest of playful wrestling;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for them there was no tripod as prize, nor for victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"was a flowery cauldron set beside, nor pasturing horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the double-voiced flute of the shrill-sounding Loves.","st":"10.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the strife was lovely for both; and in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood greedy Eros, winged Hermes of the games,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having plaited a garland of longing, narcissus with hyacinth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Both came into the middle, athletes of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winding their palms crown-wise across the back,","st":"10.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the bond of both their arms upon the loins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they locked their sides in the yoked trail of the forearm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted each other's bodies high above the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"with alternating hands; and Bacchus touched Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the honey-sweet wrestling, and had a twofold","st":"10.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely delight, being lifted and lifting; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winding his palm about the wrist of Bromius' palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing an unequally yoked knot with joined hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shut the fitted fingers together in a twofold bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the right hand of willing Dionysus.","st":"10.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"Then, winding his hands about the loins of the youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, pressing his body with love-maddened palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted Ampelus, but he, catching Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the hollow of his knee; and Euius, laughing sweetly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the soft-skinned sole of the youth of his own age,","st":"10.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped self-rolling on his back into the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as Bacchus lay willingly on the ground, on top"},{"sp":"N","t":"the boy sat upon his bare belly; but he, rejoicing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched out and poured this way and that, lay back on the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the sweet weight on his belly; and planting straight","st":"10.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ground-rubbing tip of his foot upon the sand"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raised his back in a turn, and showed a sparing"},{"sp":"N","t":"manliness, and with a rival throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a hand that refused he shook off the burden of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and slanting his sides, and bringing his elbow near the dust,","st":"10.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"the much-knowing youth leapt upon his adversary's back"},{"sp":"N","t":"aslant upon his sides, and fastening above the flanks"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tips of his foot in the hollow of the knee, planting his track beside the ankle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he girdled the middle of the belly in a twofold bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the sides, winding his sole beneath the knee","st":"10.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"spread upright; and as both rolled in the dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweat flowed, forerunner of weariness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And, having the mimicry of one conquered late, unconquerable though he was,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,39],[16,46],[26,59],[28,74]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of his father Zeus the athlete,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[19,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus was conquered willingly, because Zeus himself,","st":"10.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"the great, self-rolling, wrestling beside Alpheius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sank down, bending his willing knees to Heracles."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a playful contest was completed; and the delighted hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the youth lifted the double-voiced flute as prize."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sweating youth brightened his limbs in the stream","st":"10.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"and washed off the moist dust; and as he bathed"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his sweating skin flowed a lovely gleam."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor, having completed the limb-strong victory of wrestling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Bacchus, pasture-mate of the youth, cease from play,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but set a windy contest of foot-speed.","st":"10.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"And bringing nimble suitors of the contest to love"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the first he set as gifts the instruments of Cybelian Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze-backed cymbals and dappled skins of fawns;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the second prizes of victory were the companion of Pan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweet-voiced pipe and a sounding ox-hide","st":"10.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy with bronze; and for the third Dionysus at play set"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reddening sand of the ever-ready river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bromius, marking off the divided ground for the course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the two ends of the stretched path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting upright on the ground a sign of the course ten palms high,","st":"10.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"planting a long timber as the goal of the running; and on the opposite side"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fixed the pattern of the starting-line on the shore, lifting a thyrsus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he urged the Satyrs to contend for victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at the sharp call of leap-loving Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leneus rushed first, wind-footed, and beside him","st":"10.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood high-stepping Cissus and lovely Ampelus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and trusting in the swift sole of the straight-running foot"},{"sp":"N","t":"they stood chosen in a row; and from the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting his track aloft as the surface was scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cissus was borne along with the stormy beat of his feet;","st":"10.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"warming the back of the hurrying one with his breath"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leneus ran after him, running with the breezes of the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"near behind the one ahead, and with the tracks of his back-running"},{"sp":"N","t":"sole struck his tracks, touching the loose dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and so much of a gap was left dividing the two","st":"10.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the rod of the loom-toiling girl, seen in the middle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gets of space beside her breast, neighboring her firm bosom."},{"sp":"N","t":"And third was Ampelus, behind; and looking on"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous Dionysus melted, watching aslant"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two athletes of the games running ahead,","st":"10.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest they should conquer and Ampelus come last;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the god helped, and breathing valor into him"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the boy swifter than the well-wheeling storm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the two prize-winners hurried foremost in the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tossing his knees on the wet shore,","st":"10.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cissus slipped, falling in the sandy mud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the tottering onrush of Leneus' knee was dragged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reining back the course of his feet; and both"},{"sp":"N","t":"prize-winners were left behind, and Ampelus snatched the victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the old Sileni cried aloud a Euian echo","st":"10.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"astonished at the victory of the youth; and the soft-haired"},{"sp":"N","t":"youth received the first prizes, Leneus received the second"},{"sp":"N","t":"with jealousy, and knew the envious guile of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his longing; and with shame-faced eye Cissus looked upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"his age-mates, bringing the last prizes in a downcast hand.","st":"10.430"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Watch the eleventh and you will see lovely Ampelus carried by a man-slaying, ravishing bull."},{"sp":"N","t":"The contest was ended; and the lovely youth, glorying","st":"11.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his playful victory, skipped as playmate beside Bacchus of his own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging his wandering sole in a rolling-footed circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning his all-white right hand upon Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing him, proud in his double victory,","st":"11.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about with his feet, Iobacchus soothed him with friendly speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hasten again, dear boy, after the victory of foot-speed"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the contest on land to have a third, another contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swim untraceable against swimming Bacchus of your own age."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, having conquered me wrestling beside the sands,","st":"11.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"be also in the outpourings lighter than Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving the Satyrs still playing with their leaps"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten alone again to a third, another contest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"may you conquer on land and in the waters, and after the victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would crown your lovely locks with twin clusters,","st":"11.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"double, of unconquered Lyaeus twice conquered."},{"sp":"N","t":"This lovely stream befits you, befits alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"the beauty of your limbs, so that there may be a double Ampelus"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the gold-gleaming currents with a golden palm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as you strain for victory with naked limbs","st":"11.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the Pactolian water will adorn your beauty."},{"sp":"N","t":"Give the river an Olympian prize equal, since Phaethon too"},{"sp":"N","t":"himself sends his rosy rays to Ocean;"},{"sp":"N","t":"give Pactolus your radiance too, so that Ampelus may appear"},{"sp":"N","t":"rising like the Dawnstar; for both alike","st":"11.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"this stream flashes with reddening metal"},{"sp":"N","t":"as you with your limbs; and in its deep-rich stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"let it receive a youth having a form of the same color,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing beauty with beauty, so that I may cry to the Satyrs:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'How has rose come to rose? Whence is one gleam mingled","st":"11.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"both in reddening skin and in flashing stream?'"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that here too, boy, were the stream of Eridanus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the streaming-wealthy tears of the Heliades are, so that I might bathe"},{"sp":"N","t":"your limbs both in gold and in amber."},{"sp":"N","t":"But since I dwell very far from the Western river,","st":"11.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to nearby Alybe, where neighboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"Geudis whitens with the trail of its treasure-holding waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, bathed from the river Pactolus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, I may brighten you also in silver streams."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let fair-flowing Hermus be the care of other Satyrs;","st":"11.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"for it does not carry its stream from gold; but you alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"are a golden boy, may you have golden water too.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, he was carried through the water; and from the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus rose and followed Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sweet was the course for both from end to end","st":"11.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they swam spiraling through the wealthy river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, holding a watery contest of speed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran unsteady in the waters, casting his bare breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the streams, and kicking his feet and rowing with his hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"he inscribed the unshaken backs of the rich calm,","st":"11.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"now keeping his course level with the boy of his own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now darting ahead carefully, as far as to let"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus stay near the path in company with neighboring Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time circling his palms, as though laboring in the wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-kneed, he willingly gave the victory to the water-traveler.","st":"11.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the stream of the river he passed into the haunts of the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, holding his neck proud with the river-victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he girdled his locks with a viperish cluster"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a dreadful mimicry of serpent-tressed Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often, seeing the shifting-backed tunic of Bromius,","st":"11.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"fastening a cunning counterfeit garment on his limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he clamped his light foot in a purple buskin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a spotted robe on his skin; and in the mountain-haunting chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching Iobacchus the driver of leopards"},{"sp":"N","t":"he displayed proud playthings of the crag-loving beasts;","st":"11.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"now mounting the crest of a mountain bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reined back the grim mane of the hurrying beast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now he lashed the shaggy neck of a lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time, riding high on dappled backs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken he delighted in driving an unbridled tiger.","st":"11.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing him, Dionysus, having a gentle threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spoke comforting him with friendly prophetic speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring from reproaching lips a pitying voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Where are you carried, dear boy? Why does the wood please you so much?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Stay with me and hunt together with hunting Dionysus;","st":"11.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"feast together with Lyaeus when he touches the feast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reveling, when I rouse the revel for the Satyrs."},{"sp":"N","t":"The leopard does not harry me, nor the jaw of the wild bear;","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"do not tremble at the greedy mouth of the mountain-ranging lioness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only fear the horns of the pitiless bull.\"","st":"11.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, pitying bold Ampelus; and the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"heard the words with his ears, but his mind within was at play."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then a great sign appeared to tender-loving Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing that Ampelus was short-lived; for from the crag,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying a certain fresh-blooming fawn upon its back,","st":"11.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"a horned serpent rose, abundant in scales,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and carrying it above an altar, on top of the foundations,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it laid it low with its dreadful horn, crushed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"head first, self-rolling, and as the mountain-ranging fawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieked a sharp strain its wandering spirit flew away;","st":"11.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and self-announcing the libation to come, with a trail of blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stone altar was reddened with bloody dews,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the pattern of wine being poured. And looking upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the creeping thing, horn-bearing ravisher of the fawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Euius, learning of the horned destroyer of the witless youth,","st":"11.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixed laughter with grief, and had an unsteady purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"twofold, and in his heart was divided, groaning for the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"as neighbor of doom, laughing for the sake of sweet wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nevertheless he went as companion with the lovely boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the mountain, to the flat rock, and to the course of the accustomed hunt.","st":"11.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing him Bacchus still delighted; for eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"never breed satiety of loves for those who gaze."},{"sp":"N","t":"Often too, as Bromius sat beside the table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the youth piped, exchanging an unaccustomed Muse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and confounded the whole melody of the reeds; and as though the boy","st":"11.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"made fine music, even if he broke the tone of the song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, leaping over the floor with windy beat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clapped along with his hands, much-rattling, and while the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"was still singing, pressing his lip about his mouth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he embraced him in a loving bond on the pretext of harmony;","st":"11.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"he swore even by Cronides that so great a rhythm hymn-tending Pan"},{"sp":"N","t":"never sang, nor shrill-voiced Apollo."},{"sp":"N","t":"And death-bearing Ate, seeing the bold youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"strayed from Lyaeus as he hunted in the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a graceful youth, a boy of his own age,","st":"11.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothed Ampelus with such deceiving speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to the stepmother of Phrygian Dionysus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Your friend, fearless boy, is called Dionysus in vain;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what prize of companionship have you got? You do not carry"},{"sp":"N","t":"the divine chariot of Lyaeus, you do not drive a leopard.","st":"11.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maron got the chariot of your Bromius, stretching his hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"with beast-governing whip and fair-stoned bit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what gift is this you have from Lyaeus of the fair thyrsus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"The Pans have the harp and the murmur of sweet-sounding flutes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the Satyrs your steward Dionysus gave","st":"11.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"the circle of the loud-crashing ox-hide, and the mountain"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bassarids themselves sit upon the backs of lions."},{"sp":"N","t":"What gifts worthy of your love do you bring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loved in vain by Bacchus the driver of leopards?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Often, seated high upon the chariot of Phoebus,","st":"11.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"Atymnius drove high-visible, cutting the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have heard of Abaris himself, whom into an air-roaming course"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phoebus escorted on a flying, wandering arrow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ganymede too drove an eagle in the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a counterfeit winged Zeus, the father of your Lyaeus;","st":"11.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"never has Bacchus lifted Ampelus, a bird of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising your body with his unscratching talons."},{"sp":"N","t":"The Trojan wine-pourer is better, who has the court of Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"as his house. But you, boy, still carrying a longing for a chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"refuse to drive a colt into an unsteady course,","st":"11.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, shaken by the swift-whirling trail of its feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stormy horse shakes off its charioteer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"raging horses dashed Glaucus to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and though he shared the Poseidonian blood of a common race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"head first from the air, the offspring of the Earth-shaker,","st":"11.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-flying Pegasus shook off Bellerophon."},{"sp":"N","t":"Come here with me to the herd, where are shrill-sounding herdsmen"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lovely cattle, and seated upon a bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall make you a high-visible cattle-driving charioteer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for your lord will praise you far more, watching,","st":"11.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull-natured Dionysus, seated on the loins of a bull."},{"sp":"N","t":"This course is free of fear, since even, being female,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Europa mounted the back of an ox,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the horn in her hands and needing no bit.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded him, and the goddess plunged into the air.","st":"11.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"And from the crag ran down a certain wandering bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen, and its tongue, the witness of its thirst,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it thrust forth from its throat through open lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drank; and it stood about the boy, as beside a present herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like one that knew him; nor did it hold","st":"11.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"its slanting horn from its forehead; and as the irresistible bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched forth its drink thickly from its capacious throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the streaming moisture of its dew drenched the youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a prophet of things to come, that with an earthly trail oxen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laboring about one endless circling turning-post,","st":"11.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"would water with waters the harvest of the vine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold boy stood over the bovine forehead"},{"sp":"N","t":"handling the curving horn with fearless hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whetted by the sweet sting for the wood-ranging ox"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wished to drive the unyoked, mountain-running bull.","st":"11.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"And plucking leaves beside the deep-rushed grass"},{"sp":"N","t":"he plaited a counterfeit whip of green withies"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sharper shoots, and with a much-twisted cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bending crooked circles, he made the pattern of a bit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with dewy leaves he adorned the body of the bull,","st":"11.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bound reddening roses all around its back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hung lilies and narcissus upon its forehead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"suspending a purpling anemone at its neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he gilded the twin horns on either side"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing yellow-skinned mud with his cupped hands","st":"11.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the neighboring river. And spreading a dappled hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"over its back he mounted the spine of the bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretching the counterfeit whip against the bovine sides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though it were a fair-maned colt, he lashed his bearer."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold one shouted a word to bull-faced Mene:","st":"11.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Yield to me, horned Selene, driver of cattle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I have become both horned and a driver of the bull.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a boasting word he cried to circling Mene."},{"sp":"N","t":"And through the air the eye of envious Selene watched"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus carried by the man-slaying, ravishing bull,","st":"11.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sent against it a cattle-driving gadfly; and it, its body"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored by the bitter roaming sting, unsteadily"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran down about the hard-to-tread ridge like a horse."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the youth, seeing the unyoked bull, with raging sting"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashing its track upon the high-crested hills,","st":"11.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"in terror before his doom entreated with mourning voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Stand still today, bull, and tomorrow you shall travel swift;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not kill me high on the lonely rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that Bacchus may not hear of my unspoken doom."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not be angry, bull, because I gilded your horn;","st":"11.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not envy, because Bacchus keeps my love."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if you kill me and care nothing for Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and no pity for your mourning charioteer holds you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because I am young, because I am also the friend of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carry me to the Satyrs and subdue me there, bull,","st":"11.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that after my doom I may get the much-wept dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"yes, I beg you, dear bull; and I shall have comfort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when tearless Dionysus groans for my doom."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you deceive your horn-bearing charioteer"},{"sp":"N","t":"who has a form like your bull-faced shape,","st":"11.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"become endowed with voice and tell my doom to Lyaeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Bull, unfriendly to your Demeter and to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Bromius grieves, bountiful Deo grieves with him.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such words the rosy youth spoke, neighbor of Hades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ill-fated; and darting with the twin hooves of its feet","st":"11.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"over the hard-to-tread topmost peaks of the mountain the raging ox"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook the youth head first from its back;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fell self-rolling; and as he fell upon the knuckle"},{"sp":"N","t":"his neck, aslant, was split with a faint crack;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirling him back again over the ground","st":"11.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the whetted point of its horn it laid him low."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the corpse was headless; and of the unburied corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"the white body was reddened with reddening gore."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one of the Satyrs, seeing lovely Ampelus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the dust high above the earth, came to Bacchus with ill tidings.","st":"11.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, hearing, ran swift like the breezes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not so great a course did Heracles accomplish, when the Nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid delicate Hylas in envious streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bridegroom kept for a wet, ravishing girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as then Bacchus rushed, mountain-running; and lying in the dust","st":"11.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"he groaned for the boy, gazing at him as though alive."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he cloaked the breathless one, fastening from his shoulder"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fawnskin upon his cold breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, though he was a corpse, bound his feet in buskins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he sprinkled roses and lilies over his skin, and about his hair,","st":"11.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"as of one short-lived, fallen by a sharp sting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hung the flower of swift-perishing anemone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave a thyrsus to his palm, and covered him with his own"},{"sp":"N","t":"purple robe; and as a gift from his unshorn head"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut a single lock and laid it upon the witnessing corpse","st":"11.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a last gift; and taking ambrosia from his mother Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"he poured it over the wounds, whence the youth, changing his form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"transferred the fragrant ambrosia to his harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the graceful corpse lay stretched over the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"no pallor spread over the rosy body; and of the swift-fated one","st":"11.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"the graceful locks of the love-breeding head"},{"sp":"N","t":"fluttered upon his face with sparing breezes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was lovely even in the dust. And about the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sileni groaned, and the Bacchants lamented."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did beauty leave him, though he was dead; and like a Satyr","st":"11.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"the corpse lay, wholly like one laughing, as though forever"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a honey-sweet voice from voiceless lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"And looking at the corpse grief-free Dionysus uttered"},{"sp":"N","t":"a plaintive voice, having an unsmiling face:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Let the envious thread of the Fates fall; are even","st":"11.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"bulls jealous over youths, just like the winds?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What Zephyrus, after Phoebus, has assailed Dionysus too?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed was Phoebus Atymnius; for he got this name"},{"sp":"N","t":"from a youth; and of the Therapnaean youth himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifts a flower named for him as a remedy,","st":"11.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"having inscribed a cry of woe on the petals of the hyacinth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what garland have I too for my locks, or what"},{"sp":"N","t":"speaking flowers do I brandish, comforters of the grief for a boy?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But as avenger of your death, dragging to slaughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bring to your tomb, untimely one, the wandering bull.","st":"11.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would not kill your murderer with an ox-axe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that it may get an equal doom, its forehead battered"},{"sp":"N","t":"like bulls that are slaughtered, but I would tear open the whole"},{"sp":"N","t":"bitter belly of the bull with the point of my horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because with its long-horned point it laid you low.","st":"11.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed is the Earth-shaker, since he loved a Phrygian boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a neighbor of the homeland of my boy, and carrying him"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought him up to the golden house of Zeus as a citizen of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to him, when he hastened to the horsemanship of Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave the unwetted chariot, bride-adorner of Hippodameia.","st":"11.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone have my youth untimely; for lovely"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus did not see a life-saving marriage, nor beside a bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"did this youth yoke my bridal wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but dying he left grief to grief-free Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet, dear boy, has Persuasion left your mouth,","st":"11.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"but even in your death she dwells on your breathless lips;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and though you are a corpse your cheeks still shine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your eyes laugh even still, and still snowy"},{"sp":"N","t":"are the palms of your two hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the shrill breezes shake your lovely locks;","st":"11.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"the death-bringing hour has not quenched the roses of your limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but all these are still kept for you. Alas for the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what need was there for you to drive a pitiless bull?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If a longing for storm-footed horses dismayed you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"why did you not tell me, so that from neighboring Ida","st":"11.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"I might bring a chariot here, and from the ancient stock"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring to you the Trojan race of heavenly horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plundering the homeland of Ganymede, whom Ida reared"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a body like yours, whom from man-slaying bulls"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-flying Zeus lifted with sparing talons;","st":"11.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"if truly you longed to slay beasts in the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"why did you not tell me that there was need of a chariot?"},{"sp":"N","t":"You might have driven the harmless circle of my wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and receiving the untouched reins of my Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"you might unshaken have lashed the chariot of gentle serpents.","st":"11.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer with the Satyrs do you sing the wine-song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do you command the rattle-loving Bassarids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do you hunt together with hunting Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas, that Hades is not mild, nor for a corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"does he receive the splendid gifts of deep-rich metal,","st":"11.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I might make dead Ampelus alive again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas, that Hades is never persuaded; but if he were willing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would grant all the glittering wealth of Eridanus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plundering the river trees, and I would bring the marble,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing Erythraean stone of the Indians","st":"11.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and all the silver of wealthy Alybe, and in exchange for the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"of my boy I would give all golden Pactolus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he groaned for the sweet corpse; and looking upon him"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying in the dust he cried again in mourning voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Father Zeus, if you love me, and if you know the toil of loves,","st":"11.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"make Ampelus endowed with voice again for a single hour,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he may speak one last and only word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Why do you groan, Dionysus, for one whom you do not rouse with groans?"},{"sp":"N","t":"My ears are here, and I do not hear you crying,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my eyes are here, and I do not see you groaning;","st":"11.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"grief-free Dionysus, do not pour tears for me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leave your grief, since beside the murderous spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naiads groan and Narcissus does not hear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon does not know the plaintive distress of the Heliades.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas, that my father did not beget me mortal, so that I might be","st":"11.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"pasture-mate of the youth even in Hades, and not leave in Lethe"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely Ampelus, fallen, alone."},{"sp":"N","t":"In desire for a youth Apollo is more blessed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the beloved name of his boy; would that I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"might be Ampeloeis, just as Apollo is Hyacinthius.","st":"11.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"How long do you sleep, and no longer, boy, do you dance?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Why today do you no longer go to the outpourings of the river"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the well-watered pitcher? And in the mountain-haunting thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hour of your accustomed dance has come again."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you are angry, dear boy, with desire-stricken Dionysus,","st":"11.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"speak to the Sileni, so that I may hear your word."},{"sp":"N","t":"If a lion subdued you, I shall destroy them all together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all that the crag of Tmolus bears, nor shall I ever spare"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lions of our Rhea, but I shall subdue them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if your murderers were born with grim jaws;","st":"11.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"if a leopard laid low your body, flower of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer shall I drive the dappled body of leopards;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there are other beasts, and Artemis, ruler of the whole hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drives a horn-drawn chariot of stags;"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the fawnskin robe I shall ride a chariot of fawns;","st":"11.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"if shameless boars slew you, seizing them all in one"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would kill them, and not leave one boar alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"still living, remaining for the archer-goddess;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if a reckless bull slew you, with sharp thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would wipe out the whole race of bulls, roots and all.\"","st":"11.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"So he groaned. But Eros stood near him"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the horn-drawing form of shaggy Silenus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a thyrsus, and having fastened a spotted skin on his skin"},{"sp":"N","t":"he propped his body on the age-tending staff of a fennel-stalk;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to mourning Bromius he cried a comforting voice:","st":"11.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Loose the sparks of your loves upon another love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changing your turning sting toward a new youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"forgetting the dead; for always for an older"},{"sp":"N","t":"love a new love is the remedy; for to destroy"},{"sp":"N","t":"a love time knows, even if it has learned to cover all things.","st":"11.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you wish a pain-slaying bulwark for your distress,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tend a better boy; longing knows how to wither longing."},{"sp":"N","t":"The Laconian youth harried Zephyrus too; but when he was dead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the young Cyparissus, the lovely Wind"},{"sp":"N","t":"found a consolation for Amyclaean Hyacinthus.","st":"11.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"If you wish, ask the gardener; for seeing a flower"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying on the ground in the dust the plowman"},{"sp":"N","t":"plants another, newer, as remedy for the one perished."},{"sp":"N","t":"Listen, that I may tell a tale of mortals born long ago:"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was once a delicate boy, surpassing youth of his age,","st":"11.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the stream of many-branching river Maeander,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tall with slender form, sharp of foot, his hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"straight, beardless; and on both cheeks"},{"sp":"N","t":"a native Grace was skipping upon his face"},{"sp":"N","t":"with shame-faced eyes, and from his eyelids forever","st":"11.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"flowed a far-shooting gleam of beauty shooting arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had a body wholly like milk, and about the white"},{"sp":"N","t":"a rose purpled at the tip with a two-colored fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"Him his dear father called Calamus, who through the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"surging below, drawing his crooked stream into the light,","st":"11.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping unseen, a slanting traveler beneath the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping up sharply thrusts above the neck of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maeander of the inmost recess, bringing water hidden in the bosom."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was lovely swift Calamus. And the rosy-armed youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighted in lovely Carpus, of his own age,","st":"11.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"who got so great a beauty as no mortal man has got;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for if this youth had been among the men of former times,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would have become the bridegroom of fair-tressed Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a better form, alone putting to shame with his rosy skin"},{"sp":"N","t":"the splendor of Cephalus and the face of Orion;","st":"11.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor would Deo have embraced with fruitful palm"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iasion as bridegroom, nor Selene Endymion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but perhaps that youth, for the sake of his better form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a single husband would have wedded the beds of both goddesses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending the many-harvested bed of yellow-haired Deo,","st":"11.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having jealous Mene as common bedfellow."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was the lovely friend of Calamus, flower of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having beauty; and both, of one age, took their pleasure"},{"sp":"N","t":"high on the bank of the neighboring many-winding river."},{"sp":"N","t":"For them there was a spiral double course, and for both","st":"11.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was strife; Calamus ran like the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having an elm as starting-line and an olive as turning-post"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ran through the shores of the river from end to end &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swift-kneed Calamus willingly fell to the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and left the willing victory to graceful Carpus.","st":"11.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the boy bathed the youth bathed with him in play,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and again they had another like contest in the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and slow in the outpourings he swam, letting Carpus"},{"sp":"N","t":"go ahead, so that, cutting the swells behind with his hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he might come second beside the feet of swimming Carpus,","st":"11.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the free back of the youth running before him."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a course from the wet starting-line; both strove,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who should conquer whom, so as to come back returning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passing the twin turning-post of either bank"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the land opposite with rowing palms;","st":"11.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had the outpouring as road; and always coming near him"},{"sp":"N","t":"the youth, sparing the rush of his hurrying palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watched the rosy-skinned fingers of the swimmers' hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Calamus, ahead on the path, reined back his rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and yielded to the youth; and the stormy youth ran","st":"11.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing with his hands, stretching his neck above the swell;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now Carpus would have set foot on the field from the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the land-victory entered into the river-victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the Wind on the opposite path battered him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pitiless killed the sweet boy; for into the open","st":"11.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"throat of the youth countless water was dragged."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Calamus, escaping the storms of the envious wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entered the neighboring shores apart from the youth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and perceiving his friend neither present nor hearing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"groaning lovely, he roared with plaintive voice:","st":"11.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Naiads, speak, what Wind has snatched Carpus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, I beg you, grant me a last favor, come to another"},{"sp":"N","t":"spring, and flee the death-bearing water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of my father, and do not drink the stream that murdered Carpus."},{"sp":"N","t":"My father did not kill the youth; but grudging","st":"11.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"Calamus too, after Phoebus, the Wind destroyed Carpus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and perhaps desiring him struck him with a jealous storm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing after the discus a counter-blowing breeze against the youth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet has my star leapt up bathed in the outpourings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet has my Dawnstar shone; but with Carpus","st":"11.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"sunk in the streams, why should I still see the light?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Naiads, speak, who has quenched the light of the Loves?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And for Calamus ill in love, brother though he is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut off a single small cluster of hair as he dies,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shear all your locks for perished Carpus.","st":"11.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you still delay, boy? Why does the water please you so much?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Have you found a better friend in the waters, staying beside whom"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have cast the longings of wretched Calamus to the winds?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If one of the Naiads, a Nymph ill in love, has snatched you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me, and I arm myself against them all; but if the wedding-hymn","st":"11.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the bridal Loves of my sister delights you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me, and in the outpourings I shall kindle your bridal chamber."},{"sp":"N","t":"Carpus, do you swim past me, forgetful of the accustomed bank?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am weary calling you, and you do not hear me crying."},{"sp":"N","t":"If Notus, if bold Eurus blew, let him himself wander","st":"11.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiless, danceless, reckless enemy of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Boreas subdued you, I go to Oreithyia."},{"sp":"N","t":"But if the wave covered you and did not respect your form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and my father took you in the unsparing trail of the wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him receive his own child too in his man-slaying waters,","st":"11.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hide Calamus near perished Carpus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But falling head first, where wandering Carpus died,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall quench my hot love drinking the water of Acheron.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, gushing a flood from his eyelids; and for the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut his dark locks with downcast iron,","st":"11.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"which he nourished, which he tended, and held out his mourning hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"to his father Maeander, and spoke a last word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Receive after my locks my body too; for I cannot"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn see the light apart from Carpus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Carpus and Calamus there is one life, and both got","st":"11.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"a like sting of Eros upon the earth; let one watery"},{"sp":"N","t":"doom be for both in the outpourings too."},{"sp":"N","t":"Build, Naiads, high on the river bank"},{"sp":"N","t":"one undivided empty tomb for both, and about the tomb"},{"sp":"N","t":"let a verse be carved in mourning letters:","st":"11.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I am the grave of Carpus and Calamus, whom before, both"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for one another, the pitiless water killed.\"'"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and self-rolling slipped into the stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the child-slaying water of his father who refused."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Calamus gave to the reeds a form named for him,","st":"11.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"of like nature, and Carpus grew as the fruit of the field.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things, comforting, furious Eros soothed with friendly speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightening the sweet sting for Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And far more he was lashed in his heart with plaintive distress"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the untimely doom of the youth. — And of unsteady","st":"11.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lycabas, the storm-footed father, the daughters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rosy-faced Seasons, went to the house of Helios;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one, about her face shadowed with snow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a thin radiance of black-clouded gleam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fitted her cold feet in a hail-covered sandal,","st":"11.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"and binding tight her locks upon her wet head"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped a rain-bearing veil upon her brow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and had a green garland on her head, and with a snowy"},{"sp":"N","t":"white band covered her frosty breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another spat forth, breathing, the mortal-delighting breeze","st":"11.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the swallow winds, and of her Zephyrus-loving head"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled her hair of spring with a dewy bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughing with flowers, and darting from her robe"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long-shadowed scent of the rose opening at dawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"she wove a double revel for Adonis and Cytherea;","st":"11.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, the harvest Season, marched together with her sisters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an ear of corn bristling with clusters at its tips"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lifted in her right hand, and the jaw of the sharp-cutting sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"messenger of the reaping, and the girl's body was bound tight"},{"sp":"N","t":"in white linen, and as she whirled in the dance","st":"11.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"the secrets of her thighs appeared through the thin garment,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the wet sweat of her flushed face"},{"sp":"N","t":"her cheeks were moistened by hotter Phaethon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, leader of the well-plowed dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bound an olive branch upon her hairless temple,","st":"11.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"drenched with the waters of the seven-pathed river Nile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having the sparse withering hair of her temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had a parched body, since, being of autumn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she had shorn her tree-hair with the leaf-shedding winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for not yet with the plaited clusters of golden tendrils","st":"11.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"did grape-clusters of the vine flow upon the neck of the bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the moisture of the purple Maronian dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine-drunk beside the wine-loving vat, make her drunk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the wandering ivy run up, whirling back again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but then came the fated time, for whose sake","st":"11.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Seasons themselves ran in company to the house of Helios."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Delight your heart in the twelfth, where the young flower of the Loves, Ampelus, gave up his form to the harvest of the vine."},{"sp":"N","t":"So they, beside the brows of western Ocean,","st":"12.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"sailed to the halls of the generative sun."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they went the star Hesperus met them"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping from the hall; and rushing through she too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"newly appearing, rose, Selene, driver of cattle.","st":"12.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"And they, before the eyes of the life-bringing Charioteer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent their fruitful step. He, having just finished his course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"returned from the air; and beside the four-yoked chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fire-eyed driver gleaming Phosphorus"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid down the hot yoke-straps and the starry whip,","st":"12.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"having cleansed beside the outpourings of neighboring Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"the body of the fire-nourished horses, dripping with sweat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the colts, shaking the wet manes of their necks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stamped with marble hooves at the blazing manger."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the daughters of Time, about the flaming throne","st":"12.15","hvShift6":[[0,0],[34,27]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of the untiring Charioteer flying in a ring,","hvShift6":[[0,3],[4,9],[12,10],[16,12],[21,23],[22,37],[25,41]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the twelve circling Hours welcomed the four,","hvShift6":[[0,26]]},{"sp":"N","t":"handmaids of Helios, companions of the blazing chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mystic servants of the Year in turn; for to the ancient"},{"sp":"N","t":"guide of the whole cosmos they had bent their slave necks.","st":"12.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the grape-tressed Season shouted a word to him"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding, as witness of her supplication, the sickle of autumn:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Helios, giver of grain, nourisher of plants, lord of fruits,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when will the orchards rear the wine-bearing cluster?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And to which of the blessed ones does Aeon pledge this prize?","st":"12.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, I beg you, do not hide it, since of all my sisters"},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone am without a prize; for no harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no corn-ear, no meadow, and no rain of Zeus do I rear.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke; and the sun heartened the nurse of the harvest"},{"sp":"N","t":"to come, and beside the wall opposite,","st":"12.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising his finger, he showed the circling girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unequally yoked tablets of Harmonia, in which lie"},{"sp":"N","t":"in one place all the oracles which, fated for the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the prophetic hand of first-born Phanes inscribed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and embroidered with the stylus the fitting house for each.","st":"12.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hyperion, steward of fire, spoke some words:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"On the third tablet, whence the wine-harvest shall be,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall know, where the Lion and the Maiden are; and on the fourth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who is scepter-bearer of the grape, where, drawing sweet nectar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ganymede lifts the cup with painted hand.\"","st":"12.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"As the god so spoke, the vine-loving girl ran,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling her eyes, and beside the oracular wall"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw the first tablet, of one age with the endless cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing in one place all that scepter-bearing Ophion"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplished, all that old Cronus completed, when, cutting","st":"12.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"the male plowshare of his father, he plowed the childbed water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sowing the unsown backs of the daughter-bearing sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he who once received a stone son in his gaping throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"feasting on the counterfeit body of a lying Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the stone brought forth the stock of children within the recess,","st":"12.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling the burden of his pregnant throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the fire-shining victory of Zeus in his fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hail-storming war of the snowstorm of Cronus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the storm-footed Season, handmaid of Phaethon, had seen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she looked at the neighboring tablet in turn; and that one held","st":"12.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"how a pine labored with a mortal birth, or whence suddenly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unfolding a fertile childbed of the tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fir belched forth a son unsown, self-perfected,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whence rainy Zeus deluged all cities"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing water raised on high with sky-high seas,","st":"12.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"how Notus from Boreas, and Eurus from Lips, lashing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering chest of Deucalion, neighbor of Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted it into an air-roaming voyage without share of harbor."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when she ran with fair-footed sole to the third tablet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mystic servant of the Year, the girl stood fixed, twisting,","st":"12.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the fated, many-turning oracles of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening letters, scored with wise red ochre,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all that the shifting-storied first-born mind inscribed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"foretelling such things, and she read on the panels:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Argus, herdsman of Hera, shall change to the form of a bird,","st":"12.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the bright pattern of his grim eyelids; and she too,","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Harpalyce, after the bed of a sin-living wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having carved up her son for a father who married his daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall row an airy winged course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a stormy bird; and loom-toiling Philomela","st":"12.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall be the shifting-necked swallow twittering beneath the eaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying the testimony of her tongueless silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having written speaking devices on a wise tunic;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Niobe beside the feet of Sipylus, an intelligent rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with stony tears lamenting her row of children","st":"12.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall stand a pitiable statue. And there shall be there a neighbor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyrrhus, a Phrygian stone mad with love, still having"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lawless sting for Rhea of an unwedded wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thisbe wet water, and Pyramus, both of one age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for one another; and, desiring the fair-garlanded girl","st":"12.85","hvShift5":[[0,0],[28,29],[31,38]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Milax, Crocus shall be a flower of the Loves;","hvShift5":[[0,0],[14,5]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the bridal turning-post of the storm-footed Hymenaeans"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the apples of the Paphian, into a lion's form"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis shall goad Atalanta, transformed.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And all these in one place the restless Season passed by,","st":"12.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"until she reached the place where fiery Hyperion"},{"sp":"N","t":"had declared the tokens of prophecy to the windy girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the light-bearing Lion was wrought, where also she herself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the starry Maiden, was embroidered in counterfeit shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a wine-dark cluster, the summer-born flower of the harvest;","st":"12.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"there the daughter of Time lulled her feet, and read these things:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cissus, high-flying, lovely youth, creeping into a plant"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall be the spiral ivy among shoots; and from the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"Calamus the upright reed, arched by the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall appear as a thin shoot of the plant-nourishing field,","st":"12.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"the prop of the vines; and changing his form into a plant"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus gives his name to the fruit of the vine.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the harvest girl had seen these oracles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sought that place where beside the neighboring wall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the image of Ganymede had been scored in wrought pattern","st":"12.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping nectarous moisture from a golden cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where was the four-yoked oracle of scored words;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there the cluster-loving goddess reveled, and the lady found"},{"sp":"N","t":"these oracles kept for ivy-bearing Lyaeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"To Phoebus Zeus nodded to have the prophetic laurel,","st":"12.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reddening roses to the rosy-skinned Cyprus-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grey-green olive branch to grey-eyed Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and corn-ears to Demeter, and vines to Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the Euoi-loving girl saw in the writings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and delighted she darted off, and taking her sisters","st":"12.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"she passed to the stream of eastern Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"running with the horsemanship of Phaethon. — But for Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was no remedy for his fallen companion, nor any memory"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the dance; and shaken in his mind by the loving sting"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sang bitter dirges, and in uncaring silence","st":"12.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"he left the bronze backs of the unbeaten ox-hide;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the harp delight him. And as tender-loving Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unsmiling face lamented pitiably,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stream of reedy Lydian Hermus was held back"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it rolled swiftly with the windy throb of its outpouring,","st":"12.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did it long to flow; and in its deep-rich stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"saffron Pactolus reined back its mourning water"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the mimicry of a downcast man; and over the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sangarius checked the back-rushing trail of its spring-haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring its Phrygian stream; and the breathless image","st":"12.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Tantalus' daughter of dreadful children, drenched with groans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured double tears, as Dionysus lamented;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pine, wailing, companion of the fir of its own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whispered faintly; and the laurel itself, though it was the tree"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unshorn-haired Phoebus, shook off its hair","st":"12.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the mourning winds; and the rich, uncut olive"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured its leaves on the ground, though it was the plant of Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"As tearless Dionysus so groaned with longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dreadful threads of Fate were turned, loosed again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and consoling the lament of grieving Dionysus","st":"12.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"Atropos of the steadfast word belched forth an inspired oracle:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Your youth lives, Dionysus, and shall not cross"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bitter water of Acheron; your lament has found how to destroy"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbending threads of unturning Fate, recovered again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus is not dead, though he died; for into a lovely","st":"12.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"drink, into sweet nectar I shall change your boy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"him, with the bounding beat of a well-wheeling palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the double-voiced flute striking a dinner harmony"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall hymn, having the Phrygian rhythm or the Dorian song;"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a rhythmic man shall sing him on the stage","st":"12.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the Ismenian echo of the Aonian reed"},{"sp":"N","t":"or for the dwellers of Marathon; and the Muses shall cry Euoi"},{"sp":"N","t":"for lovely Ampelus together with Lyaeus of the vine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the crooked snaky headband of your hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall wind clustered garlands about your locks,","st":"12.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing jealousy to Phoebus, because he stretches in his hand the mourning"},{"sp":"N","t":"tree-borne dirges of the tear-loving hyacinths,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while you pursue a drink, the respite of the mortal race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earthly pattern of heavenly nectar, and the youth who is yours","hvShift6":[[0,0],[48,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"shall put to shame the flowery boast of the Amyclaean boy;","st":"12.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if his city lifts warlike bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the homeland of your youth too rears the light-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"wet rain of a river dew reddening,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all luxuriant with gold, and not rejoicing in iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if it glories in the stream of a roaring river,","st":"12.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pactolian water is better than Eurotas."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, you have given grief to grief-free Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that as your honey-dripping wine grows"},{"sp":"N","t":"you may give delight to the whole four-yoked cosmos"},{"sp":"N","t":"and libation to the blessed ones and gladness to Dionysus;","st":"12.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus the lord has wept, so that he may loose the tears of mortals.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the goddess went as companion with her sisters."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a great wonder appeared to plaintive Bacchus as witness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for leaping up, the lovely corpse, creeping like a serpent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, self-perfected, changed his shape,","st":"12.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and became a sweet flower; and as the corpse was transformed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the belly was a very long bush, the tips of the hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprouted as branches, the feet were rooted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the curls were clusters, and the fawnskin itself was shaped"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the many-patterned flower of the growing harvest,","st":"12.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the long-shadowed neck was a cluster of the vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of like nature to the elbow stretched the curving shoot"},{"sp":"N","t":"swelling with grapes, and as the head was transformed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the curved clusters imitated the pattern of a bent horn."},{"sp":"N","t":"There were endless rows of plants; and self-perfected","st":"12.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine-orchard, winding its green shoots,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled the neighboring trees with the new wine-dark fruit."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a new wonder, since then the boy at play,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding his slanting foot into a high-leaved plant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cissus, high-flying, made his form into a tree,","st":"12.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and became the crooked shoot named for him, and the newly grown"},{"sp":"N","t":"orchard of vines he girdled with a crooked bond."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with loving leaves he covered his shaded temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made his locks drunk from the wine-loving leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorying Dionysus; and of the plant-growing boy","st":"12.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"he plucked the fruit of the just-ripening harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, self-taught, without foot, outside a vat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"squeezing the cluster with the heavy wrist of his palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing with entwining hands the birth-pang of drunkenness"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed the newly flowing mass of the purple harvest,","st":"12.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and found the sweet drink; and of wine-pouring Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the white fingers of his hands reddened as they were drenched."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he had for cup the curving horn of an ox; and with the tips"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his lips Bacchus tasted the sweet-drinking dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tasted the fruit also, and, delighting his heart in both,","st":"12.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched forth a speech from an exulting throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Ambrosia and the nectar of my Zeus you bring forth, Ampelus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo, bearing two beloved shoots, did not eat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fruit of the laurel and did not drink from the hyacinth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the corn-ear does not labor with a sweet drink; be gracious, Deo;","st":"12.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give mortals food as well, and not a drink alone."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, your doom too is lovely; truly even"},{"sp":"N","t":"the threads of Fate herself turned womanish toward you and your beauty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward you Hades too was pitying, toward you even"},{"sp":"N","t":"Persephone changed her harsh purpose,","st":"12.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"and took you, a corpse, alive for her brother Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"You did not die as Atymnius died; the water of Styx,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flame of Tisiphone, the eye of Megaera you did not see;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you live still, boy, even though you died; nor did the water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Lethe hide you, no common tomb holds you; but even","st":"12.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth herself was ashamed to cover your form;"},{"sp":"N","t":"rather my father made you a plant, honoring his son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lord Cronion changed your body into sweet nectar."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nature did not, as on the painted Therapnaean clusters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"score a cry of woe upon your tearless petals;","st":"12.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"but you keep your own color even among the shoots, boy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your death has proclaimed the radiance of your limbs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet has your reddening form abandoned you."},{"sp":"N","t":"But as avenger of your death I shall never cease"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring your wine as libation over the man-slaying","st":"12.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"destroyer when he is sacrificed. And you fasten blame upon the Hamadryads"},{"sp":"N","t":"with your lovely petals; and from your fragrant clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"the moisture of your Loves breathes about me."},{"sp":"N","t":"When shall I mix the fruit of the apple in a mixing-bowl?"},{"sp":"N","t":"When shall I drip the fig into a nectarous cup?","st":"12.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"Fig and apple alike have their grace only as far as the teeth."},{"sp":"N","t":"No other plant can rival your grape-clusters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"neither rose, nor fair-hued narcissus, nor anemone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor lily, nor hyacinth is equal to the shoot of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because with the new drops of the much-trodden harvest","st":"12.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"your drink shall receive all flowers; there shall be one drink"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingled with all, and it shall come to a single scent"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixed with flowers of every kind; for your flower"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall adorn the whole grass of the meadow in spring."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yield to me, glorious archer, because with mourning","st":"12.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"bond you girdled your griefless locks with much-lamented leaves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"dirges are scored on your petals; but if in the garden"},{"sp":"N","t":"the glorious archer bears a garland, I draw sweet wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and put about me a lovely crown, and sweet-drinking"},{"sp":"N","t":"within my heart I lift Ampelus himself entire.","st":"12.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"Yield to the lord of grapes, you of the glancing helmet; for the bloody one"},{"sp":"N","t":"pours gore as libation to Ares, and the vine-god to Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reddening dew of the cluster turned to wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"Deo, you are plundered, after Pallas; for olives"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not breed gladness, and the corn-ear does not bewitch a man,","st":"12.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pear has honey-sweet fruit, the myrtle rears"},{"sp":"N","t":"fragrant flowers, and they do not with mind-charming fruit"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurl human cares to the winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I was born far better than you; for when our"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine is not present the dinners of the table are joyless,","st":"12.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"when wine is not present the dances are unbewitching."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you can, grey-eyed one, drink the fruit of your olive;"},{"sp":"N","t":"my splendid-gifted harvest has conquered your plant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, anointing their bodies with your glistening oil,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[23,21],[34,30]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[7,31],[21,52],[23,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"athlete men are joyless, and the man of dreadful suffering,","st":"12.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"having lost wife or daughter in a common doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or holding grief for dead children or mother or father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he tastes sweet wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall shake off the hateful mass of his growing distress."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ampelus, even after your doom you gladden the heart of Bacchus;","st":"12.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"in all my limbs I shall mix your draught.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And all the trees around, with head nodding downward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bend an arching neck like one entreating,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the old palm bowed down its high-stretched leaves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the apple-tree you stretch your feet, and about the fig","st":"12.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"you lean, spreading out your hands, and your harvest"},{"sp":"N","t":"they lift as handmaids lift their mistress,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when with the spiral throb of your stretching leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"you rise above the shoulder of your attendants; and the soft"},{"sp":"N","t":"varied leaves of the wide-scattered clusters growing near,","st":"12.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though you slumbered, flutter upon your face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winds cooling you with sparing breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a servant shakes the accustomed slender fan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a manufactured cool breeze for his king."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you endure the midday threat of Phaethon,","st":"12.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Etesian breeze comes as forerunner of your grape"},{"sp":"N","t":"lulling the thirsty star of fiery Maera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the course of the summer-born Season warms you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heating with the vapor of Sirius the ripening dew."},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke exulting, and cast away his former cares,","st":"12.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the fragrant harvest as the remedy for the youth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things are sung about the vine-cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how it got its name from the youth. But among the poets"},{"sp":"N","t":"there is another, older tale, how once to the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"from heaven the fruit-bearing Olympian ichor flowed","st":"12.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bore the drink of the Bacchic grape, and on the crags"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fruit of the harvest grew self-sprung, untended;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and not yet was the tame vine so named, but in the thickets"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wild vine, flourishing among much-bending celery,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fair-vined wood of wine-bearing plants sprouted,","st":"12.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing forth a wet mass crammed with dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was a great orchard, where, creeping up in rows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering cluster shook, reddening upon cluster;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of which one, half-finished, increasing its birth-pangs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpling in changing hues, appeared with varied-colored fruit,","st":"12.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, white-crested, ripened, of one color with foam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many another thrust its yoke-fellow neighbor, neighbor against neighbor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yellow in nature, while another seemed in form like pitch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"its whole bloom darkening, and from the wine-bearing leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"it made drunk the whole splendid-fruited olive grown beside it;","st":"12.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and over another's unripe fruit, freshly scored,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran the black, self-rushing breath of the silvery cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a swelling harvest in its clustered mass;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the spiral of the harvest garlanded the pine on the opposite path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shading the well-covered shoot with gathered thickets,","st":"12.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and delighted the heart of Pan; and bringing near its branches"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by Boreas, beside the cluster of the vine"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood-dipped pine shook its fragrant hair."},{"sp":"N","t":"And about it a serpent, coiled with crooked spine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"milked the delicious nectar of the fine-dripping harvest,","st":"12.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having milked the Bacchic drink with its grim jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping into its throat the draught of the cluster turned to wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the serpent reddened its beard with a purple drop."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mountain-roaming god marveled, watching the serpent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"its throat defiled with a wine-dark drop;","st":"12.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winding its turning trail with spotted scales"},{"sp":"N","t":"it entered a neighboring deep-bosomed hole in the rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shifting serpent, having seen the Euian. And looking upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cluster pregnant with reddening dew, Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"understood the older oracles of prophetic Rhea.","st":"12.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he dug the crags, and with the whetted point"},{"sp":"N","t":"of earth-digging iron he hollowed a recess of the rock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and smoothing the brows of the deepening hollows"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made the trench into the pattern of a fine-grape vat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowing the fresh-blooming clusters with his sharp thyrsus,","st":"12.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"fashioning the pattern of the later-born crook-clawed sickle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the chorus of Satyrs went with him; of whom one"},{"sp":"N","t":"was bent, gathering the vintage, another in a hollow vessel"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the clusters as they were cut, another, tearing the tangled leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook off the green refuse from the wine-loving fruit;","st":"12.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, without thyrsus and without well-whetted iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching his ironless right hand upon the branches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"broke off the tips of the coiling cluster's bunch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crouching hunched, stretching his eye toward the vine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the hollow cavity he spread the poured-out harvest,","st":"12.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"heaping the middle backs of the ravine with grapes, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set the coiling clusters one upon another in the hollow"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched out this way and that, and, as though heaps of a threshing-floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling the whole hollow he gathered them in the cavernous rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and trod the grape with the bounding beat of his feet.","st":"12.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Satyrs, shaking their raving hair into the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"learning a mimicry of like nature from Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding spotted skins of fawns tight at the shoulder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieked the Bacchic melody of a common-tongued echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the harvest with much-leaping feet,","st":"12.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing the Euian cry; and as the wine gushed"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the grape-rich hollow the ravines were purpled;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the harvest, pressed by alternating feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushed up the white foam of a reddening dew."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they drew with the horns of oxen instead of cups,","st":"12.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"which had not yet appeared, whence afterward, from that time,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this divine name belonged to mingled wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, gushing up the moisture of mind-charming Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent the curving track of his feet with a spiraling throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging his right sole for the left as it migrated,","st":"12.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drenched his shaggy beard with the moisture of Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another leapt up, shaken by the sting of drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the dreadful bellowing of the beaten ox-hide;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, drinking the irresistible flood of pain-healing wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpled his dark beard with the rosy draught;","st":"12.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, stretching his tottering gaze upward to a tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spied a half-seen, unveiled Nymph nearby,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he would have crept up into the high-leaved plant"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the mountain wood with the crook-clawed sole of his slipping foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Dionysus restrained him; and about the springs","st":"12.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, with the mind-changing throb of thought-rousing drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursued a watery, unrobed Naiad girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he would have embraced her as she swam with shaggy arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had she not forestalled him and hidden in the deep stream."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to wine-drinking Dionysus alone Rhea gave","st":"12.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"the amethyst, the defender against raging necessity."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many of the well-horned Satyrs with playful sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"reveled, goaded into the dance; of whom one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a new hot sting beneath his heart, escort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraced a Bacchant about the middle with shaggy arm;","st":"12.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, shaken by the sting of mind-wandering drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched the chaste girdle of an unwed maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging back toward Cypris the garments of the disobedient lady,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with hand thrown behind touched her rosy thighs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one reined back an unwilling initiate girl","st":"12.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she kindled the night-danced torch for Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, casting sparing fingers about her breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressed the swelling rim of her firm bosom."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the revel of his sweet harvest Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"entered exulting the caves of the Cybelian goddess,","st":"12.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching out the clustered shoots with flower-loving hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taught Maeonia his sleepless festival."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the thirteenth I shall tell of the countless army and the champion heroes gathering for Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father sent to the divine courts of Rhea","st":"13.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iris to announce to battle-rousing Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"that he should drive out of Asia the arrogant race of Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"untaught in justice, with his avenging thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowing down the horned, sea-fighting son of a river,","st":"13.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"king Deriades, and should teach all nations"},{"sp":"N","t":"the night-danced rites and the wine-dark fruit of the harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"She, with the windy rush of her rowing wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entering the roaring recess of the lion-tended cave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"planted a noiseless step, and in speechless silence","st":"13.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding her slave mouth tight, near the mountain queen"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stood bowed, and as her head was drawn down"},{"sp":"N","t":"she pressed the feet of Rhea with suppliant lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"And her the Corybants, at the unsmiling nod of Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pleased beside the mixing-bowl of the divine table;","st":"13.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in wonder, drinking the pouring of the newborn wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she delighted, driven to frenzy; and heavy-headed the goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowed the counsel of Zeus to the son of Zeus who was present:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Valiant Dionysus, your father bids you"},{"sp":"N","t":"wipe out the race of Indians, untaught in piety;","st":"13.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, lifting the warlike thyrsus in your palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do deeds worthy of the upper air, since the immortal court of Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"will not receive you apart from toils, nor will the Seasons"},{"sp":"N","t":"open the gates of Olympus to you before you have contended;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes came to heaven with difficulty, when with his rod","st":"13.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slew the herdsman Argus, flashing with eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his feet to his hair, and loosed Ares from bonds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Apollo subdued Delphyne and dwelt in the upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did your father, champion of the blessed ones, high-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go up to heaven apart from toils, the leader of the stars,","st":"13.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"before he first bound the threateners of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding the Titans in the Tartarean hollow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you too, after Apollo, after Hermes, having toiled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"have as the wage of your labors to dwell in your father's upper air.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the goddess went to Olympus; and at once all-mother","st":"13.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhea sent forth the host-gathering messenger"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyrrhichus, the dancer of the crash-loving ox-hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to announce the war of Lyaeus as he armed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And gathering a many-shaped army for Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyrrhichus ran through the seats of the ever-flowing cosmos;","st":"13.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bringing all the offspring of Europe and the nations"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the land of Asia he returned to the country of the soft-living Lydians."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the heroic stock of the wide-scattered champions"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of the shaggy Satyrs, blood of the Centaur race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the battle-line of the Sileni of the shaggy-shinned old man","st":"13.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rank of the Bassarids, tell me, Corybantic Muses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I could not sing so many tribes with ten tongues,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor with ten mouths pouring a bronze-voiced echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all that spear-wielding Bacchus gathered; but singing shrill"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the leaders I shall call Homer to help me,","st":"13.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole harbor of fair speech, since wandering sailors"},{"sp":"N","t":"call the dark-haired god the helper of their wandering."},{"sp":"N","t":"First, at the call of Dionysus of the fair thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon came swiftly, honoring his kindred blood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the seven-mouthed soil of his Aonian homeland;","st":"13.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the battle-lines of the Boeotians flowed in, who dwelt in the land"},{"sp":"N","t":"of well-towered Thebes and Onchestus, the haunt of the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Peteon, and Ocalea, and Erythrae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clustered Arne, glorying in Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who dwelt in Mideia, and the little towns of song,","st":"13.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eilesion and Scolus and sea-based Thisbe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dove-haunted harbor of Aphrodite of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the floor of Schoenus and fair-tressed Eleon"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Copae, splendid soil, where I hear there is still"},{"sp":"N","t":"a far-famed lake, named for it, the nurse of eels,","st":"13.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaggy Medeon, and those who got well-pastured Hyle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long-shinned nurse of Tychius the leather-cutter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plain of broad threshing-floors, kept for an oracle beneath the earth,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[19,18],[22,34],[24,38],[30,48],[37,49],[48,52],[55,77]]},{"sp":"N","t":"named for the later-born chariot of Amphiaraus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the city of the Thespians and deep-glened Plataea","st":"13.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watery Haliartus, divided in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the streams of the mountain-poured river from Helicon,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[44,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held farthest Anthedon, neighbor of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the little town of the ever-living fisherman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"water-living Glaucus, and those who held stormy Ascra,","st":"13.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the laurel-bearing homeland of the never-silent shepherd,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the holy city of Graia and of broad-spaced Mycalessus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"named after the mimicry of the throat of Euryale,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Nisaean land and the city named for Coronus."},{"sp":"N","t":"For these as they went toward the eastern slope of the earth","st":"13.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon was champion, and for the victory of the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"his grandfather, the laurel prophet Apollo, sneezed the omen."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another throng of Boeotians was led"},{"sp":"N","t":"by fair-haired Hymenaeus with unscored chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fresh in bloom, beloved of Bromius; and as the boy went","st":"13.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"a grey-haired child-tending champion, Phoenix by name,"},{"sp":"N","t":"followed, wholly like Laocoon, who before, having boarded Argo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ship of Jason, into the land of the Colchians"},{"sp":"N","t":"sailed as companion with Meleager as he armed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such, still a boy, having the youth of a child,","st":"13.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"soft-haired Hymenaeus entered the war of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling on either cheek the hair of his own age;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shield-bearers of the same age accompanied him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who held the city of Aspledon, and Orchomenus"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Minyas, whom the Grace never leaves, the dance-beaten grove of the Loves,","st":"13.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who inhabited Hyria, the god-receiving soil of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which keeps the name of the hospitable Hyrieus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the boundless Giant, from a bed that knew no marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orion of three fathers, leapt up from his mother Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, increased by the threefold seed of the gods,","st":"13.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"a pouring of urine was shaped into a self-perfected birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having wedded the furrow of the ox-hide that perfects generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the flank of Earth brought forth an unsown son of the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held the stranger-receiving soil of the gathered Achaeans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rocky Aulis, the foundation of the archer-goddess,","st":"13.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the heavy-wrathful goddess beside the mountain-haunting altar"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the sacrifice of a lying Iphigeneia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a mountain-roaming doe was burnt in the blameless fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the counterfeit form of the true Iphigeneia, snatched away,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom guile-weaving Odysseus had brought as the bride-to-be","st":"13.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Achilles before the battle, whence Aulis is called"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-adorner of unwedded Iphigeneia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an escorting wind whistled for the ships of the Argives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"noiselessly lashing the surface of the ship-holding calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a recovered breeze for the fawn-slaying king,","st":"13.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the girl, coming late, borne aloft to the land of the Tauri,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was taught the dreadful ordinances of inhospitable cauldrons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"butchering a man, and beside the man-slaying altar"},{"sp":"N","t":"she took alive her sea-dismayed brother Orestes."},{"sp":"N","t":"So great a host of countless Boeotian men came","st":"13.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the Indian war, accompanying Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"With them marched, from beside the wise Delphic rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighboring Phocians of the same age, who held"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seat of Cyparissus and the land of Hyampolis, which I hear"},{"sp":"N","t":"is named for the soil of the Aonian sow, which about her beauty","st":"13.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted a proud neck and strove with Tritogeneia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who got Pytho and the cliff-ringed orchard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Crisa of song, and Daulis and Panopeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Bacchus as neighbor, since laurel Apollo"},{"sp":"N","t":"shared his own inheritance with his brother Dionysus,","st":"13.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"two-headed Parnassus; and for the gathering peoples"},{"sp":"N","t":"the oracular, god-speaking Pythian rock cried out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the tripod self-sounding, and the chattering swell"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the never-silent spring of Castalia bubbled with a wise stream."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the battle-lines of the Euboeans were marshaled by the shield-bearing","st":"13.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"Corybants, tenders of growing Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who, holding the Phrygian gulf beside mountain-haunting Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"circled Bacchus with their ox-hides while he was still an infant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once, veiled in a purple wine-dark robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they found among the crags, a horned babe, where Ino","st":"13.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"had entrusted him to child-tending Mystis, mother of Corymbus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"they then all came from the island of song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Prymneus and rolling-footed Mimas and mountain-running Acmon"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Damneus and shield-brandishing Ocythous, with whom marching"},{"sp":"N","t":"came helmet-glancing Melisseus, running with the Idaean;","st":"13.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once, armed with the witless sting of impiety,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Socus their father robbed of their sea-girdled homeland"},{"sp":"N","t":"after their mother Combe, who bore seven; and they, fleeing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came to the soil of Cnossus, and again were wanderers"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Phrygia from Crete, from Phrygia to Athens,","st":"13.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"foreign dwellers sharing a hearth, until Cecrops"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed Socus with the avenging bronze of Justice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving the land of sea-washed Marathon"},{"sp":"N","t":"they bent a homeward step to the holy soil of the Abantes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earthly race of the earlier Curetes, whose song is of flutes,","st":"13.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose life is the din of sweet-sounding swords, whose care"},{"sp":"N","t":"is the circling of feet in some rhythm and the shielded dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"With them marched the warlike sons of the Abantes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who got beetling Eretria, who got both"},{"sp":"N","t":"Styra and Cerinthus, and the seats of Carystus of song","st":"13.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rocky plain of Dium, and those who held the headland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wavy headland of never-silent Geraestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Styx and the seat of Cotylaeum and the seat of Siris"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ridge of Marmarion and the plain of ancient Aegae;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with them came a people traveling together, whose homeland","st":"13.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"was Chalcis, mother-city of the back-haired Ellopians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seven leaders armed the host, but all had one"},{"sp":"N","t":"spirit for battle; and at a blazing altar they propitiated"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stars that dwell in the path of the Zodiac,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting the war to wanderers equal in number.","st":"13.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sons of Cecrops were armed by Erechtheus, unsated of battles —"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the golden blood of Erechtheus blessed in children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once within the fold of the torch-bearing maiden-chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden self-delivered reared at a male breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the child-tending, grey-eyed, unplowed one, and in shame","st":"13.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"she embraced the boy in her maiden, unaccustomed arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Hephaestus, when the ill-wedded god lame in both legs"},{"sp":"N","t":"sowed his seed in the field with a strange kind of love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling the hot, self-rushing foam of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"such came Erechtheus, champion of the Athenian army,","st":"13.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Siphnus as fellow-contender, a leader of the same city —,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who got the fertile plain of Oenoe, and those who held"},{"sp":"N","t":"the honeyed haunts of the peaks of neighboring Hymettus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the deep-treed precinct of olive-tressed Marathon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held the city of Celeus, and those who got the harbor of Athena,","st":"13.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"seaside Brauron, the empty tomb of Iphigeneia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plain of Thoricus and of Aphidna of fair childbirth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held the Eleusinian land of Deo blessed in children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mystic servants of the basket and of the fruitful goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"born from the blood of Triptolemus, who once, driving","st":"13.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"the viperish chariot of Deo through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the spotted backs of the corn-bearing serpents;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and old Acharneus, brandishing iron armor this way and that,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held it out in abundance to the sons as they armed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ranks of Attica came as newcomers, and with the spear,","st":"13.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the swords of the battle-rousing dwellers hurrying"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the fray, well-helmeted Athens raged in Bacchic frenzy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as they rushed to war the harbor of Phalerum resounded;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a golden cicada, announcing their earlier"},{"sp":"N","t":"earth-born stock, was bound tight in their well-plaited hair.","st":"13.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus too left his fatherland, whom a counterfeit bird"},{"sp":"N","t":"begot, coupling with the ravished bride, daughter of Asopus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eagle, the winged husband of Aegina, high-flying Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from that marriage Aeacus was declared; and beyond all others"},{"sp":"N","t":"he longed to help his kinsman Dionysus;","st":"13.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he marshaled the battle-lines of the Myrmidons with knowing art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who before were ants creeping upon the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"busy with their many-scattered feet, until, changing"},{"sp":"N","t":"their earth-born form from their paltry skin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-ruling Zeus remolded them into another, better body,","st":"13.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an armed host sprouted; for suddenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"the strange-natured, voiceless, unsown swarm of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ant, shaped its body into a speaking mortal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"their champion Aeacus led them, and on his well-wrought shield"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had inscribed the counterfeit Zeus, the wise bird, the sign of his race,","st":"13.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a woman with sparing talons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a river was near, burnt with fire, and close by a girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiably downcast, and though it was a breathless image"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stretched a sidelong eye, as though groaning for her father"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-kneed Asopus, and she seemed to cry this out:","st":"13.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"A fine bride-gift you have given me, having subdued my father.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And of the many-tongued dwellers of Crete the leader was"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterius, having a bright-eyed body, and as much"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he was lovely, so much was he valiant, whom once a bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having loosed for Minos the knot of her chaste maidenhood,","st":"13.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"Androgeneia of Phaestus bore in a Cydonian bed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he then, leading a hundred-citied people to wine-dark Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came honoring the kindred blood of the race"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his father; for Minos was the cousin of Semele,"},{"sp":"N","t":"both of the common stock of Cadmus; and the wide-scattered warriors","st":"13.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"all flowed together to one eager leader,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some fighters from Cnossus, others from Lyctus"},{"sp":"N","t":"joining the armies of Miletus; and with them many"},{"sp":"N","t":"citizens of high-crested Gortyn armed themselves"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dwellers of Rhytium and of fruitful Lycastus &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"13.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the land of Zeus Nodaeus and the foundations of Boebe"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plain of Cisamus, and the fair cities of Cytaeum."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a champion came from Crete; and as he went"},{"sp":"N","t":"the star of Ares, of the same name, shone for Asterius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a prophetic gleam with hotter rays,","st":"13.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"first messenger of the victory to come; but in the battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having conquered, he got a counterfeit longing for an unaccustomed land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiless; for he was not destined to see after the war of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"his ancestral, helmet-glancing cave of the Idaean cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he chose a life away from his city, and instead of Dicte","st":"13.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"the man of Cnossus was a migrant citizen in Scythia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving grey-haired Minos and Androgeneia"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came, wise, to the barbarian nations of the stranger-slaying Colchians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and called them Asterians and gave the Colchians a Cretan"},{"sp":"N","t":"name, to whom nature gave strange ordinances, and leaving","st":"13.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ancestral Cretan stream of Amnisus that had tended him"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drank with shamed lips the alien water of Phasis."},{"sp":"N","t":"Aristaeus alone came slowly, last of all"},{"sp":"N","t":"who dwelt in the land bordering the land of Hellas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who for the sweet honey of his many-celled hives","st":"13.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted a proud neck, and strove with wine-pouring Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the fruitless hope of a honey-sweet victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and all the dwellers of Olympus judged between them;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the son of Phoebus, offering to the immortals the fresh-flowing"},{"sp":"N","t":"streams of his hives, missed the honey-sweet victory,","st":"13.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the gods, receiving the thick stream of the shoot-loving bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had a sharp surfeit of the joyless dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the blessed ones were sated at the third cup, nor did they taste"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a fourth mixed, returning cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though they were very thirsty; but as Bacchus drew for them,","st":"13.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting their minds with the fine-dripping rain of his dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they drank the whole day long the unceasing pouring of wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drunk with the sweet wine each after another marveled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing with delighted heart for cup after cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an unsated gladness of mind-charming wine;","st":"13.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Zeus marveled at the works of the honey-dripping bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cunning travail of the hive-loving birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gift of Aristaeus, and to wine-pouring Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he granted to bear the first prize of a toil-releasing victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"Thence Aristaeus came slowly to the battle of the Indians,","st":"13.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"late, having lulled his earlier anger at the ravisher of his youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the Cyllenian haunt, the seat of Hermes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for not yet had he settled on the earlier island of Merops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet had he stopped the fiery vapor of the thirsty season"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing the life-giving breeze of evil-averting Zeus,","st":"13.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor had he, iron-clad, watching the gleam of the star,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding back Sirius from his smoky fever,"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothed it by night, which, still shooting hot thirsty fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"through the upper air with blazing throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winds cool with their slender breaths;","st":"13.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he still dwelt in the plain of Parrhasia. And as he came"},{"sp":"N","t":"the acorn-eating, wandering Arcadian people armed themselves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who held Lasion and the fair groves of Lycaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rocky Stymphalus, and the little town of song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhipe and Stratie and Mantinea and Enispe","st":"13.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and well-treed Parrhasia, where is the untrodden ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the goddess, still the childbed of first-born Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plain of Pheneus and the father of the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orchomenus rich in sheep, the foundation of the Apidanians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held Arcadia, the city of Arcas, whom once his mother","st":"13.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Callisto bore to Zeus, and his father, fixing him in the vault"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the stars, called him hail-bringing Boötes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so great a host did Aristaeus arm with Arcadian spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging pasturing hounds into the battle against fighting men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he whom once Cyrene, another deer-driving Artemis,","st":"13.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lion-slaying lady, bore by the love of Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when fair Apollo brought her, a migrant, to sandy Libya"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a bride-adorning, ravishing chariot."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving his prophetic laurel, his father Apollo himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed him with his own palms as he hurried;","st":"13.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he gave his son a bow, and fitted to his hand a cunning"},{"sp":"N","t":"ox-hide shield, and fastened the hollow quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"let down across his back with a raw-hide baldric."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to him from Sicily came far-shooting Achates,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shield-bearers of the same age accompanied him,","st":"13.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"a great host of Cillyrians and Elymi, and those who dwelt about"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seat of the Palici, and those who beside the lake of Catana"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelt in a city neighboring the Sirens, whom to Achelous"},{"sp":"N","t":"rosy Terpsichore bore from an ox-horned bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"joined to a stormy husband;","st":"13.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held Camarina, where with roaring stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady Hipparis belches its crooked water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the holy city of Hybla, and those who dwelt near Aetna,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where craters of fire from the kindled rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"gush up the hot radiance of Typhon's bed,","st":"13.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who shared out houses beside beetling Pelorus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the island plain of sea-roaring Pachynus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Sicilian Arethusa, where wandering Alpheius"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeps as a migrant, luxuriant with a Pisan garland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ferrying the passable swell, and through the surface of the sea","st":"13.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"draws his higher, unwetted water, a slave of Eros,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his hot fire kindled through the cold water."},{"sp":"N","t":"After them came Faunus, leaving the fire-sealed"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pelorian floor of the cliff of three-crested Sicily,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Circe bore, joined with the Cronion of the deep,","st":"13.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sister of Aeetes, rich in drugs, who beside a thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelt in the deep-shaded circles of a rocky hall."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Libyans marched by the western slope of the land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling in the cloud-neighboring cities of wandering Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there, carried by the breezes of opposing winds,","st":"13.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus dwelt for a time, having the Sithonian lady"},{"sp":"N","t":"as fellow-sailor, Harmonia still a maiden, for whose beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"war-breeding rumor armed the neighboring adversaries,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom the Libyan host called Charis instead — for a softer"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bistonian earthly grace, another, had sprung up,","st":"13.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"from whom also comes the Hill of the Graces in Libya —, for whose beauty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by the witless sting of a ravishing battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dreadful barbarian Ares armed himself, mad with love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wilderness-dwelling Moorish people; but brandishing"},{"sp":"N","t":"with woman-mad hands the spear of Libyan Athena","st":"13.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"her husband fought as champion of Harmonia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and put to flight the whole race of the western Ethiopians"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Zeus in armor, with Ares and Cytherea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there too, as they tell, beside the Tritonian lake"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering Cadmus lay beside rosy-faced Harmonia,","st":"13.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Hesperid nymphs wove a song, from whose garden"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris together with the Loves adorned the fair-wedded bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hanging the golden harvest in the bridal chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bride-gift worthy of the bride's love, from whose leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia and Cadmus beside the treasure-rich bridal bed","st":"13.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled their locks with wealthy clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of the bridal rose; and the bride was the softer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing golden gifts, the prize of golden Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rousing a festal melody of the starry lyre"},{"sp":"N","t":"her mother's father, bowed Libyan Atlas, whirled heaven","st":"13.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a ball with his bounding sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sang a song of harmony with neighboring voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as memorials of the yoking love of his bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus gave footholds to the Libyan land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"building a hundred cities, and gave to each","st":"13.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"hard-to-tread walls raised high with stone towers."},{"sp":"N","t":"Keeping his memory, warriors marched out"},{"sp":"N","t":"as champions of Enyo for Bromius as he fought,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling in the foundations neighboring the Moon at its birth"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the midday haunts of Zeus Asbystes,","st":"13.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horned prophet, where often once Ammon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the thrice-coiled likeness of a ram's horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Western Zeus, gave oracles with prophetic lips;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who dwelt by the stream of Chremetes and those who beside the water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Cinyps dwelt on the sandy floor of the parched land,","st":"13.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"Auschisae and Bacales together, whom beyond others,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting in Ares, the Zephyrian bend nourished."},{"sp":"N","t":"So great was the hundred-citied people; and of the coming"},{"sp":"N","t":"multitude the leader was Crataegonus, whom once a girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"Anchiroe bore beside the flat rock of her father Chremetes","st":"13.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"to light-minded Psyllus in a short-lived bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding in her arms a god-fighting bridegroom, whose fruits"},{"sp":"N","t":"once the hot wind Notus burned with thirsty breath;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he, arming the helmet-glancing war of ships,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathered a sea-fighting swarm, so that by an avenging ordinance","st":"13.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"he might raise Enyo against the winds of the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"eager to kill flaming Notus; and near the island"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Aeolus the shield-brandishing fleet came, but against the madness"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the man the winds armed themselves, spear-hurlers"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a stormy tumult, lashing the ship,","st":"13.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking a gathered, conspiring breeze as their helper,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entombed both the army and Psyllus in the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And shield-bearers of Thracian Samos flowed together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom their lord sent forth, of the thick-haired beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-kneed Emathion, having snowy hair,","st":"13.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"men like the Titans in their limbs, who held both"},{"sp":"N","t":"seaside Myrmex and flowery Saoce,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the land of Teumerion and the groves of the fair-meadowed field"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Phesias, shaded with a wooded thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and most holy Zerynthus, the foundation of the unsleeping Corybants,","st":"13.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the maiden called daughter of Perses, where"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cliffs are haunted by the revel of mystic torches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who dwelt about Brontion beneath the many-pointed"},{"sp":"N","t":"base of the land, and those which beside the neighboring sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"I hear are called the Paths of Poseidon of the deep.","st":"13.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"So many ranks came of one age, and they confirmed"},{"sp":"N","t":"their kindred descent from first-born Electra;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there Ares, Zeus, Cytherea gave Harmonia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stock of the upper air, blood of the sea, to Cadmus, the helper"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the gods, as wedded wife without bride-price.","st":"13.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"For them as they armed with Dionysus of the fair thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seventh star of Electra rose through the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a favorable sign of battle, and about the victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"the answering echo of the cry of the Pleiades sounded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to Dionysus who bore the blood of their sister,","st":"13.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave the army a like courage; and of those who came"},{"sp":"N","t":"the leader was Ogyrus, a second Ares in war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-headed Ogyrus, having the likeness of the Giants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"his whole body was unbending, and from his head"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sinew of his neck upon his back-haired shoulders","st":"13.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"locks like spine-bearing hedgehogs"},{"sp":"N","t":"flowed down as far as his loins; and he had a neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"long, huge, like the neck of a rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his father's barbarian character; nor did any other"},{"sp":"N","t":"better than he come to the eastern battle of the Indians","st":"13.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"apart from Dionysus; and he swore an oath by Victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"to destroy all the Indian land alone with his spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold son of Ares, leaving his Pimpleia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Oeagrus, a citizen of the Bistonian land, reveled forth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving Orpheus on the knees of Calliope,","st":"13.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"an infant still tended at the newly flowing breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Cyprian battle-lines were marshaled by proud Litrus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fair-haired Lapethus; and many armed themselves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who got Spheceia, the sea-beaten rim of the island,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cyprus, the god-receiving island of the well-winged Loves,","st":"13.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"named for self-born Cypris, of which Cyprus once"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus, tracing round its headlands with the point of his deep-sea trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rounded out a shape like a dolphin —"},{"sp":"N","t":"for when the fertile dew of Urania, streaming with male"},{"sp":"N","t":"gore, shaped the childbed foam","st":"13.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought the Paphian to birth, toward the land of horned Cyprus"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dolphin ran over the swell with an intelligent heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting Aphrodite seated upon its crest —,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held the plain of Hylates and the foundations of Sestus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Tamasus and Tembrus and the little town of Erythrae","st":"13.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the deep-treed precinct of mountain-haunting Panacrum;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from Soli a great host armed itself, and from Lapethus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which afterward they called by the name of the leader,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who then gathered the people, and in the fair-thyrsus tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"died and was buried and left his name to the citizens;","st":"13.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held the city of Cinyras, a rock still named"},{"sp":"N","t":"for first-born Cinyras, and the plain of the seat of Urania,"},{"sp":"N","t":"named for the hollow of the upper air, because it reared"},{"sp":"N","t":"citizens flashing like the pattern of the heavenly stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held Crapaseia, the sea-crowned soil of the land,","st":"13.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Paphos, the garland-bearing harbor of the soft-haired Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foothold of Aphrodite rising from the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where is the bridal water of the sea-born Paphian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely Setrachus, where often, taking a garment,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris cloaked the bathed son of Myrrha,","st":"13.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the city of first-born Perseus, for whom once Teucer,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[36,40],[41,44]]},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving Salamis and the anger of Telamon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"built the walls of a younger Salamis of song."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the soft throng of the Lydians flowed in, who held both"},{"sp":"N","t":"fine-pebbled Cimpsus and beetling Itone,","st":"13.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held broad Torebion, and those who held the nurses"},{"sp":"N","t":"of wealth, Sardis of fair childbirth, of one age with the dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the grape-tressed Bacchic land, where for his mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus of the vine, holding a cup full of wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first mixed it for Rhea, and named the city Cerassae,","st":"13.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the peaks of Oanus, and those who got the stream of Hermus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watery Metallum, where, spitting out the Pactolian silt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the yellow wealth of the dew glitters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a great host of Stataloe was armed, where Typhon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing up the hot breath of the fire-heated thunderbolt,","st":"13.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"burned the neighboring country, and with the stormy smoke"},{"sp":"N","t":"of burning Typhon the cliffs were turned to ash,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as his heads withered with the limb-devouring spark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaving the fragrant temple of Lydian Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"a priest without iron fought with goading speech,","st":"13.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"with speech for a spear, and not with cutting iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restraining with his tongue the obedient son of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a furious mouth for spear, a word for sword, a voice for shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring this word from a god-invoking throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Stand, wretch\"; and the flaming Giant, by the mystic art","st":"13.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unbreakable speech, was fixed in a wise bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing a man armed with an intelligent lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held in an ironless fetter by avenging speech;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the dread many-armed Giant so tremble at the hurler"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the thunderbolt as at the man-breaking initiate","st":"13.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting a chattering missile with his tongue, and he gave way, laboring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pierced with speaking wounds by the sharp word;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having a wound of fire, pierced with a hot spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Typhon labored under another, hotter, intelligent fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rooted his standing, unshaken viperish sole","st":"13.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"by necessity, fixed to his mother Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wounded in an unscored body by a bloodless spear-point."},{"sp":"N","t":"But those things the age brought among men of former days."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these, beating the ground shrilly with well-rhythmed sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Stabius and Stamnus armed for the fray of the Indians;","st":"13.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watching the dancing host skipping about"},{"sp":"N","t":"you would say such a word, that the champion leads"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a dance, not into battle, bringing an armed man;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for as they came, striking up a dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the battle-rousing Mygdonian lyre roared toward the fray,","st":"13.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending, instead of a dance, the host-rousing echo of war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the trumpets of war were the pipes of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the twin yoked Berecyntian flutes cried out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ox-hides bellowed with bronze crashes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a din struck on both sides from heavy-thundering hands.","st":"13.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Phrygians marched beside the rank of the battle-rousing Lydians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who got Budeia, and the little town of song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tree-tressed Temeneia, the well-shaded grove of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who dwelt in Dresie and Obrimus, which mingles"},{"sp":"N","t":"its own water with the crooked streams of Maeander,","st":"13.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plain named for Doias, and those who dwelt in Celaenae"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the golden roofs and the images of the Gorgon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with them marched also those who got to dwell in the cities"},{"sp":"N","t":"neighboring Sangarius, and the foundations of the Helespian land."},{"sp":"N","t":"Their champion led them, leaving snaky Dirce,","st":"13.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"Priasus, a migrant citizen of the Aonian land;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for when rainy Zeus deluged the plain of Phrygia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring high-running water with rainy seas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the oaks were hidden, and in the thorn-bearing glens"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thirsty hills surged like rivers,","st":"13.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"then, leaving his dripping house covered with rain"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the air-roaming flood, the hurler-down of halls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Priasus migrated to the bosom of the Aonian land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the death-bearing rainy water of Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and always among foreign men shedding tears","st":"13.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"he remembered Sangarius and sought his accustomed spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the alien water of the Aonian river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and late did highest Zeus soothe the ill-snowed swell"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the watery necessity, and from the peaks of Sipylus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drove back the water of flooded Phrygia;","st":"13.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Earth-shaker with his trident transferred the whole flood"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the deep caverns of the unfathomed sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the cliffs were laid bare of the roaring snowstorm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and then, leaving the soil of Boeotia in return,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Priasus, wise too late, entered his own homeland,","st":"13.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"and returning embraced his heavy-kneed father"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the harmless trail of his arm, whom for the sake of his pious deeds"},{"sp":"N","t":"great Zeus had taken alive from the rainy destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom they call Brombius; and from the Phrygian gulf"},{"sp":"N","t":"exultant warriors circled about Priasus.","st":"13.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Miletus, fresh in bloom, coming apart from his father"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterius, came as companion to Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his brother Caunus as fellow-contender, who then,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leading the people of the Carians, still a boy, entered the war of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for not yet did he weave a guile-weaving song of unhappy love","st":"13.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the sting of his sister who knew nothing, nor did he sing"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Hera herself, the sister joined with Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in an answering love of an emulous bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the Latmian ox-stall of the unsleeping herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blessing beneath love, beside the neighboring rock,","st":"13.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom Endymion, the care of desire-stricken Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but still Byblis was a lover of maidenhood, and still Caunus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was learning the hunt, ignorant of kindred loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor yet, when her soft-haired brother had fled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had the girl, rained upon with tears, transformed her body,","st":"13.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gushed forth the watery stream of a mourning spring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with him bold warriors pressed on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who dwelt in Mycale, and those who got the crooked water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the returning river that sinks into the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of crooked Maeander, creeping through his haunts.","st":"13.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"So many ranks came; and at the emulous march"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the gathering peoples the Cybelian courts rang,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the streets of the Mygdonian city were thronged."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XIV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Turn your mind to the fourteenth: there Rhea arms the whole divine host for the Indian war."},{"sp":"N","t":"And swift-sandaled Rhea, beside the mountain-haunting manger,","st":"14.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"having bound tight the shaggy necks of her lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted her wind-swift ankle to run with the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing the hollows of the air with her sandal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gathering the divine battle-lines for Lyaeus","st":"14.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"she passed through the seats of the cosmos like a wing or a thought,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the south, to the north, to the west, to the slope of Dawn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the oaks and the rivers she joined a single call,"},{"sp":"N","t":"summoning the Naiads and the Hadryad ranks of the wood."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the divine offspring, hearing the Cybelian cry,","st":"14.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathered from every side. And from on high to the land of the Lydians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing an unerring step, Rhea came down from the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and returning she lifted the nightly mystic pine-torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"warming the Mygdonian air a second time with her fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"But after the mortal heroic stock of champions","st":"14.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"teach me the divine army also, Phoebean breezes."},{"sp":"N","t":"First, from the fire-pointed cliff of Lemnos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stormy rumor beside the mystic pine-torch of Samos"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed two Cabeiri, sons of Hephaestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing their mother's kindred name, whom before","st":"14.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thracian Cabeiro bore to the heavenly smith,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Alcon and Eurymedon, masters of the forge."},{"sp":"N","t":"And grim warriors gathered from Crete,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Idaean Dactyls, dwellers of the rocky cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Earth-born Corybants, their companions, whose race once","st":"14.25","hvShift6":[[0,0],[20,21],[21,29],[24,35],[31,38]]},{"sp":"N","t":"sprang up self-perfected from the earth for Rhea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"they girdled the newly born infant Zeus, beside the birth-aiding rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with shield-bearing dances,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a mountain-crashing, deceiving revel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving the air to frenzy; and as the bronze was beaten","st":"14.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cloud-neighboring echo roared in the ears of Cronus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away the boyhood of Cronion with ox-hides;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as champion of the dance-weaving Corybants there led"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyrrhichus and shield-brandishing Idaeus, with whom marching"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cyrbas of Cnossus, named alike, armed the shifting tribes.","st":"14.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the envious Telchines came as newcomers to the war of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathered from the deep hollow of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking a huge spear-point with long arm"},{"sp":"N","t":"came Lycus, and Scelmis followed Damnameneus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steering the ancestral Poseidonian chariot of the sea,","st":"14.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-wandering migrants after the land of Tlepolemus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving water-dwelling spirits, whom once"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thrinax with Macareus and splendid Auges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sons of Helios, drove unwilling, cutting them off"},{"sp":"N","t":"from their ancestral land; and pursued, drawing with heavy-jealous","st":"14.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"hands the water of Styx,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[40,23]]},{"sp":"N","t":"they made the orchard of fruitful Rhodes, their nurse, unsown,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[40,54]]},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the fields with Tartarean waters."},{"sp":"N","t":"After them came the gentle twofold race of the Centaurs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Pholus, who had the form of a horse, went Chiron,","st":"14.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"strange-natured, untamed, having an unbridled beard."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the battle-lines of the Cyclopes flowed in; in battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"hills were hurled by their uncorseleted hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"as rocky spears, and cliffs were their shields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a crested, ravine-cut peak was their helmet,","st":"14.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Sicilian sparks were their flaming arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darting the radiance of their accustomed forge"},{"sp":"N","t":"the warriors armed themselves with fire-bearing palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Brontes and Steropes and Euryalus and Elatreus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Arges and Trachius and exultant Halimedes.","st":"14.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"But so great and such a one was left out of the war alone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloud-neighboring Polyphemus, offspring of the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because another Eros, dearer than war, who travels the waters"},{"sp":"N","t":"held him back to stay there; for looking upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"Galatea half-seen he made din to the neighboring sea,","st":"14.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a maiden-loving echo with his bridal pipe."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the dwellers of the crags, from their self-roofed halls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the name of Pan, their wilderness-dwelling father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pans of one age armed themselves, on whose human"},{"sp":"N","t":"form was mingled the face of a shaggy goat;","st":"14.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having the counterfeit form of a well-horned head"},{"sp":"N","t":"there were twelve horn-drawing Pans, and they are born"},{"sp":"N","t":"of one first-born Pan, the mountain-haunting father."},{"sp":"N","t":"One they named Celaeneus for the witness of his form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another Argennus, named for his nature; and to Aegicorus","st":"14.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"they gave a fitting name, since beside the pastoral flock"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had his fill of pressing the milk of goats' teats;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another divine Pan was called Eugeneius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an abundant meadow of a beard in his locks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Daphoeneus armed himself with pastoral Omestes;","st":"14.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phobos accompanied shaggy-shinned Philamnus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Glaucus came as companion to Xanthus; for with his answering"},{"sp":"N","t":"limbs Glaucus was of the same color as the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grey-gleaming, and Xanthus, having yellow-colored hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received such a name, a horned citizen of the cliff;","st":"14.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Argus came, bearing snowy hair."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with them were two Pans of the same age, whom Hermes begot"},{"sp":"N","t":"joining in chosen love with twin Nymphs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"one, seeking the bed of mountain Sosa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sowed, the prophetic one, god-speaking, full of the oracle,","st":"14.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"Agreus, wrapped in the beast-slaying care of the hunt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the other, Nomius, dear to the pasturing of sheep, when he passed"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the couch of the country Nymph Penelope,"},{"sp":"N","t":"devoted to the shepherd's pipe. With them Phorbas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raw-eating, unsated, kept a common journey.","st":"14.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"And old Silenus, propping his palm upon a fennel-stalk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed himself, the twofold-natured, horn-bearing son of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing three revel-loving sons; for into the fray"},{"sp":"N","t":"Astraeus armed himself, Maron went, Leneus followed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightening the hands of their mountain-wandering father","st":"14.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"with age-tending clubs; and the sluggish body"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the strengthless old men was lifted on a vine-staff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose years were very many, from whom sprang the hot"},{"sp":"N","t":"twofold race of the much-wedding Satyrs."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the horned Satyrs were marshaled by the leaders","st":"14.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"Poemenius and Thiasus and Hypsiceros and Orestes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phlegraeus accompanied horned Napaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Gemon came, bold Lycon armed himself; and to the deep-drinking,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughing Petraeus followed play-loving Phereus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mountain-roaming Lamis kept a common journey","st":"14.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Lenobius, and Scirtus reveled running with Oestrus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with Pherespondus went Lycus, the sounding herald,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Pronomus adorned in wits, whom Hermes begot"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoking a mighty woman in secret wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her whom once Dorus begot, a shoot of the race of Zeus,","st":"14.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"root of the stock of Hellen, and from first-born Dorus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Achaean blood of the Dorian race sprang;"},{"sp":"N","t":"to these Eiraphiotes entrusted the prize and the staff"},{"sp":"N","t":"of their father, the heavenly herald who increases the mind."},{"sp":"N","t":"Always drunk with wine-loving cups","st":"14.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"is the whole bold-hearted race of the Satyrs, but in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"only threateners, always fleeing Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lions apart from battle, but hares in wars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing dancers, more skilled than others"},{"sp":"N","t":"at drawing delicious wine from the wine-holding bowl;","st":"14.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"few of them are fighters, whom bold Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"taught every kind of care of the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to marshal a battle-line; and as Lyaeus armed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some hid their bodies in untanned ox-hides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others strengthened themselves with the shaggy skins of lions,","st":"14.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"others put on the grim coverings of leopards,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others armed themselves with long-branched clusters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others girdled about their breasts the dappled tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"of long-horned deer, a match for the stars;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and on their temples, on either side of the brow,","st":"14.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharp-stretched points of horn lengthened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sparse hair grew on the jagged head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showing at the tip above their slanting eyes, and both ears"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winged breezes fanned as they went,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched straight, flapping against their shaggy jaws","st":"14.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"at full length, and a horse's tail, stretched down the back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright, coiled about, flowed from the loins."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another stock of strange nature came, the Centaurs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shaggy race of the well-horned Pheres, to whom Hera gave"},{"sp":"N","t":"another man-shaped, horn-bearing body; for once","st":"14.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"they were children of the water-born Naiads in mortal-seeming shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom they call the Hyades, the river stock of Lamus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they nursed the fair-born offspring of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus still breathing of the much-stitched birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"child-tending guardians of Dionysus who may not be gazed upon,","st":"14.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"having no strange form; and in the dark hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"often they embraced the boy in a bent arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he babbled of the upper air, the ancestral seat of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still a child, a wise infant. And now"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was wholly like a newborn kid, hidden","st":"14.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"within the fold, and he thickened his body with long hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changed in appearance, and pouring the bleating of guileful teeth"},{"sp":"N","t":"he imitated a counterfeit hoof with goat's footsteps;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now, wearing a lying female shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he appeared a mimic girl in saffron robes,","st":"14.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"fresh in bloom, and leading astray the mind of envious Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched a female cry with answering lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clamped a fragrant veil upon his locks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing much-wrought women's robes; and about the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his breast he cast a band, and the upright rim of a breast &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"14.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a maiden's girdle, and, as though a knot of maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fitted a purple circling band to his loins."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the guile was profitless, since Hera learned it from on high,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling everywhere the all-seeing eye of her face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the ever-changing shape of Lyaeus;","st":"14.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she was enraged at the guardians of Bromius; and receiving"},{"sp":"N","t":"from Thessalian Achlys the guileful flowers of an herb"},{"sp":"N","t":"she poured sleep upon the heads of the bewitched guardians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping poisonous charms from their hair;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and smearing a soft magic ointment upon their faces","st":"14.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"she changed the earlier human form of their look;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for them a long-eared shape of nature appeared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an upright horse's tail rose through the loins"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing the hips of its shaggy-breasted bearer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an ox-horn sprouted at the temple,","st":"14.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the eyes widened in the long-horned brow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crooked locks grew upon their heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and white-toothed jaws lengthened in their chins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and strange, self-perfected, from the loins to the tips of the feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"abundant hair flowed down their shaggy necks.","st":"14.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaders marshaled them all, twelve in number,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Spargeus and dance-beating Gleneus, and, strange in nature,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grape-tressed Ceteus came running with Eurybius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Petraeus accompanied Riphonus, and the deep-drinking"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aesacus and Orthaon marched together, with whom","st":"14.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"Amphithemis and Faunus made a single journey,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Nomeion came as companion to well-horned Phanes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another twofold race of Centaurs armed itself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Cyprian, from the time when Cypris ran like the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the footstep of her desiring father,","st":"14.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she see her own father as a lawless husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zeus the father gave way, leaving untouched by marriage"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swifter, untraceable, refusing Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of the bed of Cypris he sowed in the field"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the loving rain of child-begetting plowshares;","st":"14.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the earth, receiving the bridal dew of Cronion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shot forth a strange-natured, horned generation."},{"sp":"N","t":"With them as they armed the Bacchants ran together in one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some from a Maeonian cleft, others darting"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the hill above the sky-high peaks of Sipylus.","st":"14.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"And trailing-robed mountain Nymphs with male spirit"},{"sp":"N","t":"raged along with the fair-thyrsus warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they who at the much-whirling turning-post of returning years"},{"sp":"N","t":"lived on for a long span, some dwelling on the cliffs"},{"sp":"N","t":"as sheep-tending neighbors of the shepherds, others leaving","st":"14.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wooded groves and the ridge of the wild forest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Meliae grown together with an oak of their own age; all these then"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurried together into the fray, some taking"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze-backed drums, the instruments of Cybelian Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others with their locks roofed in coiling ivy,","st":"14.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"others girdled with viperish clusters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they lifted a whetted thyrsus in their hands, whom then the Lydian"},{"sp":"N","t":"maenads accompanied, fearless, into the war of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of them the revel-loving Bassarids, better in knowing art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurried on, the nurses of Dionysus,","st":"14.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aegle and Callichore and Eupetale and Ione"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laughing Calyce and Bryusa, companion of the Seasons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Silene and Rhode and Ocynoe and Ereutho"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Acrete and Methe, and there followed, companion of Harpe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rosy Oenanthe and silver-footed Lycaste,","st":"14.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"Stesichore and Prothoe; and, laughter-loving, the old"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine-heavy Trygie armed herself last of all."},{"sp":"N","t":"Each led his own chosen host for Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and over them all Eiraphiotes led, roaring with fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing, conspicuous; and dancing into the fray","st":"14.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted no shield, no furious spear, no sword at his shoulder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set no helmet on his unshorn hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no bronze covering for his unbreakable head, but he clamped"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unplaited hair of his head in a serpent bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"circling a grim garland on his temples; and instead of a wrought,","st":"14.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"cunning greave up to the top of the knee"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set his silvery feet in purple buskins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fastening a shaggy fawnskin upon his breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a dappled breastplate, a pattern scored with stars;"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his left hand he had a horn crammed with sweet wine,","st":"14.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"golden, well-made, and from the wine-pouring horn"},{"sp":"N","t":"an upright trail of the drinker's dew flowed down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in his right hand he lifted the goading thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wrapped in wine-dark ivy, and at the topmost cluster"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bronze-heavy point was shaded by the leaves;","st":"14.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fitted a golden circling band about his loins."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when Dionysus within the Corybantic court"},{"sp":"N","t":"had put on the golden, well-made array of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the fair-weather haunts of dance-delighting Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"he passed Maeonia; and with the mountain-ranging Bacchants","st":"14.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"warriors rushed along with the clustered god;"},{"sp":"N","t":"some, steersmen of the well-wheeling wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried the young shoots of the new planting of Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many ranks of mules went, and on their backs"},{"sp":"N","t":"they lifted jars of the nectar of the vine;","st":"14.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and on the enduring backs of slow asses they set"},{"sp":"N","t":"crimson rugs and dappled skins of fawns;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and other wine-drinkers, along with golden cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought silver mixing-bowls, the weapons of the table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Corybants bustled beside the grey-gleaming manger,","st":"14.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding the necks of leopards with a yoking strap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made lions fast in ivy-bound harness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping their lips with a threatening bit."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Centaur, having a shaggy, bristling beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-bidden stretched his willing neck to the yoke, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"14.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span> and having, far more than the Satyrs, a longing for sweet wine,","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"a half-finished man mingled with a horse, he whinnied,"},{"sp":"N","t":"eager to lift Dionysus on his shoulders."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, seated on the rim of his fair-branched chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the stream of Sangarius, about the Phrygian gulf of the land,","st":"14.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"passed by the mourning rock of stony Niobe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the stone, seeing in tears the Indian throng"},{"sp":"N","t":"striving against Lyaeus, cried out again with mortal voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Do not prepare a god-fighting battle, witless Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"against the son of Zeus, lest Bacchus make you too,","st":"14.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"who threaten war, into stone, as Apollo did,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weeping, a pattern like my rocky shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest beside the stream of the river named for him you see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indian Orontes, the son-in-law of Deriades, fallen."},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhea in anger is stronger than the archer-goddess;","st":"14.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"flee Bacchus, the brother of Phoebus; for I am ashamed"},{"sp":"N","t":"to pour tears for strangers when Indians are slain.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So the stone cried out, and silence sealed it again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god, after the floor of the Phrygian cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set foot in Ascania. And all the gathered citizens,","st":"14.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"to whom Iobacchus held out his harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the rites and welcomed the dances,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bending their necks to unconquered Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wishing for the calm of a bloodless peace;"},{"sp":"N","t":"such was the horned army of the Bacchoi, with whom the Bacchants","st":"14.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed themselves for the fray. And for sleepless Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"all night the heaven thundered, weaving a starry,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fire-wheeling trail, since then with witnessing fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhea prophesied the end of the Indian-slaying victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at dawn the god went to battle, driving out the insolence","st":"14.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the dark men, so that he might unyoke the slave neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Lydians and the dweller of Phrygia and the citizen of Ascania"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the harness of a frightful tyranny."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then Bacchus sent to them two heralds of Enyo"},{"sp":"N","t":"to declare the message, either to flee or to fight;","st":"14.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as they went goat-herding Pan marched with them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a luxuriant beard that shadowed his whole breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"But swift-sandaled Hera, in the likeness of an Indian body,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bade woolly-haired Melaneus not to lift the wine-dark thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Astraeis, the spear-wielding leader of men,","st":"14.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor to honor the shout of the wine-loving Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but to raise a truceless war against Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she spoke some words, exhorting the champion of the Indians:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Sweet is he who fears the soft rank of women."},{"sp":"N","t":"Astraeis, make war; arm yourself too, Celaeneus,","st":"14.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"having bronze to cut down clustered Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a thyrsus is no match for a spear. But, Celaeneus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beware maddened Deriades, lest he subdue you"},{"sp":"N","t":"for shrinking from a paltry, ironless woman.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded them, and the goddess plunged into the air,","st":"14.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stepmother angry at battle-steadfast Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the heralds of Bromius came away; and Astraeis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, overbearing, with loveless threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raged, pursuing the ox-horned Satyrs and Pan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dishonoring the heralds of gentle Dionysus.","st":"14.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"And they bent a fleeing step with the fearful sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a homeward foot toward battle-rousing Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bacchus armed his host against the opposing ranks of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the womanish war escape gloomy Celaeneus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaping untraceable he armed the whole host of the Indians;","st":"14.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Astraeis, swelling his battle-steadfast sting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"took his stand about the roaring stream of the Astacid lake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"awaiting the coming of Dionysus of the vine."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the leaders had marshaled every rank of both,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unequally yoked peoples of the two armies,","st":"14.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a scream the dark Indians went to the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Thracian cranes, when, fleeing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wintry lash and the pouring of the airy rain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they swoop in flocks upon the heads of the Pygmies"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the streams of Tethys, and with sharp beak","st":"14.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"destroy the feeble blood of a paltry race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flying like clouds above the horn of Ocean;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the other side the warriors raged into battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbending servants of battle-rousing Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the battle-lines of the Bassarids flowed in; and as they gathered","st":"14.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"one girdled her head with a viperish bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another bound her hair tight with fragrant ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another armed her palm with the bronze-bearing thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad, while another let fall down her neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"the loosened curls of her long hair, free of bonds,","st":"14.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Maenalian girl unveiled, and upon both her shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind fanned her unplaited locks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another shook rattles fitted with twin bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tossing her coiling tresses upon her head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, gripped by a leap of madness, in her palms","st":"14.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"beating with her hands the heavy-roaring back of an ox-hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed out an upright echo answering the noise of battle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and thyrsi were spears, and covered with leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bronze-forged point of the vine shaft lay hidden;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, longing for blood-red tumult,","st":"14.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoked bonds of raw-devouring serpents upon her neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another put on a dappled covering of leopards"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon her breast, another over her skin like a tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spotted hides of crag-loving fawns,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding tight the dappled covering of a deer;","st":"14.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, holding the cub of a shaggy-breasted lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nursed it at a counterfeit human milky breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one bound a thrice-coiled serpent in her harmless bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"as an inner girdle, gaping beside her neighboring thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hissing gently, the sleepless watcher of the maidenhood","st":"14.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the wine-loving girl as she slept;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, bearing her soles bare of sandals over the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"treading brambles and sharp-haired thorns with her feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"planted a standing step on a whetted wild-pear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, darting upon a long-shanked camel,","st":"14.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut through its curving neck with her reaping thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stumbling with blind feet along the path"},{"sp":"N","t":"it was carried half-seen, and with much-bending course"},{"sp":"N","t":"the headless body of the roaming camel hurried on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, striking the deepening ground with tottering hoof,","st":"14.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"it slipped self-rolling on its back in the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, leading her track to the cattle-rearing ridge of the wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seized the hide of a bull raging uncontrollably,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with grim nails from its scored neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"stripped the unpierced covering of the bull;","st":"14.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another drew out all its entrails; and one could see"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled, unsandaled maiden high upon a rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about the rough headland of the cliff;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did she shudder at the hard-to-tread peak, nor did the sharp-set"},{"sp":"N","t":"claw of the hill score the unsandaled foot of the girl.","st":"14.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"And in great number here and there beside the mouth of the Astacid lake"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indian race was cut down by Curetic iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the warriors circled the battle-lines of the foe"},{"sp":"N","t":"with answering weapons, and imitated the rhythm"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the shield-bearing dance with the spiral beat of their feet;","st":"14.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifting a crested peak with shaggy palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting off the topmost heads of a mountain, Leneus armed himself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a jagged point against the adversaries;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Bacchant shrieked around, and a Bassarid hurled"},{"sp":"N","t":"a vine point, and many male heads of the dark-skinned","st":"14.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"race were cut down by a female thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Eupetale armed herself, cleaving a bold man"},{"sp":"N","t":"with her murderous cluster, and with grape-loving leaf"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sent the goading ivy as the crusher of iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Stesichore of the fair clusters skipped in the tumult,","st":"14.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drove off the race of the foe with a man-breaking whirl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the heavy-roaring cymbals of twin bronze."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was much battle on both sides; the pipe roared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the battle-rousing pipe, and the flute of Enyo crashed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Bassarids shrieked; and as the tumult rose","st":"14.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"the black air, bellowing with thunderous crashes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesied from Zeus the coming victory for Bromius."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a great swarm fell; and the whole thirsty field"},{"sp":"N","t":"was reddened with wet gore, and the mouth of the Astacid lake"},{"sp":"N","t":"murmured blood-dipped, mingled with the slaughter of Indians.","st":"14.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"But the god pitied his adversaries with playful heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"and poured the prize of drunkenness into the streams, and from the currents"},{"sp":"N","t":"he changed the snowy nature of the water to yellow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the river murmured, dragging honey-flowing currents,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made its outpourings drunk; and as the currents were changed","st":"14.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"fragrant breezes of the newly poured drunkenness breathed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the banks reddened; and a proud Indian, drinking,"},{"sp":"N","t":"broke out with such a much-wondering voice from his lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"A strange and unbelievable drink I have seen; this is not"},{"sp":"N","t":"the silvery milk of goats, and not black like water,","st":"14.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor is it such as I have seen in the many-celled hives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which the humming bee brings to birth in sweet wax;","hvShift6":[[0,6],[17,21]]},{"sp":"N","t":"but it has a fair-breathing scent that delights the mind."},{"sp":"N","t":"A man thirsty in the much-heating vapor of the burning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing a little poured water in his palms,","st":"14.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"at once shakes off the tempest of his parching thirst;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and honey the more breeds a swift surfeit; ah, great wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having drunk this I wish to drink again; for both"},{"sp":"N","t":"this stream is sweet and it breeds no surfeit for men."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hebe, lift your pitcher and come here, taking","st":"14.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Trojan wine-pourer, the servant of the most holy cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, drawing from the honey-dripping river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ganymede may fill the whole mixing-bowls of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Come, friends, taste the honey-dripping river."},{"sp":"N","t":"Here I gaze upon the pattern of the upper air; for self-poured","st":"14.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"that which they call the drink of Zeus, the nectar of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naiads gush up in earthly streams.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the fifteenth I sing of mighty Nicaea, the beast-slaying, rosy-armed threatener of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, the blazing Indians flowed in like clouds","st":"15.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the honey-breathing stream of the river; of whom one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stepping close, planting both feet in the silt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood half-seen, wetting his navel in the water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent, stretching his stooping back into the river,","st":"15.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drew the honey-dripping water with cupped hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another beside the outpourings, gripped by blazing thirst,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dipping his jutting beard into the purple wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading his breast from above on the river bank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew up the moisture of Bacchus with opened mouth;","st":"15.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"another lay prone, bringing his mouth near the neighboring spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, propping his wet hands upon the sandy floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the thirsty water with thirsty lips;"},{"sp":"N","t":"others drew the drunkenness with an earthen cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the base of a broken jar;","st":"15.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a great swarm drank from the reddening stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the mass of the river dew into a wooden bowl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the rustic cup of shepherds. And as the adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched wine from their capacious throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cliffs were doubled before their gazing eyes,","st":"15.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to their eyelids they seemed to see a twin-yoked river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the outpouring of the wine-loving river murmured,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing up a yellow flood of drunkenness; and the fragrant banks"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched the currents of the sweet-drinking vine."},{"sp":"N","t":"The wine-stream made the foes drunk. Then a certain Indian man,","st":"15.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by the sting of mind-robbing drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darted at a herd, and beside a fair-leaved thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged off a threatening bull, hauling it bound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seizing with bold palms the scored tips"},{"sp":"N","t":"of its two horns, as though by the twin horn","st":"15.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"he were dragging bull-natured Dionysus beneath a slave's yoke;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, holding the frightful jaw of an iron sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut through the throat of a mountain-ranging goat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slaughtered with the whetted reaping-hook, as though he were cutting"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neck of well-horned Pan with crook-clawed bronze;","st":"15.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"another crushed the horn-drawing stock of cattle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though mowing down the bull-faced shape of the Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one pursued the race of long-horned deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the much-wrought form of their dappled look,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though destroying a rank of Bassarids; for by the cunning","st":"15.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"fawnskins of like pattern his eyes were led astray;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and staining his whole breastplate with murderous drops"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark Indian reddened with the gore of his spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, shouting, armed himself against a neighboring tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing on either side, and, watching the coiling hair","st":"15.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the plants shaken by the winds of spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crushed the shoots of the soft-haired clusters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the leaves of a shaggy oak, as though with a knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting through the lock of unshorn Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting with leaves and not striving with Satyrs,","st":"15.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a profitless delight in a shadowy victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another chorus of the adversaries raged; and instead of a spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"one, taking a heavy-thundering drum by its shoulder-strap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted it, and with the ox-hide struck on both sides"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed out a double bronze-beaten melody of echo;","st":"15.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, shaken by the cry of the many-holed flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raved unsteadily with the spiraling beat of his feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, setting the pipe to inexperienced lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made melody of the double-voiced harmony of the Mygdonian flute;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, leaping beside the neighboring root of an aged plant,","st":"15.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew up the grey-green branch of a fine-dripping olive"},{"sp":"N","t":"drenched with dewy rain, as though he were squeezing"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a wine-dark drop the tip of a Maronian beard."},{"sp":"N","t":"Others with swords, with spears, with helmets,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving uncontrollably, their wits robbed by wine,","st":"15.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated the rites of the shield-bearing Corybants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling their steps about an armed dance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in spiral, alternating rushes of the palm"},{"sp":"N","t":"shields were struck with tumbling iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, watching the rites of the revel-loving Muse,","st":"15.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped along with the Satyrs in a mimic dance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, hearing the roar of the beaten ox-hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received a gentle temper, and with crash-loving purpose"},{"sp":"N","t":"flung his chilling quiver to the winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"possessed by madness; and another, a woman-mad champion of the Indians,","st":"15.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"seizing a high-necked Bacchant by her unplaited lock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging the untamed maiden into a reckless marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pinned her to the ground, and stretching her in the dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"with love-maddened hands he broke the seal of her girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried away by a vain hope; for suddenly","st":"15.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"an upright serpent crept from within her bosom, close at her loins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darted at the neck of the foe, and about his throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"wove a circling band with the coils of its tail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dark-skinned man, fleeing with terrified feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook off the hot sting of an unwedded love,","st":"15.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a necklace of the snaky spine at his neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"While the wine-drunk Indians ran among the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile sweet Sleep, folding his soft wing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fell upon the tottering eyes of the unbending Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lulled them, their minds goaded with measureless wine,","st":"15.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to Dionysus, the father of Pasithea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one slept on his back with face nodding upward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a wine-tottering breath from his sleeping nostril,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another laid his heavy head upon a stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying sluggish on the fine-shingled river bank,","st":"15.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whispered with the mind-wandering dreams of daytime,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting his fingers, stiffly fixed, about his temples;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another lay stretched prone, and had both"},{"sp":"N","t":"hands let down equally along his two thighs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one propped his head upon the wrist of his palm","st":"15.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing up wine; another, joining his curved limbs"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a serpent coiled about, lay sleeping aslant."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a chorus of the adversaries, carried to the ridge of the wood:"},{"sp":"N","t":"one slept beneath an oak, another beneath an elm bush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, falling upon his side, leaned against a beech,","st":"15.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting his left hand upon his beetling brow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a great swarm slept, a chattering corpse, sending into the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbridled murmur of a strange, unmarked echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy with wine; and another, as his head shook,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusted his broad back to the trunk of an aged laurel;","st":"15.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he slumbered heavily on the deep-strewn bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a top-tressed palm or a fair-bearing olive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a coiling shoot, fanned by the winds, whistled over him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one was stretched upon the ground in the loose dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"washing the tips of his feet in the river's outpourings;","st":"15.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"another raved with the beat of drunkenness he had never known,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entrusted his heavy head to a neighboring pine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sinews of another's brow trembled as he panted."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the foes slumbering, with laughing face"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus spoke, pouring forth a commanding voice:","st":"15.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Indian-slaying servants of unconquered Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding fast without battle the massed sons of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"take them all alive in a bloodless fighting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the Indian, having bent a slave's knee to mighty"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, serve my revel-loving Rhea,","st":"15.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking the wine-dark thyrsus, and, having flung to the storms"},{"sp":"N","t":"his silver greave, let him bind his feet in buskins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and garland his head with my ivy bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"baring his locks of the high-crested helmet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, leaving the shout of wars and the din of spears,","st":"15.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him sing the Euian cry for cluster-bearing Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke his servants busied themselves; of whom one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding a snaky bond about the neck of the foes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged a man fettered in a serpent chain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, taking the loosened trail of a shaggy beard,","st":"15.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"hauled a man up by his thick-haired chin;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, stretching his palms to a crook-haired temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged a spear-won man by the hair, unbound;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, fastening interlocked palms about his back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled the foe with a coiling bond of withies","st":"15.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the neck; and Maron whirled with trembling throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting an Indian, a weight on his aged shoulder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, taking a spearman overcome with sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging his neck entwined in a clustered bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set him upon the rim of a chariot of spotted leopards;","st":"15.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"about another as he lay the Euoi-loving wandering swarm"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped his back-stretched hands in an unloosable bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set him on the crest of stiff-footed elephants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a one, taking the strap of a well-rounded ox-hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held an Indian fettered by the shoulder-thong.","st":"15.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, lifting the crook of a shepherd,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bassarid, foaming with the mind-stealing wave of madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged with bold palm by his much-curling hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"an Indian searcher of the deep-rich sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a slave's yoke. And as Lyaeus hurried on,","st":"15.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"iron Erechtheus held a well-corseleted foe"},{"sp":"N","t":"on unbending shoulders; and a mountain Bacchant drove"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-skinned beast of a wine-tottering bearer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing the hips of spear-won elephants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hymenaeus lifted up a golden ox-hide shield,","st":"15.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"having plundered a man of the golden shield, and rejoicing"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus gazed upon the boy with love-maddened eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he flashed in the armor of his sleeping bearer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the youth shot a rich radiance in the armor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Diomedes, having taken the arms of Lycian Glaucus,","st":"15.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparkled among the warriors, flashing with rich shields."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the army of the Bacchoi took other foes as spoil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding sweet sleep as companion of the sweet wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then there was a certain maiden of the curved bow, pasture-mate"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the lonely wood, who flourished among the Astacid Nymphs,","st":"15.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-natured Nicaea, another hare-shooting Artemis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a stranger to love, untried by Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting beasts and tracking them on the hills;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was she hidden in the fragrant recess of a maiden-chamber."},{"sp":"N","t":"And for her among the crags, beside the lonely floor,","st":"15.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"her bow was her distaff, and always within the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"her long threads were winged arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the upright wood of the hunting-stakes was the loom of a mountain Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she kept company with the pure archer-goddess as fellow-contender,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and among the crags she wove the net of her accustomed hunt,","st":"15.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"dearer than any well-worked thread; never with the bow"},{"sp":"N","t":"did she touch a feeble fawn of dappled form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she did not pursue gazelles, and did not touch the hare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, yoking with a blood-red bridle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lashed the grey-green backs of shaggy-breasted lions,","st":"15.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often she lifted a spear against a raging bear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she blamed the far-shooting archer-goddess, because, leaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"the race of spotted leopards and the tribes of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she yoked her wagon to paltry deer."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did she care for perfume, and instead of honey-mixed cups","st":"15.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"she preferred the watery drink of a torrent spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring out cold water; and in a self-roofed hollow"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hard-to-tread house of the girl was the lonely cliffs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often after the course of her well-toiled accustomed hunt"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sat near leopards, and beneath a single hollow rock","st":"15.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stayed through the midday beside a lioness in childbed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the gentle beast, beneath calm brows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"licked the girl's body with unscratching jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in mimicry of a whimpering dog with timid throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the greedy mouth of the raw-bearing lioness whined softly","st":"15.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sparing lip, and, thinking her Artemis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his shaggy neck the lion bowed to the lady,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his suppliant head drawn down to the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there flourished in the thickets a mountain-reared herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"straight, huge, surpassing the youth of his own age;","st":"15.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"his name was Hymnus, who in the midst of the wild wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"pastured his lovely cattle beside the neighboring girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking the shepherd's crook in his lovely hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came into a deep love and no longer delighted in his flock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like rosy Anchises, whose white rank of mountain-ranging","st":"15.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"bulls Cypris once pastured,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the cattle-driving cestus; and about the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"the herdsman, seeing the snowy girl as she hunted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cared nothing for his herd of oxen; and roaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cow grazed self-bidden into the marsh, deserted,","st":"15.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"strayed from its old shepherd unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the heifer was carried skipping about the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking its herdsman; and the young cowherd wandered"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the rosy circles of the maiden's face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And guileful Eros goaded the longing herdsman,","st":"15.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken with a fiercer sting; for among the crags,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the maiden rushed on the untraceable course of her hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a light breeze bellied her whole robe into the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the beauty of her skin bloomed; her thighs whitened"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her ankles reddened, and like a lily, like an anemone,","st":"15.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"a rosy meadow of snowy limbs appeared;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the desire-haunted youth, with unsated gaze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watched the free curve of her uncovered thighs"},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind whirled the cluster of her back-streaming hair,","st":"15.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting it on either side, and as her locks were raised"},{"sp":"N","t":"her white-shining neck flashed, bared in the middle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mountain-roaming youth often accompanied the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now touching her hunting-stakes or handling her bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now, as the desire-striking arrow was drawn,","st":"15.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the rosy-skinned fingers of the lovely girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if ever as she shot she curved the horn with the string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her palm was bared, the youth secretly with sidelong eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed at the white arrow-shooting arm of the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning back his eye, the channel of the Loves,","st":"15.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"to see whether white-armed Hera was as white as Nicaea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stretched his gaze toward the western floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to see whether the maiden or Selene was the more silvery."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the youth, tending the wound of the Loves beneath his heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"both near her and far from her thought of the girl,","st":"15.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"how she drew her arrow to the mark against a mountain bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how she clamped her palm about a lion's neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"curving both her arms in a witnessing bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how again, sweating, she bathed in the flood of a spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-seen, and ever more he remembered her robe,","st":"15.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, shaking it and lifting it as far as her navel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind laid bare and shot forth the flower of her skin;"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the memory of that, he entreated the sweet gusts"},{"sp":"N","t":"to draw up her deep-folded tunic again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the unsteady youth, beside his well-horned flock,","st":"15.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the high-necked girl hunting nearby,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched out such a word in a jealous voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Would I were an arrow or a net or a quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"would I were a beast-slaying arrow, that she might lift me"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bare hands; and still more would I be the ox-gut string","st":"15.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the back-bent bow, that she might bring me near her snowy"},{"sp":"N","t":"breast, outside her chaste girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yes, heifer, yes, calf, outside her chaste girdle."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, you lift a blessed arrow; and your arrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"are more blessed than Hymnus the shepherd,","st":"15.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"because they touch your love-breeding palms."},{"sp":"N","t":"I envy your sweet voiceless hunting-stakes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor does longing for the stakes alone possess me; I have"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealousy even of the bow itself and of the breathless quiver."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that at midday beside a desire-striking spring","st":"15.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"she might cool her limbs, and I might see the high-necked girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yes, heifer, yes, calf, without her envious tunic."},{"sp":"N","t":"Have you never yet pitied so great a necessity, Cytherea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know no Thrinacia and no horn-drawing flock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not pasture the cattle of Helios on these mountains,","st":"15.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"my father did not announce the secret bed of Ares."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, do not drive me off, even if I lead cattle to pasture;"},{"sp":"N","t":"herdsmen have mounted heavenly beds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"rosy Tithonus was a bridegroom, whom for his beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"light-bearing Dawn snatched, halting her chariot;","st":"15.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wine-pourer of Zeus was a herdsman, whom for his beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-flying Zeus lifted with sparing talons."},{"sp":"N","t":"Come here, pasture the cattle, and I shall call you a younger"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene with another Endymion at his herding;"},{"sp":"N","t":"cast away the arrow and touch the crook, so that someone may say:","st":"15.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Cytherea pastures the cattle of Hymnus the shepherd.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So he spoke and entreated, and clasped her dear knees"},{"sp":"N","t":"with woman-mad hands, and followed her, and trembled"},{"sp":"N","t":"to tell her the sting of Eros, and blamed his own silence."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, having the boldness that serves the bridal Loves,","st":"15.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted the hunting gear of Nicaea as it lay,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raised her furious spear, and, beneath a greater sting of longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while the girl was angry he lifted her sweet quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kissed the deaf nets and the breathless arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the murderous shaft with delighted lips,","st":"15.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought it to his breast, squeezing it with unsparing hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he spoke some words in a voiceless voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"By the Paphian, speak again, oaks, as in the time of Pyrrha,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as in the time of Deucalion; convict the raging girl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, dear laurel, break forth a tree-borne cry;","st":"15.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"would that fair Nicaea had lived before, and Apollo"},{"sp":"N","t":"would have pursued the softer girl, and Daphne would not have become a plant.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So he spoke; and with his pipe, near the chaste girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the witness of his pain, he made melody of a bridal song."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden spoke, mocking the herdsman:","st":"15.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Sweet is your Pan, who pipes the melody of the Paphian;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often he sang of love and was not the bridegroom of Echo."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ah, how much Daphnis the cowherd sang; and at his song"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden hid herself the more in untrodden cliffs"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the tune of the herdsman's cry. Ah, how much","st":"15.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"Daphne heard Phoebus singing and was not charmed in heart.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she showed her furious spear to the witless cowherd."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, struck by the raging sweet sting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not perceiving that she was so loveless an Amazon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried out a love-sick voice, the escort of his own death:","st":"15.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Yes, I beg you, send your dear spear, and with your snowy"},{"sp":"N","t":"palm kill me, and I rejoice; I do not fear your lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not fear your sword, bed-fleeing girl, because it would grant"},{"sp":"N","t":"a swiftest end, so that at last I may escape the bitter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steadfast wound of Eros, the fire that feeds beneath my heart.","st":"15.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me die, since the doom is lovely; and if with an arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bow-bearing after Cypris, you too will shoot me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the Paphian, do not send it at my neck, but fix"},{"sp":"N","t":"your arrow in my heart, where the arrow of the Loves is."},{"sp":"N","t":"Rather send your spear at my neck, do not strike my heart;","st":"15.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"I need no second wound. But if it please you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall endure another shaft, so that the earth may cover me"},{"sp":"N","t":"both holding a wound of fire and pierced with iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"Kill me, unhappy in love; do not spare your bowstring."},{"sp":"N","t":"You make the iron womanish whenever you touch your arrows;","st":"15.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"I stand self-bidden as your mark, watching with delighted eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"your fingers gleaming about the notches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back at full stretch your honey-sweet string"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it comes near your rosy right breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"I die, a willing corpse of Eros, by a sweet doom;","st":"15.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"I care nothing for death and do not fear the cloud of arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching your bare snowy hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching the bow and the lovely arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"Send all the shafts of your quiver against me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"send the murderous shafts against me; for other,","st":"15.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharper, fire-pointed arrows harry me."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you kill me with your mind-charming bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden, do not burn my body with the accustomed fire;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I need no second pyre; but do you, girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"only pour sweet dust over me when I am dead","st":"15.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"with your own hand, a last small favor, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'How the maiden pitied him whom she killed.' And when I am dead"},{"sp":"N","t":"let not my flute, not my harp, lie about my grave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not cast my shepherd's crook there, the witness of my craft;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but fix your arrow high above my tomb when I am slain,","st":"15.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"still bathed in the gore of my unhappy love."},{"sp":"N","t":"And grant me one other last favor: above my tomb"},{"sp":"N","t":"let there be flowers of desire-stricken Narcissus"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the lovely crocus or Milax, the flower of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and plant the short-lived anemone of spring","st":"15.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing to all my brief-lived youth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if a pitiless sea or the hills did not bear you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shed a little tear for me, only so much as to wet"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rosy tips of your lovely cheek with its dews,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with your hand carve this verse in mourning red:","st":"15.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Here lies Hymnus the cowherd, whom the maiden Nicaea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"without share of the bed, killed and buried when he was dead.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke Nicaea was enraged; and raging"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bared the deadly lid of her venom-shooting quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drew out a straight-running arrow, and at full stretch","st":"15.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"curved the bent horn of the back-bent bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sent the windy shaft into the throat of the herdsman"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he was speaking, and the uncontrollable wandering arrow"},{"sp":"N","t":"sealed with its bond the middle of the speech he was still pouring out."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the corpse was not unwept then; and blaming","st":"15.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"man-slaying Nicaea, a mountain Nymph grieved,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mourning the corpse of Hymnus; and in her well-watered hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the water-borne Rhyndacid girl, unsandaled, groaned;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Naiads wept; and above Sipylus the neighboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"rock of Niobe groaned more with self-flowing tears.","st":"15.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a youngest girl, still ignorant of the bridal Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who had not yet shared the couch of Bucolion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naiad Abarbarea, blamed the lady again and again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the peak of Dindymon, near the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Astacid nymphs of one age blamed the ways of the Cybelian lady,","st":"15.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cried a dirge; and not so much at the blazing doom"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the Heliades weep for Phaethon when he died."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Eros, watching the untamed heart of the murderous girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cast away his bow, and swore an oath by the cowherd"},{"sp":"N","t":"to yoke the unwilling maiden to Dionysus.","st":"15.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"And with tearless eyes upon her lion-drawn chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhea of Dindymon groaned for the fallen youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother of Zeus, the queen; and at the doom of perished Hymnus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Echo, who hates marriage, lamented."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the oaks cried out: \"What so great wrong did the cowherd do you?","st":"15.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"May Cytherea never be gracious to you, nor Artemis.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And Adrasteia saw, she saw the murderous girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Adrasteia saw the corpse gasping beneath the iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and showed the freshly slain corpse to the Cyprus-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blamed Eros himself. And beside the fair-leaved thicket","st":"15.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tears of a bull were shed in pity for Hymnus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a heifer wept, and a grieving cow groaned"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the gasping herdsman, and seemed to cry this out:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"The fair cowherd has perished, and a fair girl killed him."},{"sp":"N","t":"The maiden killed one who longed for her, and instead of love-charms","st":"15.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"she gave doom as his wage, and in the blood of the desire-stricken"},{"sp":"N","t":"herdsman she dipped her bronze and quenched the torch of the Loves —"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair cowherd has perished, and a fair girl killed him —"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she grieved the Nymphs; she did not hear the mountain rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she did not listen to the elm and did not respect the pine","st":"15.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it entreated: 'Do not send the arrow, do not kill the herdsman.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And the wolf groaned for Hymnus, the shameless bears groaned,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lion with grim eyelids lamented the cowherd:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"The fair cowherd has perished, and a fair girl killed him."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seek another crag, cattle, search out, bulls,","st":"15.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"a strange mountain; for my sweet cowherd has perished in longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut down by a woman's palm. To what thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"do I lead my track? Farewell, pastures, farewell, couches of the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"The fair cowherd has perished, and a fair girl killed him."},{"sp":"N","t":"Farewell to me, peaks and mountains, farewell, springs,","st":"15.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"farewell, Naiads, and my oaks.\" And both"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pan of the pastures and Phoebus cried out: \"Let the flute wander."},{"sp":"N","t":"Where is Nemesis? Where is Cypris? Eros, do not touch your quiver;"},{"sp":"N","t":"pipe, make music no longer; the clear-voiced cowherd has perished.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And Phoebus showed the blameless gore of the wretched herdsman","st":"15.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Loves to his sister, and Artemis herself groaned"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the dead love of Hymnus, though she was ignorant of the Loves."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XVI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the sixteenth I sing of Nicaea wedded, the sleeping bedfellow of unsleeping Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was the murder of the plaintive herdsman unavenged,","st":"16.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, taking his bow and drawing a lovely arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Eros, unseen, armed himself against Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he sat by the lip of the fine-shingled river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And swift Nicaea, after the course of her accustomed hunt,","st":"16.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweating uncontrollably from her crag-loving toils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brightened her naked body in mountain-poured baths."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did far-shooting Eros delay; setting on the string"},{"sp":"N","t":"the notched tip of a winged arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he curved his bow, and in the heart of love-maddened","st":"16.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lyaeus he fixed the whole shaft. And seeing in the streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"the naked-skinned girl swimming, Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was shaken in his sweet-maddened mind by the fiery missile."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he went this way and that, wherever the hare-shooting girl was,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now watching the coiling curls of her hair","st":"16.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by the circling breezes as she sped into her course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now, as her locks were drawn aside, watching her gleaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"neck laid bare, sending the radiance of Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he neglected the Satyrs and no longer delighted in the Bacchants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gazing toward Olympus he spoke with a love-breeding voice:","st":"16.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I shall go where the dewy course is, where the quiver is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the arrow and the lovely bow are, where too"},{"sp":"N","t":"the very couches of the unwed maiden breathe of perfume;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall touch her hunting-stakes and spread her nets with my hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too shall hunt and shall kill the accustomed fawn.","st":"16.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if, like a heavy-hearted Amazon, she reproaches me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belching out the womanish mass of a honey-sweet threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bring my hand to the knees of the angry girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching her lovely skin as a suppliant, not lifting"},{"sp":"N","t":"a branch of olive, since it is the tree of Athena,","st":"16.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwed, unenchanted maiden, but instead of a bitter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glistening branch, for my honey-sweet bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting as a suppliant's token a cluster bearing the wine-dark fruit"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the honey-dripping harvest. And if the maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the curved bow is angry, let her not fix her spear in my flesh,","st":"16.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"let her not draw a murderous arrow again, but in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"let her strike my body with the very tip of her sweet bow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I care nothing for a mind-charming blow. And if she wishes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let her seize my hair with her lovely palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging the willing curls of my hair as she grips them.","st":"16.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall never restrain the maiden, but, as though in anger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall press her right hand as she grips me, with unsparing hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking her reddening fingers in a crook-clawed bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a comfort for the toil of Cypris; for the maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"has plundered all Olympian beauty. Be gracious, Cerne;","st":"16.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"a new rosy-fingered Dawn has sprung up in Astacia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another light-bearer has grown; for a younger"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene is Nicaea, having a steadfast form."},{"sp":"N","t":"In my longing I would wish to change my much-wrought shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the ancestral reverence of shame did not restrain me,","st":"16.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too, a water-traveling bull through the Tyrian water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"would swim, lifting my Nicaea unwetted over the seas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like the bridegroom of Europa, and as though unwilling"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would shake my back, so that, as the girl was startled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her all-white right hand might grip my horn.","st":"16.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would wish, if I became a winged husband, to dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a woman unshaken upon my back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Cronides lifted Aegina, so that after the bed I might make"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eagle another bird, a bridal star of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"But I would not, striking the father of my wife with the thunderbolt,","st":"16.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"give my bride the reckless doom of her father as bride-gift,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I grieve sweet Nicaea by his death."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that I were a counterfeit bird, fair-winged, since"},{"sp":"N","t":"our maiden herself loves winged arrows."},{"sp":"N","t":"Rather, longing for the watery pattern of the loves of Danae,","st":"16.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would wish that I were a golden rain as her husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"myself the wedding gifts, myself the husband, so that I might dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the loving rain of a wealthy dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for it befitted my Nicaea, the fair-faced girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a golden form, to have a golden husband.\"","st":"16.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he cried, love-maddened, in a raving voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, walking within a fragrant meadow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he watched all the flowers blooming of the same color as the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spoke some words to the misty winds:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Just now at last, Nicaea, I have seen your form here;","st":"16.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"have you not changed your beauty into flowers? For gazing"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the fair-natured rose-bed I recognized your cheeks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but your rose blooms steadfast; for you tend"},{"sp":"N","t":"a native, unceasing, reddening anemone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting my eye upon the lily I saw your snowy arms,","st":"16.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beholding the hyacinth I saw your dark-colored hair."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me as companion in your hunting; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself will bear the sweet burden of the stakes, I myself will lift"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hunting-boots and the bow and the lovely arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself; I need no Satyrs; did not Apollo himself","st":"16.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the thicket lift the nets of Cyrene?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What blame is it if I too handle the net? I do not weary"},{"sp":"N","t":"when I myself lift my Nicaea upon my shoulders."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor am I better than my father; for in the surges"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea-traveling bull lifted Europa unwetted.","st":"16.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rosy maiden, why does the wood please you so much?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Spare your lovely limbs, and let not the unspread couches"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the rocks wear away your back."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be, if you wish, your chamber-servant; and in the hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself will spread your bedding, on which I shall lay","st":"16.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"the much-wrought skins of leopards, and with them cast"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dreadful thick-haired backs of a lion's covering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"baring my own limbs; and you shall sleep a sweet sleep"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered in the dappled fawnskins of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall cast above you the fitting cover of a Mygdonian deer,","st":"16.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"having stripped the Satyrs. And if you have need of hounds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give you all the dogs of my Pan together in one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall bring other hounds from Sparta, which my Carneian"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo rears for the loves of youths,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall summon the hunting hounds of Aristaeus;","st":"16.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall bring nets with stakes and fitting gifts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hunting-boots of Nomius and of Agreus, who knew before"},{"sp":"N","t":"both the fair-meadowed pasture and the course of the well-toiled hunt."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you fear the flame of the summer-born thirsty season,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall plant shoots of the vine above your bed,","st":"16.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the fragrant breezes of drunkenness shall breathe about you"},{"sp":"N","t":"as you lie amid a much-clustered covering."},{"sp":"N","t":"Roaming maiden, pity your cheeks of a desire-striking face"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they are struck by Phaethon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the radiance of Helios wither the ray of your limbs,","st":"16.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the winds spoil your perfumed locks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"sleep among the roses and in the petals of the hyacinth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having propped your head upon your neighbor Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that you may kindle a single revel for four immortals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Phoebus and Zephyrus and Cypris and Dionysus.","st":"16.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"And I would give the dark-skinned race of the Indians as captives"},{"sp":"N","t":"to be the chamber-servants of your bridal chamber; but why have I named"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dark stock as the bride-adorners of your bed?"},{"sp":"N","t":"When does white Dawn mingle with black-cloaked Night?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Astacid girl, you are a younger Artemis; but I myself","st":"16.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"will bring you sixty dancing handmaids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may make a countless chorus your attendants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"equal in number to the handmaids of the mountain archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like the daughters of Ocean, so that Artemis may not rival you"},{"sp":"N","t":"in hunting, though she is mistress of the hunt.","st":"16.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"I grant you the Graces of most holy Orchomenus"},{"sp":"N","t":"as handmaids, transferring my children from Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"But charm your unenchanted heart with longing, and let my bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive you after the toil of hunting, so that you may appear"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis among the crags and Aphrodite in the chamber.","st":"16.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"What blame is it that you should hunt with hunting Lyaeus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you have got the sting of battle, like a glorious-bowed Amazon"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall come to the Indian war, so that you may be"},{"sp":"N","t":"persuasion apart from battle, and, when there is strife, Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive also, if you wish, the deer-shooting thyrsi of Lyaeus,","st":"16.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and become a fawn-slayer; and by your own hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"and by your own labors adorn my wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoking a leopard or lions with the bit.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he pursued the mountain girl nearby,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a word: \"Stay, maiden, and have Bacchus as husband.\"","st":"16.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"But she in anger uttered a mighty voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden, darting a greedy mouth against Lyaeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Go and say these things to some tender-loving lady."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you can drag the grey-eyed one or Artemis into marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then you have mighty Nicaea as an obedient bride too;","st":"16.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I am the companion of them both. But if the unforeseen"},{"sp":"N","t":"wedlock of Athena who knows no childbirth flees you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then you will not charm the mind of the disobedient archer-goddess either."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not seek the bed of Nicaea; and let me not see you"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching my bow and handling my quiver.","st":"16.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me not subdue you too, after the cowherd Hymnus who perished."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall wound Dionysus the unwoundable; and if you bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"limbs unscored by iron and unyielding to the shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall imitate the high-crested sons of Iphimedeia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall fetter you in bonds of iron that none can escape,","st":"16.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like your brother, and within"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bronze jar I shall keep you too, after Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until, having filled up twelve circles of Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you cast your sting for me to the winds of the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not touch my quiver with woman-mad hands;","st":"16.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have a bow, you a thyrsus; on the Astacid cliff"},{"sp":"N","t":"I send my arrow here against boars or lions"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the fellow-contender of Artemis, but above the rock of Lebanon"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you too pursue fawns, hunting together with Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not accept your bed, even if you bring the blood of Zeus;","st":"16.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if I longed to have a god as husband, I would not have had"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus as a soft-haired, ironless, feeble husband of womanish form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but there would be kept for my bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"as bridegroom either the glorious-bowed lord or bronze Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one having a bow, the other a sword as bride-gift of the Loves;","st":"16.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"but since I will accept none of the blessed ones, no sting"},{"sp":"N","t":"holds me to call your Cronion even my father-in-law."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seek another, a young obedient bride, Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Why do you hurry? Your course is untraceable, as once"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Leto pursued Daphne and as Hephaestus pursued Athena;","st":"16.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"why do you hurry? This course is fruitless; for among the crags"},{"sp":"N","t":"hunting-boots are far better than buskins.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she left Bacchus. And always beneath the pasturing thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sought the mountain-wandering maiden; and as he hurried"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hound accompanied him, running beside him with prudent heart,","st":"16.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"which once to crag-loving Dionysus as he hunted"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-horned Pan, the rearer of hounds, gave as a gift to keep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And gazing at it as though it had understanding and speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his pasture-mate, traveling the same path, partner of his toils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"love-maddened Bacchus addressed it with friendly speech:","st":"16.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why, roaming hound, are you the companion of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"worthy of Pan who forever longs? Why do you alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"track with Dionysus as he tracks the maiden?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has your steward made you pitying of loves?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Seek still our maiden, and among the rocks","st":"16.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not leave Bacchus wandering alone in the mountains."},{"sp":"N","t":"You alone pity me, and like a mortal to the ridge of the wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"you go after the crested haunts of the wandering girl."},{"sp":"N","t":"Labor for your king; and I shall give you a favor in return"},{"sp":"N","t":"for your toils; after Sirius, the star of Maera,","st":"16.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bring you within the upper air and make you starry"},{"sp":"N","t":"near the former Dog, so that you too may ripen the grape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting your radiance as the Eileithyia of the cluster."},{"sp":"N","t":"What blame is it that a third Dog should rise? You too shine"},{"sp":"N","t":"as companion of the starry Hare when it hurries.","st":"16.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"If it is lawful, pitying me, blame the chaste girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting a sidelong eye toward the Cybelian ridge of the wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because a woman still flees me, a god, as I seek her;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blame them both, Adonis and Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and chase roving Echo, restless across the mountain,","st":"16.210","hvShift6":[[0,0],[6,10],[7,25],[9,28],[10,38],[17,48],[19,49]]},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she make my bride still more a fleer of the bed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not leave your Pan, ill in love, near the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he take her and yoke her in a forced wedlock."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you see the maiden, come swiftly, announcing to Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with witnessing silence or with intelligent barking;","st":"16.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"be the messenger of my longing; let some other hound wander"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursuing boars or lions from the crag."},{"sp":"N","t":"Dear Pan, I call you most blessed, because even"},{"sp":"N","t":"your hounds have become searchers for the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"Fortune of many shapes, playing with the human race,","st":"16.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"be gracious, all-subduer; after the mortal stock perhaps"},{"sp":"N","t":"you rule the hounds too, since this ill-fated wanderer"},{"sp":"N","t":"serves after Pan the longing Lyaeus as well;"},{"sp":"N","t":"blame the maiden, dear oaks; speak, rocks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"even hounds pity, and the Amazon did not pity.","st":"16.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"There are intelligent ones even among hounds, to whom Cronion"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave a human mind and did not grant a mortal voice.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke beside a plant; and through the fair-leaved cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the voice of woman-mad Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an ancient Meliad cried out a mocking voice:","st":"16.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Others, Dionysus, hunt here with hounds for the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but you hunt with Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet is he who fears a soft-skinned unwed girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bold Bacchus has become a suppliant, a servant of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Indian-slaying palms he entreated a feeble girl.","st":"16.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"Your father did not know how, with mind-charming speech of longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lead an obedient girl to marriage, to wedlock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not entreat Semele until he got his love."},{"sp":"N","t":"He did not persuade Danae until he plundered her maidenhood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you know the wife of Ixion joined with Zeus","st":"16.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bridal whinnying and the horse-wedlock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have learned the playful ordinance of the Loves of Antiope"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the laughing Satyr, the counterfeit mimic husband.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So she spoke, mocking the timid mind of Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she went within the oak of her own age. And among the hills","st":"16.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus in distress followed the raving girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"with love-maddened feet, and the swift-sandaled Amazon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restless, passed to the hard-to-tread topmost peaks of the rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away her track from searching Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as her skin was lashed by the flaming fire of Phaethon","st":"16.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"the parched lips of the thirsty girl were dried;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, not knowing the guile of woman-mad Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw the yellow water of the wine-loving river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drank the sweet stream from which the blazing Indians drank;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the girl, her wits whirled, raved with drunkenness,","st":"16.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shook her head as it wandered with a double throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and thought she saw a twin capacious lake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling her eyes; and as her head grew heavy"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw the doubled backs of the beast-feeding hill;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and slipping in the dust with trembling feet","st":"16.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was dragged self-rolling into the wing of neighboring Sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lady, heavy-kneed, was charmed with a bridal slumber."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Eros, seeing her sleeping, showed her to Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in pity for Hymnus; and Nemesis laughed as she watched."},{"sp":"N","t":"And guileful Dionysus with noiseless buskins","st":"16.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"crept silently to the marriage with the artful beat of his feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he came near the girl; and quietly drawing the tip"},{"sp":"N","t":"he loosed the bond that guarded her unplundered girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sparing palm, lest sleep leave the maiden."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Earth, unfolding a fragrant childbed,","st":"16.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"labored with a planting, doing favor to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plaited pole, lifting a great covering of vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was weighed down with clustered fruit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bed was shaded with leaves; for a self-grown"},{"sp":"N","t":"tendril of the vine girdled the fair-vined couch;","st":"16.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a wandering cluster with wine-dark fruit, aloft"},{"sp":"N","t":"this way and that, was shaken by the winds of Cypris,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and covered them both; and with its celery-bearing cluster"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lovely yoke-fellow shoot of the vine made drunk"},{"sp":"N","t":"the plaited ivy that mounted the growing harvest.","st":"16.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"And it was a guileful marriage, having the pattern of a dream bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sleep as fellow-contender; and the maiden was robbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her maidenhood as she slumbered, and she saw as escort of the Loves"},{"sp":"N","t":"sleep, the servant of a wine-tottering wedlock."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a breeze, restless, with the high-pathed leaping of the raving wood,","st":"16.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-proclaiming, wove a hymn of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maiden Echo with shamefaced lips answered"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind-roaming melody of a mountain-haunting wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Panian who speaks last; and dancing over the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"a flute crashed out, singing shrill \"Hymen, Hymenaeus\";","st":"16.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"this is a lovely marriage,\" the mountain pine cried."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the wind-roaming soul of the herdsman, leaping up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"troubled the sleeping maiden with nightly dreams:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"There are Erinyes even for one who longs, fair-wedded girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you fled Hymnus as bridegroom, you have been wedded to Dionysus;","st":"16.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"you give crooked judgments, maiden bride of the chamber;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you kill one who longs, and do not pursue one who weds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, you gave a brazen sleep to Hymnus who loved you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden, a sweet sleep has destroyed your maidenhood."},{"sp":"N","t":"Laughing, you saw the pitiable blood of the fallen herdsman;","st":"16.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"groaning, you have seen the more pitiable blood of your maidenhood.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, like shadowy smoke there sped away"},{"sp":"N","t":"the weeping soul of the desire-stricken herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and untraceable it entered the all-receiving Tartarean court,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having jealousy of Bacchus and his wine-tottering wedlock.","st":"16.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"And striking a bridal sound on his shrill reeds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding his jealousy beneath his heart, hymn-tending Pan"},{"sp":"N","t":"spoke a reproaching melody at another's wedlock."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one of the love-maddened Satyrs beside the neighboring thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unsated gazer at a wedlock not to be gazed upon,","st":"16.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"spoke a Bacchic speech, seeing the fair-maiden bed:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Horned Pan, do you still alone hold your course toward Aphrodite?"},{"sp":"N","t":"When will you too be the bridegroom of Echo as you pursue her?"},{"sp":"N","t":"When will you too accomplish such a helping guile"},{"sp":"N","t":"as an ally of your own unwedded wedlock?","st":"16.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dear Pan, become you too a planter instead of a herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and renounce the shepherd's crook, and beside the rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave the cattle and the sheep; what will herdsmen do for you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Wake up, and plant the bride-adorning wine of the Loves too.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had his speech ended, and goat-herding Pan cried out:","st":"16.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Would that my father had taught me the guile of marriage-perfecting wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"would that I ruled the mind-tottering grape, as Bacchus does;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I would have accomplished the much-wandering sting of my loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing Echo, hard to win as a maiden, drunk and asleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let this pasture be gracious, since beside a neighboring spring","st":"16.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"I water these sheep, while with a wine-loving stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus drags unenchanted maidens into marriage."},{"sp":"N","t":"He has found in his own plant the remedy of Eros; away with the milk"},{"sp":"N","t":"of goats, away with the milk of our sheep; for it cannot"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring sleep upon desire or drag a maiden into marriage.","st":"16.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone, Cytherea, am hard pressed; alas for the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pipe of Pan fled an unwedded wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cries Euoi for the newly completed marriage of Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with self-moving strains; and still more with accustomed song"},{"sp":"N","t":"Echo in concert answered the Syrinx as it spoke.","st":"16.335","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, mortal-charming shepherd of bridal drunkenness,","hvShift5":[[1,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"you alone are blessed, because when your bride refused"},{"sp":"N","t":"you found wine as the bride-adorning ally of the Loves.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he told, Pan in his grief, ill in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"full of envy and of longing at Lyaeus, accomplisher of his wedding.","st":"16.340","hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,10],[14,11],[20,20],[23,34],[24,38],[26,66]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And, having accomplished his love and the longing of a bed by the wayside,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus was lifted away on an unspeakable sandal."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lady, waking, reproached the river spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"angry with Sleep and with Cypris and with Dionysus,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,5]]},{"sp":"N","t":"drenched with a rain of tears; and in grief","st":"16.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"she heard the remnants still of the Naiads' bridal song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she saw the couch, the herald of the bed of desire-stricken"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lyaeus, shaded with the leaves of the vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered with the bridal fawnskins of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-announcer of a secret bed; and she saw","st":"16.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"her maiden girdle itself full of the bridal dew."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she scored her rosy cheeks, and striking both"},{"sp":"N","t":"her thighs she roared with a plaintive voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Alas for my maidenhood, which the Euian water snatched;"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas for my maidenhood, which the sleep of the Loves snatched;","st":"16.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas for my maidenhood, which wandering Bacchus snatched."},{"sp":"N","t":"Away with the guileful drink of the water-nymphs, away with the bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hamadryad nymphs, whom shall I blame? For sleep, Eros,"},{"sp":"N","t":"guile, wine have plundered my maidenhood."},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis too has renounced maidens; but why did","st":"16.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"Echo herself, who flees the bed, not tell me the whole matter?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Why into my ear, so that Bacchus should not hear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Pitys not whisper and Daphne not speak:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Maiden, beware of drinking the deceitful water'?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and gushed forth a much-weeping rain from her eyes.","st":"16.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"And now she longed to press a sword against her neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now she wished to fall self-rolling from the mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipping head first into a last dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she longed to destroy the drink of the bridal spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the earlier pouring had changed the moisture of Bacchus","st":"16.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"and murmured as white water, no longer the stream of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she entreated Cronion and Artemis, to make"},{"sp":"N","t":"the haunts of the Naiads dust in a thirsty waste."},{"sp":"N","t":"And often she stretched her eye through the mountain, if anywhere she might find"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unsteady track of Dionysus who may not be gazed upon,","st":"16.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that a woman might strike a god with her arrows, so that she might subdue"},{"sp":"N","t":"the clustered god; and still more she wished to destroy"},{"sp":"N","t":"that whole bridal vine with flaming fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"And often, catching sight of the tracks of Bacchus through the mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shot at the airy gusts, taking aim;","st":"16.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often she lifted her spear, and stood facing the mark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might strike the body of unwoundable Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but she sent it in vain and did not reach Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she was enraged at the river and swore never"},{"sp":"N","t":"to drink the deceitful water of the spring with thirsty lips;","st":"16.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"she swore to keep a sleepless eye through the nights,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she swore never to sleep another sweet sleep in the mountains."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she blamed her guardian hounds, because they too"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not then arm themselves against woman-mad Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she sought the aloft bulwark of a strangling death,","st":"16.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"her neck squeezed, entwined in a throttling bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning the mocking reproach of the youth of her own age."},{"sp":"N","t":"And unwilling she left her ancient beast-nourishing wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ashamed to appear to the archer-goddess after the bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And filled with the drop of the most holy seed of Lyaeus","st":"16.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bore a burden in her belly; and when the childbirth was completed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the life-warming Seasons brought a female offspring to birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and confirmed the nine-circled course of Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the marriage of Bromius a god-sent girl bloomed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom he named Telete, who ever rejoices in festivals,","st":"16.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"a night-dancing girl, following Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting in rattles and in the ox-hide beaten on both sides."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beside the wine-loving lake the god built"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fair-stoned city, Nicaea, which he named after the lady"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Astacia and after his Indian-slaying victory.","st":"16.405"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XVII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the seventeenth I sing the first spoils of war and the stream of the honey-dripping river turned to wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor, having taken alive the unshaken race of Indians","st":"17.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"unwounded, fettered by the sleep of wine-loving drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Dionysus entrust the war to the winds of forgetfulness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but again he lifted the Phrygian thyrsus; for he hurried"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the battle of high-crested Deriades who called him,","st":"17.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving unremembered the guileful, wine-heavy love"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Amazonian girl and the sleeping wedlock."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god led the way, bearing on his gleaming face"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavenly ray, the herald of the race of Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the Lydian chariot of Giant-slaying Dionysus","st":"17.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"were ranks of thyrsus-bearers, and he was girdled by warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seen in the middle on either side, and flashed back at Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he hid them all with his beauty; seeing him you would quickly have said"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was the fiery sun amid the wide-scattered stars."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lord armed the ironless war of his host,","st":"17.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"no sword, no death-bearing ash-spear, but instead of bronze"},{"sp":"N","t":"having unbreakable ivy as his lance; and whirling it"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the cities of Asia, and fixing it in the soil of Asia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drove the wild chariot of the Cybelian goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing the migrant car with a flower-tressed whip,","st":"17.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"a baldric of vines, shaded with ivy of its own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made the eastern land drunk with the Maronian harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with Bromius the whole army of the Bacchoi flowed as fellow-contenders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having courage for the sake of the earlier battle, when"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet-mad, ironless, seizing with both his yoked arms","st":"17.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"an intelligent, voiceless corpse, lifting an armed Indian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-kneed Silenus withdrew, a sluggish traveler;"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, reveling with the double rhythm of her feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled Bacchic Mimallon rattled about"},{"sp":"N","t":"an Indian still slumbering, and binding his neck tight","st":"17.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"hunted her spoil, the self-offered prey of battle. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he passed from city to city, and came to the rich plain"},{"sp":"N","t":"of neighboring Alybe, where the neighboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"Geudis, rolling its Zeus-fallen swell with wealthy streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whitens with the trail of its treasure-holding waters,","st":"17.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"scraping the hollow of a silver floor."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, as he passed by the deep-rich rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a countryman received in his lonely hut"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus the wayfarer on foot, of one age with the ox-horned Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Brongus, a mountain-running dweller of unbuilt haunts,","st":"17.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"inhabiting a houseless house beneath the unscored"},{"sp":"N","t":"base of earth-born foundations; and as guest-host"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shepherd soothed the giver of gladness with a milky drink,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing the snow-white dew of a milked goat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with paltry foods and rustic cups,","st":"17.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosed one from the fold of his woolly sheep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might carve it as a sacrifice for Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the god restrained him; and the old man obeyed the unswerving"},{"sp":"N","t":"nods of Bacchus, and leaving the sheep untouched"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set some shepherd's meal before willing Lyaeus,","st":"17.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"making a dinner without dinner from an uncarved table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as they tell of about Molorchus of Cleonae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those things which he prepared for Heracles as he hastened"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the lion-slaying contests; and Brongus heaped upon the table"},{"sp":"N","t":"in abundance the flower of the autumn olive that swims in brine,","st":"17.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the manner of a tender-loving herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting in plaited baskets the newly curdled cheese,"},{"sp":"N","t":"moist, round; and the god laughed as he watched"},{"sp":"N","t":"the plain dinners of the country folk, and turning a gracious eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the hospitable herdsman he touched the little table,","st":"17.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"devouring unsated; and always he remembered"},{"sp":"N","t":"that small banquet of the bloodless table"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the supper of his mother, mountain-haunting Cybele."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he marveled at the rocky gateways of the circling court,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how labor-working nature had carved the house, how without art","st":"17.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cliffs had been rounded out with answering rules."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when lord Bacchus had his fill of the shepherd's fare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then indeed, shaken by the divine breath of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the countryman Brongus piped the accustomed song of Pan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the double-voiced flute of Athena,","st":"17.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"hymning Dionysus; and the god delighted his heart in the song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mixed in a bowl the newly flowing moisture of the vat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Receive, old man, this gift, the respite from every care;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have no need of milk, having the fragrant dew,","st":"17.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earthly pattern of heavenly nectar, such as Ganymede"},{"sp":"N","t":"draws to gladden great Zeus upon Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave the longing for your former milk; for the snowy"},{"sp":"N","t":"moistures of freshly delivered goats' pressed teats"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not delight men and do not loose cares.\"","st":"17.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he gave as guest-gift of the shepherd's table"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-clustered harvest, the mother of toil-releasing drunkenness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lord taught him the flower-loving work of the orchard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to curve the shoots in a well-nourishing trench of plants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, cutting the tips of the flourishing old cluster,","st":"17.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"to rear new shoots of the wine-bearing grape."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the herdsman and the ridge of the wild wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hastened to another mountain war of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurrying his mountain-running step in company with the Satyrs"},{"sp":"N","t":"he joined again his attendants, the raving Bacchants.","st":"17.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"And thirsting for slaughter and for fair-thyrsus tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the heavy-thundering trumpet of the Tyrrhenian sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bellowed on his conch a melody, escort of Enyalius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathering the host; and he made the mighty warriors drunk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing the men to battle with hotter thoughts,","st":"17.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyers of the Bacchus-less Indian race."},{"sp":"N","t":"These lord Dionysus marshaled for the war of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Astraeis, coming untraceable, announced to Orontes"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the race of the Indians was enslaved, and cried in mourning voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Son-in-law of spear-bold, battle-steadfast Deriades,","st":"17.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"hear, and hearing be not angry; and I shall teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"the drug-victory of uncorseleted Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"There was battle between Indians and Satyrs; the slanting rank"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Bassarids roared, and my people armed against Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing with shields, and watching my spear-bearing","st":"17.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"warriors the much-knowing Lydian man shuddered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there stood the champion of the unwarlike Satyrs, bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"no spear of battle in his hand, having no naked sword, nor drawing"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the string a wind-swift arrow straight at the mark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he had the horn of an ox, and in the hollow horn","st":"17.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted a liquid drug, and into the outpourings"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the silver river he poured the whole guileful dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening the sweet stream with its moisture; and out of the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"thirsting in the heat, all the blazing Indians who drank"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck up a dance, holding an intelligent madness;","st":"17.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a deadly sleep assailed them, and unbending"},{"sp":"N","t":"they raved uncontrollably and lay down upon their ox-hides;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others reclined on unspread couches of the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting their sluggish bodies to unsleeping sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spoil for the feeble Bacchants and for Dionysus.","st":"17.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"And without war and without well-whetted iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"women took them as plunder into a slave's yoke"},{"sp":"N","t":"with their heavy limbs, and over the shoulders of their foes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the warriors were lifted like living corpses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some still gushing the thievish moisture of Bacchus,","st":"17.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"enslaved by necessity to the unwarlike Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"maddened by the drugged stream. And out of the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone was left, still ignorant of the murderous dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having escaped the deceitful water with unwetted lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"But beware the drink, spear-wielder, lest after the cunning,","st":"17.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"ironless victory of bloodless Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"another guile take alive in war the remnant of the Indians.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, heavy-wrathful Orontes was enraged the more,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and came swiftly back to the fray; for the contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"was half-finished, and the foundations of another battle were being laid.","st":"17.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"While mountain-running Ares armed the Indian throng,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the Bassarids high above many-winding Taurus"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurried to the fray, and with them marched the Bacchoi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weapon-bearers, and the unarmored Pheres; some, breaking"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foundations of their haunts, lifted them, others a high-stretched","st":"17.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"headland of a hill; and as the tumult began"},{"sp":"N","t":"they assailed the adversaries; and many-cleft ravines"},{"sp":"N","t":"were hurled in spirals at Indian heads."},{"sp":"N","t":"And skipping upon the cliff with slender feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pans armed themselves, raving, of whom one,","st":"17.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"seizing a neck forced down in a well-handled bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tore open a hostile man with his goat's hooves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring his middle flank together with his sturdy breastplate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, with the well-curved points of his long-branched horns,"},{"sp":"N","t":"snatching up a wandering Indian upright, pierced through,","st":"17.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted him impaled in the middle, and into the paths of the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"shot him high-flying with his two horns,"},{"sp":"N","t":"head first, self-rolling; and another, shaking in his palm"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reed-cutting sickle of sheaf-bearing Deo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though it were the corn of battle, the handfuls of the fighting,","st":"17.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowed the offspring of the foes with crook-clawed bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making a revel for Ares, first-fruits also for Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting hostile heads; and he held out to witnessing Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"his curved sword defiled with human dew."},{"sp":"N","t":"Pouring a bloody libation over Dionysus","st":"17.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made even the Fates drunk, pouring a warlike drink;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and goat-herding Pan, gripping another as he stood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding a bond about his neck with interlocked hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashed the well-corseleted foe away with his horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his flank cleft by the two-cutting point;","st":"17.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, cutting down a man who rushed at him with his crook,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed the tips of his beetling brow in the middle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Orontes heartened the Indian army,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a threatening speech with proud voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Come, friends, let us raise a war against the Satyrs;","st":"17.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not tremble at the battle of war-fleeing Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let none of you drink the yellow-colored water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the guileful, maddening drugs of the sweet spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slaughter of the dread-fated Indians at the hand of Lyaeus;","hvShift5":[[0,4],[34,13],[61,62]]},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after so many heads sleep destroy us too.","st":"17.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"Come, let us fight again with confidence; when would unwarlike"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus openly stand against my war?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If he can, let the fugitive champion await me, so that he may learn"},{"sp":"N","t":"what champions Deriades arms for battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let him fight with leaves, and I with blazing iron.","st":"17.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"What will the Lydian do to me, who hold a bronze spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling a wooden missile of clusters? But I shall drag off"},{"sp":"N","t":"this warrior bound in heavy bonds, feeble,"},{"sp":"N","t":"woman-mad Dionysus, as the servant of Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this fellow with the soft, womanish skin — leave all","st":"17.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indians, so many as they are, and fight alone with Orontes only."},{"sp":"N","t":"Sweet is he who whirls the loosened curls of his hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet is the lovely champion of the Bassarids; but women too"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoot with beauty and not with arrows."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall join your attendants to woman-mad Indians,","st":"17.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged to the beds of a spear-won wedlock.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking hot Orontes ran upon the champions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowing the twofold harvest of Ares; nor did any dare"},{"sp":"N","t":"to await the opposing rush of so great a champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not bold fiery Eurymedon, not his brother Alcon;","st":"17.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Astraeus fled, the champion of the Satyrs, nor did any"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Sileni stay. And with stormy sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"the much-dreaded son-in-law of Deriades raged"},{"sp":"N","t":"against the Centaurs, lifting a windy stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struck Hylaeus; and of the shaggy-breasted herdsman","st":"17.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crushed the top of the brow, hurling a millstone-like rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dashed away his covering with a ravine-torn missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a lying imitation fashioned of accustomed gypsum,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a counterfeit of a helmet, the guard of a true face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it fell to the ground in many splinters, like blazing","st":"17.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"ash, and became a silvery dust; and he, laboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the rocky spear, embraced the ground with his arm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And striking the shaggy forehead of another Centaur"},{"sp":"N","t":"through his well-horned head with a double-edged ox-axe"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crushed away the curving bovine horn;","st":"17.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fell heavily to the ground, and skipping on his head"},{"sp":"N","t":"he rolled half-dead, and beat the dust with his ears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and straightening his body he raved with a last footstep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the shambling, mirthless dance of destruction;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the monster crashed out a din, like a bull sending","st":"17.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"a harsh bellowing from a grinning throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck on the head. And loveless Erembeus, hurling at Helice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove bronze into her breast, and a dark hand scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"the silvery rim of her breast with reddening blood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as she lay in the dust the spear-hurling winds","st":"17.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"joined another distress to it, drawing back her robe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lovely gore gushed from her skin; and in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"with her right hand she gathered her fleeing tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"guarding the bare rites of her snowy thigh."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, beholding the shifting victory of the foes","st":"17.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs cowering, crashed out into the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a host of nine thousand from heavy-thundering throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring an answering echo with massed mouths."},{"sp":"N","t":"And swift-kneed Orontes fought alone against Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though he was mortal, and challenged a god with mortal voice.","st":"17.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"Both came together into the fray, of whom one"},{"sp":"N","t":"had a spear, the other a whetted thyrsus. And overbearing Orontes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the top of the unwoundable head of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"battered the whetted horn of Bromius in vain;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for lord Dionysus did not have on his unharmed head","st":"17.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bull-natured pattern of a Selenean brow"},{"sp":"N","t":"to be cut by the crushing iron of an ox-axe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as horned Achelous is sung, whose horn Heracles once"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut off and took as bride-adorner; but Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"had the heavenly imitation of ox-eyed Selene,","st":"17.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the unbreakable growth of a divine horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken by adversaries; and the bold Indian, heavy-thundering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like an airy hurricane, hurled a second time"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Bacchus, and his point was bent back"},{"sp":"N","t":"when it touched the fawnskin, like a pattern of lead. And in answer","st":"17.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, sending his wine-dark thyrsus at the broad shoulder of Orontes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"willingly missed; and mocking the ivy spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Lyaeus, god-fighting Orontes said:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You there, who arm a womanish throng against my armies,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you can, make war with your womanish thyrsus,","st":"17.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you can, fight in the front; and if you delight the mind of mortals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-subduer, charm one unenchanted Orontes alone."},{"sp":"N","t":"Stand and fight, and you will know what a valiant champion"},{"sp":"N","t":"my old Indian Hydaspes rears."},{"sp":"N","t":"I was not born of Phrygia, whence the women are male,","st":"17.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"having mowed the unsown corn of an unwedded youth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am no ironless servant of feeble Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Drugs will not save your champions; and I shall plunder"},{"sp":"N","t":"your raving attendants, and out of the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bring the Sileni as servants to my king,","st":"17.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"and destroy all your cowering Satyrs with my spear.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So the champion of the army spoke, shouting; and hearing him"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus was enraged, and with his vine cluster"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck him on the breast, sparingly; and struck by the paltry"},{"sp":"N","t":"clustered flower the breastplate was split;","st":"17.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the Bacchic point touch the skin it covered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor scratch the surface of his body; but as the iron tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"was broken, heavy-thundering Orontes withdrew, naked;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stretched his gaze toward the eastern floor"},{"sp":"N","t":"opposite Phaethon and spoke a last word:","st":"17.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Helios, cutting the upper air with your flaming chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending your light over the neighboring Caucasian furrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"halt your car for me, and tell Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the enslaved race of the Indians and of self-slain Orontes"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of the few man-breaking thyrsi; tell too of the very","st":"17.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"drug-victory of battle-untried Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of the wine-drunk stream of the mind-tottering river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tell how women split apart with slender leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwearied army of iron-bearing Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you still remember the bed of your Clymene,","st":"17.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"save Deriades, the shoot of your own race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who bears the blood of Astris, called your daughter."},{"sp":"N","t":"I did not yield to womanish-minded Bromius; I call as witnesses"},{"sp":"N","t":"Helios and the boundless earth and the god of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the holy water. But farewell, and be gracious in the tumult","st":"17.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fighting Indians, and bury Orontes when he has perished.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he drew his sword, and fixing it in the middle of his belly"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavy-fated man leapt upon the self-slaying iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he rolled into the river and gave his name to the Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And watching him, still breathing and gasping,","st":"17.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus spoke, pouring a mocking echo:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Lie there, a corpse in foreign waters; and may your father"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hydaspes hide your Deriades when he dies."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall destroy you both, father-in-law and son-in-law,"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of a murderous spear and a well-whetted knife,","st":"17.295","hvShift5":[[4,0],[7,7],[9,14],[11,26],[17,27],[20,28],[28,50],[30,52]]},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking Euian thyrsi and a vine point.","hvShift5":[[0,4],[2,18],[28,20],[36,30],[43,36],[53,37]]},{"sp":"N","t":"But, killing yourself with blood-red iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you did not drink the delicate streams of the honey-dripping river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the river has covered you, and you have missed the sweet wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you wish, drink the whole stream alone; but you have no need","st":"17.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the river's streams, having drunk the deadly water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Acheron; and with a man-slaying flood and a bitter stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"having your belly swelling, pregnant with Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taste of Cocytus, and, if you wish, drink Lethe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that you may forget Ares and bloody iron.\"","st":"17.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, mocking the drenched corpse. And swollen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the corpse of Orontes was dragged by the unsteady waves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the river banks belched forth the breathless corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"swimming through the stream with cold limbs."},{"sp":"N","t":"Him the Nymphs buried and groaned a dirge,","st":"17.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Hamadryad nymphs, beside the trunk of a golden laurel"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the currents of the river, and wrote high on the tree:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Here lies the champion of an army who dishonored Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indian Orontes, cut down by his own self-slaying hand.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was that the end of the joyless battle; for the contest","st":"17.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"was half-finished and the strife unaccomplished; and high-visible"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indian Ares shouted; and in a returning tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"the raving Bacchic Enyo, belching the Lydian mass"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a maddened threat, reveled into another fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling murderous clusters at the foe,","st":"17.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving for Ares; and the foes of shoot-loving Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"were killed by wooden iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a bloody wound; and as the iron was cut apart"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the clustered spear of an uncorseleted Bacchant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bronze-tunicked Indians marveled at the sharp ivy","st":"17.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and at their bared, freshly wounded breasts; for the bearers"},{"sp":"N","t":"of no breastplate were more easily shot."},{"sp":"N","t":"And of others there was other unspeakable slaughter, beneath whose gore"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tunics of the fighters, split by leaves, were reddened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where Taurus is; and the unbending Bacchants circled","st":"17.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"crown-wise the ranks of the yoked Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold flute sang a melody of slaughter; and in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bacchoi, the servants of battle-untried Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though struck with axes and double-edged knives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were all unharmed, like a tower; while the soft-haired","st":"17.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"foes were killed by slender leaves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in a row the thick shafts of the Indians were fixed"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the long-branched trees, and the pine was pierced by a far-flying"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear, the fir was struck, the laurel was shot at,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though it was the tree of Phoebus, and among its shamefaced leaves","st":"17.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"it hid the cloud of long-winged arrows that were sent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest Apollo see it shot at with missiles."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with bare palm, without shields, apart from iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bacchant shook her rattles, and a shield-bearer fell;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drums crashed out, and warriors danced;","st":"17.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cymbals rattled, and an Indian man bowed his neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Lyaeus as a suppliant. And on the little skin of fawns"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbreakable points of javelins were bent aside;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bronze-heavy, unbending helmet was cut by foliage."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a champion of the war-maddened Satyrs, hurling at a man,","st":"17.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"threw Euian leaves, and the bronze tunic of the freshly wounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearer was split by the vine ivy."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the scales of battle with a shifting rush"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesying the victory of Indian-slaying Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Astraeis withdrew untraceable, escaping his doom,","st":"17.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the long-leaved lance of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Meanwhile Aristaeus, sprinkling life-giving drugs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"healed every wound of the Bassarids with Phoebean art."},{"sp":"N","t":"On the blows of one he cast the Centaurian herb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of another, weighed down, he cleansed the murderous dew,","st":"17.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"squeezing out the blood; and he healed a plaintive Bacchant"},{"sp":"N","t":"by crushing many kinds of plants upon the girl's wounds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether of foot or palm or breast or flank."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when another champion was struck by a murderous arrow"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew out the swift point, and pressing the wound with his hand","st":"17.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made the bloody dew shoot out little by little;"},{"sp":"N","t":"to another he brought his hand, and scoring the edges of the wound"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut with a knife the parts rotted by the poisonous venom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laying his sparing fingers with the very tip of his palm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he mixed with the green flower of the life-sustaining earth","st":"17.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cunning travail of the evil-averting bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling with his hand the pain-killing moisture of Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others who were wounded he healed with Phoebean voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"murmuring a dread, many-named hymn of song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing the rites of his father's life-sustaining art.","st":"17.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"So he healed the shifting wound. And of the fighters"},{"sp":"N","t":"the barbarian-voiced womanish war now ceased."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Bassarids took many warriors alive"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the battle; and many foes, leaving the mountains of Taurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"returned to the region of the Indian land,","st":"17.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the house of Deriades with fruitless hopes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the broad drivers of measureless-lived elephants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing the Satyrs after the strife into a wine-dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pan of the pastures cried out, pouring a victory-echo."},{"sp":"N","t":"And woolly-headed Blemys, champion of the Erythraean Indians,","st":"17.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted the bloodless branch of supplication,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bending a slave's knee to Indian-slaying Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, seeing the man bent to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"took him by the hand and raised him, and with his many-tongued people"},{"sp":"N","t":"he escorted him, keeping far from the dark Indians one","st":"17.390","hvShift6":[[0,0],[12,34],[23,44],[34,45],[36,48],[43,50]]},{"sp":"N","t":"who hated the rule and the ways of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the floor of Arabia, where beside the neighboring sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"he dwelt on rich ground and gave his name to the citizens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Blemys came swiftly to the mouth of the seven-pathed Nile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to be a scepter-bearer of the Ethiopians of like color;","st":"17.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ever-summer base of Meroe received him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the leader who gave his name beforehand to the later-born Blemyes."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XVIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the eighteenth Staphylus and Botrys arrive, calling the mountain-running son of Thyone to a feast."},{"sp":"N","t":"Already winged, many-mouthed Rumor was flying,","st":"18.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"running beneath every rank of Assyrian cities,"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaiming the name of cluster-bearing Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bold Indian war and the splendid-clustered harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Staphylus, hearing of the ironless army of Satyrs","st":"18.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the vine rites and the Euian sacraments of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed to see Bacchus; and urging on his son Botrys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lord of the Assyrians, high on a wind-swift chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"met clustered Dionysus as he passed by."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing him coming, and the silver-wheeled wagon","st":"18.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the harness of leopards and the bright reins of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshorn Botrys reined back his father's chariot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scepter-bearing Staphylus leapt down from his car"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the standing track of the leopards of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and propping the print of his crouching foot on the ground,","st":"18.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching out an olive branch with god-fearing hand, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lord soothed Dionysus with friendly speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"By Zeus of suppliants, your father, Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by god-born Semele, do not pass by my child."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have heard how Lycaon received your father,","st":"18.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"him together with the blessed ones, and, carving up his son"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nyctimus with his hand, set him before your unknowing father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and touched a single table with Zeus the all-ruler,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the floor of Arcadia; and above the peaks of Sipylus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tantalus, as they tell, entertained your father,","st":"18.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"and carving up his own son set him before the gods as food;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaping in ivory the broad shoulder of Pelops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so much as Deo had feasted on, with a counterfeit cunning adornment,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cronion brought the carved-up son to life again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"joining once more the limbs that had been divided from one another.","st":"18.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"But why have I named to you, Dionysus, child-slaying Lycaon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the host of the blessed ones, and air-roaming Tantalus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the guileful thief of the nectarous cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"declaring a man the enemy of ambrosia and nectar?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Macello entertained both Zeus and Apollo, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"18.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when the Earth-shaker uprooted all the Phlegyans in the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"breaking the whole island apart with his trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spared them both and did not overturn them with his trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you too, bearing the likeness of your hospitable father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"enter my halls for a single dawn;","st":"18.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to us both, to Botrys and to his father.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he persuaded him; and he mounted his own chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blessing his own house, as Dionysus followed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Botrys lifted the horse-whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, traveling the deserted, bull-haunted floor in spirals,","st":"18.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove his father's chariot, and led the way for Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the Assyrian land; and Maron, the charioteer of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving in the neck-harness the golden reins"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Mygdonian car, unsated of the whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending the unsparing whistle of the beast-driving lash,","st":"18.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove the stormy wagon of leopards;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs, running ahead, struck up a dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about the mountain-running chariot of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a flower-loving Bacchant ran this way and that"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the hard-to-tread path with passable foot, and measured","st":"18.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"the narrow, ladder-like fold of the rock with swift sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rattled with her palm and with well-rhythmed sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away the labor of the steep-cliffed road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad; and the Pans high on the accustomed rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"danced with well-greaved feet in the dust,","st":"18.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"passing over the headland of an untrodden cliff."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the royal court appeared to the travelers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seen from afar, gleaming with a varied adornment of stones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then fair-haired Botrys, leaving his father's car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went swift-sandaled into the house, a wayfarer going ahead,","st":"18.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"preparing all things at once, and with tender-loving purpose"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made ready the varied dinners of a rich table."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Botrys was still preparing the feast for Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the lord of varied gifts showed Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the artful beauties of the stone-paved hall,","st":"18.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"from which flowed a marble, much-wrought radiance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of one color with the sun and with the answering moon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the walls were whitened with silver metals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was the lychnis, named for the lamp,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing sparks upon the faces of mortals; and the house,","st":"18.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned with reddening blazing stone, had also"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wine-dark amethyst propped against the hyacinth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pale agate spat forth a smoky gleam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ophite sparkled with the spotted patterns of its scales;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Assyrian emerald belched a green radiance.","st":"18.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"And spread about the halls with a phalanx of columns"},{"sp":"N","t":"the golden backs of a wooden covering reddened"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the rich roofs; and with many-splintered metals"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bright floor was embroidered with fine-pebbled art;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the vast gateway, in some well-carved timber,","st":"18.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"had the delicate pattern of newly sawn ivory."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the old scepter-bearer showed to witnessing Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he scarcely bent his step within the god-receiving court"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding Dionysus by the hand; and he, with slow-obedient sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched his wandering gaze in spirals;","st":"18.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the god marveled at the starry, gleaming adornment,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the gold-wrought court of the host-king."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lord goaded his attendants and urged his servants"},{"sp":"N","t":"to carve a herd of well-fed bulls and flocks of sheep"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the Satyrs of ox-horned Dionysus.","st":"18.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as Staphylus hurried there was swift-working command"},{"sp":"N","t":"to servants in turn; and many attendants of the banquet"},{"sp":"N","t":"bustled about; and bulls were carved"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sleek ranks of pasturing sheep. And there was dancing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fragrant breezes breathed through the lyre-filled house,","st":"18.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made the streets of the sweet-smelling city steam;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and abundant moistures of wine made the whole house drunk."},{"sp":"N","t":"And cymbals clashed, and beside the sweet-sounding table"},{"sp":"N","t":"Panian pipes hummed, interwoven flutes"},{"sp":"N","t":"roared, and the circle of the heavy-thundering ox-hide","st":"18.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed through the hall with blows on both sides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was the noise of rattles after the meal. And in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maron, heavy with wine, bearing the unsteady rush of a trembling foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went this way and that, shaken by the sting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his back-rushing step upright from the floor,","st":"18.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"propping his hands upon the shoulders of two Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seen in the middle; and he was lifted by the beat of another's"},{"sp":"N","t":"foot, having a yellow skin, in the middle, sending"},{"sp":"N","t":"purple rays from his whole gleaming face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a counterfeit likeness of the horns of Selene,","st":"18.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his left hand lifting a newly flayed wineskin"},{"sp":"N","t":"pregnant with its accustomed wine, entwined by a neck-strap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in his right a cup; and the Bacchants circled"},{"sp":"N","t":"the old man as he skipped with the shifting sole of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like one about to fall as his head shook,","st":"18.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"but never falling. And wine-tottering themselves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaids and the servants raved in the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having tasted for the first time the unaccustomed sweet wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the noble-childbearing wife of king Staphylus,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[11,14],[17,23],[19,30],[24,34],[26,42],[32,47],[41,49]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Methe, the Bacchic vine dew made drunk;","st":"18.125","hvShift5":[[0,7],[10,14],[19,20],[26,22],[30,27],[31,37],[45,38]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and she, heavy-headed, asked the Bacchants to drink again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about the wine-holding bowl of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she shook her head as it wandered with a double throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning her hair upon her shoulder with a shifting rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady, turning back this way and that; and on the ground,","st":"18.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she was often about to fall with slipping sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bacchant took the raving Methe in her hands and set her upright."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Staphylus was drunk; and with a wine-loving cup"},{"sp":"N","t":"his cheeks reddened from the cluster turned to wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the boy with new-grown beard, together with his father Staphylus,","st":"18.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"bound his unplaited locks with unaccustomed ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling them crown-wise; and exchanging step for step"},{"sp":"N","t":"Botrys danced in spirals with emulous feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his right sole as it migrated from the left;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Staphylus skipped with the dancing beat of his feet,","st":"18.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"leading a curved step circled in a running trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"propping his forearm on the neck of Botrys the dancer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he praised the drink of dance-weaving Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady, shaking his loosened curls this way and that"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they darted upon his shoulder; and Methe herself danced,","st":"18.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning her forearm on both her son and her husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the middle between Staphylus and Botrys; and one could see"},{"sp":"N","t":"a thrice-coiled delight of interwoven dancing."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Pithos, an old man raw in years, shaking his grey hair to the winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crammed to the teeth with the sweet-drinking flood,","st":"18.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"danced heavy with wine, whirling a wine-tottering step;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the sweet drops belched from his throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"he whitened his yellow, foaming beard."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they drank for a whole day; and as the cups were drained"},{"sp":"N","t":"a shadowy darkness covered the whole western land,","st":"18.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"blackening at its tips, and the two-colored air darkened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lighting up the shifting stars with a slender radiance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Phaethon sank beneath the shadowy cone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still having a little remnant of Dawn behind him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and silent Night cloaked the gloom with her skin,","st":"18.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring heaven with a starry tunic."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they, after the mixing-bowl of drunkenness, after the dinners of the table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Botrys together with his father and wine-pouring Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set apart in rows upon well-strewn beds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"took the gift of sleep and consorted with dreams.","st":"18.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when with rosy sparklings the messenger of Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shining dawn-light, scored the shadow-leaving mist,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then fair-haired Bacchus sprang up at morning from his bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by hope of victory; for through the night"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had been cutting down the Indian race with his ivy thyrsus,","st":"18.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursuing the deceitful image of a sleeping battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And hearing the din of the Satyrs and the thud of javelins"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shook off the tumult of the dream fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scattering his warlike sleep; and he had in his heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dread fear of the prophetic, threatening dream;","st":"18.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he had seen the phantom of a mimic battle with Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the forerunner of things to come, because a bold lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hard to fight, within a thicket, from a crag, with raving throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified into flight Bacchus as he still skipped and touched no iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drove him as far as the sea,","st":"18.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"hidden in the waters, fleeing the threat of the beast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he saw another terror, that a bold lion pursued"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thyrsus-bearing women with gaping throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bloodying them with its claws, and as the women were scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sacraments rolled from the initiate hand into the dust,","st":"18.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cymbals lay on the ground; and a Bacchant, turning about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped bonds upon the lion's jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding a vine cord tight about its head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wove a throttling bond about the lion's neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a female throng ran upon the beast, one after another,","st":"18.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scored its grim feet and paws with thorns;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely, when it was girdled about with a coiling cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Artemis take it alive; and from the bosom of the upper air"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fiery lightning, darting down upon its face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the beast turn back, a blind wayfarer.","st":"18.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a dream Dionysus beheld; and rising upright"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his bed he put on over his breast the bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"starry tunic stained with the slaughter of Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and girdled his hair with a crooked serpent bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clamped his feet in a reddening buskin,","st":"18.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted the thyrsus in his hand, the flower-loving spear of Enyo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he called a Satyr as attendant. And hearing the divine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"answering echo of Bacchic mouths,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Botrys awoke, and put on his tunic;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when Methe heard the voice of sleeping Pithos, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"18.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"she scarcely lifted her head, and as her head grew heavy"},{"sp":"N","t":"she slept again, reluctant; and still at dawn the lady"},{"sp":"N","t":"stayed, plucking a sweeter sleep from her eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and late she left her bed with slow-obedient sole."},{"sp":"N","t":"And cluster-loving Staphylus accompanied Lyaeus","st":"18.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he hastened to the road, holding out guest-gifts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a golden jar with silver cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in which he had always drunk before the milk of milked goats;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he gave varied robes, which beside the water of the Tigris"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Persian Arachne had wrought with delicate thread.","st":"18.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lord of many gifts spoke a word to Bromius:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Fight for me, Dionysus, and do deeds worthy of your father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"show that you bear the race of Cronides; for while still fresh in bloom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still a boy, your father dashed the earth-born Titans"},{"sp":"N","t":"from Olympus; hasten you too in the tumult","st":"18.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"to wipe out the overbearing earth-born race of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"I remember a tale which to our father"},{"sp":"N","t":"Assyrian Belus once told, the city-holder of my land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my father's father, and I myself shall tell it to you:"},{"sp":"N","t":"watery Cronus, lifting the jaw of the marriage-robbing sickle,","st":"18.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he rushed upon his mother's couches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut the male corn of his father's unwedded plowing."},{"sp":"N","t":"And broad-bearded Cronus fanned up war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the forerunner of the Titans, and fought against your father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling frosty spears against Cronion,","st":"18.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting a cold, wet missile; and sharp-stretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"hail-arrows were sent from the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And fire-bearing Zeus armed himself more than Helios,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosing the petrified water with a hotter spark."},{"sp":"N","t":"So, lashing raw-devouring lions into the Indian fray,","st":"18.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not tremble at elephants, since your high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed high-headed Campe with the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose whole body was crooked and many-shaped; for a strange-natured,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting, self-coiling war was fanned up"},{"sp":"N","t":"by a thousand creeping things from her viperish feet","st":"18.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"belching long-shadowed venom; and about her neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"bloomed fifty varied heads of beasts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and some roared with lions' heads"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the pattern of the grim face of the riddling Sphinx,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[36,45],[46,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and others gushed the foam of boars' teeth,","st":"18.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a massed phalanx of many-whelped heads"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was an equally perfect imitation of the face of Scylla;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the middle of her body a twofold lady appeared"},{"sp":"N","t":"tressed with venom-shooting serpent clusters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from her breast to the topmost fold of her thighs","st":"18.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"her counterfeit shape was roughened with the scales of sea-monsters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched on high; and the claws of her wide-scattered hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"were bent aslant into the pattern of a crook-clawed sickle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from her topmost sinew, through her monstrous back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-coiling scorpion, hanging aloft by the tail of her neck,","st":"18.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"crept, whetted with a sharp, hail-cold sting."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was the coiling, varied-shaped Campe as she was lifted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirling earth and air and the deep of the brine"},{"sp":"N","t":"she flew with the unequally yoked beat of her dark wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting tempests and arming hurricanes,","st":"18.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"a black-winged Tartarean lady; and from her eyelids"},{"sp":"N","t":"a roaming flame belched far-traveling sparks."},{"sp":"N","t":"But your father, airy Zeus, killed so great a beast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and conquered the viperish war of Cronus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Become you too wholly like your father, so that I may call you too,","st":"18.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"after Cronides, the destroyer of the earth-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having mowed the hostile corn of the ground-born Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"This war befits you as a match; for your father"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashed down the first-born Indus, the shield-bearer of the Cronian"},{"sp":"N","t":"tumult, a son of the field adorned with sky-high limbs,","st":"18.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"whence the Indians got their race;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your father fought Indus, and you fight Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"Become for me wholly like Ares too, since he also"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashed down so great a god-fighting son of Echidna,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting the dread venom of misshapen Echidna,","st":"18.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"who got a double yoked shape, within the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his mother shaking the coiling circle of his spine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"him Cronus kept, immense, hurling down the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hissing at Ares with the snaky sole of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, lifting his palms above the rim of his breast,","st":"18.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made war on your Zeus, and in the airy path"},{"sp":"N","t":"set rows of clouds upon his high-crested head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, snatching birds that strayed into his crooked hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"feasted on them with capacious throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"him, as he played the champion, your brother Ares killed.","st":"18.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not call you lesser than Ares; for you might rival"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the children of Zeus, since with your murderous thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"you are as great a champion as Ares fighting with the spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you accomplish contests, like Phoebus; and another"},{"sp":"N","t":"beast-slaying son of Zeus I have entertained in my hall;","st":"18.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"for yesterday well-winged Perseus came to my house"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the neighboring bright Cydnus of Corycus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as you did, friend, and said that with swift sole he had marked out"},{"sp":"N","t":"a newly founded city named for himself among the Cilician men;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he lifted up the topmost head of the Gorgon Medusa","st":"18.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"who may not be gazed upon, while you lift the wine-dark fruit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the messenger of gladness, the forgetter of mortal distress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perseus slew a sea-monster beside the Erythraean sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you have dashed down the race of the Erythraean Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Kill Deriades, as you killed Indian Orontes,","st":"18.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"worse than a sea-monster; Perseus saved grieving"},{"sp":"N","t":"Andromeda, but do you with a greater victory save"},{"sp":"N","t":"a starry maiden bitterly forced beneath the nods"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unjust Indians, so that I may kindle a single revel"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Gorgon-slaying Perseus and Indian-slaying Dionysus.\"","st":"18.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking the delicate lord went back returning"},{"sp":"N","t":"to his own hall, the host of Bromius; and hearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the king as he spoke, thyrsus-mad Dionysus delighted"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the goading speech, and raved for the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing his father's battle with bewitched ears;","st":"18.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he reproached Cronides, and wished for a greater victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"still to come, a third, after the twofold war of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having jealousy of Cronides. And calling Pherespondus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"offspring of the heavenly herald, like the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wise son of Iphthime, he addressed him with friendly speech:","st":"18.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Child of Hermes, herald beloved of me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go and announce this to proud Deriades:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Lord, either receive the gifts of Lyaeus apart from battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or make war on Bromius and you shall be the equal of Orontes.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and the herald, swift-sandaled, going from land to land,","st":"18.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplished his path toward the eastern floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding his father's staff; and Bacchus upon his golden car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the cluster, the mind-delighting fruit of the harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"going from city to city with much-bending feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"filled the whole Assyrian land with his harvest,","st":"18.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding out to the country folk the grape-tressed flower of the orchard."},{"sp":"N","t":"While beside the wing of the blazing eastern Eurus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he measured the Syrian soil with his roaming wine-dark car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile doom assailed Staphylus too; and in the hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"the servants tore their tunics upon their breasts,","st":"18.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the handmaids shrieked; and their breasts reddened"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were struck with their palms; and the circles of the faces"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the much-lamenting women were scored with mourning nails."},{"sp":"N","t":"And late, returning upon his cluster-rich car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus came back and entered the court of Botrys,","st":"18.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the memory of the tender-loving table of Staphylus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when he saw Pithos with downcast face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he divined the doom of his Staphylus by a wise silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-taught; and calling Methe he asked her with speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Tell me, woman, what has befallen you, that you have changed your form?","st":"18.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"I see you squalid, and I left you flashing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"who has quenched your unspeakable beauty? No longer do you send"},{"sp":"N","t":"the native purple fire from your wine-dark cheeks."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, old man, do not hide it: whence do you pour these tears?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who cut your luxuriant beard, broad-bearded one?","st":"18.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who has shamed your grey hair? Who has split your tunic?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, offspring of wine-loving Methe your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"child of my Staphylus, whence have you got a hairless head?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What envy has spoiled your coiling hair?"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do your unplaited locks, poured over your silvery","st":"18.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoulders, send forth the breath of Tyrian perfume;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do your cheeks shoot a rosy sparkle"},{"sp":"N","t":"from your head in Bacchic frenzy."},{"sp":"N","t":"How do you wear these robes, filthy with loose dust?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where have the royal robes of the Tyrian sea gone from me?","st":"18.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"I no longer know you, your face is so withered."},{"sp":"N","t":"Where has scepter-bearing Staphylus gone up, that I may see him?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, who has snatched away your father in a single hour?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know your sorrow, even if you long to hide it;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of your voice; for self-taught","st":"18.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"your eyes announce your silent grief;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know your sorrow, even if you long to hide it;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your tears prophesy your pains, and your squalid"},{"sp":"N","t":"tunics cry aloud the doom of my Staphylus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And envy has snatched away my hope; for I thought","st":"18.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the Indian war, together with king Staphylus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lift in my hands the chamber-serving evening fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the completed wedlock of Botrys my fellow-fighter.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XIX"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the nineteenth Bacchus, about the tomb of Staphylus, raises a delightful contest over a fragrant mixing-bowl."},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, the boy, having a heavy sting in his newly grieving heart,","st":"19.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"sealed his lips with speechless silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"overcome by self-flowing tears; and at length his mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Methe spoke a pitiable word, rejoicing in Lyaeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"The sleepless watcher of your dance,","st":"19.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Staphylus, Dionysus, a brazen sleep has lulled to rest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Staphylus, Dionysus, the breezes of Charon have snatched away."},{"sp":"N","t":"A double heavy grief has assailed me; for clustered"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus left me, and my husband fell into sickness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I tended a common distress for both,","st":"19.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Staphylus dying and for Lyaeus not present."},{"sp":"N","t":"But of your much-dripping harvest, dear Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me a cup of yours filled full, so that by drinking"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may lull my heavy grief with your grief-free wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"My hope, Euoi-loving Dionysus, is only to see the harvest,","st":"19.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"only to see the mixing-bowl, and I shed tears no more.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He pitied her as she spoke, and mixing in a cup"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave the care-releasing moisture of evil-averting wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the young boy and to the downcast mother; and both drank"},{"sp":"N","t":"the honey-flowing mass of the harvest in a mind-delighting drop;","st":"19.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Methe soothed her groaning and Botrys his distress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the woman spoke some word to mind-charming Bacchus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You have come to me, dear Bacchus, dear light; no longer does distress,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer does grief hold me, now that Dionysus has appeared;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have come to me, dear Bacchus, dear light; for with your","st":"19.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"drink of healing wine I have soothed my tears."},{"sp":"N","t":"I groan neither for husband nor for the doom of a father, but even"},{"sp":"N","t":"from Botrys himself, if you wish, I shall part; for I have"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus as both father and son and husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"I follow, if you will have me, and I come to your house;","st":"19.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me be the companion of the Bassarids; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall lift your thyrsi and the lovely harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set the vat-flute to my lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not leave me a widow, lest I swell a double pain"},{"sp":"N","t":"both for Staphylus perished and for Dionysus gone.","st":"19.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"You have Botrys as your servant; let him be taught the dances"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rites and the sacraments and, if you wish, the war of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let me see him laughing beside the wine-loving vat"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing with his feet the birth-pangs of your harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And remember aged Pithos, do not leave him","st":"19.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"untaught of your rite or without share of the sweet wine.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So she spoke, and lord Bacchus heartened Methe"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a laughing face and said such things to the wine-loving lady:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"O woman, splendid in gifts after golden Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"giver of gladness, mortal-delighting mother of the Loves, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"19.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"feast together with Lyaeus as he touches the banquet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"be the garland-bearer for Dionysus, as Aphrodite is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled with flowers and well-nourished clusters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wreaths of your locks shall make Victory jealous."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall make you my wine-pourer after golden-throned Hebe;","st":"19.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall be, rising together with clustered Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the companion servant of the Bacchic cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they shall call you Methe, the mortal-delighting satiety of wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall call the care-forgetting fruit of my harvest Botrys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall call the cluster-bearing grape Staphyle after Staphylus,","st":"19.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"the travail of the vines and the dew of the vine."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor shall I be able to feast apart from Methe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall I ever raise a revel apart from Methe.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, beside the tomb of wine-tottering Staphylus"},{"sp":"N","t":"grief-free Dionysus set a grief-free contest;","st":"19.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dragging forth a fair-bearded goat and a male bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set them as double prizes, and summoned the contenders"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Pierian song to strive in fine lyre-playing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set the double prizes, and urging on the athletes"},{"sp":"N","t":"he soothed with speech those skilled in the sweet-sounding lyre:","st":"19.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Here we raise an Attic revel; for to the prize-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"man who conquers I shall grant the sleek-skinned bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the man defeated I shall hand over the shaggy goat.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius spoke, a lyre-striking man sprang up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Oeagrus, a citizen of the unwarmed Bistonian land,","st":"19.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the plectrum hanging beside his harp; and after him"},{"sp":"N","t":"Erechtheus rose, a dweller of hymn-tending Attica."},{"sp":"N","t":"And both came into the middle as athletes of the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drivers of harps; and they girdled their hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"with laurel leaves, and belted up their robes.","st":"19.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they began they whirled their fingers with the accustomed beat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing in turn the row of the stretched string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding the tips tight, so that it might not be too high,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ever, slackened, it make a male song womanish."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Erechtheus, dweller of Cecropia, who first obtained the lot,","st":"19.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled his lyre with cunning rhythm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing an ancestral hymn: how in most holy Athens"},{"sp":"N","t":"Celeus too entertained Deo, the all-mother of life,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his son Triptolemus and ancient Metaneira,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she granted them fruit, when, snowing the furrow of the earth,","st":"19.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"Triptolemus found sowing upon his corn-bearing chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and how, when Celeus perished, beside his newly built tomb"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deo of the first-fruits groaned with tearless eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, comforting them again with mind-charming speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"quenched the heavy grief of Triptolemus and Metaneira;","st":"19.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"so too the scepter-bearer of the Assyrians entertained"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus in his hall; and the lord, in return for the table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"granted him Euian gifts and the vine harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when Staphylus perished, the wine-loving king,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stopped his son Botrys from lamentation-loving care,","st":"19.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lulled the distress of his plaintive wife Methe."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the wise lyre-striker played; and at the rhythm"},{"sp":"N","t":"all alike were charmed; and with fair-thyrsus Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"they marveled at the fitting, lovely-voiced Attic song."},{"sp":"N","t":"Second, lord Oeagrus, weaving a shifting hymn,","st":"19.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the father of Orpheus, sharing his hearth with an accustomed Muse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck up a two-line harmony in Phoebean song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brief in words, clear in speech, after some Amyclaean ordinance:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Apollo brought fair-haired Hyacinthus back to life,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Dionysus shall make Staphylus live forever.\"","st":"19.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had the revel ended, and the peoples cried out"},{"sp":"N","t":"with auspicious words from throats of one tongue,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs crashed out in a mass; and from his seat"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus leapt up, restless, shaking his right hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"up and down, and Botrys ran forward, honoring","st":"19.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a Euian voice the well-rhythmed harmony of the singer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lord crowned the head of Oeagrus with ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the father of Orpheus, striking the ground with his sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gladly received the unyoked bull as the wage of his song;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about him his comrades skipped in rows.","st":"19.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the citizen of Athena, swelling with pain and jealousy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew off the broad-bearded goat with shamefaced palms."},{"sp":"N","t":"And fair-haired Iobacchus, bringing them with unsparing hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set worthy prizes for a dance-weaving victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an aged mixing-bowl pregnant with fragrant wine,","st":"19.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"golden, holding unspeakable measures, gushing up for the thirsty"},{"sp":"N","t":"earth a four-year-old moisture of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wise Olympian work of Hephaestus, which once Cypris"},{"sp":"N","t":"granted to her clustered brother Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he set in the middle of the contest a lesser mixing-bowl,","st":"19.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"of silver, gleaming, well-rounded, which once to Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lord of Alybe, dwelling in his house, gave as a guest-gift,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the rich floor, where, of the earthly metal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the black hollow was whitened with silver bends;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[9,16],[35,43],[40,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"about the topmost rim of it, upon the bossed center,","st":"19.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"was coiling ivy, and around it was wrought with golden adornment;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this he brought and set, still breathing in its deepening hollow"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the vat, a younger mass of the harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"new wine, the drink of an unwedded drunkenness; for it is no cause for blame"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the man defeated should drink an unintoxicating dew.","st":"19.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when Bacchus had fixed the prizes in the middle of the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he summoned those skilled in dancing with a witnessing voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Whoever shall contend, wheeling with knowing sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and conquer in the judgment of the running foot, let him take"},{"sp":"N","t":"both the golden mixing-bowl and the flood of sweet-drinking wine;","st":"19.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"but whoever falls, shaken by the trail of a tottering foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dances the worse, let him receive the lesser gifts too;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I am not alike to all; and to the prize-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"man who conquers in the delicate dance-beating contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall grant no gleaming tripod and no swift horse,","st":"19.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"no spear and no breastplate stained with the slaughter of Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall I rouse javelin-throwers to a straight-running discus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no race of foot-speed is stretched out, no point of a far-carrying"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear; but for Staphylus, the king who has perished,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a man who loved leaping, I honor play-loving feet;","st":"19.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor do I hold out gifts for limb-strong wrestling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there is no horse-race, these are not the contests of Elis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not the son-in-law-slaying course of Oenomaus; for my"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning-post is the dance, my starting-lines the skipping of soles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a running hand and a coiling leap, and the nod of a face","st":"19.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it moves restlessly, and a speaking silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the fingers and the gaze of a dancer.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"At such a word from him horned Silenus rose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and thrice-old Maron leapt up with heavy sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the great golden flashing mixing-bowl,","st":"19.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"not because being golden it was the better, but because alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"it held the oldest, fine-dripping mass of dew"},{"sp":"N","t":"up to the very rim; and love of the sweet wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"made him young, and the Bacchic scent overpowered his grey hair;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he whirled his feet, making trial of his strength,","st":"19.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest heavy old age had stopped his limbs, forgetful of the dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the old man soothed the soul of Staphylus with his voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a sober word from his shaggy throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I am Maron, fellow-contender of grief-free Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not know how to weep; what have tears to do with Dionysus?","st":"19.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"The circles of my feet are my funeral gifts beside your tomb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive me smiling; Maron knows no cares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maron knows no wailing, no mass of mourning distress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he is the lovely servant of grief-free Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious to your Maron, even if you have drunk the waters of Lethe,","st":"19.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant me the favor of drinking the flood of aged wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Silenus drink the new mass of the new harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And for Staphylus after his doom, as for one living, I shall dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since I prefer the dance to a savory table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you, Staphylus, I dance as for one living and not breathless,","st":"19.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a revel over your tomb; and I am of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no servant of Phoebus, and I have not learned to sing dirges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as lord Apollo sang shrill among the Cretans"},{"sp":"N","t":"weeping for lovely Atymnius; and of the Heliades"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have been a stranger, I am no kin of Eridanus,","st":"19.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"no bastard of Phaethon the charioteer who perished;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am no dweller of Sparta, I lift no mourning flower,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking the delicate petals of the weeping-loving hyacinths."},{"sp":"N","t":"Today, whether you sit beside Minos and judge justly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or whether you hold the flowery court of Rhadamanthys,","st":"19.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"walking delicately in the groves of the Elysian meadow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hear your Maron; and instead of cups I belch forth"},{"sp":"N","t":"an intelligent libation to you with unlibating lips;"},{"sp":"N","t":"be gracious to your Maron, and give me a wine-dark victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a victory of which all shall speak; and I above your tomb","st":"19.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall pour the fair first-fruits of my golden cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beginning from the mixing-bowl after the prizes of my victory.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking Maron danced with coiling sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging his right sole as it migrated from the left,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring a varied-worded silence with voiceless hand;","st":"19.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he whirled his wandering eyes, the image of speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weaving an intelligent rhythm with artful nod;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he shook his head and wished to shake his curls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had he not had the bare brow of a hairless head."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor, as an old man bearing Titanian blood,","st":"19.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"did he write the Titanian race in a speaking pattern,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not Cronus nor older Phanes, nor the birth"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Helios the Titan, of one age with the cosmos his age-mate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaving all these and the pouring of ancient matter"},{"sp":"N","t":"he embroidered with wise silence the wine-pourer of Cronides","st":"19.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a cup to Zeus and gladdening the chorus of the other"},{"sp":"N","t":"immortals with cups ever following one upon another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or pouring the most holy dew from the mixing-bowl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his harmony was the sweet drink; and he danced also"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Hebe herself as she drew the nectar;","st":"19.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"and looking toward the Satyrs he wrote the form of Ganymede"},{"sp":"N","t":"with speechless hands, and whenever he beheld the Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he showed golden-sandaled Hebe with prudent silence."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Maron scored, shaking his much-turning fingers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he reined back the wise rush of his well-rhythmed foot,","st":"19.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"having completed the much-bending measures of a restless dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he stood trembling, watching with sidelong eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"which would conquer which, which would come to his own house"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying the greater mixing-bowl full of drunkenness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Silenus danced; and his silent hand scored","st":"19.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"the artful tokens of a much-turning craft."},{"sp":"N","t":"And such then was the pattern in his palms: how once a great"},{"sp":"N","t":"strife fell between the son of Cyrene and Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"about drink, and the blessed ones came together; not then was boxing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not running, not then the discus the prize; but for the son of Phoebus","st":"19.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for Dionysus cups lay as the instruments of their care,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and twin mixing-bowls, the one holding the flood of aged wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the other bearing the new gifts of the shoot-loving bee;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cronides sat as judge, and for the prize-bearers"},{"sp":"N","t":"a delicate contest was stretched out for a honey-dripping victory;","st":"19.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"cups lay as the instruments; and, like gold-winged Hermes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely Eros himself stood in the middle as steward of the games,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding in one hand both ivy and a branch of olive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ivy flower for Bacchus, and holding out to Aristaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the olive branch like the wreaths of Pisa,","st":"19.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"the holy adornment of Pallas. And in a honey-mixed cup"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aristaeus first, mixing the travail of the bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held out the wise drink to the immortals, gladdening"},{"sp":"N","t":"one after another, and gave a cup to each in rows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for them as they began the fine-dripping dew","st":"19.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought a sharpest surfeit, and as the cups were drained"},{"sp":"N","t":"they refused the third, and did not touch a fourth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blamed the swift surfeit of honey; but delicate-tunicked"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, drawing from the sweet-drinking mixing-bowl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted two cups and held them out with a double motion,","st":"19.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first to Cronides, and gave the second to Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the third cup to his father's brother, the Earth-shaker;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in order he mixed for all the gods at once and for Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"his father as they delighted, and to one alone who was downcast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"smiling, he stretched out a cup, to jealous Phoebus;","st":"19.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their minds were charmed by the wide-scattered cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having drunk they grew ever the more thirsty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and asked again for another, and there was no surfeit of the cups."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the immortals shouted, and entrusted to Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first prize of victory for the sweet-drinking vine;","st":"19.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drunken untraceable Eros, the steward of the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crowned the hair of Lyaeus with clustered ivy."},{"sp":"N","t":"Weaving this with wise palm, horned Silenus"},{"sp":"N","t":"stayed his right hand, and with much-leaping sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"was lifted from the ground and sent his gaze into the air,","st":"19.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"now joining his yoked soles to one another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now, unyoking them, he leapt with shifting art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time, much-coiling, dancing above the floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright on his heels he shook with a coiling throb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unbending he propped himself on his right sole","st":"19.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the topmost toe of the other foot, or bending his knee"},{"sp":"N","t":"with massed palms, or the stretched fold of his thighs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-kneed Silenus, holding the upright rush of his foot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he lifted his left foot upon his side and shoulder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising it in spirals, and with a back-stretched craft","st":"19.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hung his curved sole aloft, winding it about his neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the dappled whirl of a returning dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bent backward, self-coiling, with circling art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showing his belly spread and arched to the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passing the same endless turning-post crown-wise;","st":"19.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his head was carried hanging, as though always"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching the ground and not touching the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Silenus, tearing the ground with shaggy foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raved restlessly this way and that with the beat of his feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then his knees gave way, and as his head shook","st":"19.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slipped self-rolling on his back upon the field;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was shaped into a river; and his body gushed water"},{"sp":"N","t":"with self-flowing streams; and as his brow was changed"},{"sp":"N","t":"his horns swelled into an arching outpouring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a surge was crested, stirred high on his head,","st":"19.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his belly was hollowed into a fishy depth with sand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as Silenus was poured out his hair was changed"},{"sp":"N","t":"into self-perfected rushes; and above the river the neighboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharp-stretched reed, shaken by the breezes, piped,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-grown. And Maron won the sweet victory,","st":"19.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding in his arms the mixing-bowl crammed with sweet wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking the silver mixing-bowl, the prize of dissolved"},{"sp":"N","t":"Silenus, he flung it into the streams, as though a libation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made the outpourings of the dance-weaving river drunk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whence the place is named for the mixing-bowl, and the Euian water","st":"19.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"of sweet-drinking Silenus is heard murmuring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Maron spoke some word to the river spring:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maron does not force you, Silenus; but into you I shall fling"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening wine and shall call you the wine-receiver."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive your own wine, unsated of drunkenness, receive the silver","st":"19.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing-bowl of Bacchus, and you shall be silver-eddying."},{"sp":"N","t":"Shambling Silenus, you dance even in your outpourings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you keep the whirl of your feet even in the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still you revel in a watery form; but be gracious"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the Bacchants and the Satyrs and the wine-giving harvests,","st":"19.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and guard the Sileni, the shoot of your own race;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do a favor to deep-drinking Maron, and let me not see you"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away jealousy of my victory even among the rivers."},{"sp":"N","t":"Rather with your waters increase the wine of the Maronian harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"be of one mind with Dionysus even among the rivers.","st":"19.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"Fool, who taught you to strive with your betters?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Another Silenus again, exchanging an overbearing flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted a proud neck and came into strife with Phoebus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he, stripping him of his shaggy skin, binding him to a shoot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made him a breathing wineskin, and often high on the tree","st":"19.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind within bellied out a mimic pattern,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though the never-silent herdsman were singing again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pitying him Delphic Apollo shaped him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made a river of the same name; still the winding water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of that shaggy Silenus is spoken of,","st":"19.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it belches a wind-roaming din, as though always"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Phrygian flute were making melody with answering reeds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you too have changed your body, having kindled strife with a better,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like the former Silenus. But do you"},{"sp":"N","t":"seek no longer an unsandaled, accustomed Bacchant lady,","st":"19.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mountain Bacchant of loosened hair; for the boundless race"},{"sp":"N","t":"of loose-haired Naiads gladdens you."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seek no longer the snaky bonds of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursuing eels, the crooked travail of streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of serpents fish fitted with spotted scales","st":"19.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"creep along in your streams."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you have been parted from clustered Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the more I count you blessed; for you too increase the cluster;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what more would you have wished, having Zeus as the nourisher"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your currents after Bacchus, the father of the whole race?","st":"19.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"Instead of your Satyrs you have the ranks of rivers; and instead of the vat"},{"sp":"N","t":"you dance above the back of roaring Ocean."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have a like form even in the waters; and it is no cause for blame"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Silenus, tressed with ox-horned brows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should have the horned bovine shape of a river.\"","st":"19.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maron spoke; and all marveled, seeing the winding water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Silenus the deep-poured tumbler,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the like-natured imitation of a much-bending river."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XX"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The twentieth follows the ox-axe of murderous Lycurgus, when Dionysus is driven into the fishy deep."},{"sp":"N","t":"The contest was dissolved; and the Satyrs with fair-thyrsus Dionysus","st":"20.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"lodged in the rich halls of Botrys."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they feasted the wine Seasons reveled in upon them;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was the noise of drums after the meal, and shrill"},{"sp":"N","t":"the abundant pipe sang, and the wine-pourers labored","st":"20.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing with cups at the unceasing dinner;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the feasters asked the attendants to draw still more"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine, fawning upon them; and a Bacchant leapt up"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling cymbals, and the unplaited, unveiled curl"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the leap-loving girl was shaken by the breezes.","st":"20.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god, calling the wife of Staphylus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wiped away her squalor and adorned her with a wine-dark robe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cleansing broad-bearded Pithos, filthy all over,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cloaked him afresh in a white tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting away the mourning garments stained with loose ash;","st":"20.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Botrys no longer groaned, wetting his cheeks"},{"sp":"N","t":"with self-flowing tears, but obeying Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he opened the fragrant chests; and as they were opened"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sparkle blazed from the many-eyed robes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"from there taking the bright royal garments of his father Staphylus","st":"20.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"he put on the cloaks stained with purple shellfish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and feasted together with Lyaeus as he touched the banquet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they delighted the star Hesperus ran up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back the light of dance-delighting dawn."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ranks of the feasters in turn within the court","st":"20.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"took the gift of sleep upon deep-strewn beds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Pithos went up beside Maron into a single bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing the fragrant drink of the nectarous vat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they made each other drunk, sending an equal breath"},{"sp":"N","t":"all night long. And Eupetale, the nurse of Lyaeus,","st":"20.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"kindling a torch, prepared for both Botrys and Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"a double sea-purple bed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in a neighboring chamber, apart from the Satyrs, apart from Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaids spread a golden couch for the queen."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a dream came to Bacchus, Strife the nurse of war,","st":"20.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated upon a mimic chariot of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the form of Rhea, the rattle-loving goddess;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Fear drove the yokes of the dream-car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a counterfeit Attis shaped with answering limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having a shrill cry, soft-skinned in male form,","st":"20.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"he mimicked the charioteer of Cybele with a female voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and standing beside the head of sleeping Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"roaming Strife was indignant, and spoke with battle-rousing voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You sleep, god-born Dionysus; and Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"calls you to battle, and here you raise a revel;","st":"20.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your stepmother Hera, watching your war-fleeing Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mocks you, while you drag your army into a dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to appear before Cronion, I stand in awe of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I stand in awe of the immortals, because you have not toiled worthily of Rhea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I flee through heaven from Ares, the destroyer of the Titans,","st":"20.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shield-bearer of his father, holding a proud neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a shield drenched with accustomed gore;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and still more I dread your sister, the self-perfected,"},{"sp":"N","t":"motherless child of the generative head of a noble-bearing father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"helmet-glancing Pallas, because she too,","st":"20.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"womanish Athena, blames the male Bacchus for his idleness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thyrsus has yielded to the aegis, since proud Pallas once,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the aegis, crowned the gates of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scattering the stormy war of the Titans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and honored the wise travail of her father's head;","st":"20.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you shame the generative fold of the thigh of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Behold, how both Hermes and Apollo laugh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one lifting a twin shaft still stained with the gore"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the high-crested children of Iphimedeia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the other holding the rod that destroyed the many-eyed","st":"20.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"herdsman who perished; and I flee my own upper air"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning the reproach of war-fleeing Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden archer-goddess, watching the thyrsi of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"at rest, blames crag-loving Dionysus the dancer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she the steerer of a chariot of paltry deer,","st":"20.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyer of dappled hares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"mountain-roaming, near mountain Rhea, she blames"},{"sp":"N","t":"the driver of leopards and the charioteer of lions."},{"sp":"N","t":"I refuse the house of Zeus my child; for in Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I stand in awe of Leto, proud and still exulting,","st":"20.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"who stretches an arrow for me as the defender of her bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slayer of earth-born, desire-stricken Tityus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I am lashed with double pains, because I see"},{"sp":"N","t":"Semele grieving and the star of proud Maia."},{"sp":"N","t":"You are not like the children of Zeus; you have not killed with an arrow","st":"20.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"threatening Otus and high-footed Ephialtes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have not slain Tityus with your winged shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor bold Orion ill in love, nor the champion of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Argus, the ox-watching son of the evil-increasing field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spy upon the ox-horned wedlock of Zeus;","st":"20.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"but beside Staphylus and Botrys you weave a revel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inglorious, ironless, singing a wine-song;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shame the earthly race of the Satyrs, because they too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching the bloodless Bacchic dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"have entrusted all their hope of Ares to cups.","st":"20.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"There is feasting also after war, there is dancing"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the Indian strife within the halls of Staphylus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"harps sound again after a warlike victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"apart from toils it is not possible to dwell in the untrodden upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the road of the blessed ones is not easy; from valor","st":"20.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"the god-sped path of Olympus draws one to the vault."},{"sp":"N","t":"Endure you too manifold toils; for angry Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesies for you the heavenly court of Zeus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she flew away. And the god leapt up from his bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still having the dread din of the threatening dream; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"20.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Botrys sprang up, and put on his tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting the rays of the Sidonian sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bound his fitted soles with a golden sandal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, guarding his two-parted collarbone with untiring shoulders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pinned the bright cloak of sea-cloaked lords,","st":"20.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting his father's proud girdle about his loins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a scepter. And the Satyrs yoked the blood-red wagon"},{"sp":"N","t":"of leopards for hurrying Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Sileni shouted; and the thyrsus-bearing Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowed; and the armies gathered for the Indian war","st":"20.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushed on in rows; and the flute of Enyo roared;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the leaders marshaled their chosen ranks."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, leaping upon its back with a mounting throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the mane of a raging bear as it sped into its course;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, pricking the shaggy belly of a wandering wild bull,","st":"20.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove it with his crook,"},{"sp":"N","t":"joining his loosened soles on both its sides;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another mounted the spine of shaggy-breasted lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gripping the locks of their necks instead of a bridle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving his father's hall together with his inheritance,","st":"20.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening Botrys, stretching out a four-yoked chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove alongside cluster-loving Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his servants behind; and the lady with her mother Methe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"white-tunicked, mounted the silver-wheeled wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phasyleia, the steerer of the yoke-harness,","st":"20.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled a golden whip at the crest of the mules;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and broad-headed Pithos, stretching out the rearmost car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"followed, serving both Botrys and Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was he unrewarded; taking him to the land of the Lydians"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus set him beside a vat pregnant with drunkenness,","st":"20.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the poured mass of the fine-dripping harvest"},{"sp":"N","t":"in wine-holding vessels, whence, keeping this name,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the jar with its purple hollow stands beside the neighboring vat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still receiving the Euian gifts of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the memorial of the former Pithos; and if it had a mortal voice,","st":"20.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"it would belch such a word to the Satyrs as it heard the revel:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I am Pithos, named for the former one, and beside the vat"},{"sp":"N","t":"I receive the sweet stream of the vines; and I am the servant"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Assyrian Staphylus and of Botrys, and both"},{"sp":"N","t":"I reared as infants, an old nurse; and still I lift","st":"20.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"them both in my flanks, as though they lived again.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things lord Bacchus was to accomplish late, after a time."},{"sp":"N","t":"And crossing Tyre and traveling through Byblos"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the fair-wedded water of the fragrant river Adonis"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the crag of Lebanon and the haunts of the Cyprus-born,","st":"20.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set foot in Arabia, and beneath fragrant trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"he marveled at the long-leaved ridge of the Nysian thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"and at a steep-built city, the nurse of javelin-bearing men."},{"sp":"N","t":"There dwelt a certain murderous man, the blood of Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having in his chilling ways the likeness of his father,","st":"20.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"lawless, dragging blameless strangers to their doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dread-raving Lycurgus; and with the heads of men"},{"sp":"N","t":"slain by iron he garlanded his own gateway,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Oenomaus and of his time, whose wretched daughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"was once kept in her father's unwedded halls,","st":"20.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"a widow, growing old, still ignorant of the bridal Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until the son of Tantalus, scoring the horse-driven swell,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the unwetted four-yoked chariot of the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a bridal course, when with wheel-shaped circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"shifting-witted Myrtilus accomplished a thievish victory,","st":"20.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"having made a deceitful axle of mimic wax,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding pity and love for lamenting Hippodameia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the course was profitless; and beneath the chariot of Helios"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wax-fixed pattern was warmed by the flaming torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the short-lived axle, loosened, shot forth its wheel.","st":"20.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was Lycurgus, of like ways; and burden-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering wayfarer men he often bound at the crossroads"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dragged to his house, and carving them up he sacrificed them"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Enyalius his father; and taking the extremities of those"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut down by the knife he covered his ill-guesting gateways.","st":"20.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when, after the war with the foes, when a javelin-bearing man"},{"sp":"N","t":"has come at last, the offerings of a new victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shields or helmets, are hung up in his hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so too beside the gateway of murderous Lycurgus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tips of the feet and the hands of the dead were hung aloft.","st":"20.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was slaughter; and beside the neighboring altar of Zeus of strangers"},{"sp":"N","t":"foreigners, groaning, were cut up with the knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like cattle and sheep, and the altars were sprinkled"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were slaughtered, and the spotted dust reddened with gore"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the doors of the house; and the citizens, forced,","st":"20.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"hastened to sacrifice to Lycurgus instead of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did you escape, Dionysus, the envy of guile-stitching Hera;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but again, angry at your god-born race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sent Iris as an ill-tidings messenger, so that she might charm you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing a lying persuasion with mind-stealing guile;","st":"20.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she gave her a god-fighting ox-axe, to carry"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the ruler of Arabia, Lycurgus son of Dryas."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the goddess delay; and with changed face"},{"sp":"N","t":"she counterfeited the bastard form of an answering Ares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking the well-helmeted crest of her head,","st":"20.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting away her cunning saffron tunics,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she covered herself with a crafty breastplate, the midwife of tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bloody breastplate, and, cunning, sending a male,"},{"sp":"N","t":"battle-rousing threat from a grim face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she imitated the tongue of Enyalius with running voice:","st":"20.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Child, seed of unconquered Ares, do you yourself really"},{"sp":"N","t":"tremble at the soft-skinned womanish threat of the Bassarids?"},{"sp":"N","t":"These are no Amazons from the Thermodon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"these are no warlike women from the Caucasus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"they bear no swift bows and shake no arrows;","st":"20.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"they have no bold horse of Ares; nor upon their shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"do they lift a barbarian, half-finished ox-hide shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to call you to the fray, because women"},{"sp":"N","t":"threaten battle against unconquerable Lycurgus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Are you at rest, Lycurgus, while Dionysus arms?","st":"20.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"This was a mortal man, untimely; he did not spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"from a heavenly stock; a tale in Greek rumor has fashioned him"},{"sp":"N","t":"to be of Zeus; but I do not know how to believe"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the childbirth of Cronion, that my father high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore in a male thigh a mortal, womanish son;","st":"20.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am not persuaded by lying tales, that a mortal man"},{"sp":"N","t":"got the childbed of my Zeus, whence Athena sprang;"},{"sp":"N","t":"my Zeus has not learned to bring forth a feeble child;"},{"sp":"N","t":"have Ares your father as witness; you have seen Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a female child of Zeus, more warlike than Dionysus.","st":"20.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"My child, you have your own strength, and have no need"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your father Enyalius, even if he rules over wars;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless, if you wish, I shall arm myself, and shall not leave you"},{"sp":"N","t":"alone in the wars; and a goddess, if there be need,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sister-wife of Zeus, Hera, shall follow you as companion","st":"20.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the fray, shielding her grandson Lycurgus. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And within your god-receiving temple I shall set up &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thyrsi of the Bassarids, bastard spears; and cutting apart"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long unshaken horns of the ox-horned Centaurs"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall make horn-drawn bows for the bow-bearing Arabs,","st":"20.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"as is right; and cutting off the long-shadowed, outstretched tail"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Sileni I shall make a shaggy horse-lashing whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"These things I bring for you after the war; and the yellow sandals"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unwarlike Bacchus and his purple womanish tunics"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the female circling band about his loins","st":"20.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"we shall keep for the foam-born, your kinswoman and my wife,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[36,18]]},{"sp":"N","t":"fitting female gifts; and the whole rank of the handmaids"},{"sp":"N","t":"of woman-mad Lyaeus I shall join to my servants"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a bride-price-less bed of forced wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as is right for the spear-won; and the paltry","st":"20.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoots of the vines, the kindly gifts of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an Arabian flame shall receive with a hotter spark."},{"sp":"N","t":"And let the mighty handmaid of dance-weaving Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bassarid, have another and unaccustomed craft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling in houses instead of the mountains, and leaving","st":"20.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dappled fawnskin let her hide her body in a tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grinding the grain with the wheel-shaped stone of a mill;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and casting away her garlands and what they call her harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let her be taught common cares under a double ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a slave by necessity both to Pallas and to Cytherea,","st":"20.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"to daily baskets and to nightly wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the shuttle and no longer the cymbals of Rhea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And let the aged Sileni beside the feast of my table"},{"sp":"N","t":"sing the Euian cry, and instead of their accustomed Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"strike up a revel for Ares and for Lycurgus.\"","st":"20.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke the golden-winged goddess Iris smiled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing the lying journey of a hawk."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Lycurgus, seeing her, divined his own victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"recognizing the swift bird, because, shaking its blood-red wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it knows how to drag feeble doves into terror;","st":"20.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he had seen, he had seen a like dream, how beside a thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maned lion armed itself with raving throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursued the horned race of dappled deer."},{"sp":"N","t":"Knowing such a dream he armed himself against the raving Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"likening the Bassarids to battle-untried fawns,","st":"20.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"and took the more courage; and the goddess, darting up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came at the nods of Hera as a forerunner to Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding the soles of her feet tight with a winged sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a rod, and like Hermes the messenger of Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she cried a guile-weaving voice to bronze-tunicked Bacchus:","st":"20.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Brother, child of Zeus of surpassing mind, bring your rites"},{"sp":"N","t":"to hospitable Lycurgus apart from battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave the fray, do not kill friends, do not flee calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be gracious to the gentle; who will subdue a mild man?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And do not raise war against your own suppliants;","st":"20.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not cover your body with a starry breastplate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not bind your head tight with a high-crested helmet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not girdle your hair with a viperish cluster;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaving your murderous thyrsi, and lifting a horn"},{"sp":"N","t":"full of sweet-drinking wine and your accustomed rod,","st":"20.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"hold out Euian gifts to cluster-loving Lycurgus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"now adorn your body with your bloodless robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now let us weave the melody of an uncorseleted dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let your army stay at rest beside the shady wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest it prepare battle against a peaceful king;","st":"20.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, casting a dear garland upon your locks, go rejoicing"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the unbarred house of most ready Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go reveling like a bridegroom; and keep your Indian-slaying"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsi for disobedient Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"Lord Lycurgus does not nurse a feeble spirit;","st":"20.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he is the Zeus-fallen blood of Ares, and in tumults,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the kindred valor of his father Enyalius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would not cower before the war of your Cronion either.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded him, and with lofty sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came within the upper air. And by the guile of the goddess","st":"20.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus shook off his battle-rousing thyrsi"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosed the crested helmet from his locks"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laid down his star-backed shield; and with bare hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted a vessel crammed with purple dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sharp horn and a grief-free cluster; and he garlanded","st":"20.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"his long unplaited hair with vine ivy."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving his well-armed host and his battle-rousing women"},{"sp":"N","t":"near Carmel, and the chariots of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate-tunicked, ironless, he reveled on as a wayfarer on foot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pipe sang a melody of gladness after the meal,","st":"20.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"and yoked pairs of flutes roared out a friendly whistling;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Bassarid, whirling in her hands the Euoi-loving rattles"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Lyaeus, skipped beside the gateway of Lycurgus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when the bold lord heard the shout of the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the melody of the Berecyntian echo of the flute as it sang","st":"20.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the clang of the pipe, and of the ox-hide itself as it was beaten,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raged, gazing at the double-sounding circles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beholding the vine god beside his doors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a sardonic laugh he cried out a mocking voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a truceless threat at the driver of the Bassarids:","st":"20.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Do you see these offerings of our halls?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Adorn my house too, friend, either with your thyrsi"},{"sp":"N","t":"or with your feet or your hands or your bloody head."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you give orders to horned Satyrs, horned Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall subdue you like cattle with my ox-axe.","st":"20.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"This is my guest-gift to you, so that someone may say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"either a god or one of mortals, that the gateway of Lycurgus"},{"sp":"N","t":"has been girdled with the half-cut limbs of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"We do not rule among Boeotians, this is not Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this is not the house of Semele, where women bear bastard children","st":"20.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"by lightning and are in travail with the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"You shake a wine-dark thyrsus, I brandish an ox-axe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting you through the middle of your bovine brow"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would break off your arching horn.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he pursued the nurses of Dionysus,","st":"20.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck with his ox-axe; and of the leap-loving women"},{"sp":"N","t":"one shook the cymbals of Rhea from her palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another laid down the drums from her rattle-loving hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another flung away the clustered harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another slipped and fell with the nectarous cups;","st":"20.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many cast into the dust, self-rolling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweet-singing pipe and the breathing flute of Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when after a storm, beside a calm thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a shepherd, seeing the mortal-delighting season of cloudless"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon, raises a revel, and the Nymphs dance with him;","st":"20.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"and suddenly from a crag the circling water of a mountain-poured"},{"sp":"N","t":"river is spilled, towered up in waves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he, piping, shakes the harp from his hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"in fear of the bold flood of the torrent river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest it hide his sheep in the swollen stream;","st":"20.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he, scattering the shout of the mind-delighting dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove the unveiled Bacchants to a high-headed mountain;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and routing the wandering womanish war, danceless,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the whetted ox-axe, the keepsake of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze-tunicked Lycurgus fought against unarmed Bacchus;","st":"20.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hard-fighting Hera crashed out a thunderous din,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavy-thundering stepmother bearing down upon Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified him; and as the heavy-jealous goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed herself on high, the knees of Bacchus shook;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he thought that Cronion was shielding Lycurgus,","st":"20.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the thunderous pattern of the airy crash;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fleeing with terrified feet, an untraceable wayfarer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he plunged beneath the grey-green wave of the Erythraean sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Thetis of the deep embraced him in a friendly arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he sank within her loud-roaring hall","st":"20.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arabian Nereus welcomed him with hospitable hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and comforting him he soothed him with friendly speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Tell me, Dionysus, why are your eyes downcast?"},{"sp":"N","t":"No army of ground-born Arabs, no mortal man"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursuing you has conquered you, and you have not fled a mortal spear;","st":"20.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Hera, the sister-wife of Zeus Cronides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed herself from heaven, fighting beside Lycurgus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera and battle-steadfast Ares and the brazen upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fourth was Lycurgus, great as he is; and often"},{"sp":"N","t":"your high-ruling father, the champion of the upper air, has yielded to Hera.","st":"20.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"The boast shall be the greater for you, when one of the blessed ones says"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the wife and sister of great Zeus, Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed her own hands against uncorseleted Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Nereus said, comforting Bromius."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as Dionysus was covered by the grey-gleaming surges,","st":"20.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lycurgus in distress broke out in a cry toward the waters:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Would that my father had taught me the works of the sea after the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I might contend even in the contest of fishermen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having hunted Dionysus, and from the underwater gulfs"},{"sp":"N","t":"might drag my Lydian servant a second time to land.","st":"20.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"But since I have not learned the works of sea-traveling fishermen"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not know the guile-stitching guile of the deep-sea hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keep the deep-flowing house of Leucothea, until from the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"I remove both you and him they call Melicertes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who bears kindred blood; and there is no need of iron,","st":"20.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"no need of the unsparing earthly ox-axe, but I want"},{"sp":"N","t":"fishermen, so that, plunging into the depth of the Erythraean brine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they may snatch Dionysus from the inmost recess of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"fishermen, searchers of the haunts of Nereus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not spread your nets for the finny creatures of the sea,","st":"20.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"but draw up Dionysus with your lines; and let Leucothea"},{"sp":"N","t":"come back to land, hunted together with Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let bold Palaemon follow to my house"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unwetted limbs, serving Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, leaving the Ephyraean course of sea-reared horses,","st":"20.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"he may yoke my chariot beside an earthly manger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he himself together with Bacchus as attendants; let there be one house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one house for them both, for Palaemon and for Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he was enraged, and threatened the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"and grey Nereus, and wished to lash the deep.","st":"20.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father cried out to monstrous Lycurgus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You are witless, Lycurgus, striving in vain with the winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"withdraw on your own feet, while your eyes still see."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have heard how once beside a mountain-poured spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"Teiresias alone beheld Athena naked;","st":"20.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted no furious spear and made no war on the goddess."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nevertheless he only saw, and lost the light of his eyes.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word high-ruling Zeus spoke through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the overbearing impiety of Lycurgus."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The twenty-first holds the wrath of the Earth-shaker and the man-breaking battle of Ambrosia and the ambush of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the son of Dryas forget his earlier battle;","st":"21.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"but taking his ox-axe a second time within the thicket &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"he went seeking the tribes of the Bassarids. And to Ambrosia"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavenly Zeus gave strength and war-mad courage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she then, raving, gripped by the swell of madness,","st":"21.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted a marble stone, hurling it at Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dashed the mighty helmet from his locks."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, taking courage, fought back with a greater,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rough stone, and struck the breast of the ox-eyed Nymph;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did he lay her low, and in anger she raised her voice:","st":"21.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Ares, lord of war, father of mighty Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watch in shame as your son, instead of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoots at a paltry, ironless woman."},{"sp":"N","t":"The sea overpowers my ox-axe; for in the surges"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus was hidden, and I, going without accomplishment,","st":"21.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall come to my own city, and shall leave my labor unfinished.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and taking Ambrosia by the middle with a limb-strong bond"},{"sp":"N","t":"he squeezed her in his hand; and he wished to fasten fetters on her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though dragging a spear-won captive to a migrant house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying off the revel-loving Nymph, the child-tender of Bromius,","st":"21.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"pricking her slavish back with his double-edged ox-axe."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did he rein her back as she stood, nor redden her"},{"sp":"N","t":"with freshly poured gore as he battered her head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but saffron-robed Ambrosia fled the bold man"},{"sp":"N","t":"and prayed to her mother Earth that she might escape Lycurgus.","st":"21.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"And fruit-bearing Earth, spreading open her hollow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received Ambrosia living in a loving bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaid of Bromius; and the Nymph, made invisible,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changed her form into a plant and became a vine shoot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and weaving a self-coiling cord about Lycurgus","st":"21.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"she clamped his yoked neck in a throttling bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting after the thyrsus with a threatening cluster."},{"sp":"N","t":"And mighty Rhea made the plant endowed with voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to Dionysus, the king of the vines;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ambrosia shrieked and cried with a breathing voice:","st":"21.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Not even being a plant shall I ever escape your battle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall wound your body even among the shoots, and instead of a chain"},{"sp":"N","t":"of bronze I would bind you tight with unloosable leaves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"against you I arm myself even as a vine, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The Bassarids kill their murderers even among leaves.'","st":"21.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"Beware of warlike plantings; for the vines"},{"sp":"N","t":"strike the adversaries, and the harvests hurl spears."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fought you living, and perishing I shall subdue you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so the nurses of Dionysus play the champion."},{"sp":"N","t":"Have you heard of the sea-dwelling remora, how in the deep","st":"21.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"a little, feeble fish has often assailed sailors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reining them back, and with the small gape of its throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"has checked a long merchant ship, held fast in a bond?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me as a remora of the land, receive the self-fettering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ironless bond of leaves in a cluster-rich tumult.","st":"21.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"Stay for me, stay there awaiting the son of Thyone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until he returns from the gulfs of the sea.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the vine Ambrosia of the long leaves said"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a clustered voice, as Lycurgus was battered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wild man, held fast in green bonds,","st":"21.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken, pinned about in the unbreakable fetters of leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shouted, threatening Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor had he strength to flee, and in vain by necessity he shook"},{"sp":"N","t":"his throat entwined with paltry tendrils;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did his voice pass through the middle of his windpipe","st":"21.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he was squeezed crown-wise; and the Bacchants circled"},{"sp":"N","t":"his neck girdled in the middle with a strangling cluster."},{"sp":"N","t":"And spear-wielding Ares snatched away the frightful axe"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his son; for he feared the raving Bacchant of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she strike the body of Lycurgus with the murderous ox-axe;","st":"21.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did he loose the son of Dryas from the green bonds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"much though he longed to, but yielded to his father's lightning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the threatening thunderous din of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Polyxo, falling upon the head of the monstrous man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tore out his long hair, roots and all;","st":"21.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"and casting a maddened hand upon the belly of the foe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seizing his breastplate, she wrenched it up with a snatching throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in anger broke it — warlike Muses, tell"},{"sp":"N","t":"what a wonder it was then, the tunic torn apart","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"by a woman's nails, though it was of iron —;","st":"21.75","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and plaiting a coiling cord of long withies,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loose-haired Cleite and vine-clad Gigarto"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddened the body of Lycurgus with a fair-leaved whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a bloody weal upon his scored back;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phleio, dread-raving, scored his sole with broader thorns;","st":"21.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Eriphe, the companion of Eiraphiotes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gripping the middle of his shaggy chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed to throw the man to the ground; and fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phasyleia, the steerer of the Bacchic dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the flank of the foe with a sharp goad;","st":"21.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Theope armed herself, the nurse of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a hide-piercing fennel-stalk; and she battered the body of Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Bromie named for Bromius; and with them the Nymph"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cisseis, cluster-loving, lashed the man with ivy."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as Lycurgus was overpowered by a war of wood,","st":"21.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, worse sorrow appeared again; for against Arabia"},{"sp":"N","t":"mountain Rhea armed the Earth-shaker of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hurler who splits foundations with a crash;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he dashed away the deep-bosomed bolt of the floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling with his trident, the sea-ruling Earth-shaker,","st":"21.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the hollows were lashed by the winds within,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth-working winds, since with a directing throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lair-dwelling gusts hollow out gaping chasms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unshaken bosom of the Arabian field was shaken,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the cloud-neighboring halls were loosed by the shaking throb;","st":"21.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"and oaks fell to the ground, and, battered by the trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Arabian base of Nysa danced, coiled about;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the elm lay on the earth, and the laurel dusted its hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the fir leaned, roots and all, against a neighboring pine."},{"sp":"N","t":"While the Earth-shaker, a violent wind beneath the earth,","st":"21.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"uprooted the recess of the nether haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile there was another, newer evil; for the wood-dwelling"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nysian women, struck by the whip of serpent-haired Megaera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowed like bulls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyers of their own children; and rushing on,","st":"21.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"one shot her wandering boy into the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he slipped head first from the air into the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another dragged off her dear babe, and did not remember her breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another reddened her palm with child-slaying iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having carved up her son, and became a raving Agave.","st":"21.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"And they ran upon their own children, and the newly born"},{"sp":"N","t":"sons whom they had borne they cut up with the knife; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span> another, cowering beneath the maddening whip of Pan,","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"an Arabian shepherd, raved into a rustic battle.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[3,2],[20,20]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the herdsman surged with in a maddening guile,","st":"21.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"carving up his own children and feasting on his sons"},{"sp":"N","t":"with child-devouring jaws; and the belly of the mind-tottering"},{"sp":"N","t":"herdsmen entombed the unreared male offspring &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the hands of the Nymphs; and with much-bending leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was pinned about and fettered, and did not bend his knee to Lyaeus,","st":"21.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not stretch a hand to Zeus, the averter of necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had no fear of the thunder; but threatening with his face"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raged at the Bassarids; and he perceived the lightning"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushing upon his head, and did not give way to Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he was struck this way and that, and of the wide-scattered blows","st":"21.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stood awaiting so great an opposing rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Ares alone as helper, alone striving"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Zeus, Poseidon, Rhea, Earth, Nereus, Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring he belched forth an unbridled cry:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Kindle fire, let us burn the whole plant; let these Bacchic","st":"21.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaves lie in the fire, and let us fling the blazing vines"},{"sp":"N","t":"through the sea to Dionysus beneath the waves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the token of Arabian manhood; and let even Thetis herself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the fire-burnt harvest beneath the wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"quench the vine ash in the sea.","st":"21.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"Loose these phantoms and the shifting charms of bonds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I see these as the Poseidonian charms of the Nereids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"loose me, and bring me near the surges; for against prophetic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drug-working Proteus I arm myself; kindle the pine-torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, going beside the sea with my avenging heart,","st":"21.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may burn up Melicertes, the host of Bromius.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, threatening both Nereus and Dionysus; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she [Hera] came near Arabia; and the weary son of Enyalius,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[7,14]]},{"sp":"N","t":"she brought back to life out of the wooden tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the iron sword of Ares; and against the Bacchants","st":"21.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bared the light-bearing back of the divine knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting the Cybelian womanish war into terror;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting apart the leaves of Ambrosia with the iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"she unclamped the clustered bonds of Lycurgus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she soothed the dark-haired shaker of the earth,","st":"21.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"her own brother, and Zeus her husband and her mother Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having saved Lycurgus, so that he might be counted"},{"sp":"N","t":"among the immortals; and the Arabs upon savory altars"},{"sp":"N","t":"propitiated the son of Dryas with sacrifices, as a god,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring gore as libation to Bacchus-less Lycurgus","st":"21.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of the honey-dripping harvest of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things aged Time was to accomplish late;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Zeus the father, lest any other proud mortal man"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the likeness of spear-bold Lycurgus"},{"sp":"N","t":"should raise reproach against blameless Dionysus,","st":"21.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"made dread-raving Lycurgus a blind wanderer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wayfarer turning back and forth in an unknown city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking an escort for his necessary path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"often stumbling with his own self-guided sandals."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things were among the crags. But in the Erythraean sea","st":"21.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughters of Nereus within their deep-waved court"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothed Dionysus at a sea-table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and casting away her envy of Semele's Zeus-fallen bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a bold hymn for vine-planting Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the midwife of Dionysus, Ino of the sea, made melody;","st":"21.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Melicertes, his foster-brother of the same years,"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured wine for Bromius, drawing sweet nectar from the bowl."},{"sp":"N","t":"So he stayed there within the deep-waved court,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the broad sea as his house, a migrant beneath the waves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and poured out he reclined in the weedy bosom of Thetis;","st":"21.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unsated he embraced his Cadmeian nurse blessed in children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ino, the very sister of his mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often clasped Palaemon in a friendly bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his foster-brother of the same years. And with noiseless sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Mimallon, no longer striking up a much-coiling dance,","st":"21.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"since Bacchus was not present, was terrified,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the tracks of sea-traveling Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the toil-loving Satyr, with an unlaughing face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"labored in a strange grief, and with mountain-wandering hooves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering Pans ran gadfly-mad through the thickets,","st":"21.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pans, searchers for unheralded Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and danceless Silenus, casting away his uncared-for cymbals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay downcast; and the Cronian Nymph whirled about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Macris, the nurse of grief-free Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharer of the Bacchic chariot of the well-greaved wagon.","st":"21.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"So they were shaken and downcast; and to them in their grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"Scelmis, leaving the recess of the waveless sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove his father's unwetted wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing beforehand the return of Dionysus as he came."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Bacchus tended the sea-reared dinners of the table,","st":"21.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile, through the Caucasian mountain to the city of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind-footed herald of vine-planting Bromius came,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull-natured, having a counterfeit look in his horn-drawing shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an answering imitation of the horns of Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having fastened about his skin the hide of a mountain-ranging goat,","st":"21.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"let down from one shoulder at the collarbone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hanging along his right side to the fold of his thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his ears on either side of both cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a long-eared ass, shaggy in body; and from the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his loins a kindred tail trailed, self-coiling.","st":"21.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the blazing Indians flowed about him laughing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until he came near, where upon a twin-yoked car"},{"sp":"N","t":"sat towering Deriades, the leader of men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reining back the standing tread of sky-high elephants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laughing at the Satyr he cried a mocking voice:","st":"21.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"What two-colored men bull-natured Dionysus sends"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Deriades, the playthings of war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"strange-natured, not men of wholly mortal-seeming shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the look of beasts, since in a twofold form"},{"sp":"N","t":"they are counterfeit bulls and men; for they have both","st":"21.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shape of an ox and of a human face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And your Bacchus is called fiery, because he rose"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the flanks of his Zeus-struck mother Thyone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and water is far better than fire; if he wishes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my father, Indian Hydaspes, with a seething flood","st":"21.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"could quench the fire-breathing breath of the thunderbolt of Zeus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and showing the forerunning tokens of war"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck his dappled-backed shield with unsparing knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the circling boss that showed in the middle; and from the beaten"},{"sp":"N","t":"ox-hide the bronze hummed a deadly echo.","st":"21.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"And loosing his astonished lips to the grim king"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swift-running herald spoke the message of Bromius:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Deriades, scepter-bearer, the god Dionysus bids"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indians, receiving the wine of the care-forgetting harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to pour libation to the immortals, apart from wars, apart from toils;","st":"21.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if they do not receive it, he arms himself, until Hydaspes"},{"sp":"N","t":"bends a slave's knee to the thyrsi of the Bassarids."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have heard a true message; speak yourself"},{"sp":"N","t":"some word to me who ask, so that I may announce it to Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the scepter-bearer belched a raving voice:","st":"21.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Alas, what a word a bold human beast has spoken."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to subdue a herald with a warlike hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one who holds no furious spear and touches no ox-hide shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have heard all that your champion suffered; Ganges has heard"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the feebleness of Bromius and the manhood of Lycurgus;","st":"21.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know your king, a bastard god, when fleeing"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slipped into the depth of the evil-averting sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you wish, bend your foot to the neighboring land of the Medes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"go there and proclaim the dances of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Enter the Bactrian soil, where Mithras is a god,","st":"21.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Assyrian Phaethon in Persia; for Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"has not learned the chorus of the heavenly blessed ones, and does not honor"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun and Zeus and the chorus of bright-shining stars."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not learned of Cronus, nor of Cronides the slayer of his father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor of crooked-counseling Cronus, the feaster on his own children,","st":"21.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"who mowed from the upper air the plant-sowing swarm of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know nothing of your gifts and of the harvest you have named;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I accept no other drink after golden Hydaspes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"my wine is a spear, and my drink again is an ox-hide shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"No Semele bore me in a fire-struck wedlock,","st":"21.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"having received a murderous flame in her chamber, but bronze-tunicked"},{"sp":"N","t":"Enyo, unsated of battles, reared us."},{"sp":"N","t":"I care nothing for the blessed children of Zeus; for two alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"are gods for me, both Earth and Water."},{"sp":"N","t":"Go and say these things to war-fleeing Dionysus:","st":"21.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Be off, flee untraceable, while I still hold back my bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be off, flee my spear; and arming for battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"your half-finished beasts and uncorseleted women,"},{"sp":"N","t":"make war on Deriades, and after an Indian victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall drag you too as companion to spear-won Dionysus.","st":"21.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor shall I make you my messenger; for you cannot"},{"sp":"N","t":"hold a servant's house-keeping work; but I shall leave you"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the banquets, fanning with your long ears.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he sent him away with a threatening face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scoring the middle hollow of a folding tablet","st":"21.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swift-worded man wrote such a word on the double leaf:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"If you can, Dionysus, arm yourself against Deriades.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Hearing such things the sounding herald ran back again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he found the Sileni rejoicing; for coming up"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the surges Dionysus mingled with the mountain Nymphs;","st":"21.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs skipped, and the Bacchants danced,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with his aged feet Maron led the song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning his forearm on the neck of a twin Bacchant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seen in the middle, gushing up a fragrant flood of wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unveiled Mimallon crashed out a melody,","st":"21.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing the footstep of Dionysus returned."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god cast away his earlier cares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entered upon delight, since he had learned within the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing everything from Proteus of Torone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the earth-shaking throb of the inhospitable Arabian field,","st":"21.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of tottering Lycurgus, a blind wanderer on his own foot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he heard too of the death-bearing sting of pastoral necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how the chorus of country folk whirled about, how in the glens"},{"sp":"N","t":"the women carved up their own birth-pangs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he heard of the chorus of the airy Hyades, he heard of","st":"21.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ambrosia herself rising in Olympus after the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ambrosia arming herself against unwearied Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of the fair-branched battle and the vine war."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they delighted the herald came back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unharmed, much-prayed-for by exulting Dionysus,","st":"21.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"telling the high-necked folly of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the double tablet pregnant with war."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was the lord careless; and with courage he called"},{"sp":"N","t":"his warriors to battle, recognizing the forerunning"},{"sp":"N","t":"tokens of Deriades scored on the witnessing tablet.","st":"21.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"And calling the wandering Rhadamanes, whom once Minos"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove unwilling from the soil of the Cretan land"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the floor of Arabia, he bade them, at the nod of Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to fix ship-timbers for a sea-battle with the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And swiftly he drove his chariot toward the eastern region of the earth,","st":"21.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing in armor like Phosphorus; and about the Caucasian"},{"sp":"N","t":"rock, passing through its crested hollow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he passed the light-bearing floor of the eastern field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"traveling the midday starting-line of the sun."},{"sp":"N","t":"While the voice of the fair-thyrsus battle was heard,","st":"21.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the army of mountain-ranging Dionysus near at hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile Deriades set a close ambush of Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending his unyoked host to the land opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting all his guile-devising hope of battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"to bronze-tunicked Ares; and he sailed high on ships","st":"21.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"having rowed his people across the Indian Hydaspes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the war of the Indians was divided among two armies"},{"sp":"N","t":"on either floor of the javelin-bearing river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thureus beside the ankles of Zephyrus, and Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"came near the opposite side beside the wing of blazing Eurus.","st":"21.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a well-shaded place there, where with thick"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoots of every kind a broad-stretched ridge of wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"was girdled, and there was a hollow cave; and flying"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wandering arrow never scored those trees,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if anyone shot; and never in the midst of the bushes","st":"21.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"was the sun carried, rushing down with sharp throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the interwoven leaves with inmost rays;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no air-roaming pouring from rainy Zeus entered"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thick-shaded wood, and scarcely, with a trail of water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the high-visible rain of Zeus wet the tips of the leaves.","st":"21.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"There in the long-trunked groves thievish Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"was hidden in the green clusters of sky-high plants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen, unshaken, and in the wooded bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"he kept the guarded tread of noiseless sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"neither combing the leafy thicket with secret foot,","st":"21.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor having fear of a crouching foot, nor a chattering echo"},{"sp":"N","t":"with stammering lip, nor pallor about his face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he had a bold and steadfast mind, and on his couch"},{"sp":"N","t":"sleeping a measured warlike sleep upon his eyelids &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"he awaited the well-rhythmed war of the coming army.","st":"21.345"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The twenty-second sings the battle of Bromius and what deeds Aeacus accomplished both on the plain and in Hydaspes."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the foot-throng of Bacchus reached the ford","st":"22.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fine-shingled river, where with deep-eddying bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indian Hydaspes belches navigable water like the Nile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then indeed the womanish song of the Bassarids made melody,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the chorus of shaggy Satyrs roared with mystic voice,","st":"22.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a Phrygian revel for Lyaeus of the night;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the whole earth laughed, and the rocks bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Naiads shrieked, and above the river the Nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled themselves in spirals with the silent streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sang shrill the yoked rhythm of a Sicilian song,","st":"22.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as the hymn-tending Sirens struck up"},{"sp":"N","t":"from honey-tongued throats; and the whole thicket whirled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wise oaks uttered a melody like a flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Hadryads shouted, and on a fair-leaved cluster"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Nymph sang half-seen, peering out above it.","st":"22.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the poured spring whitened with snowy milk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though it was of water, and in the torrent-bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naiads bathed in milky streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drank white milk; and the rocky crag purpled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belching wine from a reddening breast,","st":"22.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing new wine from an unplanted hill"},{"sp":"N","t":"in sweet-drinking drops; and from self-pouring hollows"},{"sp":"N","t":"the delicious gifts of the honey-dripping bee were shed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"needing no hives; and from freshly delivered bushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the down-covered fruit of the sharp-haired thorn ran up;","st":"22.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as oil poured of its own accord upon the branches"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tree of Athena bathed in unpressed moisture."},{"sp":"N","t":"And hares embraced the dancing hounds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and long serpents raved in the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"licking the tracks of serpent-haired Dionysus,","st":"22.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"bending their necks aside, and one after another belched"},{"sp":"N","t":"a gentle hiss from a rejoicing throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rhythm of the delighting serpent was then intelligent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the entwined trail of its long spine was whirled"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it skipped about the fearless feet of Dionysus;","st":"22.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"and skipping in spirals on the Indian cliff"},{"sp":"N","t":"tigers sported; and a great swarm of mountain-ranging"},{"sp":"N","t":"elephants leapt up within the thicket."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then the wandering Pans ran through the rugged glens,"},{"sp":"N","t":"through hard-to-tread mountains with slender hooves,","st":"22.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"dread places, over which no bold bird flew, a light wayfarer &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"measuring them with the double beat of high-traveling wings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking the mane that hung beside his throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"a lion, their pasture-mate, danced opposite a dancing boar;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and birds cried out an imitation of human song,","st":"22.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away a mimic, unfinished echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesying the victory of the Indian-slaying contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darting at full stretch their upright tails"},{"sp":"N","t":"hanging beside their green limbs; and in emulous dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"the high-flying leopard ran running with a bear.","st":"22.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Artemis reined back the rush of her dappled hounds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the dance-beating leap of a gentle lioness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in shame she unstrung her well-rounded bowstring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she shoot with her shafts the beasts that delighted."},{"sp":"N","t":"And someone, beholding these strange wonders of Bacchus,","st":"22.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting his eye through the topmost of a thick cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having wrapped leaves about the gazing circle of his eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let himself see only so much as a man looks about"},{"sp":"N","t":"through the made eyeholes of a helmet as he peers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or as when a man skilled in the tragic chorus,","st":"22.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a dread bellowing from long-sounding throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretches his inner eye through a wrought eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the lying semblance of a mortal face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he, hidden beneath the thick-shaded wood, watched"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the wonders unforeseen with a stealing face;","st":"22.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he announced it to the adversaries; and Thureus whirled in fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming Morrheus and witless Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indian throng trembled, and, caring nothing for the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook the bronze armor from their fearful hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the trees shaken with a raving rush.","st":"22.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"And now the Indian throng, taking from the neighboring bank"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grey-green branch of olive as the witness of supplication,"},{"sp":"N","t":"would have bent a slave's neck to unopposed Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but much-devising Hera, changing her body,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heartened the foes and beguiled the leader of the Indians,","st":"22.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"applying a Thessalian magic hymn against Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the potion of Circe with god-invoking incantations,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as of a drugging river that cannot be drugged."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she persuaded the swift-obeying adversaries; and she told each"},{"sp":"N","t":"never to be tripped up, gripped by blazing thirst,","st":"22.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"by drinking the beguiled water of the mind-stealing river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now the blazing Indians, leaping from their unseen haunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"would have assailed the feasting armies;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but a Hamadryad Nymph, peering out above a windy cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprang from a shaggy hollow;","st":"22.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and holding a thyrsus in her hand she seemed a Bacchant in form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her hair covered with mimic woodland ivy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and telling the guile of the foes with signifying silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"she whispered in the ears of clustered Lyaeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Vine Dionysus, plant-tending lord of fruits,","st":"22.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"your plant shall give grace and beauty to the Hadryads;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I was not born a Bassarid, I am no companion of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"only in my hand I lift a lying thyrsus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am not from Phrygia, your homeland, I do not dwell in the land"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Lydians beside the flood of a wealth-flowing river;","st":"22.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am a fair-leaved Hamadryad, where warlike"},{"sp":"N","t":"foes lie in ambush, and, caring nothing for my homeland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall save your army from death; for I bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"faithful things to your Satyrs, Indian though I am,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of Deriades I am of one mind with Dionysus;","st":"22.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I shall grant you a favor owed, because the moist"},{"sp":"N","t":"birth-pangs of the streams and the oaks are ever increased","hvShift5":[[0,0],[27,26],[31,27],[35,30]]},{"sp":"N","t":"by the rainy drop of great father Zeus who sends rain."},{"sp":"N","t":"Give me your leaves, and I shall plant them here,"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me your clusters, which loose cares.","st":"22.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, friend, do not hasten to cross the flood of the river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the neighboring Indians bear down on you in the water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretch your eye toward the oaks, and beside the fair-leaved thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"watch for the unforeseen ambush of hidden men."},{"sp":"N","t":"But what will the feeble ones within the thicket do to you?","st":"22.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"The foes live while you still hold back your thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Softly about us, lest the enemy hear nearby,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest Hydaspes announce it to the hidden Indians.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking the Hamadryad Nymph went back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a wing or a thought, and changing her form","st":"22.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"she ran, like a bird in nature, through the lair-holding wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting into an oak of her own age. But he in silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingled with the Bassarids, and told the speech of the Hamadryad"},{"sp":"N","t":"goddess into the ears of each of his champions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"signaling with nods, and with intelligent silence he bade them","st":"22.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"feast among the oaks armed in their weapons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and told the guile of the hidden, guile-stitching Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the warriors bear down upon them uncorseleted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still feasting throughout the army; and they obeyed Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he commanded, and were ready for battle","st":"22.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the silent dinner of a javelin-bearing table."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after a swift meal the shield-bearers flowed in"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the neighboring river to drink an after-dinner water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the divine nods of Dionysus of surpassing mind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest drunkenness and stupor and darkness lull the army.","st":"22.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the army fell this way and that on a war-loving bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"sleeping a brief sleep upon the warlike shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father, turning aside the guileful mind of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"checked the evening battle with a bellowing din,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the boundless echo of an all-night rain.","st":"22.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when snowy-footed Dawn, scoring the gloom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpled on a dewy rock, plucking the morning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foes, peering out over the top of the battle-rousing"},{"sp":"N","t":"shelter of the wood, pressed forward in a mass; and Thureus led,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the monstrous champion of the Indian war, like in his rush","st":"22.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"to sky-high Typhon rushing down beneath the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the armies, at the guile-minded nod of prudent Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a lying fear, though fearless, and out of the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"they withdrew of their own accord, willingly, until the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured out into the plain, leaving the haunts of the thicket.","st":"22.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Lydian man armed himself with rich armor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the gold-shining pattern of Lycian Glaucus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaiming his own soil, where the bright wealth"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the reddening dew glitters on the Pactolian bank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he flashed with rosy beams facing the Dawn,","st":"22.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking the yellow brow of a wealth-flowing helmet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the conspicuous Lydian man, and from the breast of its bearer"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sparkle of a reddening tunic blazed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking a gleaming helmet upon his temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"another champion from Alybe, playing the hero for Dionysus,","st":"22.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"displayed his ancestral wealth, and from his bright head"},{"sp":"N","t":"the marble radiance of a silver helmet shone"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting a gleam equal to snowy Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the unsteady god terrified all the warriors"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the foes, holding no naked sword, brandishing no spear,","st":"22.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"but carried in the midst of the champions like the breezes"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wheeled the right wing of the battle from the left,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling his long-shadowed thyrsus, the flower of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the cloud of Indians with his ivy spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did high-headed Thureus, great as he was, drive him back,","st":"22.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the army, nor any other champion; but poured upon one another"},{"sp":"N","t":"they gave way this way and that before rushing Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Oeagrus dashed away the dark war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowing unsated the ranks of men one after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the helmet-glancing cornfields with his Bistonian spear.","st":"22.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when a river, pouring forth a rough strength,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restless from a crag with a torrent stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"comes carried into the plain, and not even the orchards themselves"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unbreakable fences hold it back"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it scrapes through the middle backs of a stone bridge;","st":"22.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a fir is rolled along, and many a high-visible pine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"falling roots and all, is dragged in the flood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he tended the army of the foes, destroying"},{"sp":"N","t":"one after another, a footman turning about with his Sithonian lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they circled him, and what warriors call","st":"22.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mimic tortoise they towered up with their shields;"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing foot was propped on foot, and shield leaned"},{"sp":"N","t":"on shield, close-set, one after another against its neighbor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressed tight, and crest nodded against crest, and man"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched man, appearing near; and as the dust was raised","st":"22.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"by horses' hooves the warriors were whitened."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then whom first, whom last did Oeagrus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"citizen of the Bistonian field, mow down and send to Hades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing one from here and another from there, accomplishing deeds"},{"sp":"N","t":"not inferior to those of his wife, famed Calliope?","st":"22.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"One above the breast with his swift spear, and another cutting"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his hilted sword at the neck, and another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dread-raving foe, he pierced beside the navel, and from the bloody"},{"sp":"N","t":"wound he drew out his spear, and as it was dragged"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pulled the hot entrails along with the blood-red iron;","st":"22.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon another as he fought he ran down drawing his blade,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the well-whetted sword cut off his hand, and it fell"},{"sp":"N","t":"blood-dipped and gasped upon the ground, the leaping hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hand was cut off and did not let go of the ox-hide shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the edge of the dusty strap;","st":"22.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wind-roaming soul of the dead man, darting up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed for the accustomed youth of the body it had been entwined with."},{"sp":"N","t":"Another he crushed, striking with unsparing spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"piercing the top of his arm with the whetted point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struck his shield with his sword, and as the unbreakable ox-hide","st":"22.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"was battered by the iron its middle-bossed back hummed."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, shaken by the raving sting of battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled his lance as it migrated with a circling craft"},{"sp":"N","t":"either on both sides of the ribs or at the neck or close to the shoulder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking this way and that his back-turning point","st":"22.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut through the middle backs of the crowded fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift, seated high upon a high-crested horse."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when, after the shadowy season of a chilling storm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the air appears, stripped bare of covering clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the clear radiance of a springtime light;","st":"22.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he, raving fearless through the thick ranks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid bare the middle of the battle as the Indians were split apart."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then someone, setting his bronze about the mouth of a champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck his right jaw with a frightful knife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, sending a humming into the air against the adversaries,","st":"22.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled a wandering stone at a high-pathed mark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the air-roaming stone crashed upon a head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dashed the crest from the hair of a well-helmeted man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neck-strap loosed beside his chin;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as it rolled away the head of its bearer was bared.","st":"22.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not men alone then roared, but even the bronze-tunicked"},{"sp":"N","t":"horses themselves crashed out into war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trumpeting Ares with a warlike whinnying."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden who speaks last, from mountain-haunting throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"answering their din with rocky mouths,","st":"22.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"Echo neighed in mimicry a warlike melody."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many a freshly slain corpse rolled upon the fields"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting out a hot stream of blood; and of those perishing"},{"sp":"N","t":"some were suspended dead upon their sides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, struck, writhed as his flank was scored,","st":"22.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"another rolled over the ground in the loose dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another was propped up beside his navel, and one upon the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped upon the head of a gasping man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, falling, cried out, struck on the throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirled his feet about, having the dance of destruction;","st":"22.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"another lay prone, and as though angry at his slayer"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gripped the earth, wide-gaping, with maddened tooth."},{"sp":"N","t":"As another was struck by the long-pointed iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"his white tunic reddened with the gore of the javelin-caster;"},{"sp":"N","t":"as another fought, from drawn bows","st":"22.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"his blood-dipped thigh was scored by a winged arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a hostile man, setting his trumpet in vain about his lip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sounded a melody of battle-rousing echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing his reluctant, flight-loving army into the fray."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indians, hearing the cry, flowed into the tumult.","st":"22.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"And with courage they took hold of a returning battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ashamed to appear to their king without victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many well-corseleted warriors circled crown-wise"},{"sp":"N","t":"in one place Aeacus as he darted forth."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he in the middle was hard pressed, not by a helmet,","st":"22.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"not trusting in shields nor in a breastplate of war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Athena towered him wholly about, covered instead of iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his father's unbreakable clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with which he had once quenched the drought of an unwetted field"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing life-sustaining water upon a thirsty land","st":"22.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Zeus sent rain, and the fair-birthing furrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the sheaf-bearing earth were wedded to the plow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and encircled in the midst of his foes the inspired man"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed some with his swift spear, others with his knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others with rocky stones; and the ground reddened with gore","st":"22.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the Indians were killed, and by the point of the unbending man"},{"sp":"N","t":"there lay a pouring of wide-scattered corpses, of whom one"},{"sp":"N","t":"gasped half-dead, and another, beating the ground with his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-rolling on his back, kept company with a neighboring doom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they were crowded on the ground, and corpse was propped on corpse","st":"22.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying measured out, and from a freshly cut throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cold body was warmed with reddening gore;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the slaughter was unspeakable, and as they fell one after another"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth, darkening, drenched with a trail of blood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitying her sons, spoke with a torrent voice:","st":"22.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Murderous grain-giving son of Zeus — for you rule"},{"sp":"N","t":"both the fruit-bearing rain and the bloody snowfall —"},{"sp":"N","t":"with rain you have wetted the whole generative orchard"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Hellas, and you have deluged the Indian furrow with gore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you who before were sheaf-bearing, now death-bearing; for the country folk","st":"22.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"your snowfall found the corn, but you have mowed the army of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reaping men like a cornfield; and you do both,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from Zeus you bring rain, from Ares you snow with blood.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things life-bearing Earth said. But Cronion"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried out from heaven, and the trumpet of Zeus with thunderous","st":"22.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashes challenged Aeacus to the slaughter of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one among the adversaries, stretching his eye toward Aeacus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent a shaft, and Athena turned aside the arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which grazed his thigh, scratching only so much as the surface of the skin."},{"sp":"N","t":"And still more, without pain, Aeacus fought, unsteady,","st":"22.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the midst of the Indians, since the shaft touched his thigh"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a man's thin nail, when it scores the surface of the skin."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a certain man withdrew untraceable, a wayfarer on foot"},{"sp":"N","t":"with swifter steps, and wished to enter the neighboring thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where he had been hidden before; and pursuing him","st":"22.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Erechtheus drove his wind-footed horse into the course;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but when he had come as near as the upright rush of a flying"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear stretches when a champion hurls it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then indeed the man on foot, casting him behind his back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood facing him, awaiting the horseman; and he, crouching,","st":"22.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"propped his left foot upon the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting on his side, and lifted aloft the track"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his back-stretched sole, stretching upright"},{"sp":"N","t":"the toes of his right foot fixed in the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding an Indian shield of seven ox-hides, the image of a tower,","st":"22.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a sharp naked sword; and holding the bronze-backed"},{"sp":"N","t":"shield before his face the proud Indian ran on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"either to die or to strike the man or to drive off the colt"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his bold blade; and with the bossed iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"aslant, checking the jaw of the horse as it came against him,","st":"22.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"though on foot he shook the rider above him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he would have flung to the ground the citizen of motherless Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Erechtheus pierced him with his spear beside the navel's edge"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled the man to the ground, pierced through the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"with murderous sharp bronze; and with whirling rushes","st":"22.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slipped head first from the air into the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying his head as a tumbler with a dancing throb."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving him gasping, turning the course of his horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the citizen of Athena assailed other adversaries."},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span> Curving his bow, and spreading upon the string","st":"22.320","hvShift6":[[0,8]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the shaft stretched upright to the very tip of the iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew the arrow to the mark; and to his noble-bearing bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"Calliope he entrusted all his hope of victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nine long-pointed arrows he sent forth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and killed nine men; and there was an equal number","st":"22.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"of shafts sent and of warriors destroyed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one a wandering arrow split at the top of the brow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another it scored on the rim of his shaggy breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another above the flanks, another, falling upon his belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was carried in the middle as his flank was scored,","st":"22.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"another it ran through the side, and another, as he fled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it fixed upright in the stormy sole of his feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clamped his foot with an earth-yoked bond."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he drew out the windy shaft; and from his quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as one was sent after another, a rain of arrows","st":"22.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran down rushing with an airy whirl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when a man, driving bronze upon a bronze anvil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sends a fire-roaring sound with unwearied hammer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the neighboring mass, and many sparks"},{"sp":"N","t":"leap up from the beaten iron,","st":"22.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"warming the air, and with alternating rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"one goes before, another close by, another darting up"},{"sp":"N","t":"overtakes another still leaping with a blazing throb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he, shooting, scattered the facing army of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they fought, from his unceasing bow,","st":"22.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing one from here and another from there with arrows one upon another."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the middle phalanx shunned the cloud of shafts"},{"sp":"N","t":"the place was laid bare, the image of the horned Moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, shining on both sides, gleaming a little from her horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having filled the two tips with newly shining radiance,","st":"22.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"she scores the middle of her circle with slanting rays,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a twofold, divided soft fire; and the bare circles"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Moon still appeared as her middle was scored."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Aeacus cease from battle, fighting beside Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"undismayed, and he raved in the tumult,","st":"22.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing this way and that; and pursuing them from the plain"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drove the wandering host into the outpourings of the river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And massed together the warriors circled one man alone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck with swords, and he cared nothing for the knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor for the winged shaft; and with rushes one upon another","st":"22.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swift man mowed the iron cornfields of the dark"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting, and fought against them all, some upon the banks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others down in the river, cutting them down with warlike hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he filled the whole stream with corpses; and as they perished"},{"sp":"N","t":"white Hydaspes reddened, murmuring with blood.","st":"22.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a man, fleeing the windy rush of the champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped head first, self-rolling, into the stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a corpse freshly slain by the javelin-casting iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"was dragged, carried in the wavy trail"},{"sp":"N","t":"with swollen limbs; and in the underwater gore","st":"22.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naiads bathed in a blood-red stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the black water reddened with murderous drops."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many in the outpourings, casting away their points,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed supplication, unarmed, one upon the banks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another stretched beside the sands, another on the ground","st":"22.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"crouching upright, bending his arching neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Aeacus refused their prayers with face nodding upward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swelling an unbending wrath against his adversaries;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and not one Lycaon alone did he kill, an unarmed spearman"},{"sp":"N","t":"still touching him in supplication, but numberless foes","st":"22.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling on the ground with uncorseleted hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"he destroyed, defiling the stream of the river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hydaspes received many a corpse of Asteropaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Aeacus fight without a god; for, cutting down"},{"sp":"N","t":"his adversaries within the river, like the father of Peleus,","st":"22.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"he held a watery battle and a war of the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though prophesying about the flood of the river Camandrus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the half-finished fighting that was to come for Achilles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the battle of the grandfather divined the battle of the grandson."},{"sp":"N","t":"And in the outpourings an unsandaled Nymph cried out,","st":"22.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Naiad unveiled, peering above the currents:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Kinsman of the Naiads, bearing Zeus-fallen blood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pity the holy water of a Zeus-fallen river."},{"sp":"N","t":"Your spear has destroyed Indians enough; cease rousing tears"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the tearless Naiad Nymphs;","st":"22.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"your own mother too was a watery Naiad;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I hear that your Aegina was the daughter of a river."},{"sp":"N","t":"Remember who bore you, and defile the flood no longer."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to another undefiled stream, I go to the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And sea-dwelling Thetis receives me; but let this bloody","st":"22.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"stream be the care of the Erinys and of Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the twenty-third I sing of the Indian Hydaspes crossed, and the watery tumult and the smoky one."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the watery Naiad Nymph, drenched with blood,","st":"23.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"plunged into her father's blood-red water."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, striking the barbarian tribes beside the shores with his sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turned them into the outpourings; and pursued by the iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foes were killed, crowding Hydaspes in their terror.","st":"23.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"And many a one, whirling feet and hands in the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated swimmers, and wished to escape his doom"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the river streams with untried hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he was covered by the flood; and one after another in the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swelling, pregnant, was entombed in a watery doom.","st":"23.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor long beside the shore of the shield-bearing river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"encircled by so great a multitude of murderous Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Aeacus still remain, since to him in his labor came"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indian-slaying Dionysus, shaking his whetted thyrsi."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, piercing many another host with unsparing spear,","st":"23.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus dashed them down; and he raged like Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"running beside his well-corseleted brother Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus kept company with a yoked, watery battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a wet doom upon the adversaries. And if any man"},{"sp":"N","t":"swam, cutting the swells upon a cunning shield,","st":"23.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ravaged the back of the swimmers; and if any of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought half-seen, propping his sole upon the silt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck his breast or neck with the thyrsus, cutting the waves"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they sank; for he knew the bosom of the deep haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since the time when, fleeing the frightful battle of Lycurgus,","st":"23.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Nereus received him in his surging house."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many were shut in this way and that by the stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling before the mountain-running son of Zeus, of whom one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright, clamped his feet in the muddy silt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and self-fixed, unshaken, from his loins to his head","st":"23.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose half-seen, the fold of his thigh hidden;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fought against Bromius in the water more than on the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing twin spears with both his hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one he cast, sending it high toward the shores,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Aeacus opposite as his mark, and shaking another","st":"23.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear he hurled it at unwoundable Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one was fixed fast, covered to the middle of his flank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another found no way to flee, struck by the sharp thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his steps fettered in a muddy bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his sole held fast in the sands; and another stood","st":"23.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"as his shin was struck; and another, wetting the top of his knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a wet war through the bloody water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another was rooted, sunk as far as his chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted his feet, raising a bathed shoulder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the dread streams rushing down upon his face;","st":"23.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"another in the outpourings was drenched in his whole body from the sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the middle of his breast, and another, wetting"},{"sp":"N","t":"both his shoulders, and another, reddening the tip of his hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the wavy threat as it rushed upon him."},{"sp":"N","t":"Another sank into the depth, shaking his drenched lips","st":"23.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the man-slaying flood with a grinning throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a proud Indian, beholding his own comrades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some killed by the long spear, others by the knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another shot with a torrent-swift shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another cut down by the sharp, much-plaited thyrsus,","st":"23.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed the dead throng to Thureus, and in grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"tore his hair, and raved with the flaming torch of anger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping his closed lips with a sharp-toothed bond;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly, imitating self-slain Indian Orontes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing barbarian blood and swelling a barbarian temper,","st":"23.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bared his sword, and casting away his tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbreakable fence of Ares, the defender against shafts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bringing his blade, undismayed, against his own flank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-fated he broke out in a last proud speech:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Belly, receive this dear sword; for I am ashamed","st":"23.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"that some hostile, unwarlike hand should kill me."},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself shall drive the willing bronze into my own flank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest my father blame me for falling by a womanish thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he call a Satyr, or Bacchus, my slayer.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, driving his sword into his dark belly","st":"23.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bold palms, as though cutting down a stranger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he died self-slain among the adversaries, a Menoeceus,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[5,5],[13,6]]},{"sp":"N","t":"ashamed to look upon Deriades again after the battle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with tearless eyes he died by a willing doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and appeared a brazen Ajax without the madness.","st":"23.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the slaughter was unspeakable; and with a refusing stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hydaspes covered the slain and became their tomb."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one within the river poured out a last voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"And you, father, why do you hide your own children in your outpourings?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Often I went to a Bactrian war, but in his streams","st":"23.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Median Araxes never killed a Median throng;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Persian Euphrates did not hide a neighboring Persian;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often I had battle beside Taurus, but in the fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cydnus never entombed Cilicians in his bosom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tanais, bringing snowy petrified water,","st":"23.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"does not arm himself against the neighboring Sauromatian, but arming"},{"sp":"N","t":"a torrent war against his Colchian adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"has often dashed them down with a frosty missile."},{"sp":"N","t":"Eridanus was more blessed than you, because in his streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hid a foreign Phaethon and not a citizen,","st":"23.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not cover a Galatian and was no tomb for a Celt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but for his own dwellers he rolls down from wealth-flowing trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"the light-bearing amber gifts of the Heliades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iberian Rhine arms himself against infants, but as a judge,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and splitting apart the secret travail of a birth","st":"23.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"he kills a bastard offspring; but you hide the true children"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your dying dwellers, and cover no bastard blood."},{"sp":"N","t":"How can you mingle with rivers, or even with"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ocean your begetter and Tethys your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging a flood-tide of slaughter with bloody drops?","st":"23.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"Have reverence, lest you defile Poseidon with corpses."},{"sp":"N","t":"Your stream is worse than Bromius, because Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"does not rout me with his thyrsi as much as you rout me with your streams.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking the heavy-fated man received his last water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sailing was well-armed; and the peoples were lifted","st":"23.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"with swollen limbs; and as its bearer perished"},{"sp":"N","t":"a helmet, half-seen, was ferried along with floating crest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sinking little by little; and leaping upon the streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched at full length in the surges like the stern-cables of ships,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ox-hide shields sailed with swimming straps,","st":"23.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"in rows this way and that; and a man weighed down by the iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"his breastplate dragged, wet-tunicked, into the deep."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Dionysus check his warriors from battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until he had killed them all with his flesh-cutting thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving one alone as the herald of all the dead;","st":"23.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thureus alone he left, a god-fearing witness of the victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when Hera perceived the slaughter of the cut-down Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she flew from heaven, and along the high-pathed road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restless, she scored the air with windy sole."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she came to the East, and drove the Indian Hydaspes","st":"23.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"to raise a bloody war against Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the barbarian-voiced eastern Ares sank down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then indeed, contriving strange charms of seafaring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chorus of the Bacchoi was ferried over the waveless streams."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god led the way, steering through the swell","st":"23.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"a counterfeit voyage with a landsman's chariot, and the unwetted"},{"sp":"N","t":"claw of his water-traveling leopards scored Hydaspes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the armies had a voyage over the waveless river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one rowed a many-bound Indian raft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, steering the wet journey of a boat,","st":"23.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"had a native skiff of linen-stitching fishermen"},{"sp":"N","t":"that he had seized; and another sailed by a counterfeit ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making a self-trunked log like a merchant ship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding the interwoven timbers with a cunning knot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"without a rudder, apart from a sail, without oars,","st":"23.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"not calling upon ship-saving Boreas — for sending"},{"sp":"N","t":"his spear straight down beneath the water into the deep hollows"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spear-wielding sailor sailed with a water-traveling Ares —,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, cutting the swells unwetted upon a floating shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding its strap for a cable, had a shield-brandished journey,","st":"23.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring a strange seafaring with a lying ship."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the host of horsemen traversed the stream, and the horses' voyage"},{"sp":"N","t":"was made with their feet as they lifted their drivers on their backs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and then, as the fair-footed horse swam its wet course"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting upon its loins the rider above it,","st":"23.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"its high-visible neck rose unwetted through the water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the host of battle-rousing foot-soldiers, having need of a boat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a manufactured wind into swollen skins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the Indian Hydaspes with inflated hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the skins sailed pregnant with the winds within.","st":"23.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Parrhasian Pan ran across on goat's feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passing over the surface of the calm river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Lycus steered the course of sea-horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving his father's unwetted four-horse wagon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Scelmis rode across the waveless river","st":"23.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"marching with his brother Damnameneus as he crossed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Another, leaping upon its back, steered with his crook"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wandering bull, companion of the gusts, into the voyage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scored the noiseless water with bovine hooves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the aged Sileni sailed the sea","st":"23.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing Hydaspes with both feet and palms. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And pouring a defending cry like a fountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Hydaspes cried out to his surging brother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a threatening speech from his many-springed throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear brother, how long does your stream creep noiseless?","st":"23.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arm your swells, bearing down upon Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that we may hide the foot-traveler in the waters."},{"sp":"N","t":"It is a shame for you and for me, when the warriors of Bromius"},{"sp":"N","t":"split my swell with unwetted sandals;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeolus, do you too accomplish a favor for me, and arm","st":"23.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"your champions, the stormy winds, against my adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"to fight with the Satyrs, because a wet-traveling host"},{"sp":"N","t":"has made Hydaspes passable with landsman's chariots,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the drivers hold a wet course in the waters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"arm your winds against Lyaeus my ferryman;","st":"23.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the fleet of the Satyrs be dragged in the streams, and as the drivers"},{"sp":"N","t":"are dragged in the outpourings let my flood receive their chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the charioteers covered by a raving swell."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not leave this strange crossing unavenged;"},{"sp":"N","t":"it is a shame for you and for me, when the warriors of Bromius","st":"23.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"make a path for drivers and unwetted wayfarers; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall wipe out the water-traveling lions of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, how was my stream passable, and whence does a water-dipped"},{"sp":"N","t":"Naiad in the outpourings hear a whinnying"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a horse's hoof batter my fishy back?","st":"23.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to mingle with the rivers, because women"},{"sp":"N","t":"tread over us with unwetted sandals."},{"sp":"N","t":"Never did the bold Indians scrape my stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"with their sky-high chariots, and Deriades never scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"his father's water with his towering car,","st":"23.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated on the manes of high-crested elephants.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he armed his stream; and he leapt at Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling his surges as spears; and a great stormy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watery trumpet of the fighting waters bellowed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the river murmured, bringing its water raised on high,","st":"23.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting with the Satyrs; and in the loud-roaring tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"the delicate-tunicked Bassarid shook the cymbals from her hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirled her feet about, and as her sole rowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shook off the yellow bonds of her much-stitched sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the windy stream, carried as far as her head,","st":"23.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"washed over the coiling hair of the swimming Bacchant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, weighed down, laid aside her drenched robes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting her fawnskins to the swollen streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and upon her breast, as the river armed itself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a black mass was dragged against her reddening bosom;","st":"23.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Satyr, rowing the poured water with his palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled his wet tail upright through the water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rowing a wine-tottering step with aged feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maron, carried unsteady in the watery trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"left in the waves a wineskin crammed with sweet wine;","st":"23.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaken thickly, running with the twin-yoked flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Panian pipe sailed through the surface of the water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-coiling in the waves; and with a contending throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hair of shaggy Silenus streamed down his neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the river roared, dragging silt in its swell,","st":"23.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring its yellow migrant water over the plain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling Dionysus to a watery war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the battle-rousing stream, having a counter-blowing breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose cloud-neighboring, showing a drenched air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping down upon Dionysus with a seething swell.","st":"23.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not so did the war-mad water of Simois roar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not such was the stream of battle-rousing Camandrus"},{"sp":"N","t":"deluging Achilles with its wavy flood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Hydaspes then pursued the Bacchic army."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus belched a raving voice at the river:","st":"23.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why do you rout the son of Zeus, Zeus-fallen one? If I wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my father, rainy Zeus, dries up your flood."},{"sp":"N","t":"You sprang from the clouds of my father Cronides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do you pursue the offspring of Zeus the cloud-gatherer?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Beware the shaft of my father's childbed thunderbolt,","st":"23.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he arm the birth-lightning of Bromius against you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"have reverence, lest you be called heavy-kneed, like Asopus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"calm your outpouring, while I still hold back my wrath."},{"sp":"N","t":"Watery, you arm yourself against the fiery; and you cannot"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure one spark of the smoky thunderbolt.","st":"23.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you think greatly of yourself because of Asterie your bride,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[51,52]]},{"sp":"N","t":"who got the blood of the airy race of Hyperion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my father burned with his torch the bold son of Helios,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fiery charioteer, as he rode the heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hyperion, the steward of fire, groaned for his dead child,","st":"23.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"and did not make war on my father for Phaethon's sake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and did not lift fire against fire, though he rules over fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you exalt yourself because of your Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"look at Eridanus struck by the shaft of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your fire-burnt brother; and your watery forefather,","st":"23.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"dread-suffering, girdled about the rim of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring earth-holding water with so many streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"saw his son burnt, and did not make war on Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not strive with his outpourings against the fire-pointed thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"But spare your waters still, lest I see you,","st":"23.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hydaspes, burnt like Eridanus when he was scorched.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, heavy-thundering Hydaspes was enraged the more,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring his high-running water with fiercer waves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he would have hidden the whole rank of the Bacchus-less Bacchoi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Bacchus defended them; and from a neighboring thicket","st":"23.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking a fire-bearing fennel-stalk facing the Dawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"he warmed it at the sun; and from the blazing cluster"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wooden fire was brought to birth by a self-begotten spark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he flung the flame into the outpourings; and at the threatening brand"},{"sp":"N","t":"the banks seethed with the currents of the burning river;","st":"23.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"and much wandering air-roaming smoke whirled about"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the lotus burned and the galingale withered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the fire reduced the rushes to dust; and with a much-whirling rush"},{"sp":"N","t":"the coiling breath of sooty smoke made drunk"},{"sp":"N","t":"the airy arches, and the whole thicket blackened","st":"23.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the reed-beds were lashed by fragrant winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the blaze crept into the deep; and blackened fish"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid themselves in the mud; and from the underwater torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a swimming spark, the drenched silt boiled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kindled though wet; and from the haunts of the deep a smoke,","st":"23.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery, rushed forth running with a watery vapor."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ranks of the water-nymphs, unveiled, with swift sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"naked, wandered away from their wavy hall;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, refusing her father's flaming water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled Naiad, entered the unaccustomed Ganges;","st":"23.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another dwelt in the loud-roaring Indian Acesines"},{"sp":"N","t":"with parched limbs; and Choaspes, neighbor of Persia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received another wandering, mountain-roaming, unveiled"},{"sp":"N","t":"Naiad Nymph, an unsandaled maiden."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Ocean cried out, threatening Dionysus,","st":"23.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a watery bellowing from his many-springed throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sending a stream from his ever-flowing mouths like a fountain"},{"sp":"N","t":"he deluged the shores of the cosmos with a flood of words:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bedfellow of Ocean your age-mate, of one age with the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-nourishing Tethys, self-sown mother of mingled waters,","st":"23.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"ancient child-loving one, what shall we do? For smoky"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus arms himself against me and your children;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Asopus has as his slayer Cronion, the ravishing bastard bird,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the father, and Hydaspes has his son Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But bringing my water against the lightnings of Zeus","st":"23.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall cover the fiery sun with a quenching flood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hide the stars of the upper air; and Cronion shall behold me"},{"sp":"N","t":"deluging Selene with a murmuring flood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beneath the Arctic floor I shall bathe in my outpourings"},{"sp":"N","t":"the topmost heads of the axle and the unwetted trail of the Wagon;","st":"23.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ancient sailor of my deep sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavenly Dolphin, I shall make a sailor again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hidden in the waters; and I shall drag the star-roaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery Eridanus back from heaven, a migrant returning"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the land of the Celts, and shall make him watery,","st":"23.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"stripping the upper air of a watery fire; and my high-pathed"},{"sp":"N","t":"starry Fishes I shall drag back again into the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to swim in the waters after Olympus. Awake, Tethys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let us cover the stars of the upper air with waters, so that I may see"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bull, once the sailor of a waveless sea,","st":"23.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried as a wet wayfarer by fiercer waves"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the bed of Europa; and let her too be stirred,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beholding my horned, bull-faced shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull-natured, horned Selene, the driver of cattle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go high-pathed to heaven, so that I may see","st":"23.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"a drenched Cepheus and a wet-tunicked Boötes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the Earth-shaker did before, when about Corinth"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bold watery Ares shouted into a starry war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall hide the fiery Goat, the nurse of Zeus, and grant"},{"sp":"N","t":"abundant water, fitting, to the water-traveling Water-pourer.","st":"23.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tethys, and you, sea, arm yourselves; for Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"has brought forth a bull-natured bastard son, to destroy"},{"sp":"N","t":"all alike, both rivers and blameless men; for both"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thyrsus has slain the Indians and the torch has burned Hydaspes.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, seething with the swell of his deep-waved voice.","st":"23.320"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXIV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The twenty-fourth holds the unspeakable lament of the Indians and the shuttle of the loom-toiling one and the distaff of Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father turned aside the threat of his angry son,","st":"24.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing the thunderous din of massed clouds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he soothed the anger of boundless Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"checking the flaming battle of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera crashed out a boundless echo through the air,","st":"24.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back the wrath of fire-strong Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And stretching out a wet palm to pitying Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Hydaspes cried to the fiery son of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing a suppliant word from his throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Spare for me, Dionysus, a Zeus-fallen river,","st":"24.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing a favor to fruit-bearing waters; for your"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-clustered harvest sprang up from waters."},{"sp":"N","t":"I was witless, fire-reared Dionysus; for the sparkle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your torches proclaimed your heavenly birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"But longing for my children forced me; for bringing","st":"24.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"faithful things to my son Deriades I whirled the threat of my surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling a helping swell for the Indians as they were slain."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to appear to my begetter, because I drag to the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"streams mingled with murmuring blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"and defile Poseidon with a murderous drop;","st":"24.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"this, this it was that armed me to strive with Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But by your father of strangers and of suppliants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"have respect for Hydaspes, seething hot with your fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"The Naiads flee my stream; and about the springs"},{"sp":"N","t":"one dwells in a wet house, another in the thickets,","st":"24.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"companion of the Hadryads, changes to a plant after the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another as a migrant holds the Indus, and another, fleeing"},{"sp":"N","t":"with dusty feet, has entered the thirsty Caucasian"},{"sp":"N","t":"rock, and another, darting off to Choaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwells in strange streams and no longer in her father's water.","st":"24.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not destroy the reeds, the growth of my currents,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on which, as they grow, the vine shoot of the grape"},{"sp":"N","t":"is propped; for the canes, bound to one another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift up your well-watered harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not burn the reeds, whence come your Mygdonian flutes,","st":"24.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest your song-loving Athena ever blame you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, from the grim likeness of the Gorgons' heads"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they cried out, invented the Libyan pattern of yoked flutes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and have respect for the steerer of your mystic song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the concordant melody of the Panian pipe;","st":"24.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"cease withering the flood of the river with your fennel-stalk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the flood of the river increases your fennel-stalks."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have crossed no strange swell of the same name; for I brightened"},{"sp":"N","t":"in my baths another Dionysus of a different nature,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who bore the name of the younger Bromius, when Cronion","st":"24.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusted Zagreus to my child-tending Nymphs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you bear the whole body of Zagreus; but do you"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant him a late-accomplished favor, since from him you come;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you rose from the heart of the first-born, the Dionysus of song."},{"sp":"N","t":"And honor the child-rearing water of your Lamus;","st":"24.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"remember Maeonia your homeland; for Hydaspes"},{"sp":"N","t":"is the brother of your graceful Pactolus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And do you, holding out now a single favor to so many rivers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"our kinsmen, rein back your radiance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not burn the pouring of waters with fire; for from waters","st":"24.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lightning sprang, the rainy fire of your Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But soothe your anger, since I fall at your knees,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading a gentle, suppliant stream; for in wars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Typhoeus had bent his bold neck and been mild,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then, casting away the recoverable mass of his threat,","st":"24.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"your father, high-ruling Zeus, would have checked his lightning.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, Dionysus reined back his pine-torch."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Arctic wind fanned up the outpourings"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a wintry lash, bringing a rough breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling the flood of the fire-struck river,","st":"24.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"honoring the sun and Bacchus and Zeus together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and quenched the unquenchable divine fire of the surges."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Bacchus still sailed the watery Hydaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile, having the courage of Ares, Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed the Indians for the battle named after him with a towering rush,","st":"24.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting his ranks about the stream, so that his warriors"},{"sp":"N","t":"might hold back the host of the Bacchoi as they still came up."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did it escape the all-seeing eye of Zeus; and swiftly"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was carried from heaven, shielding Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And going as helpers to their own, one after another,","st":"24.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the gods who dwell in Olympus came with Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a flying leap; and for the sake of the bed of Aegina"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus was lifted a second time as a high-flying eagle"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the flood of Asopus, and gripping air-roaming Aeacus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sparing talons in a ravishing claw","st":"24.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted and brought him to the war of Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the Indian floor; and from the broad-pathed gulf"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo his father saved his son Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shining, carried on a chariot of evil-averting swans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the memory of the chamber of lion-slaying Cyrene;","st":"24.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and long-winged Hermes, prevailing, snatched his own child,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Penelope, horn-drawing, long-haired Pan;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Urania brought Hymenaeus back from destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"named for her own generative son, and scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"the airy paths, like the trail of a star,","st":"24.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to her clustered brother Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Calliope lifted Oeagrus upon her shoulders;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hephaestus cared for his own children the Cabeiri,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and snatched them both, like a sharp torch;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Actaean Pallas Athena saved Erechtheus,","st":"24.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indian-slayer, the dweller of god-founded Athens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and all the dwellers of Olympus who cared for their dear oaks"},{"sp":"N","t":"took the Hadryad nymphs alive, and beyond the others"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo of the laurel, appearing, saved the laurel nymphs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with him his mother, his companion, helped them,","st":"24.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leto, still honoring the trees of her childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanx of the Bassarids and the cluster-bearing women"},{"sp":"N","t":"were saved from the deep, loud-roaring tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the daughters of Cydnus, the Zephyrus-loving river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who knew the floating, wet course, whom for the victory","st":"24.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Indian war the father gave as a gift to Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Naiads skilled in battle, whom once Cilician Typhoeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"taught fighting when he warred against Cronion."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the army followed as companions; and Euius"},{"sp":"N","t":"outstripped them all as they hurried, upon his mountain-haunting car","st":"24.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"scraping the stream with the trail of his unwetted axle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had the Satyrs running with him, and with them the Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"traveling the water, and the Pans in their company; and beyond the others","hvShift6":[[0,0],[34,44],[44,50]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the swifter Telchines upon sea-reared horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the drivers of their father's sea-founded wagon,","st":"24.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"accompanied hurrying Dionysus into the course."},{"sp":"N","t":"And others were behind, and rushed on in the crossing"},{"sp":"N","t":"coming up from another, unseen road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the god escorted them; since, shaking his wing gently,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an eagle led the way through the mountain, Zeus in answering shape,","st":"24.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing his son aloft with sparing talons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus carried high on an airy road."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they danced, skipping upon the Indian cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hunted the crags, and lodged in the thickets,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pitching their huts in the gently shaded wood &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"24.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"some held the fawn-driving hunt of long-horned deer"},{"sp":"N","t":"together with their hounds; and the water-nymphs of shoot-loving"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus mingled with the Hamadryad Nymphs."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the ranks of the Bassarids beside the Erythraean thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"nursed the cub of a mountain-haunting lioness,","st":"24.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their breasts gushed the moisture of self-poured milk;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, pursuing a longing for a viperish cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sought through the mountain the caves of venom-shooting serpents,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and showed her hunting skill; and with javelin-hurling thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"one struck a storm-footed fawn; and another, unseen,","st":"24.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran down a raging bear with a raving leap;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another gripped the spine of dark-skinned beasts"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mounted the crest of mountain-ranging elephants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one of the archers fitted a shaft to the circling string"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shot at an elm; another had an olive as his mark;","st":"24.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another struck a fir; and much whistling of arrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent at a neighboring pine hissed in the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"For them the mountain-crashing revel roared. But in grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thureus came to king Deriades as an ill messenger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the slaughter of the Indians with speechless tears,","st":"24.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely raised a mourning voice from his lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Scepter-bearing Deriades, god-born shoot of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"we went, as you commanded, to the cliff opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"we found a lonely thicket near in the glens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there we set an ambush and waited, until thyrsus-mad","st":"24.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus should come; and as Bacchus came on"},{"sp":"N","t":"a flute crashed out, and as the untanned ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"was beaten on both sides there was a bronze-beaten echo"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the clang of a pipe; and the whole thicket whirled"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the oaks cried out and the hills danced;","st":"24.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Naiads shrieked. And I armed my warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reluctant, trembling, dragging the disobedient into the fray."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, as they call him, shaking his whetted thyrsi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting at the Indian race with paltry leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"killed on the plain an unspeakable host struck by the sharp","st":"24.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsus, and in the surges he destroyed the remnant of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"But let us ask the wise Brahmans, so that you may learn"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether this one has come to us as a god or a mortal man."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not raise a profitless nightly war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not destroy your army in a lightless fighting;","st":"24.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"already a misty gloom is stretched out; and appearing near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the star Hesperus glitters, drawing back the strife."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if longing for a hard-to-face tumult pursues you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restrain the Indian today, and tomorrow you draw them into battle.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he persuaded disobedient Deriades,","st":"24.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"obedient not for feebleness, but blaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon as he sank, and not yielding to Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And moving the Indian phalanx from the river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"overbearing Deriades withdrew in a mourning rage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated on the crests of his returning elephants.","st":"24.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indians this way and that with their towering king"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushed in flight to the city, within the towers"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing of the victory of war-mad Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already a groaning rumor flew through the city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the kindred slaughter of the newly slain Indians.","st":"24.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lament was unspeakable; and the circles of the faces"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the lamentation-loving women were scored with mourning nails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their tunics were split at the middle of the breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"baring their bosoms, and with alternating rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rim of their beaten breasts reddened,","st":"24.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"blood-dipped, beneath their palms. And a grey old man on the threshold of age"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut his snowy lock with downcast iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing that his four sons in their prime had perished,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Aeacus subdued with a single frightful knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slain pitiably; and of the heavy-enduring women","st":"24.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"one groaned for her brother, another for her father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another bride, weeping in many ways, lamented"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bridegroom fresh from the dance, like Protesilaus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another a Laodameia; and the cluster of the hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the newly yoked bride was torn, unplaited and unveiled.","st":"24.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, helpless, the wife of a fallen Indian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having filled out the near birth-pangs of her labor"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing the childbed circles of the tenth Moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"groaned with many tears over the watery destruction of her husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in anger at the river broke out in a wailing voice:","st":"24.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Never shall I drink my father's bitter Hydaspes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer shall I pass those streams, no longer, wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall I touch the river that has hidden your corpse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no, by you and by your burden, which I bear within my belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no, by you and by that love which time knows not how to wither.","st":"24.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who will take me and carry me to where my husband fell dead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may embrace the drenched corpse, so that the wave"},{"sp":"N","t":"may hide even me together with my water-traveling husband?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that I had borne a son and reared him; but now, wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swelling of my belly holds me with the birth ripening.","st":"24.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if ever I bear a child and he asks for his father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how shall I show a father to my son as he babbles?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, lamenting to the husband who did not hear."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another groaned for an unwedded wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"as her suitor perished, whom the fair-marrying hour did not see","st":"24.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered with a bridal wreath, nor in the chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the sweet-singing, life-saving flute of the Loves sing."},{"sp":"N","t":"For them in their grief there was lamentation. But about the thickets"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus set a banquet for his Satyrs and his Indian-slaying"},{"sp":"N","t":"warriors; and bulls were carved,","st":"24.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"and heifers in rows were cut up with the knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck with axes, and from the Erythraean flock"},{"sp":"N","t":"thick droves of spear-won sheep were sacrificed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seated in throngs at the well-rounded table"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sileni and Satyrs with fair-thyrsus Dionysus","st":"24.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched a single food with wide-scattered hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unspeakable wine was drunk in turn; and the wine-pourers"},{"sp":"N","t":"emptied boundless fragrant jars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing the blameless cluster of the nectarous harvest."},{"sp":"N","t":"And for them as they delighted beside the mixing-bowl,","st":"24.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leucus of Lesbos, self-taught, wove a song, singing shrill"},{"sp":"N","t":"how the earlier Titans armed themselves against Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he sang the true victory of high-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how he sealed broad-bearded Cronus, crouching beneath the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the gloomy Tartarean bosom,","st":"24.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed in vain with the watery weapons of winter."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Lapethus, the unarmed citizen of the Cyprian field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sat beside the intelligent harper, and held out to him"},{"sp":"N","t":"a rich portion of food, and asked him to sing"},{"sp":"N","t":"that delightful tale, the care of never-silent Athens,","st":"24.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"how loom-toiling Cytherea strove with Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he, harping, struck up to sing of Cypris,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how once, having the sting of distaff-loving care,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she went after the loom of Athena with untried hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a shuttle and no longer the cestus of the Loves.","st":"24.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Paphian's thread was stretched thick, like some long,"},{"sp":"N","t":"well-twisted rope of osier, which with some craft"},{"sp":"N","t":"an old shipwright has stitched in long trails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fencing in the gaping newly finished timbers of ships;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she all day and all night beside the loom","st":"24.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the work of Pallas, undone again, and wore her untried"},{"sp":"N","t":"hands with an alien, unaccustomed toil;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and combing the tunic with a many-toothed comb"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hanging a dancing weight in the middle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she wove the robe with the shuttle, and Cypris became Athena;","st":"24.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the labor was unlaughing; and as the robe was woven"},{"sp":"N","t":"the broad-stretched monstrous thread swelled; and of their own accord"},{"sp":"N","t":"the warp-threads broke as the tunic thickened;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she had for her twofold toils as witnesses of her craft"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun and the lamp and necessary Selene.","st":"24.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"The handmaids of the Paphian, the dancers of Orchomenus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"danced no dance; but with a running rush"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pasithea whirled the spindle, and Persuasion was the wool-dresser,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Aglaia gave thread and yarn to her mistress."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the life of mortal marriages wandered; and old Aeon,","st":"24.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"the charioteer of life, shaken, groaned for the useless"},{"sp":"N","t":"harmony of unwedded wedlock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unhonored Eros unstrung his flaming bowstring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the unbegotten, unplowed furrow of the cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then there was no lovely din of harps, then no pipe,","st":"24.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"no shrill flute sang, crying 'Hymen, Hymenaeus';"},{"sp":"N","t":"but as life dwindled and the race was lashed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bolts of the unloosable yoking were wrenched away."},{"sp":"N","t":"And toil-enduring Athena saw the Paphian toil-loving,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and had anger mingled with laughter, when she saw the long,","st":"24.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"rough rope of Cytherea untried in labor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she announced it to the immortals; and with heavy-jealous purpose"},{"sp":"N","t":"she spoke, blaming both Cypris and her father:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Your gift shifts about, heavenly Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do I hold the gift of the Fates; for your daughter","st":"24.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aphrodite, become a loom-worker, has plundered my portion."},{"sp":"N","t":"The portion of Athena was not snatched by queen Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sister and consort of my Zeus, but the steward"},{"sp":"N","t":"of bedchambers, a soft goddess, afflicts the Spoil-winner"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed with shields from her birth. And when did your","st":"24.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"unwarlike Cytherea fight in the front for Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or what Titans did she destroy with her womanish cestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that she should force me after wars? And do you yourself"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me, archer-goddess, when from the midst of your wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"did you see Athena shooting or hunting?","st":"24.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who calls upon the grey-eyed one when women are in labor?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke the gods who dwell in Olympus gathered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wishing to see Aphrodite plying the loom."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the labors of battle-untried Cytherea"},{"sp":"N","t":"in wonder they circled the goddess's bastard work;","st":"24.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laughing, mocking Hermes spoke again:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You have a loom, Cytherea; leave your cestus to Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you handle thread, if you stretch a shuttle in your hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then lift also the furious spear and the aegis of Tritogeneia."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know, Cytherea, why you weave the much-clattering loom;","st":"24.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"your guile has not escaped me; perhaps your bridegroom Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"demands lovely tunics of you for the wedding."},{"sp":"N","t":"Weave a robe for Ares; but on the newly spun robe"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not embroider a shield; for what have shields to do with Aphrodite?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Make Phaethon the light-bearing witness of your bed,","st":"24.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the thievish plunderer of your couch;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you wish, embroider your ancient bonds too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let a shamefaced hand fashion the god your counterfeit husband;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you too, Eros, whirling your spindle after your bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"make yarn for your distaff-loving mother Cytherea,","st":"24.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that after 'winged' I may call you 'loom-toiling' as well,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the ox-gut strings may see the fiery god"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing out the bobbin past the thread instead of shafts."},{"sp":"N","t":"Make Ares in gold beside golden Aphrodite"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a shuttle in his hand and not brandishing an ox-hide shield,","st":"24.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"embroidering a double cloak with work-toiling Cytherea."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, goddess Cytherea, cast the threads to the winds"},{"sp":"N","t":"from your distaff-loving hands and tend the cestus strap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and care for wedlock a second time; for the first-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"cosmos still wanders, while you still weave a robe.\"","st":"24.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, all the dwellers of Olympus smiled."},{"sp":"N","t":"And casting away the half-finished thread of the tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in shame before the grey-eyed one, Cytherea, the nurse"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the human race, set foot upon her own Cyprus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Eros fitted the shifting-shaped life together again with his cestus,","st":"24.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"sowing the childbed rim of the well-plowed cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a lovely-voiced song Leucus wove,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hymning Aphrodite untaught of the distaff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who raised a great quarrel against work-toiling Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when there was surfeit of the wine-loving table,","st":"24.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing up wine they fell upon a lonely bed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"some upon a dappled fawnskin, others upon leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"spread out, and others above the loose dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"strewed a couch of goatskins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others kept company with battle-rousing dreams,","st":"24.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching their brazen bodies in a warlike sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one struck an Indian seated high on a horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another pierced an Indian at the neck, and another, cutting down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck a footman with his sword, and another wounded Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, sending his air-roaming shaft on high,","st":"24.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck sky-high elephants with a dream arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the race of leopards and the wild tribes of lions"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hunting hounds of wilderness-dwelling Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept a sleepless eye in alternating watch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"waking all night within the mountain wood,","st":"24.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the war of the darkening Indians rush upon them;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and torches flashed in rows toward Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lamps of the unsleeping Bacchic dance."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the twenty-fifth you have the contest of Perseus and the judgment of Heracles against the manhood of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Muse, make war again in a wise battle with an intelligent thyrsus;","st":"25.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"for not yet, bending a slave's knee to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has the eastern Ares lulled the seven-year war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, astonished at the tip of a serpent's jaw,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the nestlings of the Indian plane-tree cry out again,","st":"25.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"the prophets of the Bacchic war. But I shall not sing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first six years, when the Indian host was within"},{"sp":"N","t":"its towers; but having completed a pattern in imitation of Homer"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall hymn the last year of the wars, and shall score"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fighting equal in number to my seventh sparrow;","st":"25.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall mix my song for seven-gated Thebes, because she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"runs about me in Bacchic frenzy, and like a bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"has bared her breast above a downcast robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembering Pentheus; and urging me to sing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Cithaeron stretched out his mourning hand,","st":"25.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"in shame, lest I cry aloud of the lawless bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"or of a father-slaying husband lying beside the mother who bore him."},{"sp":"N","t":"I hear the din of the Aonian lyre; tell me, Muses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what Amphion again draws breathless stone into a course?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence this din is; for as Thebes is sung","st":"25.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dorian echo of the Pindaric harp has crashed out."},{"sp":"N","t":"But let us kill again the race of the Erythraean Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the age never beheld another war like it"},{"sp":"N","t":"before the eastern war, nor after the Indian fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"did it see another, late-accomplished, of equal weight,","st":"25.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did so great a host come to Ilion, nor so great"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fleet of men; but striving with the new and the first-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall raise up the well-toiled sweats of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"judging the manhood of the children of Zeus, so that I may see"},{"sp":"N","t":"who labored at such a contest, who was the equal of Bacchus.","st":"25.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perseus, swift-kneed, whirling a well-winged step,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a cloud-neighboring course in the air, a wayfarer on foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if truly he flew. But what more, if, shaking his ankles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he swam with a windy sole in a strange rowing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, with the robbing wrist of a cupped palm,","st":"25.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking the wandering eye of the sleepless daughter of Phorcys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"guarding the noiseless leap of his high-treading sandals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he mowed the viperish swath of one Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose belly, still swelling, was pressed with offspring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in travail with Pegasus, and the sickle, a childbirth-helping","st":"25.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eileithyia, reaped the pregnant neck of the Gorgon lady,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first-fruits of a horse-bearing throat? And swift-sandaled"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perseus lifted the tokens of an unwarlike victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"breathless, the snaky cornfields of the Gorgon's hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the remains of a head drenched with a bloody drop,","st":"25.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-finished, hissing thinly from freshly cut throats;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he did not march in a male battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was then no din of a land war, nor on the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Ares the sailor belly out the warlike sails of ships"},{"sp":"N","t":"with battle-rousing winds for Perseus as he fought,","st":"25.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did Libyan Nereus redden with a murderous drop,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did deadly water receive a self-rolling corpse;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but trembling at the hissing of the snaky hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"of raving Stheno, winged Perseus whirled about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bearing the helmet of Hades and the sickle of Pallas,","st":"25.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"and holding the wing of Hermes and Zeus as his father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was lifted, a fugitive, on a swifter sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the bellowing of Euryale and not a trumpet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having plundered a little cave of Libya; he did not kill"},{"sp":"N","t":"an army of men, he did not turn a city to ash with a flaming brand.","st":"25.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"But such was not the war of Bromius; not creeping on foot"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Bacchus arm himself, a guileful champion, nor, lying in wait"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the migrant circle of the unsleeping guardian eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the daughter of Phorcys as she shifted the wing of Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did he accomplish a womanish contest against uncorseleted Medusa;","st":"25.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"but splitting apart the ranks of the foe with a twofold victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a land war and a water-traveling tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wetted the earth with gore, and mixing the wave with blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reddened the Nereids with a reddening stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing barbarian tribes; and many a headless cornfield","st":"25.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"of high-crested Indians was entombed upon mother Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many, perished in the seas by the sharp thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were ferried over the sea as self-moving sailors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dead throng of Indians. And for unconquered Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I pass over the wavy war of the battle-rousing river","st":"25.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"that hurled its waters as spears, when the smoky"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchic pine-torch burned the barbarian water"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a dripping spark, and watery Hydaspes boiled"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a hot wave, gushing up a river smoke."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say that Perseus killed a sea-reared monster;","st":"25.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the Gorgon eye he turned the beast of the sea to stone."},{"sp":"N","t":"What more, if Polydectes, having gazed on the eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"of murderous Medusa, changed the strange-natured form"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his human limbs and was shaped into self-perfected stone?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But the mighty labor of Indian-slaying Bacchus was no single Gorgon,","st":"25.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"no air-roaming, sea-beaten stone, nor a Polydectes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Bacchus, playing the champion, mowed the straw"},{"sp":"N","t":"of serpent-haired Giants with a little man-breaking thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, hurling warlike ivy at Porphyrion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he dashed down Enceladus and drove off Alcyoneus,","st":"25.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling leaves as spears; and thyrsi were shot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyers of the earth-born, the helpers of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the coiling people of the Field, with two hundred hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the starry rim with their many-necked heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent the knee before a slender javelin-cluster,","st":"25.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"an ivy spear; and not by the fiery thunderbolt"},{"sp":"N","t":"did so great a swarm fall as by the man-breaking thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, friends, let us judge: in the eastern field"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun marveled, watching the Indian-slaying sweats"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Dionysus, while over the western gulf","st":"25.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Hesperian Moon saw long-winged Perseus"},{"sp":"N","t":"contending at a small labor with crook-clawed bronze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and by as much a greater boast as Phaethon has obtained than Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by so much shall I declare Bacchus better than Perseus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Inachus was witness of them both, when with ivy","st":"25.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a murderous fennel-stalk the Mycenaean spears strove,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze-heavy; and fleeing the Euian-loving Ares of the Satyrs"},{"sp":"N","t":"sickle-bearing Perseus gave way to thyrsus-bearing Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he sent his furious spear, instead of warlike Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling it at paltry, ironless Ariadne;","st":"25.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not admire Perseus for killing one woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still breathing of the Loves in her bridal garments."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if he exalts himself for the sake of the golden bed of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus did not bring Danae to heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"honoring the maiden-loving rain of a generative dew,","st":"25.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"the little stolen wedding; but Semele mounted Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Zeus, touching a single table with the blessed ones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated beside her clustered son Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Danae got no Olympian house, but, being within"},{"sp":"N","t":"a water-traveling chest, the bride of Zeus sailed,","st":"25.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming the deceitful rain of the yoking Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which had the unsteady wealth of a short-lived snowfall."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know of Andromeda, that she appears within Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but she labors again even in the upper air; and perhaps, wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she has often spoken such a word with an indignant voice:","st":"25.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"'What more, if you brought me to the upper air, bridegroom Perseus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"A fine Olympian bride-gift you gave me; for the starry"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sea-Monster still routs me here too, and another, new one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the counterpart of the former; after the earth and the terror of the brine"},{"sp":"N","t":"I still have a bond even among the stars; your heavenly","st":"25.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"sickle did not save me; and in vain within Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gentle eye of a starry Medusa glitters for me;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sea-Monster still routs me, and you stretch no light wings."},{"sp":"N","t":"My grieving mother forces me, because wretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cassiepeia herself sinks through the upper air into the sea","st":"25.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling at the Nereids, and counts blessed the course of the Bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unwetted by Ocean and not touching the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing the terror of Andromeda and the Sea-Monster of Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Cepheus laments here after the earth.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word the heavy-bound bride often cried,","st":"25.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling on Perseus, and her husband did not help her."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if Perseus exults in the stars of Andromeda,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretch a sidelong eye through the upper air, where"},{"sp":"N","t":"gleaming Ophiuchus shines, lifting the coiled Serpent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall behold the circling Crown of Ariadne,","st":"25.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"running with the Sun, rising together with the Moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the desire of garland-loving Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know the war of Minos, which a womanish Enyo granted him"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a cestus and not the strap of an ox-hide shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Cypris was helmet-glancing, when Persuasion","st":"25.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandished a bronze spear and became Pallas Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the companion of Minos as he fought, and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-adorning swarm of unwarlike Loves shot arrows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lovely city-sacking Desire, when for the Nisaean"},{"sp":"N","t":"Megarian people the Cydonian trumpet roared,","st":"25.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, seeing Terror and Panic as the fellow-contenders of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brazen Ares withdrew with shamefaced steps,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching Aphrodite lifting a shield"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Desire hurling a spear, and delicate-tunicked Eros"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplished for a well-corseleted warrior a victory of fair hair;","st":"25.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Scylla sheared from her sleeping, unshorn father"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cluster of his purple hair, his age-mate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sacked a whole city with one cutting iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having mowed a lock from the city-holding head."},{"sp":"N","t":"Minos the city-sacker once found the end of the fighting","st":"25.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"by his own naked beauty, and conquered not by iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but by longing and love; but as Lyaeus armed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no Desire soothed the war of the javelin-bearing Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no Paphian armed herself fighting beside Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquering by beauty; no single girl, gadfly-mad,","st":"25.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"in love with Dionysus, brought him the end of the battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no lovely guile, no locks of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the strange-natured Indian of the wide-scattered wars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the boast of a victory ever renewed. And if you honor"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Inachus of Heracles, I myself shall test his whole labor.","st":"25.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know that, winding his forearm aslant about a lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he embraced its neck entwined in a well-handled bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing an ironless doom, where through the life-sustaining throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the breathing air is ferried through the middle of the windpipe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not admire this either; once beside a fair-leaved thicket","st":"25.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Cyrene, playing the champion with lion-slaying hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplished a like deed, since she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"subdued a male beast with an unbending womanish grip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Bacchus, still a boy fresh in bloom, playing in the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gripping a shaggy chin with one hand,","st":"25.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged a blood-red lion, and held it out to his mother Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"squeezing the gaping beast by the lock of its neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he dragged it still living, and binding it tight with harness"},{"sp":"N","t":"he clamped the beast with a steering bit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoking its slavish jaws, and, sitting high upon his car,","st":"25.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the wild backs of the frightened lions."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the race of leopards and the tribe of raw-devouring bears"},{"sp":"N","t":"were enslaved by the infant palms of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know too of the mountain-running Arcadian boar; but for Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his boyhood boars and tribes of lions were playthings.","st":"25.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"What more did bold Heracles accomplish, if with much labor"},{"sp":"N","t":"he loosed one little snaky spring, Lerna,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the self-perfected first-fruits of the lair-haunting hydra,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a many-necked planting sprouting up with serpents?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that he had killed it alone, and had not, laboring, called","st":"25.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iolaus as the crusher of the newly grown heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a light-bearing brand, until the two together"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashed down the female serpent; but I do not know how to honor"},{"sp":"N","t":"two men striving against one paltry viper;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a single labor was divided between them both; but the thyrsus-bearer","st":"25.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"Euius alone, cutting off the snaky sons of the Field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the champion of Zeus, assailed them all, over whose shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"abundant hydras flowed in turn on either side,"},{"sp":"N","t":"far greater than the Inachian hydra, and instead of Lerna"},{"sp":"N","t":"they hissed restlessly in the upper air, neighbors of the stars.","st":"25.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious, Iolaus; for you burned the body of the hydra,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Heracles alone, alone snatched the name of victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"My champion brought back to life no little Nemea, no Lerna"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, from many-roaring throats,","hvShift5":[[0,9]]},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the viperish bush of the ever-renewed hydra,","st":"25.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"but bringing Notus and the soles of Boreas and the wing of Eurus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zephyrus as herald, with a four-yoked victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"he filled Ocean, earth and sea with his contests."},{"sp":"N","t":"If a serpent brings glory to a man, if lair-haunting hydras,"},{"sp":"N","t":"these are the childbed garlands of Bacchus, these the dread","st":"25.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"snaky bonds of the serpent locks of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the time when he left the birth-perfecting fold of his father's thigh."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be silent about the golden horn of the doe; I shall not much reproach"},{"sp":"N","t":"so great a Heracles as the slayer of one deer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not recall a trembling deer; for to a fawn-slaying","st":"25.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving woman the fawn-driving hunt is a small plaything."},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave the Cnossian labor of Heracles; for I do not admire"},{"sp":"N","t":"some gadfly-mad bull which he drove off, because, shaking"},{"sp":"N","t":"so great a club, he cut off a little horn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often one woman has done this, often a Bacchant","st":"25.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"has carved up an unspeakable herd of well-horned bulls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a paltry handmaid of ox-horned Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, dragging up the whetted arching horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if a maddened ox fought with its horns,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bent the bull to its knee, though it had hurled lions aside.","st":"25.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave too the heads of three-crested Geryon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for my Dionysus with his flesh-cutting ivy"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed Alpos, the god-fighting son of the Field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Alpos tressed with a hundred viperish heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching the sun and dragging down the Moon,","st":"25.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing the starry dance with his locks."},{"sp":"N","t":"The contests of Heracles, whom immortal Zeus plowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the three-moon child-sowing bed of Alcmene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were a paltry mountain-reared labor; but the works of Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"were either a many-armed Giant or the champion of high-crested Indians;","st":"25.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"no doe, no ranks of a herd of cattle, no shaggy boar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no hound, no bull, no self-trunked gold-shining harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no dung, no restless wandering bird"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a paltry, ironless winged point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no stranger-slaying horse's jaw, no little girdle","st":"25.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Hippolyte; but the victory of Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was measureless Deriades or twenty-cubit Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"All-shining son of Meles, imperishable herald of Achaea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be gracious — your book is of one age with the dawn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not recall the Trojan fighting; for I do not liken","st":"25.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus to the son of Aeacus or Deriades to Hector."},{"sp":"N","t":"Your Muse ought to have hymned so great and such a contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Bacchus the hurler of spears against Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and left the labors of Achilles to other hymn-tenders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Thetis snatched this prize. But breathe upon me","st":"25.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"your god-sped breath to sing shrill; for I lack"},{"sp":"N","t":"your fair speech, since, singing so great a war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I dim the Indian-slaying sweats of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, goddess, bring me a second time into the midst of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a breathing spear and the shield of father Homer,","st":"25.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting against Morrheus and witless Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed together with Zeus and Bromius; and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me hear the host-gathering sound of the Bacchic pipe"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unceasing din of the wise trumpet of Homer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may kill the remnant of the Indians with an intelligent spear.","st":"25.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"So the Bacchic throng sat about the well-pastured ridge","hvShift5":[[0,0],[16,2],[26,18],[28,21]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Indian wood, citizens of a lonely cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a respite from the war; and Ganges whirled in fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitying his own children; and at the doom of the newly slain"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole city was shaken; and the streets crashed","st":"25.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the mourning beating of lamentation-loving women."},{"sp":"N","t":"And fear and wonder and shame whirled Deriades about;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already he had heard everything; and still more he grieved,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching with sidelong eye, because, changing its divine form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"black Hydaspes murmured with a wavy wine.","st":"25.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"There too a broad-bearded, ill-fated old man, whirling his sluggish foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a misty blindness of mist,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkled the yellow dew of toil-releasing drunkenness"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon his glued eyes; and as his face drew up"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wine-dark drops his eyes were opened;","st":"25.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with delighted feet the old man danced, singing shrill"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the reddening moisture of the evil-averting river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drawing the stream like a cloud with his aged hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"he filled fragrant skins with the purple drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kindled an altar to Zeus and to wine-pouring Dionysus,","st":"25.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"having beheld the late radiance of an unaccustomed Phaethon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a youth, leaving on the shore his drunken hounds"},{"sp":"N","t":"lapping the delicious water of the reddening river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hunter, companion of the mountain archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent his step toward the city, announcing to disobedient","st":"25.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades the sweet flood of the wine-tottering river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already a vine fragrance ran through the city"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made all the streets drunk with warm winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesying the victory of Indian-slaying Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the citizens lodged in the sky-high towers","st":"25.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"in fear, and the guardians of the high-crested city"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled the wall with ox-hide shields. And among the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus in distress often blamed Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, grudging again, she had reined back the war of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the winds fanned up all his hope of victory.","st":"25.305","hvShift5":[[3,1],[4,3],[15,9],[26,11],[27,13],[32,18],[35,33],[37,43],[45,45]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And gazing at his lions beside an idle manger,","hvShift5":[[1,5],[3,7],[8,25],[10,26],[14,28],[18,29],[31,31],[33,32],[40,37],[48,41]]},{"sp":"N","t":"when the returning Moon had filled out ten circles,","hvShift5":[[1,3],[3,4],[9,15],[11,26],[13,27],[18,32],[21,36],[23,38],[25,47],[30,49]]},{"sp":"N","t":"having measured the thirtieth dawn of the fighting;","hvShift5":[[5,1],[7,3],[11,9],[12,16],[13,19],[14,21],[17,29],[20,35],[23,37],[29,40],[34,43],[41,48]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he roared like a lion and groaned within the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"with tearless eyes; and to downcast Bacchus","st":"25.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"trailing-robed, unsown Attis came through the Scythian mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing his migrant chariot of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swift messenger of divine Rhea, who once, reddening"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bronze the generative corn of a marriage-perfecting youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cast away the loving swath of unwedded plowshares,","st":"25.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first-fruits of a male harvest, and sprinkling"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fold of his thigh with a child-begetting bloody drop"},{"sp":"N","t":"made his hot body womanish with the crushing iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he then, driving the chariot of the Cybelian goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came as a messenger to comfort Lyaeus in his distress;","st":"25.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing him Dionysus ran up, lest he come near"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing all-subduing Rhea to the war of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And halting his wild chariot, stretching the reins along the rail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and showing the unscored tips of a rosy chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spoke a word to Bacchus, pouring a shrill cry:","st":"25.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Vine Dionysus, child of Zeus, grandson of Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me as I ask, when will you come home to the land of the Lydians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having wiped out the woolly-headed race of the Indians?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet has Rhea beheld dark-skinned captives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet after the battle has she wiped the sweat of your","st":"25.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"Mygdonian lions at a mountain-haunting manger"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the wealth-flowing stream of Pactolus; but you roll"},{"sp":"N","t":"the noiseless whirl of ever-flowing years of war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet have you brought to the beast-tending mother of gods"},{"sp":"N","t":"as tokens of victory the eastern tribes of Indian lions.","st":"25.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"But from Hephaestus and from your immortal Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive this armor, which the Lemnian anvil wrought,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the sea together with the earth and the upper air and the chorus of stars.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had his speech ended, and proud Bacchus cried:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"The gods are cruel, jealous; for in the wars","st":"25.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"I could wipe out the city of the Indians in a single dawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"with my ivy spear; but the envy of my stepmother Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"leads me astray from victory against my will."},{"sp":"N","t":"Openly wild Ares stands as champion for Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting against the Satyrs; and I have often longed","st":"25.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"to wound him with my thyrsus; but Cronion, threatening"},{"sp":"N","t":"with thunderous crashes, has reined back my rush."},{"sp":"N","t":"But may heavenly Zeus lull today the din"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his heavy-crashing clouds, and tomorrow I shall bind Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until I cut through the well-helmeted corn of the Indians.\"","st":"25.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lydian Attis answered Dionysus as he so spoke:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Brandishing the unwoundable starry shield of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dear friend, would you not fear the wrath of Ares, the envy of Hera?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not the whole rank of the blessed ones, having all-mother Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not an army of the curved bow — only take care, in hurling spears,","st":"25.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"not to strike the sun or wound Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"Who would spoil the sword of Orion with a knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or shoot at Boötes with earthly shafts?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will speak of the horn-drawing father of Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what could Hydaspes do to you, who bear Ocean?","st":"25.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"Make war with courage a second time, because my Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"has prophesied a late-accomplished victory of the tumult;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there shall be no end of the war before the four-yoked"},{"sp":"N","t":"Seasons fill out the sixth year of the fighting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for so the eye of Zeus and the threads of unturning Fate","st":"25.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusted it to the nods of Hera; and in the seventh"},{"sp":"N","t":"year to come you shall shatter the city of the Indians.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he gave the shield to Bromius; and delighting his heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"with wine-loving cups of toil-releasing wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"he touched the banquet; and having pleased his spirit","st":"25.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the table he turned back and lashed the backs of his lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shepherding his mountain-running chariot home to Phrygia."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he drove beside the headlands of the Caucasian haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and passed the peaks of the Assyrians and the hard-to-tread mountains of the Bactrians"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the crags of Lebanon and the ridges of Taurus,","st":"25.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"until he set foot on the land of Maeonia; and he entered"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-fixed, divine court of Rhea, mother of a mighty child;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he unclamped his raw-devouring lions from the harness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bound them near the manger and gave them ambrosial fodder."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Dionysus, thyrsus-mad, having learned the inspired oracle","st":"25.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his mother, mingled with the mountain Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving to the winds the mass of his downcast distress,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking in his hand the much-wrought shield, the weapon of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wise work of Hephaestus. And the peoples gathered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the varied Olympian wonders of the craft,","st":"25.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing wonders, which a heavenly hand had wrought"},{"sp":"N","t":"in fashioning the many-colored shield, in the middle of which"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made the earth, circling, and about the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"he rounded the heaven, scored with the chorus of stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made the sea yoked to the earth; and in the upper air","st":"25.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"he embroidered the sun in gold, riding upon the rail"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his flaming chariot, and of silver metal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whitening it, he rounded the whole wheeling Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in it all the constellations, with which the coiling upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling itself crown-wise in a many-lit adornment,","st":"25.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"is embroidered about its seven zones, and beside the axle-circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twin unwetted rhythm of the heavenly Wagon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for both march beside the turning-post above Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"at each other's loins, and always by as much"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the head of the sinking Bear is bent down below,","st":"25.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"by so much is the neck of the other rising stretched up;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he embroidered the Dragon in the middle of the twofold Wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who, joining the divided limbs of both close by,"},{"sp":"N","t":"is bent in the coiling trail of his heavenly belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reining back his shifting body, like the coiling flood","st":"25.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"of slanting, roaring Maeander, who through the land"},{"sp":"N","t":"bends his arching water aslant and travels in spirals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a facing eye toward the head of Helice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his body girdled with starry scales, encircled"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the constellations of the Bears; and on the tip of his tongue","st":"25.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"a star, spitting light, glitters stretched forward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a many-toothed flame in the middle, neighbor to his lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the wise smith wrought in the middle back"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the well-made shield; and doing favor to Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made the ox-founded walls of lyre-built Thebes,","st":"25.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seven-pathed gates being built in alternating rows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zethus was there, laboring for his homeland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a rocky burden on his straining shoulder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Amphion sang shrill, striking his lyre; and at his song"},{"sp":"N","t":"a coiling hill danced, self-rolling, into its course,","st":"25.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though charmed even in the shield; and you would quickly have said, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"though it was made by art, that a light throb, playing"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a skipping motion, whirled the unmoving cliff;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watching the man devoted to his silent lyre,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a swift melody on a lying string,","st":"25.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"you would have hastened to come near, so that, setting your ear"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the tower-building harp, you might delight your own heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the stone-moving sound of the seven-toned song."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the well-turned shield, where the shifting chorus of stars is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had a fitting device, since within the court of Zeus","st":"25.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Trojan wine-pourer was embroidered with most holy art"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a well-made eagle as his winged bearer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as in paintings, held fast by a ravishing claw;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zeus seemed in fear as he flew through the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the astonished boy with unscratching talons,","st":"25.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparing in the rush of his gently moving wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest Ganymede be hidden in the murderous surges of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipping head first from the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he trembled still more at the Fates, lest the lovely youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having first given his own name to the neighboring sea,","st":"25.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"should snatch a late prize kept for Helle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the boy was fashioned beside the heavenly table of the feast"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the gods, wholly like one drawing wine; and he had a mixing-bowl"},{"sp":"N","t":"crammed with the self-poured nectarous dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and held out a cup to Zeus as he feasted; and Hera sat","st":"25.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though angry even in the shield, having in her witnessing form"},{"sp":"N","t":"the jealousy of her soul, and to the goddess Pallas"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated beside her she showed the boy, because Ganymede,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a star-roaming herdsman, poured the sweet nectar of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing in his hand the cups that the maiden Hebe had obtained.","st":"25.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he fashioned Maeonia, since she was the nurse of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Moria and the dappled serpent and the divine herb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the measureless serpent-slaying son of Earth, Damasen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Tylus, scored by a venom-shooting sharp doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a short-lived dweller of Maeonia, who once, walking","st":"25.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the brows of the Mygdonian river, neighboring Hermus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched a serpent with his hand; and it, stretching its broad neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising its head with the unsparing gape of its throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushed against the man, and lashing his hips"},{"sp":"N","t":"it whirled the stormy onset of its tail,","st":"25.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"and joining its back crown-wise about his mortal skin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping about the circles of his newly bearded chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the edges of his cheek with a many-toothed swath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it spat the moisture of Fate from venom-shooting jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as it leapt upon him his middle neck was girdled","st":"25.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the coils of its tail above his burdened shoulders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a snaky necklace of Hades, the neighbor of Fate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the corpse fell to the ground like a shoot of the earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And pitying the youth who had fallen by a witnessing doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled Naiad groaned over the corpse beside her,","st":"25.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"and then she sought to hold back the monstrous beast, that it might be subdued;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for it had not dashed down one wayfarer or one herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it did not kill Tylus alone untimely, but in the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"where it dwelt it feasted even on beasts, and often, dragging"},{"sp":"N","t":"a tree, roots and all, restless, beneath the sockets of its teeth,","st":"25.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"it hid it in its moldering throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back again the grim blast of its jaws;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, dragging a wayfarer who turned back"},{"sp":"N","t":"in terror at the inner panting, hauling him into its mouth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"visible from afar, it received a whole man in its gaping throat.","st":"25.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Moria watched her brother's slayer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing from afar, and the lady whirled in fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the many-rowed swath of its venom-shooting teeth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she saw the garland of death entwined about his throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wailing thickly beside the serpent-feeding thicket","st":"25.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"she met sky-high Damasen, the son of Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once his mother midwifed in a self-begotten birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending the shaggy circles of his chin from his birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Strife was his nurse when he was born; and spears were"},{"sp":"N","t":"his breast, and slaughters his bath-water, and a breastplate his swaddling-clothes,","st":"25.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"and weighed down with the broad burden of his long limbs"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spear-wielding infant, a valiant babe, neighbor to the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his birth brandished a kindred spear, and Eileithyia"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed the newly appeared shield-bearer of her childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Beholding him beside the well-pastured slope of the wood","st":"25.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bent in supplication, and the plaintive lady showed him"},{"sp":"N","t":"the measureless creeping thing, the slayer of her brother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Tylus, freshly scored, still gasping in the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was the Giant careless, the monstrous champion; but gripping"},{"sp":"N","t":"a tree he tore it up, trunk and all, from his mother the field,","st":"25.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stood aslant facing the raw-devouring serpent;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the coiling champion fought for a snaky honor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending the hiss of battle from the trumpet of its neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a serpent of fifty roods, circling in its trail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it clamped his feet with a twofold squeezing bond,","st":"25.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lashing the body of Damasen with its crooked coils"},{"sp":"N","t":"it opened the gates of its teeth with a raving gape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting a wet shaft from its lips, shaking its eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"raw and breathing slaughter, and against the Giant's face"},{"sp":"N","t":"it spat springs of venom from its rainy jaws,","st":"25.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting the green, long-shadowed foam of its teeth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it leapt upon the head of the high-crested Giant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting up upright with an earth-shaking throb of its limbs."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the dread Giant shook off the burden of the snaky spine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking limbs like crags;","st":"25.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he whirled his long-leaved point in his palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling the wooden shaft upright; and about its temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fixed the plant, roots and all, where about its circling neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bond of its spine was joined at the neck's point;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plant was rooted a second time; and about the ground","st":"25.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"the serpent lay unshaken, a coiling corpse. And suddenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"the female serpent, scraping the ground with a returning trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the coiled bedfellow, sought her slanting mate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a woman longing for a dead husband; and toward the crags"},{"sp":"N","t":"she whirled the trail of her long spine more swiftly,","st":"25.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"hastening to a herb-bearing mountain; and about the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having plucked the flower of Zeus with her viperish jaw,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she brought the pain-killing plant with the tips of her lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fitted it, the defender against a frightful destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the parched nostril of the corpse, and gave a flowering life","st":"25.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the unmoving venom-shooting dead;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the corpse leapt up self-coiling; and one part of it"},{"sp":"N","t":"was breathless, and another marched forward, and shaking another"},{"sp":"N","t":"it was a half-finished corpse, having a self-moving tail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretching a recovered breath through its cold jaws","st":"25.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"it hummed little by little with its accustomed throat as it opened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring forth a hiss regained; and late, going"},{"sp":"N","t":"homeward, it entered its ancient lair-hole."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Moria lifted the flower of Zeus, and about the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fitted the life-bearing plant to a life-giving nostril.","st":"25.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the grain-giving herb with its pain-healing clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"ensouled a breathing body for the corpse restored."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the soul came into the body a second time; and the cold body"},{"sp":"N","t":"was warmed by an inner helping torch;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the corpse, tending the recovered beginning of life,","st":"25.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"moved the sole of its right foot, and straightening"},{"sp":"N","t":"the standing track of the left it was propped upon the whole sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the likeness of a man who, sleeping in his bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shakes off the morning sleep from his opening eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the blood boiled again; and the hands of the newly breathing","st":"25.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"corpse were lifted; and there was harmony in his form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"walking in his feet, light in his eyes, voice in his lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cybele was scored, newly delivered, as though in her bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a mimic, unborn childbirth"},{"sp":"N","t":"with fashioned forearms, and to her loveless consort","st":"25.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"guile-weaving Rhea held out a stony travail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a chilling, heavy dinner; and the father hid"},{"sp":"N","t":"the marble son of mortal-seeming shape in his feasting throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"banqueting on the lying body of another Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bearing the childbirth-helping stone within his flanks","st":"25.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shot forth the crowded, many-childed race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting out the burden of his pregnant throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such many-turning devices of a labor-working craft"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bacchic warlike shield of many-springed Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"held, which they marveled at as they gazed, one after another,","st":"25.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they circled the bearer of the wheeling shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"praising the fiery Olympian forge."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they delighted Dawn measured out her setting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back the light of her fire-eyed face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and silent Night blackened the whole shadowy earth.","st":"25.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the peoples this way and that upon ground-strewn beds"},{"sp":"N","t":"fell after the evening meal upon a mountain couch."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXVI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The twenty-sixth got the thievish form of Athena and the great battle-rousing muster of the gathering Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to Deriades as he slept, above his downcast bed,","st":"26.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Athena stood, bringing faithful things to Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wooed another victory to come for her brother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and changing her body into the shifted likeness of Orontes"},{"sp":"N","t":"she imitated the son-in-law of high-crested Deriades;","st":"26.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he cast away the murderous sting of Enyo"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mimic vision of a guileful dream beguiled him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a word, and at the doom of the slain"},{"sp":"N","t":"she heartened the fearful man to the battle of Dionysus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You sleep, Deriades; and I blame you; for to hold","st":"26.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"an all-night sleep is alien to city-holding kings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the counselor has a measure of sleep. About your towers"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foes press on, and you lift no furious spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you do not hear the rushing din of drums, nor the melody of flutes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you do not hear the host-gathering sound of the murderous trumpet.","st":"26.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"But pity your young daughter, a mourning widow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Protonoe, lamenting her husband,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not leave your Orontes unavenged, scepter-bearer."},{"sp":"N","t":"Kill my unarmed destroyers; for though your swift-fated"},{"sp":"N","t":"son-in-law is dead, his slayers still live;","st":"26.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"look at my breast struck by the sharp thyrsus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas, that warlike Lycurgus does not dwell here,"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas, that you do not rule over the overbearing Arabs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus was no god, whom, pursuing him into the swell of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mortal man made a migrant beneath the waves.","st":"26.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have seen Deriades fleeing a womanish war."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be a fearless lion, since Dionysus in his fawnskin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing a brazen man, became like a fawn."},{"sp":"N","t":"He did not slay the race of war-mad Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but your own father slew them; for in the wars,","st":"26.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing your champions fleeing, Hydaspes subdued them."},{"sp":"N","t":"You are not like others; for you bear the heavenly blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the daughter of Phaethon, your blazing grandfather;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have no mortal body; neither sword nor shaft shall subdue you"},{"sp":"N","t":"as you bear down upon Lyaeus.\"","st":"26.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, much-devising Athena went to Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"changing the look of her dream face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at dawn Deriades bade his heralds gather"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wide-scattered nation from the cities, from the islands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a herald went this way and that with stormy sandal","st":"26.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"along the eastern floor, mustering a people of many cities;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and war-mad warriors gathered from every side"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the king's summons."},{"sp":"N","t":"First the steersmen of Enyo armed themselves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Agraeus and Phlogius, leaders of one company,","st":"26.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the freshly finished tomb of their newly perished father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two children of Eulaeus; and peoples marched with them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all who dwelt in Cyra and in Baedion of the Indian river"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the broad barbarian water of Ombelus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and well-towered Rhodoe, the plain of war-mad Indians,","st":"26.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rocky Propanisus, and those who held the rim of the island"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Graeae, where children have a male breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a milk-bearing father instead of an accustomed mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing the dew with the tips of their lips;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those of steep Sesindion, and those who towered up","st":"26.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"Gazos in a linen-fenced circle with thread-plaited buildings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unbending prop of Ares, and never did a hostile man","hvShift5":[[0,0],[28,28],[30,35],[32,41],[36,48],[39,50],[45,51]]},{"sp":"N","t":"with bronze break the rank of those linen-cloaked towers,","hvShift5":[[0,7],[2,11],[4,14],[12,17],[19,18],[25,21],[26,23],[29,26],[35,29],[42,31],[48,34],[51,45]]},{"sp":"N","t":"unbreakable, well-made on well-spun foundations.","hvShift5":[[8,1],[12,2],[17,12],[20,21],[24,24],[26,25],[52,26],[55,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And after them bold warriors marched,","st":"26.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dardae and the armies of the Prasii, and the tribes of the gold-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"Salangi, whose housemate is wealth, whose custom it is always"},{"sp":"N","t":"to eat pulse as their living; and instead of grain"},{"sp":"N","t":"they grind that with the wheel-shaped circle of a mill;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ranks of the crook-haired Zabii, whose intelligent","st":"26.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"champion was Palthanor, who hated Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being of one mind with Dionysus in his pious ways;"},{"sp":"N","t":"him lord Dionysus, leading him after the Indian war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave to lyre-built Thebes as a foreign dweller;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stayed beside Dirce, leaving his father Hydaspes,","st":"26.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the Ismenian water of the Aonian river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after them Morrheus, son of Didnasus, exulting, armed"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unspeakable host, marching with his father, who then,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a grief mingled with wretched old age, took hold of the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing a much-wrought shield with an aged hand","st":"26.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking a chin shadowed with a grey meadow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-proclaiming herald of long time,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still groaning for his short-lived son, Indian Orontes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Didnasus of the shifting tears; and lord Morrheus followed him"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding an upright avenging spear, so that he might subdue","st":"26.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole host of Bromius, and he wished to strive alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"with brother-slaying Bacchus, and longed to wound"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwoundable son of Thyone, his brother's slayer."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with them marched the many-tongued race of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who held the city of Helios, fair-built Aethre,","st":"26.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foundation of a cloudless floor, and those who held both"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thicket of Anthene and the reed-bed of Orycia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and flaming Nesaia and stormless Melaenae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the well-turned plain of sea-garlanded Patalene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"after them the thick ranks of the Dyssaeans, with whom","st":"26.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread tribes of the shaggy-breasted Sabeirae armed themselves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who have shaggy hairs upon their hearts, for the sake of which"},{"sp":"N","t":"they always have courage of soul and do not cower at war."},{"sp":"N","t":"With these marched also the Uatocoetae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose custom it is to sleep upon their long ears;","st":"26.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phringus came to them and Aspetus, drawing them into the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and exultant Danyclus their companion, with whom, marching"},{"sp":"N","t":"as fellow-contender with Hippurus, far-shooting Morrheus went;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having one mind, five blood-red leaders"},{"sp":"N","t":"marshaled the whole Uatocoetan host.","st":"26.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tectaphus came to the battle, a far-shooter, who once"},{"sp":"N","t":"with hungry lips drank the streams of a guileful breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his daughter's, the defenders against doom that tended her father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tectaphus, parched, with crumbling skin, an intelligent corpse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the scepter-bearer, with a loveless threat,","st":"26.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades, squeezing him in much-plaited cords,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shut him fettered in a moldering pit,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unfed, squalid, his body gasping with hunger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"without share of the sun or of the well-rounded moon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fettered man was hidden in an earthly depth,","st":"26.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing no drink, no food, seeing no man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but beneath a hollow rock of the ground-dug flanks"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lay in misery; and he was wasted with long hunger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching the feeble panting of famished lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"breathing, like the breathless; and as from a corpse,","st":"26.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"foul breezes blew from his parched skin."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a host of guards watching the shut-in man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom then his cunning daughter beguiled with a deceiving"},{"sp":"N","t":"speech; and she cried a heavy-groaning suppliant voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the newly delivered lady, shaking her guileful garments:","st":"26.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Do not kill me, guardians; I carry nothing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not come bringing drink or any food for my father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"tears, tears only I bring to my begetter;"},{"sp":"N","t":"my hands proclaim themselves free; if your mind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if your mind is distrustful, loose my blameless girdle,","st":"26.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"fling away my veil, shake out my tunic with your hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not come bringing a life-sustaining drink. But hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"even me together with my father in the underground pit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am no terror, no terror, even if the scepter-bearer hears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"who is angry at one who pities a corpse? Who is enraged","st":"26.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"at a dread-fated man dying? Who does not pity the breathless?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall close my father's eyes as they sink;"},{"sp":"N","t":"hide me; what envy is there of death? And as they perish"},{"sp":"N","t":"let one tomb receive them both, father and child.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded them. And the girl ran into the recess,","st":"26.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"a light-bearer to her father in the gloom; and in the pit"},{"sp":"N","t":"she poured into her father's mouth the milk of her evil-averting breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearless; and hearing the god-fearing deed of Aerie"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades marveled; and he loosed from his bonds the father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a phantom, of the girl of surpassing mind;","st":"26.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a rumor was heard on every side, and the army of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"praised the guile-weaving breast of the evil-averting lady."},{"sp":"N","t":"He then was conspicuous among the Bolinges, as in the midst of the stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"the star Hesperus glitters, brightening the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hesperus, the messenger of the lightless darkness that follows.","st":"26.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"And high-headed Ginglon and lofty-footed Thyraeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cloud-high Hippalmus, beyond the region of the farthest land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed the shifting tribes of the spear-bold Arachotae"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ranks of the Dersaeans of one company, who hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"in loose dust those slain by the iron in war","st":"26.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"(those slain in the strife they entombed in dust)."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Habrathoos came slowly, having gathered an army"},{"sp":"N","t":"of allies with the curved bow; and he armed himself in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"at his freshly cut hair, swelling with anger and grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the ox-horned king, since in witless madness","st":"26.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"overbearing Deriades had shorn off all his hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bitter reproach to the Indians. And as a warrior by necessity"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came scarcely to the battle, and with a high-crested helmet"},{"sp":"N","t":"he covered the outraged, hairless rim of his temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending a hidden rancor in his heart; and in the tumults","st":"26.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"by day he made war, but always through the night hours"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sent to Bacchus a faithful attendant as a messenger"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the purposes of Deriades; and secretly"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was armed for Deriades and openly for Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he armed the wild tribes of the Xuthi and of the battle-rousing","st":"26.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arienes and the offspring of the Zoari and the tribe of the Eares"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the race of the Caspeiri and the Arbians, and those who held","hvShift6":[[0,0],[33,37],[37,43]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Hysporus itself, gleaming with a shining stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tressed with the deep-rich metals of amber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and those who held sunny Arsanie, where the women","st":"26.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a single dawn complete a whole tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"with swift palms at the accustomed loom of Pallas."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after them the Cyraeans took their place in the tumbling war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing the sea-beaten rim of the islands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skilled in a sea-faring Ares; and they know no","st":"26.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"water-traveling merchant ships, but with untanned ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"they sail in a cunning likeness of wooden ships;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they steer a counterfeit voyage with skins, in which the sailor"},{"sp":"N","t":"sits, crossing the sea in waveless swells,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the backs of the sea with mimic ships.","st":"26.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"These Thyamis marshaled, and Holcasus, leader of men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two children of Tarbelus their javelin-bearing father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a great swarm came, leaving Areizanteia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the nurse of a strange wooden honey, where, having drunk"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grain-giving eastern watering of the airy dew,","st":"26.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tressed trees bring forth a honey-flowing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cunning travail of the wise bee, as from hives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the green drink of self-bearing leaves; for onto the plain"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon, newly appearing, when he bathes in Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shakes off the nourishing moisture of his eastern hair,","st":"26.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the plant-tending furrow of the life-bearing earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such honey does Areizanteia bear, rejoicing in which,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swimming on its wings, dancing above the leaf,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unspeakable bird flies; and a crooked serpent, creeping,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling it in spirals, entwined with the sweet tree,","st":"26.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"draws off the lily-like moisture with a ravishing throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"licking with its lips the sweet travail of the clusters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the serpents, gushing up the tree-dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pour out sweet honey, and do not spit forth as much"},{"sp":"N","t":"bitter wandering venom as they do the sweet stream of the bee;","st":"26.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there upon the honey-dripping branches sings"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horion, a sweet bird, like an intelligent swan;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it does not strike up in concert with the Zephyr breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending the windy whirr of hymn-bearing wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but makes melody with wise lips, as some man","st":"26.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"beating out a chamber-serving hymn on a bride-tending harp."},{"sp":"N","t":"And often, singing shrill above a windy tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in concert with the horion it wove a neighboring song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned with crimson wings; and you would quickly have said,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the catreus singing its eastern hymn,","st":"26.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"that a shifting-necked nightingale was weaving a morning revel."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the catreus prophesies the pouring of rain to come,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yellow in nature, clear-voiced; and from its eyelids a radiance"},{"sp":"N","t":"is sent, a match for the Dawn with its morning beams."},{"sp":"N","t":"There too was the host of battle-rousing men, whom to the fighting","st":"26.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearless Pyloetes, son of Hippalmus, armed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his brother Billaeus as a leader of the same company."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after them the Sibae armed themselves and the people of Hydarces,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another host came, leaving the city of Carmina;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of these Cyllarus led together with Astraeis, champion of the Indians,","st":"26.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two children of Brongus, honored by Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another fleet came from three hundred islands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which stand in rows in turn, one here, another there,"},{"sp":"N","t":"neighbors to one another, where with a vast crossing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-mouthed Indus, bringing its migrant, crooked water,","st":"26.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping little by little from the Indian reed-bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting over the floor beside the mouth of the eastern sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"comes self-rolling over the Ethiopian ridge;"},{"sp":"N","t":"where, lifted by the trail of its summer-born waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with self-begotten floods it grows cubit upon cubit,","st":"26.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the watery husband embraces the rich earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting his thirsty bride with moist kisses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a many-cubit sting of sheaf-bearing wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing forth ever-renewed floods in alternating measure,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Nile in Egypt and the eastern Indian Hydaspes.","st":"26.235","hvShift6":[[0,0],[35,49]]},{"sp":"N","t":"There, scraping the black-pebbled stream with its hoof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swims a wandering watery river-horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as dwells scoring the summer-born swell"},{"sp":"N","t":"of my Nile, a wet wayfarer through the water of the deep,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[10,11],[16,13]]},{"sp":"N","t":"with long jaws; and it walks upon the shore","st":"26.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the ridge of the wood with a jagged-toothed point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having a wet, unscored jaw that snatches fruits"},{"sp":"N","t":"it cuts the corn-bearing crops with a mimic sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an ironless reaper of the sheaf-bearing birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"such, they say, is the strength that the Indian river bears,","st":"26.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"like the seven-pathed Nile. And leaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"the crooked circles of the islands and the seats of the neighboring Indus"},{"sp":"N","t":"warlike men armed themselves, whose champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the man Rhigbasus, led them, having the likeness of the Giants."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did old Aretus, a heavy man, stay behind","st":"26.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Deriades called him to the battle; but fastening"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bronze-heavy tunic over his shaggy breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted an ox-hide shield upon his aged back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hanging the strap about his arching neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a warrior by necessity armed his army","st":"26.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"with five sons, Lycus and his companion Myrsus,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[30,34],[38,39]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Glaucus and Periphas and late-born Melaneus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And binding his grey lock tight with a helmet"},{"sp":"N","t":"he marched to the left of the well-wheeling battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the right wing of the war to his children,","st":"26.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose mouths nature had sealed with a bond of speechlessness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding tight the tongue, the channel of a wise voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for when, dancing beside the doorposts of the chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aretus confirmed Laobie by the ordinance of yoking marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having entered child-begetting wedlock,","st":"26.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was an inspired wonder, since beside the bridal altar"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom fresh from the dance was laboring at sacrifice"},{"sp":"N","t":"to bride-tending Aphrodite, and in the well-hymned hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"a many-bearing sow, crying out the din of childbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"from her throat, a prophet of things to come, was weighed down,","st":"26.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a strange and unbelievable childbirth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and was in travail with a bastard race of finny creatures, and from her flanks"},{"sp":"N","t":"shot forth a wet, fishy offspring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having brought to birth a birth of the brine instead of an earthly birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many-mouthed Rumor flew about the fish-bearing sow,","st":"26.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathering the people; and the wide-scattered citizens"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed at the many-childed land-born brood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a like-natured imitation of a sea-born childbirth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he asked a god-speaking prophet; and to him as he asked"},{"sp":"N","t":"the prophet foretold a coming row of speechless children,","st":"26.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"the image of a sea-born, tongueless race."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then the prophet said to hide the forewarning oracles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might propitiate the long-winged son of Maia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the leader of the tongue, the steerer of wise speech."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Laobie was in labor, and in an answering childbirth","st":"26.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore a row of children equal in number to the sow's young,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like speechless fishes, whom after the victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus pitied, and from their voiceless throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"he loosed the bond of the tongue, and drove off their age-old silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and granted to each a late-accomplished voice.","st":"26.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"With them marched shield-bearing warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who dwelt in Pylae and those who obtained, near Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inhabited Eucolla, the haunt of a warlike Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and most holy Goryandis, a well-sown furrow of land."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after them armed themselves all who obtained the rims of Oeta,","st":"26.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tree-covered mother of measureless-lived elephants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to which nature has granted to live the circles"},{"sp":"N","t":"of two hundred years at the much-bending turning-post of ever-flowing time,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or of three hundred; and one grazes after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dark-skinned from the tip of the foot to the head;","st":"26.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"having in its long jaws two projecting tusks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the reaping pattern of a crook-clawed sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a whetted cutter, it passes through a row of trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"on long-shanked feet; and having the likeness of camels"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an arching crest, on its capacious back","st":"26.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"it brings a countless swarm of drivers one after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling a standing tread with the unbending trail of its knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the broad-browed pattern of a viperish head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a little arching neck; and with eyes of like pattern"},{"sp":"N","t":"it has taken the slender likeness of the face of swine,","st":"26.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-visible, vast; and as it turns in its journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"its ears, fleshless, hanging beside its neighboring temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"are fanned by a little gust of slender winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lashing its body thickly with a directing throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"its slender, small tail is shaken restlessly.","st":"26.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"And often in wars, shaking its projecting jaw,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sky-high bull-headed beast has assailed a man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a strange jagged-toothed, two-mouthed sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling on either side of its chin its native point;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often it has lifted a well-corseleted shield-bearer","st":"26.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright, aloft, pierced through by its ravishing throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and has dashed a man down with its jagged-toothed point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled the corpse self-rolling from on high"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a whirl of dust, a wanderer turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting in spirals the rim of its crooked chin,","st":"26.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"jagged this way and that beside the projections of its teeth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"answering in coils to viperish spines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching the scored sword of its teeth down to its feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"These lord Dionysus, leading them after the Indian war"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the Caucasian floor of the Amazonian river,","st":"26.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified the well-helmeted women into flight,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated on the crests of sky-high elephants."},{"sp":"N","t":"But that was after the strife. And then to the battle of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the call of Deriades, came the champion Pyloetes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steering a straight-footed elephant into the fray,","st":"26.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"the war-mad blood of the fair-bearing race of Marathon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with him to the fighting followed a neighboring people"},{"sp":"N","t":"of another speech, leaving fair-veiled Eristobareia."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the race of the Derbices followed Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ethiopians and Sacae and the varied nations of the Bactrians,","st":"26.340","hvShift6":[[0,0],[35,36],[41,39]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and a great host of woolly-haired Blemyes. And the Ethiopians"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursue a strange-looking, cunning pattern of fighting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for bearing the rim of a dead horse's temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"they lyingly hide the true circle of their faces,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clamp a mortal head with another countenance,","st":"26.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"having fashioned the breathless into the breathing, and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"they rout an unknowing foe with a counterfeit head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the champion sends a deceitful sound from his lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a horse's whinny with a human cry."},{"sp":"N","t":"These gathered at the king's summons.","st":"26.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the lord of the Indians led them all to war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom his father Hydaspes sowed in a watery love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having entered the wedlock of fair-bearing Astris,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of Helios. But there is a tale that his mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"was the Naiad Ceto, the offspring of Ocean,","st":"26.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once, creeping about a seething chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hydaspes the bridegroom embraced in a watery marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing true Titanian blood; for from the first-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"bed of Thaumas rosy-armed Electra his consort"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore a twofold offspring, from whose couch","st":"26.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"both a river sprang and a messenger of the heavenly ones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stormy Iris and swift-flowing Hydaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one making ready a course of feet, the other of streams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and both pursue an equal journey on opposite paths,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iris among the immortals and Hydaspes among the rivers.","st":"26.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"So great a host then came; and the city was crowded with people;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the well-helmeted ranks were girdled with comrades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling the circles of the four-pathed roads in the city;"},{"sp":"N","t":"some thick at the crossroads, others in trenches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others before the sky-high wall, and others on the towers","st":"26.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"slept a sweet sleep upon javelin-bearing beds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Deriades entertained the ranks of the leaders in his hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and all upon seats set in turn"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched a single table with the host-king."},{"sp":"N","t":"For them there was care of evening dinners and of the wing","st":"26.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"of nightly Sleep, and the army slept in arms, neighbor to Ares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sleeping they kept company with a battle-rousing dream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having raised a mimic war against the Satyrs."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXVII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The twenty-seventh follows the ranks in which Cronion arms the dwellers of Olympus for battle against Bromius."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now, shaking off the wing of toil-releasing Sleep,","st":"27.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"war-bearing Dawn opened the gates of the east,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and left the light-bearing bed of Cephalus; and struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"by Phaethon facing him, black Ganges whitened;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the freshly scored cone of mist withdrew in flight,","st":"27.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"split by the beams; and from her dewy car"},{"sp":"N","t":"the morning fruit was bathed in the dews of spring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was tumult. And Phaethon, the flaming shepherd"},{"sp":"N","t":"of ever-flowing years, reined back the course of his fire-reared horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the helmet-glancing echo of neighboring Ares,","st":"27.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"and challenged the army to fight with a witnessing torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling a hot rosy shaft; and about the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus poured from heaven a strange rain"},{"sp":"N","t":"of bloody moisture, the first announcer of the slaughter of Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with the murderous drops of a warlike snowfall","st":"27.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thirsty backs of the dark dust of the Indian floor"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddened; and the radiance of freshly polished iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"blazed, a match for the beams of the sun."},{"sp":"N","t":"And overbearing Deriades armed the appearing ranks"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Indians for the fray, and urging on his warriors","st":"27.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched a speech from a threatening throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My servants, make war, trusting in your accustomed Victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and make the bold horned son of Thyone, as they call him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the servant of Deriades who has horns like his."},{"sp":"N","t":"Kill me the Pans too with crushing iron;","st":"27.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if they are gods, and it is not lawful to cut down"},{"sp":"N","t":"the body of unwoundable Pan with cutting iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall take the mountain-ranging Pans as plunder, within the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"to herd the nations of the wilderness-dwelling elephants."},{"sp":"N","t":"There are many beasts here too, to which I shall join","st":"27.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pheres together with the Pans of mountain-ranging Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall give my daughter a swarm of chamber-servants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"attendants of the table of Morrheus as he feasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And let some man from Phrygia, the companion of wine-dark Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bathe his body in the streams of the Indian river,","st":"27.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of Sangarius call Hydaspes his father's water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let another man from Alybe who followed Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"be a hireling here, and, leaving the streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his silver river, drink gold-gleaming Ganges."},{"sp":"N","t":"Withdraw from me, Dionysus, fleeing the spear of Deriades;","st":"27.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"there is a boundless sea here too; but after the wave"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Arabian sea let ours also receive you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this broader depth belches a wild water,","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and is sufficient to cover both the Satyrs and Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rank of the Bassarids; Nereus is not gentle here,","st":"27.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"no Indian Thetis shall receive you, nor shall she save you"},{"sp":"N","t":"in her bosom fleeing again after a hospitable wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in reverence for my heavy-thundering father Hydaspes."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: I bear the Olympian blood of Cronion."},{"sp":"N","t":"Earth brought forth the upper air, scored with the chorus of stars;","st":"27.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have your race from heaven; but my Earth shall cover you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raw-eating Cronus, the feaster on his young children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though born from heaven, the bosom of the field hid him."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am the champion of a spear-bold army; I am better"},{"sp":"N","t":"than Lycurgus, who drove you and your unwarlike Bacchants;","st":"27.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Zeus-fallen race does not rout me; for I have heard"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fire-struck wedlock of your dread-fated Semele;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not speak of the lightning as the bride-adorner of the bed of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not tell of the head of Cronion or of a male thigh;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the childbeds of Zeus in labor do not rout me;","st":"27.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"often have I beheld my own wife in labor."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with you, if he wishes, let your father, the self-bearing Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arm womanish Athena to help a male,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she whom they call Victory, so that, battering the headlands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may bloody the head of Pallas with a flesh-cutting stone","st":"27.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a bold spear, and from my well-horned bow"},{"sp":"N","t":"may shoot the thigh of threatening Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the leader of the ox-horned Satyrs, and when he is wounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"may fasten reproach upon Zeus and Bromius and Pallas;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if the lame god arms himself with them both,","st":"27.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have need of cunning Hephaestus, so that he may forge"},{"sp":"N","t":"much-turning armor for Deriades too."},{"sp":"N","t":"I never tremble at a womanish champion; and if he shakes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lightning of his father, I have my father's water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold man whom they call the kindred blood of Lyaeus,","st":"27.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus, the offspring of his heavenly father Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall send cut down to Hades, the Zeus of the underworld;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall Zeus snatch him away, flying through the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I hear that many sons of Cronides have fallen;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dardanus was of Zeus and perished, and Minos died,","st":"27.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the bull-faced bed of Zeus save him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if he gives judgment even in Hades, what envy is it to the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Aeacus judges among the dead? And if he wishes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him have the rule of the corpses and the scepters of the pit."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the earth-born Cyclopes who touch Olympus","st":"27.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"with their long limbs — destroy them, not by fixing the point"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a spear in the middle of the belly or at the neck, but let the bronze-heavy"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaft be driven through the wheeling eye."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not destroy the earthly Cyclopes; for I have need"},{"sp":"N","t":"of them too; and sitting beside an Indian forge","st":"27.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Brontes make me a heavy-thundering trumpet"},{"sp":"N","t":"equal in din to thunderous crashes, so that I may be"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Zeus of the earth, and let Steropes make here a new"},{"sp":"N","t":"radiance to match the lightning; and I shall put him to the test"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting with the Satyrs, so that jealous Cronides may tear","st":"27.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"his heart the more, watching Deriades crash and flash,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing the leader of the Indians as the hurler"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a high-born flaming thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"What envy is it, if I arm my warlike hand with a hurricane?"},{"sp":"N","t":"The father of my mother, the lord of the flaming stars,","st":"27.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon himself, all fiery, is my champion; and if I bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of my father the river, then with a watery shaft"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall fight and raise a wet war against Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covering the hostile heads of the Bacchoi with the streams of the river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And cutting the bodies of the Telchines of the deep","st":"27.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"with crushing iron, entomb them in the neighboring sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the care of father Poseidon, and bring to Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"as sea-gifts of victory the grey-green harness of their cunning"},{"sp":"N","t":"chariot and the race of their water-traveling horses."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the heavy-bound dweller, the offspring of Hephaestus","st":"27.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fiery, born of Athena who knew no childbirth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"burn him with a blazing torch, him they call Erechtheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he bears the far-famed blood of that Erechtheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once at her breast the bed-fleeing motherless maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pallas reared, hidden, guarded by a sleepless blazing lamp;","st":"27.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him stay covered in an Indian blazing chest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the empty fence of a gloomy maiden-chamber."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the running servants of the well-leaping ox-hide,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skilled in the well-helmeted dance of Enyalius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring me the unarmed Corybants; and let Cabeiro,","st":"27.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unveiled Lemnian, weep over her two sons as they perish;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let smoky Hephaestus, casting away his fire-tongs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold the destroyer of his own offspring"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated upon the car of the Cabeiri,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades the mounter of bronze-footed horses.","st":"27.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"I kill the sons of Zeus; but I do not grudge Morrheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"to subdue Aristaeus, the hare-shooting son of Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the driver of the paltry, war-loving bee."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you with sickles and double-edged knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill the soft ranks of the Bassarids, and let the horned","st":"27.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"son of the river destroy the high-horned son of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest any cower at the sight of a driver of a lioness"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a champion mounted on the loins of a wild bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the grim mouth of yoked beasts; for who shall escape"},{"sp":"N","t":"a leopard or a lion when elephants arm themselves?\"","st":"27.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"As the king spoke, the Indians went to the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some upon the backs of iron-bearing elephants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others marching upon storm-footed horses."},{"sp":"N","t":"And near was a measureless host of foot-soldiers, some bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"points, others a shield, another the lid of a quiver;","st":"27.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"another man lifted up a bronze-forged sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a reaper of war, and another marched raising"},{"sp":"N","t":"a shield and swift bows and windy arrows."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they set the battle beside the mouth of the neighboring Indus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"running forward into the plain. And from the well-treed thicket","st":"27.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"with shields and swords and unbreakable leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsus-bearing Dionysus armed his warriors."},{"sp":"N","t":"And facing the flaming Dawn he set the army of the Bacchoi"},{"sp":"N","t":"divided fourfold, like the four winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first beside the deep-treed ankles of the circling Bear,","st":"27.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"where, carried in the trail of wide-scattered rivers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Zeus-fallen water of the Caucasian crag comes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the second phalanx he fitted where, in the middle","hvShift5":[[4,2],[6,3],[11,7],[13,17],[15,24],[23,32],[29,39],[31,40],[32,43],[34,46],[47,48]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of the land, bending crown-wise toward the western region,","hvShift5":[[0,4],[2,8],[9,9],[11,14],[13,17],[19,37],[23,44],[40,46],[42,48],[44,54],[45,56]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-mouthed mountain-roaming Indus whirls its stream,","st":"27.155","hvShift5":[[4,0],[11,6],[16,12],[30,13],[33,39],[36,52],[38,56]]},{"sp":"N","t":"crowning Patalene girdled about with its floods;","hvShift5":[[21,0],[27,32],[39,34],[42,36],[45,39],[50,40],[55,43]]},{"sp":"N","t":"beside that same floor, where with a vast crossing","hvShift5":[[2,5],[5,24],[11,32],[15,34],[20,40],[25,43],[29,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-thundering Hydaspes brings a back-turning flood;","hvShift5":[[0,37],[1,42],[4,43],[40,47],[47,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the midday brine is drawn with a purpling wave;","hvShift5":[[4,1],[8,8],[9,12],[10,18],[12,22],[16,25],[17,28],[20,30],[23,38],[27,41],[30,45]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lord set the fourth well-armed host","st":"27.160","hvShift5":[[0,4],[6,11],[10,12],[15,19],[21,24],[28,25],[31,33],[32,38]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and he marshaled the third, where beside the southern gulf","hvShift5":[[0,0],[2,15],[10,24],[12,27],[13,36],[14,39],[16,40],[22,46],[25,48],[27,53],[30,56]]},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the floor of the east, whence, wetting the reed-bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ganges sets out, flowing down with fragrant waters."},{"sp":"N","t":"And for each chosen well-greaved phalanx"},{"sp":"N","t":"he marshaled four well-helmeted leaders,","st":"27.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"and urging on the army he cried a host-rousing voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bassarids, come dance here too, and kill"},{"sp":"N","t":"the barbarian tribes of the foes, and mingle thyrsi with spears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingle them with swords too; and instead of the accustomed table"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the battle-rousing trumpet be my harp","st":"27.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"for my Satyrs; and let the green foliage of the harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling itself against iron, conquer the whetted spears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of the night dancing of Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"let my flute sound a changed hymn of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the after-dinner Echo of mind-delighting Bromius.","st":"27.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"If Hydaspes bends a slave's knee to me"},{"sp":"N","t":"and no longer arms a hostile swell against the Bacchoi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be gracious to meet, and I shall change all his splendid water"},{"sp":"N","t":"into Euian wine with the streams of the vat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making his currents delicious, and I shall girdle the crest","st":"27.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his wild wood with leaves and make it a vineyard;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if again with his evil-averting outpourings he helps"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indians as they are slain and his son Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"man-formed, holding the horned shape of a river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then bridge the flood of the overbearing river","st":"27.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and travel the thirsty water with unwetted steps,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the well-horsed hoof of a footman scrape the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"treading parched Hydaspes on his naked sand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if the much-dreaded champion of the war-mad Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"is the offspring of the race of airy Phaethon,","st":"27.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if Phaethon should raise a fiery war against me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"honoring the horned travail of his own daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then against a Phaethontian battle I shall arm once more"},{"sp":"N","t":"the brother of my Cronides, the watery sea, the quencher of fire;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall go to the Thrinacian island, where are the flocks","st":"27.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cattle of the airy, fire-gleaming Charioteer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall drag the daughter of Helios, like a spear-won girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lampetie unwilling, beneath a slave's yoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she may bend her knee; and let Astris wander to the mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lamenting her heavy-bound servant Deriades;","st":"27.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"let her go, if she wishes, a migrant to the land of the Celts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, having become a plant together with the Heliades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she too may weep thickly with lamentation-loving streams."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hasten for me, and whiten with mystic gypsum"},{"sp":"N","t":"the circles of the dark-skinned faces of the captive Indians,","st":"27.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fasten upon bold, bronze-tunicked Deriades &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fawnskin entwined with a vine cluster;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the Indian lord, bending a slave's knee to Bromius"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the victory, fling his breastplate to the storms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covering his body with a better, shaggy breastplate,","st":"27.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bind his foot tight with purple buskins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving his silver greaves to the winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after his blood-red bows and the works of accustomed war"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him be taught the night-danced rites of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his barbarian locks over the wine-vat.","st":"27.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"And bring the heads of the foes as tokens of victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"to windy Tmolus, pierced upon a witnessing thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I shall remove many ranks of Indians from the war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking them alive after the fighting, and beside the gateway of the Lydians"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall fix the horns of maddened Deriades.\"","st":"27.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he heartened them; and the Bacchants rushed on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Sileni shouted the melody of war-loving Echo"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs cried out from throats of one voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the sounding drum a concordant echo roared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dread bellowing, and by the alternating hands","st":"27.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the rattle-loving women a twice-struck echo was beaten out;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the host-gathering pipe cried a pastoral Phrygian rhythm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Satyrs made war; and they were whitened with mystic"},{"sp":"N","t":"gypsum, and a dread thing hung upon their cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bastard likeness of a lying, speechless face.","st":"27.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"And going before the army, bearing down in the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Mygdonian flame flashed, leaping through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the fiery childbed of Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from the well-horned brow of old Silenus"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sparkle blazed; and about the unplaited hair","st":"27.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a mountain-haunting Bacchant a serpent was clamped as a band;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, lashing a maddened tiger against the adversaries,"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified the cars of yoked elephants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and grey Maron armed himself with a coiling branch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the bodies of the fighting Indians","st":"27.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a shoot of the vine. — And all who dwell in Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sitting beside Zeus within the god-receiving court,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held assembly in a body upon golden seats."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as they feasted, fair-haired Ganymede poured out"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet nectar for them, drawing from the mixing-bowl.","st":"27.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"For not yet then did an Achaean Ares roar against the Trojans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as before, so that fair-haired Hebe should mix the cups"},{"sp":"N","t":"again for the blessed ones, and the Trojan wine-pourer"},{"sp":"N","t":"should be far from the immortals, lest he hear the doom of his homeland."},{"sp":"N","t":"To them gathered together Zeus the counselor spoke,","st":"27.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"and said to Apollo and Hephaestus and Athena:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"God-speaking lord of Pytho of the oracular axle,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[36,37],[41,44]]},{"sp":"N","t":"scepter-bearer of archery, light-bearer, brother of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remember Parnassus and your own Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine of a day has not escaped you; you know too","st":"27.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twin mystic pine-torch of both crags;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but fight in the front for your brother Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching your Olympian bow as the helper of the Bassarids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and honor your own shared rock of Parnassus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where a dance-beating Bacchant cried out as she reveled,","st":"27.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"making ready a song for you and for unsleeping Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having kindled a yoked Delphic fire for both."},{"sp":"N","t":"Remember your lion-slaying Cyrene, glorious archer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to them both, to Agreus and to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Nomius, fight in the front for the race of the pastoral Satyrs.","st":"27.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ward off the heavy-minded jealousy of Hera, lest ever"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stepmother of Phoebus laugh when Dionysus flees,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she who, pursuing her anger and jealousy at my loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arms herself forever against my children; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"the childbed labor of your own mother, when Leto","st":"27.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"went much-wandering, bearing a twofold burden of children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed by the child-begetting goads of birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the flood of Peneius fled, when Dirce"},{"sp":"N","t":"refused your mother, when heavy-kneed Asopus himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a course, whirling a step behind her,","st":"27.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"until Delos helped her in childbirth, until an old palm"},{"sp":"N","t":"with paltry leaves midwifed Leto."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, fearless daughter of Zeus your father and of your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pallas, defend your brother, the adorner of your fatherland;"},{"sp":"N","t":"save your own dwellers who follow Dionysus,","st":"27.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not behold the children of your Marathon destroyed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and honor the city-bearing branch of the Attic olive;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do a favor to old Icarius; for to him too"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus of the dappled clusters shall give his harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"remember Triptolemus and well-plowing Celeus,","st":"27.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not dishonor the generative baskets of Metaneira;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for of your ally, cluster-rich Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"generative Zeus the father bore the travail in a pregnant thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he brought your womanish travail to birth from his head."},{"sp":"N","t":"But shaking your birth-lance, your age-mate,","st":"27.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darting the aegis, the steerer of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be a helper for me to the Satyrs, since they too"},{"sp":"N","t":"wear the shaggy tunics of a mountain-ranging goat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the god of country folk, the lord of the pastoral pipe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"goat-herding Pan, has need of your aegis,","st":"27.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, striving in company with my unplundered scepters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought against the Titans, and was the mountain-running shepherd"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the milk-bearing nurse, the goat Amaltheia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"save him, who hereafter shall help in an Attic battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Mede-slaying savior of shaken Marathon;","st":"27.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"shake your aegis, shielding Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your black-aegis brother, who shall save your fatherland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving out the Boeotian leader;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a citizen of Eleutherae shall sing a life-saving song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi faithfully to Apaturian, the son of Thyone,","st":"27.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Athens shall strike up a mingled Phrygian rhythm"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Eleusinian Dionysus after Bacchus of the Marshes."},{"sp":"N","t":"O shifting Olympian race; ah, great wonder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Argive Hera stands beside a stranger, Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Attic Athena refuses the ranks of the sons of Cecrops,","st":"27.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and my Thracian Ares, bringing faithful things to his mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving my son Bacchus and the Thracian army"},{"sp":"N","t":"that follows Dionysus, saves the Indian throng."},{"sp":"N","t":"But fighting beside Dionysus with flaming fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone arm myself against them all, until Bacchus","st":"27.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall wipe out the dark race, roots and all."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, maiden-loving bridegroom of birth-perfecting Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"are you at rest, Hephaestus, and do you care nothing for Marathon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where is the bridal blaze of an unwedded goddess? I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mystic sparks of your ever-shining lamp.","st":"27.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"Remember the chest of the child-tending maiden-chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in which was the boy of Earth, in which the maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"reared your self-perfected seed at a male breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift your childbirth-helping axe, so that you may save"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dwellers of your Athens with your childbed ox-axe.","st":"27.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"Are you at rest, Hephaestus, and will you not save your own children?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lift your accustomed torch as the shield-bearer of the Cabeiri,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretch your eye, and watch your ancient bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she blames you, your child-loving Cabeiro;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lemnian Alcimacheia has need of your strength.\"","st":"27.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the gods who dwell in Olympus hastened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athena and Apollo as common helpers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fiery Hephaestus accompanied Tritogeneia."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera kept company running with the other immortals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding Ares by the hand, and broad-flowing Hydaspes,","st":"27.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fellow-contender of the foes in an emulous tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with whom went Terror and Panic, with whom came also"},{"sp":"N","t":"sheaf-bearing Deo herself, the adversary of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grudging life-begetting, cluster-loving Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he had found the drink of drunkenness, putting to shame the older boast","st":"27.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Zagreus, the first-born Dionysus of song."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXVIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Look at the twenty-eighth, where you may behold the great fiery war of the Cyclopes."},{"sp":"N","t":"There was an unsoftened strife; for both together,","st":"28.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"Faunus and Aristaeus, drove into a single war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Aeacus also accompanied, doing deeds worthy"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Zeus his father, stretching over his back"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bronze, much-wrought shield, in whose circle","st":"28.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"many cunning devices were spread, which the Lemnian anvil wrought."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the many-turning army armed itself for the war of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they hurried on in throngs; one, swift with his flesh-cutting ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shepherding his much-wrought car into the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mounted upon leopards; another with bristling harness","st":"28.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoked a mountain-running chariot of Erythraean lions"},{"sp":"N","t":"and steered the grim pair; another, much-dreaded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling his spear at the dark ranks of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken, delighted to lash an unbridled bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, leaping upon the back of a Cybelian bear,","st":"28.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"assailed the foes, and whirling a wine-dark thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified the drivers of long-shanked elephants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, hurling at the army with flesh-cutting ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had no sword, no circling shield, no blood-red"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear of battle, but, shaking the coiling leaves of plants,","st":"28.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"killed a bronze-tunicked man with a slender branch."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a thunderous crash resounded like a flute;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Sileni cried out; and the Bacchants marched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having cast fawnskins upon their breasts for a breastplate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one of the mountain-ranging Satyrs, as though driving a colt,","st":"28.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"sat astride the back of a lioness with parted feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indians shouted back, and gathering the warriors"},{"sp":"N","t":"the barbarian host-gathering flute of Enyo crashed out;"},{"sp":"N","t":"garlands clashed with helmets, breastplate resounded on aegis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsus raged against spears, and answering greaves","st":"28.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"matched with buskins; and the ox-hide shields of the yoked"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearers were propped in rows against one another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and foot-soldier against foot-soldier, and with high-crested head"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pelasgian helmet pushed the Mygdonian helmet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the tumult of the champions was of a strange kind; one, lifting himself,","st":"28.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled the aloft leap of a Bacchic dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another groaned as he fell, another rattled with his sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another gasped as he was struck, another skipped for Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, sending a warlike sound from his lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sang the spear of Ares, and another the banquet of Dionysus;","st":"28.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Enyo crashed together with the rite of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Euian rattles cried out, and the trumpet, the leader"},{"sp":"N","t":"of tumult, gathering the people, sounded together with the harp,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing gore with libation, and blending slaughter with dancing."},{"sp":"N","t":"There first of all, leaping light upon his foot,","st":"28.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaleneus hurled his spear against Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struck the unbreakable iron tunic;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the deadly point, though stretched out, touch his skin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but darting past it was fixed in the ground; and raging,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing a foe rushing near Deriades,","st":"28.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"valiant Corymbasus overtook him, and as he hurried"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed the very middle of his throat, striking with his sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reaped his head; and as the head was cut off"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaleneus fell headless, blood-dipped, upon the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"And about him a many-voiced battle arose; and Dexiochus","st":"28.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"scraped the very top of the brow of Phlogius with bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the edge of his forehead as his helmet split in two;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he, in terror, shifting knee a little from knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was hidden beneath the long ox-hide shield of his brother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding his fellow-contending brother beneath his father's shield,","st":"28.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"as his brother Ajax received the archer Teucer"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Dardanian race into his sevenfold ox-hide shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at once Corymbasus, drawing his sword from the sheath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashed down the neck of Dexiochus with his knife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly, leaping upon the gasping corpse, ran round it","st":"28.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad Clytius, the champion of the foot-soldiers; and much-dreaded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift, he hurled his spear at high-crested Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Hera turned the champion's spear aside,"},{"sp":"N","t":"angry both at Clytius and at Indian-slaying Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"yet the swift champion did not miss; for piercing","st":"28.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the monstrous throat of the irresistible beast"},{"sp":"N","t":"he killed the straight-footed elephant of Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laboring in its pains the ambidextrous sky-high beast"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook the whole wagon with its dark neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darting the crooked jaw that projected from its face","st":"28.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"it reined back the blood-dipped bonds of its yoking harness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the swift-working driver, bending the many-fastened yoke"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his sword, cut through the yoked trail of the neck-straps;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Celaeneus, taking another high-visible beast, new,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the broad-trodden manger, yoked it.","st":"28.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Clytius was bold in the hope of an unreproached victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he called upon the slayer of Dexiochus with a raving voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a deadly word from an insolent throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Stand, dog, do not flee, Corymbasus, and I shall teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"what spear-hurling attendants Lyaeus has.","st":"28.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall take you as plunder to Phrygia, and this spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall ravage the cities of the Indians, and after an Indian-slaying"},{"sp":"N","t":"victory I shall make Deriades the servant of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the maiden without bride-price loose her maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the shaggy-breasted wedlock of a Satyr,","st":"28.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"an Indian woman defiled beside the Mygdonian Hermus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke Corymbasus was enraged, and cut through"},{"sp":"N","t":"the throat of Clytius as he spoke of a late battle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his head flew aloft with a leap of Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the dust with a bloody drop.","st":"28.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the dancing corpse turning back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Corymbasus, the foremost of the Indians, terrified the Sileni,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[31,10]]},{"sp":"N","t":"foremost in manhood after Morrheus and the king."},{"sp":"N","t":"And striking the spearman Sebes above the rim of his breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"he thrust the bronze spear within his flesh, and as the bloody","st":"28.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaft was drawn out he cast him into the loose dust."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he rushed upon Oenomaus; but he, a fugitive like the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"withdrew toward the army of Bromius with astonished sole;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing him he pursued from behind, and in the very middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his back fixed his spear; and darting with a rush","st":"28.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"the point leapt out beside the navel of the belly opposite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he, pierced about with the blood-red iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped prone into the dust, freshly cut down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a death-bearing mist covered him over his eyelids."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the monstrous champion cease from battle; but four","st":"28.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"well-helmeted warriors were killed by one slayer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tyndarius and Thoon and Autesion and Onites."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many a one was a corpse freshly slain, not falling to the earth","hvShift6":[[0,0],[20,18]]},{"sp":"N","t":"prone, a man not stretched on his back upon the floor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but though dead he was propped unshaken upon the ground,","st":"28.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like a champion still fighting, as though brandishing a spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though drawing swift bows and pulling a shaft to the mark."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the valiant corpse, longing for war after his doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"forced the threads of the Fates, like a light spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"still hurling itself, pierced with shafts from wide-scattered bows","st":"28.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his head to the tips of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an upright statue of Ares; and the warriors gazed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with astonished eyes at the dead spearman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still gripping his spear and not having cast away his shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dead javelin-hurler and a breathless shield-bearer.","st":"28.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, reaching an Athenian with frightful iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reaped his right hand, striking the top of the arm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it tumbled with the dancing throb of slaughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fell freshly cut, entwined with its age-mate shoulder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spotting the yellow drenched backs of the dust.","st":"28.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"And now, drawing the long spear from the leaping hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would have held a recovered war with a far-throwing spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the answering left hand would have fought, wielding the lance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but a hostile man coming against him forestalled him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struck off the left hand, roots and all, with an answering knife;","st":"28.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the palm fell to the ground, and hurling itself at the slayer"},{"sp":"N","t":"the far-shooting trail of a bloody dew sprinkled him"},{"sp":"N","t":"with purple drops, and over the floor the wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"maddened hand leapt self-rolling in bounds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddened with blood, and clamped its crooked fingers","st":"28.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the ground in a well-handled, crook-clawed middle grip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though gripping the strap of an ox-hide shield again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he spoke some word, shedding warlike tears:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I long for still another hand, so that with three palms"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may accomplish deeds worthy of Tritogeneia;","st":"28.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless even after my hands I shall pursue the hostile man;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this remnant of my manhood is still mine, so that someone may speak"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ambidextrous boast of the Athenians, that even with their"},{"sp":"N","t":"feet they play the champion when their palms are cut away.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he ran upon the champions like the breezes,","st":"28.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"preparing an ironless fighting against his destroyer."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing him they marveled, one after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the abundant warriors circled the half-finished champion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he alone received with unsparing knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shifting blow of an answering iron;","st":"28.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely did he fall to the ground; and he was an image of Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping his father for a later-born dweller."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not then was the throng of foot-travelers alone cut down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but there was slaughter among the horsemen too; and one marched"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing doom to another; and driver, overtaking driver,","st":"28.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"either cutting down with his spear the back of one fleeing before,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or striking the breast of one coming against him near at hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dashed him freshly slain from his horse into the dust."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a horse, struck by an arrow above the flank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled its driver darting off into the plain,","st":"28.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"as swift-flying Pegasus, soaring aloft, companion of the wandering"},{"sp":"N","t":"breeze, shook off Bellerophon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, much-dreaded, from the slippery back"},{"sp":"N","t":"falling upright through the horse's belly to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was propped head first, hanging beside it, and casting his head","st":"28.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the ground he rolled, leaving his feet on the horse's back."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mighty Cyclopes circled the warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the helpers of Zeus; and against the misty host"},{"sp":"N","t":"Argilipus blazed, lifting a light-bearing brand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he armed himself with an earthly, fire-pointed thunderbolt,","st":"28.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting with torches; and the blazing Indians trembled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"astonished at a fire that answered the heavenly hurricane;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was a fiery champion; and upon the heads of the adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sparks of an earth-born thunderbolt were shot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Cyclops conquered ash-spears and unspeakable swords,","st":"28.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking hot shafts and a smoky point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a brand as though it were a bow; and he burned up"},{"sp":"N","t":"unspeakable Indians, one man after another, with his shooting torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not one Salmoneus alone, and he put him to shame with a counterfeit thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he killed not one god-fighter only; no single Evadne","st":"28.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"groaned for one Capaneus alone as he withered."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Steropes armed himself, whirling a mimic radiance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a gleam that answered the lightnings of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a quenchable sparkle, which the Hesperian flame bore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seed of Sicilian fire and of a blazing forge;","st":"28.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had a covering like a cloud, and hid the light"},{"sp":"N","t":"within and showed it again with a twofold throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the pattern of a heavenly gleam; for the lightning"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it comes holds a fleeing radiance, recovered again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Brontes made war, beating out a resounding melody,","st":"28.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring an echo answering to thunderous crashes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a strange drop of a ground-born snowfall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring out a manufactured, short-lived, clear-air water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was a counterfeit cloudless Zeus with mimic drops."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the cunning din of a thunder of like pattern","st":"28.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"he armed himself with Sicilian iron for the slaughter of his adversaries,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking his hammer aloft above his shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"he battered the heads of the foes thickly with iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he struck the dark ranks, turning about, as though always"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking a hammer-wrought anvil with an Aetnaean crash.","st":"28.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"And breaking off the headland of a long-based crag"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ran down upon Deriades with a rocky spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurling the vast stone with unsparing palm"},{"sp":"N","t":"against the dark-skinned king as he armed himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck his shaggy breast with the torrent-borne point;","st":"28.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he, drunk with so great a millstone-like rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was weighed down in his whole breast; but Hydaspes warded off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slaughter of his stricken son. And the bold man, laboring with his wound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook the furious spear from his unwearied hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"twenty cubits of bronze, and flung his ox-hide shield to the ground","st":"28.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"with shamefaced palms; and stretching a feeble breath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck on the rim of his breast by the marble point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fell head first from the air from his sky-high car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a vast fir above the crest — and as it falls"},{"sp":"N","t":"it runs round the unspeakable bosom of the broad field.","st":"28.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indians, pouring about him, lifted him into his chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing the misshapen Cyclops, lest with some rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking again another high-stretched headland of a hill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he should kill the king with a rough missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a height equal to that of high-crested Polyphemus.","st":"28.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"And in the middle of the grim champion's brow blazed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wheeling sparkle of his one-eyed face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cowering before the eye of the grim Cyclops"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-skinned Indians were shaken with astonished fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"thinking that Olympian Selene from heaven,","st":"28.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"rising in the face of the earth-born Cyclops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashed at the full, shielding Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father, seeing the Cyclops' imitation of tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughed on high among the clouds, because, receiving a strange rain"},{"sp":"N","t":"from earthly clouds through an untried bosom,","st":"28.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth was snowed upon then, while the thirsty air"},{"sp":"N","t":"had no poured dew, bearing its bare backs unwetted."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Trachius armed himself; and marching with his brother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking with sky-high palm a shield like a cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloud-high Elatreus had a vast fir,","st":"28.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting hostile heads with a wooden spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"Euryalus armed himself; and cutting apart in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"a great host fleeing from the plain as far as the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shutting in the ranks of the Indians toward the fishy gulf,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he conquered the foes through the javelin-bearing deep,","st":"28.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling an upright twenty-cubit sword through the water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting a sea-neighboring rock with his long ox-axe"},{"sp":"N","t":"he flung it at the adversaries; and many perceived"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twofold, brine-drenched threads of an unburied Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by a wavy Ares and by a jagged missile.","st":"28.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a shouting in the plain; and to the Bacchants as they were stirred"},{"sp":"N","t":"Prymneus came fair-weathered, like a stern-wind"},{"sp":"N","t":"saving a sailor who rides with the storms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he came, much-prayed-for, to the army, as calm Polydeuces"},{"sp":"N","t":"comes to ships that are shaken,","st":"28.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"having lulled the heavy wave of a storm-bearing sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"With them another kinsman, playing the champion, Halimedes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a monstrous Cyclops with sky-high limbs, raved,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified the foes; and guarding his face"},{"sp":"N","t":"he held before it the bossed back of his circling ox-hide shield.","st":"28.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing him Phlogius, the avenger of the slain Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"curved his bow, and drawing a windy shaft"},{"sp":"N","t":"was about to strike him in the middle with the winged missile;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, perceiving the facing rush of the archer as he aimed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the high-headed Cyclops slanted aside and avoided the cast","st":"28.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the speeding arrow; and shaking a headland"},{"sp":"N","t":"he flung the rocky missile at Phlogius; but he, fleeing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood beside the chariot of ox-horned Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely avoided the air-roaming marble point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"staying there; and in anger about the flight of Phlogius,","st":"28.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"Halimedes, opening his deadly throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"killed twelve men with the bellowing of a single voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring the man-destroying boom of a raving cry."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the shout of the Cyclopes crashed toward Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with dreadful tongues. And the dancers of Enyo,","st":"28.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dictaean Corybants, marched into the tumult."},{"sp":"N","t":"Damneus made war, pursuing the hostile tribes; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with lighter feet Ocythous terrified"},{"sp":"N","t":"the warriors; and he killed many with a sharp doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one subduing at close quarters with his spear, another with a shaft","st":"28.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"from afar, and cutting down another with a frightful knife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another still running ahead, escaped, like the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raging he overtook, shaking his wind-footed knees,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like Iphiclus in running, who, hastening"},{"sp":"N","t":"the soles of his feet, scored the surface of an untrodden calm,","st":"28.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and held an aloft journey above the corn-ears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"treading with his feet the unbending topmost path of the asphodel."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a storm-footed man was Ocythous. And in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"Mimas set up a well-rhythmed, shambling war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified the army, whirling a dance-beating sword,","st":"28.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an unlaughing armed leap with knowing sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as when, rousing a din beneath the ears of Cronus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pyrrhichus, beating an Idaean shield with swords,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shouted the battle-steadfast melody of a lying Echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing away the hidden youth of ever-growing Zeus;","st":"28.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"having such a mimic armed leap of the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze-tunicked Mimas whirled his windy lance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting hostile heads, the iron cornfields of the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Indian-slaying axes and a double-edged knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"he held out the first-fruits of the foes to witnessing Bacchus,","st":"28.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a bloody libation to Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of a sacrifice of oxen and the accustomed wine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bright Idaeus entered the revel of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dancer of war, whirling a much-turning step,"},{"sp":"N","t":"uncontrollable, shaken by the sting of Indian-slaying battle.","st":"28.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Melisseus terrified the whole dark rank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having unshaken courage; and keeping his name"},{"sp":"N","t":"he imitated the dread stings of the bee as it arms itself;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wheeling with knowing sole on a restless foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"helmet-glancing Acmon went running with Ocythous;","st":"28.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fought unshaken like a hammer-wrought anvil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a Corybantic shield, in the middle of which"},{"sp":"N","t":"the infant Zeus often slept in the mountains;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the house of Zeus was a little cave, where that"},{"sp":"N","t":"holy goat midwifed him with a counterfeit milky breast,","st":"28.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing a strange, wise milk, when the ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"roared a shield-brandished Echo with birth-concealing crashes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck in the middle of its back by tumbling iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drawing a marble missile against the dappled Curete,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus missed Melisseus facing him,","st":"28.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"missed him; for it is not fitting to destroy Corybants with a millstone."},{"sp":"N","t":"For whose sake Rhea, having fashioned a lying stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set it on the table of Cronus in place of Cronides."},{"sp":"N","t":"And coming together into one the dancers of joyless Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"held a common, yoked war; and about the chariot","st":"28.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"they girdled Deriades crown-wise with ox-hide shields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beating their armor, and in a well-rhythmed tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"they circled a tower with shield-bearing dances."},{"sp":"N","t":"And an air-roaming sound ran up to the courts of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the fair-footed Seasons feared the din of both.","st":"28.330"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXIX"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the twenty-ninth Ares withdraws from the wars, as though hurrying to another wedlock with Cytherea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when Hera perceived the ranks of the Indians being cut down,","st":"29.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"she cast hard-fighting courage into proud Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the dread king received a greater sting of longing"},{"sp":"N","t":"for battle; and pouring a raving voice upon his champions"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ran round the whole dark fighting rank by rank,","st":"29.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back the whole fleeing host into the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winning one over with kindness, another with threat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he was the bolder; and gathered together the Indians themselves"},{"sp":"N","t":"flowed to the tumult at the king's call."},{"sp":"N","t":"And far-shooting Morrheus split the whole rank of the Satyrs,","st":"29.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"now sending against the adversaries from his back-bent bow"},{"sp":"N","t":"an air-roaming cloud of arrows one upon another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now, whirling his furious spear turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he terrified the horned race of the Sileni."},{"sp":"N","t":"And fair-haired Hymenaeus fought, shaking his swords,","st":"29.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"untouchable, seated upon the back of a Thessalian horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting down the dark Indians with a rosy hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he flashed with splendor; and you might see him in the midst of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the gleaming Dawnstar running with a misshapen gloom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he terrified the foes, since for his beauty's sake","st":"29.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus breathed inspired valor into him as he fought."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Iobacchus, seeing him playing the champion in the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighted, and did not wish the lightning of Cronion"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the fellow-contender of his own battle so much as the ash-spear of Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"If ever he drove his colt darting into the war of the Indians,","st":"29.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus lashed the neck of his cunning beasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought his chariot near the horse, staying often beside the youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the boy as Phoebus kept Atymnius; and always he stood"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, and longed to be seen by the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"as both lovely and valiant under a single ordinance; and in the tumults","st":"29.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"he touched the clouds as he fought beside Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"One thing alone stirred him, that from an earthly stock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the boy was the son of Phlegyas, and not of Cronides his father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he stayed by him always, as a father guarding his child,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing lest some archer, letting fly an arrow,","st":"29.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"should shoot the boy; and as the casts came on"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched out his right hand, shielding Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to him as he played the champion he spoke such a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Send your shaft, dear boy, and Ares rages no more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with your beauty you struck Bacchus, the archer of Giants;","st":"29.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"strike with your shafts witless Deriades too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the god-fighting king of the foes, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Hymenaeus struck them both with his arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the skin of Deriades and the heart of Dionysus.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius so spoke, lovely far-shooting Hymenaeus","st":"29.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"took hold of the fighting far more; and rejoicing in him"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad Dionysus entered Enyo the more"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified the whole dark race, roots and all;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, seeing Dionysus in the unsparing tempest of battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"as an unsated archer of Indian heads,","st":"29.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"spoke such a word to wealth-loving Melaneus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Archer, where are your bows and your windy arrows?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Soft-tunicked women shoot at us."},{"sp":"N","t":"But send your shaft against short-lived Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let not the Olympian name of rumor lead you astray;","st":"29.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"never tremble at Bacchus, who from an earthly parent"},{"sp":"N","t":"got swift-fated blood, and has lied about the race of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Come, send your shaft, and if you hit the mark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall receive unspeakable gifts from our deep-wealthy king,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he shall see Dionysus, the proud son of Thyone,","st":"29.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"mounting a pyre, subdued by your shaft;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one shaft would loose the whole war. And to both,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift your hands in water and pray to mother Earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and promise with unlying lips to make sacrifices"},{"sp":"N","t":"to both after the victory; and beside the altar","st":"29.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"let bull-natured Hydaspes have a horn-drawing bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let dark Earth receive a black-skinned lamb.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he persuaded the arrow-shooting Melaneus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a man shaken by the sting of mind-wandering possessions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and silently he bared the lid of his quiver,","st":"29.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking a jutting shaft, and drew the accustomed string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"curving his bow with the back-bent trail of his palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, propping the very tip of the iron about the bow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brought the blood-red ox-gut string near his neighboring breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the straight-running shaft was turned aside from Dionysus,","st":"29.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"since Zeus restrained it, and the thigh of fair-garlanded"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hymenaeus was scored, blood-dipped, by the winged arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the wandering shaft, lifted aloft, escape Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flying with a whistle, the companion of an unsparing breeze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he made womanish the cast of the darting arrow,","st":"29.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blinded the murderous far-shooting of Melaneus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Paphian turned aside the barbs of the missile,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[17,17],[20,20],[22,28],[33,37]]},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to her brother Dionysus in his longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and turned the shaft so much from the skin as when a mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"drives off a wandering fly from her child still slumbering,","st":"29.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"gently waving the edge of her robe across his face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And appearing near, Hymenaeus showed the savage wound"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his flesh through his reddening thigh to Bacchus beside him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shedding a lovely tear beneath his brows, so that he might perceive"},{"sp":"N","t":"the helping right hand of evil-averting Dionysus,","st":"29.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"needing a life-sustaining healer; and he, holding"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hymenaeus by his white hand, set him upon his own car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leading him away from the many-voiced tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"set him down, sluggish, upon the shadowy ground beside a neighboring oak,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with heavy head; and as Apollo groaned for Hyacinthus","st":"29.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the sharp man-slaying discus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming the breath of the storm of jealous Zephyrus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so Dionysus too tore his hair again and again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weeping with tearless eyes over Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the barbs of the arrow standing outside the flesh","st":"29.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"he took welcome courage; and handling the two-colored wound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"white turning red, from the bloody thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew out the tip of the arrow with sparing palms."},{"sp":"N","t":"And watching the tears of the lamenting youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was angry at them both, at Ares and at Melaneus;","st":"29.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wiping away the sweet sweat of Hymenaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he poured a hidden voice from reproaching lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"A bull killed Ampelus, Ares will destroy Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that he had killed all the warriors I armed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and left this one alone unwounded; for in the wars","st":"29.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"what grief routs me if a Cabeirus is cut down?"},{"sp":"N","t":"When would a Satyr's wound, when would it ever stir Bacchus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let cluster-tending Silenus fall; let the swarm of Bassarids"},{"sp":"N","t":"wander; only let me see this boy unharmed."},{"sp":"N","t":"May the glorious archer be gracious; if Aristaeus fell,","st":"29.115","hvShift5":[[1,6],[12,7],[28,35]]},{"sp":"N","t":"what grief would ever be mine for him who calls"},{"sp":"N","t":"the travail of his own bee better than the fine-dripping harvest?"},{"sp":"N","t":"It was not fated for me, perhaps, ever to escape grief for a boy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because soon again I shall groan for this boy perished."},{"sp":"N","t":"What heavy envy has assailed us both? If it is lawful to say,","st":"29.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera, watching Bacchus with a jealous eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the young reaper of the dark-skinned race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grudging the youth and Lyaeus in his longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed furious Ares to strike Hymenaeus with a shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a bastard Indian look no one knew,","st":"29.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might grieve the mind of unhappy-loving Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But stretching a shaft or drawing blood-red bows"},{"sp":"N","t":"I arm myself against the lying Melaneus, so that I may exact"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vengeance owed for lovely Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"If you die, Hymenaeus, leaving Enyo unaccomplished,","st":"29.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"I withdraw from the war and lift the thyrsus no more."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall leave all the foes alive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having mowed one man only, your slayer Melaneus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades did not kill you, even if he is angry at Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious, Cytherea; after the bold son of Myrrha","st":"29.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"unmerciful Ares has driven off another gentle Adonis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has driven him off and touched his rosy skin, and through his thigh"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gore of the Loves has just now gushed again upon the ground;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but doing favor to your Dionysus in his longing"},{"sp":"N","t":"send me here your brother Phoebus, skilled in the toil-releasing","st":"29.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"art, and he will heal the boy. Hold back, my voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave Phoebus in Olympus untroubled, lest I stir him"},{"sp":"N","t":"by reminding him of the lovely wound of Hyacinthus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Send me, if you wish, Paeon; let him come;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has no share of longings, he is a stranger to the Loves.","st":"29.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have looked upon another pattern of wound; for in the wars"},{"sp":"N","t":"one man is reddened by the point, struck in the flank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another has the labor of his palm from a sword, another"},{"sp":"N","t":"from a shaft in the side, another through the ear; but I was wounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"together with Hymenaeus, having a deadly wound in my heart.\"","st":"29.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and was shaken, glancing sidelong with slanting eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the wound of graceful Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And winding the Euian flower this way and that about his thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"handling the two-colored wound, white turning red,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brought the boy back to life with his healing ivy,","st":"29.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling evil-averting wine upon Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when swift-working rennet, hastening to curdle milk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that the goatherd may make it ready, set firm,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[12,2],[14,5],[23,7],[29,13],[40,15],[43,39],[49,43],[53,48]]},{"sp":"N","t":"having rounded it in the pattern of a basket, with a wheel-shaped base,","hvShift5":[[4,0],[6,16],[7,20],[14,26],[16,54],[19,64],[22,67],[40,68],[48,70]]},{"sp":"N","t":"stirring it, takes away the moisture of the snowy dew;","st":"29.160","hvShift5":[[3,5],[15,9],[17,12],[20,23],[27,32],[29,35],[32,36],[48,40],[50,43],[52,49],[53,52],[70,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"so he healed the blood-red wound with Phoebean art;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the youth, sound again, held a recovered war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having found the pain-healing hand of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing his air-roaming far-shooting shaft to the mark"},{"sp":"N","t":"he curved his bows again, and aiming with his missile","st":"29.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave a wound in exchange to the arrow-shooting Melaneus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold boy rushed on; and following Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck men continually and no longer left Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when a shadowy shape of a man, creeping breathless,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, unscored, travels running with the man,","st":"29.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and always follows him as he hastens, and when he stands"},{"sp":"N","t":"stands, and when he sits sits beside him, and at table"},{"sp":"N","t":"feasts with him as a fellow-traveler with mimic palms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so the boy stayed running beside wine-dark Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Dionysus cease from battle; but piercing","st":"29.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"a man through the middle he lifted him, pinned upon his thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright, soaring aloft, and in the airy path"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising the Indian he showed him to jealous Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And accomplishing Enyo with threefold surnames"},{"sp":"N","t":"divine Aristaeus, Agreus taught of Ares,","st":"29.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought as Nomius, shaking his crook in his hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the far-shooting bridegroom of Autonoe; and in the tumults,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a bow, he imitated his own glorious-bowed father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the overbearing courage of the arrow-shooting mother who bore him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cyrene the former daughter of Hypseus; and fearless Agreus","st":"29.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"took a dread-raving foe alive in bonds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hunting him like a beast; and with accustomed hand he lifted"},{"sp":"N","t":"a heavy stone, the destroyer of his adversaries, such as he had propped"},{"sp":"N","t":"to press out the poured travail of the rich olive;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he terrified the proud foes with his accustomed whirling,","st":"29.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking that bronze, which, brandishing in his palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had used to drive off the maddened stings of the roaming bee."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fire-strong citizens of Thracian Samos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two children of Lemnian Cabeiro, raved in Bacchic frenzy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their eyes shot forth kindred sparks,","st":"29.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the vapor of the reddening fire of their father Hephaestus.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[11,4],[28,31]]},{"sp":"N","t":"They had a car of adamant; and about it the colts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the beaten dust with bronze hoof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belched a harsh whinnying from their throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom their father Hephaestus wrought with inimitable art,","st":"29.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"breathing a threatening torch from their teeth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"just as for Aeetes too, the mighty ruler of the Colchians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shaped a yoked pair of bronze-footed bulls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making hot yoke-straps and a fiery plough-pole.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[12,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and in his right hand he lifted the Lemnian spear, which his father's anvil wrought,","st":"29.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and upon his well-grown thighs"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hung a light-bearing sword; and if any man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting some little stone with the very tips of his nails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the fire-running back of the whetted knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-moving sparks were shot from the iron.","st":"29.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eurymedon drove, and with a fire-struck bridle"},{"sp":"N","t":"steered the fiery jaw of his iron-footed horses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Alcon fitted his hand to a smoky missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the revel-torch of his father's Hecate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And shaking the boss of a high-crested helmet","st":"29.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dictaean Corybants marched into the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"goaded into the fray; and with emulous bronze"},{"sp":"N","t":"naked swords clashed upon the beaten ox-hides"},{"sp":"N","t":"with answering leaps; and they imitated the rhythm"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a shield-bearing dance with the coiling throb of their feet,","st":"29.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving for Ares. And of the mountain-haunting herdsmen"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indian offspring was cut down by Curetic iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one man slipped head first into the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the bellowing of the heavy-thundering ox-hide."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Bassarid, lifting the flower-loving spear of Enyo,","st":"29.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled it; and many male heads of the Bacchus-less"},{"sp":"N","t":"race were cut down by a female thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with shaggy palm lifting a crested peak,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leneus armed himself, cutting off the topmost heads of a mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a jagged point against the adversaries.","st":"29.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Bacchant shouted around; and vine arrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"were whirled in the palms of ivy-bearing women."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, weaving a melody both for Ares and for Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eupetale armed herself, and with a cluster-loving leaf"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent the goading ivy, the crusher of iron,","st":"29.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"destroying the Indian race with a woody cluster."},{"sp":"N","t":"And routing the cloud of the foes with her man-breaking thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cluster-loving Terpsichore skipped into the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the heavy-roaring cymbals of twin bronze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not so much did Heracles drive off the Stymphalian birds with his boom,","st":"29.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the heavy-thundering bronze, as Terpsichore drove off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the army of the Indians, beating out the warlike Echo of the dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"And heavy-kneed Trygie was left behind, apart from the throng,"},{"sp":"N","t":"last of all, and fixed her feet in fear; nor did any"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Sileni stay by her; they left her there alone,","st":"29.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified, needing a helper; and she stretched out her hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"to deep-drinking Maron, and Maron refused the old woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because she checked the dances of the unmixed-wine-loving Corybants"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of the Satyrs; and he prayed always to the gods that the useless"},{"sp":"N","t":"old woman might be subdued beneath the spear of Deriades;","st":"29.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Calyce fought, standing beside Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad. And Oenone was whirled with the throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"of trembling drunkenness as she ran ahead; and weighed down in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"her knees labored, and the swollen locks"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the unmixed-wine-loving girl's head were whirled about.","st":"29.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was heavy-thundering groaning; and in an emulous tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"Astraeis pursued Staphyle, and Celaeneus pursued Calyce."},{"sp":"N","t":"And spear-wielding Morrheus drove off the phalanx of the Sileni,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck with his ox-axe; and at a single shout of the driver"},{"sp":"N","t":"Astraeus was shaken, Maron fled, Leneus sank down,","st":"29.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"the three children of shaggy-haired Silenus, who apart from a bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsown, self-delivered, ran up from his mother the field."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Doryclus terrified lovely Lycaste &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"To them the god brought help, and sprinkled drugs"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the wounds of the freshly cut women; and Gorge,","st":"29.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"worn by the iron of Enyalius, unveiled, he saved by the tip of her foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding her foot tight with a vine bond of a shoot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he quenched the freshly rushing ichor of Eupetale with wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and soothed the poured blood of Staphyle with a song;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he healed the wounded palm of Myrto with myrtle,","st":"29.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"and saved Calybe, drawing the shaft from her shoulder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stopped the pain of Nysa's freshly wounded face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the drink of the vat upon her blood-red wound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"anointing her cheek this way and that with white gypsum;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with tearless eyes he groaned over Lycaste.","st":"29.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when thyrsus-mad Dionysus had soothed the pains"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Bassarids with his art, he fought in a greater battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Bassarid, gripped by the leap of wit-robbing madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a raving Enyo, went after the Indian Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"about you, Lydian god; and from the lock of the Bacchant","st":"29.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-moving, unburning fire blazed down her neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"And rousing the unequally yoked swarm of mighty champions"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flute crashed out the host-gathering Echo of Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with twin palms of the crash-loving Corybants"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rims of the double-beaten ox-hide were struck,","st":"29.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cymbals rattled, and, changing its song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweet-singing Panian pipe made melody of battles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the phalanxes of the adversaries roared; and abundant"},{"sp":"N","t":"winged arrows whistled up from the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"The bow twanged, the stone boomed, the trumpet bellowed.","st":"29.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when they came to the ford, where, carried in its trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hydaspes reddened its white water with a drunken stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then indeed Bacchus shouted from heavy-crashing throats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as loud as a swarm of nine thousand of Enyo roars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread din of mouths of one tongue; and unsteady,","st":"29.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing toward the yellow stream, the Indians sank down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others in the plain; and the army of Bacchus was divided,"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing the foes both on the floor and in Hydaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gasping with parched thirst, when the dawn of the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose to its middle, and the hot wayfarer trembled","st":"29.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the midday whip of blazing Helios."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god challenged the lord of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a threatening word from a raving throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"What fear is there? If the leader of the Indians bears the race of a river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have got my blood from heaven; and overbearing Deriades","st":"29.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"is worse than Lyaeus by as much as Hydaspes is than Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if I wish, I stand near the clouds; and if I wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my straight-running shaft shall reach as far as Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you think greatly of yourself, holding a horn-drawing shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you can, fight in the front against ox-horned Dionysus.\"","st":"29.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the warriors bellowed with a roar;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one strove with another, fighting beside Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And gentle Pan fought with goat's feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scoring sharply the whole flank of the archer"},{"sp":"N","t":"he tore open the belly of Melaneus with his whetted hoof,","st":"29.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"demanding vengeance for the wound of Hymenaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might lighten the fire-sealed distress"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Dionysus lamenting with tearless eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And raging Iobacchus ran upon the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and touched the clouds and grasped Olympus with his hands,","st":"29.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"at one time lengthening his long body, a neighbor of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fixed his sole in the earth and struck the air with his head."},{"sp":"N","t":"And upon them as they fought came the star Hesperus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosing the foundations of the Indian-slaying fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beside sleeping Ares, at the nod of Rhea,","st":"29.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"there stood a guile-weaving vision of a dream, embroidering phantoms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a word in a bastard shadowy shape:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Ares, Ares, do you sleep, unhappy in love, lying alone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bronze-tunicked? And Hephaestus holds your Paphian a second time"},{"sp":"N","t":"high upon his bed, your former Aphrodite,","st":"29.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and has driven the Grace, his jealous bride, out of his halls;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drawing his ancient wife back to a returning wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros himself shot refusing Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing favor to his father Hephaestus. And Athena herself too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"untaught of longings, persuaded great Zeus,","st":"29.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"the guile-devising maiden, so that she might escape Hephaestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembering the bastard beds of ground-reared Hymenaeans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after the doom of the former Erechtheus she should rear"},{"sp":"N","t":"at a male breast another, younger son of the field."},{"sp":"N","t":"Wake, and going to the Thracian floor of the cliff","st":"29.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold your Cytherea within his accustomed Lemnos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold how the fellow-traveling swarm of Loves has garlanded"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gateways of Paphos and the foundations of Cyprus with flowers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hear the daughters of Byblos singing of Aphrodite"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the new love of a returning wedlock.","st":"29.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares, you have been robbed of your Cypris; for the slow one"},{"sp":"N","t":"has outrun swift man-slaying Ares. Sing you too"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Aphrodite joined to fiery Hephaestus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And set foot on Sicily, and entreat me the Cyclopes"},{"sp":"N","t":"who stand beside the furnace; for they too are skilled","st":"29.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the noblest-working Hephaestus, jealous of wise works,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they will prepare a guile for you, and will make one"},{"sp":"N","t":"younger, like your ancient bond, so that you too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressing them both in guileful fetters none can escape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"may bind the thief of your marriage in your avenging bond,","st":"29.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping shambling Hephaestus tight to Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and all the gods of Olympus shall praise you"},{"sp":"N","t":"for having hunted in bonds the plunderer of your bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Wake, and become a guile-weaver yourself; wake, and care"},{"sp":"N","t":"for your ravished bride. What are the evils of Deriades to you?","st":"29.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"Softly, between us, lest Phaethon hear the word.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she flew away. And at once, shaking off his slumber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the morning light of Dawn freshly scored,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hot Ares leapt up, rousing Terror and Panic"},{"sp":"N","t":"to yoke his blood-red, swift-running chariot; and they obeyed","st":"29.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"their father as he hurried; and with a crook-toothed bridle"},{"sp":"N","t":"much-dreaded Panic, binding tight the jaw of the horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped their slavish, fettered necks in the harness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bound the yoke-strap about; and Ares mounted the car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Terror drove, steering his father's wagon,","st":"29.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried from Lebanon to Paphos, and from Cythera"},{"sp":"N","t":"he turned the restless chariot toward the land of horned Cyprus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"often, often he looked at Lemnos, and more than the others"},{"sp":"N","t":"he watched the fire-breathing forge in jealousy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tracking Cypris with a running, jealous sole,","st":"29.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"to see if he might behold her beside the furnaces of Hephaestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing as before, and he feared lest the smoke"},{"sp":"N","t":"should dim the face of Aphrodite as she blackened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after Lemnos he ran even to heaven, so that with iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"he might raise a bridal war among the blessed ones,","st":"29.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Zeus and Phaethon and Hephaestus and Athena."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXX"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the thirtieth, after the nether house of necessity, Eurymedon sends Tectaphus cut down to Hades."},{"sp":"N","t":"So jealous, heavy-wrathful Ares ran to the seven-zoned heaven.","st":"30.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"And dancing into the fray"},{"sp":"N","t":"bold Dionysus assailed the blazing host,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now leaping among the foremost with an earth-shaking throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now in the middle of the champions; and with his javelin-hurling thyrsus","st":"30.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reaped the first-fruits of the dark fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and heartened the Satyrs against the Ares of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raged, cutting down the tribes of the hostile phalanx,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Bacchus saw that Ares had left the war of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one strove with another. And entrusting to Dionysus","st":"30.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"the furious right wing of the cluster-bearing tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aristaeus ran the left horn of the fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Morrheus, still watching the servants of Bromius"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting with leaves and a flowery missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"broke into a much-wondering voice to witless Deriades:","st":"30.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Deriades, what is this wonder? My warriors fall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by thyrsi and paltry leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the shieldless destroy the weapon-bearers; and the Bassarids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbending, struck with axes and double-edged knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remain unwounded. If it is lawful to say,","st":"30.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you too, scepter-bearer, leave your bronze-forged point"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lift a murderous wine-dark thyrsus, because the foes"},{"sp":"N","t":"play the champion far better with clusters than with iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"Never have I seen such a pattern of battle; and paltry"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsi, when hurled, are better than javelins.","st":"30.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"Give me too a green shaft to whirl; for our"},{"sp":"N","t":"arrows have been conquered by an unwarlike fennel-stalk;"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me yellow sandals to wear, because even"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbreakable greaves have yielded to buskins."},{"sp":"N","t":"What more, if I have a bronze shield, when women","st":"30.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"unarmored play the champion better, and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirl cymbals, and warriors sink down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the helmet has given way to garlands and the breastplate to a fawnskin?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Often, coming against unwoundable Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I thought to tear open his unbreakable flank,","st":"30.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending well-aimed spears, and as it touched Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sharp-pointed, unbending bronze of the javelins was bent.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the bold king smiled, and at his son-in-law"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched slanting eyes with a silence that heralded anger;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he shrieked out a threatening cry at him:","st":"30.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why do you tremble at unarmored Dionysus, childish Morrheus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sweet is he who fears the playful Enyo of Satyrs.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he heartened his son-in-law with a fearless threat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And monstrous Morrheus armed himself against the champions of Bromius;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he wounded Eurymedon, scoring the middle of his groin","st":"30.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his blood-red spear; and darting through the thigh"},{"sp":"N","t":"the raving point cut the rich flesh, draining its color;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fell to the ground with sinking knee. And bronze-tunicked"},{"sp":"N","t":"Alcon did not neglect his fallen brother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but came as champion to him who was hard pressed, lifting his spear","st":"30.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his well-wheeling shield; and he covered the whole warrior,"},{"sp":"N","t":"towering the man's body about with his shield, and shaking"},{"sp":"N","t":"this way and that at the adversaries his back-turning point"},{"sp":"N","t":"brother defended brother; and standing over the wounded man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a lion over its cubs, he roared from his throat,","st":"30.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a Corybantic voice from a raving lip."},{"sp":"N","t":"And watching him wheeling with knowing sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"monstrous Morrheus, of like nature to Typhon, raved,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed against the two brothers, so that their mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cabeiro might weep for two children destroyed,","st":"30.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shield-bearer of his fallen brother Cabeirus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut down by one iron in a single dawn."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he would have given them both to an equally weighted destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Eurymedon, stretching a forced breath through his lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"called upon his Lemnian father with a cry:","st":"30.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"O father, fire-breathing lord of the labor-working art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant me the favor owed of old, when alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"threshing Deo snatched three-crested Sicily,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gifts for the first sight of Persephoneia hidden away,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and checked your western blowing bellows","st":"30.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your broad forge and your snatching fire-tongs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I terrified her, shielding my father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the helper of your anvil; and from me"},{"sp":"N","t":"the black air is warmed by your Sicilian spark."},{"sp":"N","t":"Save me your son, whom savage Morrheus has wounded.\"","st":"30.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and fiery Hephaestus, rushing from heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled about his son a many-cleft leaping fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spinning a fiery missile in his palm; and about the neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Morrheus a self-coiling, intelligent torch was whirled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling his neck with a necklace of fire-heated necessity,","st":"30.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling about; and after the fiery crown of his throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping to the farthest sole with a mounting throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it wove a fire-plaited cord about the champion's foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking on the ground a steady radiance with a footman's leap;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his head was heated as his helmet was kindled.","st":"30.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he would have been laid low, struck by the flaming missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Hydaspes, the father of Deriades, defended him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he sat watching the fighting high upon a rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull-natured, holding a bastard look in mortal-seeming shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he brought him back to life, pouring counter-blowing water,","st":"30.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling the hot breath of his fire-struck face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wiping away the ashy defilement of his helmet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and snatching Morrheus he cloaked him in a gloomy mist,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding his covered limbs in a purple cloud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the light-bearing lame god should kill him,","st":"30.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting the deadly Lemnian fire as it caught,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, after the death of the former, old child-loving Hydaspes"},{"sp":"N","t":"should see yet another son-in-law of Deriades destroyed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and weep for the doom of Morrheus together with Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And torch-bearing Hephaestus pursued all the warriors","st":"30.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing about his freshly wounded son, and lifting"},{"sp":"N","t":"his son high upon his shoulder he propped him against a neighboring oak,"},{"sp":"N","t":"apart from the tumult, and brought the fallen man back to life,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling life-bringing drugs upon his wounded groin."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was Morrheus forgetful of the earlier battle;","st":"30.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he armed himself again, having fled the fiery war"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the flashing champion and the smoky point;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and overtaking Phlogius, the much-turning son of Strophius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he killed him, the dancer of leap-loving Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who beside the banquets of tearless Lyaeus","st":"30.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"used to whirl the many-turning fingers of answering hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, shaking out the death of Phaethon with intelligent hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the feasters shed unaccustomed tears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weeping over the destruction of a lying Phaethon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and weaving a new smoky, self-rolling dance","st":"30.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave a wretched grief to griefless Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seeing him skipping, spear-wielding Morrheus said:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This is a different dance from the one you wove beside the table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching out a laughing dance beside the mixing-bowl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whence do you weave a groaning dance after battle?","st":"30.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if the sting of the dancing of Dionysus holds you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"perform your rites for Hades, and you have no need of gypsum,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the self-dyed, dusty circles of your face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you wish, dance beside lamentation-loving Lethe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let unsmiling Persephone delight in your song.\"","st":"30.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, glorying, and ran on like a tempest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified the Sileni. And with irresistible knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"shield-brandishing Tectaphus followed him, whom once Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"bound and hid within a hollow pit."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did he find how to flee doom a second time; for under necessity","st":"30.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"who can ever keep hostile fate from a man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when pitiless, all-subduing Fate commands him to die?"},{"sp":"N","t":"For guile did not find how to save Tectaphus as he died,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who then, raging, pursued the army of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting down the play-loving limbs of the well-horned Satyrs;","st":"30.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he reaped the throat of battle-rousing Pylaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struck the brow of Onthyrius with unsparing knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crushed broad-breasted Pithos with the iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he would have killed another throng of the Bacchoi one upon another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but swift Eurymedon saw him, and stood against him,","st":"30.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking in his hand the two-mouthed Corybantic blade;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he crushed the top of his brow; and as the head was split in two"},{"sp":"N","t":"an upright pipe of bloody dew gushed up;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the champion fell to the ground, and, sprinkling the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolled half-dead, and groaned for the ancient misery","st":"30.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the ground-dug chamber and the threads of a younger Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, remembering still the guileful love-charm"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his evil-averting daughter, he roared with a plaintive voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he wailed his tears ran down with the gore:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My mother and my nurse, guile-weaving, ill-wedded girl,","st":"30.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"why did you not come near me, when I came near destruction?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Why now have you not helped me again, fearless girl?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where has your life-bearing love-charm gone? Do you indeed keep"},{"sp":"N","t":"faith with your father living and not with him dying?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If guile can bring a man back from Hades,","st":"30.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"seek me another, better guile, seek a cunning"},{"sp":"N","t":"counsel against death, so that after the nether hollows"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may escape the gates of Hades even in wars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if there is a homeward path from the pit none returns from.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he scarcely spoke, and his voice obeyed no more.","st":"30.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing her father freshly wounded from high upon the tower"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pitiable girl of varied tears, aloft, gushed out"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mourning cry; and she shamed her crooked hair with dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"baring her breast as her tunic was torn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she beat her head; and to her father past all cure,","st":"30.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though he heard, she uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My son and father, heavy-fated, of your milk-bearing girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"today, upon your breathless lips as you lie dead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what other life-bringing milk have I, with which, wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may bring your soul back again into you?","st":"30.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"What other helping breast shall I hold out again?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that I could beguile Aidoneus too."},{"sp":"N","t":"One other prize is kept for you, father; for I shall not leave you"},{"sp":"N","t":"alone among the dead; and do you receive from your slain daughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of her neck too, after the milk of her former breast.","st":"30.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"Come, guardians of Deriades, and instead of that pit"},{"sp":"N","t":"show me another recess within the earth, where going"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may make my dead father live again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hades is not like the guards, that I may accomplish"},{"sp":"N","t":"another toil-releasing guile, the helper of my father.","st":"30.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"I longed for that murderous sword, so that, heavy in soul,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I might be subdued, slipping upon the father-slaying iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"You there, who cut off the head of our father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill Aerie too after Tectaphus, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'He laid low both father and child with a single knife.'\"","st":"30.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, shedding tears; and the labor grew greater."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Enyo fanned up both armies. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Morrheus killed Dasyllius of Taenarum with his sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Amyclaean citizen who never cast away his ox-hide shield"},{"sp":"N","t":"before the foes, unshaken before his adversaries,","st":"30.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving his spear against the bone of his right jaw."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he killed mountain-running Alcimacheia, who under a single ordinance"},{"sp":"N","t":"had manhood and beauty surpassing the youth of her own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of Harpalion her cluster-rich father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who was bold and went even into the house of Hera","st":"30.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting ivy, which the Argive goddess hated"},{"sp":"N","t":"as much as she loved the accustomed reddening pomegranate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she lashed the well-made image with a wine-dark thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the bronze body with a vine cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dishonoring the heavy-wrathful stepmother of Lyaeus.","st":"30.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did god-fighting Lemnian Alcimacheia escape"},{"sp":"N","t":"the frightful wrath of Hera when she laid hold of her; but in a foreign"},{"sp":"N","t":"land she was buried, and after the wars she did not see"},{"sp":"N","t":"her father Harpalion a second time, nor did she see her homeland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lemnos, the bridal chamber of Jason's Hypsipyle;","st":"30.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"but among strangers she was covered in loose dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging an avenging doom. Ah, greatly wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she missed Harpalion, and was robbed of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was furious Morrheus sated when the god-sporting maenad"},{"sp":"N","t":"Alcimache was cut down; but Codone too,","st":"30.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"who dwelt in Elis, the Olympian floor of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the stream of Alpheius the garland-loving river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he killed, still a maiden. Be gracious, Fates;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not pity the locks of her withering head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the rosy beam of her dust-covered face;","st":"30.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, seeing on her breast a bosom like a wheel-shaped"},{"sp":"N","t":"apple, did he pity it, the unbending goad of her girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did he respect the cut of her deepening thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but killed so great a beauty untimely; and wounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fell upon the ground; and pursuing numberless","st":"30.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-robed maenads, helmet-glancing Morrheus killed them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reaped Eurypyle and Sterope and Soe with his knife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cut down Staphyle, and wounded reddening"},{"sp":"N","t":"Gigarto, and piercing above her rosy breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"he purpled the bosom of Melictaena with murderous iron.","st":"30.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the envious Telchines marched into the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one holding a vast fir, another a whole cornel bush"},{"sp":"N","t":"roots and all, another, breaking off the top of a headland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed it, and went to the war of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking a javelin-hurling stone in a maddened arm.","st":"30.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"And shifting Hera, bearing down upon Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave strength and courage to proud Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and granted him, as he played the champion, a light-bearing gleam"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the terror of his adversaries; and as the bearer armed himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood-red gleam of his Indian shield flashed,","st":"30.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a leaping flame blazed above the crest of his helmet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Bacchus trembled when he saw the boss"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fire-struck ox-hide shield of Deriades flashing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the air-roaming radiance of his kindled helmet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing him Dionysus was astonished, and did not dare","st":"30.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"to meet him, and, perceiving the guile of Hera as she armed herself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"withdrew from the fighting with refusing feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then the Indians went boldly to the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since Bromius had left the battle; and watching this"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades cut down the rank of the Bacchoi one upon another,","st":"30.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his spear on either side, turned back again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Iobacchus in distress went up to the ridge of the wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entrusted the hope of battle to the winds to scatter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling at the savage wrath of his stepmother. And Athena came"},{"sp":"N","t":"from heaven; for high-ruling Zeus had sent her as a messenger,","st":"30.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might draw her brother, fleeing, terrified with fear"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Hera, back into the fray with his purpose changed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she stood behind and gripped the yellow hair of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a grim goddess appearing to him alone; and from her face"},{"sp":"N","t":"her eyes shot forth a fiery sparkle;","st":"30.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and breathing intelligent sparks upon Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she spoke in anger with a reproaching, war-loving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Where do you flee, Dionysus? Why have you fear instead of tumult?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where are your valiant thyrsi and your vine arrows?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What word shall I cry to my Cronion about you?","st":"30.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"What lord of the Indians have I seen destroyed in the fighting?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades lives and Morrheus still makes war."},{"sp":"N","t":"What heavenly, inborn valor were you displaying?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did you set foot on Libya? Did you have the contest of Perseus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did you see the eye of stone-faced Stheno, or the hard-fighting","st":"30.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowing throat of Euryale herself?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did you behold the locks of viper-haired Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and did the gape of wide-scattered dragons run round you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Semele did not bear a fighting son; but the daughter of Acrisius"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore a worthy son to my Zeus, the Gorgon-slayer;","st":"30.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"for winged Perseus did not shake off my sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but honored Hermes the giver of his sandals."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have as a neighboring witness the stony beast of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"ask me Cepheus what the sickle of Perseus wrought;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and ask the east and the west; for on both sides,","st":"30.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Nereids tremble at the consort of Andromeda,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Hesperides sing of the reaper of Medusa."},{"sp":"N","t":"Fearless Aeacus was not like Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not flee Deriades, he did not tremble at the war of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Did the Arabian champion rout you again yesterday? Ever since that","st":"30.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to see furious Ares, the father of Lycurgus,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[32,33],[38,37]]},{"sp":"N","t":"crying aloud the feebleness of war-fleeing Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Your father and mine did not tremble at the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the Titan gods armed themselves against Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"What Orsiboe have you taken as plunder, the mistress of the Indians?","st":"30.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"Your Rhea has not seen Cheirobie won by the spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious, boast of Zeus; I shall not call you brother"},{"sp":"N","t":"while you flee Deriades and the race of unwarlike Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"But take your thyrsi and remember the battle again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fight in the front for your army, and as the Bacchants arm themselves","st":"30.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall see well-corseleted Athena fighting beside them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the aegis, the unwoundable weapon of Olympus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she breathed strength into Bromius; and he in his heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made bold, fought a second time, and entrusted all his hope"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the victory to come to Tritogeneia.","st":"30.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"There whom first, whom last did Bacchus kill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Athena, unsated of battles, heartened him?"},{"sp":"N","t":"He killed a hundred of the adversaries with his pitiless thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave many a much-turning wound, striking with his spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"or with the coils of plants or with a fair-shooted cluster,","st":"30.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"or hurling a stone, a rocky missile; and those who were struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"raved in Bacchic frenzy with a ringing, divine whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he wounded the left shoulder of Phringus with his sharp thyrsus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he, leaping, withdrew untouchable; and as he fled"},{"sp":"N","t":"Melisseus laid him low with his whetted ox-axe.","st":"30.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"And thyrsus-mad far-shooting Dionysus rushed upon Egretius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his Euian spear; and flying,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bacchic point of the spear whistled through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing to strike the man, and when Egretius fled"},{"sp":"N","t":"it assailed the Bolinges, and terrified the battle-rousing","st":"30.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arachotae into flight; and by an unmixed-wine-loving leaf"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread tribes of the spear-bold Salangi were cut down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the army of the shield-bearing Arienes was terrified;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[12,42]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursuing the champions of Phringus and Egretius"},{"sp":"N","t":"Euius terrified the whole Uatocoetan host;","st":"30.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and valiant Iobacchus dashed Lygus away"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the bloody tumult; and he wounded guile-weaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"Meilanion with his wine-dark thyrsus as he sat in a tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting at the Bassarids with hidden shafts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but hard-fighting Hera took him alive unharmed,","st":"30.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he had armed himself with guile and often assailed the Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"with secret wars; and always a rock hid him"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a high-headed plant, stolen away beneath its leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he shot at men with unmarked shafts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indians hurried away from the man-slaying tumult,","st":"30.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembling at the manhood of unconquered Dionysus."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the thirty-first Hera soothes Sleep against Cronides, and Persephone against Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"So Bacchus, struck by the spell of the Indian tumult,","st":"31.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran about the bosom of the Erythraean field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his golden locks beside his snowy cheeks."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera, swelling with envious cares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the topmost air with threatening sole,","st":"31.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching from there the army of the wide-scattered Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed by the man-slaying thyrsi of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another anger rose in her beside the Erythraean sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the much-plaited remains of Andromeda's bonds"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the stone in the sand, the grim monster of the Earth-shaker.","st":"31.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"And in grief she turned her eye aside, lest beside the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"she should see the bronze-forged sickle of Gorgon-slaying Perseus."},{"sp":"N","t":"For already, whirling his swift-kneed sole in the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perseus was ferried about the thirsty Libyan neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swimming on wings; and taking the wandering eye","st":"31.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the one-eyed, unsleeping old daughter of Phorcys"},{"sp":"N","t":"he entered the hard-to-tread cave, and reaping beside the rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hissing corn, the crooked first-fruits of her hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cut through the travailing throat of the Gorgon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reddened his sickle; and as Medusa was cut down","st":"31.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bathed his blood-dipped palm in a snaky dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting off her head; and bringing forth a child with the golden sword"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twice-bearing neck brought forth the horse's offspring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And envious anger seethed through the heart of Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she belched out jealousy at Perseus and at Dionysus.","st":"31.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"And she wished to charm both the eyes and the mind of Cronides"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a beguiling wedlock and into the wing of sweet Sleep"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawn on after the bed, so that by some guileful art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Zeus still slumbered, she might bear down upon Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she went to the gloomy all-receiving court of Hades;","st":"31.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she found Persephone, and cried with a guile-minded word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I call you most blessed, because you dwell far from the gods;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have not seen Semele dwelling within Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest I behold Dionysus, whom a human belly bore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the lightning after Zagreus,","st":"31.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"or lightening the thunderbolts with earthly palms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have been plundered, fruit-bearer; beside the corn-bearing Nile"},{"sp":"N","t":"they lead a revel for another instead of your mother Demeter"},{"sp":"N","t":"who bore the sheaf, and a bastard sheaf-bearing Deo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull-natured, horned, is proclaimed — Inachian Io.","st":"31.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Ares, whom I bore, whom a heavenly belly brought forth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my son, Ephialtes bound in an inglorious earthly chain"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hid, clamped within a jar;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did my consort, heavenly Zeus, help him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he saved the offspring of Semele from the flaming torches,","st":"31.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought Bacchus back to life from the thunderbolt while still an infant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a half-finished bastard son; but when Zagreus was cut down"},{"sp":"N","t":"with knives he did not fight in the front for the heavenly Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"This has stirred me the more, that Cronides gave the vault"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the stars to Semele as bride-gift, and Tartarus to Persephoneia;","st":"31.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"heaven is kept for Apollo, Hermes dwells in heaven;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but you have this house full of gloom."},{"sp":"N","t":"What more, if, holding the lying shape of a serpent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he plundered the bond of your unplundered maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if after the bed he was to destroy your travail?","st":"31.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lord Zeus has in Olympus a house full of stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he gave his brother the ruler of the waters the salt water as a prize,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave misty darkness to your consort as a house."},{"sp":"N","t":"But arm your Erinyes against wine-dark Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I behold a mortal as bastard scepter-bearer of Olympus.","st":"31.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"Respect the bedfellow of Zeus who entreats you, respect Deo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"respect pure Themis who entreats you, so that the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"may breathe a little while Dionysus is shaken;"},{"sp":"N","t":"be my avenger in my grief, because Cronides"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave nectar to Bacchus and the gore of Enyo to Ares.","st":"31.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"And let not Athens hymn a new Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor let him obtain an honor equal to Eleusinian Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him not exchange the rites of the earlier Iacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him not dishonor the basket of Demeter with his harvest.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she confounded the whole mind of Persephoneia,","st":"31.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"her face wetted with manufactured tears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"chattering wheedling words. And goddess nodded to goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave her Megaera as companion, so that, bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"a blighting eye, she might accomplish the mind of jealous Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, darting off on stormy sandal,","st":"31.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"was lifted three times, and on the fourth reached Ganges;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she showed the dead Indian throng to unsmiling Megaera"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sweat of the army and the manhood of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Megaera, seeing the Indian-slaying labors of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grudged them in jealousy, even more than heavenly Hera.","st":"31.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera rejoiced in her heart; and laughing sardonically"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the serpent-haired goddess she broke into a downcast voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"So do the new kings of Olympus play the champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so do the bastards of Zeus hurl the spear; and from Semele"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus brought forth a single child, so that he might destroy","st":"31.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the gentle and blameless Indians; but let unjust Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Bacchus learn how great the strength of Megaera is."},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas, what a lawless mind high-ruling Zeus has;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he does not make war on the unjust Tyrrhenians, because, having learned"},{"sp":"N","t":"thievish, violent ordinances, upon ill-guesting ships","st":"31.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"they sail the Sicilian sea as snatchers of others' goods;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not kill the race of impious Dryopes, whose life is spears"},{"sp":"N","t":"and slaughter; but he has killed the Indians who cared for piety,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom perhaps Themis, of whom all speak, midwifed at her breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas, what a lawless mind he has; for a mortal man","st":"31.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"has burned so great and such an immortal Hydaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mortal man has burned him whom heavenly Zeus begot.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she flew through the upper air; and Megaera in silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath the neighboring lair-holding floor of the Caucasian cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having exchanged the dread snaky shape of her limbs,","st":"31.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"stayed there like an owl in form, until she should see"},{"sp":"N","t":"great Zeus slumbering; for so queen Hera had told her."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera herself went, devising, to the western water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Chremetes, where the heavy old man labors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Libyan Atlas bowed beneath the heavenly whirl,","st":"31.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she sought Iris, the bedfellow of Zephyrus unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the messenger of Zeus when he hastens, so that she might make her"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wind-footed envoy from the air to shadowy Sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And calling her she soothed her with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Iris, golden-winged bride of plant-increasing Zephyrus,","st":"31.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"mother of Eros blessed in childbirth, with stormy sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten to go to the gloomy Western house of Sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"seek him too about sea-beaten Lemnos; and if you find him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell him to charm the uncharmed eyes of Cronides"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn, so that I may help the Indians.","st":"31.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"But change your body, and having the misshapen form"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the black-girdled goddess Night, the mother of Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"become dark with a lying name, since I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"change my shape into Themis, into Cytherea, into Artemis"},{"sp":"N","t":"with answering limbs, whenever there is need of necessity.","st":"31.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"And promise him wedlock with Pasithea, for whose beauty's sake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing, he may accomplish my need; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"that a woman-mad man will do everything in hope.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke the golden-winged goddess Iris flew away,"},{"sp":"N","t":"surveying the air, and toward Paphos, toward the land of Cyprus","st":"31.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stretched an unwandering eye, and more, high above Byblos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she watched the fair-wedded water of Assyrian Adonis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the roaming track of wandering Sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she found him beside the slopes of bridal Orchomenus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there again he stayed, whirling a mind-wandering step,","st":"31.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"haunting the lovely gateways of Pasithea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And, changing her body, unseen Iris turned about"},{"sp":"N","t":"and put on the unknown look of dark Night;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the guile-weaver came near to Sleep; and like a mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"she cried a deceiving voice with mind-stealing whispers:","st":"31.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My child, how long will Cronides dishonor me?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Is it not enough that Phaethon forces me, that Dawn herself"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoots at me and the morning-born pursues me?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus has planted a bastard son, so that he may put my Sleep to shame."},{"sp":"N","t":"One mortal shames me and my son; for all night long","st":"31.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"kindling a light-bearing brand with a mystic spark"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus dims me, and, waking, harries you."},{"sp":"N","t":"Sleep, why are you called all-subduer? No longer do you charm"},{"sp":"N","t":"waking men, because the bastard radiance of earthly Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"has conquered my revel; for with torches brighter than ours","st":"31.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hides the flames of the stars."},{"sp":"N","t":"One light-bearing mortal shames me, because he covers"},{"sp":"N","t":"the beam of my Selene, great though she is."},{"sp":"N","t":"I stand in awe of the morning-born who laughs at Mist,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because I wear a bastard nightly radiance; for by an alien,","st":"31.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"manufactured Phaethon I, Night, shine by day."},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you, dear boy, be angry for me under a twofold ordinance"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the mystic Satyrs and at unsleeping Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and charm the uncharmed eye of high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to your grieving mother, do a favor to Hera,","st":"31.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn, so that she may help the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom the Satyrs rout and Bacchus still stirs."},{"sp":"N","t":"Sleep, why are you called all-subduer? If you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turn your eye toward me, and beside seven-gated Thebes"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall see Cronion again waking all night long;","st":"31.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"loose the recklessness of unjust Zeus; for Amphitryon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an iron-tunicked migrant far from his own chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"makes war; while Zeus lies beside Alcmene in the inmost room,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsated, holding a three-moon bridal darkness."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me not see a fourth night with Zeus awake.","st":"31.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, child, arm yourself against Cronion, lest again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest again he fill out another nine-circled darkness."},{"sp":"N","t":"Remember the earlier Mnemosyne; lying beside her"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stayed nine nights, keeping an unsleeping eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the many-childed sting of sleepless wedlock.","st":"31.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"Another all-subduing god of equal wing, like Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"little Eros, conquered Cronides with a small shaft."},{"sp":"N","t":"And pity the dark-skinned earth-born race of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor; for they are of the same color as your mother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"save the dark-skinned ones, dark-winged god; and do not harry","st":"31.175","hvShift5":[[0,0],[13,26],[27,41],[30,43]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth, the age-mate of my father, from whom alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"all who dwell in Olympus have sprung."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not tremble at Cronides, when his consort Hera is gracious;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not tremble at Semele, whom her own bedfellow burned."},{"sp":"N","t":"Neither the fiery lightning can match you,","st":"31.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the heavy-thundering thunder of clashing clouds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only shake your wings for me, and upon his unbending bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus stays unshaken as long a time, Sleep, as you command."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have heard that you long for one of the Graces; but keeping"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sting of the bridal chamber in your heart, beware, and do not harry","st":"31.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother of Pasithea, Hera the yoking chamber-servant."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you dwell beside Tethys on the Leucadian rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"help Deriades, whom Indian Hydaspes plowed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"keep faith with your neighbor, since your resounding neighbor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"roaring Ocean, was the forefather of Deriades.","st":"31.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded him. And as though hearing his mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sleep was stirred, and swore to charm the eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"of sleepless Zeus even until the course of a third Dawn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Iris asked him only to fetter Cronion"},{"sp":"N","t":"to sleep for a single course of the morning-born.","st":"31.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Sleep stayed there, awaiting his fair-wedded hour."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the swift goddess Iris flew back returning;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hastening she announced a blameless word to the queen."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she flew through the air with stormy sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wove another guile, how she might come near Zeus","st":"31.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the cestus, the mind-charming girdle of longing."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she sought the Paphian; and above Lebanon she found"},{"sp":"N","t":"Assyrian Aphrodite alone in the wilderness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated; for the Graces had set out for the varied flowers"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the gardens, the springtime dancers of Orchomenus,","st":"31.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"one plucking Cilician saffron, another longing to bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"balsam and a planting of Indian reed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another the fragrant grass of roses."},{"sp":"N","t":"And astonished, unforeseen, Aphrodite the daughter of Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"leapt up from her seat, when she saw the wife of Zeus;","st":"31.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing her grieving she cried a many-turning voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hera, consort of Zeus, why are your cheeks pale?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Why, queen, are your eyes downcast?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has rainy Zeus again become a thievish shower?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has he again become a bull, a wet wayfarer in the waters?","st":"31.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"What Europa forces you again? Or what other"},{"sp":"N","t":"Antiope, while her father Nycteus refuses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"is wedded in the shaggy bed of a lying Satyr?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Is an intelligent horse hastening anew to another wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a bastard whinnying from mimic lips?","st":"31.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has he wooed another Semele with a childbed torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirled the lightning, the steerer of the Loves?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Does he dance to the bed of a well-horned heifer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a loving bellowing? If you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you might rouse another ox-watching spy upon Zeus,","st":"31.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"a herdsman, Argus scored with unsleeping eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me as I ask, and so far as my strength goes, I will help.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"To her as she spoke the goddess replied with a guileful word:","hvShift6":[[0,0],[33,35]]},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cypris, goddess, let us leave the floor of Olympus to mortals;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus has brought Semele up to Olympus, the mother of Bacchus,","st":"31.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he will bring Dionysus too into the upper air. What house"},{"sp":"N","t":"will receive Hera? Or to what place shall I go? And I am ashamed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I should see Semele a bastard queen of Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest I see the house of gloomy Iapetus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he take me and drive me out of Olympus after Cronus.","st":"31.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"I became your mother-in-law twice over; for you were"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride of our son Hephaestus and of Ares."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest, after the earth, putting nectar to shame in the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he plant among the blessed ones the vine, as they call it."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let this never be, Justice and Earth and Water.","st":"31.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let him not bring shoots into the upper air, let me not, for wine's sake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"call heaven a vineyard after it has been starry,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor drink another drink after the sweet nectar of Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fear lest I see battle-steadfast Athena drunk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she lift her spear against Ares and Cytherea,","st":"31.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, with the tottering drop of mind-tottering Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine-struck stars, maddened against one another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raise a bold Enyo in the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest all the dwellers of Olympus, raving in Bacchic frenzy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should imitate the rites of the shield-bearing Corybants.","st":"31.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"— the Trojan youth, the servant of the cups of Zeus —","hvShift5":[[0,2]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Is that god-hated shame not enough, that I watch"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaming heaven and Hebe the wine-pourer of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he draws the sweet nectar with earthly hands?"},{"sp":"N","t":"In shame I shall go to the earth; and to them both","st":"31.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall leave the upper air, to Ganymede and to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall leave the upper air, the house of Semele. Let heaven be"},{"sp":"N","t":"one house for them both, for Perseus and for Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall come to my Argos, to the splendid city of Mycenae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling upon earth; and with his grieving mother","st":"31.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares himself will follow, your bridegroom; but do you too"},{"sp":"N","t":"set foot in your Sparta, and let it receive well-corseleted"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aphrodite in her anger together with bronze Ares."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence I have these pains; my father's Erinys"},{"sp":"N","t":"demands of me the outrage done to my father when he was forced,","st":"31.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, bearing down in the tumult of Cronus my begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera of the Titans assailed him, fighting on the side of Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fine thing for me, to see Dionysus in the midst of Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated near Eros, sharing a hearth with the foam-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the aegis after Cronides and Athena.","st":"31.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"But, goddess, help me, and as a helper of my distress"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a single dawn, so that I may charm the eyes of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me the cestus strap, your all-charming girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, while Zeus sleeps, I shall help my Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do me a late-accomplished favor, since the dark-skinned Indians","st":"31.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"are the hosts of Erythraean Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Dionysus in anger assailed, at whom also"},{"sp":"N","t":"woman-mad, unloving, child-begetting Zeus himself was enraged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirled the lightning, fighting beside Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me the helping cestus strap, in which alone","st":"31.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"you charm all things in one; and I am worthy to wear it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since I am the goddess of yoking and the fellow-contender of the Loves.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the thirty-second are tumults and the bed of sleeping Zeus and the madness of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded her; and guile-devising Aphrodite","st":"32.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"obeyed in her cunning, and drawing from her bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave the cestus of the Loves as a gift to willing Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she spoke some word about the charming strap:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Receive this strap, the helper of your distress;","st":"32.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you shall charm all things in one with the cestus, the steerer of longings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun and Zeus and the upper air and the chorus of stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unfixed stream of boundless Ocean.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and entered the Assyrian rock of Lebanon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera entered the star-roaming circle of Olympus,","st":"32.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly adorned her all-white form;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, making even the wandering cluster of her hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"that hung down to her forehead, she set it in place as a migrant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and washing her brow she covered it with a gleaming robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fastened the brooch, the key of her tunic;","st":"32.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she wetted her plaited hair with fragrant oil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from which, as it moved, after the upper air and after the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long-shadowed scent of perfume made the whole earth drunk."},{"sp":"N","t":"And on her head she had an all-varied crown, in which were many"},{"sp":"N","t":"lychnis-stones, the companions of Eros, from which,","st":"32.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"when they are shaken, the Cyprian flame sends bright sparkles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she had that stone which draws men into longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the bright name of desire-stricken Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the stone that longs for love-begetting iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Indian love-stone, because it too","st":"32.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"grew from the waters, kin to the foam-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dark hyacinth, still lovely to Phoebus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about her locks she bound the herb of love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which Cytherea loves as she loves the rose, as the anemone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wears when she is about to mingle with the son of Myrrha;","st":"32.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she bound her flanks crown-wise with the unaccustomed cestus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she had a varied, most ancient robe, on which was poured"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom blood of the bride, the remnant still of her ancient"},{"sp":"N","t":"maidenhood, from the secret love of a brother's wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might remind her consort of their wedded love.","st":"32.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"And having adorned her body and beheld it in a mirror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera ran through the upper air like a wing or a thought."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she came near Zeus; and seeing her, high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was lashed toward hotter Loves by the goading cestus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the eyes of Zeus were enslaved as he gazed;","st":"32.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watching her Cronides asked her with a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hera, why have you come to the eastern region of the earth?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What need brought you? Why do you come here today?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Are you in anger arming yourself again against wine-dark Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do you long to help the overbearing Indians?\"","st":"32.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and with a laughing mind much-devising Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mad with jealousy, spoke, beguiling her consort:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Father Zeus, another dear course holds me; for I do not come"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursuing the alien cares of the Indian Ares"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of Indian-slaying Dionysus, but I go hastening","st":"32.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the blazing courts of neighboring Helios of the east;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for winged Eros beside the water of Tethys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by the sting of Oceanid Rhodope,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has refused wedlock; and the cosmos has become a wanderer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and life is useless as weddings depart;","st":"32.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"I go here to call him back;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you know how I am called the Yoker, since my own"},{"sp":"N","t":"hands hold the power over generative birth.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she cried such a word her hot consort answered:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear bride, leave the fighting; let my proud Dionysus","st":"32.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"rejoice, mowing down the Bacchus-less race of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"roots and all; and let the bridal bed receive us both;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for no longing for an earthly wife, nor for a goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so raved in my heart with a charming cestus, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"not when I had Taygete daughter of Atlas, from whose bed","st":"32.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"elder-born Lacedaemon the city-holder grew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I did not so desire Niobe beside neighboring Lerna,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of first-born Phoroneus; nor so much Io,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the roaming Inachian bull-faced girl, who beside the Nile"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore the offspring of Epaphus and first-born Ceroessa;","st":"32.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"I did not come into so great a longing for the Paphian, for whose bed's sake"},{"sp":"N","t":"I planted the Centaurs, casting seed into the furrow of earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"as I now hold a sweet longing for you. Do you yourself indeed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being the Yoker and the ruler of generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoot your consort with the shafts of the Cyprus-born?\"","st":"32.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he whirled golden clouds tower-wise"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rounded a curved, well-turned covering into a sphere;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it was a manufactured pattern of a chamber, which then in a circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-colored form of airy Iris crowned,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purple, and for Zeus and his splendid-armed bride","st":"32.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"it was a self-made shelter of a mountain-haunting wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a self-perfected pattern of a necessary bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they kept company in the sweet ordinance of a graceful marriage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Earth, unfolding a fragrant childbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crowned the bridal bed with lovely flowers;","st":"32.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cilician saffron sprouted and the smilax grew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a twofold shoot adorned the bed of them both,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it wove male leaves together with the female grass beside it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a delicate consort breathing of longing even among the flowers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covering Zeus with saffron and his consort Hera with smilax;","st":"32.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lovely narcissus, leaping upon the anemone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed the sharp love of Zeus with intelligent silence."},{"sp":"N","t":"And none of the immortals saw the shadowy bed, not then the Nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"nearby, not all-seeing Phaethon, nor did the eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"of ox-eyed Selene herself behold that imperishable bed;","st":"32.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the shelter of the bed was girdled with thick clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Sleep, the companion of the Loves, charmed the eyes of Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Zeus lay delicately among the flowers, charmed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding his consort in his arms upon that unseen bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the roaming Erinys of many shapes in the mountains","st":"32.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed herself against Dionysus at the nods of Hera;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she crashed out a din upon the eyes of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking, heavy-thundering, her viperish whip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she whirled her head, and as her serpent locks"},{"sp":"N","t":"were dreadfully shaken a deadly echo hissed,","st":"32.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and springs of venom sprinkled the lonely peak; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another time, showing the pattern of a beast's face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bristled, dread-raving, a lion with thick-haired throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushing down upon Dionysus with blood-red gape."},{"sp":"N","t":"Him, held fast by the leap of wit-robbing madness,","st":"32.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis watched, and wished to drive off the madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but heavy-crashing Hera from on high terrified her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling a torch; and the mistress of the hunt gave way"},{"sp":"N","t":"in anger at her stepmother; but she was a guardian of Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his madness, and checked her own beasts with a threat,","st":"32.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bound her hunting hounds fast in a leash,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the much-plaited trail of the straps about their necks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest they harm the flesh of mind-tottering Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Megaera, blackening in her nether tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came again into the gloom, darting at Lyaeus","st":"32.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"phantoms of varied shape; and against Bacchus many"},{"sp":"N","t":"venom-shooting drops were shot from her head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and grim sparks; and always within his hearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sound of the Tartarean mind-robbing whip hissed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring, Dionysus within the lonely thicket","st":"32.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"passed through hard-to-tread mountains with a roaming sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by a divine panting; and against the rocks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a gadfly-mad bull, he battered his own horns,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a harsh bellowing from a raving throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, leaving Pan and her song that speaks last,","st":"32.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"ill-sounding Echo bellowed with a maddened voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"answering the bold din of Dionysus with its counterpart."},{"sp":"N","t":"And storm-swift Bacchus pursued dappled deer"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaggy lionesses, holding a mountain-running hunt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did a bold lion come near him; and a bear,","st":"32.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified, much-dreaded, hid in a lair-holding rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the madness of threatening Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the god-driven sound in its grim ears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and long serpents propped against some rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"licking gently, he cut down with his pitiless thyrsus;","st":"32.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he shook the peaks with his long-pointed horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing the suppliant tribes of unbending lions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he uprooted the oaks of the fair-fruited field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursued the Hadryads; and shooting at the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drove off the Naiad Nymphs of the river as migrants.","st":"32.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Bassarids wandered and did not touch Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs, shuddering, hid themselves in the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did they come near him, astonished at the mass of his threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he rush upon them pouring a strange-sounding echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling snowy foam, the witness of his madness.","st":"32.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Deriades, having overbearing boldness, assailed the Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Dionysus was shaken at the nods of Hera."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when, bellowing with the trail of wintry surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unnavigable sea raves with opposing tempests,"},{"sp":"N","t":"snowing the drenched air with sky-high waves,","st":"32.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the storms break the stern-cables with the unsparing"},{"sp":"N","t":"rush of the wave, and the furious wind, whirling the sail"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its breath, strips bare the bent mast"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled about with canvas, and the yard-arm is slanted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sailors in distress entrust their hope to the sea;","st":"32.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"so then the Indian Ares stirred the whole army of Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"There the strife was not in order, nor was the tumult of men"},{"sp":"N","t":"equal, nor the fighting matched; for unwearied,"},{"sp":"N","t":"returning, battle-rousing bronze Ares roared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the shape of the champion Modaeus, who more than others,","st":"32.165","hvShift6":[[0,18],[2,33],[10,42],[16,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"unsated of fighting, delighted in joyless gore,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[23,20]]},{"sp":"N","t":"to whom slaughter was more pleasing than a banquet; and on his ox-hide shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like the snaky trails of the Gorgon's locks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the painted image of fair-tressed Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was like Deriades, of the same color; and having then","st":"32.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unlaughing imitation of his shuddering face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bearing his crooked lock and the device of his shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"host-rousing Ares was carried dread-raving into the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and heartened the champions. And with a shout of one tongue,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since Bacchus was not present, the Indians roared fearless,","st":"32.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and deadly Ares crashed out a din of nine thousand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having roaming Strife as his fellow-contender; and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set Terror, and Panic as the attendant of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they goaded the army of wilderness-ranging Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades and the slumber of Zeus and Ares running with them.","st":"32.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mingled phalanxes of an emulous tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled the whole rank of the Bassarids with iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many fleeing were killed by one slayer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck with swords. Homeric Muses, tell"},{"sp":"N","t":"who died, who thudded down beneath the spear of Deriades:","st":"32.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aebialus and Thyamis and Ormenius and Opheltes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Criasus son of Argasus, Telebes and Lyctian Antheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thronius and Aretus and Molineus of the fair ash-spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"and valiant Comarchus; and one after another the army"},{"sp":"N","t":"was stretched out dead by the spear of Deriades; and as they perished","st":"32.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"one was stretched upon the floor, another floated"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the streams, bearing a wavy battle; and another in the neighboring sea","hvShift6":[[0,0],[37,38],[43,42]]},{"sp":"N","t":"was subdued, and Arabian Nereus entombed in the waves","hvShift6":[[0,0],[18,11]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the man freshly scored as he was pursued by the iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another ran through the mountain with stormy sole,","st":"32.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing his doom, and another, leaving the spear driven"},{"sp":"N","t":"middle-deep about his back, went after the midday shade of the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"needing evil-averting Dionysus, who was not there."},{"sp":"N","t":"And boastful Echelaus fell by an unburied doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the man-breaking stone of sky-high Morrheus,","st":"32.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Cyprian, still having the freshly scored circle of his first beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like a high-tressed palm; and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"the delicate, unshorn youth rolled, shaking his torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck at the top of the hip, where nature fitted"},{"sp":"N","t":"the axle of the thigh to the socket in an age-mate bond of flesh;","st":"32.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he died still gripping the mystic pine-torch as it caught,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gasping he turned his own head to ash with his own brand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"burning his much-plaited locks with the sooty torch."},{"sp":"N","t":"And mockery-loving Morrheus cried out, boasting over him:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Boy, stranger to the nurse men name as yours,","st":"32.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"youth Echelaus, you have lied about your race from Cyprus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have no descent from Pygmalion, to whom Cypris gave"},{"sp":"N","t":"a long journey of a many-yeared life;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares the bridegroom of your Paphian did not save you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did your Cytherea give you the unspeakable circles","st":"32.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"of returning years and an unlimping wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that you might flee your doom upon a doom-averting car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"forever steering the heavy-kneed course of mules."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have erred: you do bear a race from Cyprus; for swift-fated"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares subdued you too, like the son of Myrrha.\"","st":"32.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, spear-wielding Morrheus fought against the foot-soldiers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking shambling Bilithus and destroying Denthis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting down the neck of the dancer Erigbolus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his far-throwing spear, he terrified the Phrygian warriors;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he laid Sebes low with a jagged missile;","st":"32.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursuing the phalanx of the Thebans and Actaeon"},{"sp":"N","t":"he killed Eubotes, a citizen of the Cadmeian field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pasture-mate of Actaeon. And with a shout of one voice"},{"sp":"N","t":"many, fleeing the measureless strength of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped in a body into the threads of a yoked Fate,","st":"32.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"dying by the self-slaying crushing iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the rushes of one man; and falling upon one another"},{"sp":"N","t":"they were spread out in rows upon the bloody dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Crimisus, Himaleon, Phrasius, Thargelus, Iaon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with whom, as they were cut down, Coelon fell in number,","st":"32.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cyes rolled a corpse in a bloody doom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the slaughter was unspeakable; and as they were cut down by iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thirsty field bathed in willing gore, hostile to it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the strange rain of a warlike snowfall."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was tumult in the Bacchic phalanx; for unsteady","st":"32.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foot-soldiers were shaken, and the fair-stoned bridles"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the war-fleeing drivers were reined back into flight;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one, mountain-roaming, entered a hollow rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another came and sat beneath the long-leaved slope of a thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"hidden in the leaves, another a cave of lions,","st":"32.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"another went after the midday den of an irresistible bear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, escaping through a high-crested headland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passed over the topmost hill with mountain-ranging feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Bacchant passed by the haunts of a freshly delivered beast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"treading the headland with terrified sandal;","st":"32.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she no longer longed to hold the lion's rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but came upon the nest of feeble deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with feeble ways, since the Bacchant had turned her former mind"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the heart of a deer instead of a lioness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one of the storm-footed Satyrs ran with fearful sole,","st":"32.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsandaled, like the unsteady breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the god-fighting mass of the threat of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And old wandering Silenus made for the crags;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often he fell to the ground as his face grew dusty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sinking heavy-kneed on slipping sandals,","st":"32.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"and again he straightened his shaggy body; and among the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hid himself instead of fighting, and by necessity left"},{"sp":"N","t":"his Euian spear, the thyrsus, to the unwarlike storms to tend,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely avoided the point of well-helmeted Morrheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And sluggish Erechtheus withdrew with shrinking feet,","st":"32.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching his well-rounded eye as he turned back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ashamed before his battle-steadfast city-holding Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Aristaeus unwillingly refused the Maenad battle of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck on his left shoulder by an arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Melisseus shunned the army of the spear-bold Corybants,","st":"32.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"wounded down his shaggy breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tip of his nipple scored by an Erythraean point."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the shameless, grim Cyclopes hurried into flight"},{"sp":"N","t":"on fair-footed sole, astonished, with whom Faunus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing, unshaken, left the Indian war.","st":"32.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing the whole army of the well-horned phalanx into flight,"},{"sp":"N","t":"elder-born Parrhasian Pan withdrew in rout,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with silent feet entered a thick-shaded wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest restless Echo see him fleeing through the mountain"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laugh at him and call him feeble.","st":"32.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"And all the champions then fled away; and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus alone was left there, and as he fought"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lacked unconquered Dionysus, who was not present;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless he stayed there. And from the crag the Nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid themselves in the deep halls of a Naiad;","st":"32.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"some went as companions to the daughters of Hydaspes, others, fleeing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lodged in the streams of the Indus that ran near by,"},{"sp":"N","t":"others as fellow-travelers of the daughters of Sydrus, and others in Ganges"},{"sp":"N","t":"wiped off their freshly rushing gore; and them, as many"},{"sp":"N","t":"as came in throngs to the watery haunts,","st":"32.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"a silver-footed Naiad received at a hospitable gateway"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the courts of her wavy maiden-chamber."},{"sp":"N","t":"Others hid themselves in the shadowy clusters of a Hamadryad,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entering the oaken hollows as they opened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many Bassarids wailed like fountains beneath","st":"32.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"the moisture-bearing springs near a rock; and filled"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the freshly poured, tearful rain of their lamentation-loving faces"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole deep-bosomed spring purpled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mourning the heavy grief of griefless Dionysus."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the thirty-third furious Eros subdues Morrheus, setting him ablaze for the beauty of Chalcomede."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, carried along with the roaming leap of his soles,","st":"33.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-kneed, rushed like a well-horned bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a deadly panting in the swell of an alien madness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Grace, swift-sandaled, beside an Erythraean garden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plucking a fragrant planting of reeds,","st":"33.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that within the fire-breathing Paphian cauldrons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing the poured travail of Assyrian oil"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Indian flowers, she might make perfume for her queen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when she had gathered every kind of dewy herb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"surveyed the whole place; and in the thicket that ran near by,","st":"33.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the madness of her father Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she wept in grief, and with a purpose of tender love"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored her cheeks with mourning nails;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she watched the Satyrs cowering before Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and perceived Codone and short-lived Gigarto","st":"33.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying upon the unburied dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she pitied Chalcomede, fleeing with stormy sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"the point of maddened Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and was shaken with envy for the sake of the rosy-faced girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should ever conquer Aphrodite in splendor.","st":"33.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"And grieving she went up to Olympus, stealing away"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pain of her father Lyaeus with a mourning silence;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a pallor, changing the flower of her well-rounded cheek,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dimmed the glittering sparkle of her face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cypris of Adonis spoke to her as she stood downcast,","st":"33.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a comforting word; and from the face"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Pasithea she perceived her grief by a heralding silence:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear bride, what has befallen you, that you have changed your form?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, how have you altered your reddening shape?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who has quenched the springtime beam of your face?","st":"33.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer does a silver gleam flash from your limbs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer, as before, do your eyes laugh."},{"sp":"N","t":"But tell your cares; does my son wear you down,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do you love, like Selene beside a desire-stricken rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some herdsman? Has Eros lashed you too with the cestus,","st":"33.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he did the morning-born herself?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence your cheeks are pale: because wandering Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a misty bridegroom, weds you, a girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I shall not force you against your will, nor shall I join"},{"sp":"N","t":"dark-skinned Sleep as consort to white Pasithea.\"","st":"33.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke the Grace wept and answered with a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Plant-sower of the ever-flowing cosmos, mother of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no herdsman routs me, nor the bold longing of Sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am not the morning-born, unhappy in love, nor Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the roaming labor of Lyaeus distresses me,","st":"33.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"my father shuddering before the Erinyes; and do you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you can, fight in the front for your brother Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and told the queen the whole labor of her father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the boundless phalanxes of the Bassarids whom Morrheus killed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the whole routed army of the Satyrs; and she told of","st":"33.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"the divine whip of Dionysus as he was shaken,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of plaintive Gigarto gasping upon the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she spoke of Codone untimely dead; and in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"she told of the grief and the beauty of Chalcomede together."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laughter-loving Aphrodite, changing the sparkle","st":"33.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her rosy face, cast off her accustomed laughter."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she bade Aglaia go as messenger, to call"},{"sp":"N","t":"her son, furious Eros, the aloft air-roamer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the generative steersman of the human race."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Grace bent her step, and with much-turning face","st":"33.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"she surveyed sea and earth and heaven, to find somewhere"},{"sp":"N","t":"the restless track of Eros, since he shakes his wings everywhere,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling about the rim of the cosmos cut fourfold."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she found him about the topmost ridge of golden Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling nectarous drops with cups;","st":"33.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside him stood a boy of his own age, playing delicately,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-haired Hymenaeus; and, bringing the wheeling sphere,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wise work of his aloft-minded mother Urania,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who knows the course of the stars, he set it as the prize of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a counterpart image of the cunning form of Argos;","st":"33.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winged Eros, lifting his mother's well-rounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"golden necklace, set it there, belonging to Aphrodite of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bright, all-varied ornament of victory; and a silver"},{"sp":"N","t":"cauldron lay in the contest, and an image of wine-pouring Hebe"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood as a mark seen in the middle; and lovely Ganymede,","st":"33.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wine-pourer of Cronides, was the judge of the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a garland in his palms. And for the unmixed-wine-loving casts"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a lot, drawn with shifting fingers of the hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and some they straightened and held up, and others were clamped"},{"sp":"N","t":"fast to the wrist of the hand, joined in a yoked bond;","st":"33.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the strife was lovely for both. And soft-haired"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hymenaeus, drawing first, took the cup, and sending"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flying nectarous drop aloft through the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hurled it above the cauldron; and he did not then reveal his prayer"},{"sp":"N","t":"to his mother the Muse; and the drop, darting from the cup,","st":"33.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the middle of the air, soaring aloft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, turning aside its cast with a dancing throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawn back again about the face of the statue"},{"sp":"N","t":"it struck the top of the head noiselessly, without a sound;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and second, shifting-witted Eros by a cunning ordinance","st":"33.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"took the lovely cup, and prayed to the Cyprus-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"secretly in his heart, and stretching an unwandering eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hurled at the mark, sending the moisture far-shooting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the drop of the nectarous drink, turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"straight-stretched, unbending, above the temple of the statue,","st":"33.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed heavy-thundering from the air upon its brow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the delicate statue cried out, and for the son of the Cyprus-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"the golden cauldron crashed out a victory echo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ganymede, laughing, gave the delicate garland to Eros;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly taking the varied necklace and lifting the sphere","st":"33.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had the double prize of the fair-dropping contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and skipping with his feet and tumbling on his head"},{"sp":"N","t":"bold Eros danced, glorying; and often he drew down"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hand of his grieving adversary from his face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Aglaia stood near him; and she received in her hands","st":"33.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"the prizes of the mind-delighting lord; and she nodded to the boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"to come apart, and into the ears of Eros, with witnessing silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she spoke the guile-minded word of her lying queen:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"All-subduer, unsubdued, life-sower, of one age with the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten, since Cytherea is hard pressed, and none","st":"33.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her handmaids stayed by her; the Grace fled, Persuasion went off,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unfixed Desire withdrew; and she has sent me alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"to you, needing your unconquered quiver.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke Eros questioned her, so that he might learn all;"},{"sp":"N","t":"because all the young, when they hear the beginning","st":"33.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"of an endless tale, hasten to hear the end;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he shrieked out an unbridled voice from his lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Who has grieved my Paphian? So that I may arm my hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting against them all; and since my mother is forced,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall stretch my all-subduing string against Cronides,","st":"33.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"and make him again, goaded, a marriage-thieving bird of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an eagle, or some bull, a sailor of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if Pallas stirred her, and the lame god grieved her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kindling the light-bearing brand of a Cecropian lamp,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fight against them both, against Hephaestus and Athena;","st":"33.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if the hare-shooting archer-goddess draws her into anger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing the fiery Olympian sword of Orion"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would goad Artemis, and drive her out of the upper air, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting on my wings the son of Maia as her companion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling in vain on paltry Persuasion to help;","st":"33.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving my shafts and the fiery knot of my quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall lash willing Phoebus with laurel leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding him fast with the speaking hyacinth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor do I tremble at the strength of Enyalius, nor shall I labor"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lash Ares fettered with the sweet cestus;","st":"33.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall drag the twin luminaries down as servants"},{"sp":"N","t":"from heaven to Paphos, and bring to my mother as attendants"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon with Clymene, Selene with Endymion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that all may know that I subdue all things.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and whirling his straight-running sole in the air","st":"33.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"he outran Aglaia with the twofold whirr of his wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until he came to the house of Aphrodite as she hastened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And taking him about the middle with a calm face"},{"sp":"N","t":"she embraced the boy in her outspread, rejoicing arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the dear weight on her knees; and as he sat","st":"33.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"she kissed the boy's mouth and eyes; and touching"},{"sp":"N","t":"his mind-charming bow and handling his quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though breathing anger, she broke into a guile-minded voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My child, you have forgotten Phaethon and Cytherea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer does Pasiphae pursue a bellowing bed;","st":"33.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun laughs at me, and arms the blood of Astris,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the warlike Deriades, his daughter's son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyer of the Bassarids of woman-mad Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the driver of the desire-stricken Satyrs of Bromius."},{"sp":"N","t":"This has stirred me the more, that in mortal-seeming shape","st":"33.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"battle-rousing Ares, having Enyo as his fellow-contender,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparing nothing of his ancient love for Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has armed himself against Dionysus at the nods of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fellow-traveler with the Indian king. But in the battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares is for Deriades; do you fight in the front for Lyaeus;","st":"33.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has a spear, but you a mightier bow, before which"},{"sp":"N","t":"highest Zeus bends the knee, and furious Ares and lawful Hermes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorious-bowed Apollo too dreads your bow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if, dear boy, you do a favor to your foam-born mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fight in the front for the Bassarids and for our Dionysus.","st":"33.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"But going untouchable to the eastern region of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the Indian floor, where there is a handmaid of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"among the Bacchants, surpassing the youth of her own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Chalcomede by name, a lover of maidenhood — and if you should see"},{"sp":"N","t":"them both, Chalcomede and Cypris, within Lebanon,","st":"33.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"you could not, dear boy, tell which was Aphrodite —,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go there and help wilderness-ranging Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting Morrheus for the beauty of Chalcomede;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall put into your hands a prize worthy of your archery,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a well-made Lemnian crown, like the gleams","st":"33.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the flaming sun; and do you, sending a sweet shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to both, to Cypris and to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorify your bird and mine, the bride-adorner of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the herald of gladness, of life-yoking weddings.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"The goddess spoke; and greedy Eros leapt up from the bosom","st":"33.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his mother, and lifted his bow, and about his little"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoulder hung his all-subduing quiver;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winged he flew through the upper air; and about Cerne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling his light wings, facing the beams of Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he flew smiling, because with a few shafts he had set ablaze","st":"33.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"so great a charioteer of the heavenly cars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the radiance of the Loves had conquered the radiance of Helios."},{"sp":"N","t":"And swiftly coming into the midst of the Indian throng"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fixed his bow upon the neck of Chalcomede;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and directing his shaft about the circles of her rosy cheek","st":"33.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sent it into the heart of Morrheus, and rowing his course"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the unequally yoked trail of his swimming wings"},{"sp":"N","t":"he went up to his father's starry bolts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the Indian pierced by a fiery arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And always, shaken this way and that by the shaft of longing,","st":"33.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"wherever the maiden went, Morrheus went unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a gentle sword, lifting a sparing spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his bold mind was lashed by the lovely cestus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretching a love-maddened eye in a circle about her"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew his uncharmed gaze at the nods of the Cyprus-born.","st":"33.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, devising guile, beguiled the leader of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though longing for him, and the girl molded a lying"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitation of desire; and Morrheus touched the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried by an empty hope; for he thought the maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"had in her heart an equal shaft of the Loves,","st":"33.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"light-minded man, because he sought to charm a prudent girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"with dark limbs, and did not remember his own shape."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the girl, laughing at him with a play-loving guile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, provoked the man unhappy in love, and to her adversary"},{"sp":"N","t":"she told of the wind-footed knees of an unwedded bride,","st":"33.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"how once she fled Phoebus, running with the north wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how beside the liquid stream of the outstretched river"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fixed her maiden foot beside broad-flowing Orontes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the earth, gaping beside the mouth of the well-watered lake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the pursued girl in a pitying bosom.","st":"33.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke such a word Morrheus leapt up with joy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one thing alone stirred him, that Daphne, when pursued,"},{"sp":"N","t":"even a god did not overtake, and Apollo did not defile her;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he called Phoebus slow; and always he blamed the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because it hid a maiden untried in weddings;","st":"33.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he feared, trembling with the sweet fire, lest Chalcomede"},{"sp":"N","t":"herself might be a lover of maidenhood, like Daphne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, seeing her fleeing, he should come to a fruitless course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laboring toward an unaccomplished longing, as Apollo did."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when night rose, the luller of tumult,","st":"33.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"Chalcomede came to the ridge of a lonely wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the tracks of mind-wandering Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not then bearing rattles and the Euian cymbals of Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"did she celebrate the rites of unsleeping Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but downcast and not touching the dance","st":"33.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"she kept an unaccustomed silence on her never-silent lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing the sickness of her defender Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with shrinking feet slow Morrheus came scarcely on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the bride with an eye that turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming swift-running Phaethon; and he kept his mind","st":"33.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"traveling as companion with Chalcomede; and in distress"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched out a womanish cry with Cyprian whispers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting the shaft of nightly Loves beneath his heart:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Away with shaft and warlike bow; for another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovelier, mightier shaft forces me; away with the quiver;","st":"33.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cestus strap has conquered the strap of my ox-hide shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do I arm a fighting hand against the Bassarids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaving my father's god, water and earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall set up an altar both to Cypris and to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting away the bronze spear of Enyalius and Athena.","st":"33.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do I arm myself holding a torch; for the torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Loves has quenched the brand of feeble Enyalius;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am struck by another, hotter fire. Would that I too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"would that I were a woman-mad Satyr, so that I might dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the midst of the Bassarids, and, propping my forearm with my palm,","st":"33.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"might clamp a bond of love upon the neck of Chalcomede."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let Dionysus drag the attendant of Deriades to Phrygia"},{"sp":"N","t":"beneath a slave's yoke, and instead of my fatherland"},{"sp":"N","t":"let deep-wealthy Maeonia receive me as its dweller;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I wish to have Tmolus after Caucasus; and casting away","st":"33.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"my first-born Indian name, let me be called a Lydian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bending a slave neck of Eros to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Pactolus bear me; what is my father's Hydaspes to me?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let the sweet house of Chalcomede hold me; for in the wars"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris together with Bacchus, with both their shafts,","st":"33.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"assailed the sons-in-law of Deriades, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The cestus killed Morrheus, and the thyrsus killed Orontes.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things he cried; and with many-voiced care"},{"sp":"N","t":"he melted, remembering Chalcomede; for in darkness"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sparks of the Loves are always hotter.","st":"33.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"For already, leaping with a shadowy, earth-born throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the noiseless cone of cloudless mist blackened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and joined all things in a single trembling silence;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and no Indian wayfarer hastened his step through the city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did a working woman lay hold of her accustomed craft,","st":"33.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor in her palms beside the distaff-loving lamp"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the wandering spindle, going in a self-coiling circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretch restless with the dancing trail of the thread,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, heavy-headed beside the vigil-loving lamp,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the toil-enduring woman slept; and a serpent, creeping quietly,","st":"33.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay fallen, and drawing back its tail with its head"},{"sp":"N","t":"reined in the trail of the spine of its sleeping belly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and some high-footed elephant beside a neighboring wall"},{"sp":"N","t":"slept an upright sleep, propping its back against an oak."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then Morrheus alone, sleepless, darting off, creeping","st":"33.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"noiseless with returning feet, was tormented, coiling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving Cheirobie alone sleeping in the chamber;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having heard a tale from some ancient sage"},{"sp":"N","t":"among the Cilician men when he had battle near Taurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had learned the inspired sting of starry Loves,","st":"33.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and going to the clear court spread open in the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gazed at the bridegroom of Europa, the Bull of Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretching a much-wandering eye toward the axle's neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"he watched Callisto and the restless trail of the Wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing that a female received a female consort","st":"33.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the bastard body of the mimicked archer-goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unknown limbs; and rising above the Bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"he watched Myrtilus, the fire-breathing Charioteer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he helped a marriage, and for the course of Hippodameia"},{"sp":"N","t":"made a counterfeit pattern with wheel-shaped wax,","st":"33.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"until Pelops found his marriage; and near Cassiepeia"},{"sp":"N","t":"he saw the long-winged eagle, the consort of Aegina,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he longed for such a thievish guile, so that he too"},{"sp":"N","t":"might loose the maidenhood of unwedded Chalcomede,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he spoke some word, keeping a sleepless eye:","st":"33.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I have heard how high-ruling Zeus, wholly like a Satyr,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wedded the girl Antiope in a guileful shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the mimic love of leap-loving weddings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too wish to have such a body, so that I may dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"coming unknown into the army of the well-horned Satyrs,","st":"33.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may accomplish the bed of unmixed-wine-loving Chalcomede."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know, Cytherea, why you are angry at the sons of the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your shafts rout the neighbors of Helios;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have not yet lost the memory of your bonds when they were exposed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Phaethon did not beget me; why do you rout me, Aphrodite?","st":"33.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ox-watching Pasiphae did not bear me, I am no brother"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Ariadne. Speak, stones, with a rocky voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"I long for Chalcomede, and she refuses. Away, quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"away, blood-red bows and windy arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares did not save me when Aphrodite armed herself;","st":"33.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"little Eros subdued me, whom proud Bacchus did not kill.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Morrheus, unhappy in love, said in vain through the night."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the wing of mind-wandering sweet Sleep lull"},{"sp":"N","t":"bed-fleeing Chalcomede, since she had a longing for death,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing maddened Morrheus, lest the hot man,","st":"33.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging her off, should yoke her in a forced wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Bacchus was not there; and she bent her nightly step"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the Erythraean sea and cried to the deaf wave:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Melis, I count you blessed; for you once, untaught of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting your body into the sea with a self-moving whirl,","st":"33.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"escaped the bed of woman-mad Damnameneus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I count your maiden-loving doom blessed; for Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of the sea, armed a gadfly-mad bridegroom against you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sea guarded you, even if it was the mother of the Paphian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you died in the surges still a maiden. But let the water","st":"33.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the sea hide even Chalcomede herself, willing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"untried in weddings, while Morrheus longs for her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may be called a new bed-fleeing Britomartis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once the sea received and gave back again to the land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when she cared nothing for the Cyprian Loves of Minos.","st":"33.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"The love-maddened Earth-shaker did not terrify me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he did maiden-loving Asterie, whom in the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she wandered, he pursued turning back again, until"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo rooted her unshaken in the waves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restless as she rode, running with an alternating breeze.","st":"33.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me, receive me, sea, in your hospitable bosom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive Chalcomede after Melis; receive her too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a younger Britomartis refusing weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may flee Morrheus and your Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"pity Chalcomede, helper of maidens.\"","st":"33.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she was shaken in mind beside the neighboring sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now self-rolling she would have entered the wave of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Thetis helped her, doing favor to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and changing her body stood beside Chalcomede,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having the look of a Bacchant cried a comforting voice:","st":"33.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Endure, Chalcomede, do not fear the bed of Morrheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have me as a favorable omen of your unloosed maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing witness to your unwedded bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am Thetis, a bed-fleeing woman like you, and I too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Chalcomede, am a lover of maidenhood; and from heaven","st":"33.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"father Zeus pursued me and wished to drag me into marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not old Prometheus checked him in his longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesying that he would beget a son better than Cronides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ever a boy of Thetis should bear down upon his father"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drive out Cronides, as high-ruling Zeus drove Cronus.","st":"33.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"Become guileful for me, life-bearing; for if you die"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-slain, untaught of unwedded weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hostile Indian will destroy the whole rank of the Bassarids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but beguile him, and you will save from death"},{"sp":"N","t":"your routed army while Dionysus is lashed,","st":"33.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"feigning an empty longing of the Paphian; and if Morrheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"should drag you to bed while you refuse the wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have no need of a helper against Cypris; for you have"},{"sp":"N","t":"as guardian of your girdle a measureless helping serpent;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking your Dragon after the war of the Indians","st":"33.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus will fix it in the star-shining circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unceasing messenger of your unloosed maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"near his own light-bearing Crown, when he shall make"},{"sp":"N","t":"the great starry sign of Cydonian Ariadne;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your dragon, matching the Arctic Dragon,","st":"33.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall flash for mortals, flashing together with Ophiuchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hereafter you shall praise Thetis of the sea, when you behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"your own fiery star flashing together with Selene."},{"sp":"N","t":"And be of good courage about the marriage; for no consort"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall loose the steadfast knot of your maidenhood,","st":"33.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"no, by yourself and by Dionysus who has touched my table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no, by yourself and your thyrsi, and by Aphrodite of the sea.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, beguiling her; and she covered the girl in a cloud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the guards should behold her, or some spying man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wayfarer of the night keeping a thievish track with guileful foot,","st":"33.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a bold woman-mad goatherd, and should drag off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden of the evening to a wedlock by the roadside."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXIV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"While the Bacchants are killed on either side in the thirty-fourth, Deriades arms himself within his towers."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mountain-roaming girl, with swift-whirling sole,","st":"34.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping a noiseless step, passed to the ridge of the wood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did Thetis linger on the shore, but she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"entered the weedy court of her father Nereus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already, stretching his eye through the cloudless air,","st":"34.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus had his fill of gazing at the starry circles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he spoke some word, lashing his heart with cares:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My shifting mind wanders; not one counsel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not one purpose holds me; wide-scattered longings"},{"sp":"N","t":"have wheeled about me, and I know not how to accomplish one.","st":"34.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"Shall I kill lovely Chalcomede? But what shall I do,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she too kill me with longing after her doom?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Or shall I leave her living, unwounded, and openly"},{"sp":"N","t":"drag the maiden to wedlock? But in my heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"I tremble at Deriades and I pity Cheirobie.","st":"34.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall never kill the maiden; and if I strike her down,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[50,55]]},{"sp":"N","t":"how can I live, when I look upon the maiden no longer?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I labor when I am left without Chalcomede for a single hour.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Saying such things in vain much-devising Morrheus went,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seething with the pains of desire-stricken care.","st":"34.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Hyssacus, waking, saw him wandering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning back again from high on the bank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having left the bed of his lonely, forgotten bride;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and guileful as he was, he perceived the hidden goad"},{"sp":"N","t":"of untraceable Loves, a most faithful servant; and he questioned him","st":"34.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a guileful word, pouring such a deceiving speech from his throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why, leaving your bed and your sleeping bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you wander this way and that in the darkness, fearless Morrheus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has Deriades perhaps terrified you with a threat?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Is Cheirobie angry at you with a jealous heart,","st":"34.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"thinking you love some spear-won Bacchant?"},{"sp":"N","t":"For when they see their consorts love-maddened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"women are always jealous over a secret Cypris."},{"sp":"N","t":"Is bold all-subduing Desire perhaps arming against you"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridal sparks of his unsleeping quiver?","st":"34.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you long for one of the Bassarids? As I hear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there are three Graces, the dancers of Orchomenus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaids of Phoebus; but of dance-weaving Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"there are ranks of three hundred Graces, of whom one alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"shines out surpassing them all, just as Selene herself,","st":"34.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting a well-rounded sparkle, hides the radiance"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the stars with brighter beams."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she arms herself with twin shafts under a single ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting with beauty and hurling the spear with iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there is helmet-glancing Pasithea, whom the Bacchants","st":"34.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"call Chalcomede; but I myself would call her"},{"sp":"N","t":"silver-footed Artemis, or Athena of the golden shield.\"","hvShift6":[[0,0],[27,53],[31,54]]},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he fell silent; and drawing down the edge of his brow"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus, unhappy in love, spoke with shamefaced lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Truly Dionysus entered the wave of the sea","st":"34.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing Lycurgus, and out of the underwater gulf"},{"sp":"N","t":"he armed the Nereids, and came from the brine bringing"},{"sp":"N","t":"his sister Aphrodite of the sea into battle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of a bridal, fragrant tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave her an iron breastplate to wear, and instead of a cestus","st":"34.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"he granted her a bronze spear; and changing her former name"},{"sp":"N","t":"he called armed Aphrodite Chalcomede;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she is a fellow-traveler of the Bassarids; and against both"},{"sp":"N","t":"I fight unknowing, against both Cypris and Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And why do I lift my furious spear in vain? Yield, point;","st":"34.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the Paphian has conquered the hurler of the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if she subdues the scepter-bearer of wars with her own spark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if she burned flaming Phaethon with a greater torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"and routs the fiery one — what could I do with iron?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me some counsel to help against the Cyprus-born;","st":"34.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall I wound Eros? How shall I overtake the winged one?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Shall I lift my spear? He fights with fire. Shall I draw my sword?"},{"sp":"N","t":"He has a bow, and that bow is fire that touches my heart."},{"sp":"N","t":"Often I have been wounded in the fighting; but as I labored"},{"sp":"N","t":"a healer saved me with his life-sustaining art,","st":"34.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding the pain-killing flower of herbs about the wound of my limbs."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hyssacus, do not hide it: with what varied drugs sprinkled"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall I heal the wound of the Loves within my heart?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am always bold against my adversaries; but when I look upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"Chalcomede present, my spear-point turns womanish.","st":"34.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not tremble at Dionysus; but I cower before a woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, sending the radiance of her desire-striking face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shoots me with her beauty, and I stretch my bows no more."},{"sp":"N","t":"Thus I have looked upon one of the Nereids; if it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"either Thetis or Galatea fights beside Dionysus.\"","st":"34.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and treading slowly, scarcely, on the tips of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he should wake his consort sleeping in the night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came back to his chamber; and he turned his eye far"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his black-bosomed bride, and longed that Chalcomede"},{"sp":"N","t":"might appear and shine, and that the morning-born might come.","st":"34.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"And in distress he fell upon his bed, downcast beneath Eros;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sleepless servant Hyssacus, holding a longing"},{"sp":"N","t":"for sweet sleep, slept again above his ox-hide."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a vision of a dream beguiled Morrheus as he slept,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting up from the mind-stealing gates of ivory,","st":"34.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spoke some lovely, beguiling word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Receive obedient Chalcomede, bridegroom Morrheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive your bride upon your bed after the wars;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you gladdened your gaze, seeing me by day,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now sleep beside loving Chalcomede by night.","st":"34.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"There is a grace even in a sleeping wedlock, and there is"},{"sp":"N","t":"a lovely sweet sting even in the weddings of dreams."},{"sp":"N","t":"I longed to hold you in my arms, and Dawn appears near.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she flew away; and Morrheus leapt up from sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and perceived the light of marriage-robbing Dawn beginning;","st":"34.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he thought Chalcomede had longing for him; and at once in silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spoke, feeding a deceiving Cyprian hope:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Threefold, morning-born, is the light you bring, because you bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"Chalcomede, and lead the daylight, and drive off the night."},{"sp":"N","t":"Comforter of sleepless Morrheus, may you too appear,","st":"34.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"Chalcomede, rosier than rose-crowned Dawn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"never do the meadow Seasons bring such a rose."},{"sp":"N","t":"Graceful maiden, your cheeks hold a springtime"},{"sp":"N","t":"meadow, which time knows not how to wither;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your flowers bloom when the Seasons are of autumn;","st":"34.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"your lilies shine even in winter; and about"},{"sp":"N","t":"your body is an unceasing reddening anemone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which the Graces tend and the winds do not destroy."},{"sp":"N","t":"You have adorned your name by playing the champion with iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your name fits your manhood; and not in vain","st":"34.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"do they call you Chalcomede; for bronze Ares begot you"},{"sp":"N","t":"dancing on the bed of the Cyprus-born, the mother of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"All call you Chalcomede, but I alone shall call you"},{"sp":"N","t":"Chrysomede, because you have the beauty of golden Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that you have your race from Sparta; for as I think,","st":"34.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"iron-tunicked Aphrodite brought forth Chalcomede.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he spoke upon his vigil-loving bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when, sending a radiance from her reddening face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"far-shooting Dawn leapt up as forerunner of the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"host-rousing Ares armed the Indian offspring;","st":"34.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"and then, arming themselves from their well-wheeled beds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indians flowed in throngs to the chariot of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Bacchoi, since unconquered Dionysus was not present,"},{"sp":"N","t":"poured out downcast into the plain; and in their hearts"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer bold they marched into the tumult,","st":"34.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"but were shaken with fear; and no longer with man-breaking madness"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the bronze-tunicked women rave in Bacchic frenzy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did they hurl forth foam from a heavy-sounding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowing throat, but in unroaring silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"the much-beaten backs of the untanned ox-hide stayed still;","st":"34.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"no torches blazed the flame of an Enyalian pine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belching a death-bearing smoke; but beneath the goad"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a divine whip the warriors turned womanish."},{"sp":"N","t":"The Satyrs did not shout, no sound of the accustomed flute"},{"sp":"N","t":"roared battle-rousing; and in a Bacchus-less tumult","st":"34.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sileni fought sober, nor, having smeared"},{"sp":"N","t":"their faces with red ochre of the same color as blazing gore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did they redden their yellow pattern with a lying shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"for terror, nor were their brows sprinkled, defiled"},{"sp":"N","t":"with white gypsum, as before; and the storm-swift Pans","st":"34.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not rave in Bacchic frenzy in the tumult, having drunk"},{"sp":"N","t":"with their lips the hot, freshly rushing blood of a wilderness lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but had become gentle through fear; and shrinking"},{"sp":"N","t":"they beat the ground with sparing, soundless hooves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having checked the dread mountain-running leap of their dance.","st":"34.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"And overbearing Deriades assailed the male battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking the point of his horn like a helmet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Morrheus leapt raving upon the female phalanx;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Chalcomede did not stand as companion of the Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that he might have respect for her, and might check his point","st":"34.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it rushed on, purpling with the blood of women,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but then the lovely maiden, a new glorious-bowed Amazon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"took hold of the fighting as a companion of the champions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing thin robes and a flashing tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the plain; for so wise Thetis had told her, so that she might save","st":"34.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole laboring host while Dionysus was shaken."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, splitting apart the image of the Graces' face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus took eleven feeble Bassarids alive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom he chose after Chalcomede; and he clamped the hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Maenalian women behind their backs in an unloosable bond,","st":"34.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dragging the loose-haired rank beneath a slave's yoke"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave the captive handmaids to his father-in-law Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a second bride-gift for his own wife, for whose bed's sake"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had held a bride-winning battle beside high-crested Taurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he clamped in a yoking bond the young royal girl","st":"34.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Deriades, Cheirobie his age-mate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the leader of the Indians received no wedding gift"},{"sp":"N","t":"for his daughter, no lovely gold, no marble stone"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the brine, but Deriades refused herds of cattle"},{"sp":"N","t":"and flocks of sheep, and joined his daughters' weddings","st":"34.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"to battle-rousing warriors, taking no gifts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having Morrheus as son-in-law and nine-cubit Orontes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he wedded his offspring to twin champions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cheirobie to Morrheus and Protonoe to Orontes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Morrheus was not like men of earth,","st":"34.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"but with the high-necked shape of Giant limbs"},{"sp":"N","t":"he imitated the ancestral valor of the earth-born Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the native stock of sky-high Typhon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when beside the neighboring rock of the fire-reared Arimi"},{"sp":"N","t":"he displayed his kindred manhood before witnessing Cydnus,","st":"34.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing as bride-gifts of the chamber the sweats of Cilician contests,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bridegroom without possessions, who won his bride by valor."},{"sp":"N","t":"So once, before the iron that wooed the marriage of Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Assyria bent the knee, and Cilician Taurus bowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"his rocky neck to the yoke of Deriades,","st":"34.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Cydnus sank down; whence in the land of the Cilicians"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus is still called Sandes Heracles."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things were among former times; but in the later tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus took the raving women alive with unsparing spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and glorying he shrieked out an unbridled cry:","st":"34.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"To you, scepter-bearer, I bring first the Bacchants as treasures"},{"sp":"N","t":"for your daughter, and hereafter I shall grant you Bacchus himself.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Morrheus so spoke, the lord of the Indians answered:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Having Cheirobie without bride-price, helmet-glancing Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you gave me worthy bride-gifts of a shield-bearing wedlock,","st":"34.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"enslaving the cities of the Cilicians with a high-manly victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"Now again you grant new gifts; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"take other Bassarids as plunder, and fill the whole house"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Cheirobie with handmaids; and about Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of Morrheus, but fettering him in bonds none can escape","st":"34.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself shall drag Dionysus into a slave's yoke-strap."},{"sp":"N","t":"Only guard yourself for me against longing for a spear-won bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I see you wholly like the woman-mad Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not watch the eyes and the silvery neck of a Bacchant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest in your longing you make my daughter jealous.","st":"34.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when I have destroyed the whole army of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to the Maeonian land, whence I shall draw off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unspeakable wealth of the Lydians, all that Pactolus increases;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall come to vine-rich Phrygia, where Rhea is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the child-tender of Bromius, and I shall destroy the neighboring","st":"34.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"rich plain of silver Alybe, so that I may carry off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bright snowy backs of its wealth-flowing metals;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall sack the land of seven-gated Thebes, as they call it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and burn the flaming house of Semele, where the bridal chambers"},{"sp":"N","t":"bear the hot remains of a withering wedlock.\"","st":"34.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"The lawless king spoke, and Deriades received"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole rank of handmaids, the gifts of the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his warlike son-in-law; and he gave Bacchants to Phlogius"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Agraeus, dragged by the hair; and in a plaited bond"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbreakable straps were girdled about their palms.","st":"34.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Phlogius, leading them as messengers of the royal victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"paraded them bound through the city. And stretched aloft"},{"sp":"N","t":"some were squeezed beside the gateways of the well-carved hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"their necks entwined in a throttling bond;"},{"sp":"N","t":"to others he gave the hot doom of a fiery destruction;","st":"34.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others were entombed in the ground-dug streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the hollows of a well, where from the deep bosoms"},{"sp":"N","t":"the water is drawn up, forced by alternating hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, half-seen within the deepening liquid hollow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken, spoke with an answering voice:","st":"34.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I have heard that earth and water were gods for the Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and this is never said in vain; for both together"},{"sp":"N","t":"have armed themselves against me of one mind, and I am in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of an earthly death and a watery destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I have a twin-yoked doom near me; for a strange","st":"34.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"muddy bond holds me, and I lift my sole no longer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, having rooted my fixed knees in the wet clay,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I stand unshaken, ready for the Fates;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a river pursued me too, and I did not tremble at poured water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"would that this too were a resounding stream, so that here too,","st":"34.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing with my hands, I might cut through the black water.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke; and receiving little by little the streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"that flowed down into her opened throat, she died by an unburied doom."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Morrheus, fettered by the sweet goad of Chalcomede,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove the whole ironless army of the Maenalian women","st":"34.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the beetling city, pricking them from behind with his spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when a shepherd, driving into the recesses of a capacious fold"},{"sp":"N","t":"the scattered ranks of mingled sheep into one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steers the woolly flocks with his crook"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a body, and many herdsmen march with him","st":"34.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the sheep in a yoked trail of the forearm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fitted in rows, driving them headlong, lest ever"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wandering swarm should stray aside as the flock is shut in;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so he, penning the female throng within the gateway,"},{"sp":"N","t":"storm-footed Morrheus drove into the steep-built city","st":"34.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whole Bacchic rank, torn away from the fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring he had a fruitless guile, so that, leaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-womaned plunder of the tumult, the migrant prey,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he might drag Chalcomede beneath the yokes of slavery,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a companion of the other women, so that she might be","st":"34.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"forever his handmaid by day and his bedfellow by night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and might accomplish in turn the works of twin goddesses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the secret works of Cypris and the open loom of Athena. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And spear-wielding Morrheus was not careless; for to Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting at close quarters, he entrusted the routed female Enyo,","st":"34.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"and assailed the male battle of the Bacchic phalanx,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might shut in the men also; and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"they hurried into flight. And the stormy girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood adorned before the city near a tower,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled maiden; and with manufactured nods the girl","st":"34.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated the pattern of love-maddened women,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling her eyes, and outside her accustomed girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"her white tunic reddened at her reddening breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Morrheus delighted as he watched, and through her thin"},{"sp":"N","t":"robe he measured the swelling rim of her breast.","st":"34.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden, snatching up a well-made stone like a wheel-shaped"},{"sp":"N","t":"discus, a monstrous burden for a wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled it at well-helmeted Morrheus with knowing art;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the stone, carried from the air with a sharp whistle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the top of his shield, where a golden-wrought image","st":"34.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"held the bastard body of Cheirobie in a lying shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scraped away the manufactured head; and struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the marble point, as the face was scored,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it crumbled the well-rounded image of the mimic shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Morrheus leapt up again and again, counting his shield blessed,","st":"34.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laughing in his heart he uttered a hidden voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Fearless Chalcomede, new rosy-fingered Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate statue of Cypris and of well-corseleted Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchic dawn and never-setting Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have scraped away the painted image of my wife; would that","st":"34.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"you had reaped the neck of true Cheirobie herself.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he pursued the unyoked girl before the city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a threatening tongue, not arming his hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling speech, not pricking the maiden with his spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a sparing spear with a gentle palm;","st":"34.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he cried the deceiving Echo of a grim shout,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though a truly angry champion, yet gentle; for both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had a laughing mind, and showed anger in his face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And whirling gently he sent his tottering point"},{"sp":"N","t":"willingly at a useless mark; and she, fleeing,","st":"34.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran swift-kneed, a companion of the airy breezes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as she stretched out with the windy rush of her knee"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winds fanned up her clustered locks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"baring her neck that strove with Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Morrheus ran willingly with sparing feet,","st":"34.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"now gazing at the bare tracks of her foot from the well-stitched"},{"sp":"N","t":"sandals toward her rosy ankles, now watching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the coiling locks of her hair that strayed behind her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he pursued Chalcomede; and he cried with a sweet word,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a gentle speech from an unroaring throat:","st":"34.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Wait for me, Chalcomede, for a warrior who longs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your splendor saves you, and not your running; spear-points do not"},{"sp":"N","t":"know how to strike a man so much as the sparks of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not become your foe, do not fear; for as I fought"},{"sp":"N","t":"your beauty conquered my bronze point;","st":"34.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have no need of spear, of shield; for you have"},{"sp":"N","t":"the beam of your face as a sword, as a furious spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your cheeks play the champion far more than an ash-spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"The dread strength of my palm is loosed; for it is no cause for blame"},{"sp":"N","t":"if I have my furious spear conquered, since savage Ares","st":"34.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"himself turns womanish when Cypris stands by."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me as a companion for your Satyrs; for in the wars"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indians will play the champion, while I still arm my hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you wish, I shall serve Dionysus like a hireling;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you wish, subdue me at the neck or the flank;","st":"34.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"I care nothing for death when cut down by your point;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only groan for me when I have fallen; for the tears"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Chalcomede as she weeps will bring me back even from Hades."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, why do you tremble, because I lift a gentle spear?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Seeing your coiling locks above your bared","st":"34.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoulders, I have laid the helmet from my hair;"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at your fawnskin I loathe to wear a breastplate.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the woman passed by and mingled with the Bacchoi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having strayed from the murderous path of Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fought boldly and strove in the male battle.","st":"34.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"And then, leaving the whirl of the ill-sounding tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bacchic throng breathed again, while Morrheus withdrew."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Deriades pursued the phalanx of the Bassarids"},{"sp":"N","t":"before the city, striking with his sword, until he drove them near the towers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"penning the whole rank within the opened gateway","st":"34.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the high-crested wall; and pursued by the iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"they came inside the city, torn from their accustomed wood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unsteady phalanxes marched this way and that,"},{"sp":"N","t":"divided, along unaccustomed circles of road, toward the wing of Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the ridge of Zephyrus by the western region of the land,","st":"34.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others wandering beside the floor of Notus, and others"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Boreas the Bassarids were routed; and the maenads"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanged their male-spirited necessity, and became women again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and refused the fighting, and remembered the distaff-loving"},{"sp":"N","t":"craft and the baskets, and longed again to tend","st":"34.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thread of Athena and no longer the sacred emblems of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the dark-skinned man, destroying the snowy rank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held an inner tumult of a city-holding fight."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"In the thirty-fifth seek the hostile love of Morrheus, the slaughter of the Bassarids and the war of women."},{"sp":"N","t":"And measureless Deriades fought in a raving battle,","st":"35.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lord of the Indians assailed the attendants of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now hurling with his long spear, now cutting down"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his hilted sword, and he was carried along shooting"},{"sp":"N","t":"with torrent-borne missiles and with sharper arrows.","st":"35.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"So they were routed through the city within the towers"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the spear of Deriades; and in the many-tongued tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a din of both; and with reddening gore"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-stoned streets of the city were purpled"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the women were killed with a ringing cry in the city.","st":"35.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"And unbending old men upon the high-crested towers"},{"sp":"N","t":"watched the fighting; and over the roofs the women themselves"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed at the whole thyrsus-bearing rank;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a trailing-robed maiden, leaning over the hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside her nurse, looked upon the female war,","st":"35.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, heavy with tears, groaned over a slain girl of her own age."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did any man take a lovely bride and force her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the king commanded his woman-mad peoples"},{"sp":"N","t":"to flee the hostile beds of spear-won weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, caring for the Paphian, they should be careless of Enyo.","st":"35.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a maiden, skipping about the dust upon the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was stripped bare; and as her tunic was dragged aside"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was armed with splendor, and wounded her longing slayer"},{"sp":"N","t":"though she herself was wounded, and her beauty was her shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and perishing she conquered; and against her adversary her naked","st":"35.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"thighs were armed, the archers of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now, holding a breathless love for a corpse, like Achilles"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing upon another Penthesileia on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would have embraced the cold lips of the dust-covered bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had he not feared the mass of the threat of Deriades.","st":"35.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he gazed at the flesh of the naked girl who refused him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watched her white ankles and the fold of her uncovered thighs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and touched her limbs, and often laid hold of her swelling"},{"sp":"N","t":"rosy breast, still like an apple;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he wished to mingle with her in love; and at length, laboring,","st":"35.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched out such a lovely, witless voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Rosy-armed maiden, you have wounded your unhappy-loving slayer"},{"sp":"N","t":"though wounded yourself; perishing, you subdue the living,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you shoot your destroyer with your eyelids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"my spear has been conquered by your beauty; the sparkles","st":"35.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your face rout me as much as the points of javelins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have a breast like a bow, since your breasts play the champion"},{"sp":"N","t":"more than arrows, the archers of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have a strange and unbelievable longing, because I pursue"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dead love of a girl, of a wedding that has perished;","st":"35.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"a breathless sting holds me, the breathing; if it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let these lips become voiced and breathing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may hear a word from your sweet mouth, maiden, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying out such a speech: 'Leave the girl rolling on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom you killed, whom you plundered, reckless one;","st":"35.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not touch the tunic of her whom your bronze cut down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"why do you grip the flank you wounded? Cease handling"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wound of a wretched woman, which you gave me.' Away with the spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"away with the boldness of my palm, because, leaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sileni bristling with grey hair","st":"35.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the whole misshapen race of the Satyrs, instead of old men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of shaggy-breasted creatures, it subdued a delicate woman."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, handling the wound of your desire-stricken flesh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what herb shall I find, traversing the ridge of the fair-pastured wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what helper of your wound shall I call to you,","st":"35.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"what aged, life-bringing Chiron? Or whence shall I find"},{"sp":"N","t":"drugs, the rites of the toil-releasing art of Paeon?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I longed to have the Centaurian herb, as they call it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, winding the pain-killing flower about your limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I might save you living out of Hades that none returns from.","st":"35.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"What magic hymn have I, or starry song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, pouring forth a melody with a god-invoking Euian voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I may lull the blood of your wounded flank?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I longed to have here a life-bearing spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, casting pain-killing water upon your limbs,","st":"35.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"I might soothe your lovely wound, so that I might bring back"},{"sp":"N","t":"even your own soul into you, recovered again."},{"sp":"N","t":"Glaucus, who roll the whirl of wide-scattered years,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if it is lawful, leaving the recess of the barren sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"show me the life-sustaining plant, show me that one","st":"35.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"of which you once tasted with your lips, and draw an immortal"},{"sp":"N","t":"life, wheeling in the trail of ever-flowing time.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he passed on, hiding a dead longing in his heart."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bride Protonoe leapt up, the avenger of her slain"},{"sp":"N","t":"husband, groaning still for the dead Orontes;","st":"35.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she passed through the female phalanx; and one might see"},{"sp":"N","t":"another Erythraean Atalanta, a match for men."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cheirobie, taking a shield and the spear of Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"assailed the Bassarids, and was like Gorge,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, when well-towered Calydon was shaken,","st":"35.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting the ox-hide shield of her brother Toxeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought, woman though she was, against angry Meleager."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Orsiboe, appearing with her battle-rousing consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated the courage of warlike Deianeira,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when beside the ill-guesting rock of Parnassus","st":"35.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"she armed herself against the Dryopes and became a female Amazon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many were shut in within the broad-spaced halls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was unspeakable groaning of a tumult under the roofs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another maiden, battle-rousing, entered the fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the road, and over the roofs other women","st":"35.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed themselves with stony missiles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the inner phalanxes crashed out Enyo."},{"sp":"N","t":"While battle-rousing Ares roared through the city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting down the Lydian mountain-running tribes of the Bassarids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile Chalcomede stood alone before the wall,","st":"35.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"having turned her homeward course from the war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"awaiting gadfly-mad Morrheus, if from anywhere he should come;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and then, stretching a much-coiling love-maddened eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he perceived the maiden, wind-footed Morrheus came,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his knees more swiftly into a lovely course.","st":"35.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a breeze lifted the robe of the pursued girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was charmed still more by the unclothed beauty of her shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the white, unveiled bride as she ran ahead."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she beguiled him, and spoke with a shamefaced voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the swifter course of Morrheus:","st":"35.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"If you truly seek my bed, bridegroom Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay down your iron breastplate, as Ares dances"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a wedding, soft-tunicked, when he mingles with Cypris,"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered in a snowy garment, like Apollo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that Desire and Cypris may yoke us both","st":"35.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a single bolt as mounters of a bridal bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Eros yoking Morrheus, and Aphrodite Chalcomede."},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not receive a bronze husband upon my bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening with blood and squalid with dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but brighten your body in a stream, so that you may appear","st":"35.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Phaethon bathed in the outpourings of Ocean;"},{"sp":"N","t":"cast away your Enyalian shield, cast away your point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest your death-bearing spear ever strike me;"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay down for me the frightful helmet from your hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the crest of your shaken helm routs me;","st":"35.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not see the bastard look of an iron face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what longing gladdens me when your shape is covered?"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer shall I set foot in Maeonia; nor in the bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall I receive, if you wish, Bacchus as consort after Morrheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too shall be an Indian woman, friend; and instead of a Lydian","st":"35.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall glorify Erythraean Aphrodite with sacrifices,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the secret bedfellow of Morrheus; and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"let an Indian man have me, fighting beside Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for sending equal shafts at you and double ones at me"},{"sp":"N","t":"Desire has joined a single distress for us both,","st":"35.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the heart for Morrheus and into the mind for Chalcomede."},{"sp":"N","t":"I labored hiding my longing for you; for a shamefaced"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden does not challenge a consort to Aphrodite.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking the woman persuaded the warrior unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying to him; and laughing, Morrheus said, ill in love:","st":"35.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"It is no cause for blame that Morrheus the well-helmeted warrior"},{"sp":"N","t":"should have a bronze spear in the bridal chamber of Chalcomede,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may embrace you, well-named one, lifting bronze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless I renounce my blood-red spear, nor do I touch"},{"sp":"N","t":"my shield; and as you wish, bathed, I dance toward you","st":"35.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bloodless hands, and I shall be another consort,","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"naked Ares after the fighting, holding a naked Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"I refuse the daughter of Deriades; I myself shall drive"},{"sp":"N","t":"my jealous bride unwilling out of my halls;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do I arm myself against the Bassarids, if you command me,","st":"35.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I shall fight against my own dear dwellers; I shall destroy an Indian"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a wine-dark thyrsus, not lifting a bronze spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall cast away all my armor and shake slender flowers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting beside your king Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking Morrheus shook the ash-spear from his palm,","st":"35.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unclamped the crest from his sweating head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and flung away the strap of his drenched ox-hide shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bathed with the well-toiled drop of his accustomed shoulder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he loosed the bronze tunic from his breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bloody breastplate. And Cypris showed to Ares","st":"35.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"the armor of longing Morrheus lying on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquered by the unarmored shape of Chalcomede;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she spoke some word, provoking her consort:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Ares, you have been plundered; Morrheus has refused wars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing no breastplate and no sword; but longing for a lovely","st":"35.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"woman he has shaken the armor from his hands."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you too refuse your furious spear, do you too bathe"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the sea, stripped of your shields; for unwarlike"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris plays the champion more than Ares, and has no need"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a shield, and never lacks an ash-spear; for both together,","st":"35.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"my beauty was my spear, my shape was my sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the beams of my eyelids have become my arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a breast hurls farther than a spear; for lovely Morrheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"has become a chamber-servant instead of a spear-bold man."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not set foot in Sparta, where the warlike citizens","st":"35.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"have a bronze image of armed Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she, holding a spear, strike you with your own iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"You do not hurl the spear so well as eyebrows do; spear-points do not"},{"sp":"N","t":"wound men so much as eyes strike them;"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold your servants, the attendants of the Loves,","st":"35.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bend your bold neck to unconquered Cytherea."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares, you have been conquered, because, leaving his bronze spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus has put on the bridal fawnskin of Chalcomede.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Laughter-loving Aphrodite spoke, laughing at the battles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mocking Ares the bride-adorner; and near the sea,","st":"35.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving his uncared-for tunic upon the shore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Morrheus bathed, warmed with sweet cares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"naked; and he brightened his body in the cold sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the little hot shaft of the Paphian; and in the streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"he entreated Indian Erythraean Aphrodite,","st":"35.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"having heard that Cypris is the offspring of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having bathed he came up black again; and he had the shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"that nature had brought forth, and the brine did not change the body"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the man, did not change his color, reddening though it was."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he bathed his flesh upon an empty hope; for lovely,","st":"35.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"he longed to appear snowy to the unyoked girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he adorned his body with a snowy linen robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as warriors always wear beneath the breastplate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing voiceless on the shore, guileful Chalcomede"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept silence; and the girl, turning away,","st":"35.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew aside her prudent gaze from unclothed Morrheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in shame at the uncovered body of the man; for being a woman"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl was in awe to look upon a bathed male."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he saw a lonely place fitting for a bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a bold palm toward the shamefaced bride","st":"35.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"he touched the untouched garment of the prudent girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now, taking her girdled about in a man's bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would have forced the raving girl with a bridal spark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but a serpent leapt up from her undefiled bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the helper of the unwedded maiden, and about her girdle","st":"35.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"it wheeled abundant with the guarding trail of its belly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as it hissed sharply from never-silent throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rocks bellowed; and Morrheus was whirled in fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the bellowing of a bastard trumpet from its neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the shield-bearer of her unwedded maidenhood;","st":"35.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the coiling champion terrified the warrior,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling its crook-circling tail about the man's neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a furious mouth for spear; and many viperish arrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"were shot, hurling venom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"some darting from her unbound hair,","st":"35.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"others through the loins of the serpent-haired girl, others from her bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"warriors hissing Ares, raved in Bacchic frenzy."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Morrheus stood before the high-crested city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing guileful Chalcomede toward an unaccomplished wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the well-armed Enyo of the Bassarid army","st":"35.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"escaped the spear of unwearied Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"For swift-winged Hermes, leaping from Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing an answering image of the face of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"called the whole Bacchic rank with a mystic voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when the women heard the divine Euian echo,","st":"35.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"they came to a single place; and bringing the whole nation"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Maenalian women from the crossroads along crooked"},{"sp":"N","t":"circles of road, the swift-sandaled god led them, until he came near the towers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and upon the unsleeping eyes of the guards in their rows"},{"sp":"N","t":"thievish Hermes, a champion of the night, poured sweet sleep","st":"35.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his all-charming rod; and suddenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the Indians there was darkness, but for the unseen Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was an unlooked-for light; and of the soundless women"},{"sp":"N","t":"wingless Hermes was the secret guide through the city;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with divine hand he opened the mighty bolt","st":"35.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the sky-high gates, and became a sun for the Bacchants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when light-bearing Hermes had driven off the daytime night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"overbearing Deriades, holding an unaccomplished threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sought the swarm of Bassarids that had left the city as wayfarers."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when someone by night in deep-wealthy dreams","st":"35.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"delights in fruitless hopes, lifting"},{"sp":"N","t":"a pouring of short-lived wealth with rich palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"feeding a deceiving hope of slumbering possessions;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but when rosy dawn appears"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vision of the wealthy dream withdraws, loosed again,","st":"35.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he wakes with empty palms, lifting nothing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having cast away the shadowy delight of mind-stealing dreams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so then Deriades, while darkness held the streets,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighted, thinking to tend a self-offered catch"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Bassarids shut in within the closed gateways,","st":"35.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a lying, useless, shadowy victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but when the light shone, and he no longer saw the Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"everything ran off like a dream, and he cried with a mourning voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and was angry at Zeus and Phaethon and Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the fugitive Maenalian women. And about the towers","st":"35.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unveiled Bassarids cried out with a Euian voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Deriades pursued a second time; and Zeus woke"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the summits of Caucasus, casting off the wing of Sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and learning the beguiling guile of evil-working Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"he watched the Sileni routed, he saw the Bacchants","st":"35.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurrying in throngs from the crossroads, from the towers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he perceived Deriades behind them, the leader of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shearing the Satyrs and mowing down the women,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his son lying upon the ground; and about him the brides"},{"sp":"N","t":"were near, crown-wise; and in a whirl of dust he lay","st":"35.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-headed, stretching a feeble panting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling snowy foam, the witness of his madness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he convicted the ill-devised guile of envious Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blamed his guileful consort with a goading word;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he would have shut Sleep away in a misty pit","st":"35.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a sharer of the hearth of gloomy Iapetus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Night entreated him, the subduer of gods and men."},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely lulling his deadly anger he cried to Hera:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Are you not yet sated with my Semele, hard-fighting Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but are you perhaps angry at her still though she is dead? Did not","st":"35.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridal flame itself soothe your endless rancor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scattering the bed of Thyone struck by Zeus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"How long will you bear down upon Indian-slaying Dionysus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Respect your former anvils again; they are still,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those helpers are still with me, which I bound and clamped","st":"35.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon your feet; and you, restless above the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held an aloft necessity in air and clouds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Ares saw you bound aloft above the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entwined among the clouds, and did not help his mother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery Hephaestus did not avail; for he cannot","st":"35.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure one spark of the smoky thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bind your palms again in the accustomed golden bond;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall fetter Ares in unbreakable chains none can escape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lash his whole body with incurable blows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon a self-rolling wheel that runs with him, like wandering","st":"35.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"air-roaming Tantalus or migrant Ixion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until my child conquers the sons of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will do a favor to your Cronides, if you drive off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the much-dreaded madness of lashed Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not leave your consort angry, but going","st":"35.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"untouchable beneath the well-pastured slope of the Indian wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"hold out your breast to Bacchus after my mother Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that with his more perfect lips he may draw"},{"sp":"N","t":"your holy drop, the forerunner of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and make the upper air passable for earthly Dionysus;","st":"35.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"and anointing the body of Lyaeus with your milk"},{"sp":"N","t":"quench the misshapen defilements of his wit-robbing sickness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I shall prepare a worthy prize for you; for I shall fix"},{"sp":"N","t":"in Olympus a circle like your dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"named for the milk of Hera, so that I may glorify","st":"35.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"the moisture of your evil-averting breast, of which all speak;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only guard yourself against the child-loving threat of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not prepare yet another guile against Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he sent forth his hostile consort Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the averter by necessity of the Bacchic misery,","st":"35.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"gracious, fair to meet, to dismayed Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might anoint the body of Bromius with milky dews"},{"sp":"N","t":"from her god-nourishing breasts with her hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera was not careless; and anointing the body of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the divine drop of her pain-healing teat","st":"35.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shook off the savage defilements of the divine madness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she had a twofold envy with a face that hid it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing the manhood and the splendor of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with envious palms she touched maddened Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drawing aside her tunic about his mouth","st":"35.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bared her own teat full of ambrosia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing a pressed pouring from a jealous breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she brought him back to life; and with long eyes"},{"sp":"N","t":"she measured the great youth of long-haired Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wondering whether an earthly belly had ever borne such a form,","st":"35.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether spear-wielding Ares was so great, or Hermes so great,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether Phaethon was such, or lovely-voiced Apollo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she longed to have him in the upper air as the bridegroom of Hebe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not high-ruling Zeus after a time nodded to her"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Heracles of the twelve labors was her fated consort.","st":"35.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, having warded off the mad labor of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went up high-visible a second time to the chorus of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should behold the ironless army of Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting with fennel-stalk and vine cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the champions killed by a little man-breaking thyrsus.","st":"35.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was the son of Zeus careless of battle, but arming"},{"sp":"N","t":"his warriors he cried a host-rousing voice, turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his flesh-cutting ivy with a Giant-slaying hand:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Fight boldly a second time; for in the wars"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus again stands as our champion, gracious to his son","st":"35.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus, and from heaven the chorus of immortals has come"},{"sp":"N","t":"shielding Dionysus, and Hera is angry no longer."},{"sp":"N","t":"Who shall fight against the lightning of Cronides? Or when do cowardly"},{"sp":"N","t":"adversaries abide the thunderbolt as it arms itself?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall appear equal to my begetter; for in the wars","st":"35.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"my Cronion conquered the earth-born Titans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I too shall conquer the race of ground-born Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Today you shall behold, after a cluster-bearing victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades a slow-persuaded suppliant, and the chorus of Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"bowing the neck to calm Dionysus,","st":"35.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the river holding the wine-tottering Euian water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you shall see your adversaries beside the mixing-bowl of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the yellow water from the wine-bearing river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bold Indian king, held fast by wine-dark ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wound about with leaves and a vine cluster,","st":"35.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing bonds like those which, after the waves of madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the god-fearing Nysian Nymphs still cry aloud,"},{"sp":"N","t":"witnesses of my strength, when my bold-working harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having clamped the god-fighting man in a throttling bond"},{"sp":"N","t":"of ivy, terrified Arabia,","st":"35.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Lycurgus was forced with a clustered knot."},{"sp":"N","t":"But after the circles of so great a rolling tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"plunder the spoil of the foes and the treasure of the brine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the marble stones, and remove to my mother Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the women dragged by the hair;","st":"35.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"and avenge the fallen champions, for whose doom"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am worn with sharp cares; and in my heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"I both rage and grieve together, because I behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades living and Opheltes unburied,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming after his doom the idle hand of Lyaeus;","st":"35.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer does Codone arm herself, no longer does wretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear-wielding Alcimacheia fight; but Aebialus himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"too has been subdued, and I still hold back my thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed before Arestor after the fighting, lest he hear"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Opheltes, dying, found no helper as a corpse;","st":"35.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"I cannot pass through the city of Corybantic Crete,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest his father Agelaus lament his perished child,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing of the unavenged slaughter of Antheus as he perished;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to appear to Minos; for in his hut"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterius labors, stricken, whom beyond others","st":"35.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall save; for he has the race of Europa; but having saved him"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bring my kinsman back sound to his father"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the fighting, so that Cadmus may not hear"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Asterius lacked a war-fleeing Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But fight again, and I shall help you all in one,","st":"35.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"killing one single destroyer of so many.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXVI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the thirty-sixth, after the defilements of madness, Bacchus arms against Deriades, changing his shape."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he heartened his rejoicing leaders;","st":"36.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"and on the other side Deriades armed his warriors."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the gods who dwell in Olympus, divided,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set out for both phalanxes as steersmen of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"some the helpers of Deriades, others of Lyaeus.","st":"36.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"And lord Zeus, high-seated among the blessed ones, high above Cerne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held the scales of Ares aslant; and from heaven"},{"sp":"N","t":"the watery dark-haired god challenged the fiery"},{"sp":"N","t":"sun, Ares the grey-eyed goddess, Hephaestus Hydaspes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mountain Artemis stood on the opposite path to Hera;","st":"36.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hermes of the fair rod came to fight the daughter of Leto."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the twice-crashing echo of the divine war roared"},{"sp":"N","t":"for both sets of blessed ones; and as they rushed into the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"seven-acre Ares fought against Tritogeneia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sent his furious spear; and he struck the middle","st":"36.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the aegis of the unwoundable goddess, and of the head none may behold","hvShift6":[[0,0],[45,46],[49,47],[50,49],[60,51]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck the snaky crop of the Gorgon's hair,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[4,5],[20,22],[29,24]]},{"sp":"N","t":"wounding the shaggy shield of Pallas; and the sharp-stretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"point of the unbending spear, sent with a whistle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the manufactured lock of the counterfeit Medusa.","st":"36.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the battle-rousing girl, rushing on in her turn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"motherless Pallas, lifted her kindred spear against Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that spear which, bearing it as her childbed weapon of age-mate bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she leapt with from the generative head of her father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And measureless Ares, struck, bent his knee to the floor;","st":"36.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Athena, setting him upright again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave him unwounded after the fighting to Hera his dear mother."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Artemis strove with Hera as the mountain fellow-contender"},{"sp":"N","t":"of mountain-ranging Dionysus, and curved her straight-stretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"bow; and in an emulous tumult","st":"36.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hera, taking a cloud of Zeus thickened about her shoulders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held it as an unbreakable shield; and Artemis, sending"},{"sp":"N","t":"one wandering shaft after another through the airy circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"emptied her quiver at a useless mark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and thickened the whole unbreakable cloud with arrows;","st":"36.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was a mimic, air-roaming pattern of cranes"},{"sp":"N","t":"flying crown-wise in some alternating circle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the arrows were fixed in the shadowy cloud;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unscored veil had bloodless wounds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Hera lifted a rocky, wind-borne missile,","st":"36.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirling in her hand the frozen backs of hail"},{"sp":"N","t":"she dashed Artemis with a torrent-borne shaft;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the marble point crushed the curved circles of her bow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did the wife of Zeus check the battle; but she struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"the very middle of the top of Artemis's breast; and struck","st":"36.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the frosty spear she poured her quiver on the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bride of Zeus spoke, laughing at her:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Artemis, shoot beasts; why do you match yourself against your betters?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And set foot on the crags; what is war to you? Wearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"paltry hunting-boots, leave greaves to Athena;","st":"36.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shake your guile-weaving nets; for beast-slaying"},{"sp":"N","t":"hounds hunt for you, and not winged arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you do not wield a lion-slaying shaft; for the sweats"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your feeble labors are feeble hares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"care for your deer and for your well-horned chariot,","st":"36.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"care for your deer; why should you honor the son of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the driver of leopards and charioteer of lions?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you wish, keep your bow, because Eros stretches a bow too;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bed-fleeing maiden, midwife of birth, you ought to have"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cestus, the ferry of the Loves, as helper of childbirth,","st":"36.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the Paphian, with Eros; for you rule over birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, steersman of birth-perfecting generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"go to the bridal chamber of child-bearing women,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, shooting women with your childbed shafts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be like a lion beside a bride in childbed,","st":"36.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"a midwife instead of a war-lover. But cease"},{"sp":"N","t":"your prudence even for the sake of a prudent girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, wearing the pattern of your limbs, high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"weds unwedded maidens; still the Arcadian woods"},{"sp":"N","t":"cry aloud that image of yours, the marriage-thief,","st":"36.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-adorner of unwedded Callisto, and the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"still groan for your intelligent witness, the bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming the bastard, love-maddened shape of the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when a female consort entered the bed of a female."},{"sp":"N","t":"But casting away your useless quiver","st":"36.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave the fighting of your better, Hera; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"make war as goddess of childbirth against marriage-perfecting Cytherea.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and Hera passed by Artemis as she suffered."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Phoebus, bringing her, drunk with fear, from the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraced her with both downcast arms,","st":"36.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and led her and set her within a lonely thicket;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and returning untouchable he mingled in the divine fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fiery champion stood facing the champion of the deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phoebus against the Poseidonian war; and about his string"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set a shaft and lifted a torch of Delphic pine","st":"36.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"with both palms, ambidextrous, so that he might arm"},{"sp":"N","t":"his radiance and his bow against the wavy trail and the trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the smoky point and the watery arrows"},{"sp":"N","t":"fell upon one another; and as Phoebus armed himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"his father's upper air crashed out the melody of Ares,","st":"36.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"a thunderous din; and a stormy trumpet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sea-borne Echo, resounded in the ears of Phoebus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and broad-bearded Triton boomed with his accustomed conch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"man-formed, half-finished, from the loins a green fish;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Nereids shouted; and peering above the sea","st":"36.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arabian Nereus bellowed as the trident was shaken."},{"sp":"N","t":"And hearing the sound of the heavenly phalanx above him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Zeus of the underworld cried out, lest the Earth-shaker, battering"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth as it was lashed by the earth-shaking throb of the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should heave aside the harmony of the cosmos with his trident,","st":"36.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, moving the foundation of the nether pits,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he should make the seat of the unseen earth visible,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he should break open the whole vein of the deep haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring migrant water into the Tartarean recess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"deluging the moldering nether gateway.","st":"36.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"So great a din then arose as the gods came together in strife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the nether trumpets roared; and gentle Hermes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting his rod, reined them both back;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the three immortals he joined a single voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Brother of Zeus, and son, do you, glorious archer,","st":"36.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave torch and bow to the storms, and you the point of your trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the Titans laugh at the tumult of the blessed ones,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after the Cronian war that threatened Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares be a second civil strife among the immortals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I see another battle after the tumult of Iapetus,","st":"36.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lest, after Zagreus and about later-born Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus in anger, burning the whole earth with his fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should deluge a second time the rim of the ever-flowing cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raining poured upper air with waters; and let me not behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chariot of Selene drenched in airy seas;","st":"36.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"let not Phaethon have a cold gleam again instead of a fiery one."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yield to the elder steersman of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching out a favor to your father's brother, because"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Earth-shaker, the ruler of the brine, honors your sea-girt Delos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let not love of the palm and memory of the olive leave you.","st":"36.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who here again, Earth-shaker, is a Cecrops to judge,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who again is another Inachus to cry his city to Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that you should arm yourself against Apollo too, as against Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and have another battle after your earlier fighting with Hera?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And you, horned father of great Deriades,","st":"36.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"beware the radiance of Hephaestus after the torch of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he burn you with his fire-pointed thunderbolt.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he checked the civil Enyo of the gods."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then raging, heavy-wrathful Deriades took hold"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a recovered battle, when he saw the Bacchants unharmed;","st":"36.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watching the fighting of Dionysus sound again"},{"sp":"N","t":"he goaded his routed leaders into the fray;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he crashed out a barbarian threat, common to foot-soldiers"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to horsemen, from his heavy-sounding throats:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Today either I shall drag Dionysus by the hair,","st":"36.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the Bacchic war shall wipe out the race of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you set up a defending necessity against the Satyrs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Deriades arm himself against Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And burn the leaves of the vines and the varied instruments"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Bacchus, and set fire to their huts; and bring the Maenalian women","st":"36.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"as handmaids to boastful Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wither the hostile thyrsi with fire; and, having reaped"},{"sp":"N","t":"with crushing iron the cornfield of the wide-scattered heads"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the ox-horned Sileni and Satyrs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crown all my halls with their bull-horned heads.","st":"36.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let Phaethon not turn his fire-gleaming horses to the west"},{"sp":"N","t":"before I bring Bacchus and the Satyrs clamped"},{"sp":"N","t":"in fetters none can escape, and him spotted, cut down by my point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a torn tunic upon his breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having cast away his thyrsus; and turn to ash with my brand","st":"36.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine hair of the long-haired women."},{"sp":"N","t":"And be of good courage, and after the Indian battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"sing the man-glorifying victory of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that even the army of later-born men may shudder"},{"sp":"N","t":"to strive with the unconquered earth-born Indians.\"","st":"36.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and going about among his champions one after another"},{"sp":"N","t":"he goaded the drivers of measureless-lived elephants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set the escorts of the foot-soldiers upon the throng,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting tower-wise. And in an emulous tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsus-mad Dionysus drove the rank of wilderness-ranging","st":"36.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"beasts into the fray in Bacchic frenzy; and the mountain-reared"},{"sp":"N","t":"warriors raved with a roaring at the divine whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a maddened beast was armed from their mouths;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and serpents, spitting from raw-devouring teeth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent far-throwing springs of venom into the air","st":"36.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the hissing gape of a bellowing throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping aslant; and leaping at the adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"the viperish arrows had a self-chosen mark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bodies of the Indians were girdled with crooked coils"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were coiled about, and a cord clamped mortal feet","st":"36.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they darted into their course. And the war-mad women"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitated the fighting of serpent-hurling Phidaleia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, holding the goad of a womanish tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquered her adversaries with viperish clusters. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, sending a long-shadowed spear from its mouth,","st":"36.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"spat a javelin-hurling venom at Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his steel breastplate was wetted with a murderous drop."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a corpse lay on the ground, struck by a living missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"breathless, tending a breathing shaft. And upon the arched crest"},{"sp":"N","t":"of straight-footed elephants a leopard, leaping up,","st":"36.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"was suspended aloft with a leap of its soles;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it fixed itself thickly upon the beast's head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and checked the course of the long-shanked elephants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a great swarm fell, hearing from heavy-crashing throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread roaring of wilderness-ranging lions;","st":"36.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one was conquered, trembling at the bellowing of a bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing the point of the grim horn of the ox"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling itself aslant into the air; and roaming,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another rushed into flight, cowering before the jaw of a bear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one after another, resounding with the beasts' cries,","st":"36.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hound of unconquered Pan bayed with it from its throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the blazing Indians feared the barking battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Enyo was common and yoked to both;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the thirsty earth surged with the gore of slaughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were killed on either side, and as the wide-scattered were subdued","st":"36.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lethe was crowded with so great a multitude of corpses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Hades, heaving up the gloomy bolt with his hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"opened the broader gateways of his halls"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were killed on either side, and as they darted through the pit"},{"sp":"N","t":"the banks of Charon crashed out a Tartarean bellowing.","st":"36.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a great battle-rousing din, and the wound"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the slain adversaries was of a strange kind, of whom one"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped from his horse, struck on the throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another down the well-rounded rim of his breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, pierced in the middle of the flank, fell from his car;","st":"36.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, struck by a sharp-pointed arrow beside the top of the navel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-rolling, kept company with a neighboring doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another struck above the middle rim, another through the shoulder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another fell fleeing, his back pierced by a point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on foot, having left his storm-swift horse when it was struck;","st":"36.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, falling, beardless, lamented the youth he had grown with;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, his liver scored by an incurable shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed head first from an elephant into the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laying his head aside on the floor, and, winding his hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraced the bloody earth with a downcast arm.","st":"36.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a man stood slanting against a horseman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and filled the hollow of his shield with poured dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fixed his sole in the ground, awaiting the man's rush;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretching out his much-wrought shield with bold hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sprinkled the whole gaze of the horse with sand;","st":"36.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raving with its head, with face nodding upward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horse was lifted, shaking its dusty hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spat out the curved tips of its fair-stoned bridle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rubbing its crook-toothed jaw sprinkled with foam"},{"sp":"N","t":"it was shaken, stretched high, and tossing its upright neck","st":"36.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"the goaded, unbridled beast propped itself upon the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its hind feet, and, darting dust with its hoof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled its driver off into the plain."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the sharp-toothed man ran swiftly at him as he lay,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a naked, swift sword; and as the dark champion","st":"36.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay stretched upon the ground he cut through his throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"Another wandering colt, much-dreaded, withdrew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the sound of the whip of a neighboring driver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"treading over its own pitiable driver as he died,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying freshly cut down, gasping in the dust.","st":"36.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"And measureless Colletes, having a vast shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hard to fight, nine cubits high, like Alcyoneus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raged in the midst of the Bacchic fighting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he longed to drag the phalanx of the Bassarids after the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a bed of forced weddings without bride-price,","st":"36.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made war upon an empty hope, so great a man,"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as bold Otus was, treading the pathless upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for the chaste bed of the unwedded archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"such as cloud-high Ephialtes was, loving the wedlock"},{"sp":"N","t":"of pure Athena, hurling himself at Olympus;","st":"36.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"such was Colletes, surpassing, a neighbor of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the god-fighting blood of his earth-born forefather,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first-born Indus; and he was sufficient in his shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"to bind furious Ares after the sons of Iphimedeia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but a woman killed him, great as he was, with a sharp stone,","st":"36.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"Charopeia, the steersman of the Bacchic dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, seeing the high-necked girl playing the champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing wonder with anger, uttered a trembling voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Ares, Ares, leave your bows and shield and your lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares, you have been plundered, leave Caucasus; for Dionysus","st":"36.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"draws other man-slaying Amazons into the fray;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shieldless rout the weapon-bearers; for he did not bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"his women from your Thermodon."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have seen a strange and unbelievable pattern; the Amazons"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Dionysus have no shield on their shoulders, no furious spear;","st":"36.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"the well-corseleted Caucasian women do not play"},{"sp":"N","t":"the champion so well; but the Bacchants hurl leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"from shoot-loving hands, and have no need of iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas for maddened Deriades, because women"},{"sp":"N","t":"split bronze tunics apart with their nails.\"","st":"36.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, astonished at the rocky missile, such as the Bacchant"},{"sp":"N","t":"took up and killed so great a high-headed man."},{"sp":"N","t":"And untouchable Deriades ran upon the raving Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursued stone-hurling Charope; and she, fleeing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought boldly, standing beside Dionysus,","st":"36.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling her flower-loving thyrsus in a Euian battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Deriades crushed Orithallus with his iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a kinsman of the Curetes, a citizen of the Abantian field."},{"sp":"N","t":"And in anger for his fallen comrade the leader of the Abantes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Melisseus, laid low the king of the Carminians,","st":"36.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cyllarus, striking his neck with his sharp sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the son of Logasus, who alone, since he was a wise warrior,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was the care of Deriades beyond the spear-bold Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the king loved him next after Morrheus; and often, with","hvShift6":[[0,0],[28,27]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Orsiboe herself and the king, he touched a single table,","st":"36.285","hvShift6":[[0,16],[3,29],[55,55]]},{"sp":"N","t":"sharing the hearth of the king's daughters; for in both"},{"sp":"N","t":"spear and wits he surpassed the youth he had grown with."},{"sp":"N","t":"There many a champion strove with champion; and high-visible"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-footed Halimedes fought against Peucetius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Maron armed against Phlogius and Leneus against Thureus.","st":"36.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"And father Cronion tipped the scales of the fighting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Dionysus fought against mighty Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing thyrsus with spear; and against the javelin-bearing warrior"},{"sp":"N","t":"now, as he hurled, changing his shape turned about"},{"sp":"N","t":"he entered the much-wrought phantoms of every kind of form;","st":"36.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"now he armed himself as a stormy, maddened flame,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting a crooked radiance with dancing smoke."},{"sp":"N","t":"At another time, surging, he flowed as deceitful water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"liquid, shooting a wet shaft; and wearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"an imitation of like nature to a lion's face","st":"36.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted his throat upright aloft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a harsh roaring from a thick-haired throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the thunderous din of his loud-crashing father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bearing the much-wrought form of a shadowy harvest"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was shaped strange in appearance, and like a shoot of the earth,","st":"36.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-perfected, untouchable, he ran up, striking the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a pine, like a plane-tree; and as his head was changed"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made his bastard hair into a tree with mimic leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a vast bush for a belly; and he made his hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"into branches, and turned his tunics to bark,","st":"36.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rooted his feet; and beating with his boughs"},{"sp":"N","t":"he whispered against the face of the fighting king;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and weaving a mimic pattern with spotted limbs"},{"sp":"N","t":"a high-flying leopard ran up with a leap of its soles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mounted the crest of the high-crested elephants,","st":"36.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"stepping lightly; and the elephant, hanging aside, shaking the car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled its god-fighting driver into the plain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking its bright harness and the curved circles of its bridle."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the monstrous champion, though fallen, neglect it, but he fought"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Lyaeus in his new shape and wounded the leopard with his point.","st":"36.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"But the god changed his body again; for high-visible,"},{"sp":"N","t":"warming the air, a wandering torch whirled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting a flaming shaft with the winds; and about the breasts"},{"sp":"N","t":"it ran round the shaggy chest of Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a circle; and receiving the leaps of the high-traveling smoke","st":"36.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Arabian breastplate blackened on its white flanks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the spark; and the half-burnt helmet"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fire-struck bearer was heated with its seething crest. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And out of the grim lion a wandering boar appeared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"widening the great gape of its shaggy-haired throat,","st":"36.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bringing its crest near the belly of Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"it propped itself upright on the throb of a hind foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the middle of his flank with whetted claws."},{"sp":"N","t":"And overbearing Deriades fought against a deaf phantom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried by an empty hope; and always he longed","st":"36.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"to seize the untouchable image with untouching arms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fixed his spear in the brow of the answering lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a threatening word at many-shaped Bacchus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why do you cower, Dionysus? Why guile instead of tumult for you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you change your much-wrought shape, trembling at Deriades?","st":"36.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"The leopard of war-fleeing Dionysus does not rout me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shoot the bear, and I cut the tree with my sword;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall score the flank of your lying lion."},{"sp":"N","t":"But I shall arm the wise unarmored Brahmans against you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for they are naked, and often with god-invoking incantations","st":"36.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"they have drugged and drawn down from heaven"},{"sp":"N","t":"air-roaming Selene, like an unyoked bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often they have halted the journey of unsteady Phaethon"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he rides upon his hurrying chariot.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, gazing at the strange-shaped phantoms of Bacchus;","st":"36.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had an unbelieving mind; and with an uncharmed purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stitching a drugging art against Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hoped to conquer the son of Zeus with a mystic craft."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then, leaping untouchable, he ran up high upon his car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the god, watching the witless god-fighting man,","st":"36.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprouted a vine as the helper of the fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a god-driven shoot of the fair-clustered vine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping little by little toward the silver-wheeled wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped Deriades with a threatening cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fettered, entwined about; and darting the freshly blooming,","st":"36.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"grown-together, wandering cluster upon cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shadowing the face of the maddened king,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it shook, girdling the whole man; and a self-grown"},{"sp":"N","t":"tendril made Deriades drunk with its fragrant fruit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it wove an ironless fetter about his twin feet,","st":"36.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rooted the feet of the yoked elephants &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unbreakable ivy; and not so much does the remora,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entwining the whetted tips of its jaws about a merchant ship"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the sea, hold it fast in the deep with a sharp-toothed bond;"},{"sp":"N","t":"such was the likeness. And in vain the driver, urging on","st":"36.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"his elephants, whirled his heavy-thundering whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring their disobedient backs with sharper goads."},{"sp":"N","t":"And so great an Indian king, whom no measureless spear had killed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a vine tendril conquered as champion; and having"},{"sp":"N","t":"his throat held fast by a shoot of the vine,","st":"36.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades was choked, squeezed by the crooked trail."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring, unshaken, he prayed with a maenad voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a thin ululation from a god-fearing throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shedding suppliant tears with voiceless nods;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stretched out a speechless palm, crying his whole","st":"36.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"labor with witnessing silence; and his tear became his voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus, scattering his many-bound harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew off the fair-clustered fetter of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and driving away the coiling ivy, the crown of vines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosed the bound neck of the entwined elephants.","st":"36.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor, having escaped the woody bond of the long-shooted"},{"sp":"N","t":"cluster and the threatening, self-coiling necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Deriades renounce his accustomed boast of threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he was a god-fighting champion again; and he had a twofold"},{"sp":"N","t":"counsel, either to slay Bacchus or to make him a slave.","st":"36.390","hvShift6":[[0,0],[20,21],[23,23]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And the darkness running round about checked them both from battle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was fighting after the night, and an alternating Dawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed them again as they woke from their slumbering beds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was there an end of battles for hurrying Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but after the circles of so many rolling years","st":"36.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trumpet boomed the rhythm of Enyalius in vain."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already at the much-bending turning-post of battle-rousing years"},{"sp":"N","t":"the late-accomplished Bacchic Enyo raged the more."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the like-minded Dictaean Rhadamanes, being careless","hvShift5":[[0,0],[49,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"for war-mad Dionysus, leave the word that was their care","st":"36.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the forgetful winds; but for Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"they built warlike ships; and about the thickets"},{"sp":"N","t":"they bustled, one here, another there; one turned the bolts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another labored about the middle keel, another, weaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"upright decks about the alternating ribs,","st":"36.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the side of a merchant ship, and joining the long"},{"sp":"N","t":"planks fixed them fast; and in the deepening mast-box"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Arabian carpenter set upright the mast, seen in the middle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"guarded with a spread sail; and upon its top"},{"sp":"N","t":"they turned the curved wooden yard-arm,","st":"36.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"skilled in the fair-handed craft of both Hephaestus and Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"So they, laboring with an inimitable art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"built ships for Bacchus. And in distress at the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus remembered the prophecy of his Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the end of the war would appear when the Bacchoi","st":"36.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"should raise a war at sea against the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Lycus, leading the way through the topmost swell,"},{"sp":"N","t":"serving the unturning nods of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steered his unwetted wayfaring chariot of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the wise Rhadamanes, sea-wandering migrants,","st":"36.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"built ships for sea-traveling Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then, rolling the whirl of fourfold time,"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding the sixth year, crooked Aeon wheeled about. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And scepter-bearing Deriades called the race of the dark-skinned"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indians to assembly; and with hurrying sandal","st":"36.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"a herald of a strange tongue went gathering the people."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at once the ranks of the wide-scattered Indians assembled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated in rows upon successive benches;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the gathered peoples lord Morrheus spoke:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You know, friends, perhaps all of you, what I labored high upon the towers,","st":"36.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"until the Cilician land and the race of Assyrian men"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent a slave neck beneath the yokes of Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you know too all that I accomplished, hurling the spear against Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting against the Satyrs and, with reaping iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the hostile heads of the ox-horned race,","st":"36.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, dragging off the fettered swarm of Bassarids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I gave them to Deriades as a prize of war, beneath whose gore"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-stoned streets of the city were purpled"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were killed; and others, about an aloft dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were squeezed, their necks entwined in a throttling bond;","st":"36.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"and others made trial of a watery destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hidden in the recess of a ground-dug hollow."},{"sp":"N","t":"But again I weave a better counsel for the dwellers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I hear of the Rhadamanes, that with some woodcutting craft"},{"sp":"N","t":"they have built ships for war-fleeing Dionysus;","st":"36.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless I do not tremble at a sea-fighting spear; for in wars"},{"sp":"N","t":"when do women with paltry leaves kill a man"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed with a shield high upon the ships?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Or when will raving mountain-running, high-horned Pan"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut apart the ships of the Indians with whetted claws?","st":"36.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"Silenus cannot, beating the heavy-thundering water"},{"sp":"N","t":"with an unwarlike fennel-stalk, cover a fighting ship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping into a bloody dance with raving sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a death-bearing revel; nor in the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"does he subdue a man pierced with bull's horns,","st":"36.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near as his middle flank is split,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, struck, he shall lie head first by some unburied"},{"sp":"N","t":"fate in the surges; and the Bacchants shall slip"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the murderous depth of the brine, struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"by long spears; and I shall wipe out the ships of Dionysus,","st":"36.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling a twenty-cubit sea-fighting spear through a merchant ship."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, friends, fight with confidence; and let none"},{"sp":"N","t":"of you cower, watching the rank of the Bacchic"},{"sp":"N","t":"ships; for the Indians are accustomed to a sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"tumult, and play the champion better at sea","st":"36.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"than fighting on land. And with unconquered iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not take many Satyrs as plunder, but instead of"},{"sp":"N","t":"two hundred champions I shall drag off by the hair one alone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"woman-mad Dionysus, as the attendant of Deriades.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, shifting-witted Morrheus persuaded","st":"36.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"uncharmed Deriades; and the peoples cried out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"praising his word; and from throats of one tongue"},{"sp":"N","t":"an echo roared like the sound of moving swells."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the king dissolved the assembly. And a herald was sent to Bromius"},{"sp":"N","t":"declaring a sea-battle to obedient Bacchus.","st":"36.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"And both, coming together into one, made a truce"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the checked tumult for three circles of Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until they should bury the ranks of the corpses cut down in battle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was a short-lived peace, the neighbor of Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading out a calm that was in travail with war.","st":"36.480"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXVII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Where the thirty-seventh is, for the sake of victory there are funeral contests for prize-bearing men."},{"sp":"N","t":"So the prudent Indians, caring for friendship,","st":"37.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting the Bacchic Enyo to the winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"buried their dead with tearless eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as men who had fled the earthly bonds of mortal life"},{"sp":"N","t":"while the soul was sent back, whence it came, by a circling cord","st":"37.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"to its ancient starting-post; and the army of Bacchus rested."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus, seeing the friendly calm of the war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"urging on the mules and the men who went with them at dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"commanded them to bring dry mountain-nourished wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might burn with fire the dead body of Opheltes.","st":"37.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the leader of them within the pine-wood was"},{"sp":"N","t":"Faunus, familiar with the accustomed thicket of the wilderness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taught the midday haunts of his mountain mother Circe."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the trees were cut in rows with woodcutting iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"many an elm was cut with long-edged bronze,","st":"37.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"many a high-leaved oak crashed as it was felled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a pine was stretched out, and the fir leaned over"},{"sp":"N","t":"with withered leaves; and as the wide-scattered trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"were cut, little by little the cliffs were laid bare;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Hamadryad Nymph went as a migrant,","st":"37.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and untouchable mingled with an unaccustomed spring-girl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many a mountain-running man went with those who came,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a different path of the mountain; and one might see"},{"sp":"N","t":"a high-visible, jutting, slanting wayfarer coming down"},{"sp":"N","t":"with much-wandering feet; and binding the timbers thickly","st":"37.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a fitted bond of well-plaited cord"},{"sp":"N","t":"they set them upon the backs of the mules; and as the mules"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurried, their mountain-running hooves crashed in rows"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they sped, and the backs of the much-sanded dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"were weighed down with the burden of the plants dragged behind.","st":"37.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Satyrs and Pans bustled, of whom one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with woodcutting palms, lightening the logs from the trees &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"one after another in unwearied arms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clattered upon the cliff with leap-loving feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the woodmen laid them on the ground, where Euius","st":"37.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"marked out a tomb on the floor to make for Opheltes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a great swarm from many cities; and about the corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"each cut a mourning lock with downcast iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and groaning they poured about him one after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shadowing the whole corpse with their alternating hair.","st":"37.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bacchus groaned for the corpse from a griefless face"},{"sp":"N","t":"with tearless eyes, and cutting a single lock"},{"sp":"N","t":"from his unshorn head he laid it as a gift for Opheltes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Idaean servants of mountain-reared Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"made a pyre a hundred feet this way and that;","st":"37.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the middle of the pyre they spread the corpse. And about the dead man"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dictaean Asterius, drawing his hanging sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut the throats of twelve dark Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought and set them crown-wise in a close-set array;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he set there jars of honey and of oil.","st":"37.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"And many cattle and flocks of sheep were slaughtered"},{"sp":"N","t":"before the pyre; and he heaped upon the corpse the bodies"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the slain oxen, circled about, and ranks of freshly cut horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking all the fat from each of them in rows"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spread it about the corpse and wheeled a rich girdle.","st":"37.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"Then there was need of fire; and Faunus of the wilderness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of crag-loving Circe, a citizen of the Tyrrhenian field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a child taught the works of his wilderness mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought fire-bearing stones, the instruments of a mountain craft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the crag, and where the signs of Victory,","st":"37.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thunderbolts falling from the air, had confirmed it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brought the remains of divine fire, so that he might kindle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pyre of the dead man; and with Zeus-struck brimstone"},{"sp":"N","t":"he anointed the hollows of both fire-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"stones, smearing them; and scraping a thin chip","st":"37.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"from an Erythraean cluster he mixed it with the twin stones;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rubbing this way and that, and striking female against male,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew out a hidden, self-delivered stony fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set it beneath the pyre, where the wild wood was."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the kindled fire run round the pyre of the dead,","st":"37.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the god, stretching his eye toward Phaethon opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, called the eastern wind Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to bring a counter-blowing breeze to help the pyre."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at the call of Bromius the neighboring Dawnstar heard"},{"sp":"N","t":"his supplication, and sent his brother to Lyaeus","st":"37.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"to kindle the flame-shaped torch with thicker panting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the rosy chamber of his mother Dawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind fanned up the flaming pyre"},{"sp":"N","t":"all night long, darting the wind-nourished, leaping fire;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the raving breezes hurled a radiance into the air,","st":"37.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighbors of Helios. And with grieving Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dictaean Asterius, bearing kindred blood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a Cnossian two-handled cup of sweet, fragrant"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine, made the poured dust of the floor drunk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"honoring the wind-roaming soul of the son of Arestor.","st":"37.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the dawn-light, reddening, the forerunner"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the dewy chariot of Dawn, sparkled, scoring the night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then all rose up, and with alternating cups"},{"sp":"N","t":"they quenched their comrade's pyre with the moisture of Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the hot wind withdrew on dappled wings","st":"37.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the light-bearing house of Helios. And Asterius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathering the bones covered in a double layer of fat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid the remains of the dead man in a golden bowl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the running Corybants, since they had obtained the midday haunts of Ida,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the true blood of Crete, when the foundations had been dug deep,","st":"37.95","hvShift5":[[49,5]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,3]]},{"sp":"N","t":"buried the dead man, a dweller of one fatherland,","hvShift5":[[2,4],[13,5],[31,6],[38,25],[40,28],[41,35],[45,37],[49,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and rounded a tomb through a ground-dug hollow;","hvShift5":[[0,58]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[25,0],[62,3]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and they poured a last foreign dust over Opheltes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raised the grave with loftier building,"},{"sp":"N","t":"inscribing such a word upon the newly grieved tomb:","st":"37.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Here lies the short-lived corpse of the son of Arestor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Opheltes of Cnossus, the Indian-slayer, fellow-contender of Bromius.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god, bringing funeral gifts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept the people there, and held a broad contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having marked out the goal of a horse-driven course; and on the ground","st":"37.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"was a stone equal in measure to a fathom in broad measure,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the pattern of a half-cut circle, the image of the moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"well-polished on answering flanks, such as an old carpenter"},{"sp":"N","t":"turns, weaving with labor-working palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing to fashion an inspired image; and then, lifting it","st":"37.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his palms, a monstrous Cyclops set it in the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a counterpart of the stony turning-post, fixing in the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"another stone opposite, yoked, equal to that one."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the prizes were varied: cauldron, tripod, shields, horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"silver, Indian metals, cattle, Pactolian silt.","st":"37.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god set prizes of victory for the horsemen;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the first he set a bow and an Amazonian quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a half-finished shield and a war-loving woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom once, traveling on foot beneath the brows of Thermodon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he took alive as she bathed, and brought to the city of the Indians;","st":"37.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the second he set a mare running with the north wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yellow in nature, her neck shadowed with long manes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still carrying a half-finished foal, by whose burden still"},{"sp":"N","t":"her belly swelled with the horse-weight of her offspring;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for the third a breastplate, and for the fourth he set a shield;","st":"37.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one the noblest-working Lemnian anvil had fashioned,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorning it with golden cunning work, and in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the other a wheeling boss was embroidered with silver adornment;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the fifth, two talents, a prize of the Pactolian bank."},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing upright he spoke, urging on the drivers:","st":"37.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"O friends, whom Ares taught the city-sacking Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to whom the dark-haired god granted the course of horsemanship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not urge on men untaught of labors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but men accustomed to mighty toils; for our"},{"sp":"N","t":"warriors are practiced in every kind of valor;","st":"37.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"for if a Lydian man has got his race from Tmolus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shall accomplish deeds worthy of the horsemanship of Pelops;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if a citizen of fair-charioted Elis and of Oenomaus"},{"sp":"N","t":"holds the Pisan plain, the midwife of horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he knows the wild-olive-bearing branch of the Olympian olive;","st":"37.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"but this is not the course of Oenomaus, nor do the drivers"},{"sp":"N","t":"here have the goad of ill-guesting weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but this is a course of valor, free of the foam-born;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if one holds the plain of Aonia or carries the blood of Phocis,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[2,13]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,12],[8,33],[9,49],[12,51],[19,55]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he knows the Pythian contest honored for Apollo;","st":"37.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he tends the wise soil of olive-tressed Marathon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he knows the pitcher pregnant with rich dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he was a citizen of the Achaean land of fair childbirth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has learned Pellene, where men contend"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a chilling contest for a cloak-loving victory,","st":"37.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding their unwarmed limbs tight in a wintry tunic;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he grew up a dweller of sea-girdled Corinth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he knows the Isthmian contest of our Palaemon.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the leaders ran up hastening,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling their cars in turn; and first of all Erechtheus,","st":"37.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing swift-footed Xanthus, bound him beneath the yoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the male, and clamped the female Podarce beside him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Boreas sowed upon a fair-winged bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging a storm-swift Sithonian Harpy into wedlock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and them, when he snatched Oreithyia the Attic bride,","st":"37.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wind as son-in-law gave to Erechtheus as bride-gift of his love."},{"sp":"N","t":"Second, Actaeon brandished an Ismenian whip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and third was Scelmis, swift-horsed, the offspring"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Earth-shaker who rules the waters, who often scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"the water, steering his father's Poseidonian chariot of the sea.","st":"37.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"Fourth leapt up Faunus, who came into the midst of the contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"alone having a pattern equal to the mother of his begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing an imitation of Helios with four-yoked horses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fifth Achates mounted a Sicilian car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the Pisan sting of the olive-tressed river,","st":"37.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsated of horsemanship, since he dwelt in the plain of the bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Alpheius unhappy in love, who comes to Arethusa"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing unwetted garland-bearing water as bride-gift of his love."},{"sp":"N","t":"And taking bold Actaeon apart from the throng"},{"sp":"N","t":"his father gave dear counsels to his hastening son:","st":"37.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Child of Aristaeus your father of surpassing mind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know that you bear strength enough, that you carry"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flower of youth grown together and mixed with manhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing your father's Phoebean blood, and our"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arcadian horses are better as they dart into the course;","st":"37.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"but all this is in vain, and neither strength nor the running of horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"knows how to conquer so much as the wits of the driver;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you lack cunning alone; for horsemanship"},{"sp":"N","t":"needs a prudent, knowing driver."},{"sp":"N","t":"But listen to your father, and the horse-cunning of the art,","st":"37.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the many turns I have learned in time, I shall teach you too."},{"sp":"N","t":"Hasten, child, to honor your father by your valor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the course of horsemanship holds as much glory as Enyo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten to honor me in the stadium too, after the wars;"},{"sp":"N","t":"having conquered Ares, enter another victory,","st":"37.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that after 'spearman' I may call you 'prize-bearer' as well."},{"sp":"N","t":"O child, do deeds worthy of your kinsman Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"worthy of Phoebus and of skilled-handed Cyrene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and surpass the labors of your father Aristaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and display your horsemanship, bringing a cunning victory,","st":"37.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"your crafty counsel; since in the midst of the contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, untaught man, dragging his chariot along uncontrolled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wanders this way and that, and the unsteady course of his opposing"},{"sp":"N","t":"horses is not forced by the whip, and does not obey the bridle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the driver, turned about, is dragged outside the turning-post,","st":"37.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"wherever his disobedient, snatching horses carry him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but whoever is a much-devising driver, practiced"},{"sp":"N","t":"in cunning guile, even with lesser horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steers straight, watching the driver who runs ahead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"always keeping his course close about the turning-post, and bends","st":"37.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"his car, riding round the goal and never scraping the goal."},{"sp":"N","t":"Watch for me, and clamping with the steering bridle"},{"sp":"N","t":"slant your whole left-hand horse close to the turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weighing down your car aslant, leaning on its side,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near but untouching, by some necessary measure","st":"37.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"steering your course, on guard, until the hub"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your wheeling car appears as though touching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the very tip of the goal with its neighboring wheel-shaped circle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but beware the stone, lest, striking the turning-post with your axle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you destroy in one both your car and your horses.","st":"37.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"And shepherding your car this way and that along the course"},{"sp":"N","t":"be wholly like a helmsman; and both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"urging with the goad, pouring out a horse-striking threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drive your right-hand horse, drawing it into a swifter course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the loosened circles of an unsqueezed bridle;","st":"37.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"be wholly like a helmsman, shepherding your car"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a straight-running course, since by a cunning counsel"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mind of the driver is the rudder of the chariot.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he withdrew, turned back again, having taught his son"},{"sp":"N","t":"the varied cunning of the art of accustomed horsemanship.","st":"37.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"And within the accustomed helmet one after another"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched a blind hand with face averted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wishing to have a shifting lot, as a man"},{"sp":"N","t":"shakes his far-throwing fingers over a changeful die."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the drivers drew lots in turn; and horse-mad","st":"37.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"Faunus, the blood of the Phaethontian race of song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was first by the lot, and second was Achates,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after him the brother of Damnameneus, and after him"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon drew; and last, though best, stood for the course"},{"sp":"N","t":"horse-striking Erechtheus, having got the last lot.","st":"37.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the drivers lifted their ox-hide whips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing in rows upon their several cars."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Aeacus was the true watcher, so that, perceiving"},{"sp":"N","t":"the garland-loving drivers bending about the goal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a witness of truth he might loose their discordant quarrels,","st":"37.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"judging the course of the horses with unwandering eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"For them there was a course from the starting-line; and as they hurried"},{"sp":"N","t":"one was ahead, and another longed to overtake"},{"sp":"N","t":"the man ahead, and another pursued the middle, and another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, longed to score the driver behind.","st":"37.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one driver in the stadium, overtaking a driver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixed car with car, and shaking the reins in his hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified the horses with the crook-toothed bridle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, traveling with the driver who rushed at him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held an evenly balanced course in a doubtful strife,","st":"37.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting, crouching, stretched out, upright by necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bending loins, and driving a willing horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing it a little with a sparing, cunning palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slanted the circle of his gaze as he turned back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on guard against the car of the driver coming behind;","st":"37.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now the hoof of the horses would have slipped, as they darted on"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a mounting throb of feet, into the self-rolling wheel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not the driver reined back his own rush"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it still hastened, checking the car that came up behind."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, running ahead with a driver behind him,","st":"37.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"held an answering course upon the emulous cars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady, shutting in the driver who appeared near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this way and that. And Scelmis, the offspring of the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the sea-whip of Poseidon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove his father's race of sea-ranging horses;","st":"37.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did long-winged Pegasus, soaring aloft, cutting the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fly so much as the feet of the deep-sea horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"made an untouchable journey over the land."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the peoples, coming together into one, seated in rows"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon a high-crested place as watchers of the contest,","st":"37.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed from afar at the course of the hurrying horses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one stood in fear, and another, shaking"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tip of his finger, waved it, urging on a driver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, shaken by an emulous longing for the horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had a horse-mad mind running with the driver;","st":"37.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, seeing the course of his own driver in the lead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clapped with his hands and cried with a mourning voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heartening, laughing, trembling, calling to the driver."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the well-made cars, swifter than a raving bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at one time flew aloft, and now upon the ground","st":"37.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"were carried at their very tips, scarcely touching the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wandering trail of the straight-running car"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the sandy seat with a swift wheel-shaped circle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the strife was close; and the dust, rising up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was itself suspended upon the horses' breasts,","st":"37.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their manes rushed with the airy storms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the busy drivers shrieked out from throats of one tongue"},{"sp":"N","t":"a cry sharper than the whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when they were completing the last course, darting sharply,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Scelmis was first of all, stretching out his sea-running car;","st":"37.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Erechtheus, following him, lashed his horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, and you might quickly have said that the car behind"},{"sp":"N","t":"was seen mounting the chariot of the sea-Telchine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the proud, soaring horse of Erechtheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching its back-blown panting from a twin nostril,","st":"37.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"warmed the back of the alien driver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he would have gripped the mane of its neck with his hands,","hvShift5":[[0,0],[65,61],[66,66]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[39,38],[44,42],[45,44],[57,57],[59,58]]},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the driver with eyes that turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now, with a running whirl of its shaken jaws —","hvShift6":[[0,0],[32,8]]},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Erechtheus, turning aside, reined back his car,","st":"37.300","hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,6]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and drawn down the well-made reins with a snatching throb,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[8,9]]},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping little by little the jaw of the horses that appeared near —","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the horse would have halted, foaming, and spat out the tip of the bit;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[4,0],[14,9],[16,24],[19,26],[23,27],[33,41],[51,58],[61,67]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and again he drove near, having escaped the unbridled necessity."},{"sp":"N","t":"And watching him rushing on with his car,","st":"37.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"Scelmis shrieked out a threatening cry:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cease striving in vain against horses of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"once Pelops, driving another chariot of my begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquered the course of the unconquered horses of Oenomaus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall call as the steersman of horsemanship","st":"37.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horse-god who rules the waters; but you, horse-striker, stretch"},{"sp":"N","t":"all your hope of victory toward loom-perfecting Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of your little olive-shoot, but I bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"another vine garland and no paltry olive.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, swift-counseling Erechtheus was angered the more,","st":"37.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and weaving a beguiling guile and a prudent counsel"},{"sp":"N","t":"he steered his own course with his hands, and in his heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"calling upon his helper, his city-holding Athena"},{"sp":"N","t":"of horsemanship, he shrieked out a swift-worded Attic cry:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Lady of Cecropia, horse-rousing motherless Pallas,","st":"37.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"as you conquered Poseidon in your own contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so let your dweller Erechtheus, driving a Marathonian horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquer the son of Poseidon.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Crying such a word he lashed the flanks of his colts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and brought car level with car; and with his left hand","st":"37.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the jaw of his adversary's horses in a heavy bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing back the car that ran beside him, forced by the bridle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his right he lashed his own high-necked horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they hurried forward; and, removing him from the road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set the driver behind him, turned back again.","st":"37.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"And pouring a mockery-loving echo from running lips"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reproached the son of Poseidon with an answering voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a laughing look as he turned back:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Scelmis, you are conquered; Erechtheus is better than you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because my aged mare Podarce has conquered your Balius,","st":"37.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of the race of Zephyrus, a male and young horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wayfarer unwetted by the brine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you boast for the sake of the art of Pelops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"honoring the sea-running car of your begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shifting-witted Myrtilus accomplished a thievish victory,","st":"37.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"having made a deceiving axle with mimic wax;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you think greatly of yourself for the race of the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom you call the horse-god, the mounter of deep-sea cars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lord of the sea himself, the steersman of the trident —"},{"sp":"N","t":"female Athena conquered your male helper.\"","st":"37.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking the citizen of Athens ran past the Telchine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after him Faunus drove, lashing his four-horse car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fourth, crafty Actaeon followed Faunus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still remembering the cunning words of his father"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aristaeus; and last was Tyrrhenian Achates.","st":"37.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Actaeon devised a guileful counsel:"},{"sp":"N","t":"overtaking Faunus as he still ran ahead with his car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning the course of his horses with a sharper whip"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drove beside him, stealing past the driver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"getting a little ahead; and fixing his knees against the rail","st":"37.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"he scored the emulous car with his slanting chariot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scraping the horses' feet with his wheeling trail."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the car fell to the ground; and as the chariot was shaken"},{"sp":"N","t":"three horses lay fallen upon the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one upon its flanks, another its belly, another on its neck,","st":"37.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one stayed upright, leaning aside, and rooted"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tips of its feet in the ground, and shaking its restless neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"the yoke-mate propped the whole foot of the horse beside it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightening the yoke-straps and dragging the car upward."},{"sp":"N","t":"They were poured out upon the ground; and squalid","st":"37.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"the driver rolled beside the wheel, a neighbor to his car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the top of his brow was crushed, and as his chin was defiled"},{"sp":"N","t":"his sharp-stretched elbow was scored, covered with dust from the floor."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the driver leapt up more swiftly; and hastening"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stood beside his neighboring car fallen to the ground,","st":"37.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing up the stretched-out horse with a shamefaced palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lashing the downcast colt with a dappled whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Actaeon, watching Faunus laboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside his car, broke into a play-loving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cease urging on your unwilling horses in vain,","st":"37.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"cease in vain; for I shall get there first and announce to Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Faunus, leaving all the drivers ahead of him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"will come home late on the road, dragging his car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"spare your whip, since with its flesh-cutting goad"},{"sp":"N","t":"I pitied the body of your horses, seeing it scored.\"","st":"37.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, driving his unsteady car ahead"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a swifter whip; and Faunus grieved as he heard."},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely, gripping a shaggy tail on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set upright the dusty body of his fallen horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, bringing back a colt that had darted aside","st":"37.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"as its harness was loosed, he clamped it with the bridle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and setting the feet of his horses this way and that as they hurried past"},{"sp":"N","t":"he mounted high upon his car, and fixing his foot in the chariot"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lashed his horse a second time with a dreadful whip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Faunus drove on farther, urging the course of his horses,","st":"37.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursued the driver ahead of him more swiftly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he overtook those who had got ahead, since the horse-god"},{"sp":"N","t":"Earth-shaker cast strength into his horses, honoring his bold son;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing a narrow road beside a hollow rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"he wove a prudent, guile-weaving counsel, so that, overtaking him,","st":"37.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"he might dart past Achates with a cunning car."},{"sp":"N","t":"There was a deep-bosomed cleft, which the migrant water of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring from the air with a wintry lash, had broken"},{"sp":"N","t":"out of the road; and as the stream of the earth-cutting rain"},{"sp":"N","t":"was checked, the ridge of the ground was hollowed out,","st":"37.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"where Achates, coming unwilling, reined back his car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the near approach of the driver on the road;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to him as he rushed on he raised a trembling voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Still, childish Faunus, your tunics are filthy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still the rims of your car are sandy,","st":"37.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"not yet have you shaken the dust from your unadorned horses;"},{"sp":"N","t":"cleanse your defilements; why should you drive a horse so hard?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me not see you falling and gasping again."},{"sp":"N","t":"Beware of that bold Actaeon, lest, overtaking you,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,42],[3,45]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he mark your back beneath a bull's-hide whip,","st":"37.410","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,1],[7,2]]},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he hurl you head first into the dust again."},{"sp":"N","t":"Still you tend the scored circles of your cheek;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Faunus, why do you rage, fastening a common reproach"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon your father Poseidon and upon Helios your grandfather?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Respect for me the mockery-loving throat of the Satyrs.","st":"37.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"Beware the Sileni and the attendants of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest they laugh at you and at your squalid car."},{"sp":"N","t":"Where are your charms? Where your herbs? Where the varied drugs of Circe?"},{"sp":"N","t":"All have left you, all, when you came into the course of the contest.","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Who would announce to your proud mother","st":"37.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"both your overturned car and your squalid whip?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a proud word Achates shrieked out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mocking him; and Nemesis recorded so great a speech."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Faunus came near, driving his car beside him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he brought car close to car, and striking the bolt","st":"37.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the axle he shattered it, driven middle-deep, with his wheeling circle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wheel, self-rolling, coiling, leaned upon the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like the car of Oenomaus, when the deceiving"},{"sp":"N","t":"axle, loosed as the wax was warmed by Phaethon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"checked the horsemanship of the maddened driver.","st":"37.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Achates, holding the narrow road, waited,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until Faunus, seated upon the rail of his four-horse team,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passed Achates with a swifter whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though he had not heard; and he lifted his whip the more,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing untouchable the crest of his hurrying horses;","st":"37.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was behind Actaeon by as much as the long-shadowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"rush of a leaping discus when it is sent,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which a young man hurls, whirling it with a mighty palm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And madness fell upon the peoples; and one strove with another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making wagers about the untraceable victory","st":"37.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"to come; and the gifts, for the sake of the storm-footed horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were either a tripod or a cauldron or a sword or an ox-hide shield;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dweller strove with dweller, friend with comrade,"},{"sp":"N","t":"old man with old man, young with young, man with man."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the strife of both was discordant: one glorifying","st":"37.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"Achates, another convicting Faunus as the worse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fallen upon the ground from his rolling car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another contending that Erechtheus was second,"},{"sp":"N","t":"behind the sea-Telchine driver;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one strove with another, that the much-turning citizen of Athens,","st":"37.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, had conquered the course of the horses ahead of him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having darted past Scelmis the driver while he still ran ahead."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had the quarrel ceased, and Erechtheus came up near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing his horses this way and that with downswung arm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and much sweat ran down the horse's neck","st":"37.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaggy breast, and thick, squalid drops"},{"sp":"N","t":"of dust rushed down over the driver;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the car ran upon the near-passing tracks of the horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a leaping whirl; and by the wheeling iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unshaken backs of the fine dust were not stirred.","st":"37.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he, after his flying course, high upon his car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came into the middle of the contest; and with his tunic he wiped"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweat dripping from his drenched brow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly he came down from his car; and he leaned"},{"sp":"N","t":"his long whip against the well-made yoke;","st":"37.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his servant Amphidamas loosed the horses; and more swiftly"},{"sp":"N","t":"with delighted palm he lifted the first prizes of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the quiver and the bow and the well-helmeted woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing the mid-bossed back of the half-finished ox-hide shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after him came second upon his sea-borne car","st":"37.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"Scelmis, urging on his Poseidonian chariot of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as far behind as a wheeling circle is from a swift horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, as it darts on, the tips of the hairs of its outstretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling tail scarcely touch the wheel-rims;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he took the second prizes, and held out to Damnameneus","st":"37.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pregnant mare to keep, stretching it with a jealous hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And third Actaeon lifted the tokens of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gold-shining breastplate, an all-varied work of Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after him Faunus came; and drawing up his car there"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raised aloft the silver-circled boss of the ox-hide shield,","st":"37.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"still wearing those remains of squalid dust."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Sicilian servant beside the slow-wheeling car"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed the two talents of gold to downcast Achates,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiable to tender-loving Dionysus as he boasted."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then he set up a contest of harsh boxing;","st":"37.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the first he set a bull from an Indian ox-stall"},{"sp":"N","t":"to be led as a gift, and for the other, taking a treasure"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the dark-skinned Indians, he laid down a barbarian, dappled-backed shield."},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing upright he spoke, urging on the athletes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that two skilled-handed men should strive for victory:","st":"37.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This is the contest of unwearied boxing; and to the prize-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"man who conquers I shall grant a shaggy-haired bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the man defeated I shall give a many-folded shield.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius so spoke, shield-brandishing Melisseus rose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"practiced in accustomed boxing; and laying hold","st":"37.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the well-horned bull he uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Let him come who longs for the dappled shield; for I shall not leave"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fat bull to another, while I still lift my hands.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke silence sealed them all;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Eurymedon alone rose against him, to whom Hermes gave","st":"37.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"the instruments of limb-strong boxing, who before always"},{"sp":"N","t":"had been busy sitting beside his father's forge,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Hephaestus, striking the hammer-wrought anvil."},{"sp":"N","t":"Him his brother Alcon attended, much excited,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[3,30],[12,37],[15,42]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and set a belt beside him, and fitted a girdle to his loins,","st":"37.505","hvShift6":[[0,0],[11,3]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and, joining them with his long palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped the entwined trail of the dry thongs."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the champion came into the middle, holding his left hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"before his face, an inborn shield; and instead of a lance"},{"sp":"N","t":"the flesh-cutting thongs were his manufactured palm.","st":"37.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"And always he guarded against the hard-fighting rush of his adversary,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he strike him upon the brow or the forehead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or bloody him, tearing the stricken joint,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or cut him apart, hitting him at the temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"battering the edge of his intelligent brain within,","st":"37.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"or, stretching a rough palm against his temples,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay bare the eyes of a face robbed of its eyeballs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or, as his blood-red chin was battered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drive out the many-rowed swath of his sharper teeth."},{"sp":"N","t":"There, as Eurymedon rushed on, Melisseus","st":"37.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the top of his breast; and he, close before his face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched out his hand in vain, and missed, striking the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and always, trembling, he ran round him, shifting his stance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching his right hand beneath his bare breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"And both came together, advancing one against the other,","st":"37.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"shifting foot a little past foot with sparing steps;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they mixed hands with hands; and with rushes one upon another"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dread thud of the plaited thongs boomed"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the very tip of the hand; and as a cheek was scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thongs were reddened with bloody drops;","st":"37.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was a thud of jaws; and on the swelling of the face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grown broader, the cheeks surged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the eyes were hollowed on either side of the face."},{"sp":"N","t":"Eurymedon labored beneath the knowing art of Melisseus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"awaiting the unbearable radiance of the sun opposite him,","st":"37.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"which dazzled his eye; and Melisseus, darting on"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a sharper whirl, lifting his step aloft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"suddenly struck his jaw beneath the ear; and he, laboring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-rolling, propped his back upon the dust on his back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"soul-failing, wholly like a drunken man; and he held his head","st":"37.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning aside, and spat out the foam of blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"thickening thinly; and taking him out of the contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"his grim brother Alcon led him away upon his back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weighed down with a wit-robbing blow. And hastening"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted up the vast Indian ox-hide shield.","st":"37.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus, urging on two athletes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaimed a contest of wrestling for prize-bearing men;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a tripod of twenty measures stood as the prize of victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the first athlete; and he set in the middle, lifting it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a flowery cauldron, keeping it for the worse man.","st":"37.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing upright he cried again with a commanding voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Come, friends, and rouse this fair contest too.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and at the call of garland-loving Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aristaeus stood first, and after him second stood"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus, taught the works of skilled-handed wrestling.","st":"37.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"And covering with a belt the nature of an unseen shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"they stood naked as they contended; and both"},{"sp":"N","t":"first entwined both palms about each other's wrists,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this way and that, drawing one another in turn"},{"sp":"N","t":"over the backs of the poured dust, clamping","st":"37.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the very tips of a knot of hands; and man ran round"},{"sp":"N","t":"man, bringing him back again with an unequal throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing and drawn; for both held each other"},{"sp":"N","t":"with alternating hands, and arched their necks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and propped head against head with the middle of the brow,","st":"37.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbending, nodding toward the ground; and from their squeezed"},{"sp":"N","t":"brows the sweat, forerunner of toil, ran down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he sprinkled the poured dust with drenching sweat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cleansing the liquid drop with squalid sand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he slip as he was entwined in the knot of hands,","st":"37.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending hot moisture down a rubbed neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bowed backs of both, by the pull of the forearm,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,3]]},{"sp":"N","t":"were rubbed by the twin bond of an entwined palm;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[3,4]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and a self-perfected weal ran up with hot blood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpling in varied colors; and the bodies of the men were spotted.","st":"37.575"},{"sp":"N","t":"And they displayed to one another in turn the varied"},{"sp":"N","t":"tricks of the wrestler's art; and first Aristaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraced his adversary by the wrist of his palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heaving him up from the ground; but shifting-witted Aeacus"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not forget his guileful art, and with a stealing sole,","st":"37.580"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the hollow of the left knee of Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he threw his whole self-rolling body flat upon the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like a sky-high headland; and around, the peoples"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed with astonished eyes upon so great a boastful"},{"sp":"N","t":"son of Phoebus, of whom all speak, as he fell.","st":"37.585"},{"sp":"N","t":"Second, Aeacus lifted the monstrous son of Cyrene"},{"sp":"N","t":"aloft above the ground, raising him without toil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping his valor to come for his children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unwearied Peleus and broad-strong Telamon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding him in his arms, not bending his back or his upright neck,","st":"37.590"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing the man in the middle with both forearms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a pattern like those weathervanes which a carpenter makes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothing the stormy necessity of the wind."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bringing the whole man near, spread upon the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus mounted the middle of his adversary's back,","st":"37.595"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sending his foot stretched across the spread belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"joining a crooked bond about the very tip of his knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he propped sole against sole, winding it past the top of the ankle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly, stretched high above his adversary's back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding his own hands crown-wise upon one another,","st":"37.600"},{"sp":"N","t":"he cast a bond upon his neck with his forearm, bending his fingers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he was squeezed the chosen heralds, the watchers"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the contest, ran together with a sharp throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he kill him with the yoked trail of his forearm."},{"sp":"N","t":"For there was not then such a rule as men of later birth","st":"37.605"},{"sp":"N","t":"themselves devised, when, as the bonds of the neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"are stretched tight, a man weighed down with a throttling labor"},{"sp":"N","t":"woos the victory of his adversary by an intelligent silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the victorious man with a downcast hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Myrmidons, the servants of their prize-bearing king,","st":"37.610"},{"sp":"N","t":"took and embraced the tripod of twenty measures;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Actaeon lifted the cauldron with a swifter rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing his father's second prizes with a downcast hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then Bacchus set up a contest of swiftness of feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting as treasures of victory for the first athlete","st":"37.615"},{"sp":"N","t":"a silver mixing-bowl and a spear-won woman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for the second he set a dappled-necked Thessalian horse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for the last a sharp sword with a well-cut baldric."},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing upright he spoke, urging on the swift-footed men:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Let these be the prizes for swift-traveling men.\"","st":"37.620"},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the Dictaean, shaking his accustomed knees, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after him swift, shifting-witted Erechtheus ran up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the care of Pallas of Nicaea, and after him"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-footed Priasus, a citizen of the Cybelian field."},{"sp":"N","t":"For them there was a course from the starting-line; and Ocythous","st":"37.625"},{"sp":"N","t":"was first, lightened with a stormy throb of feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a straight-stretched course in the lead; and hastening"},{"sp":"N","t":"second, close on the road, Erechtheus was behind him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the back of neighboring Ocythous with his panting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and warming his head; and as far as the rod of a distaff-loving","st":"37.630"},{"sp":"N","t":"girl is from the middle of her breast, by whatever measure"},{"sp":"N","t":"a loom-toiling maiden stretches it with cunning hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so far was he behind Ocythous; and about the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck his tracks with his feet before the dust settled."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now the course would have been in doubt; but, seeing","st":"37.635"},{"sp":"N","t":"a mimic journey of equal measure, he stretched out with a lighter"},{"sp":"N","t":"sole, and ran past the man by a greater measure,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as far as a man's footstep; whence, trembling for the victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Erechtheus entreated Boreas, crying such a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Son-in-law, help your Erechtheus and your bride,","st":"37.640"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you still tend the sweet sting of the Loves for my child;"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me the dappled running of your wings for a single hour,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may pass swift-kneed Ocythous as he runs ahead.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke Boreas heard his suppliant voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made him swifter than a well-wheeling tempest.","st":"37.645"},{"sp":"N","t":"Three rushed on with the windy throb of their feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but not with equal weight; and by as much as Erechtheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was behind Ocythous who ran ahead with swift sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by so much was boastful Priasus, of Phrygian race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighbor of stormy Erechtheus. And as they hurried,","st":"37.650"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the course was still at its last with the leap of their soles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-kneed Ocythous slipped in the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where there was unspeakable dung of the oxen which beside the tomb"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus had crushed with a Mygdonian knife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but with the swift-whirling throb of a returning foot","st":"37.655"},{"sp":"N","t":"— had the course then been a little longer still, going so he would perhaps","hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,5],[18,17],[33,26],[49,48],[58,56],[59,58],[64,72],[65,74]]},{"sp":"N","t":"either have made it a tie or have passed the citizen of Athens —","hvShift6":[[0,3],[11,5],[16,6],[23,15],[26,16],[34,22],[36,25],[49,35],[51,38],[53,39],[57,40]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Ocythous was carried on, leaping after him, and hastily","hvShift6":[[0,0],[54,52]]},{"sp":"N","t":"plying an oncoming sole behind the adversary who ran ahead;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,3],[10,15],[12,18],[31,23],[53,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and swift Erechtheus lifted the dappled-backed treasure,","st":"37.660"},{"sp":"N","t":"the well-made Sidonian mixing-bowl; and Ocythous"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew off the Thessalian horse; and third, coming quietly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Priasus received the sword with its silver baldric."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the chorus of Satyrs laughed with a play-loving heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the Corybant filthy with poured dust,","st":"37.665"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting out dung that ran down from his throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus, bringing a mass of rough-cast metal, set it in the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"urging on the discus-hurling casters;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the first he brought and set two spears with a horse-plumed helmet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for the second a bright circling girdle,","st":"37.670"},{"sp":"N","t":"and for the third a bowl, and for the fourth he set a fawnskin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which the smith of Zeus had pinned with a golden clasp."},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing upright in the middle he spoke with a mind-rousing voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This contest urges on athletes to the discus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius so spoke, shield-brandishing Melisseus rose,","st":"37.675"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after him came second high-footed Halimedes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and third Eurymedon and fourth came Acmon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and four stood in rows, one after another."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Melisseus, taking the well-whirled mass, hurled it;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Sileni laughed at the man's lesser rush.","st":"37.680"},{"sp":"N","t":"Second, Eurymedon propped his palm upon the discus; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fair-crested Acmon, taking the well-whirled mass"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a steering wrist, sent forth the heavy, wheeling missile;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the air-roaming shaft ran, running with the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and passed the mark of Eurymedon by a greater measure","st":"37.685"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a sharp whirl; and high-footed Halimedes"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled the wandering discus in the air toward the mark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the mass whistled in the airy tempests,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carried from a mighty palm, as from a bow"},{"sp":"N","t":"a shaft flies upright, shaken by the unsteady breezes;","st":"37.690"},{"sp":"N","t":"and falling from the air it rolled upon the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a far-traveling leap, still carried by the throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the well-turned hand, holding a self-moving rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until it had run past all the marks; and gathered together"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the watchers of the contest crashed out,","st":"37.695"},{"sp":"N","t":"astonished at the restless rush of the leaping discus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And shaking the two spears with the high-crested helmet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"boastful Halimedes carried off the double gifts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shambling Acmon lifted the gold-gleaming girdle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and third Eurymedon, raising the unfired bowl,","st":"37.700"},{"sp":"N","t":"took the two-handled treasure; and downcast in face"},{"sp":"N","t":"Melisseus lifted up the dappled-backed fawnskin."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus set prizes of the bow for the champions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an offering of good aim; and dragging out a toil-enduring"},{"sp":"N","t":"seven-year-old mule he fixed it in the contest,","st":"37.705"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a well-made cup stood as the prize of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept for the worse man. And Euryalus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting a long ship's mast upright in the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood it above the sandy floor, and high-visible"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hung a dove bound, entwined with the mast,","st":"37.710"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding a thin thread about its two feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god cried a contest-voice to the gathered men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"urging the archers toward the air-roaming mark:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Whoever shall shoot and pierce the top of the dove,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him lead off the much-yielding mule, the witness of victory;","st":"37.715"},{"sp":"N","t":"but whoever shall stray as he draws toward the mark of the dove,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the bird unscored by his sharp-pointed arrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shall strike the top of the cord with his winged shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him shoot worse and receive the worse gifts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for instead of the mule he shall carry off a cup, so that he may pour","st":"37.720"},{"sp":"N","t":"libation to archer Phoebus and to wine-pouring Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As wealth-holding Lyaeus cried such a word,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair-haired far-shooting Hymenaeus stood in the middle, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing toward the straight-running mark facing the mast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing a Cnossian bow stretched with a circling string,","st":"37.725"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Asterius, having got the lot, sent his shaft first,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and struck the cord; and as it was cut by the missile"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering bird was carried aloft into the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the thread fell to the ground. And carrying his eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"in spirals along the high-traveling road, watching above the clouds,","st":"37.730"},{"sp":"N","t":"the archer Hymenaeus, drawing a wind-borne shaft"},{"sp":"N","t":"from a most ready string toward the air-roaming mark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent it more sharply, aiming straight at the dove;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the winged wandering shaft flew through the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"at its very tip, scraping the middle backs of the clouds,","st":"37.735"},{"sp":"N","t":"hissing with the winds; and Apollo steered the shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping faith with his brother Dionysus unhappy in love;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it struck the flying dove, and as it sped"},{"sp":"N","t":"it smote the top of its breast; and as its head grew heavy"},{"sp":"N","t":"the storm-swift bird fell through the air to the ground;","st":"37.740"},{"sp":"N","t":"and half-dead the dove beat its wings about in the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping with its feet about dance-weaving Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, leaping up at the victory of the youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clapped his hands, crying aloud for Hymenaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and all together in one, as many as stayed at the contest,","st":"37.745"},{"sp":"N","t":"marveled at the cloud-neighboring archery of Hymenaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laughing Dionysus, drawing it with his own palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave the mule as the gift owed to Hymenaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his comrades lifted the cup, the prize of Asterius."},{"sp":"N","t":"And urging the spearmen to a friendly fight,","st":"37.750"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus brought Indian prizes and set them beside the contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a pair of greaves and a stone of the Indian brine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And standing upright he spoke, and bade two warriors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a fighting that played and with iron that did not kill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplish a mimic image of a bloodless battle:","st":"37.755"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This contest, rousing two javelin-hurling men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knows a gentle Ares and a fair-weather Enyo.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius so spoke, Asterius armed himself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing iron weapons, and Aeacus stood in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a bronze spear, shaking a much-wrought shield,","st":"37.760"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a wild lion rushing at some bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"or a shaggy boar; and both, covering their bodies"},{"sp":"N","t":"in an iron tunic, rushed into the middle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spearmen of Ares; and hurling his furious spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterius, having the ancestral valor of Minos,","st":"37.765"},{"sp":"N","t":"wounded him, tearing the top of his right arm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Aeacus, lifting his iron spear against his throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"doing deeds worthy of his high-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed to pierce the very middle of his neck;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Bacchus checked him and snatched away the blood-red point,","st":"37.770"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he strike the neck with javelin-hurling iron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he reined them both back and cried with a raving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cast away these weapons, and let the Enyo you have set up be friendly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this Ares is a friend, and these contests are unwounding.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and taking the elder prizes of the battle-rousing victory,","st":"37.775"},{"sp":"N","t":"boastful Aeacus, lifting the golden greaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave them to his servant; and bringing the later gifts"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterius lifted the spear-won stone of the Indians."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXVIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Where the thirty-eighth is, with the blazing brand you have the doom of wretched Phaethon the charioteer."},{"sp":"N","t":"The contest was dissolved; and the peoples went to the midday haunts of the thicket,","st":"38.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kept to their own huts; and the country Pans"},{"sp":"N","t":"lodged in torrent-cut halls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling at evening in the self-built caves of a wilderness"},{"sp":"N","t":"lioness; and the Satyrs, entering a bear's cave,","st":"38.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"hollowed out a little rocky bed with whetted"},{"sp":"N","t":"claws and not with cutting iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until the light-bearing dawn shone, and freshly appearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the light of a calm Dawn rose for both,"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Indians and for Satyrs; for then at the circling turning-post","st":"38.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"coiling Time stretched out a truce of the Mygdonian"},{"sp":"N","t":"war and the Indian tumult; nor was there for them"},{"sp":"N","t":"slaughter or fighting then; and far from the battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the six-year-old Bacchic shield lay covered with cobwebs."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the Seasons brought the seventh year of the wars,","st":"38.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"then a heavenly sign appeared, the forerunner"},{"sp":"N","t":"for wine-dark Bacchus, an unbelievable wonder; for at midday"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unforeseen darkness was stretched out, and a midday mist"},{"sp":"N","t":"held Phaethon hidden in a blackening robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the beam was stolen the hills were shadowed;","st":"38.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and much wandering fire fell this way and that,"},{"sp":"N","t":"streaming from the heavenly chariot; and a measureless rain"},{"sp":"N","t":"deluged the tops of the earth, and the rocks surged"},{"sp":"N","t":"with airy drops, until scarcely, high upon his car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery Hyperion rose again, high-visible.","st":"38.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"And for Dionysus in his distress a favorable eagle flew"},{"sp":"N","t":"through the air, high on its road, bringing a horned serpent"},{"sp":"N","t":"in its whetted talons; and it, bending its bold neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped head first, self-rolling, into Hydaspes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a trembling silence held the whole numberless army;","st":"38.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shifting-witted Idmon, since he had learned the rites of the Muse"},{"sp":"N","t":"Urania who knows the well-rounded rim of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood fearless alone, since he had learned by a knowing art"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shadowy circles of Selene entwined with Phaethon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the flame purpling beneath the gloomy cone","st":"38.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Phaethon's unseen journey is stolen away,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the thunderous crash of clashing clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the airy bellowing, and the flashing comet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the beam of meteors, and the fiery leap of the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"Taught such works by the goddess Urania","st":"38.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stood, keeping a bold heart; and the limbs of each man"},{"sp":"N","t":"were loosed; and the old prophet Idmon, with a laughing face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having steadfast persuasion upon his lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heartened the whole people, because he knew that after a little"},{"sp":"N","t":"the victory of the long tumult would be near at hand.","st":"38.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Erechtheus asked the much-knowing Phrygian prophet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the signs of highest Zeus, whether they were favorable"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the battle for the adversaries or for Indian-slaying Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not so much longing for the end of the fighting as to hear"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tale of Olympus that is the care of mystic conversation,","st":"38.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ranks of the starry spirals and the circling moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the setting by day of the Phaethontian radiance"},{"sp":"N","t":"when it is stolen away. For the citizens of ancient Attica"},{"sp":"N","t":"are always eager to learn about god-spoken tales."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was the old seer careless, but shaking","st":"38.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Euian thyrsi of Lyaeus and not the laurel of Panopeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he belched such a prophetic word from his throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Do you wish to hear a mind-charming tale, Erechtheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which only the gods who dwell in Olympus know?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall tell it as my laurel Apollo taught me.","st":"38.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not tremble at the lightning, do not fear the wandering fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the gloomy course of Helios, nor the bird of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the first messenger of the victory of Lyaeus to come;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for as that horned serpent, scored by the points"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whetted claws, pierced by the sharp foot","st":"38.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a snatching bird, slipped into the outpourings of the river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and old Hydaspes hid the creeping corpse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so shall his father's swell cover Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who has a shape like his ox-horned begetter.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the god-speaking old man said; and at his prophetic","st":"38.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"word the whole army rejoiced; and beyond the others"},{"sp":"N","t":"the citizen of motherless Athena mixed joy with wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being such in his sweet hopes as though he were reveling"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the midst of Marathon after the war of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then to crag-loving Dionysus, left alone,","st":"38.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"his brother Hermes came from heaven, the messenger of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spoke some word, comforting him about the victory:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Do not tremble at this sign, even if there was night by day;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this, fearless Bacchus, your father Cronion has shown you"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the forerunner of an Indian-slaying victory; for to the sun","st":"38.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashing a second time I liken light-bearing Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the gloomy mist the bold dark-skinned Indian;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for this pattern in the upper air is alike; and as the darkness"},{"sp":"N","t":"dimmed the light of the fair-shining dawn as it was covered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rising again high upon his fire-gleaming car","st":"38.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun hurled away the gloomy mist,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so you too, shaking far away from your eyelids"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gloomy, unseen darkness of the Tartarean Erinys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall flash a second time upon the war like Hyperion."},{"sp":"N","t":"Never did old Aeon, the nurse, bring so great a wonder","st":"38.90","hvShift5":[[0,0],[18,24],[24,48]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[10,20],[17,23],[23,30]]},{"sp":"N","t":"since the time when, struck by the vapor of a divine fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"half-burnt Phaethon fell head first from the light-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"car of Helios, and was hidden in a Celtic river;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the daughters of Helios still groan for the bold youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the brows of Eridanus with plaintive leaves.\"","st":"38.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke Dionysus rejoiced in the hope of victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he questioned Hermes, and wished to hear still more"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tale of Olympus that is the care of the western Celts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how Phaethon rolled through the upper air, or whence the daughters"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Helios themselves, beside the stream of mourning Eridanus,","st":"38.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"were changed into a plant, and from fair-leaved trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"drop glittering tears into the streams."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to him as he asked, gentle Hermes, opening his mouth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieked out a wondrous word to inquiry-loving Bacchus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dionysus, mortal-delighting shepherd of human life,","st":"38.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"if a sweet sting of ancient words urges you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall tell the whole tale of Phaethon in order."},{"sp":"N","t":"Resounding Ocean, girdled about the rim of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing his earth-holding water about a moist turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"consorting in first-born wedlock with Tethys","st":"38.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a watery bridegroom, begot Clymene, whom once Tethys"},{"sp":"N","t":"midwifed at a liquid breast, better than the Naiads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a younger, fair-armed maiden, for whose beauty"},{"sp":"N","t":"Helios, wheeling the twelve-month year,"},{"sp":"N","t":"traveling crown-wise the seven-zoned rim of the upper air,","st":"38.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"the steward of fire, labored with another fire; and the torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Loves forced the flame of his car and the radiance of his beams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when above the horn of reddening Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bathing his fiery body in the eastern waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he beheld the maiden near his road, when naked","st":"38.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"she swam, skipping in her father's streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and flashed as she bathed; and she was such as when"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bountiful evening Moon, having filled out the wheeling"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparkle of her twin horns, blazes through the water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And half-seen, unsandaled, the girl stood in the waters,","st":"38.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting at Helios with her rosy cheeks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pattern of her flesh was scored in the outpourings; not then"},{"sp":"N","t":"did a girdle cover the girl's breast, and, lighting up the lake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the well-rounded rim of her silvery breasts reddened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And her father yoked the girl to the airy charioteer;","st":"38.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the fair-footed Seasons cried the wedding-hymn of Clymene"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the light-bearing marriage of Helios; and around,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naiad Nymphs danced; and beside a watery bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl, blessed in childbirth, was wedded in a flashing marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and received a hot consort with her cold limbs.","st":"38.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the gleam of the starry phalanx was the chamber-servant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the star of Cypris wove a melody for the Wedding-god,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dawnstar, forerunner of the yoking; and instead of a pine-torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene held her bridal beam as bride-adorner;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Hesperides shouted; and with his bride Tethys","st":"38.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ocean cried out a melody from his many-springed throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the belly of Clymene surged with a generative marriage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and travailing with the infant, as the birth ripened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bore a wondrous light-bearing son. And about the boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he was born his father's upper air cried out a melody;","st":"38.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the daughters of Ocean washed the son"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his grandfather's baths as he leapt from the childbed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they cast swaddling-clothes about him; and the stars, darting"},{"sp":"N","t":"with blazing throb into the stream of accustomed Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"circled the boy, and Selene was his Eileithyia,","st":"38.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a light-bearing sparkle; and Helios"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave his son his own name to have, fitting his witnessing"},{"sp":"N","t":"shape; for on the flashing face of the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"shone a radiance kindred to his begetter Helios."},{"sp":"N","t":"And often, playing delicately in his child-tending ways,","st":"38.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ocean lifted Phaethon and tossed him back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising him by the middle of his belly, and along a high-traveling path"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the boy back again in his arm from the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restless, self-whirling, running with a wandering breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurled him up again; and he, with the wheeling throb","st":"38.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the well-turned hand, swerving aside from Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fell with a whirling spin into the black water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a prophet of his own death; and the old man groaned as he perceived it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing the oracles, and hid it in prudent silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he tear the griefless heart of child-loving Clymene","st":"38.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"by prophesying the bitter threads of Phaethon's Fate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the boy, freshly grown, having a beardless chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now tended the house of his Clymene, now went"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the meadow of Thrinacia itself, where he often"},{"sp":"N","t":"stayed beside Lampetie, pasturing the cattle and the sheep; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"38.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"and holding a longing for his divine father the charioteer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fitted an axle to timbers with a cunning bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling a rounded pattern for a lying car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fashioning harness, and plaiting from the flower-tressed gardens"},{"sp":"N","t":"a thrice-twisted whip of slender withies,","st":"38.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set new bridles upon four rams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and making a bastard, well-made Dawnstar"},{"sp":"N","t":"of white flowers, like a wheel-shaped circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set it as the forerunner of his well-greaved wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the pattern of the eastern star; and about his hair,","st":"38.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"propping a light-bearing brand upright this way and that,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he imitated his father with lying beams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding crown-wise about the sea-beaten rim of the island."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he had grown, bearing a fair-flowering youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"often he laid hold of his father's flame, and with a little hand","st":"38.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted the hot harness and the starry whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tended the wheel, and, handling the bodies of the horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"with snowy palms, the boy delighted as he played;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with his right hand he touched the fire-struck bridle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he raved, holding a longing for horsemanship; and seating himself","st":"38.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon his father's knees, shedding suppliant tears,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he asked for the fiery car and the course of the airy horses."},{"sp":"N","t":"And his father refused; and he entreated the more,"},{"sp":"N","t":"asking with a sweet word; and comforting his young son"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the high-traveling car, he spoke with a tender-loving voice:","st":"38.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"'O child of Helios, dear offspring of Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seek another prize; what is the car of Olympus to you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave the untouchable course of horsemanship; for you cannot"},{"sp":"N","t":"steer my chariot, which I myself scarcely drive."},{"sp":"N","t":"Never did furious Ares arm himself with the flaming thunderbolt,","st":"38.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he beats out a melody with the trumpet and not a thunderous crash;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hephaestus does not gather the clouds of his begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he is not called the cloud-gatherer as Cronion is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but beside the forge he strikes an iron anvil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a manufactured wind with manufactured panting;","st":"38.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo has a winged swan, and not a swift horse;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes does not lift the fiery lightning of his begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but holds a rod, and does not raise his father's aegis."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: he gave a spark of the thunderbolt to Zagreus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Zagreus lifted the bolt, and kept company with destruction.","st":"38.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"Be in awe, child, of suffering wholly like sufferings.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and did not persuade him; and the boy, goading his begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wetted his tunics with hotter tears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with his hands he touched his father's flaming beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crouching upon the floor, bending his wheeling neck,","st":"38.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"entreating; and the father pitied the child as he watched."},{"sp":"N","t":"And plaintive Clymene asked the more; and he in his heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing steadfastly the unturning threads of Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nodded in distress, and wiping with his tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rain from the unsmiling eye of weeping Phaethon","st":"38.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"he kissed the boy's lips, and uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'There are twelve houses in all of the fiery upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fixed in the rim of the hollow Zodiac circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"divided in rows, close-set, in which alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"is the slanting, much-coiling path of the unsteady","st":"38.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"planets. And coiling Cronus changes each house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping heavy-kneed, until he scarcely completes at last"},{"sp":"N","t":"thirty circles of returning Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"above the rim of the seventh zone; and high on the sixth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"opposite him, Zeus holds a swifter course than his begetter,","st":"38.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"and passes through his course in a year; and in the third &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery Ares passes in sixty days,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighbor of your father; and rising in the fourth"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself cut the whole vault crown-wise with my car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursuing the much-bending circles of the heavenly Spirals,","st":"38.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing the measures of time, wheeled about with the four Seasons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the same turning-post, until I travel a whole house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling out the accustomed month that brings fulfillment; nor do I"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave my journey unaccomplished and change to a backward path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor again a forward one, since the other much-bending","st":"38.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering stars always go running against me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reining back again both forward and backward"},{"sp":"N","t":"they keep half-finished, re-loosed measures of their road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving from both sides my radiance that darts otherwise;"},{"sp":"N","t":"among whom horned Selene, whitening the vault,","st":"38.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling her whole circle with wise fire, brings forth the month,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seen in the middle, arched, full with her whole face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I, opposite to Mene, wheeling my sphere-like"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparkle, the nurse of the sheaf-bearing birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"travel an endless circle about the Zodiac turning-post,","st":"38.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing forth the measures of time, and changing house from house,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having completed one circle I bring a whole year."},{"sp":"N","t":"But beware the ends of the node, lest, creeping near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling the gloomy cone with your car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it steal the whole light, overshadowing your car;","st":"38.255","hvShift6":[[0,0],[24,25],[30,39]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not ride past the rim of the accustomed circle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, in the much-bending bond of the long-plaited spirals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the rim of the five parallel circles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be goaded, and leave your father's accustomed turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the roaming horses lead you astray in the upper air;","st":"38.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, surveying the twelve circles of the journey,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten from house to another house; and on your car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding the Ram, do not seek to drive the Bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not seek the neighboring Scorpion, forerunner of the plow-beam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steering it star-roaming beneath the Balance,","st":"38.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"unless you have filled out thirty degrees."},{"sp":"N","t":"But hear my word; and I shall teach you all.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The center of the whole cosmos, the mid-navel star of Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending the Ram, lifted high, I increase the spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and treading the tropic rim, forerunner of Zephyrus,","st":"38.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"balancing the night evenly poised against the morning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I steer the dewy course of the swallow Season;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and changing to the lower house opposite the Ram,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending equinoctial lights in the twin Claws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I make ready, turned back again, a day yoked equal with the mist,","st":"38.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lead the leaf-shaking course of the autumn Season,"},{"sp":"N","t":"driving with a lesser light toward a lower turning-post"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the leaf-shedding month; and I bring men the rainy"},{"sp":"N","t":"winter above the back of fishy Capricorn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that the earth may bring forth life-bringing gifts for the country folk,","st":"38.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the rain as bridegroom and the dew as midwife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I make ready the summer, the corn-tressed messenger of harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing the fiery earth with hotter beams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, stretched high beside the turning-post, I steer into his course"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Crab, opposite to unwarming Capricorn,","st":"38.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"increasing both the Nile together and the grape-cluster."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as you begin your course, go to neighboring Cerne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping Phosphorus as escort of an unwandering road,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the forerunner of your horsemanship; and with an alternating journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twelve circling Hours steer your course.'","st":"38.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he fixed a golden helmet upon the head"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Phaethon, and crowned him with his own fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding the seven-toned beams about his locks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling crown-wise a white girdle about his loins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he cloaked him in his own fiery tunic,","st":"38.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clamped his foot tight in a reddening sandal."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he gave the car to his child; and from the eastern manger"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Seasons led the fiery horses of Helios;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Phosphorus came to the yoke, and about the bright"},{"sp":"N","t":"harness he shut the horses' slavish neck.","st":"38.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Phaethon mounted; and his father Helios gave him"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reins to hold, the flashing reins and the gleaming whip;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was shaken with a trembling silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knowing his son was short-lived; and near the bank"},{"sp":"N","t":"Clymene, half-seen, the child-loving mother,","st":"38.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"leapt up with joy, watching him mount the flaming car."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already the dewy star Phosphorus sparkled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phaethon rose, treading the eastern rim,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bathed in his grandfather's waters of Ocean."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold, high-running driver of the fair-shining horses","st":"38.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazed at heaven, scored with the chorus of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeled about its seven zones; he saw the wanderers"},{"sp":"N","t":"running opposite, and beheld the earth like a center"},{"sp":"N","t":"fixed in the middle, raised with long cliffs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"towered on every side with winds beneath its roofs;","st":"38.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he watched the rivers, and the brows of Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"reining his own stream back again into his own water."},{"sp":"N","t":"While he stretched his eye toward the upper air and the pouring of the stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dappled tribes of earth and the restless backs of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing in spirals at the seats of the endless cosmos,","st":"38.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the blazing horses, whirled beneath the yoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"passed by the accustomed rim of the Zodiac circle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And untaught Phaethon, holding the fiery whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was seen lashing the horses' necks; and maddened,","hvShift6":[[0,1],[3,5],[4,10],[5,23],[6,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the goad of an unsparing driver,","st":"38.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horses ran wandering, unwilling, past the starting-line"},{"sp":"N","t":"of their ancient road, beside the axle's turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving a different crash from their accustomed driver."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beside the southern goal and the northern backs of Boreas"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was tumult. And standing beside the heavenly gateway","st":"38.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-footed Seasons marveled at a strange bastard day;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the morning-born trembled; and the star Phosphorus cried:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Where are you carried, dear boy? Why do you rave, driving the horse?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Spare your proud whip; and beware both"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering stars and the chorus of the fixed,","st":"38.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest bold Orion kill you with his sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest old Bootes strike you with his fiery club;"},{"sp":"N","t":"spare your wandering horsemanship still, and let not the Sea-Monster"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Olympus entomb you in the upper air in its long belly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let not the Lion carve you, or the Bull of Olympus,","st":"38.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"arching its neck, strike you with its flaming horn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"beware the Archer, lest from his stretched string"},{"sp":"N","t":"he kill you with a fire-pointed arrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let there not be another Chaos, and let the stars of the upper air"},{"sp":"N","t":"not appear while the day stands, and let the unsteady","st":"38.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"morning-born not meet Selene with a midday car.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke Phaethon drove on further, dragging his car"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward Notus, toward Boreas, near Zephyrus, close to Eurus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was tumult of the upper air, and he shook the harmony"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the unmoving cosmos; and the axle itself,","st":"38.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"pierced through the middle, was slanted in the whirling upper air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely, lightening the self-coiling vault of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Libyan Atlas was propped crouching, bowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a greater burden; and scraping the equinoctial circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"outside the Bear with the coiling trail of his belly,","st":"38.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dragon hissed, running beside the starry Bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Lion roared from his throat at the scorching Dog,"},{"sp":"N","t":"warming the upper air with fierce fire, and stood bold,"},{"sp":"N","t":"routing the eight-footed Crab with a shaggy-haired throb;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the hind foot of the heavenly Lion","st":"38.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"his thirsty tail lashed the Maiden near his road;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the winged girl, darting past Bootes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came near the axle and kept company with the Wagon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the western turning-post the star Phosphorus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending wandering beams, pushed Hesperus on the opposite path;","st":"38.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the morning-born strayed; and instead of the accustomed Hare"},{"sp":"N","t":"smoky Sirius gripped the thirsty Bear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving it in two, one Notus, the other Boreas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the starry Fishes skipped about Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neighbors of the Water-pourer; and with a tumbler's throb","st":"38.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"the coiling Dolphin danced, running with Capricorn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Orion trembled even among the stars at the Scorpion"},{"sp":"N","t":"near his road, strayed in spirals from the southern path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching his own sword, lest, creeping slowly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"it scrape the tips of his feet a second time with its sharp sting;","st":"38.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spitting out the half-finished radiance of her face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blackening at the tips, midday Mene leapt up;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she was not stealing a bastard light from the male fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Phaethon opposite, and did not milk her kindred radiance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the coiling seven-starred echo of the Pleiad phalanx","st":"38.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"roared through the seven-zoned heaven with a circling voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darting a din from throats equal in number"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering stars raved in Bacchic frenzy, running against one another;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris pushed Zeus, Ares pushed Cronus, and my own"},{"sp":"N","t":"migrant star came near the springtime Pleiad,","st":"38.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, mixing a kindred radiance with the seven-pathed stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose half-seen beside my mother Maia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swerving from the heavenly car, with which it always is"},{"sp":"N","t":"either a companion or a forerunner in the morning, and in the evening"},{"sp":"N","t":"sends its beams behind the setting sun;","st":"38.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when it travels with an equal course and an equal share,"},{"sp":"N","t":"those who know the stars have called it the heart of the sun;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretching a neck drenched with dewy snowflakes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bull of Olympus, bridegroom of Europa, bellowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"straightening his crooked foot into the course; and slanting","st":"38.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"his sharp-stretched horn from his crooked forehead toward Phaethon"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck the heavenly rim with flaming hooves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bold Orion drew his sword hanging at his blazing"},{"sp":"N","t":"thigh; Bootes brandished his crook;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking the aloft knees of his starry foot","st":"38.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pegasus whinnied, and, darting at the vault with his hoof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the half-seen Libyan horse ran up to the neighboring Swan,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in anger shook his wings, so that he might hurl down"},{"sp":"N","t":"another driver from the upper air, as he himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook Bellerophon from the heavenly rim;","st":"38.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"and no longer did the circling Bears dance beside one another's"},{"sp":"N","t":"loins near the high-traveling northern turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but they mingled with Notus, and beside the western lake"},{"sp":"N","t":"they bathed their unwetted step in an unaccustomed Ocean."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father laid Phaethon low with his thunderbolt,","st":"38.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-rolling from on high above the stream of Eridanus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and binding the harmony, recovered again, with an age-mate bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave the horses back to Helios, and gave the airy"},{"sp":"N","t":"car to the east, and beside the ancient turning-post"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fair-footed Seasons, attendants of Phaethon, ran.","st":"38.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the whole earth laughed a second time; and from the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rain of life-begetting Zeus cleansed all the fields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and quenched with a liquid drop the wandering fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as much as over the whole earth the blazing horses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"neighing from heaven, spat from their much-burning throats.","st":"38.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Helios rose, shepherding his returning car;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the seed grew, and the threshing-floors laughed again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the former life-bringing radiance of the upper air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father fixed Phaethon in Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like the Charioteer and named for him; and stretching out","st":"38.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"the light-bearing heavenly Car with flashing forearm"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has the pattern of a Charioteer darting into the course,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though longing for his father's car even among the stars."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fire-burnt river rose to the vault of the stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the assent of Zeus, and in a starry circle","st":"38.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"the crooked water of fiery Eridanus wheels."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sisters of the swift-fated fallen charioteer"},{"sp":"N","t":"changed their shape into a plant, and from mourning trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"they drip a wealthy dew upon their leaves.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XXXIX"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"And in the thirty-ninth you see, after the waves, Deriades fleeing the fire-blazing fleet of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, Hermes went untouchable to heaven,","st":"39.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving joy and wonder to his brother Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Bacchus still marveled at the pouring of the disordered stars"},{"sp":"N","t":"and at fallen Phaethon, how among the Celts"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slipped fire-burnt into a western stream,","st":"39.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the ships came up, which the Rhadamanes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"steering them in ranks upon the sea toward the sea-fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares of the Indians, sailed over a waveless sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the drivers of the battle, striking the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"with alternating rushes; and for hastening Lyaeus","st":"39.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"an escorting wind whistled upon the answering merchant ships."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Lycus led the way, driving his car in the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scraping the unscored stream with horses' hooves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And measureless Deriades, high above upon his towers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watched with an angry eye the sails of the ships","st":"39.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurrying like clouds, and, hearing overbearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"that an Arabian carpenter had rounded out battle-rousing ships,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he swore to raise Enyo against the woodcutting Arabs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and threatened to wipe out the city of Lycurgus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having mowed the Rhadamanes with crushing iron.","st":"39.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the fleet the fearless Indians trembled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at a sea-beaten Ares, until even the knees"},{"sp":"N","t":"of bold Deriades himself were loosed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a manufactured laugh upon his calm face"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Indian king commanded them to bring the people","st":"39.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"from three hundred islands beside the hard-to-tread ankles"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his elephant-pasturing land; and a swift herald went upon the path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"traveling from land to land with much-bending feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a sharp fleet came from the wide-scattered islands"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the king's summons; and he, stretching a bold neck,","st":"39.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing his well-helmeted merchant ships to a sea-battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heartened the whole people, and spoke with a lofty-minded voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Men whom my battle-steadfast Hydaspes reared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"now fight again with confidence; and bring blazing"},{"sp":"N","t":"fire into the battle, and kindle unspeakable pine,","st":"39.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may burn the newly come ships with a smoky brand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hide the wet-traveling army in the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with spear, with breastplate, with merchant ships, with Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"If Bacchus was a god, I shall destroy Bacchus with my fire;"},{"sp":"N","t":"is it not enough that, sprinkling many-turning drugs in the outpourings,","st":"39.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reddened my Hydaspes with Thessalian flowers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I, seeing it, kept silence, and still endured quietly"},{"sp":"N","t":"to look upon the yellow streams of my defiled river?"},{"sp":"N","t":"For if this stream had been from an alien river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and my father had not been warlike Indian Hydaspes,","st":"39.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"then I would have filled this flood with poured dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dimming the clustered scent of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, treading the drunken outpouring of my begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would have run across the unwetted water with dusty feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as among the Argives it is said that the Earth-shaker","st":"39.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the water dry, and a horse's hoof scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Inachian dust of a parched river."},{"sp":"N","t":"He is no god, no god; he has lied about his own stock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for what Olympian aegis of Cronion does he brandish?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What spark has he of the Zeus-struck thunderbolt?","st":"39.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"What heavenly lightning of his begetter does he lift?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cronides does not arm himself for war with wine-dark ivy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not liken a thunderous melody to the crash of drums,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall I call thyrsi like the bolts of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall I say the cloud of Zeus equal to an earthly breastplate;","st":"39.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"when do I liken a varied star to a cunning fawnskin?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say that he received the cluster and the pouring of wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"as gifts from his plant-increasing father Cronion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"to one who bears Trojan blood and to some country herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Ganymede the wine-pourer, Zeus gave the nectar of Olympus,","st":"39.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wine is not like nectar; yield, thyrsi."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus feasts upon the earth together with Satyrs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ganymede dines with the heavenly immortals."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if this man was mortal, born of a heavenly father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he touched a single table with Zeus and the blessed ones.","st":"39.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have heard how once he gave his own throne and the scepter"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Olympus as a prize to Zagreus, the elder Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lightning to Zagreus and the vine to wine-dark Bacchus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke and rose to battle; and the peoples rushed together"},{"sp":"N","t":"with spear, with shields, and they transferred to the sea","st":"39.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"their late hope of victory in a land war."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus called to his champions with a raving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Valiant children of Ares and of well-corseleted Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose life is the works of battle and whose hopes are contests,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten to wipe out the race of the Indians at sea also,","st":"39.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplishing a victory at sea after one on land."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, doubling your spears as the ministers of a sea fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plaited together in a twin bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-fighting, glued fast, clothed in bronze about the point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingle a sea-dismaying Enyo with your adversaries,","st":"39.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"getting there first, lest Deriades, lifting a fire-gleaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"brand in his hand, burn the warlike timbers of the ships."},{"sp":"N","t":"Fight without fear, Mimallons; for the hopes"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the wet-fighting adversaries are empty-boasting; and if the leader"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Indians, laboring, did not accomplish the battle on land,","st":"39.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"sitting upon the crests of sky-high elephants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloud-neighboring, untouchable, unwounded, a neighbor of the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then I never lack champions, nor shall I call"},{"sp":"N","t":"another helper after my father Cronion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the charioteer of sea and upper air; and if I wish,","st":"39.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall arm Poseidon, the brother of my Cronides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to dim the whole Indian rank with his trident;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I have as fellow-contender the broad-bearded champion"},{"sp":"N","t":"Glaucus, the offspring of the Earth-shaker, the neighbor of my Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea-citizen of the Aonian field of Anthedon;","st":"39.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have Glaucus and Phorcys; and Melicertes shall hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"the merchant ship of Deriades as it is lashed by the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorifying his kinsman Dionysus, whose mother once"},{"sp":"N","t":"reared the infant Bacchus, since Ino of the sea gave"},{"sp":"N","t":"a single milk to them both, to Palaemon and to Dionysus;","st":"39.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I am the friend of the prophetic old man Proteus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once foretold with a submerged voice"},{"sp":"N","t":"our victory to come upon the sea; and my Thetis"},{"sp":"N","t":"arms the daughters of Nereus for the fighting, and in the tumults"},{"sp":"N","t":"my Ino shall arm herself as fellow-contender of the Bassarids;","st":"39.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall arm Aeolus too for the war, so that I may behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eurus hurling the spear and Boreas wielding the lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son-in-law of my champion, the ravisher of a Marathonian bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ethiopian Notus as shield-bearer of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zephyrus far more, in a stormy tumult,","st":"39.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall ravage the merchant ships of the adversaries; for he has"},{"sp":"N","t":"as consort our Iris, the messenger of Zeus. But in silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"outside the fair-thyrsus Indian tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let bold Aeolus stay quiet, clamping his windy bag"},{"sp":"N","t":"tight in its accustomed bond, and let not the winds","st":"39.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"play the champion at sea with Indian-slaying pantings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I shall accomplish the battle, stretching out ship-destroying thyrsi.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he armed his confident leaders."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already the trumpet stood as forerunner of war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the flutes of Ares cried out a battle-rousing melody","st":"39.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathering the people, and the beaten ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"resounded the bronze-struck echo of a sea fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the host-gathering pipe cried a like-sounding clang;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of a rocky voice the Panian Echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"speaking last, answered with the warlike remnants of a sea voice.","st":"39.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"And for them as they fought there was tumult, and a cry rose"},{"sp":"N","t":"from those who called; and the people fought with accustomed art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling the whole host crown-wise, and in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fleet of the Indians was girdled by the yoked ships"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the pattern of fish penned in a net; and to the sons of Aeacus","st":"39.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus, prophesying the wet Ares of Salamis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he began the war shrieked out a god-fearing voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"If before, hearing our suppliant cry,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you drove the unsown drought from the broad threshing-floor of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing life-bringing water to a thirsty land,","st":"39.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant the same favor again, late-accomplished, rainy Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorifying me with water here too; and let someone say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching our victory: 'As on land"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus honored his own son, so among the seas he honors him.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And let another Achaean man say: 'Under a single ordinance","st":"39.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeacus is Indian-slayer and life-bringer; for both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shearing hostile heads and granting fruit to the furrow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave joy to Demeter and gladness to Dionysus.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And save the voyage of our merchant ship; and as to a squalid,"},{"sp":"N","t":"earthly hollow I brought life-bringing water,","st":"39.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"so I arm the death-bearing swell of the deep flanks"},{"sp":"N","t":"to fight against the armies and the merchant ships of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, father, scepter-bearer of life, scepter-bearer of tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"send me an eagle, the bird that heralds my race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the right for the champions and for your Dionysus;","st":"39.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let another bird come on the left for the adversaries;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let these different signs be for both:"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one I shall behold carried on a snatching foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a vast horned serpent, a corpse"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored by the sharp goad of whetted claws,","st":"39.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"announcing the end of a horned adversary;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let another, dark-skinned, come to the people of the foes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesying with dark wings the slaughter of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the black pattern of a self-chosen death; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesy my victory with thunderous crashes,","st":"39.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, sending the lightning of Bromius, the lights of his childbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"honor your son again with fire, and let thunderbolts"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoot the well-helmeted merchant ships of the adversaries."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, father, remember Aegina, lest you shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"the winged bird of the Loves, the bridegroom of your bride.\"","st":"39.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he made war; and casting his eye toward the airy"},{"sp":"N","t":"paths, facing the returning Bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Erechtheus entreated his son-in-law and cried a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Boreas my son-in-law, arm yourself, and stretching a helping"},{"sp":"N","t":"panting for the fighting father of your bride","st":"39.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"give a victory at sea as bride-gift of your chamber;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a ship-saving breeze to the merchant ships of Bromius"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to us both, to Erechtheus and to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lashing a maddened sea against the ships of Deriades"},{"sp":"N","t":"arm your tempests with a wavy panting —","st":"39.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you are practiced in fighting, since you too"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwell in Thrace, practiced, like Ares —"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bringing an ill-winded panting to the phalanx of the adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"arm yourself with a frosty spear against Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and setting up a stormy Enyo against the foes","st":"39.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"shoot your adversaries with a hail-laden missile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping faith with Zeus and Pallas and Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Remember Cecropia of the fair maidens, where women"},{"sp":"N","t":"embroider with the shuttle the wedding of your Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and honor Ilissus the bride-adorner, where the ravishing","st":"39.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"breezes snatched up your Attic bride, my daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated unshaken upon your unmoving shoulder."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know that another wind will come as fellow-contender,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eastern neighbor of the adversaries; but in the battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not tremble at bold Eurus, because the winged winds,","st":"39.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"all that blow, are the attendants of Boreas;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Corymbasus, the champion of the Ethiopians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"return no more to the southern edge of the land, but be subdued,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[36,35],[47,43],[48,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"having his hot fellow-contender, Ethiopian Notus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he has drunk the cold death-bearing water above the sea;","st":"39.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"I care nothing for Zephyrus when Boreas arms himself."},{"sp":"N","t":"Show your like-mindedness to your father-in-law; and from heaven"},{"sp":"N","t":"Poseidon with you, fighting with his trident, and Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall help my Bacchic armies,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she her own dwellers, and he the offspring of his brother;","st":"39.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fiery Hephaestus, honoring the blood of Erechtheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall come fair to meet into the watery Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling a fighting torch against the ships of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"And grant that I may conquer in the waters too, and after the victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Erechtheus bring his people unharmed to Cecropia,","st":"39.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Athens sing of Boreas and Oreithyia.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Crying such a word he took hold of the sea-whirling battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a cunning spear, and as a dweller of Marathon"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had a sea-fighting longing; and in the oar-loving tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ares was then a well-equipped sailor, and in his palm","st":"39.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"Terror held the rudder, and Panic, the steersman of the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosed the cables of the javelin-bearing ships."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanxes of the Cyclopes sailed the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting at the merchant ships with sea-neighboring cliffs;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Euryalus shouted, and cloud-neighboring Halimedes","st":"39.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"was goaded into the fighting in a sea-roaring tumult."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a sea Ares crashed upon the two armies"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the land fighting, and with a sea-roaring shout"},{"sp":"N","t":"the merchant ships of the Indians poured upon the Bacchic ships;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and there was slaughter on both sides, and the waves seethed with gore,","st":"39.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a great host of both fell; and with freshly poured"},{"sp":"N","t":"blood the backs of the dark sea reddened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And many, falling this way and that in a poured destruction,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sailed the sea as swollen mariners;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having the winds as ferrymen in spirals upon the surges","st":"39.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dead throng was dragged, running with an unsparing breeze;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many, self-rolling beneath the whirl of the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped into the stream, and drinking the bitter water"},{"sp":"N","t":"by necessity perceived the threads of a submerged Fate,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weighed down by their breastplates; and the black water covered","st":"39.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"the same-colored bodies of the swollen dark corpses"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a weedy depth, and with its wet-traveling bearer"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bronze tunic was covered in muddy clay;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sea was their tomb. And many were entombed"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the jaws of sea-monsters, and in a fishy throat","st":"39.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"a seal, tossing a breathless corpse, buried it,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belching a yellow stream of blood. And the sea received"},{"sp":"N","t":"the armor of the slain, and of a freshly slaughtered bearer"},{"sp":"N","t":"the crested helmet floated of its own accord through the water"},{"sp":"N","t":"as its strap was loosed, and in the stormy stream","st":"39.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"the circles of many a roaming ox-hide shield swam"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its drenched baldric. And much reddening foam"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushed up beneath the topmost waves of the grey brine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spotting the all-white pouring with a bloody trail."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Melicertes was reddened with murderous drops;","st":"39.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Leucothea shouted, the child-tender of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a proud neck, and about the Indian-slaying victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Nymph crowned her hair with a weedy flower;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unveiled Thetis, peering above the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"propping her hands upon both Doris and Panopeia,","st":"39.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched a glad eye upon fair-thyrsus Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Galatea of the deep was carried half-seen"},{"sp":"N","t":"through the sea-gulf, scraping the calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and watching the sea-dismaying Enyo of the murderous"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cyclops she was shaken, and changed her cheeks with fear;","st":"39.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she hoped to see Polyphemus in the war of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting beside Lyaeus against Deriades;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in terror she entreated Aphrodite of the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"to save the son of Poseidon as he played the champion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and besought the child-loving dark-haired god","st":"39.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"to shield his son Polyphemus as he fought."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the daughters of Nereus wheeled about the bearer"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the deep-sea trident; and propped upon his trident"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea Earth-shaker watched the neighboring battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, gazing at the army of well-corseleted Dionysus,","st":"39.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous as he beheld the Enyo of another Cyclops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"broke into a much-reproaching voice at wet-fighting Bromius:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dear Bacchus, gathering so many Cyclopes into the fray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but leaving one alone far from the fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have a many-circled contest for a seven-year time,","st":"39.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"feeding a shifting hope of endless battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because all the shield-bearers of your great contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"lack one unconquered Polyphemus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for if my son the Cyclops had come to your fighting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, fighting beside Dionysus over the plain,","st":"39.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"had whirled the point of my father's trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then, cutting down a great dread throng with my trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would have killed the whole race of the Indians in a single dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crushed the breast of ox-horned Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"Long ago another son of mine, having a hundred hands,","st":"39.285","hvShift6":[[0,0],[8,1],[21,16]]},{"sp":"N","t":"helped your father, the destroyer of the Titans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"many-armed Aegaeon, when, drawing Cronus into terror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stretched out the wide-scattered nation of his sky-high arms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a high-necked mane that shadowed the sun,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the grim Titans were driven from Olympus,","st":"39.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the Enyo of skilled-handed Briareus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he spoke in envy with an indignant voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Thoosa in shame kept downcast cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since love-maddened Polyphemus was not present at the war."},{"sp":"N","t":"And when the labor of the loud-roaring tumult was ended,","st":"39.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus beheld his accustomed sea streaming with blood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Earth-shaker marveled at the yellow backs of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing man-eating fishes, and, with the multitude of corpses,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwetted backs of the neighboring sea bridged over. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Bacchic phalanxes poured upon the blazing host.","st":"39.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a measureless army of the adversaries lay, of whom in the battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"some were struck with swords and sharp-piercing arrows."},{"sp":"N","t":"Into one a shaft fell above the flank, and of another, struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"by a bronze spear above the rim of the middle of his temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wound was deepened as his head was scored.","st":"39.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"And many of the wide-scattered rowers this way and that,"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the sea with alternating oars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whitened the dark, close-set pouring with foam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the labor of the hurrying rowers was useless,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a steersman cut the gathered cables with helping iron","st":"39.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and split the rope with his sword."},{"sp":"N","t":"And from both phalanxes, sending a whistle through the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a long-shadowed rain of unwandering arrows flowed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"of which one struck the very middle of a mast, and another, passing"},{"sp":"N","t":"through a well-whirled sail, boomed, running with the breezes,","st":"39.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"another was fixed in the forestays, and another lay"},{"sp":"N","t":"fallen in the mast-box, and another wandering shaft through the air"},{"sp":"N","t":"hit the topmost tip of the high-crested yard-arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another was stretched upon the benches; and others,"},{"sp":"N","t":"appearing near, strayed from the path of the steersman","st":"39.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scraped the restless tips of the rudder-blade;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and glorious-bowed Phlogius, drawing a wind-borne shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the deck of a ship and did not hit Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one might see in the sea a fair-winged wandering shaft"},{"sp":"N","t":"entwined about the clusters of a crooked octopus;","st":"39.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one missed another; and with Erythraean iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"another struck a pilot-fish as he hurled at Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Corymbasus hurled his spear, so that he might hit"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tail of a Satyr, and the lance, darting past,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the twin tail of a wet-traveling fish","st":"39.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its whetted point; and aiming with his iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the useless mark of unwoundable Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades sent his spear, and straying from Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the deadly point busied itself in the spine of a dolphin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the curving neck of the fish is joined to its crest,","st":"39.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dolphin, self-coiling, half-dead, skipped"},{"sp":"N","t":"about its accustomed circling turning-post with a dancer's leap of Fate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many fishes this way and that danced as tumblers"},{"sp":"N","t":"of destruction, from their scored backs."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Steropes fought in the front; and high-footed Halimedes,","st":"39.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking in his hand the headland of a sea-born hill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flung it at the adversaries; and a roaming ship sank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the wheel-shaped circle of the rough stone."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a sea-running spear-point, hurled through a merchant ship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoked two ships together as neighbor to neighbor,","st":"39.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the two in a common bond"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were crowded like clouds; and there was a discordant echo."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fleet of both held a fourfold Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of which one part was about the back of blazing Eurus opposite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another beside the wing of dewy Lips, another of Boreas,","st":"39.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another beside the southern floor. And with alternating rushes"},{"sp":"N","t":"swift-kneed Morrheus, stepping from merchant ship to merchant ship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified the sea-dismayed Enyo of the Bassarids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"playing the champion equally in the waters too; but Euius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wounding him with his thyrsus, reined him back from the wet fighting,","st":"39.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laboring in his pains Morrheus went off to the city."},{"sp":"N","t":"While the divine hand of a toil-releasing Brahman healed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Phoebean art the inspired wound he had received,"},{"sp":"N","t":"murmuring a chattering hymn beneath a divine song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the Lydian Ares assailed the adversaries.","st":"39.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"For them the sailing was battle-rousing, and Enyo held"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lead of the voyage, and of the sea-crashing tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"the turmoil of both was of a different kind; for of the adversaries,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all who were shot with rocky missiles"},{"sp":"N","t":"or with murderous leaves or with spears or with knives,","st":"39.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"rowing with unaccustomed hands into the black water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were entombed in the sea with unsteady steps;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if any warrior of Bromius fell into the brine when struck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tossing his palms he swam, cutting the waves"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sea-fighting hands, and in the sea-roaring tumult,","st":"39.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting in the surges, split the water after the men."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cronion tipped the scales of the sea battle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"preparing a watery victory for Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dark-haired god armed himself with his deep-sea trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting against the foes, and Melicertes raved in Bacchic frenzy,","st":"39.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"steering the unwetted chariot of Poseidon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the winds, riding upon the sea with four tempests,"},{"sp":"N","t":"towering the waves, armed themselves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wishing to wipe out the rank of the adversaries' ships,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Zephyrus armed himself, and Notus whistled to Eurus,","st":"39.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"some the helpers of Deriades, others of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Boreas, bringing a Thracian counter-blowing breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the savage backs of the maddened sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Strife, steering the warlike fleet of Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"led the fighting; and Victory bellied the sails","st":"39.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the ships of Dionysus with an Indian-slaying palm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And propping a warlike conch upon wet lips"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus bellowed a melody with a sea trumpet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thetis crashed out the melody of a warlike Echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shielding Lyaeus with her father's waves.","st":"39.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Eurymedon the Cabeirus, lifting his accustomed brand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"found a guile to help the fighting; for he burned"},{"sp":"N","t":"his own long ship, kindling a willing fire;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and against the ships of the enemy the roving ship ran,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,3]]},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping about the sea at the nods of Bacchus,","st":"39.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with slanting coils, passing from merchant ship to merchant ship,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering fire swam into a self-coiling circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"burning this way and that the rank of the wide-scattered ships."},{"sp":"N","t":"And an unveiled Nereid, beholding the radiance"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the fire-struck sea, entered the depths of the deep,","st":"39.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the liquid fire through the blazing water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Indian throng withdrew to the land, leaving the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phaethon laughed, because after his former bonds"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-fighting Ares fled again from the fire of Hephaestus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Deriades, untouchable, seeing the flame running with the breezes,","st":"39.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"flew into the plain, shaking his knees more swiftly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the wet Ares of sea-fighting Dionysus."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XL"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The fortieth holds the cutting down of the leader of the Indians, and how Dionysus entered Tyre, the fatherland of Cadmus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did he escape all-seeing Justice, nor the unbending","st":"40.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"threads of the unbreakable spindle of Fate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Pallas Athena, beholding him in flight —"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she sat upon a jutting cliff beside the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the sea-fighting battle of the Indians as they armed —","st":"40.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"leapt up from her watch-place, and put on a male shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with mind-stealing whispers she beguiled the leader of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the look of Morrheus, and doing favor to Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she checked Deriades, and, as though caring for the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieked out a dreadful word with a much-reproaching voice:","st":"40.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Do you flee, Deriades? To whom have you left the Ares of the ships?"},{"sp":"N","t":"How can you appear to your dwellers? Or whence shall you look"},{"sp":"N","t":"face to face on battle-steadfast Orsiboe, if she hears"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Deriades fled and did not stand against women?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Have respect for man-breaking Cheirobie, lest she perceive you","st":"40.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"cowering before the ironless fighting of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she who, holding a furious spear and heaving up a shield,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought against the Bassarids, following her consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"Withdraw from me and leave the battle to Morrheus; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I myself shall play the champion and destroy feeble Bacchus.","st":"40.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not call you father-in-law while you flee; and let your daughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cheirobie have another husband; for in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall leave your city, and shall go to the land of the Medes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to Scythia, so that I may not be called your son-in-law."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: my well-armed wife knows Enyo.","st":"40.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"There are Amazons about Caucasus, where many"},{"sp":"N","t":"women play the champion far better than Cheirobie;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there, if I wish, I shall take one spear-won, mighty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"without bride-price, into marriage; for in my chambers"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not receive the child of a war-fleeing father.\"","st":"40.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded proud Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave him courage a second time, so that he might be subdued,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the man-destroying thyrsus of Bromius as he fought."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bold man, not knowing that guileful Athena was present,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing the reproaching word of a lying Morrheus,","st":"40.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried a comforting voice with shamefaced lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Spare your words; why do you blame me, fearless Morrheus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"He is no champion, no champion, forever changing his own body."},{"sp":"N","t":"For I am helpless — whom do I fight, or whom do I strike?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hastening to strike Dionysus with a winged shaft,","st":"40.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"or to smite him with my sword in the middle of the neck, or sending my spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing to wound him through the belly, instead of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I meet a dappled-backed leopard rushing at me; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I hasten to cut the neck of a fighting lion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and instead of a lion I behold a bold, frightful serpent;","st":"40.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hastening, instead of a dragon I gaze at the back of a bear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I hurl my furious spear at its arched crest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but in vain I stretch out my long missile; for instead of the bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"an air-roaming, unwoundable flame appears, flying."},{"sp":"N","t":"Seeing a boar come on, I hear the bellowing of an ox;","st":"40.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of the boar I gaze at some bull, hurling"},{"sp":"N","t":"our elephants above his crooked brow"},{"sp":"N","t":"with gleaming horns; and I whirl my sword"},{"sp":"N","t":"against wide-scattered beasts, and I subdue not one beast."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing a plant I stretch out my shaft, but as it flees","st":"40.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"I see water arched toward an airy turning-post."},{"sp":"N","t":"Therefore, trembling at the many-drugged wonders of his art,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shun the shifting fighting of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I shall arm myself against Bromius again, until I convict"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cunning tricks of guile-stitching Dionysus.\"","st":"40.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he armed himself a second time in his accustomed madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and again the battle roared in the plain, and he armed himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Dionysus as he fought after the sea-fighting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he was forgetful of the earlier victory of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, his neck entwined in a woody bond,","st":"40.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"he raised a much-prayed supplication to witnessing Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he was a god-fighting champion again; and he had a twofold"},{"sp":"N","t":"counsel, either to take Bacchus or to make him a slave."},{"sp":"N","t":"Three times he sent his spear, and missed, striking the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but when the fourth time he ran upon wine-dark Bacchus,","st":"40.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"overbearing Deriades hurling his hanging spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"at a useless mark, he called upon his son-in-law,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fellow-contender of his own contest, and Morrheus appeared no more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Athena, changing her guile-weaving shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood beside the clustered god; and as he watched,","st":"40.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the knees of Deriades were loosed with a divine terror;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he knew that the deceiving image of a human shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore the mimicry of the answering face of Morrheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he perceived the beguiling guile of wise Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing her Dionysus rejoiced, and in his heart","st":"40.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"knew that lying Athena was fighting beside him."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then the clustered god raved in anger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched high, vast, equal to the Parnassian rock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he pursued swift-running Deriades; but he, fleeing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched out light, running with the hurrying breezes;","st":"40.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"but when they came to the place where old Hydaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"war-breeding water, murmured with a raving wave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"there he stood measureless beside the banks of the river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as having his father as fellow-contender, a resounding warrior,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a liquid javelin-hurler against Dionysus as he armed;","st":"40.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the vine god, hurling his flesh-cutting thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored only the very surface of the flesh of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, struck by the man-destroying ivy branch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped head first into his father's stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bridging the whole water of his own accord","st":"40.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his long limbs. And after the long war of the Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gods returned again to Olympus with all-ruling Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Bacchoi shouted around, crying Euoi for the fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unopposed Dionysus, and many gathered"},{"sp":"N","t":"wounding the whole body of Deriades with their spears.","st":"40.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Orsiboe wailed upon the much-lamenting towers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mourning her consort lying freshly cut down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with mourning nails she scored the circles of her face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tore the uncared-for cluster of her crooked hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and poured smoky dust upon her head;","st":"40.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she beat her dark arms, and bared"},{"sp":"N","t":"her whole silvery breast as her tunic was split;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cheirobie wailed for her perished father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Protonoe, unsandaled, scraping her cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaming the circles of her dust-covered face,","st":"40.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"wept for them both, for her husband and her father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a double pain, and cried with a mourning voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Husband, you perished young, out of due season; and you left me"},{"sp":"N","t":"a widow in the halls, untried in childbirth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I bore no infant son as a comfort; nor after the victory","st":"40.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"did I see my husband return a second time, but he was subdued"},{"sp":"N","t":"by his own iron, and gave his name to the streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and died among strangers, so that I might call my husband"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsown, self-slain, never-returning, watery Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"I mourn them both, Deriades and Orontes,","st":"40.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"perished alike in a watery doom; for the wave"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid man-slaying Deriades, and the stream covered Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not been like my mother; for Orsiboe"},{"sp":"N","t":"sang the weddings of her daughters as they were accomplished;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she saw the marriage of Protonoe, she received Orontes as son-in-law,","st":"40.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she yoked Cheirobie to an unconquered consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom even so great a Bacchus fears; and Cheirobie"},{"sp":"N","t":"tends her dear husband living, and no thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"no stream has laid him low; but I suffer doubly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my husband gone and my father destroyed.","st":"40.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cease, nurse, comforting your child in vain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant me to have my husband, and I shall not lament my father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"show me some child, a comfort for my grief for my husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"Who would take me and bring me to the holy vale of Daphne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who would take me and bring me to broad-flowing Hydaspes,","st":"40.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may kiss the dear swell of the honey-dripping river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may embrace Orontes even in his outpourings?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that I too were a lovely stream; would that I myself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rained upon with tears, might appear there as a spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where my husband, dead, rolls his fair-watered swell,","st":"40.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"a watery bedfellow; and I shall be like Comaetho,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, having loved a lovely river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delights, holding Cydnus still in her arms as consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as I hear the tale from Morrheus, my brother-in-law,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,2]]},{"sp":"N","t":"a tale that is the care of those men among the Cilicians;","st":"40.145","hvShift6":[[0,0],[18,11],[36,45],[50,56]]},{"sp":"N","t":"nor do I in my longing pass by sweet Orontes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Periboea in flight, and I shall never guard myself"},{"sp":"N","t":"against a watery consort, reining back his crooked water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if it is not fated for me to die beside neighboring Daphne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let my father's father Hydaspes hide me in his waves,","st":"40.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not see a Phrygian revel, let me not shake cymbals in my hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not celebrate a play-loving rite, nor behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maeonia, let me not see Tmolus, nor the house of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not sleep in the arms of a horned Satyr,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or bear the heavy-burdened yokes of slavery, lest someone say:","st":"40.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The daughter of Deriades the spear-bold king"},{"sp":"N","t":"serves Dionysus as plunder after the fighting.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke the women groaned pitiably with her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whose child, whose brother had died, whose fathers"},{"sp":"N","t":"or whose fresh-bearded husband untimely. And tearing","st":"40.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hair from her head Cheirobie scored her cheeks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she was lashed with a twofold pain, and did not so much"},{"sp":"N","t":"groan for her father as rage at her consort;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she had heard of the love-maddened necessity of Morrheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the beguiling, prudent guile of Chalcomede.","st":"40.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"And she spoke some word, tearing her own tunic:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Morrheus killed my father by sparing his ash-spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was he the avenger of the dead; but longing for"},{"sp":"N","t":"hateful Chalcomede he did not drive off the female Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but still does favor to the Bassarids. Tell me, Fates:","st":"40.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"what envy has sacked the Indian city? What envy suddenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"assailed both the daughters of Deriades?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orontes, dying in the fighting, made his consort"},{"sp":"N","t":"Protonoe an uncared-for, mourning widow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cheirobie's consort has refused her while she still lives.","st":"40.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"I suffer more deadly pains than my sister;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Protonoe had a husband who helped her nurse,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cheirobie had a husband who destroyed her own fatherland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a useless spearman, an attendant of the Cyprus-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"valiant, shifting, like-minded with Lyaeus.","st":"40.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"My own marriage too was armed against me; for because"},{"sp":"N","t":"our Morrheus longed, the city of the Indians was plundered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I was robbed of my father for a husband's sake; I who was proud before,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of a king, I once the mistress of the Indians."},{"sp":"N","t":"I too shall be one of the handmaids; and soon, wretched,","st":"40.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall call my slave Chalcomede my mistress."},{"sp":"N","t":"Today you hold the Indian seat, deceitful Morrheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"tomorrow you will go self-bidden to the land of the Lydians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"serving Dionysus for the beauty of Chalcomede."},{"sp":"N","t":"Take the bed of Chalcomede openly, bridegroom Morrheus;","st":"40.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you no longer tremble at the grim mouth of Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"Withdraw — the serpent calls you again, which pursues you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a hiss as the guardian of an unplundered marriage.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the heavy-weeping bride said in grief;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Protonoe wailed a second time. And leaning her hands","st":"40.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon them both, their mother cried out downcast:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"The hopes of our fatherland have fallen; no longer do I see"},{"sp":"N","t":"my husband Deriades, and no longer my son-in-law Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"Deriades is dead; the city of the Indians has been plundered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbreakable wall of my land has fallen; would that Bacchus","st":"40.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"might take me too and destroy me with my perished consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and take me and hurl me into swift-flowing Hydaspes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since I refuse the land; let my father-in-law water hold me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let me see Deriades even in the waters; and let me not behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"Protonoe following Dionysus unwilling,","st":"40.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I hear another pitiable lament of Cheirobie"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she is dragged to the love of spear-won weddings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not see another husband after my man Deriades."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let me share a hearth with the Naiads, since the dark-haired god"},{"sp":"N","t":"received even Leucothea herself alive,","st":"40.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she is called one of the Nereids; and instead of a white one"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall appear as another dark-footed water Ino.\"","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things the trailing-robed women lamented,"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing in rows upon the loud-crashing towers."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Bacchoi rattled, having cast away Enyo,","st":"40.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a word from throats of one tongue:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'We have won great glory; we have killed the leader of the Indians.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And laughing Dionysus leapt up with the joy of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, breathing again after the labor and the bloody contest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first he gave funeral rites to the rank of unburied corpses,","st":"40.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"building one vast tomb with a broad hollow"},{"sp":"N","t":"about an undivided hundred-foot pyre; and about the dead"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Mygdonian pipe of varied song crashed out a dirge,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phrygian flute-players wove a male melody"},{"sp":"N","t":"with mourning lips, and the Bacchants danced","st":"40.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Ganyctor sang delicately with a Euian voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the twin Berecyntian flutes beneath the mouth of Cleochus"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowed a dread Libyan wail, which once both"},{"sp":"N","t":"Stheno and Euryale with one many-throated voice"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieking wept over Medusa freshly cut down,","st":"40.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"as two hundred serpent heads cried out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from whose mourning locks the crooked hissing"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaimed a many-headed dirge for Medusa."},{"sp":"N","t":"And ceasing from labor, and cleansing his limbs with water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave a god-fearing lord to the war-released Indians,","st":"40.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"having chosen Modaeus; and over a common cup"},{"sp":"N","t":"they touched a single table with the feasting Bacchoi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking the yellow water from the wine-bearing river."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was an unspeakable dance; and many a Bassarid"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped, striking the floor with a goaded sandal,","st":"40.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Satyr, striking the ground with heavy-thundering sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raved aslant with a tumbler's throb of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning his forearm on the neck of a maddened Bacchant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the foot-soldiers of Bromius danced with their ox-hide shields,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, whirling the armed circles of a running dance,","st":"40.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"they imitated the rhythm of the shield-bearing Corybants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the host of horsemen stood in a helmet-glancing dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi for the all-subduing victory of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was any man soundless; and with a shout of one tongue"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Euian echo ran up to the seven-zoned vault.","st":"40.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the revel of the toil-releasing feast had passed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, taking all the plunder of victory after the war of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembered his own ancient fatherland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having loosed the seven-year foundations of the fighting."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the warriors plundered all the wealth of the foes;","st":"40.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one had Indian jasper, another the metals"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Phoebean hyacinth with its writing, and the green backs of emerald;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, beneath the watch-peaks of fair-based Imaus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"urged on the upright step of spear-won elephants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, beside the deep-caverned hill of Haemodus,","st":"40.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove a migrant chariot of Indian lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorying; and another, having fettered a knot about its neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hastened to drag a leopard to the Mygdonian shore;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Satyr was carried, reveling, lashing a spotted"},{"sp":"N","t":"tiger ahead of him with an unmixed-wine-loving leaf;","st":"40.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"another returned bringing to his Cybelian bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fragrant planting of sea-nourished reeds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a flashing stone, the prize of the Erythraean brine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many a captive dark-skinned bride was dragged by the hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"from her chamber with her freshly wedded consort,","st":"40.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"her fettered slave neck yoked beneath a harness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a god-driven Bacchant, lifting in her hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"a pouring of wealth-flowing riches, went to the peaks of Tmolus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi in revel for returning Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus divided the plunder of the battle among his army,","st":"40.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending the whole fellow-contending host back home"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the Indian war; and the peoples hurried away"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting marble, the eastern gifts of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dappled-shaped birds; and on the returning journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"all raved in Bacchic frenzy, crying Euoi in revel","st":"40.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"for unconquered Dionysus, leaving the memory"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the whole much-toiling war, scattered, running with a north"},{"sp":"N","t":"wind; and each, having the offerings of victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came home at last, turning back. But instead of his fatherland"},{"sp":"N","t":"Asterius alone then settled near the unwashed Bears","st":"40.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the stream of Phasis in an unwarmed land"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the Massagetan gulf, dwelling beneath the ill-snowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"ankles of starry Taurus, the father of his own begetter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the Cnossian city and his male-childed race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hating Pasiphae and his own father Minos,","st":"40.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he preferred Scythia to his own land. But Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his Satyrs alone and the Indian-slaying Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the Caucasian war of the Amazonian river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"set foot in Arabia a second time, where, staying often,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he taught the people of the Bacchus-less Arabs to lift","st":"40.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"mystic fennel-stalks; and of the plant-increasing thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crowned the Nysian mountains with a clustered branch."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the deep-shaded grove of the Arabian neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"he measured the Assyrian path, a wayfarer on foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and longed to see the land of the Tyrians, the fatherland of Cadmus;","st":"40.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there he bent his step, and gazing at unspeakable robes"},{"sp":"N","t":"he marveled at the two-colored cunning of Assyrian craft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beholding the silvery work of a Babylonian Arachne;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he gazed at garments drenched in the Tyrian shellfish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling the purple sparks of the sea,","st":"40.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"where a sea-working dog, feeding on the shores,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wondrous fish hidden within, with gleaming jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpled its snowy cheeks with the blood of the shellfish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening its lips with a liquid fire, by which alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bright robe of the sea-cloaked lords was reddened.","st":"40.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the city he rejoiced, which the Earth-shaker"},{"sp":"N","t":"has not girdled with a whole liquid belt of sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but it has got such an Olympian pattern as the moon weaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, near to fullness, she is wanting in a single horn."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as he gazed at the land in the middle, yoked to the brine,","st":"40.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"a double wonder took him, since Tyre lies in the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"apportioned to the land, and, joined to the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has made one girdle of three flanks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unshaken she was like a swimming girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she gave her head and breast and neck to the sea,","st":"40.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading her hands in the middle of a twin deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whitening her body with the neighboring sea-foam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she propped both her feet upon her mother earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Earth-shaker, holding the city in an unshaken bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a watery bridegroom, swims about her, as though joining","st":"40.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"the entwined neck of his bride with a seething forearm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bacchus still marveled at Tyre, in which alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"a herdsman near at hand kept company with a neighboring sailor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"piping beside the shore, and a goatherd with a fisherman"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing up his net, and beside answering oars","st":"40.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the waters the clod was scored by the plow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and near a sea-thicket shepherds as companions conversed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with woodcutters, and in one place there roared together &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"the surf of the sea, the bellowing of oxen, the whisper of leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cable, plant, voyage, grove, water, ships, merchantman, plow-handle,","st":"40.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"sheep, reed, sickle, skiffs, nets, sails, breastplate."},{"sp":"N","t":"And gazing at these things he broke into a much-wondering voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Whence have I seen an island on the mainland? If it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"never have I seen so great a beauty; for high-stretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"trees whisper beside the waves, and while a Nereid","st":"40.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"speaks in the sea a Hamadryad hears her nearby,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the Tyrian seas and the sea-neighboring fields"},{"sp":"N","t":"a delicate midday wind, blowing from Lebanon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pours a ship-saving breeze with fruit-bearing panting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling the countryman and drawing the sailor into his voyage,","st":"40.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, bringing an earthly sickle near a deep-sea trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the harvest Deo speaks here to the ruler of the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he drives his unwetted car over the deaf calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to steer an equal course upon emulous chariots,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bountiful one lashing the aloft backs of her serpents.","st":"40.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"O city of whom all speak, pattern of earth, image of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have a threefold baldric of the sea grown together with you.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he passed on, stretching his eye through the city;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as he gazed the stone-pointed streets"},{"sp":"N","t":"showed the sparkle of one metal after another;","st":"40.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he saw the house of his forefather Agenor, he beheld the courts"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the chamber of Cadmus, and he entered the unguarded"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden-chamber of Europa, the bride once snatched away,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the memory of his own horned Zeus; and he marveled more"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the first-born springs, where through an earthly hollow,","st":"40.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the water poured out, recovered again in a single hour,"},{"sp":"N","t":"much-nourishing water bubbled up with self-begotten streams;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he saw the generative flood of Abarbarea, he beheld the spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely Callirhoe, named for her, and he saw the bridal"},{"sp":"N","t":"water of Drosera herself as she belched it delicately.","st":"40.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he had beheld all with his delight-loving heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he reveled into the house of Astrochiton, and called upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lord of the stars, crying such a word with a mystic voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Astrochiton Heracles, lord of fire, leader of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Helios, long-shadowed shepherd of mortal life,","st":"40.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding the whole vault in spirals with your blazing disc,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling the twelve-month year, the son of time,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you lead circle after circle; and from your car"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeon flows, shaped in old age and in youth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"midwife of a wise travail, you bring to birth the thrice-coiled","st":"40.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"image of motherless Mene, when dewy Selene"},{"sp":"N","t":"milks the answering fire of your childbed beam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gathering her arched bull's horn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-shining eye of the upper air, you bring with your four-yoked car"},{"sp":"N","t":"winter after autumn, you lead summer, changing the spring.","st":"40.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"Night, pursued by your javelin-hurling torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"withdraws unfixed, when, drawing the silver yoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the upright horse's neck, seen at its tip, is lashed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as you shine, the dappled meadow, no longer shining"},{"sp":"N","t":"more brightly, is scored with fair-shining stars;","st":"40.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bathed in the streams of eastern Ocean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking off the generative moisture of your unwarmed hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you bring fruit-bearing rain, and upon Earth of fair childbirth"},{"sp":"N","t":"you belch the morning watering of an airy dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you make the travail of the corn-ears grow with your disc,","st":"40.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the bountiful shore through a life-bearing furrow."},{"sp":"N","t":"You are Belus on the Euphrates, called Libyan Ammon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you are Apis of the Nile, Arabian Cronus, Assyrian Zeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bringing fragrant wood with crook-clawed foot"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thousand-year wise bird upon your fragrant altar,","st":"40.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"the phoenix, bringing the end of life as a self-sown beginning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"is born as a recovered image of a time of like pattern,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosing his old age in fire he changes from fire into youth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether you are Sarapis, the cloudless Egyptian Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether Cronus, whether many-named Phaethon, or whether you are Mithras,","st":"40.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Helios of Babylon, in Hellas Delphic Apollo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether you are Marriage, whom Eros sowed in shadowy dreams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"accomplishing the beguiling longing of a mimic bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, from sleeping Zeus, as with the point of a knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"he scraped the liquid seed of a self-wedding furrow,","st":"40.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the cliffs were midwifed by heavenly drops;"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether you are pain-killing Paeon, whether you are the varied"},{"sp":"N","t":"Upper Air, and are called Astrochiton — for by night"},{"sp":"N","t":"your starry tunics light up the heaven —;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive my voice with gracious ears.\"","st":"40.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word Dionysus belched out. And suddenly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding an inspired shape within the god-receiving temple,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Astrochiton flashed; and from his fire-eyed face"},{"sp":"N","t":"his eyes shot forth a rosy sparkle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the gleaming god stretched out his palm to Lyaeus,","st":"40.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a varied garment, the pattern of the upper air, the image of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glittering with yellow cheeks and a starry beard;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gladdening him he soothed him with a friendly table."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he delighted his heart at an uncarved dinner,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching ambrosia and nectar; and it was no cause for blame","st":"40.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he drank sweet nectar after the immortal milk of Hera;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he questioned Astrochiton, pouring an inquiry-loving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Astrochiton, teach me, by the pattern of the land, by the image of the island,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what god founded the city, what heavenly hand inscribed it?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who lifted the crags and rooted them in the sea?","st":"40.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who wrought these cunning works? Whence did the springs get their names?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who mingled the island with the land, yoked to its mother the sea?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and Heracles soothed him with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bacchus, do you hear my tale; and I shall teach you all."},{"sp":"N","t":"Here dwelt men whom alone the like-sown Aeon","st":"40.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"once beheld as age-mates of the ever-flowing cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pure race of an unwedded earth, whose shape then"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unplowed, unsown silt brought forth of its own accord;"},{"sp":"N","t":"who towered an unshaken city of level floors"},{"sp":"N","t":"with earth-born craft upon rocky foundations.","st":"40.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, beside the fair-watered beds of the springs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the earth was lashed by the vapor of the fiery sun,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plucking the Lethaean wing of mind-delighting Sleep"},{"sp":"N","t":"they slept together, and, increasing a city-loving sting in my heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I hung a standing step above the head of the earth-born,","st":"40.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having the shadowy image of a mortal face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I belched an oracle from an inspired throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Scattering idle sleep, children of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"build me a strange car of the passable brine; and with sharp-cutting"},{"sp":"N","t":"axes cut me the ridge of the pine-wood;","st":"40.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"build me a wise work; and beneath thick ribs"},{"sp":"N","t":"bolting decks in a close-set array,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bind them gathered, unshaken, with a fitted bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a car of the sea, a first-sailing raft, which through the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"may heave you up; and from end to end let one long","st":"40.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"first-fixed timber receive the whole support;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bind the decks fitted to the ribs in a circle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the close pattern of a wooden wall; and let a stretched-high"},{"sp":"N","t":"timber stand upright in the middle, clamped with bonds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fasten a broad linen cloth to the middle timber,","st":"40.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"and plaited cables in turn, from whose bonds"},{"sp":"N","t":"belly out the cloth at full stretch for the airy wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pregnant from the wind, ship-saving; and fence the freshly fixed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gaping timbers with slender bolts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading them thickly upon the yoked walls","st":"40.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"with wicker mats, lest a thievish swell pour"},{"sp":"N","t":"within through the gaping trail of the hollow timber."},{"sp":"N","t":"And whirling on every side the tiller, the steersman"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the raft's journey, the much-turning driver"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the liquid path, wherever your mind draws you,","st":"40.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"score the backs of the sea with the wooden hollow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until you come to a fated place, where two"},{"sp":"N","t":"unfixed wandering rocks float in the brine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which Nature has named the Ambrosial, on which grows"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-rooted, yoked shoot of an age-mate olive,","st":"40.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the mid-navel of the water-traveling rock; and on its topmost"},{"sp":"N","t":"clusters you shall behold an eagle sitting"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a well-made bowl; and from the flaming tree"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-moving fire belches astonishing sparks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an unburning radiance feeds about the shoot of the olive;","st":"40.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a coiling serpent dances about the high-leaved plant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"increasing a wonder for both eyes and ears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the slanting dragon, creeping noiseless, does not cast"},{"sp":"N","t":"a threatening coil about the soaring eagle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, spitting out the death-bearing venom of its teeth,","st":"40.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"devours the bird with its jaws, nor does the eagle itself"},{"sp":"N","t":"snatch the many-coiled creeper by its spines"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its talons and cut the air aloft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor would it score it with a sharp-toothed beak;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor does the fire, carried upon the branches of the long-trunked","st":"40.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"plant, feed about the shoot of the unharmed olive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor does it wither the coil of serpent scales,"},{"sp":"N","t":"its pasture-mate near its road, nor does the leaping fire"},{"sp":"N","t":"touch the entwined wings of the bird itself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor does the unshaken cup, hanging aloft, fall","st":"40.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"as it slips from the clusters shaken by the winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but sends its dear radiance as a vapor into the middle of the plant."},{"sp":"N","t":"And hunting the wise bird that shares the hearth of the olive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the same age, the high-flying eagle, sacrifice it to the dark-haired god,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring gore as libation to the sea-wandering hills","st":"40.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to Zeus and the blessed ones; and the unfixed rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall wander water-borne no more, but, girdled of its own accord"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unmoving foundations, shall be joined to the unyoked rock."},{"sp":"N","t":"And fix a city lying upon both hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"on either side upon the foundation of both seas.'","st":"40.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a prophetic word I belched out; and waking"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth-born were shaken, and in the ears of each"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wondrous tale of the unwandering dreams boomed always."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to them in their distress I showed another portent"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the winged dreams, increasing their building-loving temper,","st":"40.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"since I was to be their city-holder; and peering above the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing an answering mimicry with a shape of like yoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the nautilus fish swam into a self-taught voyage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gazing at it then, like a ship of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having learned a well-made voyage without toil,","st":"40.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having fixed a raft like the fish of the deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they imitated an equal pattern of sailing upon the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a voyage; and with the equally weighted burden"},{"sp":"N","t":"of four stones they made their sailing balanced on the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and imitated the unshaken journey of cranes,","st":"40.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"which lift within their mouths a heavy stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the helper of their road, lest ever, as they fly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wind lead their light wings astray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until they beheld that place, where with tempests"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hills sailed into a self-directed voyage.","st":"40.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"And they halted their raft beside the sea-crowned island,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stepped upon the crags, where the plant of Athena was."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to them as they sought it, the air-roaming eagle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hearth-mate of the olive, stood willing for its doom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the earth-born, taking the fair-winged inspired prey,","st":"40.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"reining back its head drawn backward,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading out its bare, free throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carved the self-offering eagle with a knife"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Zeus and the ruler of the waters; and as the intelligent bird"},{"sp":"N","t":"was cut down by the iron, from its freshly slaughtered throat","st":"40.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"wondrous blood flowed, and rooted the sea-traveling"},{"sp":"N","t":"hills in the sea with divine drops"},{"sp":"N","t":"near Tyre beside the deep; and upon the unbreakable rocks"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth-born built their deep-bosomed nurse."},{"sp":"N","t":"To you, lord Dionysus, I have told the ground-reared,","st":"40.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-delivered Olympian blood of the Giants, so that you may learn"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Tyrian earth-born stock of your own forefathers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall tell of the springs; for they were once"},{"sp":"N","t":"first-born, prudent maidens, at whose girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"hot Eros was angered, and, drawing a lovely shaft,","st":"40.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"joined such a word to the marriage-averting Nymphs:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Naiad Abarbarea, lover of maidenhood, receive yourself"},{"sp":"N","t":"this shaft, which all nature has received; here I shall fix"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridal chamber of Callirhoe, and I shall sing the wedding of Drosera."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: I keep a liquid race, and from the streams","st":"40.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"I came self-perfected, and a spring was my nurse."},{"sp":"N","t":"Clymene was a Naiad and the offspring of Ocean;"},{"sp":"N","t":"yet she yielded to marriage, and she too was wedded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when she saw the dark-haired god, a better servant of Eros,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaken by a Cyprian sting; and first-born","st":"40.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ocean, commanding the rivers and all waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knows the love of Tethys and fair-watered weddings."},{"sp":"N","t":"Endure, you too, to bear the same as Tethys. And bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"her blood from so great a sea and not from a little spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Galatea longs for Polyphemus as he sings,","st":"40.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she of the deep has a land-dwelling husband, and from the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"charmed by his harp, she goes a migrant to the land."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the springs have learned my shaft; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"a watery longing; you have heard of the liquid love"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the desire-stricken spring, Syracusan Arethusa;","st":"40.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have learned of Alpheius, who beside a wet bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraces his accustomed Nymph with watery palms."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bearing the blood of a spring, why do you delight in the archer-goddess?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis did not grow from water, as Aphrodite did."},{"sp":"N","t":"You ought rather to do favor to Cypris, since she too","st":"40.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent her neck to Eros, even though she is the nurse of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell this to Callirhoe; do not hide it from Drosera either."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive the goads of longing, and I shall call you water-ranging"},{"sp":"N","t":"in your birth, and in love the sister of Aphrodite.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he spoke; and from his back-bent bow","st":"40.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sent three shafts, and beside a fair-watered bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"he joined the sons of the field in the love of the Naiads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sowed the god-born blood of the Tyrian race.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Heracles, the champion of the upper air, told to Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with mind-delighting conversation; and he delighted his heart in the tale,","st":"40.575"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave to Heracles a gold-shining, light-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing-bowl, which a heavenly art had wrought; and Heracles"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloaked Dionysus in a starry tunic;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving the star-clad god, the city-holder of Tyre,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus set foot in another, Assyrian, land.","st":"40.580"},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"The forty-first holds how, to the son of Myrrha, Aphrodite bore Amymone, another Cypris."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now, above the brows of beetling Lebanon,","st":"41.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"having fixed on the ground the splendid-fruited cluster of the harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made the wine-bearing hollows of the whole field drunk;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he saw the bridal house of the Paphian; and roofing"},{"sp":"N","t":"a deep-shaded grove with the freshly planted shoots of vines","st":"41.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave a gift of the vine to Adonis and Cytherea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a chorus of the Graces; and leaping upon the girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of vines of the plant-increasing thicket with a mounting throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the soaring ivy was girdled about a cypress."},{"sp":"N","t":"But beside the neighboring foot of law-tending Beroe","st":"41.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell, Muses of Lebanon, the hymn of Amymone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the wavy Ares of the Cronides of the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the clustered Enyo of well-hymned Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"There is a city Beroe, the keel of life, the harbor of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-fixed, fair-islanded, fair-verdant, where the ridge of the isthmus","st":"41.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"is narrow though long, where between the twin brine"},{"sp":"N","t":"its upright neck is lashed by waves on both sides;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but one part lies stretched beneath the deep-treed ridge"},{"sp":"N","t":"of blazing Eurus toward Assyrian Lebanon, where for the citizens"},{"sp":"N","t":"a life-saving breeze comes whistling upright,","st":"41.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the cypresses are shaken by fragrant winds, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the house of country folk, where often beside a thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"sickle-bearing Deo met Pan as he made music,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and some tiller of the soil, bending his neck over the plow-beam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the freshly scored ground with back-thrown seed,","st":"41.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the ankles of a pasturing wood next to a neighboring"},{"sp":"N","t":"shepherd, having yoked his bull, conversed, a bent plowman."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the city has other parts beside the seas, where it stretches"},{"sp":"N","t":"its breast to Poseidon, and a watery consort embraces"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tender neck of the girl with a drenched forearm,","st":"41.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending watery kisses to the lips of his bride;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and from his deep-sea hand the consort receives in her accustomed"},{"sp":"N","t":"bosom the bride-gifts of Poseidon, the brine-nourished flocks of the lake,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fishy, many-colored dinners of the table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping on the sea-table of Nereus,","st":"41.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the northern floor, where along the deep-waved shore"},{"sp":"N","t":"a northern channel is drawn with a long hollow."},{"sp":"N","t":"And about the midday neck of the mind-delighting land"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the southern ridge are sandy paths"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the Sidonian country, where the varied trees of the gardens","st":"41.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the grape-clusters grow their hair, and with long-shooted leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"a shady road is stretched for unwandering wayfarers."},{"sp":"N","t":"And slanting its stream the sea beats upon the shore"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the dark-faced west, where with clear-sounding foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as Zephyrus rides over the western haunts,","st":"41.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Libyan bend is fanned with a dewy whistling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where there is a flowery place, where beside the neighboring deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"the plantings bloom, and from the fair-leaved trees"},{"sp":"N","t":"the breathing wood makes melody with a booming panting;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an old shepherd, the pasture-mate of a fisherman, sang.","st":"41.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"Here dwelt men of one age with the morning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom self-begetting Nature plowed by some unwedded"},{"sp":"N","t":"ordinance apart from marriage, fatherless, unchildbedded, motherless,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, with the fourfold bond of mingled atoms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unsown silt, shaping a yoked, wise offspring","st":"41.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixed of water and of fiery vapor of air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ensouled a breathing race in pregnant clay,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to whom Nature granted a perfected form; for they did not have"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pattern of first-born Cecrops, who with the venom-shooting trail"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his foot was dragged along, scraping the earth with a snaky sole,","st":"41.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"a serpent below, and above from the loins to the head"},{"sp":"N","t":"appeared a strange-natured, half-finished, two-colored man;"},{"sp":"N","t":"they did not have the savage pattern of Erechtheus, whom Hephaestus"},{"sp":"N","t":"begot, wedding a bridal dew to the furrow of Earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, an image of the gods, an offspring from an earth-born root,","st":"41.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"a first-appearing golden corn-ear of men was midwifed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they settled the city of Beroe, a first-sown seat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which Cronus himself built, when at the nod of wise Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set a chilling dinner in his capacious throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having the stone as an Eileithyia with its heavy burden,","st":"41.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hurler-out of his squeezed, many-childed race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing the whole stream of a river like a cloud with gaping throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drank the childbirth-helping water with a seething breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosed the mass of his belly; and driving them out one upon another"},{"sp":"N","t":"his pregnant neck belched forth his twice-born sons,","st":"41.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his childbed throat as the ferry of the birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus was then a boy, still an infant; never yet"},{"sp":"N","t":"did the lightning blaze, splitting a hot cloud"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a dancing throb, and not in a Titan battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"were the helping thunderbolts of Zeus shot forth;","st":"41.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, with the bellowing whirl of clashing clouds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did a heavy-thundering rainy echo of thunder boom."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the city Beroe was earlier, which the grown-together"},{"sp":"N","t":"Aeon, first-appearing, beheld as the age-mate of the earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not then was mortal-delighting Tarsus, not then Thebe,","st":"41.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"not then was Sardis, where the rich silt of the Pactolian"},{"sp":"N","t":"bank sparkles, belching gold,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sardis, the age-mate of Helios; there was no race of men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not then was there any Achaean city, nor Arcadia"},{"sp":"N","t":"herself older than the moon; but she alone sprang up","st":"41.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"older than Phaethon, whence Selene had her light."},{"sp":"N","t":"And being before the whole earth, in her all-mothering bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"milking the newly shining radiance of the sun's gleam"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the late-accomplished light of unsleeping Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she first shook off the cone of dark mist,","st":"41.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dashed away the gloomy veil of chaos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and being before Cyprus and the Isthmian city of Corinth"},{"sp":"N","t":"she first received Cypris at a hospitable gateway"},{"sp":"N","t":"freshly born from the brine, when the pregnant water"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought forth Aphrodite of the deep from a heavenly furrow,","st":"41.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"where, having plowed the stream apart from marriage with male gore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-perfected offspring was shaped in daughter-bearing foam,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Nature was the midwife; and rising together with the goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dappled strap, wheeling a circle crown-wise about her loins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled the body of the queen with a self-made belt.","st":"41.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess, tracking a noiseless shore through the water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not run up to Paphos, not to Byblos, nor did she fit her foot"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the dry land of the Coliad beach, but with a swifter"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirl she ran past the very city of Cythera;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and rubbing her flesh with a weedy cluster","st":"41.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was the more purple; and rowing with her hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"she split the god-bearing water of the waveless sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swimming, and spreading her breast upon the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"she checked the silent brine as it was scored by her sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she lifted her body, and as the calm was split","st":"41.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"she pushed the water behind her with alternating feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she set foot on Beroe; and the dweller of Cyprus has lied"},{"sp":"N","t":"about the landing-steps of the goddess as she came from the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"She first received Cypris; and high above the neighboring harbor"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-grown meadows, belching the mosses of grass,","st":"41.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"bloomed this way and that, and in the much-sanded bay"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shores were reddened with rosy clusters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a foaming rock, pregnant with fragrant wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore a purple travail at a torrent-cut breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a white pouring murmured with a milky trail, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"41.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"a rain of dew shaded with the drops of the vat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirling aloft the vapor of self-poured perfume"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fragrant wind made drunk the paths of the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then furious Eros, the first-sown beginning of generation,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the life-bringing charioteer of the harmony of the cosmos,","st":"41.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"she brought to birth, freshly appearing, upon the brows of the neighboring harbor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the swift-footed child, shaking a male labor with his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"forestalled the childbirth-helping hour of a belly that needed no midwife,"},{"sp":"N","t":"battering the closed bosom of his unwedded mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hot before his birth; and with a tumbler's throb,","st":"41.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling his light wings, he opened the gates of the childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaping swiftly upon his mother's gleaming arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"restless Eros sprang up at her unbending breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched upon a child-tending bosom; and he had"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-taught longing for food; and biting the tip","st":"41.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the unmilked teat, squeezed with the mass of its generative drops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsated he drew off the whole milk of her swelling breasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"Root of life, Beroe, nurse of cities, boast of kings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"first-appearing, like-sown with Aeon, of one age with the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seat of Hermes, floor of Justice, city of ordinances,","st":"41.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"midday haunt of Gladness, house of the Paphian, home of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the delightful seat of Bacchus, the haunt of the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the offering of the Nereids, house of Zeus, court of Ares,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orchomenus of the Graces, star of the Lebanon field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of equal years with Tethys, running with Ocean,","st":"41.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"who begot Beroe in his many-springed bridal chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"consorting in the moist weddings of Tethys,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom they called Amymone, when her mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"bore her in the submerged bed of a watery love."},{"sp":"N","t":"But there is a younger tale, that Cytherea herself,","st":"41.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"the steersman of the human race, bore her all-white"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a mother to Assyrian Adonis;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having filled out the nine-circled course of Selene"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lightens her burden; and forestalling her with swift sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a Latin tablet, the herald of things to come,","st":"41.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hermes came as a midwife to the travail of Beroe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Themis was Eileithyia, and, unfolding the veil"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the crowded travail through her swollen bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lightened the sharp shaft of the ripening birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the ordinances of Solon; and pressed by her childbed,","st":"41.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning her heavy back upon the birth-releasing goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris was delivered, and upon an Attic book"},{"sp":"N","t":"she brought forth a wise child, as Laconian women"},{"sp":"N","t":"travail with their sons upon a well-rounded shield;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she spat out the freshly delivered birth from a female womb,","st":"41.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"having as a male midwife the judge, the son of Maia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she brought the babe into the light. And four winds"},{"sp":"N","t":"that ride over all cities bathed the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that from Beroe they might fill the whole earth with ordinances;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to her as she was born Ocean, still the first announcer","st":"41.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"of laws, gave a childbed stream for the loins of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring water girdled about with an ever-flowing baldric;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with aged hands Aeon her foster-brother lifted"},{"sp":"N","t":"the robes of Justice as swaddling-clothes to the flesh of the freshly born girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a prophet of things to come, because, changing the burden of old age,","st":"41.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a serpent shaking off the coil of its feeble scales,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would grow young again, bathed in the swells of ordinances;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the four-yoked Seasons beat out a concordant melody"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the wondrous daughter of Aphrodite as she was born."},{"sp":"N","t":"And learning of the birth-perfecting travail of the Paphian","st":"41.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the beasts raved in Bacchic frenzy; and a lion, playing delicately,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kissed the back of a bull with a gentle lip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a friendly bellowing with the tips of its mouth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a horse, striking the heavy-thundering ground with running hoof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rattled out, beating a birthday sound,","st":"41.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a dappled-backed leopard, leaping with the mounting throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a high-traveling foot, skipped about a hare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pouring the wail of a howl from a play-loving throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wolf embraced the flock with unscratching jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in the thickets a dancing hound, leaving the fawn-driving","st":"41.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"hunt, having another sweet sting, strove with a dancing boar"},{"sp":"N","t":"in an emulous dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a bear, straightening its feet and entwining itself about its neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraced a heifer in a harmless bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a calf, arching thickly the kiss-loving rim of its temple,","st":"41.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped up, licking the body of a lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a half-finished bellowing from its young jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a serpent touched the friendly tusks of an elephant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the oaks cried out; and with a calm face"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughter-loving Aphrodite sent forth her accustomed laughter,","st":"41.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the childbed play of the delighting beasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"She whirled the rejoicing circle of her gaze upon them all,"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon all together; only the delight of the boars"},{"sp":"N","t":"she did not wish to see, like a prophet, since in the image"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a boar's shape jagged-toothed Ares, mad with jealousy,","st":"41.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending death-bearing venom, was to weave doom for Adonis."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Beroe, still an infant, laughing, was received"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the knot of the hands of the nurse of the whole cosmos"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Astraea, the rearer of the golden race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reared her with a prudent breast as she prattled of laws;","st":"41.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gushing the streams of the ordinances with a maiden milk"},{"sp":"N","t":"she wetted the child's lips, and into the girl's mouth she squeezed"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made bubble up the cunning travail of the many-holed childbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the sweet-bearing Attic bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing speaking honeycombs in a wise cup;","st":"41.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if ever the girl was thirsty and asked for drink, she held out"},{"sp":"N","t":"the chattering water kept for Pythian Apollo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or the stream of Ilissus, which the Pierian breezes of Phoebus"},{"sp":"N","t":"stir upon the shore, breathing for the Attic Muse;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maidens, the dancers of Orchomenus,","st":"41.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaids of the Paphian, drew her delicate baths,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the horse-water of the prudent spring that is the care of the nine Muses."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bending a starry golden corn-ear about with a cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she set it like a necklace upon the girl's neck."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Beroe grew up running with the archer-goddess,","st":"41.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the nets of her hunter father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she had the whole kindred look of her mother the Paphian"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gleaming feet; and peering above the deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Thetis, skipping with a snowy dancing sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"saw another silver-footed Thetis; and in shame","st":"41.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"she hid herself, fearing the mouth of Cassiepeia again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And gazing at another unyoked Assyrian girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus was stirred again, and wished to change his shape;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now, having the burden of Eros in a bull-faced form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he would have been carried dipped at the surface, rowing his step through the water,","st":"41.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a woman undrenched upon his back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not the memory of the ox-horned wedding of the Sidonian"},{"sp":"N","t":"held him back, and had not the bridegroom of Europa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Bull of Olympus, bellowed a starry melody with a jealous throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest, making the image of an ox of like pattern through the upper air,","st":"41.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"he should set up a younger star of sea-traveling Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he left Beroe, owed to liquid weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as consort to his brother, guarding himself against a bridal"},{"sp":"N","t":"battle with the Earth-shaker over an earthly bride."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such was Beroe, the flower of the Graces; if ever the girl","st":"41.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"uttered a honey-flowing voice more delicious than a hive,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet-worded Persuasion stood unsated upon her lips"},{"sp":"N","t":"and goaded the prudent minds of uncharmed men;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her laughing eyes, the archers of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid the whole company of the Assyrian youth of her own age","st":"41.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"with brighter graces, as much as Selene at the full,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting cloudless beams, hides the stars the more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the lowest ankles of the girl white"},{"sp":"N","t":"tunics reddened with her purple limbs."},{"sp":"N","t":"This was never a cause for blame, even if she got so great a beauty","st":"41.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"beyond the youth of her own age, since about her face"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bright beauties of both her parents sparkled."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then Cypris, seeing her, pregnant with a prudent oracle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled her purpose more swiftly, turning it about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, riding her mind wandering over the whole earth,","st":"41.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the bright foundations of ancient cities,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because Mycenae, girdled with a walled crown"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the rules of the Cyclopes, bore the name of glancing-eyed Mycene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and because beside the southern Nile Thebe"},{"sp":"N","t":"was named for first-born Thebe;","st":"41.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she longed to mark out a city named for Beroe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the city-loving sting of answering Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And considering the rank of the evil-averting ordinances of Solon"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stretched a slanting eye toward broad-streeted Athens,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a jealousy of her judging sister; and with hurrying","st":"41.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"sandal she whistled through the airy arch"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the house of all-mother Harmonia, where the bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelt in a self-built house like the fourfold pattern"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the cosmos; and four doors of the sturdy hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unbreakable, were girdled by the four winds;","st":"41.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and handmaids guarded the house, the wheeling image of the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"this way and that; and as the doorways were divided,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the servant East ran about the gate of Eurus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and West, the rearer of Selene, the gateway of Zephyrus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Midday held the fiery southern bolt,","st":"41.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the servant North opened the gate of Boreas,"},{"sp":"N","t":"thick with clouds and sprinkled with hail."},{"sp":"N","t":"There the Grace, leaping forward, the fellow-traveler of the foam-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck the eastern doorway of Eurus; and within,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the saffron gateway of the East was beaten,","st":"41.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"the messenger Astynomeia ran up, and beholding Cypris"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing beside the gateway of the hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came with returning feet as forerunner to her queen."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, plying the much-wrought loom of Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was weaving a robe with the shuttle; and as the tunic was woven","st":"41.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sprinkled in the earth first at the navel, and about the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"she rounded heaven into a sphere, scored with the pattern of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fitted the gathered sea to the yoked earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she embroidered the rivers, and upon a human forehead"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bull-natured, horned, green image was shaped;","st":"41.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the last floor of the well-spun tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"she wheeled Ocean running round about the rim of the cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"And her handmaid came to her and near the female loom"},{"sp":"N","t":"announced that Aphrodite stood beside the doorway."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess, when she heard, casting away the threads of the tunic,","st":"41.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook the wondrous shuttle from her loom-toiling hands;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swiftly covering her body with a snowy robe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brighter, she sat above her accustomed golden seat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving Cytherea, and leaping up from her throne"},{"sp":"N","t":"she honored Aphrodite as she came, seen from afar.","st":"41.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"And long-robed Eurynome set the Paphian upon a throne"},{"sp":"N","t":"near her queen; and watching Cypris with a dismayed face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"by a downcast witnessing shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all-nourishing Harmonia soothed her with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Root of life, plant-sowing Cytherea, midwife of generation,","st":"41.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"hope of the whole cosmos, beneath the nod of your counsel"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwandering Fates spin their many-turning threads — &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesy to me as I ask, and as the nurse of life, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the rearer of the immortals, as of one age with the age-mate cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me: for which of the cities are the royal instruments of speech,","st":"41.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unshaken reins of toil-releasing ordinances, kept?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Because, shaken by the sting of a long-lasting longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the goad of Hera's wedlock with her brother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I yoked Zeus in marriage as he longed for a time"},{"sp":"N","t":"of three hundred years; and as a worthy favor for my works","st":"41.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"he nodded with prudent head the wage of his own chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that to one of the cities which I obtained he would grant"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ordinances of Justice; and I long to learn whether these gifts"},{"sp":"N","t":"are kept for the land of Cyprus or for Paphos or for Corinth"},{"sp":"N","t":"or for Sparta, whence Lycurgus came, or for the man-nourishing","st":"41.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"fatherland of our own girl Beroe."},{"sp":"N","t":"But care for justice, and grant harmony to the cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since you are life-saving Harmony; for she herself"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent me hurrying to you, the nurse of law-tending men,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the starry maiden; and still more lawful Hermes","st":"41.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"has released this prize, so that I alone may save"},{"sp":"N","t":"the men whom I sowed, when they are forced, by the ordinances of marriage.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So she spoke, and the goddess heartened her and answered with a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Be of good courage, do not fear, mother of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I have the oracles of the cosmos on seven tablets,","st":"41.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the tablets are named for the seven planets."},{"sp":"N","t":"The first is named for well-wheeling Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the second golden tablet is called that of Hermes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glittering, in which all the rites of the ordinances are wrought;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the third, rosy, bears your name; for it holds","st":"41.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"the pattern of your eastern star; and of the seven-pathed"},{"sp":"N","t":"planets the fourth, the mid-navel, is of Helios;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fifth, reddening, is called fiery Ares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sixth wanderer is called the Phaethon of Cronides;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seventh shows the name of high-traveling Cronus.","st":"41.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"In these old Ophion scored with reddening letters"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the varied fated oracles of the cosmos."},{"sp":"N","t":"But since you ask me about the straight-minded ordinances,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I keep these elder prizes for the elder of cities;"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether then Arcadia was earlier, or the city of Hera,","st":"41.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether Sardis was older, or whether"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tarsus itself of song was the first city, or some other,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not learned; but the Cronian tablet teaches all this,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which grew up earlier, which was of one age with Dawn.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke; and she led the way to the splendid oracles of the wall,","st":"41.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"until she saw the place where, about the fatherland of Beroe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the art of Ophion had written a late-accomplished oracle"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the Cronian tablet, scored in wine-dark ochre:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'First-appearing Beroe was of one age with the age-mate cosmos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"named for a later-born bride, whom migrant","st":"41.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"sons of the Ausonians, the consular lights of Rome,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall call Berytus, since she fell as the neighbor of Lebanon.' &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a god-speaking word she learned. But when the goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"had measured the wondrous beginning of the seventh tablet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she looked upon a second, where beside the neighboring wall","st":"41.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"varied cunning works of every art were scored"},{"sp":"N","t":"in prophetic words: that first of all Pan of the pastures"},{"sp":"N","t":"would devise the pipe, Heliconian Hermes the lyre,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate Hyagnis the double-voiced melody of the well-holed flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orpheus the god-speaking streams of a mystic song,","st":"41.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Linus the Phoebean fair speech, the wandering Arcadian"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twelve-month measures and the journey of Helios,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother of the years brought forth by his four-yoked car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wise Endymion, bending his shifting fingers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"would know the restless circles of returning Selene;","st":"41.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, mixing a threefold element, the yoked with the unyoked,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus would teach the rites of fair-tongued speech,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Solon the undefiled ordinances, and Cecrops the lawful"},{"sp":"N","t":"twin pair of an unloosable yoking by an Attic pine-torch."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Paphian, after all the many-turning cunning works of the Muse,","st":"41.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the thick works of wide-scattered cities;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and above the middle rim of the inscribed tablet of the cosmos"},{"sp":"N","t":"she found such a wise, many-lined word for the Greek Muse:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'When Augustus shall steer the scepter of the whole earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Ausonian Zeus shall grant to holy Rome","st":"41.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lordship, and shall give the reins of the ordinances to Beroe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when, armed upon shield-bearing ships,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shall lull the war of sea-fighting Cleopatra;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for before that, city-sacking recklessness shall never cease"},{"sp":"N","t":"to rout the city-saving peace, until Berytus,","st":"41.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"the nurse of a calm life, gives judgment"},{"sp":"N","t":"to land and sea alike, having towered the cities"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the unbending wall of the ordinances, one city, the cities of the cosmos.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess, when she had learned the whole oracle of Ophion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went back returning to her own house; and as her son sat","st":"41.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"she set her golden-wrought seat near him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taking him about the middle with a calm face"},{"sp":"N","t":"she embraced the boy in her outspread, rejoicing arm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the dear weight upon her knees; and both together"},{"sp":"N","t":"she kissed the boy's mouth and eyes; and touching","st":"41.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"his mind-charming bow and handling his quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though in distress, she broke into a guile-minded voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hope of all life, comfort of the foam-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiless Cronion has forced my children alone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for having filled out nine childbirth-helping circles of Selene,","st":"41.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing the sharp shaft of a painful birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I brought forth Harmonia, and she suffers varied pains"},{"sp":"N","t":"in grief; but Leto got a childbirth-helping daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis Eileithyia, the helper of women."},{"sp":"N","t":"Child, born of the same womb as Amymone, I need not teach you","st":"41.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"how I got my blood from the brine and the upper air; but I wished"},{"sp":"N","t":"to accomplish a worthy work, so that beside my mother the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"born from heaven, I might fix heaven upon the earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but stretching your bow at the beauty of your sister &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"charm the gods, and rather send an equal shaft under a single","st":"41.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"ordinance to Poseidon and to vine-clad Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to both the blessed ones; and I shall grant you a gift worthy"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your toils, a fitting wage for your archery;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall give you the golden bridal lyre, which Phoebus"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave to Harmonia beside her bridal chamber, and I shall put into your hands","st":"41.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"a memorial of the city that is to be, so that you may be"},{"sp":"N","t":"a lyre-striker as well as an archer, like Apollo.\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"I have woven the forty-second, where I sing shrill of the delightful love of Bacchus and the longing of the Earth-shaker."},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she persuaded him; and with aloft sandal","st":"42.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"hot Eros, untouchable, shaking a wind-borne foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the air high among the clouds with winged sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing his flaming bow. And hanging from his shoulder"},{"sp":"N","t":"his quiver too, full of gentle fire, was slung.","st":"42.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when through the cloudless upper air a sharp wayfarer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a star, stretches upright with an outstretched spark,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a portent either to an army at war or to some sailor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scores the back of the upper air with a trail of fire behind it;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so then furious Eros, carried with a sharp whistle,","st":"42.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a windy boom from his beating wings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whistled down from the air; and beside the Assyrian rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"he joined two fiery shafts upon a single string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing into a like longing, by love of the maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"two suitors of an emulous wedding,","st":"42.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"the clustered god and the charioteer of the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"Then the one, leaving the deep wave of the sea-neighboring harbor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the other, after the floor of Tyre, met in one place"},{"sp":"N","t":"within the heads of Lebanon. And from his grim car"},{"sp":"N","t":"Maron loosed a sweating leopard from its harness,","st":"42.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shook off the dust, and washed with the water of a spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling the hot, scored neck of the beasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"There Eros, coming untouchable, at the neighboring girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck both gods with a twin shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"maddening Dionysus to bring treasures to the bride,","st":"42.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gladness of life and the wine-dark cluster of the harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and goading into love the steersman of the trident"},{"sp":"N","t":"to bring a double bride-gift of love to the sea-neighboring girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sea-fighting wet Ares and the dappled dinners of the table."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he burned Bacchus the more, since wine rouses the mind","st":"42.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward longing, and far more the young with a witless goad,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding charmed, unbridled youth obedient;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros shot Bacchus, fixing the whole shaft in his heart;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he burned as much as he charmed, dripping the honey of persuasion."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he goaded them both; and through the airy path,","st":"42.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeling his step to run with the dappled winds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bastard bird was lifted on a swimming sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a mockery-loving word: \"If Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"routs men with wine, I rout Bacchus with fire.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god, stretching his eye toward her,","st":"42.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the delicate body of the fair-tressed bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a wonder that channeled him toward longing; and as they began,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the eye became the forerunner, the ferry of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus wandered within the mind-delighting wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching a secret, guarded eye toward Beroe,","st":"42.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"and little by little went behind her into the girl's path;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was there any surfeit for him as he gazed; for as much"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he saw the maiden standing, so much the more he wished to look."},{"sp":"N","t":"And, reminding him of the love of Clymene, he entreated"},{"sp":"N","t":"Helios, the champion of the stars, upon his back-bent car,","st":"42.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping his standing horse with an airy bridle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lengthen the sweet daylight, so that he might come slowly to his setting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"increasing the re-grown day with a sparing whip."},{"sp":"N","t":"And, setting step upon step in measure in Beroe's tracks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though not knowing, he ran round her; and from Lebanon","st":"42.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Earth-shaker withdrew, stealing the track of a shrinking foot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a slow-obedient sole that turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had an unfixed mind like the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seething with the waves of a many-voiced care."},{"sp":"N","t":"And unsated within the sweet Lebanon wood","st":"42.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus was left alone beside the lonely bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus was left alone. Tell me, mountain Nymphs."},{"sp":"N","t":"What else did he wish more dearly than to see the girl's"},{"sp":"N","t":"flesh alone, free of the Earth-shaker unhappy in love?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus, holding a guileful, unlaughing look,","st":"42.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"questioned the maiden about her father Adonis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a friend, as a mountain-running fellow-hunter; and as she stood"},{"sp":"N","t":"he brought his unhappy-loving hand near her breast, and as though unwilling"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressed the edge of her girdle; and touching her breasts"},{"sp":"N","t":"the right hand of woman-mad Dionysus grew numb.","st":"42.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"And creeping secretly he kissed with numberless kisses"},{"sp":"N","t":"the place where she set her foot, and the dust she trod,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden, lighting it up with her rosy sandal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Bacchus gazed at her sweet neck, and the ankles of the girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she went, and the beauty which nature gave the bride,","st":"42.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"the beauty which nature found; nor did Beroe, anointing herself"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a yellow adornment, redden the rosy circles"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her face, making her cheeks lie with a bastard sparkle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor, by the bright witness of the answering bronze of her skin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did she laugh into the breathless shape of a mimic gaze","st":"42.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"judging her own beauty, nor, by a cunning ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making the tips of her hair even again and again beside her brows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did she set in place the wandering cluster of her straying locks."},{"sp":"N","t":"But the splendors of an uncared-for face rout a woman-mad man"},{"sp":"N","t":"far more with a sharp goad,","st":"42.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unkempt locks of an unadorned head"},{"sp":"N","t":"are the more delicate, when, unplaited and wandering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they go in rows, hanging beside a snowy face."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, thirsty, she went after a neighboring spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed by the vapor of the fiery heavenly Dog,","st":"42.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"with parched lips; and with her head drawn down"},{"sp":"N","t":"she bent, arched, and again and again the girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew her father's water into her mouth with hollowed hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until, sated, she left the streams; and as she withdrew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, bending his knee to the lovely spring,","st":"42.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"hollowing his palms, imitated the lovely girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drinking water sweeter than self-poured nectar."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beholding him shaken by the sting of longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unsandaled, deep-bosomed spring Nymph cried:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"In vain, Dionysus, you have drunk cold water; for the whole","st":"42.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"stream of Ocean cannot quench the thirst of love."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ask your father, that, having crossed so great a swell,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom of Europa did not quench his lovely fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but labored still more in the waters; and you have as witness"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering Alpheius, the wet-traveling servant of Eros,","st":"42.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, dragging his waters through water with so many surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not flee a hot love, even though he was a liquid wayfarer.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking, the unveiled Naiad entered the spring water"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her own age, laughing at Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, grudging Poseidon the ruler of the waters,","st":"42.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"had fear and jealousy, since the maiden drank water"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of wine, and he broke into a voice to the deaf air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though he had the girl listening and obedient:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maiden, receive nectar; leave the maiden-loving water;"},{"sp":"N","t":"flee the drink of the spring, lest the watery dark-haired god","st":"42.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"steal your maidenhood in the waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he is guileful and woman-mad; and you know"},{"sp":"N","t":"the love of Thessalian Tyro and the wet-traveling wedding;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you too guard yourself against a guileful stream, lest a lying god"},{"sp":"N","t":"loose your girdle, as the marriage-thief Enipeus did.","st":"42.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that I too had become a stream, like the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, resounding, had embraced beside a desire-striking spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"my thirsty, unguarded Lebanon Tyro.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"The god spoke; and changing the shape of his limbs about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the maiden was, Euius entered the thick-shaded wood","st":"42.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like a hunter; and strange-natured, unknown,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he kept company with the soft-haired unyoked girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a youth, and about his face he molded an unbending,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying imitation of a prudent modesty;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he gazed at the top of a lonely cliff,","st":"42.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"now toward the deep-shaded ridge of the long-shooted wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carrying a longing eye toward a pine, at another time"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching a fir or an elm; and with his face on guard"},{"sp":"N","t":"he looked at the girl beside him with secret eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she turn aside and shun him; for when a youth","st":"42.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazes at beauty, the eyes of a girl of his own age"},{"sp":"N","t":"are a little comfort of the Cyprian Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he came near Beroe and wished to speak a word,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but was fettered with fear. O Euian, where are your"},{"sp":"N","t":"man-slaying thyrsi? Where the dread horns? Where in your hair","st":"42.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grey-green snaky bonds of ground-reared serpents?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where the heavy-roaring bellowing of your mouth? Ah, great wonder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus trembled at a maiden, at whom the tribes of Giants trembled;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fear of the Loves conquered the destroyer of the earth-born;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he mowed down the race of so many war-mad Indians,","st":"42.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"and feared one lovely, feeble girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"feared a soft-skinned woman; and among the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a beast-taming fennel-stalk he soothed the dreadful"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowing of lions, and trembled at a female threat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and much-dreaded, a wandering word beneath his mouth","st":"42.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched to the very tip of his tongue, near his lip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting from his mind and creeping back again to his mind;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, holding a bittersweet fear in a shamefaced silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drew back his voice as it hurried into the light."},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely, having beaten down the late-speaking bond beneath his mouth,","st":"42.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"he unclamped the work-delaying silence of shame,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and questioned Beroe, pouring out a lying voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Artemis, where are your bows? Who has snatched your quiver?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where have you left the tunic you wear down to the knee?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where are those sandals of yours, swifter than a circling breeze?","st":"42.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where is the chorus of your handmaids? Where the nets? Where the swift hounds?"},{"sp":"N","t":"You are not making ready a fawn-driving course; for you do not wish"},{"sp":"N","t":"to hunt, where Cypris sleeps near Adonis.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, holding a deceiving wonder; and in her heart"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden smiled; and with a purpose untried in evil","st":"42.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lifted a proud neck, glorying in the grace of her youth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, though a woman, she seemed a goddess in form;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did she know the guile of mind-wandering Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bacchus grieved the more, since the girl did not learn his longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"his heavy-toiling sting; for when a girl knows —","st":"42.170","hvShift5":[[0,0],[32,33],[36,38],[40,48],[44,51]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[33,28]]},{"sp":"N","t":"but she had a childish temper, and he wished that she might learn it —","hvShift5":[[2,39],[5,47],[13,50],[15,54],[24,59]],"hvShift6":[[0,8],[6,11]]},{"sp":"N","t":"a late hope of the Loves is still left to a youth,","hvShift5":[[2,4],[6,25],[9,37],[14,44]],"hvShift6":[[0,0],[5,0],[33,34]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of a love to come; and men long","hvShift5":[[2,0],[4,2],[12,5],[21,6],[23,8],[27,16],[28,20],[29,22],[32,23],[37,28],[39,29]]},{"sp":"N","t":"upon a fruitless purpose when women do not know.","hvShift5":[[1,3],[3,4],[4,11],[5,26],[7,29],[9,31],[12,35],[16,38],[19,41],[22,42],[27,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, day after day within the pine-wood,","st":"42.175","hvShift5":[[1,21],[2,36],[4,38],[12,41],[19,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"creeping at evening, at midday, at morning, at nightfall,","hvShift5":[[1,10],[4,12],[22,14],[24,19],[28,26],[35,27],[42,28],[44,31],[46,33],[47,39],[48,54]]},{"sp":"N","t":"stayed beside the maiden, and wished to stay still longer;","hvShift5":[[3,13],[40,17],[42,22],[46,38],[48,39],[53,41],[54,44],[56,48],[57,55]]},{"sp":"N","t":"for there is surfeit of all things among men, of sweet sleep","hvShift5":[[3,20],[8,24],[17,30],[20,34],[22,37],[25,44],[30,48],[31,50],[33,51],[35,54],[43,57]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and of fair-sounding song, and when a man bends","hvShift5":[[8,0],[11,17],[16,22],[22,26],[26,30],[32,32],[34,35],[38,36],[44,37],[52,44]]},{"sp":"N","t":"into a dancing course; but for the woman-mad man alone","st":"42.180","hvShift5":[[0,2],[10,3],[19,6],[25,9],[27,11],[30,19],[31,21],[32,30],[46,36],[48,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"there is no surfeit of longings; the book of Homer has lied.","hvShift5":[[1,9],[3,22],[7,24],[9,27],[13,32],[16,38],[21,53],[27,57]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring, Dionysus roared beneath his breath in silence,","hvShift5":[[2,8],[31,9],[34,13],[36,15],[37,35],[39,49],[43,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by a divine whip, digesting within him","hvShift5":[[0,1],[2,17],[4,20],[6,25],[8,28],[20,30],[31,31],[46,37],[48,41]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the hidden wound of the unsleeping Loves beneath his heart."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when a bull, untouchable, traveling within a flat place,","st":"42.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the accustomed swarm of mountain-ranging bulls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"goaded from the herd — one that beside a fair-leaved thicket,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[21,20],[23,28],[27,31]]},{"sp":"N","t":"all unforeseen (its body struck by but a little sting),","hvShift6":[[0,0],[2,3],[22,16],[41,39],[55,54]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the ox-striking gadfly scored with its sharp goad;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[15,22],[29,38]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and so great a beast was dashed about, and rearing upright","st":"42.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"above its back, reining it back, it drew its tail again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arched, rubbing the ridge of the crags, and slanted"},{"sp":"N","t":"its sharp horn in answer, striking the unwounded air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so a little Eros goaded Dionysus too, whom victory"},{"sp":"N","t":"had often crowned, striking him with an all-charming goad.","st":"42.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"And at length, seeking a sweet drug for love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to shaggy-chested Pan, in a speech pregnant with the Paphian,","hvShift6":[[0,50],[7,54],[12,56],[30,57],[43,60]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he revealed his sleepless Cyprian necessity,","hvShift6":[[0,7],[7,9],[18,16],[19,18],[20,21],[51,22]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and asked for counsel, the defender against the Loves.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And hearing the fire-breathing labors of Bacchus","st":"42.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"horned Pan laughed, and was broken in his purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitying one unhappy in love, being himself unhappy in love; and he spoke a counsel"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Cypris; and he had a little comfort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing another burned by the spark of one quiver:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Suffering the same, dear Bacchus, I have pitied your cares;","st":"42.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whence has bold Eros conquered you too? If it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros has emptied his quiver into me and Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But I shall tell you the many-turning character of guileful longing."},{"sp":"N","t":"Every woman longs more than a man, and in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"hides the goad of Eros, though she too is love-maddened,","st":"42.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and labors far more, since the sparks of the Loves"},{"sp":"N","t":"are hotter when women hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shaft of the Loves fixed deep within their hearts."},{"sp":"N","t":"For when they tell one another the necessity of their longings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they steal away their Cyprian cares with toil-releasing","st":"42.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"whispers. But do you, Bacchus, wearing a deceiving blush"},{"sp":"N","t":"of mimic shame as the channel of your Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though holding a prudent, unlaughing gaze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stand near Beroe as though unwilling; and shaking her nets"},{"sp":"N","t":"gaze at the rosy-faced girl with a guileful wonder,","st":"42.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"praising her beauty, that Hera did not obtain so great a one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, striking your brow with an unsparing open hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"show your lying astonishment with an intelligent silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and call the Graces her inferiors, and fasten blame"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the shape of both Artemis and Athena,","st":"42.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"and declare Beroe brighter than Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the girl, hearing your lying reproach,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stands the longer, delighting in the praise; for she does not wish"},{"sp":"N","t":"for all the golden wealth so much as to hear"},{"sp":"N","t":"about her rosy shape, that her beauty surpassed the youth of her own age.","st":"42.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"And charm the maiden into love with an intelligent silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending facing nods with moving eyelids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but fear holds you near the prudent girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me, what will one maiden do to you? She brandishes no spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stretches no shaft with a rosy palm; the girl's spears","st":"42.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"are her eyes, the archers of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maiden's missiles are her rosy cheeks."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as bride-gifts of your longing, treasures for your bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not shake Indian stone or pearls in your hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as is lawful for a woman-mad man; for against the Paphian","st":"42.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"you keep your own shape, which is enough, and women long"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a beauty good to touch and not for gold."},{"sp":"N","t":"I need no other witness; for what gifts"},{"sp":"N","t":"did Selene receive from soft-haired Endymion?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What bride-gift of the Loves did Adonis show to Cypris?","st":"42.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"Orion gave no silver to the morning-born;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cephalus gave no lovely wealth; but alone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"being lame, Hephaestus, for the sake of an uncharmed shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"granted varied gifts, and did not persuade Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"his childbed axe did not help him; but longing for the goddess","st":"42.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"he missed her. But I shall teach you another charm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"better, if you wish, for a yoking wedlock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"sing shrill with your hand upon the lyre, the offering of your Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the delicate, wine-loving statue of Cypris; and with both"},{"sp":"N","t":"plectrum and lips pour out a twofold echo.","st":"42.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sing first of Daphne and the course of unsteady Echo"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the late-speaking din of the never-silent goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because they hated the gods who longed for them; and sing too"},{"sp":"N","t":"of bed-fleeing Pitys herself, running with the mountain breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning the unwedded weddings of Pan;","st":"42.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"sing the earth-born doom of her perishing; blame the earth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And perhaps she will weep for the pains of the mourning bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pity her doom; and do you delight your heart in silence,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the honey-sweet tears of the girl as she mourns;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was laughter ever such, since with a wine-dark shape","st":"42.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"women are lovelier when they groan."},{"sp":"N","t":"Sing of Selene love-maddened for Endymion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sing of the marriage of graceful Adonis, tell also"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Aphrodite herself, squalid, unsandaled, wandering,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tracking her mountain-running bridegroom; and she will not flee you","st":"42.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she hears the honey-minded ordinance of her father's Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"All this I declare to you, Bacchus unhappy in love;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but do you too teach me a charm for my Echo.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he sent away the rejoicing son of Thyone."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, in her childish ways, the girl asked","st":"42.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Zeus who was present, who he was and whose son;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scarcely he found a pretext beside the doorway of Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine orchard and the rich cornfields of the earth"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dewy meadow and the dappled trees, gazing"},{"sp":"N","t":"with cunning ways; and, like a farming man,","st":"42.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spoke some word about marriage with an unmarked voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I am a farmer of your Lebanon; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I water your land, I make your fruit grow."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know the course of the four Seasons; and watching"},{"sp":"N","t":"the autumn turning-post as it stands, I shall cry this:","st":"42.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The life-giving Scorpion rises, and is the herald"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a fair-fruited furrow; let us yoke the oxen to the plow.","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"The Pleiades are setting; when shall we sow the fields?","hvShift6":[[0,0],[13,17],[16,24]]},{"sp":"N","t":"The furrows travail, when the dew falls upon the ground","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,1]]},{"sp":"N","t":"parched by Phaethon.' And near the Arcadian Wagon,","st":"42.290","hvShift6":[[0,0],[20,21]]},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing Arcturus when winter has rained, I shall say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The thirsty earth is wedded at last to the rain of Zeus.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"When spring rises I shall cry to you at morning:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Your flowers bloom; when shall I pluck the lilies and the roses?'","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the grape when summer is present I shall say:","st":"42.295","hvShift5":[[1,5],[7,10],[12,22],[16,33],[17,41],[20,43],[21,47],[23,52],[52,54]]},{"sp":"N","t":"'The young vine ripens, without share of the sickle;","hvShift5":[[0,27],[3,40],[11,47],[31,49]]},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden, your kinsman has come; when shall we gather the harvest?","hvShift5":[[1,5],[3,7],[7,14],[10,20],[12,21],[13,28],[20,30],[47,37],[52,61]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Your corn-ear has grown and needs the reaping;","hvShift5":[[6,1],[8,8],[9,18],[10,23],[13,28],[19,30],[21,32],[40,33],[46,42],[51,45]]},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall reap the corn-bearing cornfield, and instead of to Deo","hvShift5":[[35,1],[43,9],[46,10],[51,12],[57,24],[60,36],[61,47]]},{"sp":"N","t":"I would make first-fruits to your mother the Cyprus-born.","st":"42.300","hvShift5":[[1,30],[6,35],[7,39],[33,40],[38,48],[43,55]]},{"sp":"N","t":"See how the hyacinth has run up to the neighboring myrtle,","hvShift5":[[1,3],[3,4],[12,7],[17,15],[18,33],[20,39],[22,44],[25,46],[32,53],[36,56],[39,57]]},{"sp":"N","t":"how the narcissus laughs as it leaps upon the anemone.'","hvShift5":[[2,2],[40,3],[48,10],[49,15],[53,39],[55,40],[56,53]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me as an earth-toiling servant of your threshing-floor;"},{"sp":"N","t":"take me as the plant-tender of your foam-born mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may fix a life-bringing plant, and may know","st":"42.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"the freshly blooming unripe grape of the vines, handling it with my hands."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence apples ripen; I know how to plant"},{"sp":"N","t":"the long-leaved elm propped against a cypress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I mix the male palm, rejoicing, with the female,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I make the saffron grow fair beside the smilax, if you wish.","st":"42.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bring me no gold for the sake of my tending; I have no need of wealth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have two apples for my wage, one cluster of a single harvest.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things he said in vain, and the girl did not answer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not understanding the rank of words of woman-mad Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Eiraphiotes devised another guile upon guile,","st":"42.315","hvShift6":[[0,0],[4,9],[5,13],[6,15]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and received from the hand of Beroe the nets of the hunt"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though marveling at their cunning, and shaking them thickly"},{"sp":"N","t":"he whirled them about for a time, and asked the girl again and again:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"What god made these gear, what heavenly art wrought them?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who made them? For I have an unbelieving mind, that","st":"42.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealousy-mad Hephaestus would make hunting gear for Adonis.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, leading astray the mind of the uncharmed girl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, above the spread leaves of an anemone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he slept a sweet sleep; and the girl came to him in a dream"},{"sp":"N","t":"covered in a bridal garment. For a man beholds by night","st":"42.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"the answering work which he accomplishes by day;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a herdsman sleeping drives his horned oxen to pasture;"},{"sp":"N","t":"a vision of dream shows nets to a hunter;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and farmers, sleeping, plow their fields"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sow a corn-bearing furrow; and a man at midday,","st":"42.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"held fast by a parching, blazing thirst,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a deceiving sleep drives toward a stream, toward a channel."},{"sp":"N","t":"So Dionysus too, holding the images of his toils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"escorted his winged mind with a mimic dream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and kept company with a shadowy wedding. And waking","st":"42.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not find the maiden, and wished to sleep again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he brought away an empty grace of a short-lived bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sleeping among the petals of a swift-perishing anemone."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he blamed the pouring of the voiceless petals; and in grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"he entreated Sleep together with Eros and the evening Aphrodite","st":"42.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"to see the same vision of dream again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longing for the deceiving phantom of a marriage. And near a myrtle"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus slept again and again, and did not obtain a bridal sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he had a sweet labor, and limb-loosing Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"himself had his limbs loosed by a desire-stricken care.","st":"42.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"And as the companion of Beroe's father, the son of Myrrha,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he showed his hunting; and with a javelin-hurling thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he put on the spotted hides of freshly slaughtered fawns,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching Beroe secretly; and as he stood"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden, guarding herself against the restless eye of Dionysus,","st":"42.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid her flashing cheek with her robe."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she burned Bacchus the more, because the servants of the Loves"},{"sp":"N","t":"gaze still more at women who are ashamed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and long the more for a face that is covered."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, beholding the unyoked girl of Adonis alone,","st":"42.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came near, and changed his shape from a human form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stood before the girl as a god;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he told her his race and his name, and the slaughter of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the vine dance, and the pouring of sweet-drinking wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he had found it for men; and with a tender-loving purpose,","st":"42.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing boldness with a shamelessness alien to modesty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spoke such a varied-worded voice, fawning:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maiden, for your love I dwell in heaven no longer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the caves of your fathers are better than Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I love your fatherland more than the upper air; I do not long","st":"42.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the scepters of Zeus my father so much as for the wedding of Beroe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your beauty is above ambrosia; and your tunics"},{"sp":"N","t":"breathe of fragrant airy nectar."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, I hold wonder when I hear that Cypris is your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the cestus left you uncharmed; and how did you alone","st":"42.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"have Eros as kinsman and not learn the sting of the Loves?"},{"sp":"N","t":"The grey-eyed one did not bear you, nor Artemis. But you, girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing the blood of Cypris, why do you flee the rites of Cypris?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: the grey-eyed one is untried in weddings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athena grew up apart from marriage and knows no marriage.","st":"42.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not shame your mother's race; and if you truly"},{"sp":"N","t":"bear the blood of graceful Assyrian Adonis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be taught the delicate ordinances of your marriage-perfecting father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and obey the cestus, the girdle of your age-mate the Paphian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beware the hard-to-meet wrath of the bridal Loves;","st":"42.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Loves are pitiless, when they demand of women the debt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vengeance for a love unaccomplished;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you know how, dishonoring fiery Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden-loving Syrinx paid the wage of her pride,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[4,0],[23,20]]},{"sp":"N","t":"because, becoming a plant in a bastard reedy shape,","st":"42.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fled the love of Pan, and still sings the longings of Pan;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the daughter of Ladon, the river of song,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hating the works of marriage, made her body a tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whistling with breath, and, fleeing the bed of Phoebus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with an oracular cluster she crowned the hair of Phoebus.","st":"42.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"And do you too guard against a frightful anger, lest hot,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy-wrathful Eros harry you; and sparing nothing of your girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"tend Bacchus in a double part, as attendant and as consort;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifting the nets of your father Adonis myself"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would spread the bed of my sister Aphrodite.","st":"42.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"What worthy gifts will the Earth-shaker bring you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Shall salt water be spoken of as your bride-gift of marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and will he spread the skins of seals breathing a foul"},{"sp":"N","t":"smell of the sea, the Poseidonian robes of the deep?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not receive the skins of seals; but for your chamber","st":"42.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall grant you Bacchants as handmaids, Satyrs as servants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive from me the bride-gifts of marriage and the vine harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you wish for the furious spear of Adonis, like a girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have my thyrsus as a lance; leave the point of the trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"Flee, dear one, the evil sound of the never-silent sea,","st":"42.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"flee the Poseidonian sting of hard-to-meet Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"The dark-haired god lay beside another Amymone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the woman after that bed became a spring of the same name;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he slept beside Scylla and made her a rock of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he pursued Asterie, and she became a lonely island;","st":"42.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he rooted the maiden Euboea in the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"This one woos Amymone, so that he may make her too"},{"sp":"N","t":"stone after the bed; this one will grant"},{"sp":"N","t":"as bride-gift of his chamber a little stream or the moss of the brine"},{"sp":"N","t":"or some deep-sea shell. But I stand in distress for you,","st":"42.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"for your shape's sake, wondering what gifts, what gifts to bring you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the child of golden Aphrodite has no need of gold."},{"sp":"N","t":"But I shall bring you many treasures from Alybe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the silver-armed one refuses silver. To you I shall bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"the glittering gifts of light-bearing Eridanus;","st":"42.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"but your shape shames all the wealth of the Heliades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"white turning red, and, a match for the beams of Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the neck of Beroe sparkles like amber &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and like a flashing stone; the look of your flesh convicts"},{"sp":"N","t":"precious marbles; let me not bring you a lychnis-stone","st":"42.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a blazing lamp — your eyes send the radiance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not bring you roses as gifts, leaping up"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the cup of a rosy cluster — your cheeks are rose-faced.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he spoke; and the girl, propping both hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"within her ears, fenced her twin hearing,","st":"42.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she hear another word again that was the care of Eros,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hating the works of marriage; and for desire-stricken Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she mixed labor with labor. What is more cruel than the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"than when, with the raving goad of a heart-devouring longing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"women shun the men who long for them","st":"42.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bring a greater sting by being prudent? And a love"},{"sp":"N","t":"hidden within is double, when a maiden flees a man."},{"sp":"N","t":"So he was lashed by the goading cestus of longing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stayed away from the maiden; and toward the ungirdled girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"he escorted his wandering mind as it ran hunting,","st":"42.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a bittersweet goad. And darting up from the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking wet tracks through a thirsty mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Poseidon sought the maiden as a migrant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the dry land with a watery sole;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to him as he still hastened beside the well-pastured slope of the wood","st":"42.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"the topmost heads of the mountain were shaken by the throb of his feet; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gazed at Beroe, and from foot to head"},{"sp":"N","t":"he measured the inspired youth of the standing girl;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sharply, through her thin garment as through a mirror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with unwandering eyes he inferred the pattern of the girl,","st":"42.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though gazing sidelong at her bared, gleaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"breasts, and he blamed the jealous girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"with its many-plaited bonds that hid her bosom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling in spirals the love-maddened eye of his face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing unsated at her whole body; and gadfly-mad","st":"42.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Earth-shaker, the ruler of the brine, entreated"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cytherea of the sea in his labor, and beside a country flock"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothed the maiden as she stood with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"One woman convicts all Hellas of fair women;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Paphos is sung no more, nor Lesbos, no longer is the name","st":"42.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Cyprus blessed in childbirth spoken; no longer shall I sing"},{"sp":"N","t":"Naxos of song, fair in maidens; but even Lacedaemon"},{"sp":"N","t":"has been conquered in childbirth and in travail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"not Paphos, not Lesbos — but the east, the nurse of Amymone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has plundered the whole glory of Orchomenus,","st":"42.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"tending one single Grace; for younger than the three"},{"sp":"N","t":"Graces, Beroe grew up a fourth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Maiden, leave the land, as is right; your mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not grow from the earth — Aphrodite is the daughter of the brine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have my endless deep as bride-gift, greater than the earth;","st":"42.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten to contend with the wife of Zeus, so that someone may say"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the consort of Cronides and the bedfellow of the Earth-shaker"},{"sp":"N","t":"rule everywhere, since Hera holds the scepter"},{"sp":"N","t":"of snowy Olympus, and Beroe has got the power of the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not put mad-eyed Bassarids into your hands,","st":"42.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall grant no skipping Satyr and no Silenus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but as chamber-servant of your marriage-perfecting bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall make Proteus and Glaucus your attendants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive Nereus too, and, if you wish, Melicertes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall call resounding Ocean your servant,","st":"42.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"broad, girdled about the rim of the ever-flowing cosmos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall grant you all the rivers as attendants for a bride-gift."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you delight in handmaids too, I shall bring you"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughters of Nereus; and let your chamber-servant be,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though she refuse, your Ino, the nurse of Dionysus.\"","st":"42.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke; and leaving the angry, disobedient girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"he said a word to the air, pouring a windy voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Blessed son of Myrrha, having got a race blessed in children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you alone hold a twofold honor; you alone are called"},{"sp":"N","t":"both the father of Beroe and the bridegroom of the foam-born.\"","st":"42.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"So the Earth-shaker was lashed by the goading cestus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stretched out many gifts to Adonis and Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-gift of his love for the girl. And struck by the same"},{"sp":"N","t":"burning shaft, Dionysus, bringing wealth, as much as beside"},{"sp":"N","t":"neighboring Ganges the gold-shining travails of the metals midwifed,","st":"42.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"entreated Aphrodite of the sea much and in vain."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Paphian was shaken, and feared both suitors"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her much-wooed girl; and seeing the longing"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the jealousy of the Loves of both alike,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she proclaimed to bridal Ares a contest for Beroe","st":"42.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a spearman marriage and a lovely Enyo."},{"sp":"N","t":"And covering her wholly in some womanish adornment"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cypris set her upon the acropolis of her own fatherland,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden as the disputed, delicate prize of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to both gods she joined a single voice:","st":"42.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I could wish that I had two daughters, so that I might join"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one owed to the Earth-shaker, and the other to Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but since I did not bear twins, and the undefiled ordinances"},{"sp":"N","t":"of marriage do not bid me yoke one girl as consort"},{"sp":"N","t":"to two alternating husbands,","st":"42.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"let there be a bride-adorning battle over one wife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the bed of Beroe is not without toil; and about the bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"let you both contend in a contest that goes before the marriage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whoever conquers, let him take Beroe without bride-price. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"Let there be a friendly oath for both; since I greatly fear","st":"42.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"for the neighboring city of the girl, where I am called city-holder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I destroy the fatherland of Beroe for the beauty of Beroe;"},{"sp":"N","t":"make covenants before the marriage, lest after the fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea Earth-shaker, cheated of the victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wipe out the land with the point of his trident,","st":"42.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest Dionysus in anger over the bed of Amymone"},{"sp":"N","t":"dim the vine threshing-floor of the city."},{"sp":"N","t":"And be gracious after the tumult; and let both of you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a jealousy of love under an ordinance of like-mindedness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorn the fatherland of the bride with a brighter beauty.\"","st":"42.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"As she spoke the suitors assented; and for both"},{"sp":"N","t":"the steadfast oath was Cronides and Earth and Upper Air"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the drops of Styx; and the Fates confirmed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the covenants; and Strife grew, the escort of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Turmoil; and bride-adorning Persuasion armed them both.","st":"42.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"And coming from heaven as watchers of the contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"all who dwell in Olympus stayed with Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"witnesses of the fighting high upon the Lebanon rock."},{"sp":"N","t":"There a great sign appeared to desire-stricken Dionysus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"a storm-swift falcon, slackening a wing pregnant with wind,","st":"42.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"pursued a feeding dove; and suddenly a sea-eagle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"snatching her from the ground, flew into the deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the bird aloft with sparing talons."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing it Dionysus spat away his hope of victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nevertheless he came to the battle. And at the tumult of both","st":"42.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"father Cronion rejoiced with a smiling eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching from on high the fighting of his brother and his son."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Seek the forty-third besides, where I sing the wavy Ares and the vine Enyo."},{"sp":"N","t":"So battle-rousing Ares, the channeler of the Loves,","st":"43.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"shouted the chamber-serving echo of a bridal fight,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fixed the foundations of a marriage war;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and darting the tumult against the Earth-shaker and Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hymenaeus was furious, and dancing into the fighting","st":"43.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted the bronze spear of Amyclaean Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bellowing the harmony of Ares with a Phrygian flute."},{"sp":"N","t":"And for the king of the Satyrs and the charioteer of the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden was the prize; and refusing in silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"to have the migrant bed of a suitor of the brine","st":"43.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"she feared the wet chamber of submerged Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wished for Bacchus the more; and she was like Deianeira,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, when a bridal contest roared around her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wished for Heracles, and stood trembling"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the ox-horned wedding of an unsteady river.","st":"43.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"And with a self-bidden course in a coiling whirl"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cloudless upper air trumpeted a warlike melody;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pouring a grim bellowing from a raving throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-haired god armed himself with an Assyrian trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his sea spear. And threatening the sea,","st":"43.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus reveled into the fray with a wine-dark thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated upon the car of his mountain-ranging mother Rhea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a self-perfected vine, growing beside the rail"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Mygdonian chariot, covered the whole body of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling his shadowed locks with yoked ivy;","st":"43.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a lion, shaking its entwined neck beneath the yoke-strap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending a harsh bellowing from a grinning lip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scored the surface of the ground with whetted paw;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an elephant, creeping slowly beside a neighboring spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"digging the upright support of its unbending foot,","st":"43.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"drew up the rain-water with parched lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dried the outpourings; and as the streams turned to dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"it drove off the thirsty, tunicless Nymph of the spring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god who rules the waters armed himself; and there was tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"among the Nereids; and drenched from the backs of the sea","st":"43.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"the spirits marched out; and with long-shooted clusters"},{"sp":"N","t":"the house of Poseidon, the sea water, was lashed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the crested earthly hollow was battered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vines of Lebanon were heaved aside with the trident."},{"sp":"N","t":"And upon some dark-skinned herd of cattle feeding near the sea,","st":"43.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"the herd of Poseidon, the raving women rushed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a furious leap; and of a wide-eyed bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"one, laying hold, split its spine, and another crushed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unshaken tips of the twin horn of its brow;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one cut apart its belly with a crushing thyrsus;","st":"43.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"another cut the whole side of the ox; and half-dead,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-rolling, the bull sank upon its back on the field;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the freshly cut ox rolled in the dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"one drew off its hind feet, and another, taking hold,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragged the forefeet, and with a much-whirling rush","st":"43.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"she sphered its twin hoof upright into the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Dionysus marshaled the leaders of his army,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting five phalanxes for the watery Enyo."},{"sp":"N","t":"The first rank was led by Cilician Oeneus of the fair vines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the son of Ereuthalion, whom he plowed near Taurus,","st":"43.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"consorting in the country wedding of Phyllis;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the second was led by dark-haired Helicaon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yellow in nature with rosy cheeks, and about his neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"the coiling cluster of his well-whirled hair trailed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Oenopion led the third, Staphylus fought in the front of the fourth,","st":"43.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two children of Oenomaus, an unmixed-wine-loving father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Melanthius led the fifth, a chief of the Indians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Oenone the daughter of Cisseus bore, and about the boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"his mother, plaiting the tips of the leaves of a fragrant"},{"sp":"N","t":"planting, wound clustered swaddling-clothes about him,","st":"43.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"having washed her son in a vat pregnant with drunkenness."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a phalanx armed itself, shooting with ivy-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"shafts, running with the clustered Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he armed his army, pouring out a host-rousing echo:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bassarids, make war; and as Lyaeus arms himself","st":"43.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"let my horned flute, beating out a warlike sound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"speak an answering melody against the bellowing conch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the drums thud out the bronze-voiced echo"},{"sp":"N","t":"of battle with twin crashes; and dancing for Enyalius"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Maron shoot Glaucus with a man-breaking thyrsus;","st":"43.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bind the locks of Proteus with unaccustomed ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, leaving the Egyptian water of the Pharian sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wearing a dappled-backed fawnskin after the skins of the seal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him bend his bold neck to me; and if he can,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Melicertes arm himself against a drunken Silenus;","st":"43.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"and teach old Phorcys, after his weedy haunts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lift the thyrsus as a dweller of Tmolus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let a battle-steadfast Satyr, shaking his fennel-stalk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remove thirsty Nereus from the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the old man become a vine-tending farmer of the land","st":"43.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"with country palms; and from freshly planted gardens"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdle the locks of Palaemon with a wine-dark bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bring the sea charioteer to my mother Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a servant after the depth of the Isthmian brine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a steersman of lions with a sea-whip;","st":"43.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I shall no longer leave my cousin in the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And I shall behold the phalanx of the spear-won sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned with a fawnskin; and give cymbals to the Nereid"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nymphs who have not tried them; mingle the water-maidens"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Bacchants; but guard the house of Thetis alone,","st":"43.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hospitable goddess, even though she is of the race of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fit the unsandaled soles of Leucothea to buskins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Doris, appearing on land as the companion of a Euian"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchant, lift my revel-loving pine-torch;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Panopeia of the deep, shaking off the moss of the brine,","st":"43.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdle her locks with a viperish cluster;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let Eidothea receive the much-beaten rattles unwilling;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and since she has a longing equal to his who is love-maddened himself,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what blame is it that Galatea should serve Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, as a chamber-serving bride-gift for Amymone,","st":"43.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"she may make a robe with loom-toiling hand for the Lebanon queen?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But leave the race of Nereus; for I do not wish"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-traveling handmaids, lest I rouse jealousy in Beroe."},{"sp":"N","t":"And let my mountain-roaming Pan, pressing with unarmed hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"the point of his long-shooted, hairy brow,","st":"43.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"strike Poseidon with his whetted horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hitting the middle of his breast with well-curved points"},{"sp":"N","t":"or with a crested crag, and let him break with his hooves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twofold yoked circle of the spine of Triton."},{"sp":"N","t":"Let Glaucus, the messenger of the brine-drenched Earth-shaker,","st":"43.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"serve Bacchus, lifting in his hands the much-beaten"},{"sp":"N","t":"drums of Rhea, hanging from a neck-strap."},{"sp":"N","t":"I fight not for Beroe alone, but for the fatherland"},{"sp":"N","t":"of our bride herself; let not the Earth-shaker, the ruler"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the brine, batter her as she stands unshaken","st":"43.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dim her with his trident, sea-girt though she be,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because I shall arm myself against him as he arms; for both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if she has got a neighboring deep, she has the countless plants of Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the token of our victory; for of the sea-neighboring &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"but after the elder Pallas let another Cecrops","st":"43.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"come as judge with Bacchus for witness, so that the vine too"},{"sp":"N","t":"may be sung as city-bearing, as the olive is."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having made another pattern of the city I shall not leave it"},{"sp":"N","t":"near the sea, but, cutting the rocky hills with my fennel-stalk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall bridge the depth of the brine that neighbors Berytus,","st":"43.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"making dry land with crags of the petrified water of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a rough road is leveled by a sharp thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But make war again, Mimallons, bold in your accustomed"},{"sp":"N","t":"victory; and my fawnskin, wearing the freshly flowing"},{"sp":"N","t":"blood of slain Giants, blackens; and still the east itself","st":"43.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"trembles at me, and the Indian Ares bends his neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the ground, and old Hydaspes, shedding suppliant tears"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Bromius, wavy tears, has shuddered."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having the Lebanon bride after the wet fighting"},{"sp":"N","t":"I grant one prize to the Earth-shaker in his longing:","st":"43.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he wishes, let him sing the wedding-hymn of my Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"only let him not stretch a slanting eye upon my Beroe.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he spoke; and with a threatening speech"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-haired god answered, mocking Dionysus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"In shame, Dionysus, I arm myself, because you have striven","st":"43.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a spearman of the trident, you who fled the ox-axe of Lycurgus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I never admire this, light-bearer; for from a mother-slaying"},{"sp":"N","t":"fire you grew, whence you do deeds worthy of fire."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, dear Tritons, help me, bind the Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making them sea-travelers; and let the drums of Silenus,","st":"43.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"their mountain-haunting bearer, be deluged by the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he is dragged by the wave, and in a swelling stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the Euian-loving flute of a swimming Satyr wander"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a self-coiling voyage; and in a fair-watered hall"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the Bassarids spread my bed instead of Lyaeus's.","st":"43.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of Satyrs, I draw no Maenads into the deep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Nereids are better; but let the thirsty Mimallons"},{"sp":"N","t":"be hidden in the sea, and instead of poured drunkenness"},{"sp":"N","t":"let them drink the salt water of my brine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let some Bassarid, driven by the wet point of Proteus,","st":"43.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"slip self-rolling into the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having tumbled a dance of death for Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Come here, Thetis, look: your Dionysus, having escaped,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave fair life-ransom to the hospitable sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And drag off the phalanxes of the Ethiopians and the ranks of the Indians","st":"43.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"as plunder for the Nereids, and bring to Doris"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slave children of the evil-tongued bride Cassiepeia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a late-accomplished vengeance; and with an irresistible stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Ocean remove from Olympus the fiery star of Maera"},{"sp":"N","t":"bathed in his water, the forerunner of the unsleeping dance","st":"43.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the vat, the vine Sirius."},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you, Lydian Bacchus, leaving your inferior thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seek another shaft for yourself, and lay down the dappled skins"},{"sp":"N","t":"of fawns, a little covering of your limbs; and if"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridal flame of heavenly Zeus midwifed you,","st":"43.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"now make war with fire, fire-reared one, now with your father's"},{"sp":"N","t":"thunderbolt fight in the front against the steersman of the trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lift the lightning and brandish the aegis of your father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for it is not Deriades who awaits you as champion, nor is this"},{"sp":"N","t":"the contest of Lycurgus, a little battle of Arabs, but of so great","st":"43.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sea; and heaven itself, trembling still at my sea point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has learned my deep-sea Enyo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and high-traveling Phaethon, the champion, made trial"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the point of my trident, when about Corinth"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hard-fighting sea Ares armed himself into a starry battle;","st":"43.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the sea was lifted toward the upper air, and in Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"the thirsty Wagon bathed, and in the waters of the neighboring brine"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Dog of Maera, dipping its hot jaws, was cooled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hollows of the deep haunts were raised up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"towering the waves, and as the sea was lashed","st":"43.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"a sea-dolphin met the heavenly Dolphin.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he shook the recesses of the sea with his trident,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a resounding surge and a swelling stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trails of the water boomed, lashing the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the army of the brine armed itself with wet shields;","st":"43.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beside the brine-drenched manger of the Cronides of the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"Melicertes whirled his submerged lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoking his Isthmian car, and hung the spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the water-traveling king beside his sea-greaved wagon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the backs of the sea with a threefold point,","st":"43.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoking his Isthmian car; and with a horse's whinnying"},{"sp":"N","t":"he clattered against the roaring of the Indian lions."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he drove a wet course; and as his car stretched out"},{"sp":"N","t":"an undrenched, unwetted hoof scored the surface of the water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And broad-bearded Triton crashed out into the raving battle,","st":"43.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"who in his twin limbs has a mortal-seeming shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"strange-natured, green, half-finished from the loins"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the head; and hanging from the trail of his wet loins"},{"sp":"N","t":"a double tail is bent about in a fishy pattern."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with a wet whip, beside a sea manger","st":"43.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoking his car carried by a swift-traveling tempest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Glaucus lashed the crest of his unwashed horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pursued the Satyrs. And in the sea-roaring tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"horned Pan, a light wayfarer upon the untrodden waters,"},{"sp":"N","t":"undrenched, beating the brine with goat's hooves,","st":"43.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped restless, striking the sea with his crook,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whistling a melody of war upon his pipe; and in the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hearing a mimic, windy image of his own voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he ran across the sea water with mountain-ranging feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking another sound; and the sea echo itself,","st":"43.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"wind-borne, born of the pipe, was pursued."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another, whirling a fair-based island hill,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled it at the water-maidens, and the rock, straying,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook the inner court of Palaemon of the Nereids."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Proteus, leaving the Isthmian swell of the Pallenian brine,","st":"43.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed himself with a sea breastplate, the skin of a seal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about him the blazing Indians poured crown-wise"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the call of Bacchus, and ranks of woolly-haired men"},{"sp":"N","t":"embraced the many-shaped herdsman of the seals;","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the old man was clamped his image was of changing color;","st":"43.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Proteus, weaving a mimic pattern in his limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spotted his own shape as a dappled-backed leopard;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he stood upright upon the ground as a self-perfected plant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having made his limbs a tree, and as his leaves were shaken"},{"sp":"N","t":"he whistled a lying whisper to the north wind;","st":"43.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, scoring his back adorned with painted scales,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crept as a serpent, and, as his middle flank was squeezed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted a coil aloft, and with a dancer's throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"the tips of his stretched circling tail were whirled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he raised his head, and spitting from his jaws","st":"43.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"a javelin-hurling venom he hissed with gaping throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having a shifting body in a shadowy shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bristled as a lion, rushed as a boar, flowed as water; and the chorus of Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamped the liquid stream in a threatening bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the deceiving water with slipping hands;","st":"43.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the cunning old man, changing his much-wrought shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had the many-turning devices of the form of Periclymenus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom Heracles killed, when, joining two fingers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he crushed the lying imitation of a bastard bee."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sea-monster flocks wheeled about the old man's","st":"43.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"journey on land, and the heavy-thundering water seethed"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the opened throat of a sand-loving seal."},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing the phalanx of his daughters into the Euian fray"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Nereus armed himself with a wavy spear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping down upon the elephants with a sea-traveling trident,","st":"43.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"dread to look upon; and many neighboring banks beside the shore"},{"sp":"N","t":"were slanted by the sea point of Nereus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the offspring of the Nereids beat out with their father"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shout of battle; and into the fray high upon the deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"the half-seen, unsandaled chorus of the brine raved in Bacchic frenzy.","st":"43.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"And unsteady Ino, rushing ironless into the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Satyrs, ran up to her ancient madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"belching the white foam of a maddened chin."},{"sp":"N","t":"And grim Panopeia, darting through the calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashed the grey-green backs of a sea lioness;","st":"43.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifting the club of Polyphemus unhappy in love"},{"sp":"N","t":"Galatea of the sea armed herself against a raving Bacchant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifting her unshaken upon its brine-nourished back"},{"sp":"N","t":"a pilot-fish bore Eido undrenched through the water."},{"sp":"N","t":"And as when a riding driver, by a wheeling art,","st":"43.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting his whole left-hand horse near the turning-post,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bends the right, the bridle let loose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"urging with the goad, pouring out a horse-striking threat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crouching, arched, fixing his knees against the rail"},{"sp":"N","t":"with bending loins, and, driving a willing horse,","st":"43.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashes it a little with a sparing, cunning palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"casting his eye behind, and with his face drawn back"},{"sp":"N","t":"guards against the car of the driver coming behind;"},{"sp":"N","t":"so then the Nereids drove their fishes, like swift-traveling"},{"sp":"N","t":"horses, about the wet turning-post of the contest.","st":"43.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"And another held a sea-running course on the opposite path,"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding the calm upon a fishy back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a driver of a dolphin, peering above the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and held a wet-maddened course; and some maddened wet wayfarer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a dolphin seen in the middle, split the yoked dolphins apart.","st":"43.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the rivers cried out into the fighting of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heartening their lord; and from the ever-flowing throats"},{"sp":"N","t":"of gaping Ocean the Poseidonian trumpet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the messenger of the battle, roared a watery bellowing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the seas were arched, fighting beside the trident;","st":"43.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Myrtoan ran upon the Icarian, and appearing near"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sardinian upon the western, the Iberian was dragged upon the Celtic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swelling with its seas, and with the accustomed twin deep"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unfixed Bosporus mingled its crooked water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hollows of the Ionian sea, darting together","st":"43.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a tempest, lashed the streams of the Aegean,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoked; and beside the ankles of the raving Sicilian brine"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Adriatic brine, towered with waves, crashed together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloud-neighboring; and taking his conch beneath the water of Syrtis"},{"sp":"N","t":"Libyan Nereus bellowed with a sea trumpet;","st":"43.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a land wayfarer, leaping up from the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"propped his left foot upon a watch-peak, and with his right,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting the topmost heads of a mountain with an earth-shaking sole,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled them at the head of an untouched maenad;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hurling his deep-sea trident against Dionysus","st":"43.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"Melicertes raved in Bacchic frenzy with his mother's leaps."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanxes of the Bassarids marched into the tumult,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one, shaking the migrant cluster of her hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed herself for the watery fray with a roaming madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady, goaded with the dancing throb of her feet;","st":"43.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, who had settled beneath the Thracian cave"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Cabeiri in Samos, skipped beside the Lebanon cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting a barbarian melody of Corybantic echo;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another from Tmolus, high upon a lioness in childbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling her male hair with a snaky bond,","st":"43.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled Maeonian Mimallon roared beneath her breath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fixed the track of her foot aloft above the bank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"foaming at the sea with mimic jaws."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Sileni, gushing up the Cilician dew,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed themselves as drivers of Mygdonian lions,","st":"43.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, skipping with a clang upon the throng of the deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raised in their palms the vine shoot of Enyo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretched out their palms to a lion's neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gripping its mane, and boldly reined back"},{"sp":"N","t":"their irresistible bearers with shaggy bridles.","st":"43.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"And snatching up the neck of a torrent-cut haunt"},{"sp":"N","t":"Silenus fought against Palaemon, and drove off roaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ino through the water with an ivy spear."},{"sp":"N","t":"And one strove with another; and a Bacchant did not shrink"},{"sp":"N","t":"from rushing down upon a trident with javelin-hurling thyrsus,","st":"43.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bacchant though she was a woman; and shielding the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus the migrant strove against crag-loving Pan"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a seething forearm; and a mountain Bacchant drove off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Pallenian spirit with blood-red ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did he dash her away; and as Glaucus came on","st":"43.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"against Lyaeus, Maron shook him off with a javelin-hurling thyrsus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a cloud-high elephant, whirling its standing step"},{"sp":"N","t":"with an earth-shaking throb of its limbs, with the unbending mass of its knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fought with its long lips against a ground-bedded seal;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs rushed on in a tumbling tumult,","st":"43.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull-natured, trusting in their horns, and as they hurried"},{"sp":"N","t":"a strange-appearing upright tail hung loose from their loins."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanxes of the Sileni poured in, of whom one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mounted with parted feet upon the loins of a bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pressed out the twice-sounding melody of plaited flutes.","st":"43.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"And darting her locks together with the dappled tempests"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Mygdonian Bacchant rattled her yoked cymbals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lashed the arched crest of a raving bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"facing a beast of the deep; and a wild mountain-roaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"leopard was driven on by a goading thyrsus.","st":"43.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, held fast by the leap of a wit-robbing madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipped upon the sea with unwetted steps,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though skipping upon the head of Poseidon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she struck the waves with the heel of her foot, and threatened the silent"},{"sp":"N","t":"deep, and a water-borne Bassarid lashed the deaf water","st":"43.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"with her thyrsus; and from the lock of the bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-moving, unburning fire blazed down her neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wonder to see. And plaintive Psamathe, beside the shore"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the neighboring deep, watching the battle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of sea-fighting Dionysus, dread-suffering, broke into a much-fearing voice:","st":"43.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"If you know the favor of Thetis and of skilled-handed Briareus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you have learned of Aegaeon, the helper of your ordinances,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Zeus, check maddened Bacchus; and let me not behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slavery of Nereus upon the death of Glaucus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let not Thetis of the shifting tears serve Lyaeus,","st":"43.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not see her a handmaid beside Bromius, looking upon"},{"sp":"N","t":"the land of the Lydians after the sea, groaning in a single grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Achilles, Peleus, Pyrrhus, grandson, husband, son;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pity mourning Leucothea, whose consort"},{"sp":"N","t":"took her son and cut him down, whom the child-slaying","st":"43.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"points of an unloving father’s knife carved apart.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So she spoke, and high-ruling Zeus heard through the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entrusted the wedding of Beroe to the Earth-shaker,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and soothed the bride-adorning battle; for from heaven,"},{"sp":"N","t":"checking the unaccomplished bridal Enyo,","st":"43.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"threatening thunderbolts wheeled about Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god, shaken by a bridal shaft,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed for the girl; but his father, high-ruling Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"checked him, beating out the melody of a thunderous trumpet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his father's echo reined back his longing for the fighting.","st":"43.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"And with shrinking feet he withdrew, a sluggish wayfarer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grim, watching the girl with a backward-thrown eye;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with shamefaced ears he heard in jealousy"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wedding of Amymone sung upon the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the sea-roaring pipe cried the half-finished marriage,","st":"43.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking an unquenchable bridal fire in the waters"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus the chamber-servant cried the bridal chamber of Amymone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Phorcys wove a melody; and in an emulous journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"Glaucus skipped up, and Melicertes raved in Bacchic frenzy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Galatea, beating out a yoking dance,","st":"43.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled unsteady with a dancer's throb of her feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spoke a bridal melody, since she had learned to sing fair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taught by the shepherd's pipe of Polyphemus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And consorting in the liquid wedding of Beroe"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom Earth-shaker loved the fatherland of his bride;","st":"43.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to the dwellers of Beroe he gave as a treasure of his own bed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea victory of a sea Ares."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the marriage was blessed, since beside the deep-sea bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"Arabian Nereus brought a worthy bride-gift of love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wise work of Hephaestus, Olympian cunning work, for the bride,","st":"43.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing a necklace and bearing buds and stretching out spirals,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as many as the Lemnian worker made for the Nereids"},{"sp":"N","t":"with inimitable art beside the waves; and in the midst of the brine","hvShift6":[[0,0],[36,37],[42,41]]},{"sp":"N","t":"he brandished a fiery anvil and a submerged fire-tongs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretching the circling panting of a blowing furnace","st":"43.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"with manufactured winds, and as the forge was kindled"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unquenchable fire boomed hidden within the surges."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nereus gave these many-turning gifts; and the Persian Euphrates"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave the girl the much-wrought work of the spider;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Iberian Rhine gave gold; and old Pactolus came","st":"43.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing like gifts of wealth-holding metals"},{"sp":"N","t":"with guarded hands, because he feared the champion of the Lydians,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus his own king, and feared his neighbor Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the city-holder of his own Mygdonian land; and Eridanus"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave glittering gifts, the amber of the Heliades","st":"43.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"from wealth-flowing trees; and from a silver rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the metals that Strymon and all that Geudis brings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dark-haired god gave as a bride-gift to Amymone."},{"sp":"N","t":"So the Earth-shaker, fresh from the dance, rejoiced"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside his submerged bridal chamber; and to unsmiling Lyaeus","st":"43.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"his brother Eros cried a comforting voice as he grudged:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why, Dionysus, do you still blame the bride-tending cestus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"The marriage of Beroe did not befit Bromius, but this marriage"},{"sp":"N","t":"was fitting for the sea, because, taking the child of"},{"sp":"N","t":"deep-sea Aphrodite, I yoked her to a sea-traveling consort;","st":"43.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I have kept a more delicate Ariadne for your chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kindred from the race of Minos; and leave in the sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"the paltry Amymone who bears sea blood."},{"sp":"N","t":"But leaving the crest of Lebanon and the water of Adonis"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall come to Phrygia of fair maidens, where there awaits you","st":"43.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unwetted bed of Titanian Aura, daughter of Helios;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having fashioned a crown of battle and a bridal chamber for the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bride-tending Thrace shall receive you, where spear-wielding"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pallene herself calls you, beside whose bridal chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall crown you as prize-bearer with bridal clusters","st":"43.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"when you have accomplished the lovely wrestling of Aphrodite.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things furious Eros said to his woman-mad brother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus; and sending the fiery boom of his wings"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bastard bird was lifted in an airy journey,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and came to the house of Zeus. And from the Assyrian gulf","st":"43.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"soft-tunicked Dionysus went up to the land of the Lydians"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the floor of Pactolus, where with gold-gleaming clay"},{"sp":"N","t":"the black water reddens with a rich chalk;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he set foot in Maeonia, and stood before his mother Rhea"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding out the royal gifts of the Indian sea.","st":"43.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the streams of the deep-wealthy river"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Phrygian hollow and the race of delicate-living men"},{"sp":"N","t":"he planted his harvest beside the northern floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"going to the cities of Europe after the towns of Asia."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLIV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"I have woven the forty-fourth, where you may behold women maddened and the mass of the threat of Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now, leaving the Daulantian nation of the Illyrian field","st":"44.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the plain of Haemonia and the peak of Pelion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came near Hellas, and beside the Aonian floor"},{"sp":"N","t":"he set up dances. And hearing the melody of the bellowing flute"},{"sp":"N","t":"a shepherd set up revel-bands for Pan of Tanagra;","st":"44.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a spring cried out, where, battering the surface of the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a liquid horse's hoof carved out the water named for it;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Asopus danced, dragging fire-breathing streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirling his outpourings; and with her father Ismenus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dirce danced, darting her circling floods.","st":"44.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"And once a Hamadryad, leaping up from an oaken cluster,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sang shrill, half-seen, high upon her tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorifying the name of cluster-bearing Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and an unsandaled spring Nymph cried out with the same voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mountain-roaming crash of the untanned ox-hide","st":"44.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"crashed upon the truceless ears of Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lawless king was enraged at wine-bearing Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and armed a fighting host, and bade the citizens"},{"sp":"N","t":"fence about the gateways of the seven-pathed city;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they shut them in turn, but suddenly","st":"44.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bolts of the gates opened of their own accord,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the servants cast the long bars upon the gateway in vain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striving against the airy winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not then did any gatekeeper see and rein back a Bacchant;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shield-bearing spearmen trembled at the old, unarmed","st":"44.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sileni; and with a shout of one tongue,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sparing nothing of the threat of the king who called them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they danced again and again, and with their well-made ox-hide shields"},{"sp":"N","t":"they set up the shield-brandishing leap of a circling dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dread raving bears cried out among the mountains;","st":"44.30","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,1]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and a leopard, darting its jaw and its high-flying"},{"sp":"N","t":"rush, was suspended; and a lion, playing delicately,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in answering imitation of the crash-loving Corybants,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,1]]},{"sp":"N","t":"sent a gentle roaring to a lioness of its own age."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already the court of Pentheus shook, self-coiling,","st":"44.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping sphere-wise from its unbending foundations;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the gateway was shaken, darting with an earth-shaking throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the forerunner of the misery to come; and of its own accord"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stone altar of Onca Athena was whirled about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which Cadmus once built, when with a slow-obedient rush","st":"44.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"the city-bearing hoof of the tower-building heifer sank down;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the divine image of the city-holding goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"a god-driven sweat bubbled with self-moving drops,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing terror to the dwellers; and from foot to head"},{"sp":"N","t":"the statue of Ares flowed with gore, a messenger of things to come.","st":"44.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the dwellers were shaken; and the mother of boastful Pentheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was whirled with fear, and raved in her purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembering an earlier blood-red dream"},{"sp":"N","t":"that prophesied bitter things, since before, high upon her bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ever since Pentheus had snatched his father's lordship,","st":"44.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"phantoms of a mimic dream all night long,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaping through the unwandering gateway of horn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified Agave as she slept in slumbering whispers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she thought that Pentheus, a delicate dance-beating wayfarer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having adorned his male flesh with a woman's robe,","st":"44.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"cast the purple-backed cloak of lords upon the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightening a thyrsus and no longer the bearer of a scepter;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cadmeian Agave thought she saw him again"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated aloft high upon a shadowy tree;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beasts wheeled about the high-headed plant","st":"44.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"where bold Pentheus sat, and held a savage rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heaving the tree with threatening tooth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with rough jaws; and as the tree was shaken"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pentheus was whirled head first, self-rolling, coiling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raving bears mangled him as he fell;","st":"44.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a wild lioness, rushing down upon his face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tore off his hand at the root, and the beast, uncontrollably mad,"},{"sp":"N","t":"propping her foot upon the throat of half-cut Pentheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cut through his throat with whetted claws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted his bloody head with a snatching paw","st":"44.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he was pitiably cut apart, and showed it to Cadmus as witness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"brandishing it, and broke into a sinful mortal voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I am your beast-slaying daughter; and I am the mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"of most blessed Pentheus, your child-loving Agave."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have destroyed so great a beast; and receive this head","st":"44.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the first prey of my strength, of a lion-slaying victory;"},{"sp":"N","t":"never did my sister Ino kill so great a beast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Autonoe did not kill one; but do you fix the tokens"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your daughter Agave, the noblest-working, before your hall.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a grim-eyed dream did pale Agave see.","st":"44.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"Whence, much-dreaded, shaking off the wing of Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at dawn she called the god-speaking son of Chariclo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and told the prophet the murderous dreams of things to come;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Teiresias the seer bade her sacrifice a male"},{"sp":"N","t":"bull, a helper against the blood-red dream,","st":"44.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the god-invoking altar of evil-averting Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside the tree of a long fir, where Cithaeron"},{"sp":"N","t":"is spread out high-headed; and he signified to sacrifice"},{"sp":"N","t":"a female sheep to the Hamadryad Nymphs beside the thicket."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he knew that the intelligent beast and hunting Agave","st":"44.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"were the travail of her own belly and a child-destroying contest"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the head of Pentheus; and in voiceless silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"he hid the deceiving image of the dream victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he anger his heavy-wrathful king Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And obeying the wise old man, child-loving Agave","st":"44.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"went as companion with Cadmus to the high-headed mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pentheus following; and beside a well-horned altar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where was a grove of Zeus full of mountain wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus son of Agenor joined in a single sacrifice"},{"sp":"N","t":"a female sheep together with a horned male bull","st":"44.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Zeus and the Hadryads, kindling a god-delighting altar,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to make offering to both; and as the torch was kindled"},{"sp":"N","t":"the roaming coiling savor swam together with the smoke"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a fragrant whirl, and as the bull was cut down"},{"sp":"N","t":"an upright, self-rushing pipe of bloody dew","st":"44.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"purpled the hands of Agave with reddening slaughter; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winding crown-wise about the sinew of his neck"},{"sp":"N","t":"a gentle serpent slanted its swollen, arched neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled about with a coiling trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the mild snake wheeled its head about the head of Cadmus","st":"44.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a trailing garland, and its tongue licked about his beard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting a friendly venom from its gentle jaws"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they opened; and a female serpent girdled the temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Harmonia, entwined about her yellow clusters."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Cronion turned the limbs of the two serpents to stone,","st":"44.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"because beside the mouth of the serpent-feeding Illyrian sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"Harmonia and Cadmus, their faces changed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"were to have a stony, snaky shape."},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a phantom she once saw, and remembering"},{"sp":"N","t":"the oracular dream child-loving Agave was shaken with fear.","st":"44.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"And holding another fear after the earlier fear of sleep"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came home returning with her son and her father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already Rumor was flying through the seven-pathed city"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaiming the rites of dance-weaving Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was any man in the city without a dance; and the streets","st":"44.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"were girdled with the springtime leaves of the country folk;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shadowing with a green cluster the chamber of Semele,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still breathing of the bridal spark of the thunderbolt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-grown tendril made it drunk with fragrant fruit."},{"sp":"N","t":"And gazing at the dread, many-shaped wonders of Bacchus,","st":"44.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding an overbearing jealousy, king Pentheus surged;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pouring out the proud boast of an empty threat"},{"sp":"N","t":"reckless Pentheus cried such a word to his servants:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bring me my Lydian servant, the womanish wanderer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the attendant of the table of Pentheus as he feasts,","st":"44.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping another drink into a wine-receiving cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"either milk or a sweet stream; and my mother's sister","hvShift6":[[0,0],[29,30],[35,34]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Autonoe, I shall lash with alternating blows;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,4],[3,8],[24,21]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and casting the resounding cymbals to the winds"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Berecyntian crash and the Euian drums of Rhea,","st":"44.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"drag off the maddened Bassarids, drag off the Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the handmaids and companions of Bromius, whom in Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling them into the liquid haunts of Ismenus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mingle as Naiads with the Aonian river Nymphs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"their age-mates; and let old Cithaeron receive the Hadryads","st":"44.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"as yokemates for other Hadryads instead of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let us cut the locks of unshorn Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bring fire, servants, since by an avenging ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Bacchus came from fire, I shall give Bacchus to fire;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus subdued Semele, I shall destroy Dionysus.","st":"44.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if he should make trial of our thunderbolt too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shall know what earthly radiance I have; for my answering"},{"sp":"N","t":"fire has got hotter sparks than the heavenly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"today I shall make the vine-clad one smoky."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if he should set up a fight, lifting a warlike thyrsus,","st":"44.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shall know what earthly spear I have; and I shall destroy him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not tending a wound of foot, of flanks, of breast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of hollows; nor shall I cut with an ox-axe"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two curved horns of his bull-horned brow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall I cut through the middle of his neck; but I shall strike him","st":"44.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a bronze spear, pierced in the fold of the thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because he has lied about being the offspring of the thigh of great Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the vault as his own house; and instead of that hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"instead of the gateway of Zeus, I shall send him down to Hades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or, as he flees self-rolling, I shall cover him","st":"44.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the waves of Ismenus, and there is no need of the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not accept a mortal man as a bastard god; if it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall lie about my own race, as Dionysus does; I do not bear"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of earthly Cadmus; but my father, the chief of the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Helios begot me, and Echion did not sow me;","st":"44.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene bore me, and Agave did not bring me forth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am of the race of Cronides, and I am a citizen of the upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"star-roaming heaven is my city; be gracious, Thebes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pallas is my consort, immortal Hebe is my wife;"},{"sp":"N","t":"queen Hera held out her breast to Pentheus after Ares,","st":"44.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and holy Leto bore Pentheus after Phoebus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall wed Artemis at her own desire; nor does she flee me,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as once she fled Phoebus, the suitor of her maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning the reproach of a wedlock with her brother."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if the heavenly flame did not burn your Semele,","st":"44.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus burned his own house for the reproach of his daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and called the ground-born, kindling fire a lightning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and named the radiance of torches a spark of the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"As the king so spoke, his warriors marched out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weapon-bearers striving against empty winds;","st":"44.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a measureless host was within the pine-wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the tracks of Lyaeus who could not be seen."},{"sp":"N","t":"While king Pentheus gave commands to the dwellers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile Dionysus too, gazing at the lightless night,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried such a word toward Olympus to the circling Moon:","st":"44.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"O child of Helios, much-turning, all-nourishing Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene, steersman of a silver car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you are many-named Hecate, and by night"},{"sp":"N","t":"shake the revel-loving pine-torch with a torch-bearing palm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"come, night-roamer, whelp-rearer, since the nightly","st":"44.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"hound-rousing echo of a whimpering whine delights you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you are deer-shooting Artemis, and among the hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hastening, hunt together with fawn-slaying Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"be the helper of your brother; for having got the blood"},{"sp":"N","t":"of first-born Cadmus I am driven outside Thebes,","st":"44.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the fatherland of my mother Semele; for a swift-fated"},{"sp":"N","t":"mortal man, a god-fighter, routs me; and as goddess of the night"},{"sp":"N","t":"help Dionysus of the night as he is driven off;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you are Persephoneia, the rouser of the dead, and souls"},{"sp":"N","t":"serve your Tartarean thrones,","st":"44.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me see Pentheus a corpse, and let your soul-escorting Hermes"},{"sp":"N","t":"lull the tear of grieving Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with the Tartarean whip of your raving Tisiphone"},{"sp":"N","t":"or Megaera stop the mind-robbing threat"},{"sp":"N","t":"of earth-born Pentheus, since ill-devising Hera","st":"44.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"has armed a later-born Titan against young Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you subdue the lawless man, so that you may honor"},{"sp":"N","t":"the name of first-born Zagreus Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Lord Zeus, do you too watch the threat of a maddened man;"},{"sp":"N","t":"hear, father and mother; and as Lyaeus is put to shame","st":"44.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"let your bridal lightning be the avenger of Semele.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke, bull-faced Mene cried out from on high:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Night-shining Dionysus, plant-tender, companion of Mene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"care for your grape; and the rites of Bacchus are my care,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the earth ripens the travail of your plants","st":"44.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving the dewy sparkle of unsleeping Selene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do you, dance-beating Bacchus, stretching out your thyrsi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"care for your own race, and do not tremble at a race of feeble"},{"sp":"N","t":"men, whose mind is always light, whose threats"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whips of the Eumenides check by necessity.","st":"44.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"With you I arm myself against the adversaries; and equally with Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"I rule over a shifting madness; and I am a Bacchic"},{"sp":"N","t":"Mene, not only because I wheel the months in the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but because I am mistress of madness and rouse frenzy."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not leave your earthly outrage unavenged;","st":"44.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"for already Lycurgus, who threatened Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who was before swift-kneed, who sharply pursued the Maenads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wanders blind and lacks a guide."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already about the necks of the Erythraean reeds"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dead throng of the Indians lies this way and that,","st":"44.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-announcer of your strength, and with a refusing stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"his father Hydaspes has hidden witless Deriades,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by an ivy spear; and he, fleeing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fell downcast into his father's heavy-roaring stream;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Tyrrhenians have learned your strength, when the upright","st":"44.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"mast of their ships was changed and became a vine shoot"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-perfected, and the sail, beneath shadowy leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grew into a fair-clustered covering of vines,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the forestays hissed with a viperish, venom-shooting"},{"sp":"N","t":"cluster, and the foes, casting away their mortal shape","st":"44.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their intelligent counsel, their faces changed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"swim the sea as witless dolphins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and still they revel for Dionysus even in the surges,"},{"sp":"N","t":"skipping upon the calm like tumblers."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a corpse, struck by your sharp thyrsus,","st":"44.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"Indian Orontes is covered by Assyrian streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still fearing the name of Bacchus even in the waters.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word the golden-reined goddess cried to Bromius."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Bacchus still kept company with the circling Moon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile Persephone, doing favor to Zagreus Dionysus,","st":"44.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed the Erinyes, and in grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"helped her later-born brother Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they, at the hard-to-meet nod of the head of the Zeus of the underworld,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Eumenides, marched against the hall of Pentheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one, leaping from the gloomy pit,","st":"44.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled a Tartarean viperish whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drew the stream of Cocytus and the water of Styx,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sprinkled the house of Agave with an earthly drop &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though prophesying the wailing and the tears of Thebes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the spirit brought an Attic knife from Athens,","st":"44.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"the ancient murderous knife of Itylus, with which once the mother"},{"sp":"N","t":"Procne, lion-hearted, together with man-slaying Philomela,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cutting apart her beloved travail with the iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"carved a dear food for child-devouring Tereus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing that in her hand, the Erinys, the channeler of murders,","st":"44.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"digging the dust with her evil-beginning nails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hid the Attic sword beside the mountain-grown root"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a long fir, where the Maenads, where Pentheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"was to die headless; and gathering in a shell"},{"sp":"N","t":"the freshly flowing blood of the newly slain Gorgon Medusa","st":"44.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"she anointed the tree with purple Libyan dews."},{"sp":"N","t":"These things the maddened Erinys wrought among the crags."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with gloomy feet night-shining Dionysus entered"},{"sp":"N","t":"the house of Cadmus, having a bull-faced shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting the maddening Cronian whip of Pan;","st":"44.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, driving the unbridled wife of Aristaeus to frenzy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he called Autonoe, and cried with a raving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Blessed Autonoe, more than Semele; for in the wedding"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your freshly wedded son you contend even with Olympus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have snatched the boast of the upper air, since Artemis got","st":"44.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon as her delicate consort, as Selene got Endymion."},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon did not die, he did not get the look of a beast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not the long-pointed horn of a dappled deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not receive a bastard shape, and did not lie about his form,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not see his own hunting hounds as his slayers;","st":"44.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"but with the empty-minded tale of evil-tongued mouths"},{"sp":"N","t":"the herdsmen lied about the doom of your son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hating the bridegroom of an unwedded goddess."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know whence this guile comes; over the weddings of others,"},{"sp":"N","t":"over marriage, over the Paphian, women are jealous.","st":"44.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"But leaping with stormy sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten to go untouchable to the mountains; and going there"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall see Actaeon hunting with Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding Artemis near him, and wearing the dappled nets"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the hunt and hunting-boots, and handling his quiver.","st":"44.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed Autonoe, more than Semele, because you are"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother-in-law of the archer-goddess as she comes to a wedding;"},{"sp":"N","t":"more blessed than Ino of the fair children, because your son"},{"sp":"N","t":"has got the bed of a goddess, which proud Otus did not get."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bold Orion was not the bridegroom of the archer-goddess.","st":"44.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"And grown young with joy for the sake of your son's bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Cadmus revels beside the mountain-haunting bridal chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his snowy hair in the airy winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Wake, and do you too be a bride-adorner, mother blessed in childbirth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this Eros is fitting, because chaste Artemis has taken","st":"44.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"as bridegroom her brother's son and not a stranger as consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the bed-fleeing goddess shall one day bear a child,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the son of the prudent archer-goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"in a child-tending arm, show him to jealous Agave."},{"sp":"N","t":"What blame would this ever be, if the hound-rouser beside a bridal chamber","st":"44.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"wished to bear a hare-shooting hunter son,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like Actaeon and crag-loving Cyrene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"riding upon the swift car of his mother's deer?\""},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLV"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"You shall look upon the forty-fifth, where Pentheus binds fast a bull instead of horn-drawing Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius so spoke, the bride ran out of her halls","st":"45.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"held fast by a raving joy, so that she might behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"Actaeon the bridegroom seated beside the archer-goddess;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with her as she hurried with tottering foot, running with the breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maenad Agave went unveiled to the mountain,","st":"45.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, her heart lashed by the goad of the Cronian whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieked out an unmarked voice with maddened lip:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I arm myself against paltry Pentheus, so that he may learn"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Cadmus begot in Agave a bold Amazon."},{"sp":"N","t":"I too am full of manhood; and if I wish,","st":"45.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall subdue the whole of Pentheus with bare palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cut down a well-armed host with an unarmed hand."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have a thyrsus; I need no ash-spear, I brandish no lance;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a vine spear I strike a spear-wielding man;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I wear no breastplate, and I shall subdue the well-corseleted.","st":"45.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"And darting my cymbals and my double-beaten ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"I glorify the son of Zeus, and do not honor Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Give me Lydian rattles; why do you delay, raving Seasons?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go to the crags, where the Maenads are, where women"},{"sp":"N","t":"of my own age hunt together with Lyaeus as he hunts.","st":"45.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have jealousy, Dionysus, of lion-slaying Cyrene;"},{"sp":"N","t":"spare me Bromius, god-fighter, spare him, Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"untouchable I shall go to the crags, so that I too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing the Euian cry, may whirl a dance-beating step;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do I refuse the rites of clustered Bacchus,","st":"45.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do I hate the chorus of the Bassarids; but I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"fear Dionysus, whom an imperishable bed plowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom the thunderbolts of high-ruling Zeus washed."},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be swift-sandaled, lifting the nets"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the archer-goddess my companion, and not the threads of Athena.\"","st":"45.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she flew off, a young Mimallon skipping,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the Euian-loving leap of a vat dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi for Bacchus and singing of Thyone;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she called Semele the wife of highest Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sang shrill of the radiance of the fair-shining bridal thunderbolts.","st":"45.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was a great dance among the crags; and about them the rocks"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried out; and a many-shaped echo ran round the plain"},{"sp":"N","t":"of seven-gated Thebes; and with a shout of one tongue"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the singers heavy-thundering Cithaeron crashed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the dewy din of the sea resounded; and one might see","st":"45.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trees reveling and the cliff speaking."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a girl leapt dance-beating out of her chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"when the horned flute cried through its pierced channels;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the much-shouted crash of the untanned ox-hide"},{"sp":"N","t":"drove the maidens to frenzy, and from their well-made halls","st":"45.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"sent the Bacchants as wanderers to the high-headed mountain."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a girl, goaded, with stormy sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her hair let loose, darted out of her maiden-chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the shuttle and the loom-perfecting Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and casting away the uncared-for veil of her locks","st":"45.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"she mingled with the Bassarids and became an Aonian Bacchant."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Teiresias made sacrifice to evil-averting Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"setting up an altar, so that he might check the outrage of Pentheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scatter the unsoftened anger of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he entreated in vain, since the thread of Fate had come.","st":"45.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the prudent seer called the father of Semele,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that they might share in the dancing of Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And old Cadmus danced with heavy feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having crowned his snowy locks with Aonian ivy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Teiresias, going with him, whirling his sluggish foot,","st":"45.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a Phrygian revel for Mygdonian Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went as companion with Cadmus as he darted into the dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"propping his aged forearm upon a god-fearing fennel-stalk."},{"sp":"N","t":"And beholding the two old men together with a slanting eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Teiresias and Cadmus, reckless Pentheus cried:","st":"45.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Cadmus, why do you rage? For what god do you rouse a revel?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus, lay down the ivy from your defiled hair,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lay down the fennel-stalk of mind-wandering Dionysus too;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lift the prudent bronze of Onca Athena."},{"sp":"N","t":"Childish Teiresias, garland-bearer, fling to the winds","st":"45.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"these leaves of your locks, a bastard crown; and instead of a thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift rather your Ismenian laurel of Phoebus."},{"sp":"N","t":"I respect your old age, and honor the grey lock"},{"sp":"N","t":"that witnesses your very years, measureless in life;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for if this old age and your hair did not restrain me,","st":"45.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"I would have wound your hands in fetters none can escape"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sealed you bound in a gloomy hall."},{"sp":"N","t":"Your mind has not escaped me; for in jealousy of Pentheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"you make a bastard god into a man with guileful prophecies,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having taken gifts from a deceiving Lydian man,","st":"45.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gifts of a river called rich in gold."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say that Bacchus found the wine-bearing harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine always draws drunken men toward Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wine rouses the mind of an unsteady man toward slaughter."},{"sp":"N","t":"But has he the body or the tunics of Zeus his father?","st":"45.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"Wearing golden robes, not fawnskins, high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashes among the blessed ones; and Ares fights against men"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a bronze spear, not lifting a wine-dark thyrsus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Apollo is not horned with an ox's horns."},{"sp":"N","t":"Did a river wed Semele, and did the bride bear","st":"45.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bastard horned son to an ox-horned consort?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: grey-eyed Pallas Athena comes to a male"},{"sp":"N","t":"fight, holding her kindred spear and shield. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you too stretch out the aegis of your father Cronides.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Pentheus so spoke, the prudent seer answered:","st":"45.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Why do you rout Dionysus, whom high-ruling Zeus plowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom father Cronides travailed with in a pregnant thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and god-bearing Rhea reared with child-tending milk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom before, half-finished, still breathing of his mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unburning sparks of the thunderbolt washed?","st":"45.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"He alone strives with sheaf-bearing Demeter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a fair-clustered harvest to answer her corn-ears."},{"sp":"N","t":"But guard against the anger of Bromius; and about impiety,"},{"sp":"N","t":"child, if you wish, I shall tell you a Sicilian tale."},{"sp":"N","t":"Once the sons of the Tyrrhenians sailed the sea,","st":"45.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"stranger-slayers, wandering mariners, snatchers of wealth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seizing the shore-side flocks of sheep from every side;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many an old sailor, half-dead, was rolled"},{"sp":"N","t":"this way and that from spear-won ships into a watery doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, a burly shepherd shielding his own flock,","st":"45.110","hvShift6":[[0,0],[15,14],[17,15],[24,20]]},{"sp":"N","t":"reddened his grey hairs with slaughter."},{"sp":"N","t":"If a merchant then sailed the sea, if ever a Phoenician"},{"sp":"N","t":"had for sale the sea-purple robes of the Sidonian deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the wandering Tyrrhenian, taking him upon the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was carried off unforeseen upon his wealth-flowing ships;","st":"45.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"and some man, having lost a boundless cargo unavenged,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Phoenician, was ferried in bonds to Sicilian Arethusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having left his city, without share of his plundered wealth."},{"sp":"N","t":"But Dionysus, changing his shape by a thievish guile,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beguiled the Tyrrhenians; and he put on a bastard form,","st":"45.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a lovely boy with unscored chin,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a golden-wrought adornment at his neck; and about his temple"},{"sp":"N","t":"was the self-moving gleam of a flashing garland"},{"sp":"N","t":"of unquenchable lychnis, and the green backs of emerald,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Indian stone, the sparkle of the gleaming sea;","st":"45.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he put upon his flesh robes brighter than the circling Dawn"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she is freshly scored, stained with the Tyrian shellfish."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he stood beside the brows of the shore, as though he too"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed to board a merchant ship. And they, leaping out,"},{"sp":"N","t":"took the bright, guile-weaving son of Thyone as plunder","st":"45.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stripped him of his possessions; and a running cord"},{"sp":"N","t":"was girdled about the hands of Dionysus drawn behind him."},{"sp":"N","t":"And suddenly the youth became great in a wondrous shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"man-formed, horned, lifted as high as Olympus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"piercing the shelter of the airy clouds; and with a fair-sounding","st":"45.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"throat he bellowed like a host of nine thousand."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the long cables became viperish trails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaped, breathing, into the curved backs of serpents;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the forestays hissed; and a wind-borne horned snake"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran up with trailing coils to the yard-arm of the mast;","st":"45.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the mast, shadowed with green leaves, a neighbor of the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was a topmost cypress; and in the mast-box"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-flying ivy went up, a neighbor of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plaiting a self-coiling cord about the cypress;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the rudders, peering above the sea,","st":"45.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bacchic vine-pole was weighed down with clustered fruit;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the stern was weighed down with sweet-drinking Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"a spring, gushing up wine, raved with drunkenness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And about all the benches, coming up through the prow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beasts grew; and bulls bellowed,","st":"45.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a lion roared a grim din from its throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Tyrrhenians cried out, and raved with madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"goaded into terror. And the wavy trails of the plant-increasing"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea spat out flowers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rose grew, and above, as in a garden,","st":"45.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"the foam-bearing hollows of the sea reddened,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lily sparkled in the surges. And as they gazed"},{"sp":"N","t":"their eyes raved in Bacchic frenzy at the lying meadows,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a deep-treed mountain appeared to them and a pasture of wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a chorus of country folk and the flocks of shepherds,","st":"45.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they thought they perceived the sound of a clear-voiced herdsman"},{"sp":"N","t":"making melody upon a shepherd's pipe,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, hearing the melody of clear, well-holed flutes"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they sailed high upon the middle of the endless deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"they thought they saw land; and beneath a wit-robbing madness,","st":"45.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting into the depth, they danced upon the calm,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-traveling dolphins; and as their faces changed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the race of men was shaped into a fishy nature."},{"sp":"N","t":"And do you too, child, beware the guileful anger of Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: I wear a valiant body, and I tend","st":"45.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread self-sown blood of the tooth-grown Giants."},{"sp":"N","t":"Flee the divine hand of Giant-slaying Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once beside the base of Tyrrhenian Pelorus"},{"sp":"N","t":"crushed Alpos, the god-fighting son of Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he fought with crags and hurled hills as spears;","st":"45.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cowering before the rank of throats of the maddened Giant"},{"sp":"N","t":"no wayfarer then trod that head of the rock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if anyone, not knowing, was carried along the untrodden road"},{"sp":"N","t":"lashing a bold horse, the son of Earth, perceiving him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entwined him with his wide-scattered hands above the crag","st":"45.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entombed driver and colt alike in his throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"And often, dragging them to pasture through a well-treed mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the old shepherd carved up his sheep as they rested at midday."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not then, sitting beside the goat-folds or beside the pens,"},{"sp":"N","t":"did music-tending Pan make melody with his gathered reeds,","st":"45.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did late-speaking Echo answer the crash of his harp;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, chatterer though she was, the silence of never-silent Pan"},{"sp":"N","t":"sealed her who sounded with his accustomed flute,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because the Giant then assailed all; not then did a herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did any chorus of woodcutters grieve the Nymphs of their own age","st":"45.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"by cutting ship-timbers, and no wise carpenter"},{"sp":"N","t":"bolted a timber-fixed merchantman, the wayfaring car of the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until Bacchus passed by those heads as he traveled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking his Euian thyrsi; and as Lyaeus passed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the cloud-high, vast son of Earth assailed him,","st":"45.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a rocky shield upon his shoulders;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had a crag for missile, and he leapt upon Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding a neighboring woody, high-running point,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling either a pine or a plane-tree at Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"He had a pine for club, and, whirling it like a swift sword,","st":"45.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted an olive bush by the root from its base."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he had emptied the mountains of their far-throwing hills,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the deep-treed ridge of the shadowy wood was laid bare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then thyrsus-mad Bacchus hurled his own shaft"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the mark with an accustomed whistle, and struck sky-high Alpos","st":"45.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"in his broad throat, and through the middle of his windpipe"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sharp-stretched, green Bacchic point darted;"},{"sp":"N","t":"there the Giant, pierced by a little sharp thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolled half-dead and fell into the neighboring deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"filling the whole deep-bosomed hollow of the sea;","st":"45.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"and raising its streams through the Typhaonian rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea deluged the hot backs of his brother's bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cooling a fiery body with a watery trail."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, child, beware, lest you too perceive the like,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the sons of the Tyrrhenians did, as the bold son of Earth.\"","st":"45.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and did not persuade him; and with fearless sandal"},{"sp":"N","t":"he went with Cadmus to the high-headed mountain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might take part in the dance. And to his iron-bearing warriors"},{"sp":"N","t":"helmet-glancing Pentheus cried, lifting his shield:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My servants, going through the city and the middle of the wood,","st":"45.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring me this feeble wanderer in heavy bonds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, struck by the alternating whips of Pentheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he may no longer charm women with a drugged drink,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but may bend his knee; and from the crags remove"},{"sp":"N","t":"my own child-loving mother Agave, raving in Bacchic frenzy,","st":"45.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"from the roaming, sleepless dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dragging her by the unveiled cluster of her raving hair.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Pentheus so spoke, his attendants ran with swift sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the hard-to-enter ridge of the high-tressed wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the tracks of mountain-wandering Dionysus.","st":"45.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely, beholding thyrsus-mad Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"near a lonely rock, the warriors rushed on;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they clamped thongs about the palms of Bromius,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wishing to cast bonds upon unconquered Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he was unseen, having darted off untouchable","st":"45.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"on his winged sandal, and in voiceless silence"},{"sp":"N","t":"the servants were enslaved by a divine necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning in terror the wrath of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"who could not be seen. And Bacchus, like a shield-bearer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taking an unyoked bull, gripped it by the horn with his hand,","st":"45.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though a servant of Pentheus threatening a lying"},{"sp":"N","t":"horned Dionysus, and, as with an angry face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came near maddened Pentheus, and as the raving king"},{"sp":"N","t":"sat, playing with his proud boast,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsmiling he cried a dread, thievish voice:","st":"45.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This is the man, scepter-bearer, who has goaded your Agave;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this is the man who desires the royal seat of Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but take the horned, guile-minded wandering Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bind in fetters none can escape the suitor of your throne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beware the head of ox-horned Dionysus,","st":"45.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he take you and strike you with his long-pointed horn.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Bromius so spoke, held fast by an intelligent madness"},{"sp":"N","t":"the god-fighter Pentheus cried a threatening word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bind him, bind this plunderer of my throne;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this man arms himself against my scepters, this man comes","st":"45.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"desiring the Cadmeian, ancestral seat of Semele."},{"sp":"N","t":"A fine thing for me, that Dionysus, whom a secret bed plowed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should have some man-formed bull as a partner of his honor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lighting up a bastard shape upon an ox-horned brow,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom perhaps Semele bore to a bull, after Pasiphae,","st":"45.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"joined to a horned, pasturing consort.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, and pressing the feet of the country bull"},{"sp":"N","t":"he clamped it in fetters none can escape; and taking it instead of Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he led it fettered near a horse's manger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though penning the bold son of Semele and not a bull.","st":"45.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanx of the Bassarids, entwined in a knot of hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he sealed bound in a moldering hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in some hollow cavity, a house of joyless necessity,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hard-to-escape imitation of the Cimmerians, without share of Dawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the revel-loving handmaids of Bromius, about whose squeezed","st":"45.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"palms thongs were girdled beneath the bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a bronze chain was sealed upon their feet."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the course of the swift-whirling dance came,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maenads danced; and a stormy Bacchant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirled restlessly with a dancing throb of her feet,","st":"45.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"shook off the trail of the unbreakable thongs, loosed again,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,1],[7,9],[20,13]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and with her palms rattled a free Euian echo"},{"sp":"N","t":"with well-rhythmed crashes; and beneath the whirl of her soles"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swelling, bronze-heavy chain of her feet was split."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a god-driven gleam crowned the gloomy house","st":"45.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Bassarids, dripping from the dark hall;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the gateways of the dark pit were unfolded"},{"sp":"N","t":"of their own accord; and astonished with a trembling leap of their soles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the guards hurried into flight at the roaring of the Bassarids"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the savage foam of their teeth. And they, fleeing,","st":"45.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"bent a homeward step to the ridge of the lonely wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one carved a herd of cattle with her hide-piercing"},{"sp":"N","t":"thyrsus, and defiled her hands with gore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"splitting the rough covering of a bull with her nails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another with a blood-red cluster cut apart","st":"45.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbreakable flocks of woolly sheep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another killed goats; and the crags were reddened"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the bloody drops of gore from the cut-down flock."},{"sp":"N","t":"And another, snatching a three-year-old child from its father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood lifting it aloft above her shoulders, fearless,","st":"45.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken, unbound, the boy that was the care of the tempests,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seated, laughing, and not falling into the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the boy asked the Bacchant for milk, as though she were his mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and handled her breasts; and the breasts of the unwedded girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushed up a self-moving milky moisture.","st":"45.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"And spreading open her shaggy tunics for the hungry child"},{"sp":"N","t":"she held out the freshly flowing teat to his infant lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maiden sated the boy with an unaccustomed dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and many nursed the children of a freshly delivered,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaggy-breasted lioness, removed from their mother.","st":"45.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"Another struck the thirsty ground with a sharp thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beating the freshly split top of the mountain; and the rocky"},{"sp":"N","t":"crag purpled, belching self-perfected wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as milk was poured from the beaten rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the springs whitened with self-poured streams.","st":"45.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"Another cast a serpent against an oak; and about the tree"},{"sp":"N","t":"the snake wheeled its coil, and became a wandering ivy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling the trunk with a coiling, crooked trail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"imitating the knot of serpents entwined about it."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a Satyr was carried along, bringing a grinning beast,","st":"45.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"a threatening tiger seated high upon his back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a savage temper and not touching its bearer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and old Silenus, dragging a boar by the tips of its jaws,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurled the sharp-toothed beast into the air in play;"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, with a stormy mounting throb of his feet,","st":"45.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"was suspended untouchable upon the crest of a camel;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, leaping upon its back, rode a bull."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things were among the crags; but in lyre-built Thebes"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus showed varied wonders to all the citizens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the women raved in Bacchic frenzy with tottering feet &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>","st":"45.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and foam-tressed lips; and all Thebes was whirled about,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the streets hurled forth flaming sparks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"all the foundations were shaken, and as from the throats of oxen"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unbending gateways of the halls bellowed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the unshaken house roared with tumult,","st":"45.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring a self-moving echo with a stony trumpet."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Dionysus cease from anger; and he bellowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"a divine, air-roaming sound from his throat"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the rim of the seven-pathed stars, like a raving bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, routing maddened Pentheus with a witnessing torch,","st":"45.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"he filled the whole house with a sparkle; and about the walls"},{"sp":"N","t":"opposite, a many-cleft leaping fire blazed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushing down with a kindled spark; and about the purple"},{"sp":"N","t":"robes and the breast of the sea-cloaked king"},{"sp":"N","t":"a coiling torch was carried, and did not burn his tunics;","st":"45.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with divided beams, torn away, with a hot leap,"},{"sp":"N","t":"from foot to the middle of his back, through his loins to the top of his spine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"migrant gleams ran about the neck of Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, with a dancing throb of self-traveling fire,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wondrous radiance spat unburning sparks","st":"45.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the well-spread bed of the earth-born king."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the self-coiling radiance Pentheus roared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bade his servants bring defending water,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that they might quench the kindled flame of the torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the house with evil-averting streams;","st":"45.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the water of the hollow cavities appeared, laid bare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the spring, great though it was, had its stream dried up"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the river was drawn off in numberless vessels."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the labor was useless and the water in vain."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the fire, struck by the liquid drops, grew","st":"45.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"with hotter beams; and as from many bulls"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a sound beneath the roof of resounding bellowing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the court of Pentheus crashed with thunders hidden within."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLVI"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Look besides upon the forty-sixth, where you shall perceive the head of Pentheus and child-destroying Agave."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when the bold king knew that, their bonds","st":"46.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"loosed of their own accord from his iron-bearing hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maenads had rushed off as migrants to the ridge of the wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and knew the shifting guile of Dionysus whom none could see,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Pentheus surged, unsteady, with an insolent anger;","st":"46.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing him present again, his locks girdled"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the accustomed ivy, and beholding the unplaited"},{"sp":"N","t":"trail of his long hair loosed from above his shoulders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shrieked out such a word with a raving throat:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Sweet is he who sends deceiving Teiresias to me;","st":"46.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"your seer cannot beguile my mind;"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell this to others. How did the goddess Rhea hold out"},{"sp":"N","t":"her breast to a son not Zeus, and rear the son of Thyone?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ask the helmet-glancing cave of the Dictaean cliff,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how Zeus increased his strength, and did not draw the milk of Rhea;","st":"46.15","hvShift6":[[0,4]]},{"sp":"N","t":"ask the Corybants too, where once the boy was at play,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[42,46]]},{"sp":"N","t":"milking the child-rearing breast of the goat Amaltheia.","hvShift6":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"You too have taken the ways of your guileful mother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cronides burned lying Semele with the thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"beware, lest Cronides subdue you too after your mother.","st":"46.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not keep a barbarian race either; first-born"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ismenus begot me, and liquid Hydaspes did not bear me;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not know Deriades and I am not called Lycurgus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But with your Satyrs and raving Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave the streams of Dirce, and, if you wish, with your thyrsus","st":"46.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill among the Assyrians another, younger Orontes."},{"sp":"N","t":"You are not the Olympian race of Cronides; for the lightnings"},{"sp":"N","t":"cry aloud the reproaches of your mother as she perished,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the thunderbolts are witnesses of a secret bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Rainy Zeus did not burn Danae after the bed,","st":"46.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, carrying off Europa the unshaken sister of my Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he guarded her, and did not hide her in the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know indeed that a heavenly flame destroyed you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unborn infant still, after your mother was burnt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and loosed the half-finished bastard travail of a withering birth;","st":"46.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if it did not subdue you, because you are guiltless"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the earthly wedlock, of your mother's secret love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded, as you say, and unwillingly shall call you the child"},{"sp":"N","t":"of heavenly Zeus, and not one burnt by the thunderbolt."},{"sp":"N","t":"And do you teach me this with a true, witnessing word:","st":"46.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"when did father Zeus bring forth Phoebus or Ares from his thigh?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If you have got the blood of Zeus, go to the circle of Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dwell in the upper air; leave Thebes as fatherland to Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"You should have told another fitting, blameless tale,"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixing mind-charming Persuasion with a cunning lie,","st":"46.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"that Cronides bore you from his accustomed child-bearing head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"perhaps that word would not have been so unbelievable, that after Pallas"},{"sp":"N","t":"he bore Bacchus too from an unwedded head."},{"sp":"N","t":"I could wish, if you had got an Olympian race — would that"},{"sp":"N","t":"high-ruling Cronion had begotten you, so that, pursuing the blood of Zeus,","st":"46.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"I might conquer Dionysus, being called the son of Echion.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As he spoke the god was indignant and answered with a word,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding the mass of a divine threat beneath his heart:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I count blessed the land of the Celts that bears barbarian ordinances,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the Rhine, judging the pure travail of new infants,","st":"46.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"the law-tender of an unmarked birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"knows how to convict the bastard race of an unknown blood."},{"sp":"N","t":"But I am not judged by the paltry streams of the river"},{"sp":"N","t":"called Rhine, but thunderbolts are heralds"},{"sp":"N","t":"more trustworthy to me than streams;","st":"46.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not seek a better witness than the lightning, Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let the Galatian trust in water, but do you trust the witnessing torch."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of the earthly hall of Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the house of Dionysus is his father's upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if there were a judging between earth and starry Olympus,","st":"46.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me as I ask, which would you yourself call the better,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the seven-zoned heaven or the land of seven-gated Thebes?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have no need of the earthly hall of Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Only honor the honey-dripping flower of my harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not dishonor the drink of vine-clad Dionysus.","st":"46.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not fight against Indian-slaying Bromius, but, if you can,"},{"sp":"N","t":"make war against one woman, a man-breaking Bacchant."},{"sp":"N","t":"Perhaps the prophetic Fates set you a fair name,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the forerunner of your own death; and it is no cause for blame"},{"sp":"N","t":"that dread-suffering Pentheus, bearing the earth-born blood","st":"46.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a ground-reared father, should bear the likeness of the Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor is it a cause for blame that Bacchus, having the Olympian"},{"sp":"N","t":"blood of the race of Zeus, should bear the likeness of his Giant-slaying father."},{"sp":"N","t":"Ask Teiresias with whom you are angry; ask Pytho"},{"sp":"N","t":"who lay beside Semele, who plowed the child of Thyone.","st":"46.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you wish to learn the rites of dance-delighting Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leave your royal robes and endure, Pentheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to wear a woman's dress, and become a female Agave;"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest the women dart past you as you hunt."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you stretch beast-slaying bows in your hand,","st":"46.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus will praise you for hunting together with your mother."},{"sp":"N","t":"Strive with Bacchus alone, and, if it is lawful, with the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may call you the lion-slayer after Actaeon."},{"sp":"N","t":"Lay down these weapons; my women kill"},{"sp":"N","t":"iron-bearing warriors with uncorseleted hands;","st":"46.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if they conquer you, adorned in armor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in an unarmed female battle, what citizen would praise"},{"sp":"N","t":"a man who gasped his life away in a womanish fighting?"},{"sp":"N","t":"A Bassarid does not tremble at a winged shaft, does not flee a spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, covering your unknown face with a hidden guile,","st":"46.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall see all the rites of dance-weaving Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he persuaded him, since, lashing the man's mind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shook him, held fast by the leap of a roaming madness; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Mene, fighting beside Bromius, assailed Pentheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a divine whip; and, joining with Lyaeus,","st":"46.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"the raging, bold sting of wit-robbing Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showing varied-shaped phantoms to maddened Pentheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turned the dread son of Echion from his former purpose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crashed upon the tottering hearing of Pentheus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beating out the avenging din of a divine trumpet;","st":"46.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"and terrified the man. And Pentheus came into his house"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad, longing for the rites of revel-loving Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he opened the fragrant chests, where lay the women's"},{"sp":"N","t":"sea-purple robes of the Sidonian deep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he put upon his flesh the dappled-backed robe of Agave;","st":"46.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he clamped the veil of Autonoe upon his locks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdling his royal breast with a circling art;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he clamped his feet in women's sandals;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in his hand he lifted a thyrsus; and as he went after the Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dappled tunic trailed along his tracking sole.","st":"46.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"And with mimic feet coiling Pentheus danced,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweetly mad; and with slanting sandal he rattled the floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting foot after foot; and making his two hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"womanish he whirled them in turn with a twofold throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a woman playing at the dance; and, beating out","st":"46.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"a twice-sounding harmony with a rattle of unequally yoked bronze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he let his wandering lock loose to the airy breezes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a Euian Lydian melody. Indeed you might have said"},{"sp":"N","t":"you saw a raving Bacchant reveling wildly."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he beheld two suns and two Thebes;","st":"46.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he thought to heave the gateway of seven-pathed Thebes"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid upon his unwearied shoulders from above."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the citizens wheeled crown-wise about him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one holding a wheeling hill of earth, another high-visible"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon a rock, another, propping his forearm upon a man's shoulder,","st":"46.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted his step, fixing his fingers upon the ground;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one went after a sharp-pointed mass of the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another was upon a jutting battlement, another, watching,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretched a slanting eye from the high-crested towers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, winding his hands crown-wise about the middle of a rail,","st":"46.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"went up climbing a column with the tips of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at Pentheus shaken with a leap of madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting a thyrsus and darting his veil."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already he had run past the walls of seven-pathed Thebes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the gateways opened with self-moving hinges;","st":"46.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"and already before the city, shaking his locks into the air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he had passed the delicate streams of serpent-feeding Dirce;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and whirling a dance-beating step with raving foot"},{"sp":"N","t":"he held a journey behind the vine god."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he came to the place where the oaks were, where the dances,","st":"46.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the revel-loving rites of Bromius, where the unsandaled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fawn-driving hunt of the Bassarids itself was,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then vine-clad Bacchus, within the mountain thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeing an ancient fir, a vast tree of the same height"},{"sp":"N","t":"as a neighboring rock, rejoiced, beneath whose bush","st":"46.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloud-neighboring hills were shadowed with leaves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, pressing down the topmost cluster with unsparing hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the ground, to the ground Pentheus drew, stretched down to the earth, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"the high-flying branch, and, clamping its bearer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gripped with his hand the clusters stretched on high,","st":"46.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, whirling his feet this way and that, turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady Pentheus was lifted in a dancer's fashion."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then the dancing Seasons came to the Bassarids;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they called to one another, and girded themselves with their robes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cast fawnskins about them; and mountain-roaming Agave","st":"46.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"shrieked out a cry with foam-tressed lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Autonoe, let us hasten to where the dance of Lyaeus is"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the mountain-roaming crash of the accustomed flute is heard,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may weave a Euian-loving melody, so that I may learn"},{"sp":"N","t":"who shall be first to set up a dance for Dionysus,","st":"46.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"who shall conquer whom in making sacrifice to Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"You delay, danceless one, and Ino has come before us;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer does she hold the sea as a migrant, but she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"has come running from the brine with water-traveling Melicertes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has come shielding Dionysus as he is pursued,","st":"46.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest lawless Pentheus bear down upon Lyaeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Initiates, to the crags; come, Ismenian Bacchants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let us set up the rites, and in an emulous dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"let us contend with the Lydian Bassarids, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'The Maenad Agave has conquered the Mygdonian Mimallon.'\"","st":"46.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she gazed at her god-fighting son seated"},{"sp":"N","t":"high upon the tree, the mother at her son, as at a wild lion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she showed him to the raving Bacchants as they gathered;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she called her son an intelligent beast with a raving voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the women wheeled crown-wise about him","st":"46.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he sat among the leaves; and with a well-handled bond"},{"sp":"N","t":"they embraced the tree, and wished to cast to the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shoot together with Pentheus; and clamping about the bush"},{"sp":"N","t":"the trail of her yoked palm, with an earth-shaking throb"},{"sp":"N","t":"Agave tore up the tree by the root from its base.","st":"46.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the plant fell to the ground; and Cithaeron was laid bare;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the bold king, self-coiling, with a dancing throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolled head first from the air and fell — Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And then the madness of mind-tottering Dionysus left him,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he had his former wits a second time; and upon the ground,","st":"46.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"having his doom near, he uttered a plaintive voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Hamadryad Nymphs, hide me, lest my child-loving Agave"},{"sp":"N","t":"subdue me with child-slaying palms."},{"sp":"N","t":"My mother, ill mother, hold back your unsoftened madness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"whence do you call me, your son, a beast? What shaggy breast","st":"46.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"do I bear? What roaring do I send forth?"},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer do you know me whom you reared, no longer do you see;"},{"sp":"N","t":"who has snatched your wits and your eye? Farewell, Cithaeron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"farewell, these trees and mountains; be safe, Thebes;"},{"sp":"N","t":"be safe you too, dear child-slaying mother Agave.","st":"46.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"Look at this freshly bearded chin, look at my human"},{"sp":"N","t":"shape; I am no lion; you do not behold a beast."},{"sp":"N","t":"Spare your own travail, pitiless one, spare your breasts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you gaze at me, Pentheus, whom you reared. Cease, my voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keep back your words; Agave does not hear.","st":"46.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"But if you kill me, doing favor to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"subdue your son alone, heavy-groaning one, and do not let"},{"sp":"N","t":"your son be subdued by the bastard palms of the Bassarids.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he entreated, and Agave did not hear."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the grim women rushed upon him","st":"46.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"with emulous hands; and as he rolled in the dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"one drew off his hind feet, and another, taking hold,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tore his right hand up by the root, and Autonoe"},{"sp":"N","t":"pulled at the left; and the mother, led astray,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fixed her foot upon her son's breast, and as he lay","st":"46.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"she cut through his bold neck with a sharp thyrsus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and swift-kneed she ran up with the bloody joy of madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and showed the bloody head to joyless Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and glorying in the grace of her hunt of a lying lion"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shrieked out such a word with a raving throat:","st":"46.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Blessed Cadmus, I call you more blessed; for among the crags"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis watched Agave playing the champion"},{"sp":"N","t":"with uncorseleted hands, even though she is mistress of the hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding her jealousy of your lion-slaying daughter;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Dryads marveled at my labor; and the father","st":"46.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"of our Harmonia, bronze Ares, helmeted with his accustomed lance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"marveled at your ironless daughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she hurled the thyrsus, the crusher of lions,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorying; but do you, Cadmus, call Pentheus here,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mounter of your thrones, so that with envious eyes","st":"46.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"he may gaze upon a woman's beast-slaying sweats."},{"sp":"N","t":"My servants, go, and beside the gateway of Cadmus"},{"sp":"N","t":"fix this head, the offering of my victory."},{"sp":"N","t":"Never did my sister Ino kill so great a beast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Autonoe, look, and bend your neck to Agave;","st":"46.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"for you have not got a boast like mine, and I have shamed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the still-spoken-of victory of the mother of Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your mother-in-law, lion-slaying Cyrene.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, lifting the dear weight; and hearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the alien-minded threat of his glorying daughter,","st":"46.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus answered, mixing his words with tears, in a mourning voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"What an intelligent beast you have subdued, child Agave!"},{"sp":"N","t":"What a beast you have subdued, whom your own belly bore!"},{"sp":"N","t":"What a beast you have subdued, whom Echion sowed!"},{"sp":"N","t":"Look at your lion, whom, lifting him while still little —","st":"46.245","hvShift6":[[0,0],[23,36],[36,42]]},{"sp":"N","t":"look at your lion, whom Harmonia your mother —","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,1]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Cadmus used to raise in his child-tending, rejoicing arm;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[11,9],[17,14],[24,20],[29,27]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and she often lifted him, and held out her breast for him to milk.","hvShift6":[[0,8],[12,25]]},{"sp":"N","t":"You seek your child as a beholder of your toils;"},{"sp":"N","t":"how shall I call Pentheus, whom you lift in your palms?","st":"46.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"Look at your beast, and you will perceive your son."},{"sp":"N","t":"Whom you killed unknowing — whence shall I call your child?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Fair are the nursing-gifts you bring, Dionysus, to your Cadmus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"fair was the marriage of Harmonia that Cronion gave me;"},{"sp":"N","t":"these things are worthy of Ares and of heavenly Aphrodite.","st":"46.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ino has the sea, Cronion burned Semele,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Autonoe mourns a horned child, and ah, greatly wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Agave has killed the only son she bore, untimely."},{"sp":"N","t":"And my Polydorus labors, a wanderer without a fatherland."},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone am left, a breathing corpse; to whom shall I flee,","st":"46.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"with Pentheus perished and Polydorus gone?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What alien city will receive me? Perish, Cithaeron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have killed the tenders of Cadmus's old age, and you hold"},{"sp":"N","t":"both — Pentheus a corpse, and you cover Actaeon.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"As Cadmus so spoke, pouring his lament like a spring,","st":"46.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Cithaeron wept with fountain tears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the oaks mourned, and the Naiad Nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"cried their dirges. And Dionysus respected the grey hair"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Cadmus and his groaning; and mixing a tear"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the laughter of a griefless face, he changed the mind of Agave,","st":"46.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"and made her intelligent again, so that she might groan for Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, her mind turned about and her gaze unbelieving,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stood for a time, self-rooted, voiceless, the mother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gazing at the head of Pentheus dead"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fell self-rolling, and upon the floor, wretched,","st":"46.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"she rolled in the poured dust, shaming her locks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she flung the shaggy tunics from her breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the revel-loving bowls of Bromius, reddening"},{"sp":"N","t":"her breast with a trail of blood and the fold of her uncovered bosom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she kissed her son's eye and the green circles of his face","st":"46.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the graceful locks of his reddening head;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wailing sharply she uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Pitiless Dionysus, unsated of your own race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"give me my former madness again; for now I have another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a worse madness, a prudent one; give me that","st":"46.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"witlessness, so that I may call my son a beast a second time."},{"sp":"N","t":"I thought to strike a beast; and instead of a lion's crest"},{"sp":"N","t":"I lift the freshly cut head of Pentheus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed is heavy-weeping Autonoe, because she groaned"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Actaeon dead, and the mother did not kill her son;","st":"46.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"I alone have become a child-slayer; my migrant Ino"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not kill Melicertes or Learchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the father subdued the son he plowed. Ah, greatly wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Zeus lay beside Semele, so that I might groan for Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"father Zeus bore Dionysus in his own thigh,","st":"46.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might wipe out the whole Cadmeian race."},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious, Dionysus — he has destroyed the whole race of Cadmus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But after the bridal feast of a god-invoked table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the bed of Harmonia, after the chamber of my Cadmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let Apollo himself, shaking his ancient lyre again,","st":"46.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"strike one dirge for both Autonoe and Agave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing shrill of swift-fated Pentheus and Actaeon."},{"sp":"N","t":"Dear boy, what drug is there for my grief?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet have I lifted the bride-adorning fire in your chamber;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not heard the wedding-hymn of your yoking Loves;","st":"46.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"what child of yours shall I see as comfort? Would that another"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchant had crushed you, and not much-toiling Agave."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not blame your maddened mother, ill-fated Pentheus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"blame Bacchus rather; Agave is guiltless."},{"sp":"N","t":"My hands, dear boy, drip with your dew","st":"46.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"from your reaped neck; and from your self-poured head"},{"sp":"N","t":"your blood has reddened your mother's whole tunic."},{"sp":"N","t":"Yes, I entreat you, give me the cup of Bromius; for instead of wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"I pour the gore of my Pentheus as libation to Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"For you, untimely one, I who love tears raise a tomb","st":"46.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"with my own hands, having hidden your headless body"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the dust; and upon your grave I shall score this:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'I am the corpse of Pentheus, wayfarer; the child-tending belly"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Agave bore me, and a child-slaying hand killed me.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, raving with a wise mind; and as she mourned","st":"46.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"Autonoe, lamenting, cried a comforting voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I have jealousy and longing for your misery, Agave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because you embrace the sweet face of Pentheus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and his mouth and dear eye and the ends of your son's hair."},{"sp":"N","t":"Sister, I count you blessed, even though you killed your son as a mother;","st":"46.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"for instead of Actaeon, from a shape that was changed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I wept for a fawn, and instead of my son's head"},{"sp":"N","t":"I gave burial to the long, bastard horn of a deer."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there is little comfort for your pain, that of your son"},{"sp":"N","t":"in death you did not see another form, not a fawn's hair,","st":"46.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you lifted no useless hoof or horn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I alone looked upon my son as a bastard corpse, and wailed"},{"sp":"N","t":"over a strange-natured, dappled, speechless image of a shape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I am called the mother of a deer and no longer of a son."},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you, maiden-loving daughter of Zeus, honoring","st":"46.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"Phoebus the father of my husband Aristaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"change my mortal-seeming shape into a deer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do a favor to Apollo; and after Actaeon give wretched"},{"sp":"N","t":"Autonoe too as food to those same whelps"},{"sp":"N","t":"or to your hounds; and Cithaeron shall behold","st":"46.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mother after the son torn by dogs; and do not, wretched as I am,"},{"sp":"N","t":"yoke me, wearing the horn-drawing shape equal to that of your deer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to your car, lashing me wildly."},{"sp":"N","t":"Farewell, plant of Pentheus, farewell, pitiless Cithaeron;"},{"sp":"N","t":"farewell also, fennel-stalks of wit-robbing Dionysus;","st":"46.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"be safe for me, mortal-delighting Phaethon; shine upon the hills;"},{"sp":"N","t":"shine for both, for the daughter of Leto and for Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if with your beams you know how to subdue men too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"strike with your pure fire both Autonoe and Agave;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and be the avenger of Pasiphae, so that you may laugh,","st":"46.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"grieving Aphrodite the mother of Harmonia.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and child-destroying Agave mourned the more."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the dear mother entombed the corpse she had killed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing up a tearful spring from her face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the citizens built a well-made tomb.","st":"46.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"So they groaned downcast; and seeing it"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus pitied them, and checked the lamentation-loving"},{"sp":"N","t":"women as they mourned, since to each in turn"},{"sp":"N","t":"he mixed a toil-releasing drug with honey-sweet wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave a Lethaean drink; and he soothed the mourning","st":"46.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"lament of Cadmus with a healing word;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he lulled both Autonoe and Agave,"},{"sp":"N","t":"showing the first-announcing oracles of a hope to come."},{"sp":"N","t":"And to the Illyrian land, to the country of the western sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he escorted both as wanderers, Harmonia leaving her fatherland","st":"46.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"as companion of her age-mate Cadmus, to whom creeping time"},{"sp":"N","t":"granted to have a stony, snaky shape."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having Satyrs and Pans, and lashing lynxes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate Bacchus reveled into never-silent Athens."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLVII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Go to the forty-seventh, where Perseus is, and the doom of Icarius, and soft-tunicked Ariadne."},{"sp":"N","t":"And already Rumor flew this way and that through the city,","st":"47.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-shouting messenger that cluster-rich Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"had come to the Attic land; and for unsleeping Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athens, blessed in childbirth, raved in Bacchic frenzy into the dance."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a great revel roared; and the citizens, gathered together,","st":"47.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"cloaked the streets with cunning garments"},{"sp":"N","t":"in their wide-scattered hands; and with the leaves of the vines"},{"sp":"N","t":"of plant-increasing Bacchus Athens was girdled"},{"sp":"N","t":"of its own accord; and the women girded bowls"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon their iron-bearing breasts, upon their mystic bosoms,","st":"47.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"and maidens danced, and crowned their temples,"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding their Attic hair about with the flower of ivy."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Ilissus whirled its breathing water about the city,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorifying Dionysus; and in an emulous dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"the banks of Cephisus struck up a Euian melody.","st":"47.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"And a planting rose, and from the earthly bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"a self-grown cluster of sweet ripening travail"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddened olive-bearing Marathon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the oaks whispered, and as their petals opened"},{"sp":"N","t":"the meadow Seasons belched forth the two-colored rose,","st":"47.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the hills midwifed the self-perfected lily."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the flute of Athena crashed together with Phrygian flutes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Acharnian reed, squeezed by the palms,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sang shrill a twofold din; and from throats of one tongue"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bacchant of the city, heavy-thundering, sounding together","st":"47.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"with an unyoked Mygdonian girl, cried a twice-sounding harmony,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning her forearm upon a young Pactolian bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted up a night-danced flame with a twin pine-torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"for first-born Zagreus and later-born Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and remembering Itylus and loom-toiling Philomela","st":"47.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Attic nightingale of the shifting throat cried out with them,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the chattering bird of Zephyrus poured her melody beneath the roof,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having flung all memory of Tereus to the storms."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor was any man without a dance through the city. But rejoicing"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus came to the house of Icarius, who was better","st":"47.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"than other country folk at planting trees of every kind."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the old vine-dresser danced with country feet"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he beheld Dionysus arriving, and entertained"},{"sp":"N","t":"the lord of fair-planted vines at a little table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Erigone mixed the drink, drawing goats' milk;","st":"47.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Bacchus checked her, and to the tender-loving old man"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave skins pregnant with toil-releasing drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and holding in his right hand a fragrant cup of sweet wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"he held it out to Icarius; and greeted him with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Receive this gift, old man, which Athens has not known.","st":"47.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"O old man, I count you blessed; for the citizens will sing of you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a word: that Icarius found glory"},{"sp":"N","t":"to convict Celeus, and Erigone Metaneira."},{"sp":"N","t":"I have jealousy of the elder Demeter, because Deo too"},{"sp":"N","t":"gave the rich corn-ear to another tiller of the soil.","st":"47.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"Triptolemus found the corn-ear, and you the wine-dark grape of harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"gracious one, you alone strive with heavenly Ganymede,"},{"sp":"N","t":"more blessed than the elder Triptolemus; for corn-ears"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not loose heart-devouring cares, but the wine-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"clusters are the healers of human grief.\"","st":"47.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he told him, and to the hospitable old man"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave a delicate cup full of mind-rousing wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the old planter, the vine-dresser, drank one cup after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an unsated sting for the fair-dripping dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the girl, drawing a pouring of wine instead of milk,","st":"47.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"held out the cup with her hand, until she made her father drunk."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when he found surfeit of the cup-receiving table,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slanting, coiling about, whirling a much-slipping step,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine-dresser skipped up with alternating feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a Euian hymn of Zagreus for Dionysus.","st":"47.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the plant-tending god gave the country old man"},{"sp":"N","t":"clustered vine-shoots, the Euian-loving gifts of the table;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the lord taught him by a plant-increasing art"},{"sp":"N","t":"to prune and to dig trenches and to set the shoots in the pits."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the old planter, the vine-dresser, bringing the gifts","st":"47.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Bromius and the vine harvest to other country folk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taught them the wine-planting husbandry of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and casting a measureless stream of wine into a pastoral mixing-bowl"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gladdened the feasters with cup after cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unfolding the fragrant pouring of the wine-receiving skins.","st":"47.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, drinking the stream of sweet, mind-rousing wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"soothed the father of Erigone with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Tell me, old man, whence have you found the nectar of Olympus on earth?"},{"sp":"N","t":"You do not bring yellow water from Cephisus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you do not carry honey-sweet gifts from the Naiads;","st":"47.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"for springs do not gush up honey-flowing streams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor is the stream of Ilissus reddened with a poured trail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this is not the drink of the shoot-loving bee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"which brings the sharpest surfeit to mortals; but strange-natured"},{"sp":"N","t":"you bring a water sweeter than sweet honey;","st":"47.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Attic olive does not bear this ancestral drink;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have a drink pleasanter than milk, steadfast always,"},{"sp":"N","t":"than the gathered drops of a honey-mixed posset."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if the rosy-armed Seasons knew how to bring"},{"sp":"N","t":"a drink for mortals from the cups of plant-increasing gardens,","st":"47.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too would have called this the springtime drink"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Adonis or of Cytherea, the fragrant dew of roses."},{"sp":"N","t":"You bring a toil-releasing and strange drink; for it has scattered"},{"sp":"N","t":"my wandering cares to the airy winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"Did immortal Hebe give you a gift from the upper air?","st":"47.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did your city-holding Athena bring you this?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who snatched from heaven the mixing-bowl from which Ganymede"},{"sp":"N","t":"draws and mixes the cup for Zeus and the immortals?"},{"sp":"N","t":"More blessed than hospitable Celeus, did you too"},{"sp":"N","t":"entertain a gracious dweller of Olympus from heaven?","st":"47.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that another god revelled into your hall,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave this drink of your friendly table as a gift"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the Attic land through his dinner, as Deo gave the corn-ear.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, marveling at the sweet drink; and from his lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweetly mad, he shouted, pouring out a country song.","st":"47.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the country folk, drawing cup after cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"all raved in Bacchic frenzy, their wits taken by the wine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and their eyes wandered, and with the unmixed-wine-loving cups"},{"sp":"N","t":"their white cheeks purpled, and the breasts of the tillers"},{"sp":"N","t":"were warmed, and their temples were weighed down with the drink,","st":"47.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the veins of their swelling heads surged;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as they gazed the bosom of the field was shaken"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the oaks danced and the cliffs skipped;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and full of unaccustomed wine with its tottering drops"},{"sp":"N","t":"a man fell self-rolling on his back upon the ground.","st":"47.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the chorus of country folk, shaken by a murderous sting,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran down upon wretched Icarius with a raving madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as though he had mixed the guile of a drugged wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"one holding an iron ox-axe, another arming"},{"sp":"N","t":"his hands with a mattock, another lifting a corn-cutting","st":"47.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"sickle, another raising a vast stone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another goaded, stretching out a crook in his hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the old man; and one, taking a whip nearby,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pierced the body of Icarius with a flesh-cutting goad."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring, the old planter, the vine-dresser, fell to the ground","st":"47.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck with clubs, and skipping against the table"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck the mixing-bowl of drunkenness, and rolled half-dead"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the pouring of gleaming wine; and as his head grew heavy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"struck by the alternating blows of the country folk,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bloody dew reddened the wine of the same color.","st":"47.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"And scarcely, a neighbor of Hades, he cried a word from his lips:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Wine of my Bromius, the rest of mortal care,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet to all but pitiless to me alone; for you gave gladness"},{"sp":"N","t":"to all men, and to Icarius you gave doom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"sweet to Erigone, but a foe; for grief-less","st":"47.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus has made my girl a girl of grief.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had his word ceased; but doom came before his voice."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he lay there a corpse, far from his prudent daughter,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with eyes spread open. And upon an unspread bed on the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"the murderers slept a sweet sleep above the floor,","st":"47.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy with wine, like corpses; and waking"},{"sp":"N","t":"they groaned for the man they had killed unknowing; and lifting"},{"sp":"N","t":"the corpse high upon their shoulders they carried it up to the ridge of the wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"with intelligent hearts, and in a fair-watered stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"they cleansed his wounds beside a mountain-poured spring;","st":"47.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the murderers with man-slaying palms entombed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the freshly cut corpse they had killed in a witless madness."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the soul of Icarius rushed, wholly like smoke,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the house of Erigone; and the light phantom"},{"sp":"N","t":"of a shadowy dream-vision was matched to a mortal shape,","st":"47.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"wholly like the freshly wounded man, and the wretched thing had"},{"sp":"N","t":"a spotted tunic, the herald of an unmarked murder,"},{"sp":"N","t":"reddening with blood and squalid with dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"torn with the blows of alternating iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he stretched out his palms; and he showed the wounds","st":"47.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"of his freshly slaughtered limbs for the girl beside him to see."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden wailed within her lamentation-loving dreams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"when she saw so many wounds of his head, when the wretched girl saw"},{"sp":"N","t":"the freshly flowing gore of his reddening throat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the shadowy father spoke a word to his girl of grief:","st":"47.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Wake, wretched one, and seek your father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"wake, and search out my drunken murderers;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am your heavy-suffering father, whom for the sake of wine"},{"sp":"N","t":"the grim country folk destroyed with iron."},{"sp":"N","t":"O child, I count you blessed; for you did not hear","st":"47.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"the clang of your slain father's battered head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you did not see his grey hair reddening beneath the gore,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor the freshly cut corpse gasping in the dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you did not behold the father-slaying clubs; but a god"},{"sp":"N","t":"kept you away from your father, and guarded your gaze,","st":"47.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest you should behold the doom of your father cut down."},{"sp":"N","t":"Look at my tunics purpling with blood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for yesterday the country folk, made drunk with cup after cup,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gushing out the moisture of unaccustomed Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wheeled about me; and as I was cut down with iron","st":"47.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"I called the shepherds, and they did not hear my cry;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only late-speaking Echo heard my din,"},{"sp":"N","t":"groaning for your father with answering dirges."},{"sp":"N","t":"No longer, lifting your crook out of the middle of the wood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you come to the flowery pasture and the meadows,","st":"47.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"feeding your herd with a rustic husband;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[23,28],[24,32],[29,33],[30,37]]},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer, handling your tree-tending mattock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you bring channeled water to your garden of fair childbirth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, unsated of my honey-dripping harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"weep for me your father fallen; and I shall behold you","st":"47.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"living an orphan, untried in weddings.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking the winged vision of the dream ran past."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the girl, waking, tore her rosy cheeks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scraped her unbending breasts with mourning nails,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tore up by the root the cluster of her long hair;","st":"47.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"and seeing the cattle still standing beside the rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden in grief roared with a plaintive voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Where is the corpse of Icarius? Dear hills, speak;"},{"sp":"N","t":"accustomed bulls, tell the doom of my father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"who are the murderers of my slain father?","st":"47.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"Where has my sweet father gone? Does he wander,"},{"sp":"N","t":"teaching a neighbor the new shoots of the fair-planted harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sitting beside the country folk, or does he stay"},{"sp":"N","t":"with some tree-tending herdsman, sharing his hearth at a feast?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me as I mourn, and I shall endure until he comes.","st":"47.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"If my father still lives, I shall water the shoots"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the garden again, living together with my father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if my father is dead and plants trees no more,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall behold an equal doom upon my perished father.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she ran swift-kneed to the ridge of the wood,","st":"47.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the tracks of her freshly slaughtered father."},{"sp":"N","t":"But no bold goatherd told her as she asked, nor beside the thickets"},{"sp":"N","t":"did any herdsman of the flock, pitying the maiden,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell the unsteady track of her unheralded father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did an old shepherd show her the corpse of Icarius;","st":"47.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"but she wandered in vain; and scarcely a vine-dresser found her"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cried the ill news with plaintive lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and showed her the nearby tomb of her freshly slain father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden, hearing, raved with a prudent madness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and tearing her locks she laid herself beside the dear tomb,","st":"47.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden unveiled, unsandaled, and she had her tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"washed with ever-flowing, self-poured tears."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she sealed silence upon her voiceless lips"},{"sp":"N","t":"for a time; and an intelligent dog, the companion of Erigone,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went with the girl of grief, whimpering a mourning whine,","st":"47.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"and mourned with her as she mourned. And raving"},{"sp":"N","t":"she ran up into a high-headed tree; and about the tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping her neck entwined in a throttling bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl perished aloft with a self-slaying whirl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and died, and had a willing doom; and about the girl","st":"47.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"— she shaking both her feet with a dancing throb —","hvShift6":[[0,6]]},{"sp":"N","t":"the dog was shaken thickly, and cried a mourning echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring intelligent tears from a beast's eyes."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did the dog leave the lonely girl unguarded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but stayed beside the tree, driving off any beast that came,","st":"47.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"a leopard or a lion; and to the wayfarers as they passed"},{"sp":"N","t":"it showed with voiceless nods the unyoked girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"entwined in throttling bonds high upon the tree."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they, pitying her, went up into the tree of the wood"},{"sp":"N","t":"on the tips of their feet, and from the fair-leaved clusters","st":"47.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought down the unwedded maiden; and they hollowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"the earth close at hand with ground-digging mattocks."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with them the dog too labored with a prudent heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and deepened the ground with mourning, skilled paw,"},{"sp":"N","t":"scoring the surface of the poured earth with whetted claws.","st":"47.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the wayfarers gave funeral rites to the freshly cut corpse;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and each, holding the mass of a common grief beneath his heart,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran up to his own work with swift foot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the dog alone stayed beside the neighboring tomb"},{"sp":"N","t":"for love of Erigone, and perished by a willing doom.","st":"47.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Zeus the father pitied them; and in the starry circle"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fixed Erigone beside the back of the Lion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the country maiden holds a corn-ear; for she did not wish"},{"sp":"N","t":"to lift the wine-dark cluster, the slayer of her father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leading old Icarius as companion of the girl beside him","st":"47.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"into the star-roaming vault, he named him bright"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bootes, who touches the Wagon of the Arcadian Bear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he made the flashing Dog, darting down upon the Hare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fiery star, where about the circle of Olympus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sea-borne Argo sails in a starry pattern.","st":"47.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things Achaean tale has molded, mixing"},{"sp":"N","t":"its accustomed persuasion with a lie; but the truth is that high-ruling Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"assigned the soul of Erigone as yokemate to the star"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the heavenly Maiden of the corn-ear, and beside the airy"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dog he set a dog like it in the pattern of its shape,","st":"47.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sirius, whom they call the autumnal, and the air-roaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"soul of Icarius he joined to Bootes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And these things Cronides granted to the wine-planted Attic land,"},{"sp":"N","t":"preparing one honor both for Pallas and for Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the honey-flowing streams of Ilissus","st":"47.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"delicate Bacchus reveled to the vine-clad rim of Naxos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about him bold Eros shook his wings, and as Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"came to his marriage Cytherea led the way."},{"sp":"N","t":"For lately, leaving her sleeping upon the shores,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pitiless Theseus sailed away from the maiden who had left her fatherland,","st":"47.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"and entrusted his covenants to the winds. And seeing"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ariadne sleeping and lonely, Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"mixed love with wonder; and to the dance-weaving Bacchants"},{"sp":"N","t":"he spoke a guarded word with an astonished tongue:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bassarids, do not shake your rattles, let there be no sound","st":"47.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"of foot or pipe; let Cypris sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but she has no cestus, the token of the Cyprus-born."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that a Grace is wedded to guileful Sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but since dawn has shone and Dawn appears near,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wake Pasithea as she sleeps; who beside Naxos,","st":"47.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"who has cloaked the unclothed Grace? Can it be Hebe?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But to whom has she left the cup of the blessed ones? Can gleaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"Selene, the driver of oxen, be lying beside the sea?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And whence does she sleep apart from her accustomed Endymion?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do I behold silver-footed Thetis upon the shores?","st":"47.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"But she has not a naked rosy body. If it is lawful to say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Naxian archer-goddess rests from the labors of the hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having wiped off her beast-slaying sweats in the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for labor always brings forth sweet sleep; but in a thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"who has seen Artemis in a trailing tunic? Wait, Bacchants.","st":"47.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"Stand, Maron; do not dance here; cease your singing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dear Pan, lest you scatter the morning sleep of Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to whom has Pallas left her spear? And who lifts"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bronze helmet or the aegis of Tritogeneia?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such things Bacchus said; and from the sand the wretched,","st":"47.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"unhappy-loving girl woke, scattering her sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and did not see the fleet nor her deceiving husband;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the Cydonian bride groaned with the halcyons,"},{"sp":"N","t":"haunting the shores, the heavy-roaring bride-gift of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she called the youth by name; and she raved beside the sea,","st":"47.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the merchant ship; and she was angry at jealous sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and blamed the sea, the mother of the Paphian, far more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she entreated Boreas, and called the wind to his oath,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to his oath by Oreithyia, that he might bring the youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"back to the land of Naxos, and she might see the sweet ship a second time;","st":"47.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"she asked uncharmed Aeolus rather; and to her prayer"},{"sp":"N","t":"he was persuaded and nodded, and sent a counter-blowing"},{"sp":"N","t":"wind to breathe; but Boreas, unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cared nothing for the desire-stricken girl; but the very breezes"},{"sp":"N","t":"were perhaps jealous and angry at the maiden,","st":"47.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"they who then carried the ship to the Attic land. And Eros"},{"sp":"N","t":"himself marveled at the maiden, and in griefless Naxos"},{"sp":"N","t":"he thought he beheld Aphrodite mourning;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she was brighter even in her pains, and grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"adorned her as she sorrowed; and to lamenting Ariadne","st":"47.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"laughter-loving Aphrodite yielded a little in the judging,"},{"sp":"N","t":"smiling lovely, and the eyes of Persuasion yielded,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and of the Graces and of Eros, to the lovely tears of the girl."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at length, weeping, she uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Sweet sleep came to me, while sweet Theseus went away;","st":"47.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"would that he had left me still in my delight! And in my sleep","hvShift6":[[0,0],[13,37],[21,51],[24,53],[30,56]]},{"sp":"N","t":"I saw Cecropia, and within the court of Theseus"},{"sp":"N","t":"there was a delicate wedding-hymn as Ariadne was sung of,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a dance, and my own delighted hand adorned"},{"sp":"N","t":"the altar of the Loves, blooming with springtime leaves;","st":"47.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I had a bridal garland; and Theseus was near me"},{"sp":"N","t":"in bridegroom's garments, sacrificing to Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas, what a sweet dream I saw; but fleeing me"},{"sp":"N","t":"he went away, leaving me still a maiden; be gracious, Persuasion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"these things the gloomy, bride-adorning darkness gave me,","st":"47.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and jealous light-bearing Dawn snatched them all away;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and waking I did not find my longing. Are then the very"},{"sp":"N","t":"images of answering Loves jealous,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because I beheld the lovely, deceiving vision"},{"sp":"N","t":"of marriage-perfecting dreams, and lovely Theseus fled?","st":"47.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"To me even dear Sleep is hostile; tell me, rocks,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me who am unhappy in love: who has snatched the citizen of Athens?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If Boreas should blow, I come to Oreithyia;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Oreithyia is angry at me, because she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"bears the blood of Marathon, whence dear Theseus came.","st":"47.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"If Zephyrus stirs, show to the Zephyrian bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to Iris the mother of Desire, Ariadne forced;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if Notus, if bold Eurus, I come to the morning-born,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming the mother of the roaring winds, unhappy in love."},{"sp":"N","t":"Give me again the empty, dear favor, Sleep, sending","st":"47.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"another lovely dream like that one, so that I may behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweet, deceiving bed of Cypris in sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"only linger upon my eyes, so that I may perceive"},{"sp":"N","t":"the breathless sting of Eros in dream weddings."},{"sp":"N","t":"If the snatching breezes carried your voyage from Naxos","st":"47.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"to the Attic land, thievish bridegroom Theseus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell me as I ask, and I go at once to Aeolus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming the jealous and unholy winds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if he set me, who left my fatherland, upon lonely Naxos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the pitiless sailor sailed while you knew nothing,","st":"47.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"he has sinned against Theseus and against Themis, against Ariadne;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let this sailor never again see an escorting wind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor let calm Melicertes behold him graciously"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he rides with unsteady tempests;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but let Notus blow when there is need of Boreas;","st":"47.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"let him find Eurus when he wants Zephyrus; and when the springtime"},{"sp":"N","t":"winds blow for all sea-travelers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then let him alone keep company with a wintry sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"This lawless sailor has sinned; but I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"erred in longing for the citizen of prudent Athens.","st":"47.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that I, unhappy in love, had not longed for him; for toward the Paphian"},{"sp":"N","t":"Theseus was as savage as he was lovely;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not tell me this while he still shook my thread;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he did not tell me this beside our labyrinth."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that the pitiless bull had killed him — cease, my voice,","st":"47.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"from witlessness, do not kill the sweet youth; alas for the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Theseus sailed alone to Athens blessed in childbirth."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know why he has left me; perhaps he has a love"},{"sp":"N","t":"of one of the maidens sailing with him, and he dances in Marathon"},{"sp":"N","t":"at another wedding, another's, while I still walk in Naxos.","st":"47.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"Naxos is my bridal chamber, thievish bridegroom Theseus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have lost both father and bridegroom; alas for the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I do not see Minos, and I do not behold Theseus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have left my Cnossus, and I have not seen your Athens;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am robbed of father and fatherland; ah, greatly wretched,","st":"47.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"the water of the sea is the bride-gift of my love; to whom shall I flee?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What god will snatch me up and carry me to Marathon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ariadne, pleading her case against Cypris and Theseus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who will take me and carry me through the swell? Would that I too"},{"sp":"N","t":"might see another thread as the escort of my road;","st":"47.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too wish to have such a thread, so that I may escape"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swell of the Aegean sea and cross to Marathon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may embrace you, even if you hate Ariadne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that I may embrace you, my oath-breaking consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"Receive me as chamber-servant of your bed, if you wish;","st":"47.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I, Ariadne, shall spread your bed after Crete, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a spear-won woman; and for your most blessed bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall endure, as a handmaid, to weave the loud-clattering loom"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to lift an unaccustomed pitcher on jealous shoulders,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to bring after-dinner water to sweet Theseus;","st":"47.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"only let me see Theseus. My own mother too once"},{"sp":"N","t":"served country folk, and bent her neck to a herdsman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and conversed with a pasturing, speechless bull,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bore a bull to an ox; and when a herdsman made melody"},{"sp":"N","t":"she had no longing for the harp, but only to hear bellowing.","st":"47.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"But I shall not touch a crook, nor stand beside"},{"sp":"N","t":"a manger; I shall be near my mistress"},{"sp":"N","t":"while Theseus speaks, and I shall not hear bellowing;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall sing the lovely wedding-hymn of your marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hiding my jealousy of your freshly yoked bride.","st":"47.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"Halt beside the Naxian shores as you sail,"},{"sp":"N","t":"halt your ship for me. Why, sailor, do you too vex me?"},{"sp":"N","t":"So then you too are of Marathon; if you come"},{"sp":"N","t":"to your lovely land, where the house of the Loves is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive me, wretched, so that I may see the city of Cecrops;","st":"47.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"but if you leave me, pitiless one, and sail on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell your Theseus of lamenting Ariadne,"},{"sp":"N","t":"blaming the unaccomplished, thievish oath of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know why heavy-wrathful Eros made the promise"},{"sp":"N","t":"of deceiving Theseus unaccomplished; for instead of Hera,","st":"47.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom they call the Yoker, he swore a marriage oath"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the undefiled, unwedded goddess Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"he swore the oath of Pallas; what has Pallas to do with Cytherea?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus delighted, hearing her lament;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he learned of Cecropia and knew the name of Theseus","st":"47.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the deceiving voyage from Crete; and near the girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"he flashed, wearing an inspired shape; and the maiden"},{"sp":"N","t":"roaming, furious Eros lashed toward another, better longing"},{"sp":"N","t":"with his goading cestus, so that he might yoke the daughter"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Minos, persuaded, to his brother Dionysus.","st":"47.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"And comforting lamenting Ariadne, unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus spoke such a word with his mind-charming voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maiden, why do you groan for the deceiving citizen of Athens?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave the memory of Theseus; you have Dionysus as consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an imperishable husband instead of a short-lived one; and if the mortal","st":"47.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"body of a youth of your own age delights you, never does Theseus"},{"sp":"N","t":"strive with Dionysus in valor and beauty."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will say: he reddened the dweller of the ground-dug labyrinth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the two-natured man yoked with a bull;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you know your thread was his helper; for the club-bearing","st":"47.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"citizen of Athens would not have found a contest to strive in,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not a rosy-skinned woman defended him; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Paphian and of Eros and of the distaff of Ariadne."},{"sp":"N","t":"You will not say that Athens is greater than the upper air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Minos your father was not wholly like all-ruling Zeus;","st":"47.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"Cnossus is not like Olympus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did this fleet depart from my Naxos in vain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Desire guarded you for a better wedding;"},{"sp":"N","t":"blessed are you, because, leaving the worse bed of Theseus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you shall behold the couch of lovely Dionysus.","st":"47.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"What greater boast did you wish for? For both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you have heaven for a house, and Cronion is your father-in-law."},{"sp":"N","t":"Cassiepeia shall not be able to match you"},{"sp":"N","t":"through her daughter's Olympian adornment; for Perseus gave"},{"sp":"N","t":"Andromeda airy bonds even among the stars;","st":"47.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I shall make you a starry crown, so that you may be called"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gleaming bedfellow of garland-loving Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, comforting her; and the girl leapt up with joy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having flung all memory of Theseus to the sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having received the promise of a wedding with a heavenly","st":"47.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"suitor. And Eros adorned the bridal chamber for Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a wedding dance crashed out; and about the chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"all flowers bloomed; and with springtime leaves"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dancers of Orchomenus wheeled about Naxos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a Hamadryad sang shrill of the chamber, and beside the springs","st":"47.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unveiled, unsandaled Naiad Nymph praised"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ariadne joined to the clustered god;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Ortygia wailed for joy, and striking up a bridal hymn"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Lyaeus the brother of her city-holding Phoebus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she skipped into the dance, unshaken though she is.","st":"47.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"And crowning a wheeling flower with purple roses"},{"sp":"N","t":"fiery, prophetic Eros plaited a garland of the same color as the stars,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the forerunner of the heavenly Crown; and about the Naxian"},{"sp":"N","t":"bride the bride-adorning swarm of the Loves skipped."},{"sp":"N","t":"And keeping company in the yoking chamber of the wedding-hymn","st":"47.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"the consort Chrysopator sowed a many-childed offspring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And rolling the long whirl of grey time"},{"sp":"N","t":"he remembered his mother Rhea, blessed in childbirth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leaving blameless Naxos full of the Graces"},{"sp":"N","t":"he went after all the cities of Hellas; and near horse-pasturing","st":"47.475"},{"sp":"N","t":"Argos he came, even though Hera had got Inachus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And they did not receive him, but pursued the dance-weaving"},{"sp":"N","t":"women and the Satyrs, and refused the thyrsi,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ever Hera should harm the Pelasgian seat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous, heavy-wrathful, bearing down upon Lyaeus;","st":"47.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they checked the old Sileni. And in grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus goaded all the Inachian women to frenzy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Achaean women shouted with bellowing; and they assailed"},{"sp":"N","t":"those they met at the crossroads; and the wretched women whetted"},{"sp":"N","t":"knives against their own freshly born infants,","st":"47.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one drew a sword and killed her son as a mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another crushed her three-year-old offspring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one hurled into the air a wandering boy"},{"sp":"N","t":"still seeking his dear milk; and Inachus"},{"sp":"N","t":"raved at the doom of the freshly born infants as they perished;","st":"47.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a mother killed her son, and there was no longing for the child-tending"},{"sp":"N","t":"breasts, no memory of the necessary birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Asterion, where many first-fruits of the greater youth"},{"sp":"N","t":"of young men were shorn, the flowers of the hair-shedding head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"received the boys themselves and no longer the locks of their hair.","st":"47.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"And one, seeing some servant of Lyaeus as he came,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a citizen of the Pelasgian field, told such a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This is the cluster-bearer, a two-natured race; Argos, worthy of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"has Perseus and has no need of Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have another son of Zeus and do not want Bacchus.","st":"47.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"With much-skipping feet Dionysus treads the harvest;"},{"sp":"N","t":"with high-traveling steps my offspring cuts the air."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not match ivy with a sickle; for Perseus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sickle-bearer is better than thyrsus-bearing Bacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if he killed the Indian host, I shall call the contest equal","st":"47.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"for Gorgon-slaying Perseus and Indian-slaying Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if beside the western clime of the much-washed sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fixed a stony Tyrrhenian merchant ship in the deep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"my Perseus turned a whole vast sea-monster to stone."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if your Dionysus saved sleeping Ariadne","st":"47.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the shores beside a lonely sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winged Perseus loosed the bonds of Andromeda,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having as a worthy bride-gift the petrified beast of the sea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"never did Perseus rescue Andromeda for the sake of the Paphian,"},{"sp":"N","t":"never his own bride longing for a Theseus;","st":"47.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he got a prudent marriage. And the fiery thunderbolts"},{"sp":"N","t":"did not turn Danae to ash, as they did Semele;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the father of Perseus, the Olympian rain of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came golden to the marriage, and not a flaming consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"I never admire this champion; for in his palm","st":"47.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"what furious spear of Ares does he hold? Hold back, Perseus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not fight with your Gorgon-slaying sickle against womanish ivy;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not defile your hand with women's buskins;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not shake the helmet of Hades upon your temples"},{"sp":"N","t":"against a vine garland; but if you wish,","st":"47.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"arm Andromeda against uncorseleted Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Withdraw from me, Dionysus, and leaving horse-pasturing Argos"},{"sp":"N","t":"drive the women of seven-gated Thebes to frenzy again;"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill a new Pentheus; what has Perseus to do with Dionysus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Refuse swift-flowing Inachus; and let the slow river","st":"47.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Aonian Thebes receive you; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"of heavy-kneed Asopus, still seething with the thunderbolt.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he told, laughing at Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Pelasgian Hera armed the Argive phalanx;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she was like the seer Melampus; and in anger","st":"47.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"she broke into a warlike voice to Gorgon-slaying Perseus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Offspring of a heavenly race, helmet-glancing Perseus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift your sickle, lest with some unwarlike thyrsus"},{"sp":"N","t":"feeble women wipe out your Argos;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not tremble at one single serpent, the girdle of his hair,","st":"47.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"since your blood-red, beast-slaying reaping-hook"},{"sp":"N","t":"mowed the cornfields of so many serpents of Medusa;"},{"sp":"N","t":"arm yourself against the phalanx of the Bassarids; and remember"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bronze-roofed maiden-chamber, where through the bosom of Danae"},{"sp":"N","t":"rainy Zeus poured a marriage-thieving golden shower,","st":"47.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest Danae after her bed, after her golden wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"should bend a slave's knee to paltry Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"show that you bear the true blood of Cronion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"show that you have a golden race, and proclaim"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bed of your heavenly, wealth-holding snowfall;","st":"47.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"and make war on the Satyrs; and as Lyaeus arms himself"},{"sp":"N","t":"stretch out the bloody eye of serpent-haired Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the bitter king of much-washed Seriphos"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall behold another new Polydectes of stone."},{"sp":"N","t":"With you all-subduing Argolic Hera arms herself,","st":"47.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"the stepmother of Bromius; and shielding Mycenae"},{"sp":"N","t":"lift your city-saving sickle, so that I may behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ariadne following Perseus as a spear-won woman;"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill the rank of ox-horned Satyrs; and with a Gorgon eye"},{"sp":"N","t":"change the mortal gaze of the Bassarids","st":"47.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"into a self-perfected image like them; and adorn your streets"},{"sp":"N","t":"with an answering stony beauty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"making varied statues for the Inachian assemblies."},{"sp":"N","t":"Why do you tremble at Dionysus, whom the bed of Zeus did not plow?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, what could he do to you? When will a footman","st":"47.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"upon the floor overtake a winged one, an aloft air-roamer?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, heartening him; and Perseus flew into the fray."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the Pelasgian trumpet roared, calling the dwellers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one lifted the spear of Lynceus the spearman,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another that of elder Phoroneus, another of Pelasgus,","st":"47.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"another raised in his hand the Abantian ox-hide shield"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the ash-spear of Proetus, and another man lifted"},{"sp":"N","t":"the quiver of Acrisius, and another stood bold in the fray"},{"sp":"N","t":"holding the sword of Danaus, which once, lifting it bare,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he armed his daughters with for a man-slaying wedding,","st":"47.575"},{"sp":"N","t":"another was gripping a great axe, which beside the altar"},{"sp":"N","t":"Inachus, the inspired sacrificer of city-holding Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"used to stand lifting, the cutter of oxen's foreheads."},{"sp":"N","t":"And a tumult-rousing host upon high-footed horses"},{"sp":"N","t":"ran after Perseus as he fought; and one stood beside them,","st":"47.580"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending the shout of battle from rough lips,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a foot-soldier, and fitted his bows to a circling string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted a hollow quiver above his back;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Perseus the sickle-bearer was the champion of the Argives,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he clamped his feet in airy sandals,","st":"47.585"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lifted the head of Medusa whom none may look upon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Iobacchus armed his loose-haired women"},{"sp":"N","t":"and horned Satyrs; and he raved in Bacchic frenzy in the tumult"},{"sp":"N","t":"when he saw the airy journey of the winged champion;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in his hand he lifted a thyrsus, raising before his face","st":"47.590"},{"sp":"N","t":"adamant, a stone petrified by the shower of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the defender against stone-eyed Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that he might flee the hostile gleam of the face none may look upon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing the phalanxes of the Bassarids and the emblems of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"helmet-glancing Perseus laughed dreadfully and said:","st":"47.595"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Sweet is he who holds a thyrsus, a green shaft, and coming against me"},{"sp":"N","t":"arms himself with paltry leaves, playing at Ares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you have got the blood of Zeus, show forth your race;"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you have the golden Pactolian water of a river,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have gold for a begetter, and my father is rainy Zeus;","st":"47.600"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold the reddening foundations of the maiden-chamber,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still bearing those remains of the wealth-flowing snowfall."},{"sp":"N","t":"But flee glorious Argos, since battle-steadfast Hera"},{"sp":"N","t":"has got these seats, the destroyer of your mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she make you, the goader, also the goaded,","st":"47.605"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I behold you at last sharpened again with madness.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he fought in the front; and all-subduing Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"arming Ares and the reaper of Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"terrified the Bacchants; and darting down upon Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"an imitation of lightning, a god-driven leaping fire,","st":"47.610"},{"sp":"N","t":"she hurled a light-bearing, gleaming lance at Bromius."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laughing Dionysus answered with a raving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You have no such flashing, ironless point;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you cannot rout me, even if you have got a fiery spear;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor does the lightning of Zeus harm me; for the thunderbolts","st":"47.615"},{"sp":"N","t":"washed Bacchus while he was still a half-finished infant,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pouring an unburning breath upon unharmed Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And do you too, boastful sickle-bearing Perseus, cease;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this is no battle of a lesser Gorgon, no single bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"Andromeda in heavy bonds is the contest; but you bring","st":"47.620"},{"sp":"N","t":"a fight against Lyaeus, who has the race of Zeus, to whom alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhea once held out a life-bringing breast, whom once"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gentle flame of a bridal lightning midwifed with its torch,"},{"sp":"N","t":"at whom the west and the Dawnstar marveled, to whom the ranks of Indians"},{"sp":"N","t":"yielded, trembling at whom both Deriades and Orontes","st":"47.625"},{"sp":"N","t":"fell, having the measureless likeness of sky-high Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to whom bold Alpos sank down, the son of Earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a vast, cloud-neighboring body, to whom bends the knee"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Arab people, and the Sicilian sailor still sings"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the bastard sea-running shape of the Tyrrhenians, whose human","st":"47.630"},{"sp":"N","t":"form I once changed and turned about, and instead of men"},{"sp":"N","t":"dancing fishes skip upon the sea."},{"sp":"N","t":"And you have heard the lament of seven-gated Thebes; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"of dread-maddened Pentheus and child-destroying Agave;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you need no rumor or witness that your Argos","st":"47.635"},{"sp":"N","t":"has made trial of Lyaeus, and that the Achaean women themselves"},{"sp":"N","t":"still groan for their own travail."},{"sp":"N","t":"But fight, friend, and perhaps you will praise Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he hurls his clusters, because you shall see the wings"},{"sp":"N","t":"of your sandals yielding to my unbreakable buskins;","st":"47.640"},{"sp":"N","t":"never shall you scatter the battle of the Bassarids, never shall I cease"},{"sp":"N","t":"sending my wine-dark thyrsus, until I show your throat in Argos"},{"sp":"N","t":"pierced by an ivy spear"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your sickle conquered by leaves; my Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall not save you, nor the grey-eyed one your kinswoman, nor your Hera,","st":"47.645"},{"sp":"N","t":"however angry she may be at battle-steadfast Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but I shall kill you, and boastful Mycenae"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall behold the reaper of Medusa reaped;"},{"sp":"N","t":"or, clamping you in a chest with a greater bond,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall hurl you afloat a second time upon your accustomed sea;","st":"47.650"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you wish, set foot again at last upon your Seriphos."},{"sp":"N","t":"And if you boast greatly about your golden race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"have paltry golden Aphrodite as your fellow-contender.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he fought in the front; and the Bacchants made war,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the Satyrs fought. And above the head of Bromius","st":"47.655"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perseus flew aloft, darting his light wings;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Iobacchus, heightening his body, a neighbor of the upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wingless, high-traveling, was lifted with a greater sole"},{"sp":"N","t":"higher than flying Perseus, and to the seven-pathed"},{"sp":"N","t":"upper air he brought his hand, and kept company with Olympus,","st":"47.660"},{"sp":"N","t":"and squeezed the clouds; and Perseus was whirled with fear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the untouchable right hand of Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"touching the sun and laying hold of the moon."},{"sp":"N","t":"But leaving Dionysus he fought against the raving Bacchants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shaking in his palm the death-bearing eye of Medusa","st":"47.665"},{"sp":"N","t":"he made Ariadne stone as she armed herself."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bacchus roared the more, seeing his bride of rock;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now he would have sacked Argos and thrown down Mycenae"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reaped the whole rank of the Danaans, and now he would have wounded"},{"sp":"N","t":"unwoundable Hera herself as she fought unknown","st":"47.670"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the bastard mortal-seeming shape of the answering seer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and now swift-sandaled Perseus would have perished beyond his doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had not Hermes, appearing behind him on winged sandal,"},{"sp":"N","t":"taken him by his golden lock and reined him back,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and soothed him with an evil-averting, friendly word:","st":"47.675"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"True blood of Zeus, bastard of jealous Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"you know how I saved you from the Zeus-fallen torches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and gave you to the Nymphs, the daughters of the river Lamos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"while you were still a child, and lifting you again in my hands"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought you to the house of your child-rearing Ino;","st":"47.680"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do you, bringing a favor to your rescuer the son of Maia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lull the kindred battle, brother; for both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perseus and Dionysus are the offspring of one father;"},{"sp":"N","t":"do not blame the host of the Argives, do not blame the sickle of Perseus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for he does not arm himself for war willingly; but Hera","st":"47.685"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed him, and in the shape of the seer Melampus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fights openly; but do you withdraw and leave the fight,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest ill-devising Hera bear down upon you again."},{"sp":"N","t":"But you will speak of the doom of your wife; she has died"},{"sp":"N","t":"fighting, by a glorious doom; and you ought to count","st":"47.690"},{"sp":"N","t":"Ariadne blessed in her death, because she found so great a slayer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"born of a heavenly and not of a mortal stock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reaper of the sea-monster and of horse-bearing Medusa;"},{"sp":"N","t":"the threads of the Fates obey no one; for heavenly"},{"sp":"N","t":"Electra died, the bedfellow of Zeus, and she herself,","st":"47.695"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sister of your Cadmus, Europa, wedded to Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went away after her Olympian bed, and your own"},{"sp":"N","t":"mother perished still bearing your birth in her belly;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Semele did not cross the gates of Olympus before her doom,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but when she had received it. And your bride, in her perishing,","st":"47.700"},{"sp":"N","t":"shall come to the star-roaming heaven, and shall appear"},{"sp":"N","t":"near our Maia of the seven-pathed Pleiad."},{"sp":"N","t":"What more dear did she wish than to shine upon the earth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dwelling in the upper air, Ariadne after Crete?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you lay down your thyrsus, and leave Enyo to the winds,","st":"47.705"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set the self-perfected image of earthly Ariadne"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the image of heavenly Hera stands."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not sack the city where the blood of your parents is,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but honor the floor of your own ox-horned Io,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lulling your thyrsus; and hereafter you shall praise","st":"47.710"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Achaean women, since they shall set up an altar"},{"sp":"N","t":"of bull-faced Hera and of your fair-chambered bride.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a word he told, and leaving horse-pasturing Argos"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came again to the vault, having mixed for both"},{"sp":"N","t":"an ordinance of like-mindedness, for Perseus and for Dionysus.","st":"47.715"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Argolic Hera stay there for long;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but changing her bastard mortal-seeming shape"},{"sp":"N","t":"she went back to Olympus, having her wondrous form again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And old Melampus spoke to the Inachian phalanx,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the blood of first-born Lynceus, of god-fearing Pelasgus:","st":"47.720"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Obey the seer, and shake for wine-dark Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shake the bronze rattles and the Euian drums of Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he wipe out the whole Inachian race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after the infant children he destroy the youths too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest after the doom of the children he kill the women;","st":"47.725"},{"sp":"N","t":"but make a god-delighting sacrifice to Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"and to Zeus, and dance for Perseus and for Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he persuaded them; and the peoples gathered together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking up a night-danced song for Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set up the rites; and in a god-invoking dance","st":"47.730"},{"sp":"N","t":"the rattles clattered, and the soles beat time,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and torches blazed; and the citizens, gathered together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"anointed their cheeks with mystic white gypsum;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the drums clattered, and as the bronze was beaten"},{"sp":"N","t":"a twice-sounding din roared; and the altars reddened","st":"47.735"},{"sp":"N","t":"from bulls slaughtered in rows, one after another,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and unspeakable flocks were killed; and at the gleaming altar"},{"sp":"N","t":"the men soothed Bacchus and the women propitiated him;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a female cry crashed out an air-roaming melody,"},{"sp":"N","t":"exchanging it for a life-ransoming revel; and the Inachian","st":"47.740"},{"sp":"N","t":"maenads flung their mind-forgetting madness to the winds."},{"sp":"HDR","t":"Book XLVIII"},{"sp":"SEC","t":"Seek in the forty-eighth the blood of the Giants, and behold Pallene and the child of sleeping Aura."},{"sp":"N","t":"But he, mounted upon the rail of his leopard chariot,","st":"48.1"},{"sp":"N","t":"reveled roaming through the Thracian field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving the horse-pasturing floor of first-born Phoroneus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Inachian Hera soften her renewed anger"},{"sp":"N","t":"when Argos was goaded, but, keeping the memory","st":"48.5"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the madness of the Achaean women, she armed herself again against Bacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she made guileful prayers to Earth the mother of all,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying aloud the works of Zeus and the manhood of Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"who had destroyed the measureless cloud of earth-born Indians;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and when the life-bringing mother heard that the son of Semele","st":"48.10"},{"sp":"N","t":"had wiped out the swift-fated Indian race,"},{"sp":"N","t":"remembering her children she groaned the more; and against Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she armed the mountain-running tribes of the self-begotten Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"goading her high-crested sons into the tumult:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"My children, fight against cluster-bearing Dionysus","st":"48.15"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sky-high crags, and overtake the destroyer","hvShift6":[[0,0],[3,2],[6,4]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of my race, the Indian-slaying son of Zeus; and let me not see"},{"sp":"N","t":"a bastard scepter-bearer of Olympus ruling with Zeus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Bind, bind Bacchus, so that he may be a chamber-servant"},{"sp":"N","t":"when I grant Hebe in marriage to Porphyrion","st":"48.20"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cytherea to Chthonius, when I sing the grey-eyed one"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the bedfellow of Enceladus and Artemis of Alcyoneus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring me Dionysus, so that I may vex Cronion"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he beholds the slavery of spear-won Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"or, wounding him with crushing iron,","st":"48.25"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill him for me, wholly like Zagreus, so that someone may say,"},{"sp":"N","t":"either a god or one of mortals, that Earth in anger"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed twin murderers against the offspring of Cronides,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the elder Titans against the earlier Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the younger Giants against the later-born Dionysus.\"","st":"48.30"},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she goaded the whole rank of the Giants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanxes of the earth-born marched into the tumult."},{"sp":"N","t":"One, holding a Nysaean foundation, another, having hollowed"},{"sp":"N","t":"a cloud-high, torrent-cut flank with iron,"},{"sp":"N","t":"armed himself against Dionysus, hurling crags as spears;","st":"48.35"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, a rocky hill of the sea-based field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"another, having broken off the ridge of a sea-girdled isthmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hastened to the fray. And with measureless arms"},{"sp":"N","t":"Peloreus hurled high-headed Pelion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laying bare the hollow house of Philyra; and as the unroofed","st":"48.40"},{"sp":"N","t":"crag was snatched away, old Chiron was whirled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"man-formed, half-finished, entwined with an age-mate horse."},{"sp":"N","t":"And holding a cluster of vines, the destroyer of Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus ran down upon high-crested Alcyoneus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having no furious spear, lifting no bloody sword,","st":"48.45"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he cut the wide-scattered hands of the Giants,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling coiling shoots; and with an unmixed-wine-loving leaf"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread tribes of ground-reared serpents were cut apart;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as the viper-haired heads of a Giant were struck"},{"sp":"N","t":"the reaped necks danced in the dust.","st":"48.50"},{"sp":"N","t":"And unspeakable tribes were killed; and as the Giants were cut down"},{"sp":"N","t":"rivers of ever-flowing blood ran, and with freshly poured"},{"sp":"N","t":"purple surges the gullies reddened."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanxes of the earth-born raved in Bacchic frenzy,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fearing the serpent locks of snaky-haired Dionysus.","st":"48.55"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Bacchus fought with fire, hurling into the air a brand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyer of his adversaries; and along a high-traveling road"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Bacchic, self-coiling, leaping flame ran on,"},{"sp":"N","t":"darting down upon the Giants with a limb-devouring spark;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one, a half-burnt serpent, bearing the radiance upon its threatening","st":"48.60"},{"sp":"N","t":"throat, hissed with a fire-heated gullet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spitting out smoke, and hurling no deadly venom."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there was unspeakable tumult; and above the heads of his adversaries"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus was suspended, lifting a warlike brand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and warmed the bodies of the Giants with an earthly hurricane,","st":"48.65"},{"sp":"N","t":"the answering imitation of the Zeus-hurled thunderbolt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the torches blazed; and above the head of Enceladus"},{"sp":"N","t":"a wandering torch was whirled, warming the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but it did not subdue him, and Enceladus did not bend his knee"},{"sp":"N","t":"at the vapor of earthly fire, since he had been guarded against the thunderbolt.","st":"48.70"},{"sp":"N","t":"And measureless Alcyoneus leapt upon Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"helmeted with Thracian crags; and against Bacchus"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lifted the cloud-high ridge of wintry Haemus"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward a useless mark, unwoundable Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he hurled the watch-peak; and touching the unbreakable","st":"48.75"},{"sp":"N","t":"fawnskin of Lyaeus the hills were split apart;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a new Typhoeus, high-visible, wholly like the earlier one,"},{"sp":"N","t":"laid bare the heads of Emathia — he who once lifted"},{"sp":"N","t":"many torn hollows of his mother the field,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hurling rocky missiles at Dionysus.","st":"48.80"},{"sp":"N","t":"And drawing the sword of one gasping upon the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"lord Bacchus armed himself against the Giants' heads,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shearing the snaky cornfields of their venom-shooting locks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and cutting the self-perfected host with an unarmed hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"he fought raging, holding the long-leaved ivy","st":"48.85"},{"sp":"N","t":"that mounts green trees, the hurler at Giants."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he would have killed them all with his man-breaking thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he withdrew willingly from the fight, turned back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping his foes alive for his own father."},{"sp":"N","t":"And now he would have run swiftly to Phrygia with hurrying foot,","st":"48.90"},{"sp":"N","t":"but another contest checked him, so that among so many"},{"sp":"N","t":"dead he might kill one man, a murderer,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the death-bearing father of Pallene, who once,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a lawless sting for the girl, of guilty weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"checked her yoking, and cutting down measureless","st":"48.95"},{"sp":"N","t":"suitors about to be wed, reaped them, beneath whose gore"},{"sp":"N","t":"as they were killed the wrestling-grounds reddened with a clang,"},{"sp":"N","t":"until Bacchus came as the champion of Justice; and standing beside"},{"sp":"N","t":"the father of Pallene, unhappy in love as her marriage neared,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he asked for the reckless wedding of the shuddering girl,","st":"48.100"},{"sp":"N","t":"and held out varied gifts; and to Lyaeus as he asked"},{"sp":"N","t":"the dread man proclaimed a wrestling for the marriage;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leading him he set him in the ill-guesting wrestling-ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where the bold, spear-wielding girl stood"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightening a bridal ox-hide upon her shoulders.","st":"48.105"},{"sp":"N","t":"And then Cypris was the judge of the contest; and in the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"was naked Eros, and he held out a wedding garland to Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wrestling was the bride-adorner; and with a silvery"},{"sp":"N","t":"garment Persuasion cloaked her delicate body,"},{"sp":"N","t":"prophesying the victory of Lyaeus soon to wed.","st":"48.110"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the girl stripped the robes from her sturdy limbs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and laid aside her furious spear for the wedding, and more delicate"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sithonian girl stood unveiled, unsandaled,"},{"sp":"N","t":"womanish, ironless, and with a reddening band"},{"sp":"N","t":"she girdled the wheeling rim of her unbending breasts;","st":"48.115"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her body was uncovered, and the unplaited locks"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her measureless hair flowed down the girl's neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she showed her shins and the fold of her uncovered thighs"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the bare knee appearing; and about her thighs"},{"sp":"N","t":"she fitted a white weaving, the covering of a woman's shame;","st":"48.120"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she had her skin stained with rich oil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her palms far more, so that, when squeezed, the girl's"},{"sp":"N","t":"moist flesh might slip from hands not to be loosed.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[45,34],[51,44],[52,49]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And threatening Lyaeus with grim lips"},{"sp":"N","t":"she stood beside the bride-tending suitor, and cast","st":"48.125"},{"sp":"N","t":"a twofold bond about his neck with a yoked trail of her forearm;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Bacchus, turned back again, loosed his own neck,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flinging off the soft-skinned fingers of the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shaking the neck entwined in womanish bonds;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and winding his two hands crown-wise about her waist","st":"48.130"},{"sp":"N","t":"he shook Pallene with a shifting throb of his feet;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he gripped her rosy palm, and held"},{"sp":"N","t":"a Cyprian comfort, pressing her snowy hand;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did he so much long to roll the child upon the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"as to touch her soft flesh, delighting in a sweet","st":"48.135"},{"sp":"N","t":"labor; and he grew weary, stretching a guile-weaving breath"},{"sp":"N","t":"like a mortal, and left the victory by a willing delay."},{"sp":"N","t":"And lovely Pallene, with the skilled throb of wrestling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted the body of Lyaeus with womanish palms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor did she raise him, so great a weight, but growing weary","st":"48.140"},{"sp":"N","t":"she left the male limbs of unmoving Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god, taking the lovely maiden with an answering"},{"sp":"N","t":"binding knot of his hands, as though lifting a thyrsus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raised her slanting and coiling above his shoulder;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a sparing hand he shook off the sturdy girl,","st":"48.145"},{"sp":"N","t":"and stretched Pallene whole and unshaken upon the ground;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he whirled his gaze with guileful eyelids,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beholding the dust-covered limbs of the soft-haired girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the squalid locks of her uncared-for head."},{"sp":"N","t":"But leaping up again from the dust, turned back,","st":"48.150"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl fixed her steps upright a second time;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Dionysus, with the swift, unsparing rush of his knee,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gripping the belly of Pallene with a shifting throb,"},{"sp":"N","t":"longed to roll the maiden upon the floor,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and shifted his palms, winding them about her sides,","st":"48.155"},{"sp":"N","t":"arching his neck aslant, and about the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of her back he wheeled his fingers, bending them behind,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching to catch either ankle or shin or knee."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the god fell self-rolling, willing, to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquered by a paltry palm; and he had a lovely","st":"48.160"},{"sp":"N","t":"drug of love, and in the sweet dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting the lovely burden of the Loves upon his belly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stayed upon his back, and did not shake off the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but clamped her in the mind-charming bond of longing."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she, with the swift-whirling, guiding throb of her feet,","st":"48.165"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted her step, and loosed the male hand"},{"sp":"N","t":"of love-maddened Lyaeus; and the god, with a lesser rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning her limbs about, stretched out the rosy girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"spreading her on the floor; and the girl lay upon the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"with her hands spread out; and as she stretched upon the earth","st":"48.170"},{"sp":"N","t":"he clamped her neck with a well-handled, yoking bond."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with swifter feet her father rushed into the middle"},{"sp":"N","t":"and reined back his daughter, who wished to contend,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and checked the bridal contest of the wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting the lovely victory to Dionysus,","st":"48.175"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest he should kill her, holding her in an unshaken bond."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at the assent of Zeus, after the prize-bearing victory,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros crowned his brother with the escorting cluster of marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who had accomplished the lovely wrestling of the wedding."},{"sp":"N","t":"And such a contest was like that other, when, rolling","st":"48.180"},{"sp":"N","t":"golden bridal gifts before the girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hippomenes conquered Atalanta as she hurried."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when Bacchus had accomplished the contest of bride-tending"},{"sp":"N","t":"wrestling, still dripping with the bridal sweats of the struggle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he threw down Sithon, struck by the sharp thyrsus,","st":"48.185"},{"sp":"N","t":"the destroyer of suitors; and as he rolled in the dust"},{"sp":"N","t":"he gave the girl the thyrsus, the blood-stained bride-gift of the Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the marriage was much-hymned; and in the never-silent chamber"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Sileni resounded, and the Bacchants danced,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drunken Satyrs wove a hymn of the Loves,","st":"48.190"},{"sp":"N","t":"singing the yoking of a prize-won wedding."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the phalanxes of the Nereids, beneath the ankles of the neighboring isthmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"girdled Dionysus with a bridal dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and uttered a melody; and beside the Thracian sea"},{"sp":"N","t":"old Nereus, the host of Bromius, danced,","st":"48.195"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Galatea, skipping about the bridal sea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sang shrill of Pallene joined to Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Thetis skipped, even though she is untaught of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Melicertes crowned the bridal ridge of the sea-girdled isthmus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying Euoi for the wedding-hymn of Pallene;","st":"48.200"},{"sp":"N","t":"and one of the Hamadryads, beside neighboring flaming Lemnos,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an Athos-nymph, kindled the Thracian bridal pine."},{"sp":"N","t":"And comforting his bride with friendly whispers"},{"sp":"N","t":"as she mourned her father, the Euian-loving consort said:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maiden, do not groan for your father unhappy in love;","st":"48.205"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden, do not groan for the suitor of your own maidenhood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what father ever sowed a daughter and led her to marriage?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Leave your empty grief, since, now your father is slain,"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Sithon, Justice dances laughing,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, kindling the bridal fire with maiden hands,","st":"48.210"},{"sp":"N","t":"she who knows no marriage still sings of your marriage,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beholding another Oenomaus dead;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Oenomaus perished, and with her father destroyed"},{"sp":"N","t":"Hippodameia delights in her freshly wedded consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"And do you too, flinging the longings of your father to the storms,","st":"48.215"},{"sp":"N","t":"delight, joined to a clustered consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning a father's reproach; I need not teach you"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Sithon's hostile love and his delaying of the wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who, lifting a son-in-law-slaying spear with a murderous hand,"},{"sp":"N","t":"made you grow old, untried in Aphrodite,","st":"48.220"},{"sp":"N","t":"and scattered the yoking of your unwedded bed."},{"sp":"N","t":"Look at the rotting remains of the dead suitors,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whom the Paphian adorned and a furious Erinys killed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold those heads, the first-fruits of your halls,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still dripping with the gore of ill-guesting weddings.","st":"48.225"},{"sp":"N","t":"You do not keep the earthly race of Sithon; I am persuaded"},{"sp":"N","t":"that your Thracian Ares, a heavenly one, bore you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that Cytherea travailed with your birth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you have taken the ways of both your parents,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the temper of Ares and the splendor of Aphrodite;","st":"48.230"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that lord Hermes of the contests begot you,"},{"sp":"N","t":"coming to the delicate bed of marriage-perfecting Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and taught you wrestling, the escort of the Loves.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, comforting her griefs with a healing word,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and lulled the lovely tears of the mourning girl.","st":"48.235"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he lingered a time beside his bridal bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"delighting in the love of a freshly yoked wedding."},{"sp":"N","t":"And leaving the halls of Pallene and Thracian Boreas"},{"sp":"N","t":"he came to the house of Rhea, where beside the Phrygian floor"},{"sp":"N","t":"were the Cybelian courts of the goddess blessed in childbirth.","st":"48.240"},{"sp":"N","t":"There, hunting beside the ankles of the Dindymon rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mountain-roaming Rhyndacian maiden Aura grew up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still untaught of Eros, running with the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"fleeing the thoughts of unwarlike maidens,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a younger Lelantian Artemis, whom once the Titan","st":"48.245"},{"sp":"N","t":"Lelantus the elder, having wedded Periboea the offspring"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Ocean, plowed as a storm-footed girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a girl who matched men, untried in Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"She grew up taller than the youth of her own age,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lovely, rosy-armed, always delighting in the hills;","st":"48.250"},{"sp":"N","t":"and often, hunting, she ran down a raving bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sent her furious spear, hurling it at a lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not killing deer and not shooting hares;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but lightening a blood-red quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shot the mountain-running tribes of raw-devouring lions","st":"48.255"},{"sp":"N","t":"with beast-slaying arrows; and in name and in deed"},{"sp":"N","t":"she had the swiftest running, a companion of the mountain breezes."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, in the fire-gleaming hour of thirsty heat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden, sleeping, rested from the labors of the hunt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spreading her body high upon the Cybelian grass,","st":"48.260"},{"sp":"N","t":"laying her head aside upon the shoot of a prudent laurel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she slept at midday, and beheld a lovely, prophetic"},{"sp":"N","t":"vision of a dream of the wedding to come,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that the fiery god, stretching a shaft upon a gleaming string,"},{"sp":"N","t":"furious Eros, was shooting hares within a thicket,","st":"48.265"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting the rank of beasts with paltry arrows;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Cypris, laughing, went as companion with the boy as he hunted,"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the son of Myrrha; and the maiden Aura stood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lightening upon her shoulder, after the bow of Artemis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unaccustomed quiver of Eros the hunter;","st":"48.270"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he killed the beasts, until he had his fill of the bowstring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"striking the grim mouth of leopards and the jaw of a bear,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, having taken a lioness alive with his all-charming cestus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he showed the squeezed beast to his play-loving mother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the maiden thought in the darkness that she too,","st":"48.275"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaning her forearm upon Adonis and Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was goaded by greedy Eros, who bent for Aphrodite"},{"sp":"N","t":"the slave knee of the captive, overbearing lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying such a word: 'Garland-bearing mother of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I bring you maiden-loving Aura, bending her neck to you;","st":"48.280"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, dancers of desire-stricken Orchomenus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"crown the bride-adorning cestus strap, because it has conquered"},{"sp":"N","t":"so great a purpose of an unconquered lioness.'","hvShift5":[[0,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Such a prophetic word the mountain-dweller Aura beheld;"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor was the dream in vain toward the Loves, since they too","st":"48.285"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring a man into the net and hunt a woman."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the girl, waking, raged at the prudent laurel,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fought with the Paphian and with Eros, and was angry"},{"sp":"N","t":"still more at bold Sleep, and threatened the Dream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and was indignant at the leaves, and spoke with a voiceless voice:","st":"48.290"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"It is no cause for blame to behold such dreams beside a myrtle;","hvShift5":[[0,1]]},{"sp":"N","t":"Laurel, why do you rout me? What has Cypris to do with your tree?","hvShift6":[[0,0],[1,0]]},{"sp":"N","t":"I erred in sleeping beneath your neighboring boughs,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hoping your plant was maiden-loving, and I did not find"},{"sp":"N","t":"your report or my hope; so then, Laurel,","st":"48.295"},{"sp":"N","t":"having changed your body, you have found how to change your mind too?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Do you serve bridal Aphrodite after your doom?"},{"sp":"N","t":"This tree is not a prudent woman's, but a freshly wedded bride's;"},{"sp":"N","t":"this dream is worthy of a wanton. Was it Persuasion,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was it your laurel Apollo who planted you with his hand?\"","st":"48.300"},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, angry alike at the plant and at Eros and at Sleep."},{"sp":"N","t":"And once, hunting through the mountains, the mistress of the hunt,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her flesh lashed by the torch of smoky heat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis made ready her car, so that together with the Naiad Nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"she might cool her hot body in mountain-poured baths,","st":"48.305"},{"sp":"N","t":"when it was the middle of the flaming summer, when, brandishing"},{"sp":"N","t":"the fiery midday sound of a rough whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sun blazed upon the back of the Lion."},{"sp":"N","t":"And mountain-roaming Artemis shut her deer in yoking"},{"sp":"N","t":"harness; and mounting the car","st":"48.310"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Aura took both whip and reins,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and drove the horned, stormy wagon."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the unveiled daughters of ever-flowing Ocean"},{"sp":"N","t":"rushed on as handmaids, companions of the archer-goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"of whom one was swift-kneed, going before her queen,","st":"48.315"},{"sp":"N","t":"and another, of equal pace, having drawn up her tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"was near, and another, laying hold of the basket-rail"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the long-greaved wagon, kept a course running with it."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the archer-goddess, lighting up the radiance of her face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flashed above her handmaids, as when upon her airy","st":"48.320"},{"sp":"N","t":"car Selene, sending the flame of vigil-loving torches,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting cloudless beams,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rose at the full in the midst of the fire-fed stars,","hvShift6":[[0,0],[22,19]]},{"sp":"N","t":"dimming the whole heavenly rank with her face;"},{"sp":"N","t":"having a radiance equal to hers Artemis ran through the wood,","st":"48.325"},{"sp":"N","t":"until she came to the place where with a resounding stream"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Zeus-fallen water of the river Sangarius is drawn along."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Aura checked her coiling whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, reining back the deer with golden bridles,"},{"sp":"N","t":"halted the light-bearing car of her queen beside the streams;","st":"48.330"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the goddess ran down from the car; and from her shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"Oupis received the bow, and Hecaerge the quiver,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the daughters of Ocean the well-plaited nets of the hunt;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and Loxo loosed the hounds and the hunting-boots from her feet. &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"And at midday she kept the maiden-loving reverence of modesty","st":"48.335"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the outpourings, creeping into the streams"},{"sp":"N","t":"with sparing steps, and from foot to head,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping her feet about with twin thigh,"},{"sp":"N","t":"drawing up her tunic little by little, dipped at the tip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the girl washed her whole body, hidden by measure.","st":"48.340"},{"sp":"N","t":"And glancing sidelong through the water, sharp-sighted Aura"},{"sp":"N","t":"with the bold eyelids of a shameless face"},{"sp":"N","t":"measured the pure body of the girl none may look upon,"},{"sp":"N","t":"gazing at the wondrous form of her prudent queen;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and spreading her feet with palms outstretched","st":"48.345"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Aura swam together with the swimming goddess."},{"sp":"N","t":"And half-seen, unfinished, within the river bank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"wild Artemis wiped the moist drops from her hair; &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"and near her the huntress Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"handling her breasts, cried a god-fighting voice:","st":"48.350"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Artemis, you have only the maiden-loving name of a girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since you have the female breast of the Paphian, not the male breast of Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because through your bosom the rim of your nipple hangs loose,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and your cheeks shoot forth rosy sparks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but, keeping the body of a desire-stricken goddess,","st":"48.355"},{"sp":"N","t":"do you too rule over weddings with soft-haired Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"receiving some bridegroom into your chamber; and if you wish,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lie beside Hermes and Ares, and leave Athena;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if you wish, lift the shaft and bow of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if you keep the bold sting of an arrow-bearing quiver.","st":"48.360"},{"sp":"N","t":"Be gracious, your form; I am far better than you;"},{"sp":"N","t":"look how I keep a sturdy body; behold a male"},{"sp":"N","t":"shape, and the step of Aura swifter than Zephyrus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"look how my arms swell; behold my breasts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unripe, swelling, unsuckled; indeed you might say","st":"48.365"},{"sp":"N","t":"that yours gush up a milky dew;"},{"sp":"N","t":"how do you keep a soft-skinned palm? How is it that your breasts"},{"sp":"N","t":"have no wheeling circle, such as Aura's,"},{"sp":"N","t":"self-moving heralds of an unplundered maidenhood?\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, mocking; and downcast in silence","st":"48.370"},{"sp":"N","t":"her companion the goddess surged with swelling anger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her eyes shot forth murderous sparks;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she leapt up out of the stream, and put on her tunic again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fitted her girdle a second time to her pure flanks"},{"sp":"N","t":"in grief. And she went after Nemesis; and she found the girl","st":"48.375"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside cloud-high Taurus, where beside neighboring Cydnus"},{"sp":"N","t":"she had stopped the high-necked boast of the threat of Typhaon;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a self-rolling wheel was beside the feet of the queen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"signifying that she drags all the proud down to the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"whirling them from on high with the avenging circle of justice,","st":"48.380"},{"sp":"N","t":"the all-subduing goddess, turning about the journey of life;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about her, beside her throne, flew an avenging bird,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a winged griffin, lifted by the throb of four feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the self-announcer of the flying goddess, that she too"},{"sp":"N","t":"passes through the four-parted seats of the cosmos;","st":"48.385"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping high-crested men with an unloosable bridle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an answering likeness — and as with the whip of evil,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as with a self-rolling wheel, she rolls a proud man down."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess knew, when she saw with a pale face"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis grieving and full of murderous threat,","st":"48.390"},{"sp":"N","t":"and questioning her she soothed her with a friendly word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Your eyes cry aloud your anger, archer-goddess;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, what god-fighting son of Earth routs you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What Typhoeus has sprouted again above the floor?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has Tityos, come back, stretching a love-maddened eye,","st":"48.395"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched the untouchable garment of your mother?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, where are your bows and the arrows of Apollo?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What Orion forces you again? Still he lies,"},{"sp":"N","t":"that wretch who touched your tunic,"},{"sp":"N","t":"a breathless corpse within his mother's flanks; and if some man","st":"48.400"},{"sp":"N","t":"has laid hold of your robes with desire-stricken hands,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raise up another scorpion, the avenger of your girdle;","hvShift6":[[0,0],[8,0],[13,8]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and if bold Otus again or boastful Ephialtes"},{"sp":"N","t":"has longed for the untouchable yoking of your Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kill the suitor of your unwedded maidenhood;","st":"48.405"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if some woman of many children vexes your Leto,"},{"sp":"N","t":"let another stony Niobe weep for her offspring;"},{"sp":"N","t":"what envy is it, if I make another stone above Sipylus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Does your father stir you over a marriage, after the grey-eyed one?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Has Cronion nodded your marriage to Hermes,","st":"48.410"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he did the wedding of pure Athena to Hephaestus?"},{"sp":"N","t":"But if a woman routs you, like your mother Leto,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall be the avenger of the grieving archer-goddess.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"Not yet had her word ceased; and to the evil-averting goddess"},{"sp":"N","t":"the whelp-rearing girl got in first and cried such a word:","st":"48.415"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"All-subduing maiden, steersman of birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"not Zeus, not Niobe routs me, and not bold Otus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tityos did not drag back my deep-robed Leto;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no new Orion forces me, no son of Earth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but mocking me with a sharp word, heavy-mouthed,","st":"48.420"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of Lelantus, ill-maiden Aura, has grieved me;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but why should I tell you all this? For I am ashamed"},{"sp":"N","t":"to speak the shame of my limbs and the reproaches of my breasts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I have suffered a grief like my mother's; for both together,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in Phrygia Niobe grieved Leto the bearer of twins,","st":"48.425"},{"sp":"N","t":"and again in Phrygia god-fighting Aura has grieved me;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but she, changing to a bastard shape, paid the penalty,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of Tantalus, dread in childbirth, and still sheds tears"},{"sp":"N","t":"from stony eyes; but I alone, grieved,"},{"sp":"N","t":"have an unavenged shame, since maiden-loving Aura","st":"48.430"},{"sp":"N","t":"had no stone washed with tears, and saw no spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaiming the reproach of her unsparing throat."},{"sp":"N","t":"But do you, honoring your Titan stock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"grant another favor after my mother's, so that I may behold"},{"sp":"N","t":"the unshaken body of Aura changed to stone;","st":"48.435"},{"sp":"N","t":"and do not leave your own kinswoman mourning,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I see unturned Aura laughing at me again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or let your bronze-forged reaping-hook goad her.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"The goddess heartened her as she spoke, and answered with a word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bed-fleeing daughter of Leto, hound-rouser, sister of Phoebus,","st":"48.440"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall not subdue a Titan child with my sickle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"nor shall I make her a rocky bride in Phrygia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"since I too am born of the most ancient blood of the Titans,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest father Lelantus should ever blame me when he hears;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but one thing I grant you, archer-goddess: the huntress Aura","st":"48.445"},{"sp":"N","t":"has convicted a maiden, and she shall be a maiden no longer;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you shall behold her beside a mountain-poured bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"still mourning her girdle with fountain tears.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, comforting her; and the girl left the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, seated in her four-yoked car of deer,","st":"48.450"},{"sp":"N","t":"and set foot in Phrygia. And in an emulous journey"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden Adrasteia went after hard-fighting Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having yoked contending griffins beneath the bridle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she was carried swiftly through the air in her sharp car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and fixed her course above the heads of Sipylus","st":"48.455"},{"sp":"N","t":"before the stone-eyed face of the daughter of Tantalus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"clamping the crooked bridles of her winged four-footed beasts."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she came near proud Aura; and the high-minded"},{"sp":"N","t":"neck of the wretched girl she struck with a snaky whip,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and dashed her down with the wheeling circle of justice,","st":"48.460"},{"sp":"N","t":"and bent the witless mind of the unbending girl; and about the girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the maiden Argolic Adrasteia whirled"},{"sp":"N","t":"her viperish whip; and doing favor to the goddess,"},{"sp":"N","t":"though she was greatly angry at her brother Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she armed another love, even though she is untaught of the Loves,","st":"48.465"},{"sp":"N","t":"after the bed of Pallene, after Ariadne perished,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the one left behind in her fatherland, the other in an alien"},{"sp":"N","t":"land turned to stone, like the Achaean image of Hera,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and far more after the unaccomplished bed of Beroe."},{"sp":"N","t":"And Nemesis flew beside snow-struck Taurus,","st":"48.470"},{"sp":"N","t":"until she came to Cydnus a second time; and about the girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"Eros goaded Dionysus with a sweet-striking arrow,","hvShift6":[[0,21]]},{"sp":"N","t":"and wheeling his wings he mounted lightly to Olympus.","hvShift6":[[0,0],[24,23]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mountain-roaming god was lashed with a greater torch;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for there was not the least comfort; he had no Cyprian hope","st":"48.475","hvShift6":[[0,0],[16,21],[19,26],[23,27]]},{"sp":"N","t":"of the girl then, no drug of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but Eros burned him the more with a mind-charming torch"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the late-accomplished marriage of raving, disobedient Aura."},{"sp":"N","t":"And laboring he hid his longing, nor in the thickets"},{"sp":"N","t":"did he keep company with Cyprian whispers near Aura,","st":"48.480"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should flee him. What is more cruel than when men"},{"sp":"N","t":"alone long, and women do not desire?"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he kept in his heart the fixed shaft of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the maiden held a hound-rousing course within a thicket;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and as her tunic was lifted by Cyprian winds","st":"48.485"},{"sp":"N","t":"wandering Bacchus was made womanish, gazing at her thigh."},{"sp":"N","t":"And at length, shaken by a seething longing for Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"helpless Bacchus cried a word with a raving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I have the pattern of Pan unhappy in love, because a wind-roaming"},{"sp":"N","t":"maiden flees me, and with a lonely sandal","st":"48.490"},{"sp":"N","t":"wanders unsteady, more than Echo whom none may see."},{"sp":"N","t":"Blessed Pan, far more than Bromius, because in seeking"},{"sp":"N","t":"you found a drug of love in a mind-charming voice;"},{"sp":"N","t":"unsteady, late-speaking Echo answers your sound,"},{"sp":"N","t":"uttering a like chattering echo; would that the maiden Aura","st":"48.495"},{"sp":"N","t":"too belched one word from her lips."},{"sp":"N","t":"This love is not like all others; for she herself"},{"sp":"N","t":"does not grow a temper like that of other maidens."},{"sp":"N","t":"What drug is there for my pain? Shall I charm her"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a Cyprian nod? When, when is Aura ever charmed","st":"48.500"},{"sp":"N","t":"by moving eyelids? Stretching a love-maddened eye,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who leads astray the wits of a bear with bridal whispers"},{"sp":"N","t":"toward the Paphian, toward Eros? Who has conversed with a lioness?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who has spoken a word to an oak? Who has beguiled a breathless pine?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who has persuaded a cornel, and led a rock to marriage?","st":"48.505"},{"sp":"N","t":"What man could charm the uncharmed mind of Aura?"},{"sp":"N","t":"What man could charm her? And to an ungirdled girl"},{"sp":"N","t":"who shall speak of marriage or the cestus, the helper of love?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who of the sweet sting of Eros or the name of the Cyprus-born?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Athena would perhaps be persuaded sooner; nor does fearless","st":"48.510"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis flee me as much as maiden-loving Aura does."},{"sp":"N","t":"Would that with dear lips she might say this one word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Bacchus, you long in vain, do not seek the maiden Aura.'\""},{"sp":"N","t":"He spoke, traveling within a flowery meadow"},{"sp":"N","t":"with springtime winds; and beside a fragrant myrtle,","st":"48.515"},{"sp":"N","t":"resting sweetly at midday, he lulled his feet, and about the tree"},{"sp":"N","t":"he lay, having a whistling Zephyrian breeze,"},{"sp":"N","t":"held fast by both weariness and love; and as he sat"},{"sp":"N","t":"a maiden Hamadryad Nymph, unveiled, peering out"},{"sp":"N","t":"from her age-mate cluster, her own dwelling, spoke,","st":"48.520"},{"sp":"N","t":"bringing faithful things to Cypris and to lovely Lyaeus:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Bacchus can never bring Aura to his bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unless he fetter her heavy-bound in chains none can escape,"},{"sp":"N","t":"winding her feet and hands with Cyprian bonds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"or, yoking her in wedlock while she sleeps,","st":"48.525"},{"sp":"N","t":"steal the maidenhood of the girl without bride-price.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she hid herself again in her age-mate bush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entering her oaken house once more; but Bacchus, weary,"},{"sp":"N","t":"escorted his mind with love-bearing dreams."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the wind-roaming soul of Ariadne who had perished,","st":"48.530"},{"sp":"N","t":"standing beside Dionysus as he slept sweetly,"},{"sp":"N","t":"jealous after her doom, spoke in a dream word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Dionysus, forgetful of your former wedding,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the jealousy of a breeze holds you, and you care nothing for Ariadne;"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas for my Theseus, whom a bitter wind snatched away,","st":"48.535"},{"sp":"N","t":"alas for my Theseus, whom Phaedra got as husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"Perhaps it was not fated for me to escape an oath-breaking consort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"if the sweet youth left me sleeping, and instead of him"},{"sp":"N","t":"I was wedded to Lyaeus, unhappy in love and a deceiver."},{"sp":"N","t":"Alas that I did not have a swift-fated mortal consort,","st":"48.540"},{"sp":"N","t":"and then, arming myself against love-maddened Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I too should have become one of the women of Lemnos."},{"sp":"N","t":"But, mounter of wide-scattered bridal beds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"oath-breaking bridegroom, after Theseus I shall call you so too;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if your bride demands of you a gift of Eros,","st":"48.545"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive from me a distaff, the loving bride-gift of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that you may give, lawless one, to your crag-loving bride"},{"sp":"N","t":"the gifts of your wife the daughter of Minos, so that someone may say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'She gave a thread to Theseus and a distaff to Dionysus.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And you too, changing bed after bed like Cronion,","st":"48.550"},{"sp":"N","t":"have imitated the works of your woman-mad father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an unsated sting for an alternating Aphrodite;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I know the marriage of your Sithonian wife, of the freshly yoked"},{"sp":"N","t":"wedding of Pallene, and the wedding-hymn of Althaea;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall keep silent about the love of Coronis, from whose bed","st":"48.555"},{"sp":"N","t":"the three Graces were born together; but, Mycenae,"},{"sp":"N","t":"cry aloud my doom and the savage eye of Medusa,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and, shores of Naxos, shout of jealous Ariadne"},{"sp":"N","t":"forced into love; bridegroom Theseus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the daughter of Minos calls you, angry at Dionysus.","st":"48.560"},{"sp":"N","t":"But why do I remember Cecropia? For toward the Paphian"},{"sp":"N","t":"I blame them both, both Theseus and Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking she rushed away, wholly like shadowy smoke."},{"sp":"N","t":"And bold Bacchus woke, scattering the wing of Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and pitied mourning Ariadne of the dream.","st":"48.565"},{"sp":"N","t":"And he sought a shifting guile, the escort of the Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he remembered the former bed of the Astacid bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"how he had wedded the lovely girl with a guileful drink,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having sleep as the escort of a drunken-tottering wedding."},{"sp":"N","t":"While Bacchus wished to make ready the guile of the bed,","st":"48.570"},{"sp":"N","t":"meanwhile the roaming Lelantian girl ran"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking a spring, held fast by a gleaming thirst."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did unsteady, mountain-running Aura escape Dionysus"},{"sp":"N","t":"in her thirst; and darting swiftly to the base of a rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"he struck the ground with his thyrsus; and the split hill","st":"48.575"},{"sp":"N","t":"brought forth of its own accord a drunkenness from a fragrant breast"},{"sp":"N","t":"with a purpling stream; and doing favor to Lyaeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"the Seasons, handmaids of Helios, scored with flowers"},{"sp":"N","t":"the topmost brows of the spring, and with fragrant breezes"},{"sp":"N","t":"the sweet air of the freshly planted meadow was lashed;","st":"48.580"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it had the leaves named for the clusters of Narcissus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the graceful youth, whom beside fair-leaved Latmos"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom Endymion sowed upon horned Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"who once, fair of skin, at the deceiving shape of a speechless"},{"sp":"N","t":"water, seeing it molded into a self-perfected pattern,","st":"48.585"},{"sp":"N","t":"died, gazing at the shadowy phantoms of his own form;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and it had the breathing plant of the Amyclaean hyacinth;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and springtime nightingales sang shrill above the leaves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"flying in flocks upon the flowery cluster."},{"sp":"N","t":"And there thirsty Aura ran at midday,","st":"48.590"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about her eyelids Eros poured a mist,"},{"sp":"N","t":"to see whether anywhere in her thirst she might behold a pouring of Zeus"},{"sp":"N","t":"or some spring or the stream of a mountain-poured river."},{"sp":"N","t":"But when she saw the deceiving Bacchic spring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"then indeed Persuasion drove the shadowy cloud from her eyelids,","st":"48.595"},{"sp":"N","t":"crying to Aura such a word, the first herald of marriage:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maiden, come here, and of the marriage-perfecting spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"receive the streams into your mouth, and a consort into your bosom.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"And the girl saw it with joy; and pouring herself beside the spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"she drew up the moisture of Bacchus with opened lips.","st":"48.600"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the maiden, having drunk, uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Naiads, what is this wonder? Whence is this sweet water?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who gushed up this drink? What heavenly belly bore it?"},{"sp":"N","t":"At any rate, having drunk this, I walk to my course no more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but my feet grow heavy, and I am charmed with sweet sleep,","st":"48.605"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I send forth a tottering, soft-sounding echo from my lips.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and had an unsteady journey of her foot;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she went this way and that with much-wandering rushes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her head shaken thickly about her temples;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she laid her head aside, propped near her shoulder;","st":"48.610"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she slept upon the floor beside a long-shooted tree,"},{"sp":"N","t":"entrusting her unguarded maidenhood to a bed on the ground."},{"sp":"N","t":"And fiery Eros, watching heavy-kneed Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leapt down from heaven, and with a calm face,"},{"sp":"N","t":"smiling, spoke, of one mind with Dionysus:","st":"48.615"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"You are hunting, Dionysus; and the maiden Aura awaits you.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking he hastened to Olympus, and shaking his wings"},{"sp":"N","t":"he withdrew, having scored this upon the springtime leaves:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Bridegroom, accomplish the bed while the maiden still sleeps;"},{"sp":"N","t":"silence between us, lest sleep leave the maiden.'","st":"48.620"},{"sp":"N","t":"And seeing her upon her unspread bed on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"plucking the Lethaean wing of bridal Sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iobacchus, soundless, unsandaled, creeping on the tips of his feet,"},{"sp":"N","t":"went after the deaf couch of speechless Aura;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with a sparing hand he laid aside the hollow quiver","st":"48.625"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the maiden, and hid her bow in a hollow rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should shoot him when she shook off the wing of Sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he clamped the girl's feet in unloosable bonds,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and sealed a cord about her palms in coils,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should flee him; and having spread out in the dust","st":"48.630"},{"sp":"N","t":"the heavy-sleeping maiden, most ready for Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he stole the bridal harvest of sleeping Aura."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he was a husband without bride-price; and upon the floor the wretched girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy with wine, unshaken, was wedded to Dionysus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and clamping the body of Aura with shadowy wings","st":"48.635"},{"sp":"N","t":"Sleep was the bride-adorner of Bacchus, since he too"},{"sp":"N","t":"has made trial of the Paphian, and is the yokemate of Selene,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and is the fellow-traveler of the Loves in nightly love;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the marriage was like a dream. And with a much-skipping dance"},{"sp":"N","t":"the hill leapt up into a self-coiling dance,","st":"48.640"},{"sp":"N","t":"and a half-seen Hamadryad shook her age-mate pine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and only the maiden Echo was without a dance among the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and in shame she hid herself untouchable at the base of a rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should behold the marriage of woman-mad Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having accomplished the wedding-hymn upon that soundless bed","st":"48.645"},{"sp":"N","t":"the vine bridegroom, lifting a guarded step,"},{"sp":"N","t":"kissed the lovely lip of his bride, and loosed"},{"sp":"N","t":"her unbending feet and hands, and taking from the rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"the quiver and bow in his hand he laid them beside the bride again."},{"sp":"N","t":"And he came near the Satyrs, still breathing of the wedding,","st":"48.650"},{"sp":"N","t":"having entrusted the bed of sleeping Aura to the winds."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the bride ran up from her love; and she shook off"},{"sp":"N","t":"the limb-loosing sleep, the witness of unheralded Loves;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and beholding with astonishment, outside her prudent girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"her bared breasts and the fold of her uncovered thigh","st":"48.655"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her tunic spotted with a bridal drop,"},{"sp":"N","t":"proclaiming her maidenhood snatched away without bride-price,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she raved as she gazed; and she fitted her circling girdle,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shadowing her bosom again, and the curve of her accustomed breast","hvShift6":[[0,0],[18,16],[20,19],[31,40]]},{"sp":"N","t":"she clasped in vain with the bond of a maiden belt.","st":"48.660","hvShift6":[[0,0],[12,0],[19,3],[42,25],[44,28],[49,50]]},{"sp":"N","t":"And in grief she wailed, held fast by a leap of madness;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she pursued the country folk, and near a fair-leaved bank,"},{"sp":"N","t":"punishing her guileful husband by an avenging ordinance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she cut down shepherds; and with pitiless iron"},{"sp":"N","t":"she killed an oxherd rather, since she had learned that the bridegroom of Dawn,","st":"48.665"},{"sp":"N","t":"graceful Tithonus, was a herdsman unhappy in love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and that Selene the driver of oxen had herself"},{"sp":"N","t":"Latmian Endymion, who was the care of herds of cattle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"she had heard too of the Phrygian, whom another maiden killed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bitter love of Hymnus, the desire-stricken herdsman;","st":"48.670"},{"sp":"N","t":"she killed a goatherd rather, she killed a whole flock of goats,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dread-suffering, because the girl had seen Pan unhappy in love"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping a shape like that of a shaggy-haired goat;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she thought this above all, that, shaken by longing for Echo,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the shepherd Pan had forced her as she slept;","st":"48.675"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she subdued tillers of the soil far more, because they too"},{"sp":"N","t":"serve Cypris, since a man who worked the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Iasion, was the consort of sheaf-bearing Demeter;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she killed a hunter, obeying a more ancient"},{"sp":"N","t":"tale; for she had heard that Cephalus, the citizen of motherless Athena,","st":"48.680"},{"sp":"N","t":"a hunter, was the husband of rose-crowned Dawn;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she cut down the servants of the Bacchic harvest,"},{"sp":"N","t":"because, gushing the dew of unmixed-wine-loving drunkenness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"heavy with wine, unhappy in love, they are the attendants of Lyaeus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for she had not yet learned the guile of Dionysus","st":"48.685"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the deceiving drink of unmixed-wine-loving Cytherea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but she emptied the huts of the crag-loving herdsmen,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sprinkling the hills with blood-red gore."},{"sp":"N","t":"And darting her mind about, held fast by a leap of madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came to the house of Cypris; and loosing the threatening","st":"48.690"},{"sp":"N","t":"belt of the cestus from her freshly spun tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lashed the delicate body of the unconquered goddess;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and snatching the image of marriage-perfecting Cytherea"},{"sp":"N","t":"she came near Sangarius, and gave naked Aphrodite,"},{"sp":"N","t":"rolling in the streams, to the naked Naiads.","st":"48.695"},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the divine statue and the self-coiling whip"},{"sp":"N","t":"she hurled the delicate likeness of Eros into the dust;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she left empty the house of the Cybelian foam-born."},{"sp":"N","t":"And roaming untouchable she entered her accustomed thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and touched her hunting-stakes, and remembered the hunt again;","st":"48.700"},{"sp":"N","t":"and with moist eyelids she groaned for her own maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and wailing sharply uttered so great a voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"What god has loosed the bonds of my maidenhood?"},{"sp":"N","t":"If counseling Zeus, stealing his shape, forced me"},{"sp":"N","t":"as I slumbered upon a lonely bed,","st":"48.705"},{"sp":"N","t":"and did not even respect our neighbor Rhea,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall shoot the vault of stars after the wild beasts;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if Phoebus Apollo lay beside me as I slept,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall sack all rocky Pytho of whom all speak;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if Cyllenian Hermes plundered my bed,","st":"48.710"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall destroy Arcadia root and all with my shafts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and I shall make golden-crowned Persuasion my handmaid;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and if with the bridal guiles of dream weddings"},{"sp":"N","t":"unforeseen Dionysus plundered my maidenhood,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall go where the house of Cybele is, and I shall drive","st":"48.715"},{"sp":"N","t":"gadfly-mad Dionysus from high-crested Tmolus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and hanging a murderous quiver upon my shoulders"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall arm myself against Paphos, against Phrygia; for against both"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall stretch my bow, against Cypris and against Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"With you, archer-goddess, I am more angry, because, girl,","st":"48.720"},{"sp":"N","t":"you did not kill me while I still slept a maiden, nor did you arm yourself"},{"sp":"N","t":"with your pure shafts against my consort himself.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and reined back her trembling voice,"},{"sp":"N","t":"conquered by tears. And filled with the child-bearing seed"},{"sp":"N","t":"of marriage-perfecting Lyaeus, ill-maiden Aura","st":"48.725"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifted a double burden; and the woman raved at her load,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving unchecked, ill-maiden Aura, at the seed, &lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"whether it was self-delivered or from the wedding of a man"},{"sp":"N","t":"or of a guileful god; and she remembered the bride of Zeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Plouto of Berecyntus, dread in childbirth, from whose bed","st":"48.730"},{"sp":"N","t":"Tantalus sprang. And she wished to cut her belly open,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that from a cloven womb, in a witless madness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she might wipe out the unreared, half-finished offspring."},{"sp":"N","t":"And she lifted a sword, and longed to draw the blade"},{"sp":"N","t":"through her bare breast with an unsparing right hand.","st":"48.735"},{"sp":"N","t":"And often she went after the caves of a freshly delivered lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that she might slip into the nets of a willing Fate;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the mountain-roaming beast fled from her in terror,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest she should kill it, and hid in the recess of a rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"leaving its cub to a lonely bed on the ground.","st":"48.740"},{"sp":"N","t":"And often through her swollen woman's bosom"},{"sp":"N","t":"she longed, self-slaying, to drive a willing sword,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so that, self-destroyed, she might escape the reproaches of her belly"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the mockery-loving mouth of the delighted archer-goddess;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she longed to know her husband, so that she too","st":"48.745"},{"sp":"N","t":"might carve up her son for a consort who refused her,"},{"sp":"N","t":"herself both child-slayer and bedfellow, so that someone might say:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Ill-wedded Aura was a new child-destroying Procne.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"And watching her pregnant with new children"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis came near with a laughing face,","st":"48.750"},{"sp":"N","t":"and goaded the wretched girl, and spoke with an unloving voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"I saw Sleep, the chamber-servant of the Paphian, I saw the deceiving"},{"sp":"N","t":"streams of the yellow bridal spring of the Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"where with a guileful drink young girls loose the girdle"},{"sp":"N","t":"of their own age by a bridal dream that snatches maidenhood;","st":"48.755"},{"sp":"N","t":"I saw the slope, I saw where beside the yoking rock"},{"sp":"N","t":"a woman, unforeseeing, is wedded to guileful sleep;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I saw the loving mountain of Cypris, where consorts,"},{"sp":"N","t":"stealing the maidenhood of women, slip away."},{"sp":"N","t":"Tell me, bed-fleeing woman, why do you walk slowly today?","st":"48.760"},{"sp":"N","t":"You who were stormy before, why do you travel heavy-kneed?"},{"sp":"N","t":"You were wedded unwilling, and you do not know your consort;"},{"sp":"N","t":"you cannot hide a secret marriage; for your swelling,"},{"sp":"N","t":"freshly milky breasts proclaim your husband."},{"sp":"N","t":"And tell me, heavy-sleeper, boar-slayer, maiden, bride,","st":"48.765"},{"sp":"N","t":"how do you keep your reddening shape turned pale?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Who defiled your bed? Who snatched your maidenhood?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Yellow Naiads, do not hide the bridegroom of Aura."},{"sp":"N","t":"I know, heavy-burdened woman, your secret consort;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your marriage has not escaped me, even if you long to hide it,","st":"48.770"},{"sp":"N","t":"your husband has not escaped me; weighed down in body with sleep,"},{"sp":"N","t":"unshaken, you were wedded as bedfellow to Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"But leave your bow; and refusing your quiver"},{"sp":"N","t":"celebrate the rites of your woman-mad Bacchus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"bearing drums in your hand and the sound of fair-horned flutes.","st":"48.775"},{"sp":"N","t":"And I beseech you by your marriage-perfecting bed on the ground,"},{"sp":"N","t":"what bride-gifts did your consort Dionysus give you?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did he give you a fawnskin, the self-announcer of your bed?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Did he give you bronze rattles as playthings for your children?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I am persuaded that he gave you a thyrsus, the hurler at lions;","st":"48.780"},{"sp":"N","t":"and perhaps he gave you cymbals, which nurses shake"},{"sp":"N","t":"as drugs for infants' lamentation-loving pains.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, mocking; and the goddess went back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shooting beasts a second time; and in grief"},{"sp":"N","t":"she let her cares go to the airy winds.","st":"48.785"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the mountain-roaming girl, unwitnessed, high upon a rock,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the sharp shaft of a dread-suffering birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"had the dreadful roaring of a lioness in childbed;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the rocks cried back; and answering the sound"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the loud-crashing girl, ill-sounding Echo bellowed.","st":"48.790"},{"sp":"N","t":"And clamping her palms about her womb like a lid,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she shut in the swift travail of her ripening birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and checked the birth just accomplished; for she did not wish"},{"sp":"N","t":"to call hateful Artemis to her travail;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she refused the daughters of Hera, lest ever, as children","st":"48.795"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the stepmother of Bacchus, they should bear down upon her childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the girl in distress broke into a downcast cry,"},{"sp":"N","t":"pierced by the goads of an unaccustomed necessity of travail:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"So may I see the archer-goddess and furious Athena,"},{"sp":"N","t":"so may I behold them both pregnant;","st":"48.800"},{"sp":"N","t":"midwife Seasons, convict Artemis in travail"},{"sp":"N","t":"by the witness of birth, and say to Tritogeneia:"},{"sp":"N","t":"'Grey-eyed maiden, freshly delivered motherless mother.'"},{"sp":"N","t":"So may I see maiden-loving Echo suffering the same,"},{"sp":"N","t":"lying beside Pan or beside evil-beginning Dionysus.","st":"48.805"},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis, you too, when you have borne a child, shall be Aura's comfort,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping female childbed milk from a male breast.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, lamenting the heavy-paining goads of childbed."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the archer-goddess held back the birth, and gave the child-bearing"},{"sp":"N","t":"bride the labor of a birth that was checked.","st":"48.810"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Nicaea, the steersman of the rite of Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"watching the labor and the shame of raving Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"broke into such a hidden, pitying voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Aura, who have suffered the same, do you too lament your maidenhood;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and having in your belly the burden of a dread-suffering birth","st":"48.815"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure, after the bed, to have the goads of childbed too,"},{"sp":"N","t":"endure to hold out an unaccustomed breast to infants."},{"sp":"N","t":"Whence did you too drink wine, the plunderer of my girdle?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Whence did you too drink wine, until you were pregnant, Aura?"},{"sp":"N","t":"You too have suffered, bed-fleeing one, what I suffered; but do you too","st":"48.820"},{"sp":"N","t":"blame the deceiving sleep of the bride-tending Loves."},{"sp":"N","t":"One guile fitted marriage to us both, one husband of Aura"},{"sp":"N","t":"made the maiden Nicaea the mother of children;"},{"sp":"N","t":"no longer do I hold a beast-slaying bow, no longer, as before,"},{"sp":"N","t":"do I draw a string and a shaft; but I am a wretched","st":"48.825"},{"sp":"N","t":"loom-toiling woman, and no longer a furious Amazon.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, pitying the birth-accomplishing labor of Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as one who had herself made trial of a toilsome birth."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the daughter of Leto, hearing the din of heavy-voiced Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"came boastful a second time near the bride;","st":"48.830"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she goaded her as she was worn, and cried with a goading word:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Maiden, who made you a childbed mother of children?"},{"sp":"N","t":"How did she who knows no marriage get milk in her breast?"},{"sp":"N","t":"I have not seen, I have not heard, that a maiden bears a son."},{"sp":"N","t":"Has my father changed nature? Do women","st":"48.835"},{"sp":"N","t":"give birth apart from marriage? For you, maiden-loving girl,"},{"sp":"N","t":"travail with new children, even though you hate Aphrodite."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do women in childbed no longer call upon Artemis,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the steersman of necessary birth, because you alone"},{"sp":"N","t":"have no need of the wild archer-goddess for your delivery?","st":"48.840"},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Eileithyia, the driver of your offspring,"},{"sp":"N","t":"behold your Dionysus from an unmidwifed bosom;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but the thunderbolts midwifed him half-finished."},{"sp":"N","t":"Do not be angry that you bear children among the crags;"},{"sp":"N","t":"Rhea the queen of crags made trial of birth where you do;","st":"48.845"},{"sp":"N","t":"what blame is this ever? You bear children in the mountains,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as the mountain-roaming wife of mountain-ranging Dionysus.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke; and the childbed bride grieved in anger,"},{"sp":"N","t":"ashamed before Artemis even in her pains. Ah, greatly wretched,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she was near her delivery and wished to be a maiden.","st":"48.850"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the babe came to the light more swiftly; for while Artemis"},{"sp":"N","t":"was still speaking her birth-hurling word"},{"sp":"N","t":"the twofold birth of Aura was midwifed self-bidden"},{"sp":"N","t":"as her travail was loosed, whence from the twin children"},{"sp":"N","t":"the high-headed mountain of Rhea was called Dindymon.","st":"48.855"},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess, beholding the new offspring of fair children,"},{"sp":"N","t":"turning back spoke such a word with an answering voice:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Nurse, solitary woman, twin-bearing ill-wedded bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"hold out an unaccustomed breast to your sons, maiden mother;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your boy cries 'papa,' demanding a father of you;","st":"48.860"},{"sp":"N","t":"tell your children your secret consort."},{"sp":"N","t":"Artemis knows no marriage, and did not rear a son at the breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"your bed is these mountains, and instead of an accustomed tunic"},{"sp":"N","t":"the swaddling-clothes of your infants are the varied skins of fawns.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"She spoke, and swift-sandaled entered the thick-shaded wood.","st":"48.865"},{"sp":"N","t":"And calling Nicaea his Cybelian bride,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Dionysus, smiling, showed her Aura in childbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"still blaming her own marriage; and boasting of the freshly wedded"},{"sp":"N","t":"marriage of the lonely girl, he spoke:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"Now at last, Nicaea, you have found a comfort for the Loves;","st":"48.870"},{"sp":"N","t":"now again Dionysus has accomplished a thievish bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and has snatched the marriage of another maiden; and among the hills"},{"sp":"N","t":"the mountain-dweller Aura, who before shunned even the name"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the Loves, has beheld a pattern equal to your chamber."},{"sp":"N","t":"Not you alone received sweet sleep, the escort of the Loves,","st":"48.875"},{"sp":"N","t":"not you alone drank the thievish wine, the snatcher of a girdle;"},{"sp":"N","t":"but from a new spring opened unknown"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bride-tending wine gushed up again, and Aura drank."},{"sp":"N","t":"But since you have known the shaft of necessary birth,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I beseech you by Telete, your dance-weaving daughter,","st":"48.880"},{"sp":"N","t":"hasten to lift up my son, lest my ill-devising Aura"},{"sp":"N","t":"destroy him with bold palms;"},{"sp":"N","t":"for I know that of the twin babes she will subdue one child,"},{"sp":"N","t":"raving unchecked; but do you defend Iacchus;"},{"sp":"N","t":"be the guardian of the better travail, so that","st":"48.885"},{"sp":"N","t":"your Telete may be handmaid both to son and to father.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"So speaking proud Bacchus went back again,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorying in both his Phrygian weddings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"in the elder wife and in the younger bride."},{"sp":"N","t":"And having heavy grief beside the birth-accomplishing rock,","st":"48.890"},{"sp":"N","t":"lifting her children, the childbed mother cried:"},{"sp":"N","t":"\"This marriage is from the air; I shall fling my offspring to the air;"},{"sp":"N","t":"I was wedded to winds and saw no mortal bed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and breezes named for Aura came to her wedding;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and let the winds have my childbed travail.","st":"48.895"},{"sp":"N","t":"Perish from me, new children of a guile-stitching father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"I did not bear you; what have I to do with the evils of women?"},{"sp":"N","t":"Openly now, lions, come free to the pasture"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the wood, with confidence, since Aura fights no more;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and you, quick-eyed hares, are better than whelps;","st":"48.900"},{"sp":"N","t":"jackals, delight in me; and beside my bed on the ground"},{"sp":"N","t":"I shall behold a fearless leopard skipping;"},{"sp":"N","t":"bring the bear as my companion, undismayed; for the bronze-tunicked"},{"sp":"N","t":"arrows of child-bearing Aura have turned womanish."},{"sp":"N","t":"I am ashamed to keep the name of bride after that of maiden,","st":"48.905"},{"sp":"N","t":"lest I ever give my sturdy breast to children;"},{"sp":"N","t":"let me not squeeze out bastard milk with my palm, nor in the thickets,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having been a beast-slayer, be called a child-loving woman.\""},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span>"},{"sp":"N","t":"&lt;...&gt; <span class=\"lac\" title=\"lacuna in the Greek\">†<\/span> She set them beneath a cave as a childbed dinner for a lioness;","st":"48.910","hvShift6":[[0,8]]},{"sp":"N","t":"but a leopard, licking with raw-devouring jaws"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bodies of the new fair offspring of Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having an intelligent heart, midwifed them with a wise breast;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and astonished serpents wheeled about the childbed"},{"sp":"N","t":"with venom-shooting mouths, since, guarding his new children,","st":"48.915"},{"sp":"N","t":"the bridegroom of Aura made even the beasts gentle."},{"sp":"N","t":"And with roaming foot the Lelantian girl leapt up,"},{"sp":"N","t":"having the savage temper of a shaggy-breasted lioness,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and untouchable she hurled into the airy tempests"},{"sp":"N","t":"one child, snatched from the beasts' jaws;","st":"48.920"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the freshly delivered boy, in a whirl of dust,"},{"sp":"N","t":"slipped head first from the air upon the ground;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and snatching him up she entombed him in her own throat,"},{"sp":"N","t":"feasting on a dear dinner. And in terror of the unloving mother,"},{"sp":"N","t":"the maiden archer-goddess snatched the other child","st":"48.925"},{"sp":"N","t":"of childbed Aura, and passing through the thicket"},{"sp":"N","t":"she lightened the boy in an unaccustomed child-tending arm."},{"sp":"N","t":"And after the bed of Bromius, after the whirl of childbed,"},{"sp":"N","t":"shunning the reproach of marriage, the huntress Aura,"},{"sp":"N","t":"keeping the reverence of her ancient maiden-loving modesty,","st":"48.930"},{"sp":"N","t":"came near Sangarius; and together with her back-bent bow"},{"sp":"N","t":"she flung her uncared-for quiver into the outpourings,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and leapt head first into the deep stream,"},{"sp":"N","t":"refusing the light of Dawn with shamefaced eyes,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and was covered by the surges of the river; and Cronion","st":"48.935"},{"sp":"N","t":"changed her into a fountain; and of the mountain-poured spring"},{"sp":"N","t":"her breasts were the jet, her body the outpouring, her hair the flowers,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her bow became the bull-natured horn of the fair-horned"},{"sp":"N","t":"river, and her bowstring, changed, became a rush,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her arrows, become reeds, whistled,","st":"48.940"},{"sp":"N","t":"and her quiver, poured out, rushing through the muddy depth"},{"sp":"N","t":"of the river, murmured into a hollow recess."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the archer-goddess lulled her anger; and about the thicket,"},{"sp":"N","t":"seeking the tracks of crag-loving Lyaeus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"she went, lifting the freshly delivered babe of Aura,","st":"48.945"},{"sp":"N","t":"raising a bastard weight upon her forearm; and in shame"},{"sp":"N","t":"she gave the male child to her brother Dionysus."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the father gave his son to Nicaea, the nursing bride;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she lifted him, and, pressing from the very tip of the nipple"},{"sp":"N","t":"the life-bringing moisture of her child-tending breasts,","st":"48.950"},{"sp":"N","t":"she reared the boy. And taking him high upon a car,"},{"sp":"N","t":"Bacchus set down the infant Bacchus, the son named for his father,"},{"sp":"N","t":"beside Attic Athena, initiate of the mysteries,"},{"sp":"N","t":"as he cried 'Euoi, papa'; and the goddess Pallas received him"},{"sp":"N","t":"within her temple in an unwedded, god-receiving bosom;","st":"48.955"},{"sp":"N","t":"and she held out to the boy the breast which Erechtheus alone had drawn,"},{"sp":"N","t":"dripping self-poured bastard milk from an unripe breast."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the goddess set him down beside the Eleusinian Bacchants;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and about the boy Iacchus the ivy-bearing nymphs"},{"sp":"N","t":"of Marathon wheeled in a dance, and for the freshly born","st":"48.960"},{"sp":"N","t":"god they lifted the night-danced Attic pine;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and they propitiated the god after the son of Persephoneia,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the son of Semele, and set up sacrifices"},{"sp":"N","t":"for later-born Lyaeus and for first-born Dionysus,"},{"sp":"N","t":"and crashed out a new hymn for the third, Iacchus.","st":"48.965"},{"sp":"N","t":"And Athens raved in Bacchic frenzy with threefold rites;"},{"sp":"N","t":"and the citizens struck up a late-accomplished dance,"},{"sp":"N","t":"glorifying Zagreus together with Bromius and Iacchus."},{"sp":"N","t":"Nor did Bacchus forget the Cydonian Loves,"},{"sp":"N","t":"but he remembered his former bride, even though she had perished;","st":"48.970"},{"sp":"N","t":"and he fixed in Olympus the wheeling Crown of Ariadne"},{"sp":"N","t":"who was gone, the witness of his own love,"},{"sp":"N","t":"an unceasing messenger of garland-loving weddings."},{"sp":"N","t":"And the vine god, going to his father's upper air,"},{"sp":"N","t":"touched a single table with his father blessed in childbirth,","st":"48.975"},{"sp":"N","t":"and after the mortal feast, after the earlier pouring of wine,"},{"sp":"N","t":"he drank heavenly nectar from better cups,"},{"sp":"N","t":"sharing a throne with Apollo, sharing a hearth with the son of Maia."}],[]);})();
